{"record_id":"D18-B21-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"HTTP connection reuse","subtopic":"Persistent connections","concept":"Connection reuse reduces repeated transport setup but requires correct lifecycle and timeout management.","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should an HTTP client reuse established connections when protocol and server behavior permit it?","answer":"Connection reuse can avoid repeated TCP and TLS establishment, reducing latency and connection-management overhead. Reuse must remain bounded by connection lifetime, idle timeout, protocol state, and server behavior.","principle":"Reuse connections only while their protocol state and lifecycle remain valid.","constraints":["Connection must be associated with the correct destination and security context.","Idle or expired connections must be removed.","A connection must not be concurrently used in a way prohibited by its protocol/version."],"failure_mode":"Reusing a stale or invalid connection can produce transport errors or protocol failures.","security_implication":"Connection pools must preserve destination and TLS identity boundaries and must not accidentally reuse authenticated state across incompatible security contexts.","performance_implication":"Reuse can reduce handshake and setup overhead, but excessive idle pooling can consume file descriptors and memory.","verification_method":"Test reuse across sequential requests, stale connection eviction, server-side closure, and pool exhaustion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B11-R001","D18-B18-R004"],"relationships":["extends","depends_on"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B21-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"HTTP connection reuse","subtopic":"Stale pooled connection","concept":"A pooled connection may become unusable while idle because the peer or an intermediary closes it.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What failure occurs when a client retrieves a stale connection from a pool?","answer":"The first operation using the connection may fail because the peer has already closed the transport or an intermediary has invalidated the path.","cause":"Peer-side or intermediary-side connection closure while the client retained the connection in an idle pool.","symptom":"Connection reset, broken pipe, end-of-stream, or protocol-level failure during reuse.","solution":"Detect unusable connections, remove them from the pool, and retry only when the operation and protocol semantics permit retry.","security_implication":"Retry handling must not replay non-idempotent operations without explicit application-level authorization.","testing_guidance":"Create a controlled test where the peer closes an idle connection before the client attempts reuse.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B21-R001","D18-B12-R003"],"relationships":["specializes","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B21-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"HTTP request body handling","subtopic":"Streaming request bodies","concept":"Large request bodies should not automatically require full in-memory buffering.","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should an HTTP client stream a request body?","answer":"Streaming is appropriate when request data is large, generated incrementally, or otherwise does not need to be materialized completely before transmission.","requirements":["The transport must support incremental body transmission.","The application must define behavior for partial transmission and failure.","Retry behavior must account for whether the body can be reproduced."],"tradeoff":"Streaming lowers peak memory usage but increases lifecycle and retry complexity.","edge_case":"A connection failure after partial transmission may prevent safe replay of a non-reproducible body.","security_implication":"Streaming implementations must avoid accidental disclosure through logging or buffering layers.","performance_implication":"Streaming can improve memory scalability for large payloads but may increase per-chunk overhead if poorly implemented.","verification_method":"Test small, medium, and large bodies plus mid-stream transport failure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B14-R002","D18-B15-R004"],"relationships":["extends","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B21-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"HTTP response bodies","subtopic":"Resource consumption","concept":"Response bodies are resources that require bounded consumption and deterministic cleanup.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should an HTTP client do with a response body when the body is no longer needed?","answer":"It should consume, close, release, or otherwise finalize the response according to the client library's lifecycle contract so the underlying connection can be reused or released.","failure_mode":"Leaving response bodies unfinished can prevent connection reuse and eventually exhaust the connection pool.","testing_guidance":"Issue many requests while intentionally varying response-body consumption and verify that resource usage remains bounded.","verification_method":"Inspect connection-pool behavior and resource counters after repeated requests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B18-R004"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B21-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"HTTP semantics","subtopic":"Status code handling","concept":"Transport success and application success are different dimensions.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must an HTTP client distinguish transport completion from application-level success?","answer":"A successfully transmitted HTTP response can still represent an application error, authorization failure, validation failure, or unavailable resource.","principle":"Do not equate successful network exchange with successful business operation.","edge_case":"A response may contain a syntactically valid response with a status indicating that the requested operation did not succeed.","verification_method":"Test successful transport with multiple response classes and verify that application logic interprets status semantics explicitly.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROTOCOL_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B14-R001","D18-B19-R005"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B22-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"HTTP/2","subtopic":"Multiplexed streams","concept":"HTTP/2 allows multiple logical streams to share a single connection.","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is HTTP/2 multiplexing?","answer":"HTTP/2 multiplexing permits multiple independent request and response streams to operate concurrently over one underlying connection.","principle":"Logical request concurrency does not imply multiple independent TCP connections.","performance_implication":"Multiplexing can reduce connection establishment overhead and improve utilization, but application concurrency still requires bounded resource management.","edge_case":"One connection failure can affect all streams carried by that connection.","security_implication":"Authentication, authorization, and origin semantics remain application concerns even when streams share a transport connection.","verification_method":"Use a protocol-aware client/server test and verify concurrent streams over a shared connection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PROTOCOL_CONCEPT","related_records":["D18-B21-R001"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B22-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"HTTP/2","subtopic":"Stream lifecycle","concept":"HTTP/2 request state is associated with individual streams rather than only with the connection.","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","objective":"Represent request lifecycle without confusing stream state with connection state.","requirements":["Track stream completion independently.","Handle stream-level errors distinctly from connection-level failures.","Release per-stream resources after completion."],"failure_mode":"Treating one stream failure as equivalent to connection failure can cause unnecessary retries or teardown.","testing_guidance":"Test successful completion, stream-level error, cancellation, and connection loss independently.","verification_method":"Protocol-level integration testing with explicit stream-state assertions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"IMPLEMENTATION_GUIDANCE","related_records":["D18-B22-R001"],"relationships":["specializes"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B22-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"HTTP/2","subtopic":"Connection concurrency","concept":"Application concurrency must be bounded even when a protocol supports multiplexing.","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Does HTTP/2 eliminate the need for client-side concurrency limits?","answer":"No. Multiplexing removes some connection-count pressure but does not remove limits imposed by CPU, memory, server stream limits, application capacity, downstream services, or file descriptors.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","tradeoff":"Higher concurrency may improve throughput until another bottleneck becomes dominant, after which latency or failure rates may increase.","verification_method":"Measure throughput and latency while gradually changing concurrency under a controlled workload.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","related_records":["D18-B22-R001","D18-B18-R005"],"relationships":["extends"]}

