{"record_id":"D18-B22-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"HTTP protocol comparison","subtopic":"HTTP/1.1 versus HTTP/2 versus HTTP/3","concept":"HTTP versions differ in transport and multiplexing behavior while preserving HTTP application semantics.","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What architectural distinction matters when supporting multiple HTTP versions?","answer":"HTTP/1.1 commonly uses request/response exchanges over TCP connections, HTTP/2 provides multiplexed streams over a connection, and HTTP/3 maps HTTP semantics onto QUIC. Implementations must therefore avoid assuming that connection and request lifecycles are identical across versions.","tradeoff":"Supporting multiple versions increases interoperability but expands protocol-state and testing requirements.","verification_method":"Run protocol-specific interoperability and failure tests for every supported version.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PROTOCOL_COMPARISON","related_records":["D18-B22-R001","D18-B22-R004"],"relationships":["compares"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B23-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Resolution lifecycle","concept":"DNS resolution is a multi-stage dependency rather than a simple string-to-address conversion.","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What stages should a network-aware application conceptually account for during DNS resolution?","answer":"The application may need to account for local configuration, resolver selection, cache state, recursive resolution, response validation, address selection, and eventual connection establishment.","constraint":"Application code should not assume that one DNS result is permanent or that resolution always succeeds.","failure_mode":"Temporary resolver failure, negative response, stale cache, unavailable address family, or downstream connectivity failure.","verification_method":"Test positive resolution, negative resolution, timeout, resolver failure, and multiple-address responses.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"NETWORKING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B16-R001"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B23-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"TTL","concept":"DNS records have time-to-live semantics that influence caching duration.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should applications avoid treating a resolved address as permanently valid?","answer":"DNS records can change, and caches are expected to respect their TTL semantics. Long-lived application state should therefore not blindly assume that an earlier resolution remains valid indefinitely.","edge_case":"A service can change addresses while an existing client still holds a previously resolved destination.","security_implication":"Incorrect DNS caching behavior can cause traffic to stale or unintended destinations.","verification_method":"Test behavior across controlled DNS record changes and cache expiration.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"PROTOCOL_CONCEPT","related_records":["D18-B23-R001"],"relationships":["extends"]}

