{"record_id":"D18-B49-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Network recovery","subtopic":"Retry storms","concept":"Independent clients can synchronize retries after a common failure and unintentionally create a traffic surge.","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a retry storm?","answer":"A retry storm occurs when many clients repeatedly retry failed operations in a coordinated or highly correlated pattern, increasing load on an already degraded dependency.","cause":"Immediate or synchronized retries without sufficient backoff and coordination.","solution":"Use bounded retries, appropriate backoff, jitter, circuit breaking, and server capacity signals where semantically safe.","verification_method":"Simulate many clients receiving the same failure and compare retry traffic with and without jittered backoff.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B46-R005","D18-B47-R001"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B49-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Network recovery","subtopic":"Jitter","concept":"Randomized retry delay can reduce synchronization among independent clients.","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is jitter commonly added to retry backoff?","answer":"Without jitter, clients that experience the same failure at approximately the same time can retry together, creating synchronized bursts. Jitter spreads retry attempts over time.","performance_claim_type":"INFERRED","verification_method":"Compare aggregate retry timing under deterministic and randomized backoff using the same simulated client population.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B49-R001"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B49-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Network recovery","subtopic":"Retry budget","concept":"Retries should consume an explicit resource budget rather than continue indefinitely.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is a retry budget?","answer":"A retry budget limits how much additional work the system will spend attempting recovery, using constraints such as attempt count, elapsed deadline, or total retry time.","constraint":"Retry work must remain within the caller's overall correctness and resource budget.","verification_method":"Force persistent failure and verify retries terminate at the configured budget.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B48-R001"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B49-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Network recovery","subtopic":"Retry classification","concept":"Not every network failure should trigger a retry.","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Which factors should determine whether a failed network operation is retryable?","answer":"Retry policy should consider failure type, operation semantics, remaining deadline, dependency health, server signals, resource budget, and whether the operation may already have taken effect.","security_implication":"Automatic retry must not bypass authorization or duplicate sensitive state changes.","verification_method":"Build a failure matrix and verify each failure class maps to the intended retry decision.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESILIENCE_DECISION_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B30-R002","D18-B48-R004"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B49-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Network recovery","subtopic":"Retry observability","concept":"Retry attempts should be observable without exposing sensitive payloads.","knowledge_type":"OBSERVABILITY_PATTERN","question":"What retry information is useful for diagnosis?","answer":"Useful metadata includes operation category, attempt number, failure category, elapsed time, remaining deadline, backoff duration, and final outcome.","constraint":"Do not log credentials, tokens, private keys, or unrestricted request bodies merely to explain retry behavior.","verification_method":"Trigger controlled failures and verify structured retry telemetry.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"OBSERVABILITY_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B29-R004","D18-B49-R003"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B50-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Network architecture","subtopic":"Failure-domain separation","concept":"A networked system should identify independent failure domains rather than treating the entire network path as one undifferentiated dependency.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What failure domains should a distributed network operation consider?","answer":"Relevant domains can include DNS, local host resources, routing, firewall policy, transport, TLS, proxies, load balancers, service instances, downstream dependencies, and application processing.","verification_method":"Create a failure matrix covering each domain and verify diagnostic and recovery behavior independently.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_ARCHITECTURE","related_records":["D18-B29-R001","D18-B40-R005"],"relationships":["integrates"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B50-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Network architecture","subtopic":"End-to-end request model","concept":"A network request should be modeled as a sequence of dependent stages rather than a single atomic operation.","knowledge_type":"MODEL","question":"What stages can compose an end-to-end network request?","answer":"A request may involve name resolution, endpoint selection, route selection, connection establishment, TLS negotiation, protocol exchange, request transmission, server processing, response transmission, response parsing, and application handling.","principle":"Failure at any stage should preserve enough context to distinguish that stage from later stages.","verification_method":"Instrument each stage and inject controlled failures independently.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"NETWORK_SYSTEM_MODEL","related_records":["D18-B29-R001","D18-B48-R001"],"relationships":["integrates"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B50-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Network architecture","subtopic":"Dependency graph","concept":"Network behavior can be modeled as a dependency graph where downstream availability affects upstream request success.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why is dependency-graph modeling useful for networked services?","answer":"It helps identify which external systems, intermediaries, and local resources contribute to an operation and which failures can propagate into user-visible behavior.","verification_method":"Map a representative request path and verify every dependency has an explicit failure policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B50-R001","D18-B50-R002"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B50-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Network architecture","subtopic":"Blast radius","concept":"Network failures can propagate across shared infrastructure and create correlated failures.","knowledge_type":"RELIABILITY_CASE","question":"Why should shared network infrastructure be treated as a potential blast-radius boundary?","answer":"A shared proxy, resolver, load balancer, connection pool, route, or service dependency can affect many otherwise independent application requests simultaneously.","solution":"Identify shared dependencies and design bounded failure, fallback, isolation, and observability mechanisms where appropriate.","verification_method":"Simulate failure of each shared dependency and measure affected request classes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RELIABILITY_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B50-R003","D18-B47-R001"],"relationships":["extends"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B50-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Network architecture","subtopic":"Verification matrix","concept":"Networking correctness requires cross-layer verification rather than isolated unit success.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a world-class networking verification matrix cover?","answer":"It should cover protocol correctness, framing, lifecycle, timeouts, cancellation, retries, idempotency, TLS validation, DNS behavior, routing assumptions, proxy behavior, load balancing, connection pooling, concurrency, backpressure, resource limits, observability, and failure recovery.","verification_layers":["unit","integration","protocol","failure-injection","security","performance","resource-lifecycle","concurrency","end-to-end"],"constraint":"A passing test suite is evidence for tested scenarios, not proof of universal correctness.","verification_method":"Map every important networking invariant to at least one deterministic verification scenario and identify remaining unknowns explicitly.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFICATION_ARCHITECTURE_PRINCIPLE","related_records":["D18-B30-R005","D18-B40-R005","D18-B50-R001"],"relationships":["integrates"]}

{"record_id":"D18-B51-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"HTTP/2","subtopic":"Multiplexing","concept":"HTTP/2 multiplexes multiple request and response streams over a single connection.","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why does HTTP/2 multiplexing reduce application-level head-of-line effects compared with HTTP/1.1 connection serialization?","answer":"HTTP/2 assigns independent logical streams to frames carried over one connection, allowing frames belonging to different requests to be interleaved instead of requiring one response sequence per connection. A transport-level loss can still stall delivery because HTTP/2 normally uses TCP, so multiplexing does not eliminate transport-layer head-of-line blocking.","principle":"Application-layer stream multiplexing does not remove transport-layer ordering constraints.","constraints":["HTTP/2 commonly runs over TCP","Streams share the underlying transport connection"],"failure_mode":"Assuming HTTP/2 multiplexing makes packet loss unable to affect unrelated streams.","solution":"Distinguish HTTP/2 stream multiplexing from TCP transport behavior when analyzing latency and loss.","security_implications":"Connection-level resource consumption remains shared across streams and must be bounded.","performance_implications":"Multiplexing can reduce connection overhead and improve utilization, but TCP loss can still affect multiple streams.","edge_case":"One large or stalled stream may interact with connection-level flow-control limits.","testing_guidance":"Inject controlled packet loss and compare stream-level completion behavior against a non-loss baseline.","verification_method":"Verify that multiple logical streams can be interleaved while transport ordering remains TCP-based.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B11-R*","D18-B45-R*"],"provenance":"HTTP/2 protocol semantics; RFC 9113."}
{"record_id":"D18-B51-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"HTTP/2","subtopic":"Stream lifecycle","concept":"HTTP/2 stream identifiers and lifecycle","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must an HTTP/2 implementation preserve when managing stream identifiers and lifecycle state?","answer":"An implementation must enforce the protocol-defined stream state transitions and stream identifier rules rather than treating streams as reusable arbitrary integer handles.","requirements":["Maintain per-stream protocol state","Reject invalid state transitions","Respect stream identifier directionality and sequencing rules"],"failure_mode":"Reusing a completed stream identifier as though it were a new request.","root_cause":"Application code models stream identifiers as resource handles instead of protocol state identifiers.","solution":"Separate application request identifiers from protocol stream identifiers and maintain explicit lifecycle state.","security_implications":"Malformed stream-state handling can become a protocol robustness or resource-exhaustion weakness.","testing_guidance":"Test valid and invalid stream transitions, duplicate identifiers, closed streams, and protocol errors.","verification_method":"Compare implementation behavior against the HTTP/2 stream state machine.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B51-R001"],"provenance":"HTTP/2 stream state machine; RFC 9113."}
{"record_id":"D18-B51-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"HTTP/2","subtopic":"Flow control","concept":"HTTP/2 flow control","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must HTTP/2 clients and servers account for both stream-level and connection-level flow control?","answer":"HTTP/2 can constrain transmitted DATA through both individual-stream and connection-wide flow-control windows. An implementation that tracks only one level can incorrectly conclude that data is writable while the other window prevents transmission.","failure_mode":"A sender queues indefinitely because the connection-level window is exhausted even though the stream-level window remains available.","root_cause":"Flow-control accounting models only per-stream state.","solution":"Track and update both stream and connection windows and coordinate writes with both constraints.","performance_implications":"Incorrect accounting can cause stalls, excessive buffering, or unnecessary latency.","edge_case":"Many active streams can collectively exhaust the connection window even when each individual stream has capacity.","testing_guidance":"Create multiple concurrent streams and vary window updates while monitoring write eligibility.","verification_method":"Verify that DATA transmission occurs only when both applicable flow-control constraints permit it.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B51-R001","D18-B45-R004"],"provenance":"HTTP/2 flow-control semantics; RFC 9113."}
{"record_id":"D18-B51-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"HTTP/2","subtopic":"Priority model","concept":"HTTP/2 prioritization","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should an HTTP/2 implementation avoid assuming that priority metadata guarantees a particular transmission order?","answer":"HTTP/2 priority mechanisms influence scheduling but do not convert network transmission into an absolute application-level ordering guarantee. Implementations must distinguish scheduling preference from correctness requirements.","principle":"Protocol scheduling hints must not be used as correctness dependencies unless the protocol explicitly guarantees the ordering.","failure_mode":"Application correctness depends on a lower-priority stream never being transmitted before a higher-priority stream.","solution":"Encode correctness dependencies in application or protocol semantics and treat prioritization as a performance mechanism.","performance_implications":"Priority-aware scheduling can improve perceived responsiveness but requires fair resource allocation.","testing_guidance":"Vary stream priorities under congestion and verify that correctness remains independent of scheduling order.","verification_method":"Inspect application invariants and confirm that no correctness property depends solely on stream priority.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B51-R001"],"provenance":"HTTP/2 protocol specification; RFC 9113."}
{"record_id":"D18-B51-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B51","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"HTTP/2","subtopic":"Connection limits","concept":"Concurrent stream limits","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What is the engineering risk of opening more HTTP/2 concurrent streams than the peer permits?","answer":"The peer can constrain concurrently active streams using protocol settings. Clients must handle stream-limit changes rather than assuming unlimited concurrency.","failure_mode":"A client continuously creates streams and receives stream-limit-related failures or queues requests without bounded control.","root_cause":"Concurrency policy ignores peer-advertised protocol limits.","solution":"Track the effective peer limit and apply bounded request scheduling.","performance_implications":"Unbounded concurrency can increase memory usage, queueing, contention, and latency.","security_implications":"Unbounded stream creation can contribute to resource exhaustion.","testing_guidance":"Exercise the client with progressively smaller peer stream limits and verify bounded behavior.","verification_method":"Confirm that request scheduling respects the currently applicable concurrency limit.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B46-R001","D18-B51-R003"],"provenance":"HTTP/2 settings and stream concurrency; RFC 9113."}

{"record_id":"D18-B52-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"HTTP/3","subtopic":"Transport","concept":"HTTP/3 uses QUIC instead of TCP","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What transport distinction is fundamental when comparing HTTP/2 and HTTP/3?","answer":"HTTP/2 is defined over TCP, while HTTP/3 is defined over QUIC. QUIC provides multiplexed streams over a UDP-based transport with transport features implemented above UDP.","principle":"Application protocol behavior must be analyzed together with the transport semantics underneath it.","performance_implications":"QUIC's stream-oriented transport can avoid TCP's connection-wide retransmission blocking between independent streams.","security_implications":"QUIC integrates TLS 1.3 cryptographic handshake mechanisms into the transport design.","testing_guidance":"Test loss affecting one stream and observe whether unrelated streams can continue independently.","verification_method":"Confirm negotiated protocol and transport characteristics from connection telemetry or packet analysis.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B51-R001"],"provenance":"HTTP/3 specification; RFC 9114. QUIC transport; RFC 9000."}
{"record_id":"D18-B52-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B52","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"HTTP/3","subtopic":"QUIC streams","concept":"Independent QUIC streams","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What transport property allows QUIC to reduce cross-stream head-of-line blocking?","answer":"QUIC provides independently managed streams so loss affecting data on one stream does not require unrelated streams to wait for that stream's missing data before their data can be processed, subject to connection and congestion-control constraints.","failure_mode":"Assuming that independent streams imply unlimited independent throughput.","solution":"Model stream independence together with shared connection-level congestion control and flow control.","performance_implications":"Stream independence can improve latency under loss, while shared congestion control still couples overall throughput.","testing_guidance":"Run concurrent streams while selectively introducing packet loss and measure completion behavior without claiming unmeasured performance values.","verification_method":"Verify that stream-level retransmission does not impose TCP-style ordered delivery across unrelated streams.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B52-R001"],"provenance":"QUIC transport specification; RFC 9000."}
{"record_id":"D18-B52-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B52","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"HTTP/3","subtopic":"Connection migration","concept":"QUIC connection migration","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What problem does QUIC connection migration address?","answer":"QUIC can maintain a connection across certain changes in the network path, such as a client changing network address, subject to protocol validation and endpoint behavior. This can reduce the need to recreate application sessions when connectivity changes.","principle":"Transport connection identity can be separated from a single fixed network path.","edge_case":"Migration can fail or be disallowed when path validation or network policy prevents the new path from being used.","security_implications":"Path validation is important to prevent attackers from redirecting traffic or abusing address changes.","testing_guidance":"Test controlled network-interface changes and verify connection behavior through protocol telemetry.","verification_method":"Verify whether the same QUIC connection identifier remains active across a permitted path change.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B52-R001"],"provenance":"QUIC connection migration and path validation; RFC 9000."}
{"record_id":"D18-B52-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B52","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"HTTP/3","subtopic":"ALPN","concept":"Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"How should an HTTPS client determine whether an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 protocol was negotiated?","answer":"The client should rely on the protocol negotiation mechanisms and connection configuration rather than assuming the protocol from the URL alone. ALPN is used for TLS-based application protocol negotiation, while HTTP/3 negotiation also depends on QUIC and HTTP-specific discovery mechanisms.","failure_mode":"Application code assumes HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 without confirming the negotiated protocol.","solution":"Expose negotiated protocol information from the transport/client layer and make protocol-specific behavior conditional on that result.","testing_guidance":"Test servers configured to offer different protocol combinations and verify the client's reported negotiated protocol.","verification_method":"Inspect the negotiated application protocol and transport state.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B52-R001"],"provenance":"TLS ALPN; RFC 7301. HTTP/3; RFC 9114."}
{"record_id":"D18-B52-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B52","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"HTTP/3","subtopic":"Fallback","concept":"HTTP protocol fallback","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should an HTTP client treat HTTP/3 failure and fallback as separate transport events rather than silently classifying them as an application failure?","answer":"A client can encounter transport-specific inability to establish HTTP/3 while an alternative HTTP version remains usable. Observability should preserve the attempted protocol, failure stage, fallback decision, and final protocol so operators can distinguish negotiation or path problems from application failures.","failure_mode":"Telemetry records only a generic request failure and loses the protocol negotiation history.","solution":"Record protocol attempt, transport outcome, fallback path, and final application outcome separately.","observability_requirements":["Attempted protocol","Negotiation result","Transport failure category","Fallback decision","Final protocol","Application result"],"testing_guidance":"Simulate unavailable HTTP/3 while keeping an alternative protocol available and inspect telemetry.","verification_method":"Verify that fallback events remain distinguishable from application response errors.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B48-R*","D18-B50-R*"],"provenance":"Derived engineering practice from HTTP version negotiation and failure classification."}

{"record_id":"D18-B53-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B53","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"DNS TTL and cache lifetime","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What does DNS TTL control?","answer":"DNS TTL provides a caching lifetime associated with DNS data. A resolver or cache uses the TTL to determine how long the data can normally be retained before it needs to be refreshed.","constraint":"TTL is a caching directive and not a guarantee that every network component will immediately forget the record when the TTL expires.","failure_mode":"Treating DNS TTL as an instantaneous global propagation mechanism.","solution":"Design DNS-dependent systems with bounded staleness assumptions and tolerate cached answers during their permitted lifetime.","testing_guidance":"Change DNS records in a controlled environment and observe resolver behavior across multiple cache layers.","verification_method":"Inspect authoritative records and resolver cache state while accounting for the configured TTL.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B11-R*"],"provenance":"DNS protocol and operational semantics; RFC 1034, RFC 1035."}
{"record_id":"D18-B53-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B53","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Negative caching","concept":"Negative DNS caching","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can negative DNS caching affect service recovery after creating a previously nonexistent record?","answer":"Resolvers can cache certain negative DNS responses according to DNS negative-caching rules. Consequently, clients may continue observing name absence for some time after an authoritative configuration has changed.","failure_mode":"An operator creates a DNS record and assumes all clients will immediately resolve it.","root_cause":"A recursive resolver retains a valid negative response in cache.","solution":"Account for negative-cache lifetime during DNS changes and avoid using immediate visibility as the only deployment success criterion.","testing_guidance":"Query through controlled recursive resolvers before and after creating a record and track response transitions.","verification_method":"Inspect response codes and resolver cache behavior over the relevant negative TTL period.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B53-R001"],"provenance":"DNS negative caching; RFC 2308."}
{"record_id":"D18-B53-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B53","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Authoritative versus recursive resolution","concept":"Resolver roles","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the difference between an authoritative DNS server and a recursive resolver?","answer":"An authoritative server provides DNS data for zones for which it is authoritative. A recursive resolver obtains and caches DNS answers on behalf of clients, potentially querying authoritative servers and other DNS infrastructure.","principle":"DNS resolution responsibility is distributed across distinct roles.","failure_mode":"Debugging a stale recursive response by changing an authoritative record without checking resolver cache state.","solution":"Identify the authoritative source, recursive resolver, and client-side cache separately during diagnosis.","testing_guidance":"Query the authoritative source directly and compare it with one or more recursive resolver responses.","verification_method":"Trace the resolution path and identify which component supplied each response.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B53-R001","D18-B53-R002"],"provenance":"DNS architecture; RFC 1034, RFC 1035."}
{"record_id":"D18-B53-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B53","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Search domains","concept":"Resolver search-list behavior","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can a short hostname behave differently across environments?","answer":"Resolver configuration can define search domains that cause a relative or short name to be expanded into one or more candidate fully qualified names. Different environments may therefore generate different DNS queries from the same application-level hostname.","failure_mode":"Application assumes that an unqualified hostname resolves identically on every host.","root_cause":"Environment-specific resolver search configuration.","solution":"Use explicit fully qualified names where deterministic resolution is required, or make resolver configuration an explicit deployment dependency.","security_implications":"Unexpected search-domain resolution can cause requests to unintended domains in some environments.","testing_guidance":"Compare DNS query traces under different resolver search configurations.","verification_method":"Inspect the actual DNS names queried by the resolver.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B53-R003"],"provenance":"DNS resolver behavior; RFC 1034 and platform resolver specifications."}
{"record_id":"D18-B53-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B53","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"CNAME chains","concept":"Alias resolution chains","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What operational problem can excessive DNS alias chains create?","answer":"Multiple aliases can increase lookup work and complicate diagnosis, and DNS processing is constrained by protocol rules and resolver behavior. Service discovery should therefore avoid unnecessarily deep or cyclic naming structures.","failure_mode":"A deployment introduces long alias chains and later cannot easily identify the authoritative endpoint or diagnose resolution latency.","root_cause":"Indirection layers accumulate without an explicit resolution design.","solution":"Keep DNS indirection understandable and validate resolution chains during deployment.","testing_guidance":"Resolve names with tracing enabled and inspect each alias transition.","verification_method":"Verify that the complete chain terminates at an intended address-bearing record without invalid loops.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B53-R003"],"provenance":"DNS naming and resolution semantics; RFC 1034, RFC 1035."}

{"record_id":"D18-B54-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B54","topic":"Service discovery","subtopic":"Dynamic endpoints","concept":"Service discovery lifecycle","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should a service-discovery-aware client do when an endpoint disappears?","answer":"The client should stop selecting endpoints that are no longer valid according to the discovery system, while preserving bounded failure handling for already-established connections. Discovery refresh and connection lifecycle should be treated as related but separate mechanisms.","failure_mode":"A client refreshes discovery but continues indefinitely using dead pooled connections.","root_cause":"Endpoint membership and connection lifetime are modeled as one state.","solution":"Separate endpoint availability state from existing connection state and enforce bounded connection retirement.","performance_implications":"Aggressive discovery refresh can increase control-plane load; slow refresh can prolong use of stale endpoints.","testing_guidance":"Remove an endpoint from discovery while existing connections remain active and observe selection and retirement behavior.","verification_method":"Verify that new requests stop selecting invalid endpoints while existing connections follow explicit lifecycle rules.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B35-R*","D18-B50-R*"],"provenance":"Derived distributed-systems engineering principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B54-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B54","topic":"Service discovery","subtopic":"Staleness","concept":"Discovery staleness","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a service-discovery client still connect to an unavailable endpoint after a successful discovery query?","answer":"Discovery information can become stale between retrieval and connection attempt. Endpoint health can change after discovery, so discovery is evidence about endpoint membership rather than a permanent guarantee of reachability.","principle":"Discovery state and runtime reachability are separate observations.","failure_mode":"Client treats a discovered endpoint as guaranteed healthy.","solution":"Use discovery for candidate selection and retain transport-level failure handling and health feedback.","testing_guidance":"Remove an endpoint immediately after discovery and verify that the client classifies the resulting connection failure correctly.","verification_method":"Correlate discovery timestamp with connection attempt and endpoint outcome.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B54-R001","D18-B49-R*"],"provenance":"Derived distributed-systems consistency and failure model."}
{"record_id":"D18-B54-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B54","topic":"Service discovery","subtopic":"Load distribution","concept":"Client-side endpoint selection","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What trade-off exists when clients select service endpoints themselves?","answer":"Client-side selection can reduce dependence on an additional proxy hop and can use service-specific information, but it moves balancing, health interpretation, connection management, and discovery consistency into clients.","tradeoffs":{"benefits":["Potentially fewer network hops","Service-aware selection","Local endpoint decisions"],"costs":["More client complexity","Discovery consistency requirements","Per-client balancing differences","Health-state coordination"]},"security_implications":"Clients must validate endpoint identity and authorization boundaries rather than trusting discovery data as proof of identity.","performance_implications":"Selection quality can vary with client-local observations and stale state.","verification_method":"Review endpoint selection logic independently from service-discovery retrieval logic.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B54-R001","D18-B54-R002"],"provenance":"Derived distributed networking architecture practice."}
{"record_id":"D18-B54-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B54","topic":"Service discovery","subtopic":"Identity","concept":"Endpoint identity versus endpoint address","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should a service-discovery system avoid treating an IP address as the complete identity of a service?","answer":"Addresses can change, be reused, or represent shared infrastructure. Service identity should therefore be established through the appropriate naming, certificate, authentication, or authorization mechanism rather than inferred solely from an address.","failure_mode":"Authorization policy allows any endpoint possessing a previously trusted IP address.","root_cause":"Network location is incorrectly used as service identity.","solution":"Separate routing location from authenticated service identity.","security_implications":"Address reuse or infrastructure changes can otherwise create unintended trust relationships.","testing_guidance":"Change endpoint addresses while preserving service identity and verify that authorization remains identity-based.","verification_method":"Inspect authentication and authorization evidence rather than only network-address matching.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B54-R003","D18-B19-R*"],"provenance":"Derived zero-trust and service-identity engineering principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B54-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B54","topic":"Service discovery","subtopic":"Failure handling","concept":"Discovery control-plane failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should a client reason about temporary failure of the service-discovery control plane?","answer":"A client should distinguish discovery-control-plane failure from data-plane endpoint failure. Depending on system guarantees, bounded use of previously known endpoints may be possible, but stale endpoint use must remain bounded and observable.","failure_mode":"Discovery outage either causes unnecessary total service outage or unrestricted indefinite use of stale endpoints.","root_cause":"No explicit policy exists for discovery information freshness during control-plane failure.","solution":"Define a bounded stale-data policy with explicit freshness limits, observability, and failure behavior.","testing_guidance":"Disable discovery while keeping selected data-plane endpoints healthy and evaluate client behavior over time.","verification_method":"Verify that stale endpoint usage follows the configured freshness policy.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B54-R001","D18-B54-R002"],"provenance":"Derived control-plane/data-plane resilience principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B55-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B55","topic":"IPv4/IPv6","subtopic":"Dual-stack","concept":"Dual-stack connection strategy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a dual-stack client attempt both IPv4 and IPv6 connectivity rather than permanently preferring one protocol?","answer":"IPv4 and IPv6 availability can differ across networks and paths. A dual-stack strategy can select a working path based on actual connectivity while avoiding indefinite delay caused by an unavailable preferred address family.","principle":"Address-family selection should account for observed connectivity and bounded timing.","failure_mode":"Client waits for an unreachable IPv6 path until a long timeout before trying IPv4.","solution":"Use an address-selection and connection-racing strategy consistent with the platform and protocol standards, while preserving bounded resource use.","performance_implications":"Parallel or staggered connection attempts can reduce connection latency but consume additional temporary resources.","testing_guidance":"Test environments with working IPv4 only, IPv6 only, both, and one family blackholed.","verification_method":"Verify correct address-family selection and bounded fallback behavior in each environment.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B9-R*","D18-B48-R*"],"provenance":"IPv6 address selection and dual-stack connection behavior; RFC 8305."}
{"record_id":"D18-B55-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B55","topic":"IPv4/IPv6","subtopic":"Address representation","concept":"Canonical address handling","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What should application code do when comparing IPv4 and IPv6 addresses?","answer":"Application code should use a protocol-aware IP address representation and comparison operation rather than string equality. Textual representations can differ while representing the same logical address or equivalent address-family semantics.","incorrect_code":"if address_string == '2001:db8::1':\n    allow()","failure_mode":"String-based comparison fails because equivalent addresses can have different textual forms.","correction":"Parse addresses using a standards-aware IP address type and compare normalized address objects or explicitly defined network membership.","security_implications":"Incorrect textual matching can produce authorization or routing-policy bypasses.","testing_guidance":"Test compressed and expanded IPv6 forms, IPv4 representations, invalid addresses, and network-prefix comparisons.","verification_method":"Verify comparisons operate on parsed address semantics rather than formatting.","language":"PYTHON","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B55-R001"],"provenance":"IP addressing semantics; RFC 4291 and standard IP address libraries."}
{"record_id":"D18-B55-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B55","topic":"IPv4/IPv6","subtopic":"IPv4-mapped IPv6","concept":"IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must security-sensitive code understand IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses?","answer":"Some APIs represent IPv4 peers using IPv4-mapped IPv6 forms when operating through an IPv6 socket abstraction. Security policy that checks only one textual address representation can therefore misclassify a peer.","failure_mode":"ACL checks recognize native IPv4 text but fail to recognize the corresponding mapped IPv6 representation.","root_cause":"Policy implementation assumes one address-family representation.","solution":"Normalize and classify peer addresses using the socket/address abstraction and explicit policy semantics.","security_implications":"Representation mismatches can cause access-control bypasses.","testing_guidance":"Exercise security rules with native IPv4, native IPv6, and IPv4-mapped IPv6 peer representations.","verification_method":"Verify equivalent security identities receive equivalent policy treatment where intended.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B55-R002"],"provenance":"IPv4-mapped IPv6 addressing; RFC 4291 and platform socket specifications."}
{"record_id":"D18-B55-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B55","topic":"IPv4/IPv6","subtopic":"Literal URLs","concept":"IPv6 literals in URLs","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How must an IPv6 literal be represented in a URI host component?","answer":"IPv6 address literals in URI host syntax are enclosed in square brackets so that the colons in the address are not confused with the port delimiter.","correct_example":"https://[2001:db8::1]:8443/","failure_mode":"Constructing an IPv6 URL without bracketed host syntax when a port is present.","solution":"Use standards-compliant URI construction rather than manual string concatenation.","testing_guidance":"Test IPv6 URLs with and without ports and with path/query components.","verification_method":"Parse the resulting URI using a standards-compliant URI parser.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B55-R002"],"provenance":"URI syntax; RFC 3986."}
{"record_id":"D18-B55-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B55","topic":"IPv4/IPv6","subtopic":"Testing matrix","concept":"Address-family coverage","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What minimum address-family scenarios should a network library test?","answer":"A meaningful test matrix should cover IPv4, IPv6, dual-stack operation, unavailable preferred family, address-family mismatch, loopback, invalid address input, and security-policy handling for both families where applicable.","test_case":["IPv4 success","IPv6 success","Dual-stack success","Preferred family unavailable","Address family unsupported","Invalid address","IPv4-mapped IPv6 representation","IPv6 URL literal","Policy enforcement per family"],"expected_test_result":"Each supported scenario follows the documented connection, parsing, and security behavior without relying on one address family.","verification_method":"Execute the matrix in controlled network environments when runtime verification is required.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B55-R001","D18-B55-R003","D18-B55-R004"],"provenance":"Derived comprehensive dual-stack testing methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B56-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B56","topic":"NAT","subtopic":"Address translation","concept":"Network Address Translation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does NAT change in a packet path?","answer":"NAT devices translate network addressing information between network domains according to configured state and rules. Depending on the NAT form, translation can affect source or destination addresses and associated transport identifiers.","principle":"The address observed by an endpoint may differ from the address used within another network segment.","failure_mode":"Application assumes the peer's observed network address is always the original endpoint address.","solution":"Use protocol-supported identity mechanisms and explicit proxy or forwarding metadata when original-client identity is required.","security_implications":"Address translation can complicate attribution, ACL design, logging, and end-to-end identity assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Trace packets across controlled NAT boundaries and compare pre-translation and post-translation addressing.","verification_method":"Verify translation behavior using packet capture or authoritative gateway telemetry.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B54-R004"],"provenance":"NAT architecture and address translation standards; RFC 3022 and related IETF specifications."}
{"record_id":"D18-B56-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B56","topic":"NAT","subtopic":"State expiry","concept":"NAT state expiration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a long-idle connection fail after passing through a stateful NAT?","answer":"Stateful NAT devices can remove translation state after an inactivity period. Subsequent packets may then fail to traverse the NAT unless the connection is re-established or the required state is recreated.","failure_mode":"Application assumes an idle TCP connection remains usable indefinitely.","root_cause":"Intermediate state expires while the application-level connection remains logically open.","solution":"Use appropriate keepalive or connection-retirement policies and classify post-idle failures correctly.","performance_implications":"Aggressive keepalives consume network and device resources.","testing_guidance":"Allow an established connection to remain idle beyond the controlled NAT state lifetime and then attempt communication.","verification_method":"Correlate connection failure with NAT state expiration telemetry where available.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B31-R*","D18-B37-R*"],"provenance":"Derived stateful NAT and long-lived connection behavior."}
{"record_id":"D18-B56-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B56","topic":"NAT","subtopic":"Inbound connectivity","concept":"NAT traversal constraints","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can an endpoint behind NAT be difficult to reach from an external peer?","answer":"Inbound packets may not have an existing translation state or explicit forwarding rule that maps them to the internal endpoint. NAT therefore can prevent direct inbound reachability unless the network architecture provides an appropriate traversal or forwarding mechanism.","failure_mode":"External client attempts direct inbound connection without a valid mapping.","solution":"Use explicit port forwarding, a relay, or a protocol-specific traversal mechanism where appropriate and authorized.","security_implications":"Opening inbound mappings expands reachable attack surface and requires access controls.","testing_guidance":"Test connectivity before and after creating a controlled mapping and verify destination behavior.","verification_method":"Inspect gateway mapping and packet path evidence.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B56-R001"],"provenance":"NAT traversal architecture; IETF NAT traversal specifications."}
{"record_id":"D18-B56-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B56","topic":"NAT","subtopic":"Logging","concept":"Original client identity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can application logs show the proxy or NAT address instead of the original client address?","answer":"Address translation or intermediary proxying can cause the application transport peer address to represent the immediate intermediary rather than the original client. Recovering original identity requires a trusted, authenticated mechanism appropriate to the architecture.","failure_mode":"Application treats an untrusted client-supplied forwarding header as authoritative identity.","root_cause":"Transport location and application identity are conflated.","solution":"Accept forwarded client identity only from explicitly trusted intermediaries and define the trust boundary.","security_implications":"Untrusted forwarding headers can enable spoofed source attribution or incorrect access control.","testing_guidance":"Send forged forwarding metadata from an untrusted peer and verify that identity extraction rejects it.","verification_method":"Verify that only trusted intermediary paths can establish forwarded client identity.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B56-R001","D18-B54-R004"],"provenance":"Derived proxy/NAT identity and trust-boundary principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B56-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B56","topic":"NAT","subtopic":"Protocol assumptions","concept":"End-to-end reachability through middleboxes","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is assuming unrestricted end-to-end connectivity a networking anti-pattern?","answer":"Real deployments can contain NAT, firewalls, proxies, load balancers, and policy enforcement points that alter reachability and connection behavior. Software that assumes direct unrestricted paths can fail outside controlled environments.","incorrect_example":"connect_to_peer_forever_without_path_timeout_or_intermediary_awareness()","failure_mode":"Connection attempts hang or fail unpredictably when an intermediary blocks or expires state.","solution":"Model network paths as potentially mediated and enforce bounded connection, read, write, and idle behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test through representative intermediary configurations rather than only direct host-to-host connections.","verification_method":"Verify behavior across documented network topologies.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B48-R*","D18-B50-R*"],"provenance":"Derived real-world network architecture principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B57-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B57","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Path MTU","concept":"Path Maximum Transmission Unit","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the role of MTU in network communication?","answer":"MTU defines the maximum packet or frame payload size supported by a network link or path under the relevant protocol constraints. A path can have a lower effective MTU than an endpoint's local interface.","principle":"Local interface capacity does not automatically equal end-to-end path capacity.","failure_mode":"Application or transport behavior assumes the local interface MTU is valid across every path segment.","solution":"Rely on protocol mechanisms and network configuration that correctly account for path MTU.","testing_guidance":"Test traffic across paths with different MTU constraints and inspect packet sizes and fragmentation behavior.","verification_method":"Measure or inspect the effective path MTU using controlled network diagnostics.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B57-R002"],"provenance":"IP protocol and path MTU specifications; RFC 791, RFC 8201."}
{"record_id":"D18-B57-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B57","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Packet Too Big","concept":"IPv6 Packet Too Big signaling","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What important difference exists in IPv6 packet fragmentation handling?","answer":"IPv6 routers do not fragment packets in transit. Packet size constraints are communicated through mechanisms including ICMPv6 Packet Too Big, allowing the sending endpoint to adjust packet size.","failure_mode":"A network assumes an IPv4-style router fragmentation behavior for IPv6 traffic.","solution":"Ensure ICMPv6 control messages required for path MTU discovery are not incorrectly blocked and ensure endpoints handle them correctly.","security_implications":"Overly aggressive filtering of necessary ICMPv6 messages can break connectivity.","testing_guidance":"Use a controlled lower-MTU path and verify that endpoints adapt rather than relying on router fragmentation.","verification_method":"Inspect ICMPv6 Packet Too Big signaling and subsequent packet-size adjustment.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B57-R001"],"provenance":"IPv6 path MTU discovery; RFC 8201."}
{"record_id":"D18-B57-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B57","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Black-hole detection","concept":"PMTU black holes","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is an MTU black hole?","answer":"An MTU black hole can occur when packets larger than a path segment's supported size cannot successfully traverse the path and the signaling needed for the sender to reduce packet size is missing, blocked, or otherwise ineffective.","failure_mode":"Small requests succeed while larger payload transfers stall or repeatedly time out.","root_cause":"Path MTU exceeds the usable packet size and the sender does not learn the required reduction.","solution":"Investigate path MTU, intermediate filtering, and required control signaling rather than immediately blaming the application protocol.","testing_guidance":"Compare small and large payload behavior while capturing packets and relevant ICMP signaling.","verification_method":"Confirm whether oversized packets are dropped and whether appropriate MTU feedback reaches the sender.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B57-R001","D18-B57-R002"],"provenance":"Derived network troubleshooting methodology based on path MTU discovery."}
{"record_id":"D18-B57-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B57","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Application payloads","concept":"Application chunk size versus network MTU","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should application code avoid treating its write or message size as the network packet size?","answer":"Application writes and protocol messages can be segmented, framed, buffered, or transformed by transport and intermediary layers. The application therefore should not assume that one write corresponds to one network packet.","failure_mode":"Code relies on packet boundaries to infer application message boundaries.","root_cause":"Transport-level segmentation is incorrectly exposed as an application framing guarantee.","solution":"Use explicit application framing and treat network reads and writes as byte-stream or protocol-defined units.","testing_guidance":"Send messages of varying sizes and inspect whether application message boundaries remain correct when network segmentation changes.","verification_method":"Verify framing at the application protocol layer independently of packet boundaries.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B6-R*","D18-B57-R001"],"provenance":"Transport and application framing principles; TCP and HTTP protocol specifications."}
{"record_id":"D18-B57-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B57","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Testing","concept":"MTU-aware network regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should an application test behavior across constrained MTU paths?","answer":"The test should execute representative requests through a controlled path with a reduced MTU, include payloads below and above relevant thresholds, and capture transport/control-plane evidence. The test must distinguish application failure from packet-size or path-signaling failure.","test_case":["Small payload success","Larger payload success when supported","Reduced-MTU path","IPv4 path","IPv6 path","Control signaling available","Control signaling blocked in a controlled diagnostic case"],"expected_test_result":"Application behavior is correctly classified and does not depend on undocumented packet-size assumptions.","verification_method":"Combine application result with packet capture and network diagnostics.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B57-R001","D18-B57-R003","D18-B57-R004"],"provenance":"Derived network regression-testing methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B58-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B58","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"Exhaustion","concept":"Connection pool exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when a client connection pool reaches its configured capacity?","answer":"New requests may wait for an available connection, fail immediately, or follow a configured acquisition timeout policy. The correct behavior depends on the client implementation, but it must be bounded and observable.","failure_mode":"Requests wait indefinitely for a connection from an exhausted pool.","root_cause":"Pool acquisition has no effective timeout or queue bound.","solution":"Define bounded pool acquisition behavior and expose pool saturation telemetry.","performance_implications":"Pool exhaustion can create queueing latency and amplify upstream load.","testing_guidance":"Hold all pooled connections and submit additional requests while measuring acquisition behavior.","verification_method":"Verify that pool acquisition follows the configured timeout and failure semantics.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B37-R*","D18-B48-R*"],"provenance":"Derived connection-pool resource management principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B58-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B58","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"Idle connections","concept":"Idle connection retirement","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should pooled connections have an explicit idle-lifetime policy?","answer":"Long-idle connections can become invalid because of server, proxy, NAT, firewall, or network state changes. A pool should therefore define how idle connections are validated, retired, or reused rather than assuming indefinite validity.","failure_mode":"Pool repeatedly returns stale connections that fail on first use.","solution":"Configure bounded idle retirement and, where appropriate, lightweight validation or failure-triggered eviction.","performance_implications":"Too-short idle retirement increases connection establishment overhead; too-long retention increases stale-connection risk.","testing_guidance":"Place a pool behind an intermediary with a shorter idle lifetime and observe reuse behavior.","verification_method":"Verify that stale connections are retired or recovered according to policy.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B56-R002","D18-B58-R001"],"provenance":"Derived connection-pool and intermediary-state engineering practice."}
{"record_id":"D18-B58-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B58","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Pool size is not equivalent to throughput","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can increasing a connection pool size fail to improve application performance?","answer":"Throughput may be constrained by server capacity, connection-level flow control, CPU, downstream dependencies, network bandwidth, locks, or queueing. More connections can instead increase contention and overload.","tradeoffs":{"larger_pool":["Higher concurrency potential","Lower wait probability under legitimate load"],"smaller_pool":["Lower resource usage","Natural concurrency bound"],"risk_of_excess":["Server overload","Client memory growth","Queue amplification","Connection setup overhead"]},"performance_claim_status":"UNKNOWN","testing_guidance":"Measure throughput, latency, error rate, resource usage, and downstream saturation while varying pool size; do not assume a benchmark result without measurement.","verification_method":"Use controlled load testing and compare measured metrics against the stated objective.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B46-R001","D18-B58-R001"],"provenance":"Derived queueing and resource-management principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B58-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B58","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"Connection ownership","concept":"Pool connection ownership","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is manually closing a pooled connection different from closing a non-pooled socket?","answer":"In a pool, returning a connection to the pool and permanently closing the underlying transport are distinct lifecycle operations. Application code must use the client library's pool-release mechanism rather than assuming generic socket closure semantics.","failure_mode":"Application permanently closes pooled connections, causing pool depletion and unnecessary reconnection.","root_cause":"Underlying transport ownership is confused with logical request ownership.","solution":"Follow the pool API's documented acquisition and release lifecycle.","testing_guidance":"Inspect pool size and connection reuse after repeated acquire/release cycles.","verification_method":"Verify that logical request completion returns the connection to the pool when reuse is expected.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B58-R001","D18-B58-R002"],"provenance":"Derived resource-ownership principle for pooled transports."}
{"record_id":"D18-B58-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B58","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"Leak detection","concept":"Pool leak detection","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence can indicate that application code is leaking pooled connections?","answer":"Useful evidence includes steadily increasing active-connection counts, pool acquisition timeouts, decreasing available capacity without corresponding workload growth, and requests that fail to release resources on exceptional paths.","failure_mode":"Pool exhaustion appears as random downstream network failure.","root_cause":"Resource release is skipped on one or more application paths.","solution":"Instrument acquisition and release lifecycle and use structured cleanup mechanisms.","testing_guidance":"Inject exceptions at each stage between acquisition and release and verify pool capacity returns to its expected state.","verification_method":"Compare active and available pool metrics before and after repeated successful and exceptional request paths.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B58-R001","D18-B58-R004"],"provenance":"Derived resource-leak verification methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B59-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B59","topic":"Ephemeral ports","subtopic":"Client-side port exhaustion","concept":"Ephemeral port exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a client exhaust ephemeral ports?","answer":"A client can consume a large number of local ephemeral transport ports through high connection churn or many simultaneous outbound connections, especially when connections remain in states that prevent immediate tuple reuse.","failure_mode":"New outbound connections fail despite the destination being reachable.","root_cause":"Local transport resource limits are exhausted.","solution":"Reuse connections where appropriate, bound concurrency and connection churn, and diagnose local port-state usage before changing application-level retry behavior.","performance_implications":"Excessive connection creation increases handshake and resource overhead.","testing_guidance":"Generate controlled outbound connection churn and monitor local ephemeral-port and socket-state utilization.","verification_method":"Correlate connection failures with local transport resource exhaustion evidence.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B58-R003","D18-B49-R*"],"provenance":"Derived TCP endpoint-resource management principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B59-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B59","topic":"Ephemeral ports","subtopic":"Connection reuse","concept":"Connection reuse as resource control","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can connection reuse reduce local networking resource pressure?","answer":"Reusing established connections can reduce the number of simultaneous connection establishments and local transport endpoints required for a given request workload. The benefit depends on protocol behavior, workload, server policy, and pool configuration.","performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","failure_mode":"Application opens a new connection for every request despite a protocol and client that support safe reuse.","solution":"Use documented persistent-connection mechanisms and bounded pools where reuse is appropriate.","testing_guidance":"Compare connection churn and resource utilization between controlled reuse and new-connection workloads without claiming universal performance improvement.","verification_method":"Measure connection establishment count, active sockets, latency, and error behavior.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B58-R003","D18-B59-R001"],"provenance":"Derived transport resource-management principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B59-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B59","topic":"Ephemeral ports","subtopic":"Tuple uniqueness","concept":"Transport endpoint tuple","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"Why can the same local port participate in multiple connections in some circumstances?","answer":"Transport connection identity is based on endpoint addressing and transport identifiers rather than the local port alone. A connection is distinguished by its relevant source and destination address/port tuple and protocol state.","principle":"A local port number is not by itself a unique connection identifier.","failure_mode":"Application telemetry groups unrelated connections solely by local port.","solution":"Use a complete transport endpoint identity in connection tracking and diagnostics.","testing_guidance":"Create multiple connections with different remote endpoints and inspect their full endpoint tuples.","verification_method":"Verify connection identity using protocol-aware endpoint fields.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B59-R001"],"provenance":"TCP/UDP endpoint and connection semantics; RFC 9293 and related specifications."}
{"record_id":"D18-B59-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B59","topic":"Ephemeral ports","subtopic":"Retry amplification","concept":"Port exhaustion caused by retries","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can aggressive retries worsen ephemeral-port exhaustion?","answer":"Retries that create fresh connections increase connection churn and consume additional local transport resources while the original failed connections may remain in transport states. The retry mechanism can therefore amplify the underlying resource problem.","failure_mode":"Connection failures trigger immediate new connections until local resources are exhausted.","root_cause":"Retry policy ignores connection-resource consumption.","solution":"Combine retry budgets with connection reuse, concurrency limits, and resource-aware failure handling.","security_implications":"Resource amplification can become a denial-of-service vector against the client or service.","testing_guidance":"Introduce controlled connection failures and observe socket-state growth under different retry policies.","verification_method":"Verify that retry rate and connection count remain bounded during persistent failures.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B49-R003","D18-B59-R001"],"provenance":"Derived retry and transport-resource interaction principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B59-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B59","topic":"Ephemeral ports","subtopic":"Diagnostic procedure","concept":"Local versus remote connection failure","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an engineer distinguish local ephemeral-port exhaustion from remote connection refusal?","answer":"Inspect local socket/resource metrics, transport error codes, connection-attempt counts, and packet-level evidence. A local resource exhaustion pattern should be established before attributing failures to the remote service.","procedure":["Check local ephemeral-port and socket-state utilization","Inspect connection-attempt and failure counts","Capture representative transport errors","Inspect packet path when available","Compare behavior after reducing connection churn"],"failure_mode":"Remote service is blamed without evidence when the local host has exhausted transport resources.","verification_method":"Require at least one independent evidence source beyond the application exception before classifying the root cause.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B59-R001","D18-B59-R004","D18-B50-R*"],"provenance":"Derived network troubleshooting methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B60-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B60","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Correlation","concept":"End-to-end network correlation ID","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should networked systems correlate application requests with transport and intermediary events?","answer":"A single application request can traverse clients, proxies, load balancers, gateways, and services. Correlation identifiers allow evidence from these layers to be associated without assuming that one layer's error message identifies the root cause.","principle":"Cross-layer diagnosis requires evidence correlation across the complete request path.","failure_mode":"Each component logs an isolated timeout with no shared request identity.","solution":"Propagate a controlled correlation identifier and record request lifecycle events at relevant boundaries.","security_implications":"Correlation identifiers must avoid embedding sensitive data and should follow tenant and authorization boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Trace one controlled request through every available network boundary and verify correlation continuity.","verification_method":"Confirm that the same logical request can be reconstructed from independently produced telemetry.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B50-R*","D18-B48-R*"],"provenance":"Derived distributed observability methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B60-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B60","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Latency decomposition","concept":"Network latency decomposition","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC","question":"How should an engineer decompose an unexpectedly slow network request?","answer":"Separate connection establishment, name resolution, protocol negotiation, request transmission, server processing, response transmission, queueing, intermediary delay, and connection acquisition where those measurements are available.","procedure":["Measure DNS resolution","Measure connection acquisition","Measure TCP or QUIC establishment","Measure TLS or protocol negotiation","Measure request transmission","Measure server processing","Measure response transfer","Compare intermediary timing"],"failure_mode":"Total request latency is attributed entirely to network transit time.","root_cause":"Insufficient phase-level telemetry.","solution":"Instrument distinct lifecycle phases and compare them against the overall deadline budget.","performance_claim_status":"UNKNOWN","testing_guidance":"Use controlled requests with phase timing enabled and compare successful and slow samples.","verification_method":"Verify that component durations approximately explain the observed end-to-end latency without inventing missing measurements.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B48-R*","D18-B50-R*"],"provenance":"Derived latency-analysis methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B60-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B60","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Error taxonomy","concept":"Layered network error classification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is required for a useful network error taxonomy?","answer":"The taxonomy should distinguish at minimum name-resolution failure, connection establishment failure, transport reset or refusal, TLS/protocol negotiation failure, timeout, cancellation, protocol response error, intermediary failure, resource exhaustion, and application failure where the evidence supports those distinctions.","failure_mode":"All failures become a generic NETWORK_ERROR category.","root_cause":"Error classification discards lifecycle and protocol evidence.","solution":"Preserve the failure stage, underlying error, peer/intermediary context, retryability classification, and correlation identifier.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at each lifecycle stage and verify that classification remains distinct.","verification_method":"Map observed failures to taxonomy categories using explicit evidence rather than exception-name matching alone.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B49-R*","D18-B50-R*","D18-B60-R001"],"provenance":"Derived network failure-classification methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B60-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B60","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Packet capture interpretation","concept":"Packet evidence versus application evidence","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should packet capture evidence and application logs be treated as complementary rather than interchangeable?","answer":"Application logs describe application-visible state, while packet captures expose network-level events and protocol exchanges. Either can be incomplete: encryption hides payload contents from ordinary packet inspection, while packet capture may not reveal application processing or internal queueing.","principle":"Root-cause analysis should correlate evidence across layers rather than treating one observation source as universally authoritative.","failure_mode":"Engineer concludes that no application error exists because packets were transmitted successfully.","solution":"Correlate packet timing and protocol events with application lifecycle telemetry.","security_implications":"Packet captures can contain sensitive information and require controlled access and retention.","testing_guidance":"Compare packet traces with application logs for the same correlated request.","verification_method":"Require cross-layer evidence when the failure cannot be localized from a single layer.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B60-R001","D18-B60-R003"],"provenance":"Derived layered-network-diagnostics principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B60-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B60","topic":"Network architecture","subtopic":"Final diagnostic model","concept":"Network failure localization matrix","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What structure can an autonomous software-engineering system use to localize network failures?","answer":"A useful diagnostic model maps each request lifecycle stage to expected evidence, possible failure classes, retryability, resource impact, security implications, and verification methods. The model should preserve uncertainty when evidence is insufficient instead of forcing a root-cause conclusion.","implementation_structure":{"stages":["Discovery","Address selection","Connection acquisition","Transport establishment","Security negotiation","Request transmission","Server processing","Response transmission","Connection release","Outcome verification"],"fields":["observed_event","expected_event","evidence_source","failure_class","candidate_causes","root_cause_status","retryability","resource_impact","security_impact","verification_method"]},"failure_mode":"Diagnostic engine selects a root cause solely from a single exception string.","root_cause":"No cross-layer evidence model exists.","solution":"Use evidence-backed lifecycle localization with explicit UNKNOWN and CONFLICTED states.","verification_method":"Validate the diagnosis against independent evidence sources and preserve unresolved hypotheses when evidence is insufficient.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B50-R*","D18-B60-R001","D18-B60-R003","D18-B60-R004"],"provenance":"Derived autonomous network-diagnostics architecture methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B61-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"Forward proxy","concept":"Forward proxy request semantics","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a forward proxy in a client network path?","answer":"A forward proxy receives requests from clients and makes or forwards requests toward external destinations on the clients' behalf. The client is therefore connected to the proxy rather than necessarily connecting directly to the destination.","principle":"A proxy introduces an explicit intermediary boundary into the request path.","failure_mode":"Application assumes the destination peer is directly reachable from the client.","solution":"Model proxy configuration, proxy authentication, proxy reachability, and destination reachability as separate stages.","security_implications":"Proxy policy can control destination access and can become a trust boundary for metadata and traffic handling.","testing_guidance":"Test direct and proxied paths independently and verify that proxy failures are classified separately from destination failures.","verification_method":"Inspect the actual connection path and proxy configuration.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B50-R*","D18-B56-R001"],"provenance":"HTTP proxy architecture and URI/request-target semantics; RFC 9110."}
{"record_id":"D18-B61-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"CONNECT","concept":"HTTP CONNECT tunneling","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is the purpose of HTTP CONNECT when establishing a tunnel through a proxy?","answer":"CONNECT asks an HTTP proxy to establish a tunnel to a target authority. After successful tunnel establishment, subsequent protocol bytes can be exchanged through that tunnel according to the applicable protocol.","principle":"Proxy tunnel establishment and the protocol carried through the tunnel are separate lifecycle stages.","failure_mode":"Application classifies a proxy CONNECT rejection as though the destination service itself rejected the application request.","solution":"Record proxy negotiation outcome separately from tunneled protocol outcome.","security_implications":"Proxy authorization controls which authorities can be tunneled and must not be bypassed by application-level destination handling.","testing_guidance":"Test permitted and denied CONNECT targets and verify separate proxy and destination telemetry.","verification_method":"Verify that tunnel establishment succeeds before interpreting tunneled-protocol behavior.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B61-R001","D18-B61-R003"],"provenance":"HTTP CONNECT semantics; RFC 9110."}
{"record_id":"D18-B61-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"Proxy authentication","concept":"Proxy authentication versus origin authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must proxy authentication be kept separate from origin-server authentication?","answer":"Proxy authentication establishes authorization to use the intermediary, while origin authentication establishes identity or authorization with the destination service. A successful proxy authentication does not establish trust with the origin.","failure_mode":"Application reuses proxy credentials or proxy authentication state as though it authenticated to the destination.","root_cause":"Intermediary and destination security contexts are conflated.","solution":"Maintain separate authentication contexts and credential scopes for each trust boundary.","security_implications":"Credential confusion can expose credentials to unintended parties or create authorization bypasses.","testing_guidance":"Use distinct test identities for proxy and origin authentication and verify that each is sent only to its intended peer.","verification_method":"Inspect authentication exchanges and credential routing at each boundary.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B61-R001","D18-B61-R002"],"provenance":"HTTP authentication and proxy semantics; RFC 9110."}
{"record_id":"D18-B61-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B61","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"Environment configuration","concept":"Proxy configuration precedence","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can the same application behave differently across hosts because of proxy configuration?","answer":"Applications can obtain proxy behavior from explicit client configuration, environment configuration, operating-system settings, runtime libraries, or deployment infrastructure. Differences in configuration can therefore alter the actual request path without changing application code.","failure_mode":"A production request unexpectedly traverses a proxy because runtime configuration differs from development.","root_cause":"Network-path configuration is treated as implicit and undocumented.","solution":"Make proxy behavior observable and define configuration precedence explicitly.","security_implications":"Unexpected proxies can alter trust boundaries, certificate validation paths, and destination visibility.","testing_guidance":"Run tests with direct, explicit-proxy, and environment-configured proxy modes.","verification_method":"Record the effective proxy configuration used for each request.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B61-R001","D18-B60-R001"],"provenance":"Derived deployment and proxy-configuration engineering principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B61-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B61","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"Failure localization","concept":"Proxy failure localization","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC","question":"How should an autonomous diagnostic system distinguish proxy failure from destination failure?","answer":"It should record proxy resolution, proxy connection, proxy authentication, tunnel establishment where applicable, destination connection, and destination protocol stages independently. A failure should be assigned to the earliest evidence-backed failed stage rather than inferred solely from the final client exception.","procedure":["Determine whether proxying is configured","Resolve proxy endpoint","Establish proxy connection","Perform proxy authentication if required","Establish tunnel if required","Establish destination protocol","Evaluate application response"],"verification_method":"Correlate client telemetry with proxy and destination evidence and preserve UNKNOWN when the failed stage cannot be established.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B60-R003","D18-B61-R002"],"provenance":"Derived layered network-diagnosis methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B62-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"Distribution","concept":"Load-balancer request distribution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What does a load balancer add to a network request path?","answer":"A load balancer selects among multiple backend destinations according to configured algorithms and health or availability information. It introduces an additional decision point between the client-facing path and backend services.","principle":"Backend selection is an independent decision from client request generation.","failure_mode":"Client assumes that repeated requests necessarily reach the same backend.","solution":"Treat backend selection as potentially dynamic unless session affinity or another explicit guarantee exists.","performance_implications":"Distribution can improve resource utilization, but poor selection or unhealthy backends can increase latency and failures.","testing_guidance":"Observe requests across multiple healthy and unhealthy backends and verify selection behavior.","verification_method":"Correlate load-balancer decisions with backend request evidence.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B50-R*","D18-B54-R001"],"provenance":"Derived load-balancing architecture principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B62-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"Algorithms","concept":"Load-balancing algorithm semantics","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should an application not assume that all load-balancing algorithms distribute traffic equally?","answer":"Algorithms such as round-robin, least-connections, weighted selection, hashing, and latency-aware selection make different assumptions and produce different distributions. Actual behavior also depends on health state, weights, connection lifetime, request duration, and implementation details.","comparison":{"round_robin":"Cycles through eligible destinations","weighted":"Allocates according to configured weights","least_connections":"Prefers destinations with fewer tracked connections","hash_based":"Maps requests according to a hash key","latency_aware":"May incorporate observed response or network latency"},"constraint":"The exact algorithm and implementation must be verified from the deployed load balancer rather than inferred from the product category.","verification_method":"Inspect configuration and observe controlled traffic distribution.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B62-R001"],"provenance":"General load-balancing algorithm semantics."}
{"record_id":"D18-B62-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"Session affinity","concept":"Session affinity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What trade-off does session affinity introduce?","answer":"Session affinity can keep a client's requests associated with one backend, which may support stateful applications, but it can reduce distribution flexibility and make backend failure or uneven session populations more significant.","tradeoffs":{"benefits":["Backend-local session continuity","Reduced dependence on shared session state"],"costs":["Uneven load distribution","More difficult failover","Stateful backend dependency"]},"failure_mode":"A large population of sessions concentrates on one backend despite otherwise balanced request counts.","solution":"Prefer stateless services where practical or explicitly design and monitor affinity behavior.","performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","testing_guidance":"Create uneven session populations and remove an affinity-selected backend while observing recovery behavior.","verification_method":"Verify both distribution behavior and failure recovery under affinity.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B62-R001","D18-B62-R002"],"provenance":"Derived load-balancing and state-management architecture principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B62-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"Health checks","concept":"Health-check scope","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should load-balancer health checks test the property required for traffic eligibility?","answer":"A process being alive does not necessarily mean that the service is ready to accept the intended workload. Health checks should represent the relevant readiness or serving condition without creating excessive dependency or load.","failure_mode":"Load balancer sends production traffic to a process that responds to a shallow liveness check but cannot serve requests.","root_cause":"Health-check semantics do not match traffic eligibility.","solution":"Separate liveness, readiness, and deep dependency checks according to the architecture's failure model.","security_implications":"Health endpoints should not expose sensitive internal information.","testing_guidance":"Make the service process alive while disabling a critical serving dependency and verify the intended traffic state.","verification_method":"Confirm that health status changes according to the documented eligibility condition.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B62-R001","D18-B47-R*"],"provenance":"Derived health-check and service-readiness methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B62-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"Failover","concept":"Backend failure propagation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens if backend health state changes more slowly than actual backend failure?","answer":"The load balancer can continue routing traffic to an unavailable backend until its health mechanism detects the failure and updates eligibility. Detection interval, failure thresholds, and recovery rules therefore influence observed outage duration.","failure_mode":"Requests fail repeatedly before health state converges.","root_cause":"Health-state detection lag.","solution":"Tune health detection according to measured failure-recovery requirements without creating excessive health-check traffic.","performance_claim_status":"UNKNOWN","testing_guidance":"Fail a backend under controlled conditions and measure health-state transition time.","verification_method":"Compare actual backend state with load-balancer eligibility state over time.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B62-R004","D18-B49-R*"],"provenance":"Derived load-balancer failure-detection methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B63-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Network queues","subtopic":"Queueing","concept":"Queueing delay","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can network latency increase even when packet propagation time is unchanged?","answer":"Packets and requests can spend additional time waiting in client, server, intermediary, transport, or application queues. Queueing delay can therefore dominate total latency during congestion without any change in physical path distance.","performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","failure_mode":"Latency increase is attributed to network distance when queue depth has actually increased.","solution":"Measure queueing and service time separately from transport propagation where possible.","testing_guidance":"Increase controlled workload and observe latency alongside queue depth and resource utilization.","verification_method":"Correlate latency changes with queue and resource evidence.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B60-R002","D18-B63-R002"],"provenance":"Queueing theory and distributed-systems performance principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B63-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Network queues","subtopic":"Bufferbloat","concept":"Excessive buffering","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can excessive network buffering increase latency?","answer":"Large queues can absorb bursts while causing packets to wait for service, increasing latency even when packet loss remains low. This can create a system that appears reliable but responds slowly under load.","failure_mode":"Latency grows substantially while packet-loss metrics remain acceptable.","root_cause":"Queues accumulate excessive backlog relative to service capacity.","solution":"Monitor queueing delay and capacity utilization rather than relying only on packet-loss metrics.","performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","testing_guidance":"Generate controlled congestion and measure queueing latency, throughput, and loss together.","verification_method":"Compare latency against queue occupancy or equivalent network evidence.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B63-R001"],"provenance":"Queueing and network congestion principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B63-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B63","topic":"Network queues","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"End-to-end backpressure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should high-throughput network consumers implement backpressure?","answer":"If incoming data can arrive faster than downstream processing, unbounded buffering transfers the overload into memory growth and latency. Backpressure limits outstanding work and allows upstream producers to adapt or be rejected in a controlled way.","failure_mode":"Consumer buffers indefinitely during producer overload.","root_cause":"No bounded queue or flow-control policy exists.","solution":"Bound buffers and propagate demand or rejection according to protocol and application semantics.","security_implications":"Unbounded buffering can become a resource-exhaustion vector.","performance_implications":"Proper backpressure can prevent memory growth and uncontrolled latency under overload.","testing_guidance":"Produce data faster than the consumer can process and verify bounded memory and queue behavior.","verification_method":"Measure queue size and memory while sustained producer rate exceeds consumer capacity.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B45-R004","D18-B51-R003"],"provenance":"Derived streaming and resource-control principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B63-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Network queues","subtopic":"Queue limits","concept":"Bounded buffering","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified when a network component introduces a bounded queue?","answer":"Verification should establish the maximum queue capacity, behavior when capacity is reached, whether data is dropped or requests are rejected, how backpressure is propagated, and whether queue saturation is observable.","test_case":["Queue below capacity","Queue at capacity","Queue above capacity","Producer cancellation","Consumer failure","Recovery after saturation"],"expected_test_result":"Queue behavior remains bounded and follows explicit overload semantics.","verification_method":"Exercise each saturation state and verify both externally visible behavior and resource limits.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B63-R003"],"provenance":"Derived bounded-resource verification methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B63-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B63","topic":"Network queues","subtopic":"Load shedding","concept":"Controlled overload rejection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can rejecting excess work be safer than accepting unlimited network traffic?","answer":"When capacity is exceeded, bounded rejection prevents queues and resource consumption from growing without limit. The rejected workload can be retried, redirected, or surfaced to callers according to explicit policy.","failure_mode":"System accepts every request and eventually exhausts memory, threads, sockets, or downstream capacity.","root_cause":"No admission-control policy exists.","solution":"Define explicit admission limits and overload responses aligned with request criticality.","security_implications":"Admission control can reduce exposure to resource-exhaustion attacks.","performance_implications":"Load shedding can preserve bounded latency for accepted work at the cost of rejected requests.","testing_guidance":"Exceed capacity under controlled load and verify deterministic rejection and recovery.","verification_method":"Confirm resource usage remains within defined bounds during overload.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B46-R001","D18-B63-R003"],"provenance":"Derived overload-control architecture principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B64-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"Certificate validation","concept":"TLS certificate verification","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What does successful TLS encryption alone fail to prove?","answer":"Encryption alone does not establish that the peer is the intended authenticated service. Certificate-chain validation, hostname or identity verification, trust-anchor handling, and applicable protocol validation are required according to the TLS client policy.","failure_mode":"Client accepts any certificate because the connection is encrypted.","root_cause":"Confidentiality is incorrectly treated as authentication.","solution":"Use standards-compliant certificate and identity validation and fail closed when required validation cannot be completed.","security_implications":"Disabling certificate validation can enable man-in-the-middle attacks.","testing_guidance":"Use a deliberately invalid, expired, mismatched, or untrusted certificate in a controlled test and verify rejection.","verification_method":"Inspect certificate-validation results and confirm that invalid identities cannot establish an accepted secure session.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B19-R*","D18-B61-R003"],"provenance":"TLS 1.3 and PKIX certificate validation standards; RFC 8446 and RFC 5280."}
{"record_id":"D18-B64-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"Hostname verification","concept":"Server identity verification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must a TLS client verify the server identity against the intended hostname or service identity?","answer":"A certificate can be validly signed yet belong to a different service. Identity verification binds the authenticated certificate identity to the destination the client intended to contact.","failure_mode":"Client validates the certificate chain but skips hostname or service-identity verification.","solution":"Use the TLS library's standards-compliant identity verification rather than custom string checks.","security_implications":"Skipping identity verification can allow an attacker with another trusted certificate to impersonate the destination.","testing_guidance":"Present a trusted certificate for the wrong hostname and verify connection rejection.","verification_method":"Confirm that certificate-chain validity and destination identity are both required.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B64-R001"],"provenance":"TLS server identity verification; RFC 6125 and PKIX."}
{"record_id":"D18-B64-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B64","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"Certificate chains","concept":"Trust-chain construction","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why can a certificate be valid when presented alone yet fail validation in a client?","answer":"Certificate validation depends on constructing a trusted certification path using available certificates, trust anchors, constraints, validity periods, and applicable policies. A client may therefore reject a chain that cannot be built or validated under its trust configuration.","failure_mode":"Application assumes that possession of a leaf certificate is sufficient for trust.","root_cause":"Certificate-path validation requirements are not represented in deployment configuration.","solution":"Manage trust stores and certificate chains explicitly and test validation in the target runtime environment.","security_implications":"Incorrect trust-store modification can either break legitimate connections or broaden trust excessively.","testing_guidance":"Test complete and incomplete chains against the exact client trust configuration.","verification_method":"Inspect path-building and trust-anchor validation results.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B64-R001"],"provenance":"PKIX path validation; RFC 5280."}
{"record_id":"D18-B64-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B64","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"Session resumption","concept":"TLS session resumption","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What is TLS session resumption intended to reduce?","answer":"TLS session resumption allows compatible clients and servers to establish subsequent secure connections using previously established session information, reducing some handshake work compared with a full new handshake.","performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","constraint":"Exact latency and CPU benefits depend on protocol version, implementation, network path, and workload.","testing_guidance":"Compare full and resumed handshakes under the same controlled environment without inventing performance measurements.","verification_method":"Inspect handshake messages and negotiated resumption state.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B64-R001","D18-B59-R002"],"provenance":"TLS 1.3 session resumption; RFC 8446."}
{"record_id":"D18-B64-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B64","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"Configuration testing","concept":"TLS configuration regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a TLS regression matrix cover?","answer":"A meaningful TLS matrix should cover valid trust chains, invalid chains, hostname mismatch, expired certificates, unsupported protocol versions where intentionally tested, revoked or policy-invalid credentials where applicable, session reuse behavior, proxy-mediated connections, and certificate rotation.","test_case":["Valid certificate","Untrusted issuer","Hostname mismatch","Expired certificate","Invalid chain","Certificate rotation","Proxy tunnel","Session resumption","Protocol-policy rejection"],"expected_test_result":"Only configurations satisfying the documented TLS security policy are accepted.","verification_method":"Execute against controlled test certificates and protocol configurations and preserve exact evidence for each result.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B64-R001","D18-B64-R002","D18-B64-R004"],"provenance":"Derived TLS security regression methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B65-R001","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B65","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"Nonblocking I/O","concept":"Nonblocking socket state","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why can a nonblocking socket operation require repeated readiness-driven processing?","answer":"A nonblocking socket operation can report that an operation cannot currently complete without blocking. The application must then wait for the appropriate readiness or completion event and continue the operation rather than interpreting temporary unavailability as permanent failure.","failure_mode":"Application treats a temporary nonblocking condition as a fatal network error.","root_cause":"Socket state is modeled as binary success/failure instead of progress state.","solution":"Use the platform's documented nonblocking I/O semantics and event mechanism.","testing_guidance":"Force partial readiness and verify that the application resumes the operation correctly.","verification_method":"Confirm that temporary nonblocking conditions do not incorrectly terminate a valid connection.","language":"PYTHON","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B45-R001","D18-B63-R003"],"provenance":"Python socket documentation and operating-system nonblocking socket semantics."}
{"record_id":"D18-B65-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B65","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"Partial writes","concept":"Partial send","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must a socket implementation not assume that one send operation transmits all requested bytes?","answer":"A stream socket send operation can accept fewer bytes than the application supplied. The application must retain unsent data and continue according to the socket API semantics.","incorrect_code":"sock.send(payload)\nmark_payload_sent()","failure_mode":"Application marks the entire payload delivered when only part was accepted by the socket.","root_cause":"Application equates API call completion with complete payload transmission.","solution":"Use the API's complete-send mechanism or explicitly handle partial progress.","security_implications":"Incorrect framing or incomplete messages can cause protocol confusion or data corruption.","testing_guidance":"Force partial write conditions and verify that the complete logical message is eventually transmitted or correctly failed.","verification_method":"Compare requested byte count with accepted/transmitted byte count at each operation.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B57-R004"],"provenance":"Stream socket semantics; POSIX socket APIs and Python socket documentation."}
{"record_id":"D18-B65-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B65","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"Partial reads","concept":"Stream read boundaries","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can one socket read return less data than the application protocol message requires?","answer":"A stream transport provides an ordered byte stream rather than preserving application message boundaries. A read can therefore return any currently available portion of the stream, subject to API semantics.","failure_mode":"Parser assumes one read corresponds to one complete application message.","root_cause":"Transport framing is incorrectly substituted for application framing.","solution":"Implement explicit message framing or incremental parsing appropriate to the application protocol.","testing_guidance":"Split a logical message across multiple reads and combine multiple logical messages into one read where the protocol permits.","verification_method":"Verify parser correctness independent of transport read boundaries.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B45-R005","D18-B57-R004"],"provenance":"TCP byte-stream semantics; RFC 9293."}
{"record_id":"D18-B65-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B65","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"Resource cleanup","concept":"Deterministic socket cleanup","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should socket ownership and cleanup be explicit?","answer":"Sockets consume operating-system resources and can retain network state. Explicit ownership and deterministic cleanup reduce resource leaks and make failure paths predictable.","failure_mode":"Exception paths leave sockets open until garbage collection or process termination.","root_cause":"Resource lifecycle is not tied to structured ownership.","solution":"Use context-managed or equivalent deterministic resource cleanup mechanisms.","security_implications":"Leaked sockets can expose unintended listening or connected resources and contribute to denial of service.","performance_implications":"Leaks increase descriptor and connection pressure.","testing_guidance":"Inject exceptions before, during, and after connection establishment and verify resource counts return to baseline.","verification_method":"Observe operating-system socket/resource state before and after repeated success and failure paths.","language":"PYTHON","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B58-R005","D18-B59-R001"],"provenance":"Derived deterministic resource-management principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B65-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B65","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"Socket options","concept":"Socket-option portability","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should application code avoid assuming that every socket option behaves identically across operating systems?","answer":"Socket APIs expose platform-specific behavior, option availability, defaults, and semantics. Portable software should use documented cross-platform abstractions where possible and isolate platform-specific options behind capability-aware code.","failure_mode":"A deployment fails because an option assumed to exist is unavailable or behaves differently on another operating system.","solution":"Detect supported capabilities and document platform-specific dependencies.","testing_guidance":"Run socket configuration tests on each supported operating-system family.","verification_method":"Verify option availability and behavior against the supported platform matrix.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B65-R004"],"provenance":"Operating-system socket API portability principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B66-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Service records","concept":"SRV-based service discovery","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What information can DNS SRV records provide for service discovery?","answer":"SRV records can identify service targets together with associated ports and priority/weight information, allowing a client to discover service endpoints without embedding a fixed host and port pair in application configuration.","constraint":"The application must implement or use a resolver/library that understands the service-record semantics.","failure_mode":"Client queries only address records and ignores the service-specific port and selection information.","solution":"Use the appropriate service-discovery record type and preserve its semantics during endpoint selection.","testing_guidance":"Publish controlled SRV records with multiple targets and verify target and port selection behavior.","verification_method":"Compare resolver output with authoritative service-record configuration.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B54-R001","D18-B53-R003"],"provenance":"DNS SRV specification; RFC 2782."}
{"record_id":"D18-B66-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Service records","concept":"SRV priority and weight","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How do SRV priority and weight differ conceptually?","answer":"SRV priority identifies preference among service targets, while weight influences selection among targets at the same priority. Implementations must follow the defined selection semantics rather than treating the two fields as interchangeable load percentages.","failure_mode":"Client interprets weight as an absolute global percentage across all records regardless of priority.","root_cause":"Priority and weight semantics are collapsed into one selection value.","solution":"Implement the protocol-defined ordering and weighted selection process.","testing_guidance":"Create records with multiple priority levels and different weights and verify that lower-priority targets are not selected while eligible higher-priority targets exist under normal semantics.","verification_method":"Compare selection behavior against the SRV algorithm specification.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B66-R001"],"provenance":"SRV record semantics; RFC 2782."}
{"record_id":"D18-B66-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Service discovery failure","concept":"Stale service-discovery records","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can DNS-based service discovery continue returning an endpoint that is no longer serving?","answer":"DNS records describe published naming state and are subject to caching. They do not inherently guarantee that the target is currently healthy at the moment a client connects.","failure_mode":"Client repeatedly selects an unavailable target because DNS still provides a valid cached record.","root_cause":"Naming state is treated as equivalent to runtime health state.","solution":"Combine discovery with connection failure handling or a health-aware discovery architecture.","testing_guidance":"Keep a DNS record valid while making the corresponding service unavailable and observe client behavior.","verification_method":"Correlate DNS answer validity with actual endpoint reachability.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B53-R001","D18-B54-R002"],"provenance":"Derived DNS discovery and runtime-health distinction."}
{"record_id":"D18-B66-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B66","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"Resolver timeout","concept":"DNS resolution timeout budget","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why should DNS resolution have an explicit deadline within the overall request budget?","answer":"DNS resolution consumes part of the end-to-end request time. Without a bounded resolution budget, the application can spend most or all of its request deadline waiting for name resolution before transport establishment begins.","failure_mode":"Application has a total request timeout but DNS resolution can consume an uncontrolled portion of it.","root_cause":"Timeout is applied only after DNS resolution completes.","solution":"Propagate the overall deadline into the resolver operation where the runtime and resolver architecture permit it.","performance_implications":"Unbounded resolution delay increases tail latency and can reduce useful work time for downstream operations.","testing_guidance":"Delay DNS responses and verify that request cancellation occurs within the end-to-end deadline.","verification_method":"Compare DNS duration against remaining request budget.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D18-B60-R002"],"provenance":"Derived deadline-propagation principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B66-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B66","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"DNS failure taxonomy","concept":"DNS error classification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should NXDOMAIN, timeout, SERVFAIL, and transport failure not be collapsed into one DNS error?","answer":"These outcomes imply different evidence and possible causes. NXDOMAIN indicates that the queried name does not exist according to the responding DNS authority context, while timeout and SERVFAIL represent different classes of resolution failure. Accurate classification improves diagnosis and retry policy.","failure_mode":"All DNS errors trigger identical retries and alerts.","root_cause":"Resolver response semantics are discarded.","solution":"Preserve DNS response code, transport outcome, resolver identity, query name/type, and timing in diagnostics.","testing_guidance":"Inject each failure category independently and verify distinct classification.","verification_method":"Map observed resolver evidence to explicit DNS failure categories.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B53-R003","D18-B66-R004"],"provenance":"DNS response-code and resolver semantics; RFC 1035 and related DNS standards."}

{"record_id":"D18-B67-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"HTTP intermediaries","subtopic":"Request transformation","concept":"Intermediary request transformation","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can HTTP intermediaries affect the representation of a request or response?","answer":"Proxies, gateways, caches, and other intermediaries can process HTTP messages according to protocol rules and configured policies. Therefore the bytes or metadata observed at one network boundary are not necessarily identical to those observed at another.","principle":"End-to-end application semantics must be distinguished from hop-by-hop intermediary behavior.","failure_mode":"Application assumes headers or transport properties observed at the client must be identical at the origin server.","solution":"Document trusted intermediary transformations and preserve end-to-end security and identity semantics.","security_implications":"Unexpected transformations can affect authentication, caching, routing, and security policy.","testing_guidance":"Compare request and response metadata across each trusted intermediary boundary.","verification_method":"Capture or inspect message metadata at controlled network boundaries.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B61-R001","D18-B61-R003"],"provenance":"HTTP intermediary semantics; RFC 9110."}
{"record_id":"D18-B67-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B67","topic":"HTTP intermediaries","subtopic":"Hop-by-hop metadata","concept":"Hop-by-hop versus end-to-end fields","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must HTTP intermediaries distinguish hop-by-hop controls from end-to-end metadata?","answer":"Some HTTP control information applies only to a particular connection or intermediary hop, while other metadata is intended to travel with the request or response. Treating hop-scoped information as end-to-end state can produce protocol errors and incorrect behavior.","failure_mode":"Intermediary forwards connection-specific control metadata to an unrelated downstream hop.","root_cause":"Message forwarding does not account for hop scope.","solution":"Use a standards-compliant HTTP intermediary implementation and explicitly handle connection-specific controls.","security_implications":"Incorrect forwarding can create request-smuggling or policy-confusion risks in vulnerable implementations.","testing_guidance":"Send messages containing hop-specific controls through a controlled proxy chain and inspect forwarding behavior.","verification_method":"Verify forwarding against the HTTP specification and intermediary configuration.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B67-R001"],"provenance":"HTTP field forwarding and connection-option semantics; RFC 9110."}
{"record_id":"D18-B67-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B67","topic":"HTTP intermediaries","subtopic":"Request smuggling","concept":"Message-framing consistency","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is consistent HTTP message framing important across intermediaries?","answer":"Different interpretations of message boundaries by a frontend and backend can cause one component to parse a request differently from another. Security-sensitive HTTP stacks must therefore implement consistent standards-compliant framing and reject ambiguous or invalid combinations rather than relying on parser tolerance.","failure_mode":"Frontend and backend disagree about where one HTTP request ends and another begins.","root_cause":"Inconsistent message-framing interpretation across HTTP components.","solution":"Use compliant parsers, reject ambiguous framing, and test multi-hop parsing behavior.","security_implications":"Parser disagreement can create request-smuggling vulnerabilities.","testing_guidance":"Use safe protocol-conformance tests containing ambiguous framing cases and verify consistent rejection or interpretation.","verification_method":"Compare frontend and backend parsing results for the same controlled messages.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B67-R001","D18-B67-R002"],"provenance":"HTTP message framing and request-smuggling security guidance; RFC 9112 and related security literature."}
{"record_id":"D18-B67-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"HTTP intermediaries","subtopic":"Header limits","concept":"Header-size limits","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should HTTP systems define header-size limits?","answer":"HTTP headers consume memory and parsing resources. Without bounded limits, unusually large or numerous fields can consume excessive resources or create inconsistent behavior between intermediaries.","failure_mode":"One component accepts a large header set while another rejects it, causing interoperability or security problems.","root_cause":"Header-size policy is undefined or inconsistent across network layers.","solution":"Define and consistently enforce documented header count and size limits appropriate to the application.","security_implications":"Unbounded parsing can contribute to resource exhaustion and parser inconsistencies.","testing_guidance":"Test near-limit, over-limit, and multi-intermediary header sets.","verification_method":"Verify consistent limits and failure behavior across all HTTP components.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B67-R003"],"provenance":"Derived HTTP parser resource-bound methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B67-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"HTTP intermediaries","subtopic":"Protocol consistency","concept":"Frontend/backend HTTP compatibility","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should be tested when multiple HTTP implementations are chained?","answer":"Tests should verify request framing, header parsing, method semantics, authority handling, transfer behavior, connection reuse, error responses, and security-sensitive normalization across every frontend/backend combination.","test_case":["Valid request forwarding","Malformed framing","Large headers","Repeated headers","Connection reuse","Authentication metadata","Redirect handling","Protocol-version transition","Backend rejection"],"expected_test_result":"All components interpret shared requests consistently according to their documented protocol versions and security policies.","verification_method":"Use identical controlled requests at each boundary and compare parser and forwarding results.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B67-R001","D18-B67-R003","D18-B67-R004"],"provenance":"Derived multi-intermediary compatibility testing methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B68-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Network concurrency","subtopic":"Connection concurrency","concept":"Concurrency budget","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should a network client define a concurrency budget instead of allowing unlimited simultaneous requests?","answer":"Each active request consumes client, transport, server, and intermediary resources. An explicit concurrency budget limits resource growth and prevents application-level concurrency from overwhelming downstream systems.","failure_mode":"Workload generates more simultaneous requests as latency rises, causing queue and resource amplification.","root_cause":"Concurrency is uncoupled from capacity and deadlines.","solution":"Define bounded concurrency and coordinate it with connection-pool capacity, rate limits, and deadlines.","security_implications":"Concurrency limits reduce resource-exhaustion exposure.","performance_implications":"The optimal limit is workload-dependent and must be measured rather than assumed.","testing_guidance":"Increase concurrent requests progressively and observe latency, errors, and resource usage.","verification_method":"Verify that concurrency remains within the configured bound under both normal and failure conditions.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B46-R001","D18-B58-R001","D18-B63-R003"],"provenance":"Derived concurrency-control methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B68-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B68","topic":"Network concurrency","subtopic":"Concurrency versus rate","concept":"Concurrency-rate distinction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between a concurrency limit and a rate limit?","answer":"A concurrency limit bounds the number of operations simultaneously in progress, while a rate limit bounds how frequently operations may begin or occur over a time interval. Either can be insufficient alone when request duration and burst behavior vary.","comparison":{"concurrency":"Limits active work","rate":"Limits operation frequency","interaction":"High latency can make a fixed concurrency limit produce a lower start rate; a high rate with slow requests can create excessive concurrency"},"verification_method":"Measure both active work and operation starts when diagnosing overload.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B46-R001","D18-B68-R001"],"provenance":"Derived traffic-control distinction."}
{"record_id":"D18-B68-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Network concurrency","subtopic":"Cancellation","concept":"Cancellation propagation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should cancellation propagate through the entire network request lifecycle?","answer":"If a caller no longer needs a result, continuing DNS resolution, connection acquisition, transmission, server-side work where cancellable, or response buffering can waste resources. Cancellation should therefore propagate to each operation that can safely stop.","failure_mode":"Caller cancels but network operation continues until its original timeout.","root_cause":"Cancellation exists only at the outer API boundary.","solution":"Propagate cancellation through resolver, pool, transport, protocol, and application layers according to their supported semantics.","performance_implications":"Proper cancellation can release resources earlier and reduce unnecessary work.","testing_guidance":"Cancel requests at each lifecycle stage and verify that underlying resources are released.","verification_method":"Inspect transport and pool state after cancellation and verify bounded cleanup.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B48-R002","D18-B65-R004"],"provenance":"Derived asynchronous cancellation and resource-lifecycle principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B68-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Network concurrency","subtopic":"Cancellation versus timeout","concept":"Cancellation classification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should cancellation be distinguished from timeout?","answer":"Cancellation means the operation was intentionally stopped by a controlling caller or system policy, while timeout means an operation exceeded an allowed time budget. Both may terminate network work but have different operational meanings and retry implications.","failure_mode":"Cancelled requests are automatically retried as though they were transient network failures.","root_cause":"Failure taxonomy loses termination intent.","solution":"Preserve cancellation as a distinct outcome and apply retry rules according to the caller's intent.","testing_guidance":"Cancel active requests and separately allow equivalent requests to exceed their deadlines.","verification_method":"Verify that the resulting outcomes are classified differently.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B48-R002","D18-B49-R002"],"provenance":"Derived network lifecycle classification methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B68-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Network concurrency","subtopic":"Fairness","concept":"Tenant-aware concurrency","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why can a global concurrency limit be insufficient in a multi-tenant system?","answer":"One tenant or workload can consume the entire global budget, preventing other tenants from receiving service. Per-tenant or weighted concurrency controls can provide isolation when fairness or contractual resource boundaries require it.","failure_mode":"A single high-volume tenant exhausts all network concurrency.","root_cause":"Resource control exists only at the global level.","solution":"Apply tenant-aware quotas or weighted scheduling where the architecture requires isolation.","security_implications":"Tenant isolation reduces cross-tenant resource interference.","performance_implications":"Fairness controls can reduce peak utilization for one tenant in exchange for predictable allocation.","testing_guidance":"Run competing tenant workloads and verify that one tenant cannot consume resources beyond its configured policy.","verification_method":"Measure per-tenant active operations against configured limits.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B68-R001","D18-B63-R005"],"provenance":"Derived multi-tenant resource-isolation principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B69-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Network reliability","subtopic":"Failure domains","concept":"Network failure-domain isolation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should network dependencies be mapped into separate failure domains?","answer":"DNS, proxies, gateways, load balancers, transports, services, and external dependencies can fail independently. Explicit failure-domain mapping prevents a single symptom from being mistaken for a single shared root cause and supports targeted resilience controls.","implementation_structure":{"failure_domains":["Name resolution","Client network","Proxy","Gateway","Load balancer","Transport path","TLS/security negotiation","Backend service","Downstream dependency"]},"verification_method":"For each dependency, identify independent failure evidence and recovery behavior.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B50-R005","D18-B60-R005"],"provenance":"Derived distributed failure-domain methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B69-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Network reliability","subtopic":"Dependency amplification","concept":"Failure amplification through dependencies","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can one network dependency failure create failures in otherwise healthy services?","answer":"A shared dependency such as DNS, proxy infrastructure, or a gateway can affect many otherwise healthy application instances simultaneously. Retries and synchronized traffic can further amplify the dependency failure.","failure_mode":"Many services independently retry a shared failed dependency until its remaining capacity is exhausted.","root_cause":"Shared dependency and retry coupling are not represented in the architecture model.","solution":"Map shared dependencies, apply bounded retries and jitter, and define dependency-specific fallback behavior.","security_implications":"Shared dependency exhaustion can create broad availability impact.","testing_guidance":"Disable a shared dependency under controlled load and observe downstream amplification.","verification_method":"Correlate failure propagation with dependency topology and retry activity.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B49-R003","D18-B69-R001"],"provenance":"Derived distributed dependency-failure methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B69-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B69","topic":"Network reliability","subtopic":"Fallback","concept":"Fallback validity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must be true before a network fallback is considered safe?","answer":"A fallback must preserve the application's correctness, security, authorization, and data-integrity requirements. Merely finding an alternative endpoint or protocol does not make fallback valid.","failure_mode":"System falls back to an alternate destination that has different security or data semantics.","root_cause":"Fallback policy considers reachability but not semantic equivalence.","solution":"Define explicit fallback invariants and verify the alternative path satisfies them.","security_implications":"Fallback can accidentally bypass security controls or trust boundaries.","performance_implications":"Fallback may have different latency or capacity characteristics and should remain bounded.","testing_guidance":"Force primary-path failure and verify every required security and correctness invariant on the fallback path.","verification_method":"Use a fallback verification matrix rather than success of connection establishment alone.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B52-R005","D18-B69-R001"],"provenance":"Derived resilient-fallback correctness principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B69-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Network reliability","subtopic":"Graceful degradation","concept":"Network graceful degradation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is graceful degradation in a networked application?","answer":"Graceful degradation means reducing or disabling nonessential functionality when network dependencies become unavailable while preserving core correctness and safety. It is different from silently returning incomplete or incorrect results.","failure_mode":"Application continues operating but returns misleading data because degraded behavior is not explicit.","root_cause":"No defined distinction exists between optional and correctness-critical network dependencies.","solution":"Classify dependencies by criticality and define explicit degraded modes and user-visible or machine-readable status.","verification_method":"Disable optional dependencies and verify that required invariants remain satisfied.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B69-R003"],"provenance":"Derived resilient-architecture principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B69-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Network reliability","subtopic":"Recovery verification","concept":"Recovery is not equivalent to connectivity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should network recovery verification check application correctness after reconnection?","answer":"A connection can be re-established while application state remains incomplete, stale, duplicated, or inconsistent. Recovery must therefore verify the application outcome rather than treating transport reconnection as proof of successful recovery.","failure_mode":"System reports recovery immediately after a new socket is established.","root_cause":"Transport state is used as a proxy for application state.","solution":"Perform application-level outcome verification after network recovery.","verification_method":"Verify expected application state, response integrity, and idempotency after reconnection.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B48-R002","D18-B50-R005","D18-B69-R003"],"provenance":"Derived end-to-end correctness principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B70-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Network verification","subtopic":"Invariant checking","concept":"Network operation invariants","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariants should an autonomous software-engineering system verify for network operations?","answer":"Relevant invariants include bounded deadlines, bounded concurrency, correct destination identity, valid TLS policy, complete request or message framing, deterministic resource cleanup, correct retry classification, connection-pool limits, and application-level outcome verification.","verification_matrix":{"deadline":"Operation terminates within the applicable budget","identity":"Destination identity is authenticated according to policy","framing":"Application messages are parsed without relying on transport boundaries","resources":"Sockets and pooled resources return to expected state","retry":"Only retryable outcomes trigger retries","outcome":"Transport success is not treated as application success"},"verification_method":"Evaluate each invariant against observed evidence rather than relying on a single success signal.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B60-R005","D18-B69-R005"],"provenance":"Derived network verification framework."}
{"record_id":"D18-B70-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Network verification","subtopic":"Fault injection","concept":"Network fault-injection matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What network faults should be represented in a serious engineering test matrix?","answer":"The matrix should include DNS delay and failure, connection refusal, connection timeout, packet loss, packet delay, connection reset, TLS validation failure, proxy failure, load-balancer backend failure, pool exhaustion, cancellation, deadline expiration, and resource exhaustion.","test_case":["DNS timeout","DNS negative response","Connection refusal","Connection timeout","Connection reset","Packet loss","Packet delay","TLS certificate failure","Proxy unavailable","Proxy authentication failure","Backend unhealthy","Pool exhaustion","Cancellation","Deadline expiration","Ephemeral-port pressure"],"expected_test_result":"Each injected fault is classified at the correct lifecycle stage and produces the documented bounded recovery or failure behavior.","verification_method":"Use controlled fault injection and collect application, transport, and intermediary evidence.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B60-R003","D18-B69-R001"],"provenance":"Derived comprehensive network fault-injection methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B70-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Network verification","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Network regression corpus","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What makes a network regression case useful for an autonomous coding system?","answer":"A useful regression case preserves the original failure condition, expected protocol behavior, relevant code or configuration, observed evidence, corrected behavior, and verification method. The case should be replayable without depending on undocumented external state.","test_case_schema":["regression_id","trigger","preconditions","network_topology","input","expected_failure_or_behavior","observed_evidence","root_cause","correction","expected_result","verification_method","environment_requirements"],"failure_mode":"Regression test checks only that the request eventually succeeds and loses the original failure condition.","solution":"Preserve both failure reproduction and corrected behavior.","verification_method":"Replay the failure condition and then verify the correction independently.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B70-R002","D18-B65-R002","D18-B65-R003"],"provenance":"Derived software-regression dataset methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B70-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Network verification","subtopic":"Evidence hierarchy","concept":"Network diagnosis evidence hierarchy","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should network-diagnosis evidence be weighted when signals disagree?","answer":"Direct protocol or packet evidence should generally be distinguished from application assumptions, inferred causes, and heuristic correlations. Conflicting evidence should remain explicitly conflicted until additional evidence resolves it.","evidence_levels":["DIRECT_PROTOCOL_EVIDENCE","DIRECT_SYSTEM_TELEMETRY","CONTROLLED_REPRODUCTION","CORRELATED_OBSERVATION","INFERENCE","HEURISTIC","UNKNOWN"],"rule":"Evidence level must not be upgraded merely because a hypothesis is plausible.","verification_method":"Require explicit supporting evidence before converting an inferred cause into a verified fact.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B60-R005","D18-B70-R001"],"provenance":"Derived evidence-driven diagnostic methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B70-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Networking","subtopic":"Autonomous software engineering","concept":"Network reasoning record model","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What structured representation should an autonomous software-engineering system use when reasoning about a network failure?","answer":"The record should connect the observed request lifecycle event to topology, protocol, evidence, candidate causes, confidence, contradictions, affected resources, security implications, remediation options, testing requirements, and verification status. It must preserve UNKNOWN and CONFLICTED states rather than forcing a conclusion.","implementation_structure":{"record_id":"stable identifier","request_context":"logical operation metadata","topology":"network path and dependencies","observations":"raw or normalized evidence","failure_stage":"first evidence-backed failed lifecycle stage","candidate_causes":"ranked hypotheses without presenting ranking as fact","root_cause":"verified or unresolved conclusion","constraints":"known environmental limits","remediation":"candidate corrective actions","test_plan":"reproduction and regression strategy","verification":"evidence required for closure","status":"UNKNOWN|CONFLICTED|INFERRED|VERIFIED"},"deduplication_rule":"Do not create a new diagnostic record when it adds only a rewording of an existing failure pattern; extend relationships or specialize the existing record when the new knowledge is materially distinct.","verification_method":"Validate record completeness, provenance, contradiction handling, and relationship integrity before accepting it into the knowledge fabric.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B60-R005","D18-B70-R001","D18-B70-R004"],"provenance":"Derived architecture for evidence-driven autonomous network reasoning."}

{"record_id":"D18-B71-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"IPv4 and IPv6","subtopic":"Dual-stack behavior","concept":"Dual-stack connection selection","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why can an application behave differently when both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses are available?","answer":"A hostname can resolve to multiple address families, and the client may attempt or select among them according to resolver, operating-system, library, and protocol behavior. The selected path can have different reachability, latency, routing, firewall, and proxy characteristics.","failure_mode":"Application assumes that successful DNS resolution proves all returned address families are reachable.","root_cause":"Name resolution is treated as equivalent to endpoint reachability.","solution":"Model address-family selection and connection attempts as separate stages and preserve the selected address family in diagnostics.","testing_guidance":"Test IPv4-only, IPv6-only, and dual-stack environments independently.","verification_method":"Record resolved addresses, selected address family, connection outcome, and timing.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B66-R005","D18-B69-R001"],"provenance":"IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack and socket-address selection principles; RFC 6724."}
{"record_id":"D18-B71-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"IPv4 and IPv6","subtopic":"Address representation","concept":"IP address family correctness","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must networking code preserve the address family when creating sockets?","answer":"An IPv4 address and an IPv6 address require compatible socket-family handling. Treating an address as an opaque string and selecting a fixed socket family can cause otherwise valid destinations to fail.","incorrect_code":"sock = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM)\nsock.connect((ipv6_address, port))","failure_mode":"An IPv6 destination is passed to an IPv4 socket.","root_cause":"Socket family is hard-coded instead of derived from the resolved endpoint.","solution":"Use resolver results containing address-family information and create the compatible socket for each candidate endpoint.","testing_guidance":"Run the same client against IPv4-only, IPv6-only, and dual-stack destinations.","verification_method":"Verify that socket family, destination address, and resolver result are compatible before connection.","language":"PYTHON","language_version":"3.11+","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B71-R001","D18-B65-R001"],"provenance":"Python socket API and IPv4/IPv6 socket-family semantics."}
{"record_id":"D18-B71-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"IPv4 and IPv6","subtopic":"IPv4-mapped IPv6","concept":"IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should applications avoid assuming that every IPv6-formatted address represents a native IPv6 peer?","answer":"IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses can represent IPv4 peers within IPv6-aware APIs. Applications performing address classification, authorization, logging, or access control must therefore use standards-compliant address parsing and normalization rather than simplistic textual checks.","failure_mode":"Security policy treats an IPv4-mapped IPv6 representation differently from the equivalent IPv4 address.","root_cause":"Address identity is compared textually instead of semantically.","solution":"Normalize addresses using a standards-compliant IP library before policy evaluation.","security_implications":"Incorrect normalization can produce authorization inconsistencies or bypasses.","testing_guidance":"Test equivalent IPv4 and IPv4-mapped IPv6 representations against access-control rules.","verification_method":"Verify that equivalent network identities receive the intended policy treatment.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B71-R001"],"provenance":"IPv4-mapped IPv6 address architecture; RFC 4291."}
{"record_id":"D18-B71-R004","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"IPv4 and IPv6","subtopic":"Configuration","concept":"Address-family configuration drift","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can configuration drift create IPv4/IPv6-specific failures?","answer":"A service can be configured to listen, advertise, firewall, or route traffic for one address family while the application or discovery system assumes both are available. The resulting failure may affect only part of the client population.","failure_mode":"Some clients connect successfully while others consistently fail based on address family.","root_cause":"Advertised reachability does not match actual listener or routing configuration.","solution":"Validate advertised addresses against actual listeners, routing, firewall policy, and health checks for each supported address family.","testing_guidance":"Validate service reachability independently over IPv4 and IPv6.","verification_method":"Compare DNS advertisements, listener state, routing, and successful connection evidence.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B62-R004","D18-B71-R001"],"provenance":"Derived dual-stack deployment verification methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B71-R005","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"IPv4 and IPv6","subtopic":"Testing","concept":"Dual-stack regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a dual-stack networking regression matrix contain?","answer":"The matrix should test IPv4-only, IPv6-only, dual-stack resolution, address-family selection, connection failure in one family, proxy behavior, TLS identity handling, logging, and authorization for equivalent endpoint identities.","test_case":["IPv4-only success","IPv6-only success","Dual-stack success","IPv4 unavailable","IPv6 unavailable","Mixed DNS answers","TLS over both families","Proxy over both families","Address-policy normalization"],"expected_test_result":"Supported address families behave according to documented policy and failure in one family does not create undocumented behavior in the other.","verification_method":"Execute each environment against controlled endpoints and preserve address-family evidence.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B71-R001","D18-B71-R004"],"provenance":"Derived dual-stack testing methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B72-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Network addressing","subtopic":"NAT","concept":"Network address translation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does NAT change in a network path?","answer":"Network address translation modifies address information as traffic crosses a translation boundary, commonly allowing multiple internal endpoints to share an externally visible address. NAT therefore introduces state and an intermediary boundary into connection behavior.","principle":"An address observed by one network participant is not necessarily the address assigned to the originating endpoint.","failure_mode":"Application assumes peer-visible addressing is equivalent to local interface addressing.","solution":"Treat translated addresses as path-level observations and do not infer endpoint identity solely from the observed source address.","security_implications":"Source-address-based identity can be unreliable across translation boundaries.","verification_method":"Compare local interface, translated, and remote-observed addresses in a controlled environment.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B69-R001","D18-B73-R001"],"provenance":"NAT architecture; RFC 3022 and related IP networking standards."}
{"record_id":"D18-B72-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Network addressing","subtopic":"NAT state","concept":"NAT state expiration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a long-idle connection behave differently across a NAT boundary?","answer":"Many NAT implementations maintain translation state with expiration behavior. A sufficiently idle flow can lose its translation state, causing subsequent packets to fail or require a new connection.","failure_mode":"Application believes a previously established connection remains usable indefinitely.","root_cause":"Connection lifetime exceeds intermediary state lifetime.","solution":"Use protocol-appropriate keepalive or reconnect behavior where long-lived idle connections are required.","performance_implications":"Keepalives consume network and endpoint resources and should be bounded by actual requirements.","testing_guidance":"Allow controlled idle periods and verify behavior after intermediary state expiration.","verification_method":"Correlate connection failure with observed NAT or intermediary state lifetime where evidence is available.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B44-R002","D18-B59-R002"],"provenance":"Derived stateful-network-intermediary behavior."}
{"record_id":"D18-B72-R003","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Network addressing","subtopic":"Endpoint identity","concept":"Source address is not durable identity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should a server avoid treating a source IP address as a durable user identity?","answer":"Addresses can be shared, translated, reassigned, proxied, or changed during network transitions. IP information can be useful as network context but does not by itself establish application identity.","failure_mode":"Authorization or account attribution is based solely on source IP.","root_cause":"Network location is conflated with authenticated identity.","solution":"Use authenticated application identity and treat source address as contextual evidence.","security_implications":"Shared or translated addresses can cause incorrect attribution and access decisions.","testing_guidance":"Test access from multiple users behind one address and through intermediary translation.","verification_method":"Verify that authorization remains tied to authenticated identity rather than source address alone.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B64-R001","D18-B72-R001"],"provenance":"General IP networking and authentication security principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B72-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B72","topic":"Network addressing","subtopic":"Port translation","concept":"NAT port mapping","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can local source ports differ from the source port observed outside a NAT boundary?","answer":"A NAT implementation may translate transport-layer port information as part of maintaining unique external mappings. Therefore, the remote peer's observed source tuple is not necessarily identical to the local socket tuple.","failure_mode":"Application correlates local and remote connection tuples without accounting for translation.","root_cause":"NAT translation is omitted from topology reasoning.","solution":"Model translated five-tuples as separate network observations.","verification_method":"Compare local socket metadata with externally observed connection metadata in a controlled NAT environment.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B72-R001"],"provenance":"NAT behavior and transport mapping principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B72-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B72","topic":"Network addressing","subtopic":"NAT traversal","concept":"NAT traversal dependency","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why can peer-to-peer connectivity require explicit NAT traversal mechanisms?","answer":"Two endpoints behind stateful translation boundaries may not have directly reachable public endpoint mappings. Connectivity can therefore require coordination, address discovery, relaying, or protocol-specific traversal mechanisms.","constraint":"Traversal behavior depends on NAT type, firewall policy, endpoint capabilities, and network topology.","failure_mode":"Application assumes that discovering a local endpoint address is sufficient for remote reachability.","solution":"Model traversal as a separate connectivity stage and preserve failure evidence for discovery, signaling, and transport establishment.","verification_method":"Test connectivity across representative controlled NAT topologies.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B72-R001","D18-B72-R004"],"provenance":"Derived NAT traversal architecture principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B73-R001","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Network ports","subtopic":"Ephemeral ports","concept":"Ephemeral port allocation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is an ephemeral port in a client network connection?","answer":"An ephemeral port is a dynamically selected transport-layer port commonly used by a client for outbound connections. Its availability is finite and depends on operating-system and network-state constraints.","failure_mode":"Application assumes outbound connections can be created without practical port limits.","solution":"Include ephemeral-port availability in high-concurrency and connection-lifecycle analysis.","performance_implications":"Large numbers of short-lived connections can increase port pressure and related connection-state overhead.","testing_guidance":"Observe local port allocation and connection-state growth under controlled connection churn.","verification_method":"Correlate connection failures with local port availability and transport state.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B68-R001","D18-B72-R004"],"provenance":"TCP/UDP transport and operating-system port-allocation principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B73-R002","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Network ports","subtopic":"Port exhaustion","concept":"Ephemeral-port exhaustion","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can excessive outbound connection churn cause ephemeral-port exhaustion?","answer":"Rapid creation of many outbound connections can consume available local source-port and connection-state resources. Depending on operating-system and protocol behavior, recently closed connections can also continue occupying state for some period.","failure_mode":"New outbound connections fail despite the destination being reachable.","root_cause":"Local transport resources are exhausted rather than the remote service being unavailable.","solution":"Reuse connections where appropriate, bound concurrency, reduce unnecessary connection churn, and diagnose local transport state before retrying aggressively.","security_implications":"Attack-like connection churn can create local resource exhaustion.","performance_implications":"Connection reuse can reduce repeated setup overhead, but pool sizing must remain bounded.","testing_guidance":"Generate controlled connection churn and observe local transport resource state.","verification_method":"Verify that reducing connection churn restores connection creation without changing destination availability.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B58-R001","D18-B73-R001"],"provenance":"Derived transport-resource exhaustion methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B73-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B73","topic":"Network ports","subtopic":"Connection reuse","concept":"Connection reuse versus connection churn","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What trade-off exists between persistent connection reuse and frequent new connections?","answer":"Connection reuse can reduce repeated connection and protocol setup work but keeps resources occupied for longer. Frequent new connections release individual connection resources sooner but increase setup overhead and can create port and handshake pressure.","tradeoffs":{"reuse":["Lower setup frequency","Potentially lower latency","Persistent resource occupancy"],"new_connections":["Simpler isolation per operation","Higher setup frequency","Greater connection churn"]},"performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","testing_guidance":"Compare controlled workloads with different reuse policies while measuring setup operations, active connections, latency, and resource use.","verification_method":"Select policy based on measured workload behavior rather than an assumed universal optimum.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B59-R002","D18-B73-R002"],"provenance":"Derived connection-lifecycle performance methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B73-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B73","topic":"Network ports","subtopic":"Port-aware diagnostics","concept":"Local versus remote resource exhaustion","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC","question":"How should an autonomous system distinguish local ephemeral-port exhaustion from remote connection refusal?","answer":"It should inspect the stage and source of the failure, local transport state, source-port allocation, destination reachability, and remote evidence where available. A failure before a connection is established should not automatically be attributed to the remote service.","procedure":["Inspect local connection creation result","Classify local socket error","Check available source-port resources","Inspect local transport state","Test destination independently","Compare with remote-side evidence"],"verification_method":"Require evidence that identifies whether failure occurred locally or after packets reached the remote endpoint.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B73-R002","D18-B70-R004"],"provenance":"Derived network failure-localization methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B73-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B73","topic":"Network ports","subtopic":"Regression testing","concept":"Connection-churn regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a connection-churn regression test verify?","answer":"It should verify bounded connection creation, connection reuse behavior where intended, cleanup after success and failure, stable resource usage, and correct behavior after sustained short-lived connection workloads.","test_case":["Repeated short-lived connections","Repeated failures","Connection reuse enabled","Connection reuse disabled","Concurrent connection bursts","Cancellation during connect"],"expected_test_result":"Connection resources remain bounded and new connections continue to behave according to configured capacity.","verification_method":"Measure connection and transport-resource state before, during, and after the test.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B73-R002","D18-B65-R004"],"provenance":"Derived connection-lifecycle regression methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B74-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Path MTU","concept":"Path maximum transmission unit","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the path MTU relevant to an IP connection?","answer":"The path MTU is the largest packet size that can traverse a network path without requiring fragmentation under the applicable protocol and path conditions. It can differ from the local interface MTU.","constraint":"Path characteristics can vary between destinations and can change over time.","failure_mode":"Application assumes the local interface MTU is always the effective path MTU.","solution":"Allow transport and network protocols to handle path constraints correctly and avoid application assumptions about a universal MTU.","verification_method":"Inspect or measure path behavior using appropriate network diagnostics.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B69-R001"],"provenance":"IP packet-size and path-MTU standards; RFC 8201 and RFC 1191."}
{"record_id":"D18-B74-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B74","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Packet fragmentation","concept":"Fragmentation risk","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can packet fragmentation or oversized packets affect network reliability?","answer":"Packets larger than a path segment can support may require fragmentation or may be dropped depending on protocol version and network behavior. Filtering or mishandling of required control messages can create black-hole connectivity problems.","failure_mode":"Small requests succeed while larger transfers consistently stall or fail.","root_cause":"Effective path packet-size constraints are incompatible with actual path behavior.","solution":"Diagnose path-MTU behavior and ensure network infrastructure correctly handles required protocol signaling.","testing_guidance":"Compare small and larger controlled payloads across the same path and inspect packet-level evidence where available.","verification_method":"Correlate size-dependent failure with path-MTU observations.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B74-R001"],"provenance":"IP fragmentation and PMTU discovery standards."}
{"record_id":"D18-B74-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B74","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Application payloads","concept":"Application message size versus network packet size","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should an application not equate its message size with one network packet?","answer":"Application messages can be segmented across multiple transport packets, and transport packets can contain portions of application messages. Network packet boundaries are therefore not application message boundaries.","failure_mode":"Application parser assumes one packet corresponds to one logical message.","root_cause":"Layer boundaries are incorrectly coupled.","solution":"Use transport and application framing independently.","verification_method":"Test messages larger and smaller than common packet sizes and verify identical application semantics.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B65-R003","D18-B74-R001"],"provenance":"Layered networking and TCP stream principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B74-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B74","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Diagnostics","concept":"Size-dependent failure diagnosis","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC","question":"What evidence is useful when only larger network payloads fail?","answer":"Useful evidence includes payload size, transport behavior, retransmissions, connection state, PMTU-related signaling, intermediary configuration, and whether failures occur at a consistent size threshold.","procedure":["Test multiple payload sizes","Identify failure threshold","Compare protocol behavior","Inspect path/intermediary evidence","Check MTU configuration","Repeat across paths"],"verification_method":"A size-dependent hypothesis should remain inferred until packet/path evidence supports it.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B74-R002","D18-B70-R004"],"provenance":"Derived size-dependent network troubleshooting methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B74-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B74","topic":"MTU","subtopic":"Testing","concept":"MTU regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an MTU-focused network test matrix cover?","answer":"Tests should include small payloads, boundary-sized payloads, large payloads, IPv4 and IPv6 paths where supported, direct and intermediary paths, and controlled changes to MTU-related configuration.","test_case":["Small payload","Near-boundary payload","Large payload","IPv4 path","IPv6 path","Proxy path","Tunnel path","Changed MTU"],"expected_test_result":"Application behavior remains correct across supported path conditions or fails with an explicit, diagnosable error.","verification_method":"Record payload size and network-path configuration with each test result.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B74-R001","D18-B74-R004"],"provenance":"Derived MTU regression methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B75-R001","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Network protocols","subtopic":"Protocol negotiation","concept":"ALPN protocol negotiation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is ALPN used for in TLS-based application protocols?","answer":"Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation allows a TLS client and server to negotiate an application protocol during the TLS handshake, enabling protocol selection without requiring an additional application-level negotiation round trip.","constraint":"The selected protocol must be supported and interpreted consistently by both endpoints.","failure_mode":"Client and server have incompatible protocol expectations despite successful TLS establishment.","solution":"Configure compatible ALPN protocol sets and treat protocol negotiation as distinct from certificate authentication.","verification_method":"Inspect negotiated application protocol and compare it with client/server policy.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B64-R001","D18-B76-R001"],"provenance":"ALPN specification; RFC 7301."}
{"record_id":"D18-B75-R002","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Network protocols","subtopic":"Protocol negotiation","concept":"Negotiation failure classification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should successful TCP and TLS establishment not be interpreted as successful application-protocol negotiation?","answer":"TCP establishes transport connectivity and TLS can establish a secure channel while the negotiated application protocol remains unsupported or incorrectly configured. Each lifecycle stage must be verified independently.","failure_mode":"Client reports a healthy connection even though the application protocol cannot be used.","root_cause":"Layered negotiation results are collapsed into one connection-success state.","solution":"Record transport, TLS, and application-protocol outcomes separately.","verification_method":"Verify negotiated protocol explicitly after TLS establishment.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B75-R001","D18-B70-R001"],"provenance":"Derived layered-protocol verification principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B75-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B75","topic":"Network protocols","subtopic":"Protocol compatibility","concept":"Protocol-version compatibility","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should protocol-version compatibility be represented explicitly in network systems?","answer":"Different endpoints can support overlapping but non-identical protocol versions or features. Explicit compatibility modeling prevents a system from assuming that network reachability implies semantic compatibility.","comparison":{"transport":"Endpoint can establish a network connection","security":"TLS policy and authentication can succeed","application_protocol":"Negotiated protocol can be supported","feature_set":"Specific required capabilities can be available"},"verification_method":"Check each required compatibility layer independently.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B75-R002"],"provenance":"Derived layered protocol compatibility methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B75-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B75","topic":"Network protocols","subtopic":"Capability negotiation","concept":"Feature capability discovery","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should clients verify negotiated capabilities before using optional protocol features?","answer":"Protocol support at the version level does not necessarily imply support for every optional feature. A client should use a feature only after the protocol or endpoint explicitly establishes that it is available.","failure_mode":"Client sends an optional feature that the server does not support and misclassifies the resulting error as a transient network failure.","root_cause":"Capability state is inferred from protocol reachability rather than negotiated evidence.","solution":"Represent negotiated capabilities explicitly in connection state.","verification_method":"Test feature-supported and feature-unsupported endpoints and verify correct branching.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B75-R003"],"provenance":"Derived protocol-capability negotiation principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B75-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B75","topic":"Network protocols","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Negotiation regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a protocol-negotiation regression suite verify?","answer":"It should cover supported protocol combinations, unsupported combinations, fallback behavior where defined, authentication failures, capability mismatches, and correct telemetry for each negotiation stage.","test_case":["Compatible protocol","No common protocol","TLS success with application mismatch","Optional feature unavailable","Protocol fallback","Proxy-mediated negotiation"],"expected_test_result":"Negotiation outcomes are explicit and no unsupported protocol is silently assumed.","verification_method":"Inspect negotiated protocol and capability state for every test case.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B75-R001","D18-B75-R004"],"provenance":"Derived protocol-negotiation regression methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B76-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"HTTP/2 and HTTP/3","subtopic":"Protocol architecture","concept":"HTTP/2 multiplexing","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What major transport behavior does HTTP/2 enable compared with HTTP/1.1 connection usage?","answer":"HTTP/2 multiplexes multiple streams within a single connection, allowing concurrent logical requests and responses without requiring one independent connection for every request.","principle":"Logical request concurrency and physical connection count are separate dimensions.","performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","constraint":"Actual performance depends on server, client, congestion, stream limits, workload, and intermediary behavior.","verification_method":"Inspect HTTP/2 stream activity and connection reuse rather than inferring multiplexing from request count.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B68-R002","D18-B75-R001"],"provenance":"HTTP/2 specification; RFC 9113."}
{"record_id":"D18-B76-R002","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"HTTP/2 and HTTP/3","subtopic":"Head-of-line blocking","concept":"HTTP/2 stream interaction","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What important transport-level limitation remains for HTTP/2 when using TCP?","answer":"HTTP/2 multiplexes application streams, but all streams share the underlying TCP connection. TCP loss recovery can therefore delay delivery of subsequently received bytes to higher layers even when unrelated HTTP streams remain logically independent.","tradeoffs":{"benefit":"Application-level stream multiplexing over one connection","limitation":"Shared TCP transport can couple delivery during packet loss"},"performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","verification_method":"Use controlled packet-loss experiments and compare stream-level completion behavior.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B76-R001","D18-B76-R003"],"provenance":"HTTP/2 over TCP and transport behavior; RFC 9113 and RFC 9293."}
{"record_id":"D18-B76-R003","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"HTTP/2 and HTTP/3","subtopic":"QUIC","concept":"HTTP/3 over QUIC","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What transport does HTTP/3 use?","answer":"HTTP/3 uses QUIC as its transport substrate and maps HTTP semantics onto QUIC streams. QUIC provides stream multiplexing and transport features without relying on TCP as the underlying transport.","constraint":"HTTP/3 requires compatible client, server, and intermediary support.","verification_method":"Inspect the negotiated protocol and transport characteristics rather than inferring HTTP/3 from an HTTP request alone.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B75-R001","D18-B76-R001"],"provenance":"HTTP/3 and QUIC specifications; RFC 9114 and RFC 9000."}
{"record_id":"D18-B76-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B76","topic":"HTTP/2 and HTTP/3","subtopic":"Connection migration","concept":"QUIC connection migration","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why can QUIC connection migration matter on changing network paths?","answer":"QUIC identifies connections independently of a single fixed four-tuple and supports connection migration under defined conditions. This can allow an established connection to survive certain network-path changes that would otherwise require transport reconnection.","constraint":"Migration behavior is subject to QUIC protocol rules, endpoint support, validation, and network conditions.","verification_method":"Use controlled network-interface or address-path changes and inspect whether the QUIC connection remains valid.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B76-R003","D18-B71-R001"],"provenance":"QUIC connection migration; RFC 9000."}
{"record_id":"D18-B76-R005","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"HTTP/2 and HTTP/3","subtopic":"Protocol selection","concept":"HTTP protocol fallback","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should HTTP protocol fallback be explicit and observable?","answer":"A client may support multiple HTTP versions and may negotiate or select among them based on server capability and network conditions. Silent fallback can hide compatibility or path problems and make diagnostics ambiguous.","failure_mode":"Client silently falls back to another protocol and reports only final request success.","root_cause":"Negotiation history is discarded.","solution":"Record attempted and negotiated protocol versions and classify fallback separately from primary success.","verification_method":"Inspect protocol negotiation telemetry for both successful and fallback paths.","language":"PROTOCOL","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B75-R001","D18-B76-R003"],"provenance":"Derived protocol-negotiation observability principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B77-R001","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Correlation","concept":"End-to-end network correlation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should an end-to-end network trace correlate?","answer":"A trace should connect the logical operation to DNS resolution, connection acquisition, transport establishment, TLS negotiation, protocol negotiation, request transmission, response receipt, retries, cancellations, and final application outcome where those stages exist.","implementation_structure":{"operation_id":"Stable logical-operation identifier","attempt_id":"Identifier for each network attempt","dependency":"Target dependency identity","stage_timings":"Per-stage duration","protocol":"Negotiated protocol information","outcome":"Stage-specific and final outcome","retry_state":"Retry attempt and reason","resource_state":"Connection-pool and concurrency context"},"verification_method":"Verify that a single logical operation can be reconstructed from stage-level evidence without relying on one final status field.","language":"OBSERVABILITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D18-B70-R005"],"provenance":"Derived end-to-end network observability model."}
{"record_id":"D18-B77-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B77","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Timing","concept":"Stage-level latency decomposition","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why is total request latency insufficient for diagnosing network performance?","answer":"Total latency combines multiple stages such as name resolution, queueing, connection establishment, TLS, server processing, transmission, and response consumption. Without stage-level timing, the dominant contributor cannot be reliably identified.","performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","testing_guidance":"Record monotonic timestamps at each lifecycle boundary and calculate durations without using wall-clock time for elapsed-time measurement.","verification_method":"Ensure stage durations sum consistently with the measured end-to-end duration within instrumentation overhead.","language":"NETWORKING","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D18-B63-R001","D18-B77-R001"],"provenance":"Derived latency-decomposition methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B77-R003","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Error telemetry","concept":"Structured network error records","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What information should a structured network error record preserve?","answer":"A structured error should preserve operation identity, dependency identity, lifecycle stage, normalized error category, original error information where safe, timing, retry state, protocol, endpoint family, proxy context, and evidence confidence.","constraints":["Do not log secrets or credentials","Do not expose sensitive payloads unnecessarily","Preserve enough context to reproduce classification"],"failure_mode":"Only a human-readable exception string is stored.","root_cause":"Network lifecycle context is lost at the error boundary.","solution":"Normalize errors into structured fields while preserving the original low-level evidence where safe.","security_implications":"Telemetry must avoid credentials, tokens, and sensitive payload content.","verification_method":"Inject representative failures and verify that each produces sufficient structured context for diagnosis.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B70-R004","D18-B77-R001"],"provenance":"Derived structured-observability methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B77-R004","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Cardinality","concept":"Network telemetry cardinality control","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why should network telemetry control high-cardinality dimensions?","answer":"Unbounded dimensions such as arbitrary URLs, connection identifiers, payload values, or user-controlled labels can cause excessive metric cardinality and storage cost. Structured tracing and bounded labels should be used to retain diagnostic value without uncontrolled telemetry growth.","failure_mode":"Monitoring system becomes expensive or unusable because every request creates unique metric series.","root_cause":"User-controlled or unbounded identifiers are used as metric dimensions.","solution":"Keep high-cardinality values in traces or sampled logs and use bounded dimensions for aggregate metrics.","security_implications":"Sensitive identifiers can also leak through telemetry labels.","performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","testing_guidance":"Generate high-cardinality traffic and observe telemetry-series growth.","verification_method":"Verify that metric cardinality remains within documented bounds.","language":"OBSERVABILITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B77-R003"],"provenance":"Derived observability cardinality-control principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B77-R005","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Network observability","subtopic":"Sampling","concept":"Trace sampling versus diagnosis","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can aggressive trace sampling reduce network-diagnosis capability?","answer":"Sampling lowers telemetry volume but can omit rare failure paths and the exact evidence needed to diagnose intermittent network problems. Sampling policy should therefore account for failure severity, rarity, and diagnostic requirements.","tradeoffs":{"higher_sampling":"More diagnostic evidence and higher telemetry cost","lower_sampling":"Lower telemetry cost and greater risk of missing rare failures"},"performance_claim_status":"INFERRED","testing_guidance":"Evaluate sampling against historical or controlled rare-failure scenarios rather than normal traffic alone.","verification_method":"Verify that critical failure classes remain observable at the chosen sampling policy.","language":"OBSERVABILITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B77-R001","D18-B77-R004"],"provenance":"Derived observability sampling methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B78-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Network security","subtopic":"SSRF","concept":"Server-side request forgery defense","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can unrestricted server-side URL fetching create SSRF risk?","answer":"A server that accepts attacker-controlled destinations may be induced to make requests to resources that are reachable from the server but not intended to be exposed to the requester. Defense requires explicit destination validation and network-policy enforcement.","failure_mode":"Server fetches arbitrary attacker-supplied URLs.","root_cause":"Destination authority is controlled by untrusted input without sufficient policy enforcement.","solution":"Use allowlists or strongly constrained destination policies, validate resolved addresses, and enforce network-level restrictions.","security_implications":"SSRF can expose internal services, metadata endpoints, or privileged network resources.","testing_guidance":"Test controlled public and prohibited private/internal destinations without targeting real sensitive systems.","verification_method":"Verify that prohibited destination classes are rejected before network access or are blocked by an independent network control.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B72-R003","D18-B73-R004"],"provenance":"OWASP SSRF prevention guidance and network security practice."}
{"record_id":"D18-B78-R002","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Network security","subtopic":"URL validation","concept":"URL parser consistency","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should SSRF defenses avoid naive string-prefix URL checks?","answer":"URL syntax supports multiple representations, redirects, credentials, ports, encoded components, and address forms. A simple textual prefix can therefore disagree with the parser's actual destination interpretation.","failure_mode":"Validation approves a URL string that resolves to a prohibited destination.","root_cause":"Security policy operates on raw text rather than parsed and resolved network identity.","solution":"Parse according to the relevant URL specification, normalize security-relevant components, resolve the destination, and enforce policy on the resulting network identity.","security_implications":"Parser-policy disagreement can create SSRF bypasses.","testing_guidance":"Use a safe corpus of alternate URL representations and verify identical policy decisions for equivalent destinations.","verification_method":"Compare policy evaluation against the actual destination used by the network client.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B78-R001","D18-B71-R003"],"provenance":"OWASP SSRF prevention and URL parsing security principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B78-R003","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Network security","subtopic":"Redirects","concept":"Redirect destination validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must SSRF destination policy be applied to redirects as well as the initial URL?","answer":"A server-side HTTP client can follow a redirect to a destination different from the initial URL. If validation occurs only before the first request, the redirect can bypass the intended destination policy.","failure_mode":"Initial public URL is allowed and redirects to a prohibited internal destination.","root_cause":"Destination validation is performed only once before redirect processing.","solution":"Validate every effective destination before following a redirect and enforce a bounded redirect policy.","security_implications":"Redirect-based policy bypass can expose internal services.","testing_guidance":"Use a controlled redirect chain from an allowed endpoint to a prohibited test endpoint and verify rejection.","verification_method":"Record and validate every effective destination in the redirect chain.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B78-R001","D18-B78-R002"],"provenance":"OWASP SSRF prevention and HTTP redirect semantics."}
{"record_id":"D18-B78-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B78","topic":"Network security","subtopic":"DNS rebinding","concept":"Resolution-time versus connection-time validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security-sensitive destination validation account for DNS resolution and connection behavior?","answer":"A hostname can resolve to different addresses over time, and the address used for policy evaluation must correspond to the address actually contacted. Security policy that validates one resolution but connects using a different resolution can become inconsistent.","failure_mode":"Policy validates a permitted address but the client later connects to a prohibited address for the same hostname.","root_cause":"Validation and connection use independently resolved destinations.","solution":"Bind policy evaluation to the effective resolved destination and use a network client architecture that preserves that correspondence.","security_implications":"Resolution-policy races can enable SSRF or network-policy bypasses.","testing_guidance":"Use a controlled resolver that changes answers and verify that policy remains bound to the actual connection target.","verification_method":"Compare validated and connected addresses for each request attempt.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B78-R001","D18-B66-R001"],"provenance":"Derived DNS-resolution security methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B78-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B78","topic":"Network security","subtopic":"Egress policy","concept":"Defense in depth for outbound networking","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should application-level destination validation be supplemented with network-level egress controls?","answer":"Application validation can contain implementation defects or parser inconsistencies. Independent egress controls provide a separate enforcement layer that can restrict prohibited network destinations even when application logic fails.","principle":"Security-critical outbound access should not depend on a single validation layer.","security_implications":"Network-level restrictions reduce the impact of application-layer SSRF validation failures.","testing_guidance":"Bypass or intentionally weaken the application policy in a controlled environment and verify that network egress policy still blocks prohibited destinations.","verification_method":"Test both layers independently and together.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B78-R001","D18-B78-R004"],"provenance":"Defense-in-depth network security principle."}

{"record_id":"D18-B79-R001","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Network authentication","subtopic":"Credential transport","concept":"Credential confidentiality","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must credentials not be transmitted over an untrusted plaintext network path?","answer":"Plaintext transport can expose credentials to parties able to observe the network path. Authentication credentials should be protected using an appropriate authenticated secure channel and credential-handling policy.","failure_mode":"Username/password or token is transmitted over plaintext HTTP or another unprotected channel.","root_cause":"Application treats authentication as independent from transport confidentiality and integrity.","solution":"Require an appropriate secure transport and reject or redirect insecure access according to policy.","security_implications":"Credential interception can lead to account compromise.","testing_guidance":"Inspect controlled traffic and verify that credentials are never transmitted on prohibited insecure paths.","verification_method":"Use protocol capture or server-side evidence to verify protected credential transport.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B64-R001","D18-B67-R001"],"provenance":"General secure-transport and authentication security principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B79-R002","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Network authentication","subtopic":"Token handling","concept":"Authorization-header forwarding","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should authentication headers not be blindly forwarded across destination changes?","answer":"A credential intended for one authority may be valid only for that authority. Blind forwarding during redirects, proxy transitions, or cross-origin requests can expose credentials to unintended recipients.","failure_mode":"Client sends an authorization credential to a different authority after a redirect.","root_cause":"Credential scope is not bound to destination identity.","solution":"Bind credential forwarding to explicit authority and protocol policy and strip credentials when crossing disallowed trust boundaries.","security_implications":"Credential leakage can compromise accounts or services.","testing_guidance":"Test same-authority and cross-authority redirects and verify credential behavior.","verification_method":"Inspect request headers at every destination in a controlled redirect chain.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B78-R003","D18-B79-R001"],"provenance":"HTTP authentication and credential-forwarding security practice."}
{"record_id":"D18-B79-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B79","topic":"Network authentication","subtopic":"Mutual TLS","concept":"Mutual TLS authentication","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What additional authentication does mutual TLS provide compared with server-authenticated TLS?","answer":"Mutual TLS allows the server to authenticate the client using a client certificate while the client authenticates the server through normal TLS server authentication. It therefore establishes certificate-based identity in both directions when configured and validated correctly.","constraint":"Certificate issuance, trust configuration, identity mapping, and revocation or lifecycle policy must be correctly managed.","verification_method":"Test both valid and unauthorized client certificates and verify server-side identity decisions.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B64-R001","D18-B64-R003"],"provenance":"TLS client authentication standards and PKIX principles."}
{"record_id":"D18-B79-R004","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Network authentication","subtopic":"Authentication failure","concept":"Authentication versus authorization failure","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC","question":"Why should authentication failure and authorization failure be classified separately?","answer":"Authentication determines whether the presented identity can be established, while authorization determines whether an established identity may perform the requested action. The two failures imply different remediation and security investigations.","failure_mode":"All access denials are classified as authentication failures.","root_cause":"Identity establishment and permission evaluation are collapsed.","solution":"Preserve separate authentication and authorization outcomes in network diagnostics.","security_implications":"Correct classification supports appropriate credential rotation, policy review, and audit handling.","verification_method":"Test invalid identity credentials separately from authenticated-but-disallowed identities.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B79-R001","D18-B79-R003"],"provenance":"General authentication and authorization security model."}
{"record_id":"D18-B79-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B79","topic":"Network authentication","subtopic":"Secret leakage prevention","concept":"Credential-safe network logging","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What should network logs avoid recording when diagnosing authentication failures?","answer":"Logs should avoid raw passwords, bearer tokens, private keys, session secrets, and unnecessary credential material. Diagnostics should retain safe identifiers and normalized outcomes rather than secret values.","failure_mode":"Debug logging captures complete Authorization headers or TLS private material.","root_cause":"Diagnostic verbosity is not separated from secret-handling policy.","solution":"Redact secrets at the logging boundary and test redaction against structured and unstructured error paths.","security_implications":"Logs can become a secondary credential-compromise channel.","verification_method":"Inject controlled secret values into failure paths and verify they do not appear in logs.","language":"SECURITY","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","relationships":["D18-B77-R003","D18-B79-R002"],"provenance":"General secure logging and credential-protection practice."}

{"record_id":"D18-B80-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Networking architecture","subtopic":"End-to-end lifecycle","concept":"Complete network request state machine","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should an autonomous software-engineering system represent the complete lifecycle of a network operation?","answer":"The lifecycle should represent configuration, destination resolution, endpoint selection, proxy handling, resource acquisition, transport establishment, TLS and protocol negotiation, request transmission, response consumption, retries, cancellation, cleanup, and application-level outcome verification as distinct states.","implementation_structure":{"states":["CONFIGURED","RESOLVING","ENDPOINT_SELECTED","PROXY_NEGOTIATING","RESOURCE_ACQUIRING","CONNECTING","SECURING","NEGOTIATING_PROTOCOL","TRANSMITTING","RECEIVING","COMPLETING","RETRYING","CANCELLING","CLEANING_UP","VERIFYING_OUTCOME","FAILED"]},"rule":"A transition should be evidence-backed and should not infer later-stage success from an earlier-stage success.","verification_method":"Validate state transitions against controlled success, failure, cancellation, and timeout scenarios.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B50-R005","D18-B70-R001","D18-B77-R001"],"provenance":"Derived network lifecycle state-machine methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B80-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B80","topic":"Networking architecture","subtopic":"Failure classification","concept":"First-failed-stage principle","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should network diagnostics identify the earliest evidence-backed failed lifecycle stage?","answer":"Later symptoms can be consequences of an earlier failure. Identifying the earliest stage supported by evidence reduces misleading root-cause attribution and prevents downstream symptoms from being treated as independent failures.","example":"If DNS resolution fails, a subsequent connection attempt does not exist and should not be classified as a TCP refusal.","verification_method":"Construct controlled multi-stage failures and verify that diagnostics preserve the earliest failed stage.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B66-R005","D18-B70-R004","D18-B80-R001"],"provenance":"Derived failure-localization principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B80-R003","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Networking architecture","subtopic":"Autonomous remediation","concept":"Evidence-gated network remediation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should an autonomous software-engineering system require before applying a network remediation?","answer":"It should require a sufficiently supported failure hypothesis, explicit remediation preconditions, bounded impact, rollback or recovery behavior where applicable, and post-remediation verification. A plausible diagnosis alone is insufficient for autonomous high-impact change.","requirements":["Evidence-backed failure classification","Known remediation scope","Safety constraints","Rollback or recovery plan when applicable","Post-change verification","Audit record"],"failure_mode":"System changes network configuration solely because one error pattern resembles a historical case.","root_cause":"Similarity is treated as proof.","solution":"Gate remediation on evidence and verify the resulting network and application state.","verification_method":"Require before/after evidence and preserve UNKNOWN when verification cannot be completed.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B70-R004","D18-B69-R005"],"provenance":"Derived autonomous-remediation governance principle."}
{"record_id":"D18-B80-R004","dataset_id":"D18","version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Networking architecture","subtopic":"Knowledge relationships","concept":"Network knowledge-fabric relationships","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should networking knowledge records connect concepts, failures, implementations, tests, and verification?","answer":"Records should use explicit typed relationships such as implements, violates, causes, caused_by, mitigates, tests, verifies, depends_on, supersedes, specializes, conflicts_with, and observed_in. This allows retrieval to traverse from a conceptual rule to implementation examples, failure cases, tests, and evidence.","relationship_types":["implements","violates","causes","caused_by","mitigates","tests","verifies","depends_on","supersedes","specializes","conflicts_with","observed_in"],"deduplication_rule":"Use relationships to connect existing records rather than duplicating the same networking principle in multiple records.","verification_method":"Validate relationship targets, direction, semantic consistency, and absence of orphaned critical records.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B70-R005","D18-B80-R005"],"provenance":"Derived knowledge-fabric relationship methodology."}
{"record_id":"D18-B80-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B80","topic":"Networking","subtopic":"Dataset completion","concept":"D18 networking implementation coverage gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What conditions should be satisfied before D18 Networking is considered complete?","answer":"Completion should require coverage of core networking concepts, protocol behavior, implementation patterns, invalid implementations, important edge cases, failure localization, security controls, performance considerations, testing, verification, version-sensitive protocol behavior, evidence provenance, contradiction handling, and cross-record relationships. Completion must be based on scope coverage rather than record count.","completion_gate":{"conceptual_coverage":"REQUIRED","implementation_coverage":"REQUIRED_WHERE_APPLICABLE","invalid_implementation":"REQUIRED_WHERE_APPLICABLE","edge_cases":"REQUIRED","failure_modes":"REQUIRED","testing":"REQUIRED","verification":"REQUIRED","security":"REQUIRED_WHERE_RELEVANT","performance":"REQUIRED_WHERE_RELEVANT","provenance":"REQUIRED","deduplication":"REQUIRED","contradiction_audit":"REQUIRED","relationship_integrity":"REQUIRED"},"status":"NOT_LOCKED","reason":"B71-B80 adds substantial coverage but a final lock requires whole-dataset audit across D18 B1-B80 and any remaining scope gaps.","verification_method":"Run the D18-wide coverage, deduplication, contradiction, provenance, implementation, testing, and verification audits before locking.","language":"ARCHITECTURE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","relationships":["D18-B50-R005","D18-B70-R005","D18-B80-R004"],"provenance":"Derived dataset completion and quality-gate methodology."}

{"record_id":"D18-B81-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"negative caching","concept":"DNS negative responses can be cached","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why can a DNS lookup continue returning a previous NXDOMAIN or negative result after the underlying record is created?","answer":"Resolvers may cache negative DNS responses according to the applicable negative caching rules and TTL information. A newly created record may therefore remain unresolvable through that resolver until the negative cache entry expires or is otherwise refreshed.","principle":"DNS absence is also cacheable state; record creation does not necessarily become visible immediately to every resolver.","constraints":["Negative caching duration depends on authoritative DNS configuration and resolver behavior.","Applications must not assume immediate global visibility after DNS changes."],"failure_mode":"A newly created hostname remains unresolved from some clients while other resolvers resolve it.","root_cause":"A resolver is serving a still-valid cached negative response.","testing_guidance":"Query multiple controlled resolvers before and after the authoritative change and record response type, TTL, and observation time.","verification_method":"Compare resolver responses against authoritative DNS responses while accounting for the reported TTL.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS operational standards and resolver negative-caching behavior"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B72-R001"],"supports":["D18-B81-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B81-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"TTL","concept":"DNS TTL controls cache lifetime rather than guaranteeing propagation time","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should DNS TTL be interpreted when diagnosing propagation delays?","answer":"TTL indicates how long a cached DNS response may be considered usable under the DNS caching model; it should not be interpreted as a universal guarantee that every client will observe a change exactly at that interval.","principle":"TTL is a cache-control parameter, not a global propagation timer.","edge_case":"Different resolvers may have cached different versions of a record at different times.","testing_guidance":"Capture resolver identity, response data, TTL, authoritative answer, and observation timestamp when diagnosing inconsistent DNS results.","verification_method":"Verify both the authoritative record and the resolver's cached response independently.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS standards concerning TTL and caching"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B81-R001"],"extends":["D18-B72-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B81-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"CNAME","concept":"CNAME chains add resolution dependencies","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can CNAME chains complicate DNS troubleshooting?","answer":"A hostname may resolve through one or more aliases before reaching the final address records. Failure or stale data at any dependency can affect the final resolution result, so inspecting only the first visible hostname may hide the actual failing link.","failure_mode":"The alias appears valid but the final target cannot be resolved or returns an unexpected address.","root_cause":"A downstream CNAME target or its address records are missing, stale, unreachable, or incorrectly configured.","testing_guidance":"Trace the complete DNS resolution chain and inspect each authoritative answer separately.","verification_method":"Verify each alias target and final address record independently.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS specifications and resolver behavior"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B81-R001"],"supports":["D18-B81-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B81-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"resolver search behavior","concept":"Search domains can transform an apparently simple hostname into multiple DNS queries","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can a short hostname produce unexpected DNS traffic?","answer":"Resolver configuration may apply search domains to names that are not treated as fully qualified names. A request for a short name can therefore result in multiple candidate queries before resolution succeeds or fails.","failure_mode":"An application resolves an unexpected host or experiences multiple DNS attempts for one apparent lookup.","root_cause":"Resolver search-list configuration changes name interpretation and query expansion.","security_implications":"Unexpected search behavior can increase DNS traffic and may cause sensitive internal names to be queried against unintended domains if configuration is incorrect.","testing_guidance":"Test short, absolute, and explicitly qualified names under the actual resolver configuration.","verification_method":"Capture DNS queries and compare them with the resolver's configured search and qualification behavior.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS resolver specifications and operating-system resolver behavior"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B81-R002"],"supports":["D18-B81-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B81-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"split-horizon DNS","concept":"Different DNS views can intentionally return different answers","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why might internal and external clients receive different DNS answers for the same name?","answer":"Split-horizon or view-based DNS can provide different responses depending on the querying network, resolver, identity, or policy context. Troubleshooting must therefore identify the resolver path and client context rather than assuming one globally authoritative answer is visible everywhere.","constraints":["Resolver context must be treated as part of DNS evidence.","An internal answer must not automatically be assumed to represent the public DNS view."],"testing_guidance":"Test the same name from each intended network context and record resolver identity and response data.","verification_method":"Compare authoritative/view-specific responses with client-observed responses.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS operational architecture and resolver view mechanisms"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B81-R002"],"supports":["D18-B81-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B82-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"encrypted DNS","concept":"DNS over TLS and DNS over HTTPS change transport privacy rather than DNS semantics","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What does encrypted DNS protect and what does it not automatically solve?","answer":"Encrypted DNS transports DNS messages through an encrypted channel, reducing exposure of DNS traffic to network observers on the transport path. It does not by itself guarantee that the requested domain is trustworthy, that the resolver is authoritative for the answer, or that application-layer security is correct.","principle":"Protect DNS transport confidentiality separately from validating DNS data and application security.","security_implications":["Encrypted transport does not make a malicious domain safe.","Resolver trust and endpoint authentication remain separate concerns."],"testing_guidance":"Verify the configured resolver, encrypted transport establishment, certificate validation where applicable, and returned DNS data independently.","verification_method":"Inspect the DNS transport handshake and compare the received answer with the expected resolver policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS over TLS and DNS over HTTPS standards"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B79-R003"],"supports":["D18-B82-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B82-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"DNS transport failure classification","concept":"DNS transport failure differs from a valid negative DNS answer","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"Why should NXDOMAIN and DNS transport failure be represented differently?","answer":"NXDOMAIN is a DNS-level statement that the queried name does not exist in the relevant namespace. A timeout, connection failure, malformed response, or unavailable resolver does not establish nonexistence and should therefore be classified separately.","failure_mode":"An application converts every DNS failure into 'host does not exist'.","root_cause":"Transport, resolver, and DNS semantic outcomes are collapsed into one error category.","solution":"Preserve distinct error classes for successful resolution, valid negative answers, temporary resolver failure, timeout, malformed response, and unavailable resolver.","testing_guidance":"Inject each failure class independently and verify that the application preserves the distinction.","verification_method":"Assert error-class mapping against controlled DNS scenarios.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS protocol semantics and resolver error behavior"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B49-R002"],"supports":["D18-B82-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B82-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"stale resolver data","concept":"Resolver observations must be interpreted with cache state","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"What evidence is needed before concluding that DNS is incorrectly configured?","answer":"A useful DNS diagnosis should compare the authoritative answer, resolver identity, cached response, TTL, response status, query name/type, and observation time. A single client response is insufficient to distinguish configuration error from expected caching behavior.","principle":"DNS diagnosis requires authoritative-versus-recursive evidence separation.","testing_guidance":"Collect observations from at least one authoritative source and the resolver path used by the affected client.","verification_method":"Determine whether the resolver response is consistent with the authoritative state and cache rules.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS troubleshooting practice derived from caching and resolution semantics"],"relationships":{"supports":["D18-B82-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B82-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"resolver resilience","concept":"DNS dependency failures can affect otherwise healthy application services","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should DNS availability be treated as a dependency in application architecture?","answer":"Applications frequently need DNS resolution before establishing network connections. Resolver unavailability, excessive latency, or incorrect responses can therefore prevent otherwise healthy services from reaching their destinations.","failure_mode":"Application health is judged solely by application-process status while DNS dependency is failing.","root_cause":"Dependency health and application process health are measured independently.","solution":"Model DNS resolution as an observable dependency and include resolver failures in network diagnostics and resilience testing.","testing_guidance":"Test application behavior when DNS resolution is delayed, unavailable, or returns valid negative responses.","verification_method":"Confirm that dependency failure is detected without incorrectly reporting downstream application success.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Distributed-systems dependency and DNS operational principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B50-R002"],"supports":["D18-B82-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B82-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"DNS test matrix","concept":"DNS tests should separate resolver, cache, transport, and semantic outcomes","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What dimensions should a robust DNS test matrix include?","answer":"A DNS test matrix should vary successful resolution, NXDOMAIN, temporary failure, timeout, malformed response, cached data, expired data, multiple record types, resolver differences, and relevant search or split-horizon contexts.","test_case":{"dimensions":["success","negative_answer","temporary_failure","timeout","malformed_response","cache_state","record_type","resolver_context","search_behavior","split_horizon"]},"expected_test_result":"The application classifies each scenario correctly, preserves useful evidence, and applies only the recovery behavior appropriate to that outcome.","verification_method":"Compare observed classification and recovery behavior against the scenario's expected semantic category.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS protocol behavior and network testing methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B82-R002","D18-B82-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B83-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Routing","subtopic":"longest-prefix match","concept":"Routing selects the most specific applicable prefix","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why can two routes appear to match the same destination while only one is selected?","answer":"Routing tables can contain overlapping prefixes. The longest-prefix match principle selects the route covering the destination with the greatest prefix specificity before other applicable route-selection rules are considered.","principle":"A more specific route can override a less specific route for the same destination range.","edge_case":"An unexpectedly specific route can create a local blackhole or bypass an intended path.","testing_guidance":"Inspect the complete routing table and calculate which prefixes match the destination before diagnosing path selection.","verification_method":"Compare the selected route with the destination's matching prefixes and routing-policy rules.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["IP routing standards and routing implementation principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D18-B83-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B83-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Routing","subtopic":"asymmetric routing","concept":"Forward and reverse traffic can use different paths","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why can a connection work in one direction while return traffic fails?","answer":"Network routing does not universally require symmetric paths. Forward and reverse traffic can traverse different interfaces, routers, security controls, or providers. Stateful devices may therefore reject return traffic if they do not observe the expected connection state.","failure_mode":"Forward traffic reaches the destination but responses never reach the client.","root_cause":"The reverse path differs from the expected stateful security or routing path.","testing_guidance":"Trace both directions independently and inspect stateful network controls on each path.","verification_method":"Compare forward and reverse route paths and verify state tracking at intermediate controls.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["IP routing behavior and stateful network architecture"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B83-R001"],"supports":["D18-B83-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B83-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Routing","subtopic":"blackholes","concept":"A route can exist while traffic remains unusable","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why does the presence of a routing-table entry not prove that the destination is reachable?","answer":"A route describes a forwarding decision, not successful end-to-end delivery. The selected next hop, downstream routes, filtering, interface state, MTU, or return path can still prevent delivery.","failure_mode":"An operator concludes connectivity exists because a route is present.","root_cause":"Control-plane reachability evidence is mistaken for end-to-end data-plane success.","solution":"Verify forwarding and return traffic with controlled probes and path evidence rather than relying only on route-table presence.","testing_guidance":"Check route selection, interface state, forwarding behavior, packet filtering, downstream routing, and return path.","verification_method":"Require successful end-to-end evidence before classifying a destination as reachable.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["IP routing and network troubleshooting principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B83-R002"],"supports":["D18-B83-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B83-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Routing","subtopic":"policy routing","concept":"Policy-based routing can make path selection depend on packet context","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why can identical destinations use different network paths?","answer":"Policy routing can select paths using attributes beyond destination prefix, such as source address, interface, traffic class, or other policy inputs depending on the platform. Destination-only reasoning can therefore miss the rule responsible for a path choice.","constraints":["Policy behavior is platform-specific.","Routing diagnosis must inspect applicable policy rules in addition to destination routes."],"testing_guidance":"Test identical destinations with controlled changes to the attributes used by routing policy.","verification_method":"Identify the matching policy rule and selected route for each controlled input.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Policy-routing implementation concepts and operating-system networking documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B83-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B83-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Routing","subtopic":"route convergence","concept":"Routing changes can temporarily produce inconsistent paths","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a routing change cause transient connectivity problems even when the final configuration is correct?","answer":"Distributed routing systems may require time to propagate and converge on a new topology. During convergence, different devices can temporarily hold different forwarding information, creating transient drops, loops, or unreachable paths depending on the protocol and topology.","principle":"The transient state during routing convergence is operationally distinct from the intended steady state.","testing_guidance":"Perform controlled topology changes and measure reachability during and after convergence without assuming a fixed convergence duration.","verification_method":"Verify that the network reaches the intended stable routing state and that transient behavior matches documented system expectations.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Routing-protocol convergence principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B83-R003"],"supports":["D18-B83-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B84-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"L4 versus L7","concept":"Layer-4 and layer-7 load balancing operate with different visibility","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the architectural difference between L4 and L7 load balancing?","answer":"L4 load balancing can make decisions using transport-level information such as addresses and ports, while L7 load balancing can inspect application-protocol information when it terminates or otherwise processes that protocol. L7 therefore enables application-aware routing but introduces additional protocol processing and trust boundaries.","tradeoffs":{"L4":"Lower application-level visibility and simpler protocol handling.","L7":"Greater application-aware routing capability with additional processing and protocol termination complexity."},"security_implications":"An L7 proxy becomes part of the application trust boundary and must correctly handle authentication, headers, protocol framing, and sensitive data.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Network architecture principles and HTTP proxy semantics"],"relationships":{"supports":["D18-B84-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B84-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"health checks","concept":"A load balancer health check is not automatically equivalent to application correctness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can a service pass a load-balancer health check while real requests fail?","answer":"A health check may test only a narrow endpoint or dependency set. Real requests can exercise different code paths, authentication, downstream dependencies, payload sizes, or resource limits that the health check does not cover.","failure_mode":"Traffic is routed to an instance that reports healthy but cannot successfully serve production requests.","root_cause":"The health check does not represent the critical serving path or required dependencies.","solution":"Design health checks according to the intended routing decision and explicitly distinguish process health, readiness, dependency readiness, and end-to-end serving capability.","testing_guidance":"Test health-check success alongside representative request scenarios and dependency failures.","verification_method":"Verify that the health state used for routing corresponds to the intended operational readiness contract.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Load-balancing and service-readiness engineering practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B50-R002"],"supports":["D18-B84-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B84-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"connection draining","concept":"Connection draining prevents abrupt removal of active connections","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should a load balancer drain existing connections before removing an instance?","answer":"Abruptly removing an instance can terminate active work or cause avoidable connection failures. Draining allows existing connections or requests to complete according to the applicable protocol and timeout policy while preventing new traffic from being assigned to the retiring instance.","constraints":["Drain duration must be bounded.","Long-lived connections require protocol-specific handling.","Draining does not guarantee successful completion of every request."],"testing_guidance":"Remove an instance under controlled traffic and verify new traffic stops while eligible existing work receives the configured drain opportunity.","verification_method":"Observe routing decisions, connection termination behavior, and completion outcomes during controlled removal.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Load-balancer connection-draining patterns"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B44-R002"],"supports":["D18-B84-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B84-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"sticky sessions","concept":"Session affinity changes distribution behavior","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the main architectural trade-off introduced by sticky sessions?","answer":"Session affinity can preserve state locality when applications depend on instance-local session state, but it reduces distribution flexibility and can concentrate load when clients are unevenly distributed. It also complicates failover when the preferred instance becomes unavailable.","tradeoffs":{"benefit":"Requests from an affinity group can continue reaching the state-holding instance.","cost":"Load distribution and failover become more constrained."},"solution":"Prefer externally shared or durable session state when practical, then use affinity only when its semantics are explicitly required.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Distributed application session-management patterns"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B84-R002"],"supports":["D18-B84-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B84-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"consistent hashing","concept":"Consistent hashing can reduce reassignment when membership changes","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is consistent hashing useful for distributed routing of keyed workloads?","answer":"Consistent-hashing schemes can arrange keys and nodes so that membership changes remap a smaller portion of keys than naive modulo-based partitioning typically does. This can reduce cache movement or state reassignment when nodes join or leave, subject to the specific algorithm and configuration.","constraints":["Distribution quality depends on the hashing scheme and node representation.","It does not eliminate rebalancing.","Correctness still depends on handling node failure and membership changes."],"testing_guidance":"Measure key-to-node assignments before and after controlled membership changes using a defined workload and algorithm.","verification_method":"Calculate the fraction of keys reassigned and verify it against the algorithm's expected behavior; do not assume a universal percentage.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Consistent-hashing literature and distributed-systems design principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B84-R004"],"supports":["D18-B84-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B85-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"HTTP CONNECT","concept":"CONNECT establishes a tunnel through an HTTP proxy","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What problem does HTTP CONNECT solve for a proxy client?","answer":"CONNECT can request that an HTTP proxy establish a tunnel to a target authority, allowing subsequent protocol bytes to traverse the established tunnel. The proxy therefore becomes part of the connection establishment and authorization path.","security_implications":"Proxy authorization and target-policy enforcement must be applied before allowing the tunnel.","testing_guidance":"Test allowed and denied target authorities and verify that tunnel establishment occurs only when policy permits it.","verification_method":"Inspect proxy authorization, target-policy decision, tunnel establishment, and subsequent byte forwarding separately.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP specifications defining CONNECT semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B79-R001"],"supports":["D18-B85-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B85-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"forwarded headers","concept":"Forwarded client metadata must have an explicit trust boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is blindly trusting client-supplied forwarding metadata unsafe?","answer":"Headers such as Forwarded or X-Forwarded-* can be supplied or modified by an untrusted client unless a trusted proxy establishes and sanitizes them. Applications that derive security decisions from such metadata must know which network component is authoritative for the value.","failure_mode":"An application treats attacker-controlled forwarding metadata as the original client identity or transport security state.","root_cause":"Trust in proxy-generated metadata is not bound to an authenticated proxy boundary.","solution":"Define trusted proxy boundaries, sanitize or replace forwarded metadata at that boundary, and ensure downstream applications trust only the documented source.","testing_guidance":"Send conflicting client-supplied forwarding headers through trusted and untrusted paths and verify the resulting canonical metadata.","verification_method":"Verify that application security decisions depend only on metadata from the authorized proxy boundary.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP forwarding-header specifications and reverse-proxy security practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B78-R002"],"supports":["D18-B85-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B85-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"proxy authentication","concept":"Proxy authentication is distinct from origin authentication","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should proxy authentication and destination-service authentication be modeled separately?","answer":"Proxy authentication establishes whether the client is permitted to use the intermediary, while origin authentication establishes identity or authorization with the destination service. A successful proxy authentication does not imply authorization at the destination.","principle":"Each trust boundary requires its own explicit authentication and authorization semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test valid and invalid proxy credentials independently from valid and invalid origin credentials.","verification_method":"Verify that each failure is reported against the correct authentication boundary.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP proxy authentication and application authentication semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B79-R004"],"supports":["D18-B85-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B85-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"explicit versus transparent proxying","concept":"Proxy deployment mode changes what the application and client can observe","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do explicit and transparent proxying differ operationally?","answer":"Explicit proxying generally requires the client or application stack to know and use the proxy configuration, while transparent interception can redirect traffic without the application explicitly selecting the proxy. This changes configuration, observability, policy enforcement, and troubleshooting assumptions.","tradeoffs":{"explicit":"Clear client-side proxy configuration and policy path.","transparent":"Centralized interception can reduce client configuration but can make the traffic path less obvious to applications and operators."},"testing_guidance":"Document the actual network path and test behavior with and without proxy interception.","verification_method":"Confirm the traffic path independently from application configuration assumptions.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Proxy deployment architecture principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B85-R001"],"supports":["D18-B85-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B85-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Proxies","subtopic":"proxy failure classification","concept":"Proxy failure must be separated from origin failure","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"Why should a client distinguish proxy connection failure from destination connection failure?","answer":"A request can fail before reaching the destination because the proxy is unavailable, denies the request, cannot resolve the target, or cannot establish the tunnel. These cases differ from failures returned by the destination service and require different diagnostics and recovery strategies.","failure_mode":"All network failures are reported as generic destination-unreachable errors.","root_cause":"Proxy and origin stages are collapsed into one error category.","solution":"Represent proxy-selection, proxy-authentication, tunnel-establishment, target-resolution, destination-connect, and application-response stages separately.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at each stage and verify stage-specific classification.","verification_method":"Assert that the recorded failure stage corresponds to the first independently evidenced failure.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Proxy architecture and staged network diagnostics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B80-R002"],"supports":["D18-B85-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B86-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"pool limits","concept":"Connection pools require explicit capacity limits","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should a connection pool define total and per-destination capacity?","answer":"Without bounded connection capacity, concurrency can create excessive sockets, memory use, file-descriptor consumption, upstream load, and ephemeral-port pressure. Total and per-destination limits allow resource use to be controlled according to the application's dependency topology.","constraints":["Pool limits must be compatible with application concurrency.","A large pool does not guarantee better throughput.","Different destinations may require different limits."],"testing_guidance":"Increase concurrency beyond configured limits and verify that pool acquisition behavior remains bounded and observable.","verification_method":"Measure active, idle, waiting, and failed acquisitions against the configured capacity contract.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Connection-pooling and resource-management engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B73-R001"],"supports":["D18-B86-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B86-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"pool starvation","concept":"A healthy network can still appear unavailable when all pooled connections are leased","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What causes connection-pool starvation?","answer":"Pool starvation occurs when available connections are exhausted and callers wait for leases instead of creating or obtaining usable connections. Causes include excessive concurrency, slow requests, leaked leases, undersized limits, or cancellation paths that fail to release resources.","failure_mode":"Requests time out waiting for a connection even though the destination is reachable.","root_cause":"Pool capacity is exhausted rather than the network path being unavailable.","solution":"Instrument lease wait time, active leases, idle connections, pool limits, and release behavior before increasing capacity.","testing_guidance":"Simulate long-lived requests and concurrent acquisition beyond pool capacity.","verification_method":"Verify that observed wait time correlates with pool exhaustion and that leases are released after request completion or cancellation.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Connection-pool resource management and distributed-systems practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B86-R001"],"supports":["D18-B86-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B86-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B86","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"lease timeout","concept":"Pool lease timeout should be distinct from network connection timeout","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should pool acquisition timeout and socket connection timeout be represented separately?","answer":"A pool lease timeout occurs while waiting for an available client-side resource. A connection timeout occurs while establishing a network connection. They indicate different bottlenecks and should not be collapsed into one generic network timeout.","failure_mode":"Operators increase network timeout values when requests are actually blocked waiting for pool capacity.","solution":"Record separate timing and error fields for pool acquisition, connection establishment, request transmission, response waiting, and total deadline.","testing_guidance":"Exhaust the pool and separately make the destination slow or unreachable.","verification_method":"Verify that each controlled scenario produces the corresponding timeout category.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Connection-pooling and network-timeout architecture"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B48-R001"],"supports":["D18-B86-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B86-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"cancellation safety","concept":"Cancellation must not leak pooled connections","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must cancellation paths explicitly release connection-pool resources?","answer":"A request can be cancelled while waiting for a pool lease, establishing a connection, sending data, or receiving a response. If cancellation bypasses cleanup, the pool can retain resources and progressively lose usable capacity.","failure_mode":"Repeated cancellations reduce available pool capacity until unrelated requests begin timing out.","root_cause":"Resource cleanup is not guaranteed across all cancellation paths.","solution":"Use structured resource-management mechanisms appropriate to the language and client library and verify cleanup on normal, exceptional, and cancelled execution paths.","testing_guidance":"Cancel operations at each stage and inspect active leases and connection counts afterward.","verification_method":"Assert that resources return to the expected pool state after every cancellation scenario.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Resource-lifetime and asynchronous cancellation engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B45-R002"],"supports":["D18-B86-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B86-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B86","topic":"Connection pools","subtopic":"idle eviction","concept":"Idle connection reuse requires lifecycle management","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can reusing idle connections sometimes fail even when pooling is beneficial?","answer":"Idle network connections can become unusable because of remote closure, intermediary timeouts, server-side limits, protocol state, or network changes. A pool therefore needs defined idle-lifetime and stale-connection handling behavior rather than assuming every retained connection remains valid indefinitely.","edge_case":"A pooled connection appears locally open but the peer or intermediary has already discarded the corresponding connection state.","testing_guidance":"Allow connections to remain idle through controlled intermediary or server timeout conditions and then reuse them.","verification_method":"Verify that stale connections are detected and recovered without leaking resources or violating request deadlines.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Connection-pooling lifecycle and TCP connection-management principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B73-R002"],"supports":["D18-B86-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B87-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"lifecycle","concept":"A listening socket and an accepted connection have different lifecycles","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why should server code distinguish the listening socket from accepted client sockets?","answer":"A listening socket accepts new connection requests, while each accepted socket represents an individual connection with its own read, write, shutdown, error, and close lifecycle. Closing or mismanaging one must not accidentally terminate the listener unless that behavior is explicitly intended.","testing_guidance":"Create multiple accepted connections and close one independently while verifying that the listener remains available.","verification_method":"Observe independent lifecycle events for the listener and accepted sockets.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Socket API and TCP server programming specifications"],"relationships":{"supports":["D18-B87-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B87-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"backlog","concept":"Listen backlog is a queueing mechanism, not an end-to-end capacity guarantee","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should socket backlog not be interpreted as the maximum number of clients a server can support?","answer":"Backlog controls a kernel or protocol-stack aspect of pending connection handling according to platform semantics. It does not define total application concurrency, throughput, memory capacity, or the number of established connections the application can safely maintain.","constraints":["Exact backlog semantics are platform-dependent.","Application capacity must be modeled separately from pending-connection queueing."],"testing_guidance":"Vary connection arrival rate and application acceptance speed while observing pending and established connections.","verification_method":"Confirm backlog behavior using the target operating system and runtime documentation rather than assuming a universal mapping.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Operating-system socket APIs and TCP listen semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B86-R001"],"supports":["D18-B87-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B87-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B87","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"nonblocking I/O","concept":"Nonblocking sockets require readiness or asynchronous I/O handling","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What changes when a socket is configured for nonblocking I/O?","answer":"Operations that would otherwise wait can return an indication that the operation cannot currently complete. Application code must integrate the socket with an appropriate readiness or asynchronous I/O mechanism and retry the operation only when the runtime indicates progress may be possible.","failure_mode":"Code treats a temporary would-block condition as a permanent connection failure.","root_cause":"Blocking and nonblocking I/O semantics are mixed without proper state handling.","testing_guidance":"Force partial reads, partial writes, and temporary would-block conditions and verify stateful retry behavior.","verification_method":"Assert that the application preserves unsent or unread data and resumes correctly after readiness notifications.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Socket API nonblocking I/O semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B45-R001"],"supports":["D18-B87-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B87-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B87","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"partial writes","concept":"A successful socket write may consume only part of the requested data","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must socket send logic handle partial writes?","answer":"Socket APIs can report that only part of the supplied byte sequence was accepted. Correct stream-oriented code must retain the unsent portion and continue according to the socket's readiness and deadline rules.","failure_mode":"Application assumes the entire buffer was transmitted after one successful write call.","root_cause":"Application-level message completion is incorrectly equated with one socket-write operation.","solution":"Track the byte offset explicitly and continue sending until the complete intended byte sequence is handled or an error/deadline occurs.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled partial-write conditions and verify that no bytes are duplicated or lost.","verification_method":"Compare transmitted byte sequence with the intended byte sequence after multiple partial writes.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Socket stream I/O semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B87-R003"],"supports":["D18-B87-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B87-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B87","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"half-close","concept":"TCP shutdown of one direction does not necessarily terminate the other direction immediately","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is a TCP half-close and why can it matter to application protocols?","answer":"TCP supports independently shutting down one direction of a connection. An application can therefore signal that it has finished sending while still being able to receive data, provided the protocol and implementation support that lifecycle.","edge_case":"Application code treats end-of-input as equivalent to immediate full connection closure and discards a valid response.","testing_guidance":"Test protocols that use one-way completion followed by continued response data.","verification_method":"Verify that the implementation distinguishes receive end-of-stream from complete socket closure.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TCP and socket shutdown semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B45-R001"],"supports":["D18-B87-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B88-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"TCP","subtopic":"flow control","concept":"TCP flow control protects the receiver from uncontrolled sender rate","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why can a sender's application-level output rate be lower than expected even when the network path appears healthy?","answer":"TCP flow control limits the amount of unacknowledged data according to receiver-advertised capacity. A receiver that processes data slowly can therefore constrain the sender's effective transmission rate.","principle":"Application throughput is influenced by transport flow control as well as application and network capacity.","testing_guidance":"Use a controlled receiver with varying processing rates and observe send-side blocking or reduced progress.","verification_method":"Correlate observed throughput with receiver behavior and transport-level flow-control evidence.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TCP transport specifications"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B45-R002"],"supports":["D18-B88-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B88-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B88","topic":"TCP","subtopic":"congestion control","concept":"TCP congestion control responds to perceived network conditions","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why can TCP throughput change without an application configuration change?","answer":"TCP congestion-control algorithms adapt sending behavior based on transport-level observations of the network path. Changes in loss, delay, acknowledgements, or other protocol signals can therefore affect throughput even when application configuration is unchanged.","performance_implications":"Observed application throughput should not automatically be attributed to application-layer processing without examining transport and path conditions.","testing_guidance":"Measure application latency and throughput alongside transport and network-path observations under controlled load.","verification_method":"Separate application processing time from transport delivery behavior before attributing a performance regression.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TCP congestion-control specifications and networking literature"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B88-R001"],"supports":["D18-B88-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B88-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B88","topic":"TCP","subtopic":"retransmission","concept":"TCP retransmission can increase latency without application-visible packet-level errors","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can TCP retransmission appear as increased application latency rather than an explicit network error?","answer":"TCP attempts to recover lost segments transparently through retransmission. The application may therefore observe increased delivery latency or reduced throughput without receiving an explicit packet-loss event.","diagnostic_rule":"Elevated latency should be correlated with transport and path evidence before being attributed solely to application processing.","testing_guidance":"Collect transport-level loss or retransmission observations together with application timing during controlled network degradation.","verification_method":"Compare application latency with transport retransmission evidence for the same request interval.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TCP reliability and retransmission behavior"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B77-R002"],"supports":["D18-B88-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B88-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B88","topic":"TCP","subtopic":"keepalive versus heartbeat","concept":"TCP keepalive and application heartbeats serve different purposes","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should TCP keepalive not automatically replace an application heartbeat?","answer":"TCP keepalive operates at the transport layer and can help detect certain stale or unreachable peer conditions according to operating-system and socket configuration. An application heartbeat can additionally verify that the peer application is responsive according to application-specific semantics.","tradeoffs":{"tcp_keepalive":"Transport-level liveness mechanism with platform-dependent configuration.","application_heartbeat":"Application-level semantic liveness check but adds protocol traffic and implementation complexity."},"testing_guidance":"Test transport reachability separately from application responsiveness.","verification_method":"Verify that each mechanism detects only the failure classes it is designed to detect.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TCP keepalive behavior and application heartbeat design principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B44-R004"],"supports":["D18-B88-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B88-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B88","topic":"TCP","subtopic":"half-open connections","concept":"A locally retained connection does not prove the peer is still application-available","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can an application believe a TCP connection is alive when the peer has become unavailable?","answer":"Connection state observed locally may not immediately reflect all forms of remote failure, especially when no traffic is exchanged that causes the failure to become observable. Applications requiring stronger liveness semantics need appropriate traffic, deadlines, keepalive, or protocol-level health checks.","failure_mode":"Long-idle connections remain in local state until a later operation fails.","root_cause":"Liveness is inferred from stored connection state rather than current evidence.","solution":"Define an explicit liveness contract and use protocol-appropriate observation mechanisms.","testing_guidance":"Test peer disappearance, network partition, and idle intervals before the next operation.","verification_method":"Verify that the chosen mechanism detects the intended failure within the application's allowed deadline rather than assuming immediate detection.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TCP connection-state behavior and distributed-systems liveness principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B88-R004"],"supports":["D18-B86-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B89-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"SNI","concept":"TLS server-name indication supports certificate and virtual-host selection","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why can the same server address require SNI during TLS connection establishment?","answer":"A TLS endpoint can host multiple secure names on a shared address. SNI allows the client to indicate the intended server name during the TLS handshake so the endpoint can select the appropriate certificate and configuration where supported.","failure_mode":"A client reaches the correct address but receives an unexpected certificate or TLS configuration.","root_cause":"The intended server name was not supplied or was not processed as expected.","testing_guidance":"Test multiple names sharing the same endpoint and inspect the server certificate and negotiated configuration.","verification_method":"Verify that the requested hostname, selected certificate, and endpoint configuration correspond to the intended service.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TLS protocol specifications concerning server name indication"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B75-R001"],"supports":["D18-B89-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B89-R002","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B89","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"hostname verification","concept":"Certificate chain validity and hostname identity are separate checks","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is a trusted certificate chain not sufficient by itself for TLS hostname validation?","answer":"A certificate can be issued by a trusted authority while identifying a different hostname. Correct TLS client validation therefore requires both appropriate trust evaluation and identity matching against the intended server name according to the TLS implementation's rules.","failure_mode":"Client accepts a certificate because its issuer is trusted without validating the requested hostname.","root_cause":"Certificate-chain trust is treated as equivalent to endpoint identity verification.","solution":"Use a TLS implementation that performs standard certificate and hostname validation and avoid disabling hostname checks merely to bypass configuration errors.","testing_guidance":"Test a trusted certificate issued for the wrong hostname and verify connection rejection.","verification_method":"Confirm that the client rejects identity mismatch even when the certificate chain is otherwise trusted.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TLS certificate validation specifications and standard client behavior"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B79-R002"],"supports":["D18-B89-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B89-R003","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B89","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"trust stores","concept":"TLS trust configuration is an explicit dependency","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why can TLS connections fail after a certificate change even when the server is reachable?","answer":"The client may not trust the issuing certificate authority, may use an outdated or restricted trust store, or may apply application-specific trust policy. Network reachability and TLS trust are therefore separate diagnostic stages.","failure_mode":"TCP connection succeeds but TLS handshake fails with a certificate verification error.","root_cause":"The client's trust policy does not accept the presented certificate chain.","solution":"Inspect the actual trust store and certificate chain used by the runtime instead of disabling verification.","testing_guidance":"Test with the intended trust configuration and capture the certificate chain and verification error category.","verification_method":"Verify chain validation against the exact trust configuration used by the application.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TLS certificate-path validation and runtime trust-store behavior"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B89-R002"],"supports":["D18-B89-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B89-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B89","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"session resumption","concept":"TLS session resumption can reduce repeated handshake work but does not remove security requirements","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the purpose of TLS session resumption?","answer":"TLS session resumption allows a client and server with appropriate prior session state or tickets to establish subsequent secure connections with reduced handshake work compared with a full initial handshake. The implementation must still enforce the protocol's security and policy requirements.","tradeoffs":{"benefit":"Potentially lower handshake latency and computational overhead.","cost":"Additional state or ticket-management complexity and protocol-specific security considerations."},"performance_claim":"Qualitative only; no benchmark is asserted.","testing_guidance":"Compare full and resumed handshakes under the target TLS implementation while validating certificate and policy behavior.","verification_method":"Verify negotiated resumption behavior and security properties using protocol-level instrumentation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TLS protocol specifications on session resumption"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B75-R001"],"supports":["D18-B89-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B89-R005","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B89","topic":"TLS","subtopic":"0-RTT","concept":"TLS 0-RTT can introduce replay considerations","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should application protocols treat TLS 0-RTT differently from ordinary handshake-completed requests?","answer":"TLS 0-RTT can allow application data to be sent earlier during connection establishment, but early data has replay-related considerations that affect which operations are safe to perform. Applications should therefore avoid treating replay-sensitive state-changing operations as automatically safe merely because transport encryption is present.","security_implications":"Replay-sensitive operations require protocol and application safeguards appropriate to early data semantics.","testing_guidance":"Classify application operations by replay tolerance and verify that early-data policy prevents unsafe operations from being accepted without suitable protection.","verification_method":"Verify the client's and server's early-data policy and test replay-sensitive request handling explicitly.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TLS 1.3 specifications concerning early data and replay considerations"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B89-R004"],"supports":["D18-B90-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B90-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"HTTP security","subtopic":"request smuggling","concept":"HTTP request parsing disagreement across intermediaries can create security boundaries","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is inconsistent HTTP message parsing between a proxy and backend dangerous?","answer":"When intermediaries and origin servers interpret message framing differently, an attacker may be able to cause one component to see a different request boundary from another. This can desynchronize request processing and create security vulnerabilities.","failure_mode":"Proxy and backend disagree about where one HTTP request ends and another begins.","root_cause":"Inconsistent parsing or handling of conflicting message-framing information.","solution":"Use standards-conformant, consistently configured HTTP implementations and reject ambiguous framing rather than attempting to normalize it differently at different layers.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled parser-differential test cases against the exact proxy and backend versions without deploying them against untrusted systems.","verification_method":"Verify that all components reject or interpret ambiguous framing consistently according to their documented HTTP behavior.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP message-framing specifications and established request-smuggling research"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B85-R001"],"supports":["D18-B90-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B90-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"HTTP security","subtopic":"Host and authority","concept":"HTTP target authority participates in routing and virtual-host selection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must HTTP authority information be validated consistently across proxies and origin servers?","answer":"The destination authority can influence which virtual host, backend, certificate context, or policy applies. If intermediaries and origin services derive routing or security decisions from inconsistent authority values, requests can cross unintended trust boundaries.","failure_mode":"Proxy routing and origin authorization use different interpretations of the requested authority.","root_cause":"Authority normalization and validation rules differ across network layers.","solution":"Define one canonical authority-processing policy and ensure every trusted intermediary applies compatible validation and routing rules.","testing_guidance":"Test equivalent, malformed, and conflicting authority representations through the complete proxy chain.","verification_method":"Verify that the selected backend and security policy are identical for all accepted canonical representations.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP authority semantics and reverse-proxy security practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B85-R002"],"supports":["D18-B90-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B90-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"HTTP","subtopic":"range requests","concept":"HTTP range requests allow partial representation transfer","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why are range requests useful for large HTTP representations?","answer":"Range requests allow a client to request selected byte ranges of a representation when the server supports the relevant semantics. They can support resumable or partial retrieval but require correct handling of range validity, response status, content-range metadata, and representation identity.","edge_case":"A resource may change between range requests, making independently retrieved ranges inconsistent unless the client uses appropriate validators or application-level consistency rules.","testing_guidance":"Test valid, unsatisfiable, multiple, and stale range requests against a controlled resource.","verification_method":"Verify response status and range metadata and confirm that reconstructed content corresponds to the intended representation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP semantics for range requests and validators"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B41-R003"],"supports":["D18-B90-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B90-R004","dataset_id":"D18","batch":"B90","topic":"HTTP","subtopic":"multipart framing","concept":"Multipart HTTP bodies require boundary-aware parsing","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must multipart HTTP parsers treat boundary handling as structured framing rather than simple string splitting?","answer":"Multipart bodies contain explicit boundary delimiters and headers that define individual parts. Correct parsing must account for framing rules, boundary recognition, headers, content bytes, and malformed or truncated input rather than assuming arbitrary string splitting is safe.","failure_mode":"Parser corrupts binary content or misidentifies a boundary appearing inside a part.","root_cause":"Multipart framing semantics are replaced with naive text-based parsing.","solution":"Use a standards-compliant multipart parser or implement the complete framing grammar with bounded resource handling.","security_implications":"Parser inconsistencies and unbounded multipart processing can create request desynchronization, resource-exhaustion, or input-validation vulnerabilities.","testing_guidance":"Test binary payloads, boundary-like byte sequences, truncated bodies, oversized headers, and malformed delimiters.","verification_method":"Compare parser output against a trusted reference implementation or specification-derived test vectors.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP multipart specifications and secure parser design principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B45-R005"],"supports":["D18-B90-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B90-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Networking","subtopic":"D18 coverage gate","concept":"D18 requires cross-layer implementation and verification coverage before locking","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be true before D18 Networking can be considered ready for final dataset locking?","answer":"The complete D18 corpus must be audited across DNS, TCP/IP, HTTP, TLS, sockets, routing, proxies, load balancing, timeouts, retries, connection pools, failure modes, security, performance, edge cases, implementation examples, tests, verification, provenance, versioning, and cross-record relationships. Record count alone is not a completion criterion.","verification_method":"Run schema validation, ID uniqueness checks, relationship integrity checks, duplicate and near-duplicate analysis, contradiction review, provenance review, implementation-coverage audit, invalid-code coverage audit where applicable, test/verification linkage audit, and retrieval-oriented coverage analysis across the entire D18 corpus.","completion_criteria":["No unexplained duplicate stable IDs.","No silently modified prior locked records.","Required schema fields are normalized in the authoritative version.","No fabricated execution or benchmark claims.","Important scope gaps are explicitly identified and resolved or documented.","Security and performance claims have appropriate evidence labels.","Implementation examples are version-aware where applicable.","Tests and verification guidance are linked to relevant implementation and failure records.","Relationships remain valid across the complete D18 dataset."],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D18 dataset quality specification and knowledge-fabric governance rules"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B80-R005"],"governs":["D18-B81-R001","D18-B82-R001","D18-B83-R001","D18-B84-R001","D18-B85-R001","D18-B86-R001","D18-B87-R001","D18-B88-R001","D18-B89-R001","D18-B90-R001"]}}

{"record_id":"D18-B91-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"DNSSEC","concept":"DNSSEC provides authenticity and integrity mechanisms for DNS data but does not provide confidentiality","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What security property does DNSSEC provide that encrypted DNS transport does not?","answer":"DNSSEC provides mechanisms for validating the authenticity and integrity of signed DNS data through a chain of trust. It does not encrypt DNS queries or responses, so it should be distinguished from transport-protection mechanisms such as DNS over TLS or DNS over HTTPS.","principle":"DNS data authenticity, DNS transport confidentiality, and application endpoint security are separate security properties.","security_implications":"A system should not treat encrypted DNS as equivalent to DNSSEC validation or treat DNSSEC as a confidentiality mechanism.","testing_guidance":"Test DNSSEC validation independently from encrypted DNS transport and verify the expected behavior for valid, invalid, unsigned, and unavailable validation states.","verification_method":"Verify that the resolver's DNSSEC validation result is represented separately from transport encryption status.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNSSEC protocol standards","DNS transport-security standards"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B82-R001"],"supports":["D18-B91-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B91-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"TTL and deployment strategy","concept":"DNS record changes should account for cached state before planned endpoint migration","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why can lowering DNS TTL before a migration be useful while still not guaranteeing immediate cache expiration?","answer":"Lowering TTL before a planned change can reduce the intended future lifetime of newly cached records, but previously cached responses may remain according to their existing cache state and resolver behavior. Migration planning therefore requires a controlled lead time rather than assuming a TTL change immediately removes existing cached data.","constraints":["Existing cached responses are governed by their previously received cache metadata.","TTL planning does not guarantee simultaneous resolver behavior.","Authoritative changes and application migration must be coordinated."],"testing_guidance":"Perform a controlled migration in a non-production environment and observe multiple resolver populations before and after the change.","verification_method":"Compare authoritative state, resolver cache state, and application connection targets across the migration timeline.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS caching semantics and operational migration practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B81-R002"],"supports":["D18-B91-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B91-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"DNS","subtopic":"service discovery","concept":"DNS-based service discovery can expose stale endpoints during service replacement","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a DNS-based service-discovery client connect to an endpoint that has already been removed?","answer":"Clients or recursive resolvers may retain previously resolved addresses according to caching semantics. Endpoint removal at the authoritative source therefore does not guarantee that every client immediately stops using the old address.","failure_mode":"A client continues attempting connections to a retired service instance.","root_cause":"Client-side or resolver-side cached address state outlives the service endpoint.","solution":"Design service replacement around explicit endpoint lifecycle, bounded cache behavior, connection failure handling, and graceful draining rather than relying solely on immediate DNS visibility.","testing_guidance":"Retire an endpoint while clients hold cached addresses and verify recovery to currently valid endpoints.","verification_method":"Verify that stale-endpoint failures are detected and that service discovery eventually converges to the intended endpoint set.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["DNS service-discovery patterns and distributed endpoint lifecycle principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B84-R003"],"supports":["D18-B91-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B91-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Service discovery","subtopic":"endpoint freshness","concept":"Service discovery requires explicit handling of stale endpoints","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should a network-aware service discovery system know about an endpoint beyond its address?","answer":"Useful endpoint state can include identity, protocol capability, health or readiness state, expiration information, discovery source, observed failures, and applicable routing metadata. Treating an address alone as complete service identity makes endpoint lifecycle and failure diagnosis difficult.","constraints":["Health information may become stale.","Discovery metadata must have explicit authority and freshness semantics.","Endpoint identity should not be inferred solely from a mutable address."],"testing_guidance":"Simulate endpoint replacement, stale health information, and address reuse and verify that the client distinguishes endpoint identity from address data.","verification_method":"Verify endpoint selection using explicit identity and freshness rules.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Service-discovery architecture and distributed-systems endpoint-management principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B81-R005"],"supports":["D18-B91-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B91-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Service discovery","subtopic":"failure classification","concept":"Discovery failure and destination failure are different stages","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"Why should service-discovery failure be recorded separately from connection failure?","answer":"A client can fail to obtain an endpoint before attempting a network connection, or it can obtain a valid endpoint and then fail during connection establishment. Combining these states obscures whether the failure belongs to discovery, routing, connection establishment, or the destination service.","solution":"Represent discovery, endpoint selection, connection establishment, protocol negotiation, request execution, and response processing as separate observable stages.","testing_guidance":"Inject discovery unavailability independently from destination connection failure.","verification_method":"Confirm that each scenario produces a stage-specific failure classification.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Service-discovery and staged network diagnostics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B85-R005"],"supports":["D18-B100-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B92-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"socket option portability","concept":"Socket option semantics can vary across operating systems","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why should portable networking code avoid assuming identical socket-option behavior across operating systems?","answer":"Socket APIs expose common concepts but individual options, defaults, error codes, lifecycle behavior, and platform-specific semantics can differ. Portable implementations must therefore isolate platform-specific behavior and verify assumptions against the target operating systems.","constraints":["POSIX-like APIs do not imply identical runtime behavior everywhere.","Platform-specific options should not be treated as universal.","Tests must run against supported target environments."],"testing_guidance":"Build a platform compatibility matrix for every nontrivial socket option used by the application.","verification_method":"Verify behavior on each supported operating-system/runtime combination or document unsupported combinations explicitly.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Operating-system socket API documentation and portability practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B87-R002"],"supports":["D18-B92-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B92-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"address reuse","concept":"Address-reuse options do not have one universal semantic contract","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should SO_REUSEADDR or equivalent options not be copied blindly between networking implementations?","answer":"Address-reuse options have platform-specific semantics and interact with connection state, binding rules, wildcard addresses, and sometimes other socket options. Code that assumes one operating system's behavior can fail or create unintended binding behavior elsewhere.","failure_mode":"A server either cannot restart as expected or permits an unintended binding arrangement after a configuration change.","root_cause":"Platform-specific address-reuse semantics were treated as portable guarantees.","solution":"Document the intended binding behavior and validate the exact socket options on each supported platform.","testing_guidance":"Test restart, simultaneous bind, wildcard bind, and conflicting-bind scenarios on supported platforms.","verification_method":"Verify actual bind results and error conditions against the platform-specific contract.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Operating-system socket option documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B92-R001"],"supports":["D18-B92-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B92-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"resource ownership","concept":"Socket ownership should be explicit across asynchronous code","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should asynchronous networking code define explicit ownership of every socket and connection object?","answer":"Asynchronous operations can outlive the initiating function and can be cancelled or fail independently. Without explicit ownership, multiple components may close the same resource, retain it indefinitely, or attempt operations after its lifecycle has ended.","failure_mode":"Intermittent connection-closed errors, resource leaks, or use-after-close behavior occur during cancellation and shutdown.","root_cause":"Resource lifetime is implicit rather than governed by a single ownership contract.","solution":"Define which component creates, owns, transfers, and closes each network resource and make cancellation-safe cleanup part of that contract.","testing_guidance":"Exercise concurrent completion, cancellation, timeout, and shutdown paths while tracking ownership transitions.","verification_method":"Verify that every created resource reaches exactly one terminal lifecycle state.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Asynchronous resource-lifetime engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B86-R004"],"supports":["D18-B92-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B92-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"backpressure","concept":"Socket-level backpressure must propagate into application-level flow control","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What happens when an application continuously produces data faster than a socket can transmit it?","answer":"Queued outbound data can grow until buffers or application memory limits are reached. Correct systems propagate backpressure to producers, bound queues, or apply controlled shedding according to the application's delivery contract.","constraints":["Unbounded buffering is unsafe for sustained producer-consumer mismatch.","Shedding is acceptable only when the application semantics permit data loss."],"testing_guidance":"Create a controlled slow receiver and measure queue growth under a faster producer.","verification_method":"Verify that queue size remains bounded and that producer behavior changes according to the configured backpressure policy.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Network backpressure and asynchronous stream-processing principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D45-R002"],"supports":["D18-B92-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B92-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Sockets","subtopic":"shutdown sequencing","concept":"Graceful network shutdown is a protocol lifecycle rather than an immediate close operation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should a graceful network shutdown consider?","answer":"A graceful shutdown should stop accepting new work, prevent new dependent operations, allow eligible in-flight operations to finish within bounded deadlines, close or cancel remaining resources, and record final outcomes. The exact sequence depends on the protocol and application contract.","constraints":["Shutdown must have a bounded completion deadline.","Long-lived connections require explicit policy.","Forced termination remains necessary when graceful completion exceeds the allowed deadline."],"testing_guidance":"Trigger shutdown under idle, active, blocked, slow, and failing connection conditions.","verification_method":"Verify that no new work is accepted after the shutdown barrier and that all owned network resources eventually reach terminal states.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Graceful-shutdown and network resource lifecycle patterns"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B87-R005"],"supports":["D18-B100-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B93-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"health-check hysteresis","concept":"Health-state transitions should avoid oscillation caused by isolated failures","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a load balancer require multiple observations before marking an endpoint healthy or unhealthy?","answer":"A single failed or successful probe may be caused by transient network or service conditions. Consecutive-success and consecutive-failure thresholds can reduce rapid state oscillation, provided they are chosen according to the service's recovery and failure-detection requirements.","tradeoffs":{"benefit":"Reduces sensitivity to isolated probe failures.","cost":"Delays recognition of real failure or recovery."},"testing_guidance":"Inject intermittent probe failures and verify the configured state-transition policy.","verification_method":"Compare observed health-state transitions with the documented threshold and timing rules.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Load-balancer health-management patterns"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B84-R002"],"supports":["D18-B93-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B93-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"capacity-aware routing","concept":"Health alone does not describe endpoint capacity","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why can routing only on binary health produce overload even when every endpoint is healthy?","answer":"An endpoint can be healthy while approaching CPU, memory, connection, queue, or application-specific capacity limits. Routing decisions that consider only binary health can continue assigning work to an increasingly constrained endpoint.","constraints":["Capacity signals can be noisy or stale.","Capacity-aware routing must not create unstable feedback loops."],"testing_guidance":"Increase controlled load while observing endpoint capacity and routing decisions.","verification_method":"Verify that routing policy reacts only to defined, authoritative capacity signals and remains stable under changing load.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Load-balancing and capacity-management engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B46-R002"],"supports":["D18-B93-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B93-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"retry amplification","concept":"Retries behind load balancers can amplify traffic toward already degraded endpoints","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can retries make a partially failing load-balanced service less stable?","answer":"A failed request may generate additional attempts, increasing total traffic while the service is already experiencing degraded capacity. If retries are concentrated on the same healthy-looking but overloaded endpoints, load can increase further.","root_cause":"Retry behavior is designed independently from load-balancer health and capacity behavior.","solution":"Coordinate retry budgets, endpoint selection, deadlines, and health/capacity signals so recovery attempts remain bounded.","testing_guidance":"Inject partial endpoint failure and measure original requests, retries, endpoint distribution, and queue depth.","verification_method":"Verify that total retry-generated load remains within the defined retry budget.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Distributed retry and load-balancing failure patterns"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B49-R001"],"supports":["D18-B93-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B93-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"connection versus request distribution","concept":"Connection distribution and request distribution are not always equivalent","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"Why can a load balancer distribute connections evenly while application requests remain unevenly distributed?","answer":"Persistent protocols and multiplexed connections can carry different numbers of requests or different amounts of work. Equal connection counts therefore do not necessarily imply equal request or workload distribution.","performance_implications":"Capacity analysis should use the workload unit that actually consumes resources, such as requests, streams, bytes, or processing time.","testing_guidance":"Measure both connection distribution and request/work distribution under persistent and multiplexed traffic.","verification_method":"Compare routing distribution at the connection level with application workload distribution.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Persistent-connection and load-balancing architecture principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B76-R001"],"supports":["D18-B93-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B93-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Load balancing","subtopic":"drain verification","concept":"A successful drain requires verification of both routing and connection state","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a load-balancer drain operation be verified?","answer":"Verification should confirm that new traffic is no longer assigned to the draining endpoint, eligible existing work is handled according to policy, active connections reach terminal states, and the endpoint is removed from the serving set only after the intended lifecycle conditions are satisfied.","verification_method":"Correlate load-balancer routing decisions, active connection state, request completion outcomes, and final endpoint membership.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Load-balancer lifecycle verification practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B84-R003"],"supports":["D18-B100-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B94-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"HTTP","subtopic":"connection reuse","concept":"Persistent HTTP connections require correct request and response lifecycle management","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"What must an HTTP client consider before reusing a connection?","answer":"The client must know whether the protocol permits reuse, whether the previous response was completely consumed or otherwise safely terminated, whether the connection remains valid, and whether protocol-specific state permits another request. Reuse must never occur across an ambiguous or incomplete message boundary.","failure_mode":"A client sends a new request over a connection whose previous response was not safely completed.","root_cause":"Connection reuse is based only on socket-open state rather than HTTP lifecycle state.","solution":"Tie connection reuse to protocol framing, response consumption, connection state, and cancellation-safe cleanup.","testing_guidance":"Test complete responses, truncated responses, cancellation, server closure, and malformed framing before attempting reuse.","verification_method":"Verify that only protocol-safe connections return to the pool.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP connection-management specifications"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B86-R005"],"supports":["D18-B94-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B94-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"HTTP","subtopic":"content-length and framing","concept":"HTTP message framing must be interpreted according to protocol rules","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should application code avoid independently guessing the end of an HTTP message?","answer":"HTTP defines message framing rules involving request or response semantics and applicable framing metadata. Application code that independently guesses message boundaries can disagree with the HTTP implementation and create truncation, hanging, or request-desynchronization behavior.","failure_mode":"Application waits indefinitely, truncates data, or interprets bytes belonging to a subsequent message as part of the current message.","root_cause":"Application-level framing assumptions conflict with the HTTP parser's framing rules.","solution":"Use a standards-compliant HTTP implementation and expose complete message boundaries to application logic.","testing_guidance":"Test empty bodies, fixed-length bodies, streamed bodies, malformed framing, and connection closure conditions.","verification_method":"Compare parser-produced message boundaries with specification-derived expectations.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP message-framing standards"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B90-R001"],"supports":["D18-B94-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B94-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"HTTP","subtopic":"header normalization","concept":"HTTP header names are case-insensitive but header values have field-specific semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should applications avoid applying one universal normalization rule to all HTTP headers?","answer":"HTTP field names have case-insensitive comparison semantics, but individual field values can have different grammar, ordering, combination, whitespace, and structured-data rules. Treating every header value as an arbitrary case-insensitive string can change protocol meaning.","failure_mode":"Application changes a header value during normalization and unintentionally alters its semantics.","root_cause":"Field-specific syntax is replaced by a generic normalization algorithm.","solution":"Use a standards-aware HTTP library and apply normalization according to each field's defined semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test headers containing tokens, quoted values, structured fields, and values where case has semantic meaning.","verification_method":"Verify normalized output against the specific field's protocol grammar.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP field-name and field-value specifications"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B85-R002"],"supports":["D18-B94-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B94-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"HTTP","subtopic":"redirect policy","concept":"HTTP redirects create a new destination decision and must not automatically inherit every security assumption","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should an HTTP client validate redirects instead of blindly following them?","answer":"A redirect can change the destination authority, scheme, path, or network location. Credentials, authorization context, network policy, and SSRF protections must therefore be evaluated for the redirected target rather than copied blindly from the original request.","security_implications":["Prevent credentials from crossing unintended authorities.","Reapply destination and egress policy after every redirect.","Do not assume the final destination is equivalent to the original URL."],"testing_guidance":"Test redirects across authorities, schemes, ports, internal destinations, and blocked network ranges.","verification_method":"Verify that each redirect destination is independently validated before connection and credential forwarding.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP redirect semantics and secure URL-fetching practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B78-R003"],"supports":["D18-B94-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B94-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"HTTP","subtopic":"HTTP client implementation","concept":"Safe HTTP clients separate URL parsing, DNS resolution, connection policy, TLS validation, request policy, and response handling","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What architectural separation reduces networking security and reliability errors in an HTTP client?","answer":"A robust client separates destination parsing, policy validation, endpoint resolution, connection establishment, TLS identity validation, HTTP request construction, response parsing, redirect policy, resource cleanup, and observability. This prevents one layer from silently overriding assumptions belonging to another.","verification_method":"Review the client architecture for explicit boundaries and tests for each stage rather than one monolithic request function.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["HTTP client architecture and secure network-client engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B80-R001"],"supports":["D18-B100-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B95-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Networking implementation","subtopic":"Python","concept":"Python asynchronous networking requires explicit cancellation and resource cleanup","knowledge_type":"CODE_PATTERN","language":"Python","version_range":"3.11+","question":"What implementation pattern should Python async networking use for connection cleanup?","answer":"Asynchronous network resources should be owned by a clearly scoped lifecycle and cleaned up through structured resource management. Cancellation and exceptions must execute the same cleanup path as successful completion.","correct_example":"async def fetch(reader, writer):\n    try:\n        writer.write(b\"PING\")\n        await writer.drain()\n        return await reader.read(1024)\n    finally:\n        writer.close()\n        await writer.wait_closed()","explanation":"The finally block establishes a cleanup boundary for normal completion, exceptions, and cancellation reaching the cleanup path.","invalid_example":"async def fetch(reader, writer):\n    writer.write(b\"PING\")\n    await writer.drain()\n    return await reader.read(1024)","failure_mode":"Connection resources can remain open when the read or write operation fails.","testing_guidance":"Test successful completion, read failure, write failure, cancellation, and peer closure.","verification_method":"Execute under a controlled test harness and verify socket/resource counts return to the expected baseline.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":["Python asyncio documentation and structured resource-management practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B86-R004"],"supports":["D18-B95-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B95-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Networking implementation","subtopic":"JavaScript","concept":"JavaScript network code must distinguish promise cancellation or abort semantics from ordinary rejection","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","language":"JavaScript","version_range":"ES2022+","question":"Why should JavaScript network clients explicitly model abort state?","answer":"An aborted asynchronous operation may reject with an error that differs semantically from connection failure, timeout, or server failure. Application logic should preserve the distinction so cancellation does not trigger inappropriate retry or error escalation.","failure_mode":"User-initiated cancellation is treated as a transient network failure and automatically retried.","root_cause":"Abort semantics are collapsed into generic request failure.","solution":"Represent cancellation separately from retryable transport and application failures.","testing_guidance":"Abort requests during connection establishment, upload, response waiting, and streaming.","verification_method":"Verify that cancellation terminates the operation without entering retry logic unless explicitly required.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":["JavaScript asynchronous programming and Fetch/AbortController semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B48-R002"],"supports":["D18-B95-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B95-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Networking implementation","subtopic":"TypeScript","concept":"TypeScript can encode network result categories explicitly but static types do not prove runtime network behavior","knowledge_type":"CODE_PATTERN","language":"TypeScript","version_range":"5.x","question":"How can TypeScript make network error classification explicit?","answer":"A discriminated-union result model can represent success, cancellation, timeout, transport failure, protocol failure, and application failure as separate states. Runtime validation is still required because external network data cannot be trusted merely because TypeScript types exist.","correct_example":"type NetworkResult<T> =\n  | { kind: \"ok\"; value: T }\n  | { kind: \"timeout\"; detail: string }\n  | { kind: \"cancelled\" }\n  | { kind: \"transport_error\"; detail: string }\n  | { kind: \"protocol_error\"; detail: string };","explanation":"The discriminant forces callers to handle explicit outcome categories while retaining the distinction between static type design and runtime evidence.","security_implications":"Untrusted network payloads still require runtime validation before use.","testing_guidance":"Construct one test case for every result variant and verify exhaustive handling.","verification_method":"Use compiler checking for exhaustive discriminant handling and runtime tests for actual network mappings.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["TypeScript type-system documentation and network error-modeling practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B82-R002"],"supports":["D18-B95-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B95-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Networking implementation","subtopic":"Java","concept":"Java networking code should distinguish connection, read, interruption, and protocol failures","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","language":"Java","version_range":"17+","question":"Why should Java network exception handling avoid catching every exception as one generic retryable failure?","answer":"Different exceptions can represent connection failure, timeout, interruption, invalid protocol state, resource exhaustion, or programming errors. Catching all exceptions and retrying can conceal defects and amplify load.","failure_mode":"A programming or protocol error enters the same retry path as a transient network failure.","root_cause":"Exception taxonomy is discarded at the network boundary.","solution":"Map exceptions to explicit operational categories and retry only categories whose semantics justify retry.","testing_guidance":"Inject connection timeout, interruption, malformed response, and application exception scenarios.","verification_method":"Verify exception-to-outcome mapping and ensure non-retryable failures terminate without retry amplification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":["Java networking APIs and exception-handling principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B49-R002"],"supports":["D18-B95-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B95-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Networking implementation","subtopic":"C/C++","concept":"C and C++ socket code requires explicit handling of partial I/O and resource ownership","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","language":"C++","version_range":"C++17+","question":"What networking correctness obligations are especially important in C and C++?","answer":"Low-level socket APIs expose resource ownership, partial reads and writes, error codes, shutdown semantics, and lifetime management directly. Implementations must explicitly preserve unsent or unread data and release descriptors through every control path.","failure_mode":"A single send or receive call is treated as complete message transfer.","root_cause":"Transport-level byte operations are incorrectly treated as message-level operations.","solution":"Implement explicit byte offsets, framing state, error classification, and deterministic descriptor ownership.","testing_guidance":"Test partial I/O, interrupted operations, peer closure, malformed framing, and repeated connection lifecycle transitions.","verification_method":"Compare transmitted and received byte streams against expected framing while verifying descriptor cleanup.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":["POSIX socket APIs and C/C++ network programming practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B87-R004"],"supports":["D18-B95-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B96-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Networking implementation","subtopic":"Rust","concept":"Rust ownership can encode important network-resource lifetime constraints but cannot prove protocol correctness","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","language":"Rust","version_range":"1.70+","question":"What networking correctness benefits can Rust ownership provide?","answer":"Ownership and borrowing can make many resource-lifetime and aliasing errors harder to express, including certain classes of use-after-free and double-release bugs. They do not automatically prove correct framing, timeout policy, protocol semantics, peer behavior, or security configuration.","principle":"Memory-safety guarantees and network-protocol correctness are complementary properties.","testing_guidance":"Combine compiler checks with protocol-level tests, failure injection, timeout tests, and interoperability tests.","verification_method":"Separate compile-time ownership guarantees from runtime protocol and integration verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":["Rust ownership model and networking engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B92-R003"],"supports":["D18-B96-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B96-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Networking implementation","subtopic":"Go","concept":"Go network deadlines provide bounded I/O behavior but do not replace application-level cancellation contracts","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","language":"Go","version_range":"1.21+","question":"Why should Go network deadlines and context cancellation be modeled separately?","answer":"A socket deadline bounds a network operation according to the connection's I/O semantics, while a context represents broader application cancellation and lifecycle intent. They can interact but are not interchangeable concepts.","tradeoffs":{"deadline":"Bounds network I/O according to socket semantics.","context":"Carries application cancellation and request lifecycle semantics across components."},"testing_guidance":"Test context cancellation, read deadline expiry, write deadline expiry, and normal completion independently.","verification_method":"Verify that each mechanism produces the intended outcome without converting cancellation into an inappropriate retry.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","provenance":["Go net package and context package semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B48-R002"],"supports":["D18-B96-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B96-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Cross-language networking","subtopic":"error normalization","concept":"Cross-language networking systems should normalize semantics rather than raw exception names","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should a multi-language system represent network failures consistently?","answer":"A shared error model should represent semantic stages and properties such as cancellation, deadline exhaustion, DNS failure, connection failure, TLS failure, protocol failure, remote rejection, and resource exhaustion. Language-specific exception classes should map into that model rather than being copied across languages.","constraints":["Mapping must preserve meaningful source detail.","Unknown exceptions must not be silently classified as retryable.","The model should support protocol-specific extensions."],"verification_method":"Create a cross-language failure-mapping matrix and test each source-language failure against the canonical semantic category.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Cross-language distributed-system error-modeling principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B82-R002"],"supports":["D18-B96-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B96-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Cross-language networking","subtopic":"wire compatibility","concept":"Protocol interoperability requires wire-level compatibility rather than language-level similarity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a networking implementation be verified across different programming languages?","answer":"Interoperability should be verified using the protocol's actual wire representation, framing, encoding, ordering, error behavior, and lifecycle semantics. Similar APIs or data structures across languages do not establish wire compatibility.","testing_guidance":"Use language-independent test vectors and capture protocol exchanges between implementations.","verification_method":"Compare encoded messages, framing, negotiated capabilities, and decoded semantics against shared protocol test vectors.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Protocol interoperability and wire-compatibility testing principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B95-R003"],"supports":["D18-B96-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B96-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Cross-language networking","subtopic":"conformance matrix","concept":"A language implementation matrix should separate protocol support from runtime behavior","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a cross-language networking conformance matrix measure?","answer":"The matrix should cover protocol versions, framing, TLS validation, timeouts, cancellation, retries, connection reuse, malformed input handling, error classification, resource cleanup, and interoperability against a common reference implementation.","test_case":{"dimensions":["protocol_version","framing","TLS_validation","timeout","cancellation","retry","connection_reuse","malformed_input","error_mapping","cleanup","wire_interoperability"]},"expected_test_result":"All supported implementations satisfy the declared protocol contract and produce semantically equivalent outcomes for shared test vectors.","verification_method":"Run shared conformance tests and record unsupported features separately from failed implementations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","provenance":["Cross-language protocol conformance methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B96-R003","D18-B96-R004"],"supports":["D18-B100-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B97-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Networking testing","subtopic":"fault injection","concept":"Network resilience tests should inject failures at individual lifecycle stages","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should network failure testing inject faults at specific stages instead of only disabling the entire network?","answer":"Whole-network failure does not distinguish DNS, connection, TLS, protocol, response, pool, routing, or downstream failures. Stage-specific injection verifies that the system classifies and recovers from each failure according to its semantics.","test_case":{"stages":["DNS","route_selection","connect","TLS_handshake","request_write","response_wait","response_read","pool_acquisition","connection_reuse","remote_service"]},"expected_test_result":"The system identifies the first evidenced failing stage, applies only appropriate recovery, preserves deadlines, and records sufficient diagnostics.","verification_method":"Compare observed stage classification and recovery behavior with the injected fault location.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","provenance":["Network fault-injection and resilience-testing methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B80-R002"],"supports":["D18-B97-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B97-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Networking testing","subtopic":"property-based testing","concept":"Protocol parsers benefit from invariant-oriented testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What properties should network protocol parsers be tested against beyond individual examples?","answer":"Useful properties include bounded resource use, deterministic classification of malformed input, preservation of valid payload bytes, correct framing boundaries, rejection of ambiguous input where required, and absence of parser state corruption after malformed messages.","constraints":["Generated inputs must remain within a controlled test environment.","Property tests do not replace standards-derived examples and interoperability vectors."],"testing_guidance":"Generate structured valid and invalid messages while asserting parser invariants.","verification_method":"Run property-based tests and retain minimized failing cases as regression vectors.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Property-based testing and protocol-parser verification practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B90-R004"],"supports":["D18-B97-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B97-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Networking testing","subtopic":"regression corpus","concept":"Every discovered networking defect should become a deterministic regression case when reproducible","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a network defect become reusable knowledge?","answer":"Capture the triggering conditions, protocol inputs, topology or dependency assumptions, observed failure, expected behavior, corrected behavior, and verification method. Reduce the scenario to the smallest deterministic regression case that preserves the defect.","principle":"A verified networking defect should improve the future test corpus rather than disappear after one fix.","testing_guidance":"Store the minimal reproduction together with environment/version metadata and expected outcome.","verification_method":"Run the regression test against both the defective and corrected implementation when available and preserve the observed distinction without inventing execution results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","provenance":["Software regression-testing and incident-learning methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B97-R002"],"supports":["D18-B97-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B97-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Networking testing","subtopic":"deterministic reproduction","concept":"Timing-sensitive network bugs require controlled environmental variables","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"Why can a networking bug disappear when debugging is enabled?","answer":"Timing, concurrency, scheduling, connection reuse, cache state, and network conditions can change when instrumentation or debugging is introduced. Reproduction therefore requires controlling relevant variables and recording the conditions under which the failure occurs.","testing_guidance":"Record runtime version, protocol version, topology, endpoint state, concurrency, deadlines, cache state, and relevant packet or trace evidence.","verification_method":"Repeat the scenario under controlled conditions and determine which variables are necessary to reproduce the failure.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Concurrency-debugging and network-failure reproduction methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B77-R001"],"supports":["D18-B97-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B97-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Networking testing","subtopic":"failure-injection safety","concept":"Network fault injection must be isolated from uncontrolled production impact","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What controls are required for safe network fault injection?","answer":"Fault injection should use explicitly authorized targets, bounded scope, reversible configuration, controlled traffic, monitoring, termination conditions, and isolation from unrelated systems. The purpose is to validate resilience without creating uncontrolled operational damage.","constraints":["Testing must remain within authorized environments.","Failure duration and blast radius must be bounded.","Test artifacts must not contain unnecessary secrets."],"verification_method":"Verify target identity, authorization, rollback path, monitoring, and termination criteria before injection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","provenance":["Resilience-testing and controlled fault-injection practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B50-R005"],"supports":["D18-B97-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B98-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Network performance","subtopic":"latency decomposition","concept":"End-to-end latency should be decomposed before optimization","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What stages should be considered when decomposing a network request latency budget?","answer":"A useful decomposition can include queue or pool acquisition, DNS resolution, route or endpoint selection, connection establishment, TLS handshake, request transmission, server processing, response transmission, and client parsing. The exact stages depend on protocol and architecture.","performance_claim":"The decomposition is a diagnostic model, not a benchmark.","testing_guidance":"Instrument each stage with a consistent monotonic timing source and preserve correlation identifiers.","verification_method":"Verify that component timings explain the end-to-end measurement within instrumentation overhead and known concurrent behavior.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Distributed latency-analysis methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B77-R002"],"supports":["D18-B98-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B98-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Network performance","subtopic":"connection reuse measurement","concept":"Connection reuse should be measured using workload-specific outcomes rather than assumed to be faster","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How should connection reuse be evaluated without inventing a benchmark?","answer":"Measure the target workload under controlled conditions and compare connection establishment count, handshake count, latency distribution, resource consumption, errors, and throughput. A qualitative expectation of reduced handshake work is not a measured performance result.","performance_claim":"UNKNOWN until measured for the target workload and environment.","testing_guidance":"Run controlled reused-connection and newly-established-connection scenarios using identical workload and environment conditions.","verification_method":"Record actual measurements with environment metadata and statistical methodology before claiming an observed performance difference.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Performance-measurement methodology and connection-management principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B89-R004"],"supports":["D18-B98-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B98-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Network performance","subtopic":"tail latency","concept":"Average latency can conceal severe network tail behavior","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why should network performance analysis include latency percentiles rather than only an average?","answer":"Averages can hide a smaller population of requests experiencing severe delays from retries, queueing, retransmission, overloaded endpoints, or dependency failures. Tail measurements help expose these operationally significant cases.","constraints":["Percentiles require enough representative observations.","The selected percentile must match the application's service-level objective.","Measurements must identify the population and time window."],"testing_guidance":"Collect latency distributions with request outcome and dependency metadata rather than only aggregate averages.","verification_method":"Compare percentile measurements across controlled workload conditions and preserve sample-size and population metadata.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Performance engineering and latency-analysis literature"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B93-R003"],"supports":["D18-B98-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B98-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Network performance","subtopic":"resource saturation","concept":"Network bottlenecks can originate in client-side resources rather than the network path","knowledge_type":"DIAGNOSTIC_RULE","question":"Which local resources should be considered before concluding that an external network is slow?","answer":"Relevant resources include file descriptors, ephemeral ports, connection-pool capacity, CPU available for encryption or parsing, memory for buffers, event-loop capacity, thread or task scheduling, and local queue depth. Exhaustion of any can manifest as network latency or failure.","testing_guidance":"Correlate network timing with local resource metrics during the affected workload.","verification_method":"Identify whether local resource saturation precedes the network symptom before assigning root cause to the external path.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Network performance diagnostics and systems resource analysis"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B73-R001","D18-B86-R002"],"supports":["D18-B98-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B98-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Network performance","subtopic":"optimization verification","concept":"Networking optimizations require regression verification across correctness and resource dimensions","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be checked after optimizing a networking path?","answer":"Optimization verification should cover functional correctness, latency, throughput, resource consumption, failure behavior, timeout semantics, connection lifecycle, security controls, and compatibility. An improvement in one metric is not sufficient if it introduces regressions elsewhere.","verification_method":"Run a predefined correctness and performance regression matrix against the baseline and optimized implementation and record actual results without fabricating unexecuted measurements.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Performance-regression and systems-optimization methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B100-R003"],"supports":["D18-B100-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B99-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Networking security","subtopic":"credential handling","concept":"Network observability must prevent accidental credential disclosure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Which network artifacts should generally be treated as potentially sensitive?","answer":"Authorization headers, cookies, tokens, client certificates or private-key material, signed URLs, session identifiers, internal hostnames, request bodies, and certain query parameters may contain sensitive information. Logging policy should classify fields before collection rather than relying on later redaction.","security_implications":"Sensitive network data in logs can create a secondary exposure path even when the original network channel is secure.","testing_guidance":"Send requests containing representative secrets and inspect logs, traces, exceptions, metrics, and debugging output for accidental disclosure.","verification_method":"Verify that sensitive fields are excluded, redacted, hashed only where appropriate, or otherwise handled according to explicit policy.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure logging practice and network credential-handling principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B79-R005"],"supports":["D18-B99-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B99-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Networking security","subtopic":"TLS configuration regression","concept":"TLS security configuration must be regression-tested rather than assumed from defaults","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should TLS configuration be included in regression tests?","answer":"Runtime defaults, trust stores, supported protocol versions, certificate chains, cipher availability, hostname validation, and deployment configuration can change across environments or upgrades. Security properties should therefore be tested explicitly for the supported configuration.","testing_guidance":"Test certificate validation, hostname mismatch, unsupported protocol versions, trust-store changes, and intended successful handshakes.","verification_method":"Verify expected acceptance and rejection outcomes against the documented TLS security policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["TLS standards and secure TLS configuration practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B89-R002","D18-B89-R003"],"supports":["D18-B99-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B99-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Networking security","subtopic":"egress policy","concept":"Outbound network access should be governed by explicit destination policy when the application handles untrusted input","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should SSRF defenses combine URL validation with egress controls?","answer":"Application-level URL validation can contain parser and redirect risks but may miss network-level destination changes or resolution behavior. Egress controls provide an additional enforcement boundary that can restrict destinations even when application validation is bypassed or incomplete.","principle":"Defense in depth should enforce destination policy at multiple independent stages.","testing_guidance":"Test direct destinations, redirects, alternate address representations, DNS changes, and blocked network ranges within an authorized isolated environment.","verification_method":"Verify that prohibited destinations remain unreachable even when application-level validation is intentionally stressed.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["SSRF security research and network egress-control principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B78-R005","D18-B94-R004"],"supports":["D18-B99-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B99-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Networking security","subtopic":"parser differentials","concept":"Security-sensitive network parsing should avoid multiple inconsistent parsers for the same authority decision","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is parsing the same network identifier differently in different layers dangerous?","answer":"If one layer validates a hostname, URL, authority, address, or protocol field using different parsing rules from the layer that ultimately connects or authorizes the destination, the attacker may exploit the difference. Security decisions must therefore use canonicalized, consistently interpreted data.","failure_mode":"Validation accepts a value that the connection layer interprets as a different destination.","root_cause":"Security validation and network execution use different parsers or normalization rules.","solution":"Define a canonical representation and ensure validation, policy, connection, and authorization layers operate on compatible semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use adversarial but controlled parser-differential test vectors and compare every stage's interpretation.","verification_method":"Verify that all security-sensitive layers agree on the canonical destination identity.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure parser design and network security research"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B90-R001","D18-B94-R004"],"supports":["D18-B99-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B99-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Networking security","subtopic":"security verification matrix","concept":"Network security verification must cover transport, destination, credentials, parsing, and observability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What dimensions should a final network security verification matrix contain?","answer":"The matrix should cover TLS identity and trust, DNS integrity assumptions, destination validation, redirect handling, proxy trust, credential forwarding, HTTP parsing, malformed input, egress policy, secret-safe observability, resource exhaustion, and authorization boundaries.","verification_method":"Map every security control to at least one negative test, expected rejection or containment behavior, evidence source, and regression identifier.","completion_criteria":["Every security control has a defined verification method.","Negative cases are represented.","Sensitive-data handling is tested.","Parser and destination-policy boundaries are tested.","No security property is inferred solely from successful connectivity."],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Network security verification methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B99-R001","D18-B99-R002","D18-B99-R003","D18-B99-R004"],"supports":["D18-B100-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B100-R001","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Networking","subtopic":"end-to-end lifecycle","concept":"A network request should be modeled as a sequence of evidence-producing lifecycle stages","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What complete lifecycle should an autonomous software-engineering system reason about for a network request?","answer":"A practical lifecycle is intent construction, destination parsing, policy validation, service discovery or DNS, route selection, connection acquisition, transport establishment, TLS negotiation, protocol negotiation, request transmission, remote processing, response reception, parsing, resource release, outcome verification, and learning from the observed result.","principle":"A network request is not equivalent to a single API call; each stage can fail independently and produces different evidence.","verification_method":"Map implementation telemetry and failure records to lifecycle stages and ensure the first independently evidenced failure is preserved.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["D18 lifecycle model synthesized from networking protocol and systems-engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B80-R001","D18-B91-R005"],"supports":["D18-B100-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B100-R002","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Networking","subtopic":"failure reasoning","concept":"Network diagnosis should distinguish observed evidence from inferred root cause","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an autonomous engineering system avoid confusing a network symptom with its root cause?","answer":"The system should preserve raw observations, classify the directly evidenced failure stage, generate candidate causes, identify supporting and contradicting evidence, and mark the root cause as confirmed only when the available evidence satisfies the applicable verification rule.","principle":"Observation, classification, hypothesis, root cause, remediation, and verification are separate knowledge states.","constraints":["A timeout alone does not prove the remote service is slow.","A connection refusal does not prove the application is unhealthy.","A successful retry does not prove the original diagnosis was correct."],"verification_method":"Require explicit evidence links between observation, hypothesis, intervention, and verified outcome.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["D18 evidence-model and diagnostic reasoning methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B80-R002"],"supports":["D18-B100-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B100-R003","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Networking","subtopic":"implementation verification","concept":"Generated networking code must pass syntax, semantic, security, failure, and lifecycle validation before being considered verified","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What validation gate should networking code pass before the Autonomous Software Engineering Brain treats it as verified implementation knowledge?","answer":"The implementation should undergo structural validation, semantic review, protocol conformance checks, security review, failure-path testing, resource-lifecycle testing, relevant performance measurement when performance claims exist, and provenance review. Successful generation or parsing is not verification.","verification_method":"Require explicit status fields for generation, static validation, execution, testing, verification, and requirement satisfaction instead of collapsing them into one status.","completion_criteria":["No fabricated execution result.","No unsupported benchmark claim.","Resource ownership is tested.","Failure paths are represented.","Security-sensitive behavior is tested.","Protocol assumptions are version-aware.","Expected behavior is linked to tests and verification evidence."],"evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain quality gates and D18 implementation methodology"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B95-R001","D18-B97-R003","D18-B98-R005"],"supports":["D18-B100-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B100-R004","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Networking","subtopic":"knowledge-fabric integration","concept":"D18 records should connect networking concepts, implementations, failures, tests, and verification rather than existing as isolated facts","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should D18 integrate into the Autonomous Software Engineering Brain knowledge fabric?","answer":"Concept records should connect to implementation records, invalid implementations, failure modes, diagnostic evidence, security cases, performance cases, test cases, verification rules, version constraints, and related protocol concepts. Relationships should identify dependency and specialization rather than duplicate the same knowledge in multiple records.","required_relationships":["implements","fails_as","caused_by","corrected_by","tested_by","verified_by","security_implication","performance_implication","depends_on","specializes","related_to"],"verification_method":"Run relationship-integrity validation and identify orphaned high-value records that lack required semantic connections.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Knowledge-fabric architecture and D18 dataset relationship model"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B80-R005"],"supports":["D18-B100-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D18-B100-R005","dataset_id":"D18","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Networking","subtopic":"final completion gate","concept":"D18 completion is determined by coverage and validation quality rather than record count","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What final conditions must be satisfied before D18 Networking can be locked?","answer":"D18 should be locked only after the entire B1-B100 corpus is audited for scope coverage, schema consistency, stable identifiers, duplicate and near-duplicate records, contradictions, provenance, version awareness, implementation coverage, invalid implementation coverage where applicable, testing, verification, security, performance, language coverage, relationship integrity, and retrieval usefulness. B100 completion alone does not constitute runtime verification of the dataset.","completion_criteria":["B1-B100 coverage is reviewed as one corpus.","All stable record IDs are unique.","Schema inconsistencies are normalized only through an explicit dataset version update.","Known duplicates and semantic overlaps are resolved through deduplication or relationships.","Contradictory claims are explicitly represented and investigated.","Provenance is present for substantive technical claims.","Execution status is never fabricated.","Performance claims distinguish measured, estimated, inferred, and unknown states.","Implementation records identify language and version where applicable.","Tests and verification guidance are linked to relevant implementation and failure records.","Security-sensitive networking behavior is represented.","Important networking edge cases are covered.","Retrieval-oriented queries can reach conceptual, implementation, debugging, testing, security, and performance knowledge.","Any remaining gap is explicitly documented rather than hidden."],"final_status":"NOT_LOCKED","reason":"B91-B100 completes the planned final coverage batch, but the authoritative D18 lock still requires whole-corpus audit and normalization. In particular, earlier B71-B80 records contain schema inconsistencies such as 'version' versus 'dataset_version'; these must be corrected through a controlled new dataset version rather than silently mutating prior records.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","provenance":["D18 dataset specification","Autonomous Software Engineering Brain quality-first dataset governance"],"relationships":{"governs":["D18-B81-R001","D18-B82-R001","D18-B83-R001","D18-B84-R001","D18-B85-R001","D18-B86-R001","D18-B87-R001","D18-B88-R001","D18-B89-R001","D18-B90-R001","D18-B91-R001","D18-B92-R001","D18-B93-R001","D18-B94-R001","D18-B95-R001","D18-B96-R001","D18-B97-R001","D18-B98-R001","D18-B99-R001"],"completes":["D18-B100-R001","D18-B100-R002","D18-B100-R003","D18-B100-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B01-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"security foundations","concept":"Security properties","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What core security properties should software security analysis distinguish?","answer":"Confidentiality limits unauthorized disclosure; integrity protects against unauthorized or unintended modification; availability concerns authorized access to required functionality; authenticity concerns whether an entity or data source is genuine; authorization determines what an authenticated or identified principal is permitted to do; accountability supports attribution through appropriate evidence.","principle":"Security properties are related but non-equivalent. Satisfying one does not establish the others.","security_implications":"A design that encrypts data may provide confidentiality while still having authorization, integrity, or availability weaknesses.","verification_method":"Map each security requirement to an explicit control and at least one verification method.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Cybersecurity Framework","NIST security engineering guidance","ISO/IEC 27001 security principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B01-R002","D19-B01-R003"],"related_to":["D18-B99-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B01-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"security requirements","concept":"Security requirements must be explicit and testable","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security requirements be written as testable properties rather than vague objectives?","answer":"A requirement such as 'the system must be secure' cannot establish implementation or verification criteria. A useful security requirement identifies the protected asset, actor or threat, security property, required behavior, relevant conditions, and verification method.","example":"Requirement: An authenticated user may read only records belonging to tenants for which the authorization policy grants read access.","testing_guidance":"Construct authorized, unauthorized, cross-tenant, missing-identity, and expired-authorization test cases.","verification_method":"Verify that each requirement maps to implementation controls and executable or reviewable verification evidence.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Security requirements engineering practice","NIST security engineering guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B01-R001"],"supports":["D19-B01-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B01-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"assets","concept":"Asset identification","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should security analysis identify before selecting controls?","answer":"Identify assets, their security properties, owners or responsible authorities, trust boundaries, legitimate users, dependencies, sensitive data classes, and unacceptable outcomes. Controls should then be selected against the actual assets and threats rather than against abstract categories alone.","required_fields":["asset","owner","security_properties","sensitivity","dependencies","trust_boundaries","unauthorized_outcomes"],"testing_guidance":"Review whether every high-value asset has identified protection requirements and an accountable owner.","verification_method":"Perform an asset-to-control traceability review.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST risk-management and security-engineering practices"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B01-R005","D19-B02-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B01-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"security invariants","concept":"Security invariants","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is a security invariant?","answer":"A security invariant is a property that must remain true across all relevant system states and transitions. Examples include 'a tenant can never access another tenant's protected data' or 'plaintext credentials are never persisted in application logs'.","principle":"Security invariants provide stronger reasoning targets than isolated security checks.","verification_method":"Identify state transitions capable of violating each invariant and verify those transitions are controlled or rejected.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Formal security reasoning principles","Security engineering practice"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B04-R002","D19-B06-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B01-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"defense in depth","concept":"Independent security layers","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security-sensitive systems use multiple independent controls?","answer":"A single control can fail because of implementation defects, configuration drift, unexpected input, compromised dependencies, or incorrect assumptions. Independent layers can constrain the impact of a failure, although duplicated controls should not be mistaken for independent protection when they share the same failure mode.","tradeoff":"Additional controls increase complexity and operational cost, so each layer should have a defined security purpose.","verification_method":"Document the security property enforced by each layer and identify shared dependencies that could invalidate apparent independence.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST defense-in-depth guidance","Secure architecture principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B02-R005","D19-B07-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B02-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Threat Modeling","subtopic":"trust boundaries","concept":"Trust boundary identification","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is a trust boundary in threat modeling?","answer":"A trust boundary is a point where data, authority, identity, execution context, or assumptions about trust change. Examples include browser-to-server boundaries, service-to-service boundaries, tenant boundaries, process boundaries, privilege boundaries, and external dependency boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Enumerate every crossing and identify what data, authority, and validation assumptions cross it.","verification_method":"Review the architecture diagram and verify that each security-sensitive crossing has an explicit control or documented trusted assumption.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Microsoft threat modeling methodology","NIST security architecture guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B02-R002","D19-B02-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B02-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Threat Modeling","subtopic":"data-flow analysis","concept":"Security-relevant data flow","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should a security-focused data-flow model represent?","answer":"It should represent actors, processes, data stores, communication paths, trust boundaries, privileged operations, sensitive data, authentication and authorization decisions, external dependencies, and security-relevant transformations.","verification_method":"Check whether every sensitive data flow has an identified source, destination, trust boundary, protection requirement, and validation point.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Microsoft threat modeling methodology","Data-flow threat modeling practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B02-R001"],"supports":["D19-B02-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B02-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Threat Modeling","subtopic":"attacker modeling","concept":"Threat actor capability","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should threat modeling specify attacker capabilities instead of assuming an unlimited attacker?","answer":"Security controls should be evaluated against realistic access, knowledge, privileges, resources, persistence, and opportunities available to the relevant threat actor. Explicit capability assumptions make the analysis reproducible and reveal where stronger controls are needed.","constraints":["Assumptions must be documented.","Changing attacker capability can invalidate previous conclusions.","External exposure and insider access may require different models."],"verification_method":"Record attacker capabilities and verify that every security conclusion is scoped to those assumptions.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Threat modeling methodology","NIST risk assessment guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B02-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B02-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Threat Modeling","subtopic":"abuse cases","concept":"Security abuse cases","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is an abuse case in software security?","answer":"An abuse case describes how a system could be intentionally used in a way that violates security requirements or produces an unacceptable outcome. It complements ordinary functional use cases by representing adversarial behavior and security consequences.","example":"An ordinary case permits a user to export their authorized records; an abuse case examines whether the same export mechanism can be used to obtain records outside the user's authorization scope.","testing_guidance":"Convert high-impact abuse cases into explicit negative security tests.","verification_method":"Verify that each important abuse case has prevention, detection, or containment controls.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Threat modeling and misuse-case methodologies"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B02-R002"],"supports":["D19-B03-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B02-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Threat Modeling","subtopic":"risk treatment","concept":"Threat-to-control traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should threat-model results connect to engineering work?","answer":"Each significant threat should map to one or more controls, residual-risk assumptions, verification activities, and responsible components. An undocumented threat with no treatment decision should not disappear merely because implementation has started.","verification_method":"Maintain a threat → control → test → verification traceability chain.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST risk-management practice","Threat modeling methodology"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B10-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B03-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Authentication","subtopic":"identity proof","concept":"Authentication versus identification","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the difference between identification and authentication?","answer":"Identification claims which identity or principal is being represented. Authentication provides evidence supporting that claim. Authentication does not by itself establish what the principal is authorized to access.","principle":"Identity proof and authorization are separate security decisions.","verification_method":"Review authentication flows and confirm that authorization decisions are performed separately using trusted identity context.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Digital Identity Guidelines"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B03-R002","D19-B04-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B03-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Authentication","subtopic":"authentication factors","concept":"Authentication factor categories","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What major categories of authentication factors are commonly distinguished?","answer":"Common categories include something the claimant knows, something the claimant possesses, and something the claimant is. Strong authentication can combine independent factors, but the actual assurance depends on the implementation and threat model rather than on the factor label alone.","security_implications":"A nominal multi-factor design can lose its intended independence if both factors depend on the same compromised channel or secret.","verification_method":"Evaluate factor independence, enrollment, recovery, authentication, and revocation paths.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Digital Identity Guidelines"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B03-R001"],"supports":["D19-B03-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B03-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Authentication","subtopic":"session lifecycle","concept":"Session lifecycle security","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What security properties should a session lifecycle enforce?","answer":"Session creation, rotation where required, expiration, revocation, renewal, logout, concurrent-session policy, and protection against unauthorized session use should be explicitly defined. Session identifiers must be treated as credentials and protected accordingly.","security_implications":"A secure login flow can still be undermined by insecure session creation, storage, transport, renewal, or revocation.","testing_guidance":"Test login, logout, expiry, revocation, session rotation, concurrent sessions, and reuse of invalidated identifiers.","verification_method":"Verify each lifecycle transition against the documented session security policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Session Management guidance","NIST Digital Identity Guidelines"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B03-R004","D19-B03-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B03-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Authentication","subtopic":"authentication failure","concept":"Authentication failure handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should authentication failure handling avoid exposing unnecessary account-state information?","answer":"Detailed differences between invalid usernames, invalid credentials, disabled accounts, or other internal states can reveal information useful for account enumeration. User-facing errors should expose only information necessary for legitimate operation while internal telemetry retains appropriate diagnostic detail.","tradeoffs":"Overly generic messages can impair usability and support workflows; the appropriate balance depends on the threat model and recovery design.","testing_guidance":"Compare responses for unknown identities, invalid credentials, disabled identities, and rate-limited attempts.","verification_method":"Verify that externally observable behavior does not unnecessarily disclose protected account state.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Authentication guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B03-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B03-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Authentication","subtopic":"credential abuse resistance","concept":"Authentication abuse controls","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What controls can reduce automated authentication abuse?","answer":"Depending on the threat model, controls can include rate limiting, adaptive challenges, credential-stuffing detection, breached-credential screening, strong authentication, monitoring, and account recovery protections. Controls should avoid creating denial-of-service conditions for legitimate users.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated failures, distributed attempts, valid credentials from anomalous contexts, recovery abuse, and legitimate high-frequency usage.","verification_method":"Verify that abuse controls activate according to documented thresholds and that legitimate recovery paths remain functional.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Authentication guidance","NIST Digital Identity Guidelines"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B10-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B04-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Authorization","subtopic":"authorization models","concept":"Authentication does not grant authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must authorization be evaluated after authentication?","answer":"Authentication establishes or supports identity; authorization determines whether that identity or principal may perform a specific operation on a specific resource under current policy. A valid identity therefore does not imply unrestricted access.","correct_example":"if not policy.allows(principal, action, resource):\n    raise AuthorizationError()","invalid_example":"if authenticated:\n    return resource","failure_mode":"Any authenticated principal can access resources that should require additional authorization.","root_cause":"Authentication state is incorrectly used as an authorization decision.","solution":"Evaluate explicit authorization policy for the principal, requested action, resource, and relevant context.","testing_guidance":"Test authenticated-but-unauthorized access and authorized access across resource boundaries.","verification_method":"Verify authorization independently for every protected operation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","NIST access-control guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B04-R002","D19-B04-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B04-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Authorization","subtopic":"object-level authorization","concept":"Object-level authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must authorization be evaluated against the specific object being accessed?","answer":"A principal may be authorized to use a resource type or endpoint while still lacking access to a particular object. Authorization must therefore consider the target resource identity and applicable ownership or policy constraints.","invalid_example":"GET /records/{record_id} -> return record whenever the caller is authenticated","corrected_pattern":"GET /records/{record_id} -> resolve the record and evaluate caller permission for that specific record before returning it","testing_guidance":"Attempt access to an owned object, another user's object, an inaccessible tenant's object, and a nonexistent object.","verification_method":"Verify that every object access passes an object-specific authorization decision before protected data is returned.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Broken Access Control guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B04-R001"],"supports":["D19-B04-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B04-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Authorization","subtopic":"deny by default","concept":"Default-deny authorization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is default-deny preferable to default-allow for protected operations?","answer":"Default-deny ensures that a new or unrecognized operation is not automatically granted access merely because an explicit deny rule was forgotten. Access is granted only when a policy evaluation establishes that the principal has the required permission.","tradeoffs":"Default-deny requires complete policy administration and can create legitimate access failures if policies are incomplete.","verification_method":"Test unknown actions, missing policies, malformed identities, and newly introduced resources.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST access-control principles","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B04-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B04-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Authorization","subtopic":"tenant isolation","concept":"Tenant isolation invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What security invariant should multi-tenant software enforce?","answer":"A principal associated with one tenant must not obtain protected resources belonging to another tenant unless an explicitly authorized cross-tenant policy exists.","test_case":{"authorized":"principal accesses resource belonging to principal's tenant","unauthorized":"principal attempts to access resource belonging to another tenant","expected":"unauthorized access is rejected"},"verification_method":"Test tenant boundaries at every protected resource path, including direct object lookup, search, export, background jobs, caches, and administrative operations.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Multi-tenant authorization engineering principles","OWASP access-control guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B01-R004"],"supports":["D19-B04-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B04-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Authorization","subtopic":"privilege boundaries","concept":"Least privilege","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does least privilege require in software systems?","answer":"Each principal, process, service, or component should receive only the authority required for its legitimate responsibilities, for only as long as that authority is required, where practical.","security_implications":"Excess privilege increases the impact of credential compromise, implementation defects, dependency compromise, and operator mistakes.","verification_method":"Review permissions against actual required operations and test that unnecessary privileged actions are rejected.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST access-control guidance","Principle of least privilege"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B05-R005","D19-B07-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B05-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"secret classification","concept":"Secrets versus ordinary configuration","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What makes a value a security-sensitive secret?","answer":"A secret is information whose disclosure or unauthorized use could enable impersonation, unauthorized access, decryption, signing, privilege escalation, or another security-impacting operation. Examples include passwords, API tokens, private keys, session credentials, and signing secrets.","principle":"Secret handling depends on impact, not merely on whether a value is stored in a configuration file.","verification_method":"Classify configuration values by the consequence of disclosure or modification.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST key-management guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B05-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B05-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"secret storage","concept":"Secrets should not be embedded in source code","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is embedding secrets directly in source code unsafe?","answer":"Source repositories can be copied, logged, cached, included in artifacts, exposed through build systems, or retained in historical revisions. Removing the secret from the current source does not necessarily remove it from repository history or deployed artifacts.","invalid_example":"API_KEY = \"hard-coded-secret-value\"","corrected_pattern":"Retrieve the secret from an approved secret-management mechanism at runtime and restrict access to the smallest required scope.","security_implications":"A leaked credential must be considered compromised and rotated or revoked according to its security policy.","testing_guidance":"Use automated secret scanning and repository-history review where appropriate.","verification_method":"Verify that sensitive credentials are absent from source, build artifacts, logs, and test fixtures unless explicitly authorized.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B05-R001"],"supports":["D19-B05-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B05-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"secret lifecycle","concept":"Secret lifecycle management","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What lifecycle states should a managed secret have?","answer":"A secret lifecycle should account for creation or issuance, controlled distribution, use, rotation where applicable, revocation, expiration where applicable, replacement, archival requirements, and secure disposal. The lifecycle should also define who or what is authorized to perform each operation.","verification_method":"Trace a representative secret through each applicable lifecycle state and verify access and audit controls.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST key-management guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B05-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B05-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"rotation","concept":"Secret rotation is not equivalent to security recovery","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can rotating a credential be insufficient after compromise?","answer":"Rotation prevents future use of the replaced credential when successful, but previously issued copies may remain in logs, artifacts, caches, backups, devices, or other systems. Recovery must identify and revoke affected credentials and address the exposure path in addition to issuing replacements.","testing_guidance":"Simulate a controlled credential exposure and verify revocation, replacement, dependency update, and residual-exposure handling.","verification_method":"Confirm that the old credential is rejected wherever the security policy requires revocation.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B05-R003"],"supports":["D19-B05-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B05-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"logging","concept":"Secret-safe observability","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should secrets be excluded from logs even when logs are access-controlled?","answer":"Logs are commonly copied to monitoring systems, archives, debugging environments, support tools, and third-party systems. Every additional copy increases the exposure surface and complicates revocation.","testing_guidance":"Exercise authentication, authorization, error, retry, and debugging paths using controlled secret values and inspect all emitted telemetry.","verification_method":"Verify that secret values are absent or transformed according to an explicit safe-logging policy.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Logging guidance","OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D18-B99-R001"],"supports":["D19-B10-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B06-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Input Validation","subtopic":"validation boundaries","concept":"Server-side validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must security-sensitive input validation occur on the server?","answer":"Client-side validation improves usability but executes in an environment controlled by the user and therefore cannot be treated as a security boundary. Security-sensitive constraints must be enforced by the server or trusted execution boundary that owns the protected operation.","invalid_example":"Browser validates that amount > 0, and server trusts the submitted amount without validation.","corrected_pattern":"Server independently validates type, range, format, authorization context, and business constraints before performing the protected operation.","verification_method":"Send malformed and policy-violating inputs directly to the server without the normal client validation layer.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B06-R002","D19-B06-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B06-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Input Validation","subtopic":"allowlisting","concept":"Allowlist validation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is allowlist validation preferable to denylist filtering?","answer":"When the valid input domain can be defined precisely, accepting only values that satisfy the required grammar, type, range, or character set is generally stronger than attempting to enumerate every malicious value. Denylists can still have limited value as defense in depth but should not be the sole security boundary.","testing_guidance":"Test valid values at boundaries and malformed values that differ by encoding, length, type, or unexpected syntax.","verification_method":"Verify that accepted input belongs to the explicitly defined valid domain.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R001"],"supports":["D19-B06-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B06-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Input Validation","subtopic":"canonicalization","concept":"Validate after canonical interpretation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can validating one representation and using another representation create a security flaw?","answer":"Encoding, normalization, path representation, Unicode processing, URL parsing, and other transformations can change the security meaning of data. If a security decision is made before canonical interpretation while execution uses a different representation, validation may apply to the wrong value.","failure_mode":"A value passes validation in one representation but is interpreted differently by the component that executes it.","root_cause":"Security validation and execution use inconsistent representations.","solution":"Define the canonical representation required for the security decision and ensure validation occurs against the representation actually used by the protected operation.","verification_method":"Test equivalent encoded and normalized representations and verify consistent security decisions.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation guidance","Secure canonicalization principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B06-R004","D19-B08-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B06-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Input Validation","subtopic":"size limits","concept":"Resource-bounded validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should validation include size and complexity limits?","answer":"Even syntactically valid input can consume excessive CPU, memory, storage, parsing time, or downstream resources. Security validation should therefore include appropriate bounds for size, nesting, quantity, and processing complexity where those dimensions affect availability.","testing_guidance":"Test boundary values and oversized or deeply nested inputs in an isolated environment.","verification_method":"Verify that resource-consuming inputs are rejected or bounded before they can exhaust protected resources.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation guidance","Secure resource-management principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R002"],"supports":["D19-B07-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B06-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Input Validation","subtopic":"type safety","concept":"Type and semantic validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is syntactic validation alone insufficient for security-sensitive input?","answer":"Input can be syntactically valid while violating authorization, business, range, state, or security constraints. Security validation should therefore distinguish representation validity from semantic validity and authorization validity.","example":"A syntactically valid account identifier can still refer to an account the current principal is not authorized to access.","verification_method":"Test syntactic validity separately from semantic constraints and authorization checks.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure input-validation practice","OWASP authorization guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B04-R002","D19-B06-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B07-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"SQL injection","concept":"Parameterized database queries","knowledge_type":"CODE_PATTERN","language":"Python","version_range":"3.11+","question":"How should application code prevent SQL injection when using a relational database?","answer":"Use parameterized queries or an equivalent safe database abstraction that separates SQL structure from untrusted values.","correct_example":"cursor.execute(\"SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE email = ?\", (email,))","invalid_example":"cursor.execute(\"SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE email = '\" + email + \"'\")","failure_mode":"Untrusted input becomes part of SQL syntax rather than remaining a bound value.","root_cause":"Application constructs executable SQL by concatenating untrusted input.","solution":"Use parameter binding and keep query structure separate from data.","testing_guidance":"Test ordinary input, quotes, delimiters, malformed values, and unexpected lengths using a controlled test database.","verification_method":"Review database calls for parameter binding and execute negative regression cases.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet","Python DB-API parameter binding documentation"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B07-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B07-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"command injection","concept":"Argument separation for subprocess execution","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","language":"Python","question":"Why is passing untrusted input through a shell more dangerous than passing an argument list to a subprocess API?","answer":"A shell interprets metacharacters and shell syntax, while an argument-list API can pass values as individual arguments without asking a shell to parse them as commands. The safest design is still to avoid invoking external commands when a direct library API provides the required functionality.","invalid_example":"subprocess.run(\"tool \" + user_value, shell=True)","corrected_pattern":"subprocess.run([\"tool\", user_value], check=True)","constraints":["The target executable must itself be trusted.","Argument values may still require application-level validation.","Avoid shell invocation when unnecessary."],"testing_guidance":"Test values containing whitespace, separators, quotes, and shell metacharacters in an isolated environment.","verification_method":"Verify that untrusted data remains an argument and cannot alter command structure.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet","Python subprocess documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R002"],"supports":["D19-B07-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B07-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"template injection","concept":"Context-specific output encoding","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is there no universal escaping function that safely handles every output context?","answer":"HTML, JavaScript, CSS, URL, SQL, shell, and other interpreters have different grammars and escaping rules. A transformation safe for one context may be meaningless or unsafe in another.","principle":"Use context-specific safe APIs, parameterization, or encoding appropriate to the interpreter receiving the data.","verification_method":"Identify the target interpreter and verify that the selected defense matches its syntax and execution model.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet","OWASP Injection Prevention guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B07-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B07-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"path traversal","concept":"Safe filesystem path handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should applications avoid constructing filesystem paths directly from untrusted path fragments?","answer":"Untrusted fragments can contain traversal components, absolute paths, alternate representations, symbolic links, or platform-specific path semantics. Secure designs constrain access to an intended root and verify the resolved target according to the application's authorization policy.","invalid_example":"open(base_directory + \"/\" + user_path)","corrected_pattern":"Resolve the candidate path using a controlled filesystem API and verify that the resolved target remains within the authorized root before access.","testing_guidance":"Test normal child paths, traversal attempts, absolute-path forms, encoded separators, symlink cases where relevant, and nonexistent paths.","verification_method":"Verify the final resolved filesystem target against the authorized root before performing the operation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Path Traversal guidance","Secure filesystem API practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R003"],"supports":["D19-B07-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B07-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"injection defense architecture","concept":"Interpreter boundary control","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What common architectural principle applies across SQL, shell, template, filesystem, and similar injection classes?","answer":"Untrusted data should remain data when crossing an interpreter boundary. Secure APIs should represent structure separately from values whenever possible, and validation should enforce the intended semantic domain before interpretation.","principle":"Do not construct executable language syntax by concatenating untrusted data.","verification_method":"Inventory interpreter boundaries and verify that structured APIs or context-appropriate defenses are used at each one.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP injection-prevention guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B07-R001","D19-B07-R002","D19-B07-R003","D19-B07-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B08-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Cryptography","subtopic":"cryptographic primitives","concept":"Cryptographic primitive selection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should software select established cryptographic constructions rather than implementing primitives itself?","answer":"Cryptographic security depends on subtle algorithmic, implementation, randomness, key-management, protocol, and side-channel properties. Established libraries provide reviewed implementations and safer interfaces, although their correct use still depends on appropriate configuration and lifecycle management.","security_implications":"A mathematically sound primitive can still be insecure when used with incorrect modes, keys, nonces, randomness, or protocol composition.","verification_method":"Verify the selected primitive, library, mode, parameters, key lifecycle, and threat-model assumptions against authoritative guidance.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST cryptographic standards","OWASP Cryptographic Storage guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B08-R002","D19-B08-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B08-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Cryptography","subtopic":"password storage","concept":"Password hashing versus encryption","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"Why should user passwords generally be stored using password-specific hashing rather than reversible encryption?","answer":"Password verification normally requires determining whether a supplied password matches the stored verifier; the original password does not need to be recoverable. Password-specific password-hashing schemes are designed to make large-scale guessing more expensive and should be configured according to current authoritative guidance and supported implementation capabilities.","security_implications":"Reversible storage creates a recovery path for plaintext-equivalent credentials if the encryption key is compromised.","verification_method":"Verify that password storage uses an approved password-hashing construction and that individual passwords receive appropriate salts and configured work factors.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet","NIST Digital Identity Guidelines"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B03-R002"],"supports":["D19-B08-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B08-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Cryptography","subtopic":"nonce and randomness","concept":"Cryptographically secure randomness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must security-sensitive random values use a cryptographically appropriate random source?","answer":"Security tokens, session identifiers, nonces, salts in applicable contexts, reset values, and cryptographic keys may depend on unpredictability. General-purpose pseudo-random facilities designed for simulation or convenience may not provide the required security properties.","testing_guidance":"Review the source of randomness for every security-sensitive value and verify that it meets the relevant cryptographic requirements.","verification_method":"Trace security-sensitive random values to the approved cryptographic random source and verify lifecycle handling.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Randomness Recommendations","OWASP Cryptographic Storage guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B08-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B08-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Cryptography","subtopic":"key management","concept":"Keys are lifecycle-managed security assets","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is key management as important as algorithm selection?","answer":"Cryptographic protection depends on key generation, storage, access control, use restrictions, rotation where applicable, backup or recovery policy, revocation, destruction, and separation of duties. Correct algorithms cannot compensate for exposed or improperly managed keys.","verification_method":"Create a key inventory and trace each key through generation, use, access, rotation, revocation, and destruction requirements.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Key Management Guidelines","OWASP Cryptographic Storage guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B05-R003"],"supports":["D19-B08-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B08-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Cryptography","subtopic":"integrity and authenticity","concept":"Encryption does not automatically provide application-level authenticity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should confidentiality and authenticity be analyzed separately?","answer":"Encryption protects confidentiality according to its construction and use, while authenticity and integrity require appropriate authenticated encryption, signatures, MACs, or other mechanisms depending on the protocol. Using encryption without the required integrity protection can leave data vulnerable to modification or ambiguity.","verification_method":"Identify the exact integrity and authenticity requirement and verify that the chosen construction provides it under the intended threat model.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST cryptographic standards","Authenticated-encryption principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B01-R001"],"supports":["D19-B08-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B09-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Serialization Security","subtopic":"untrusted data","concept":"Untrusted serialized data","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should applications treat serialized input as untrusted even when it originated from another internal service?","answer":"Internal systems can be compromised, misconfigured, upgraded incompatibly, or supplied with corrupted data. Deserialization can also invoke complex parsing or object-construction behavior. Trust should therefore be established by the security boundary and validated according to the data contract rather than assumed from origin labels.","testing_guidance":"Test malformed, unexpected, oversized, version-mismatched, and semantically unauthorized serialized input.","verification_method":"Verify schema, size, type, and authorization constraints before sensitive processing.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Deserialization guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B09-R002","D19-B09-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B09-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Serialization Security","subtopic":"unsafe object deserialization","concept":"Avoid unsafe object deserialization of untrusted input","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can general-purpose object deserialization be dangerous for untrusted input?","answer":"Some serialization mechanisms reconstruct objects with behavior, class resolution, references, or other semantics beyond passive data parsing. If attacker-controlled input can influence those mechanisms, deserialization can create serious security consequences depending on the runtime and library.","solution":"Prefer data-only serialization formats and explicit schema validation where possible. If object serialization is unavoidable, apply documented safe-type restrictions and trust-boundary controls.","testing_guidance":"Use framework-specific security tests for untrusted deserialization and verify that unexpected types or behaviors are rejected.","verification_method":"Review deserialization APIs and confirm that untrusted data cannot select arbitrary executable object behavior.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Deserialization guidance","Language/runtime serialization security guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B09-R001"],"supports":["D19-B09-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B09-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Serialization Security","subtopic":"schema validation","concept":"Schema validation is distinct from authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why does validating a serialized message schema not prove that the sender is authorized to request the operation?","answer":"Schema validation establishes that data conforms to an expected structural contract. It does not establish sender identity, permission, ownership, or business authorization. Those properties require separate security decisions.","example":"A message can contain a syntactically valid user_id while still being unauthorized for the caller to modify that user's data.","verification_method":"Test structurally valid messages with authorized and unauthorized principals.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure API design principles","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B04-R001","D19-B06-R005"],"supports":["D19-B09-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B09-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Serialization Security","subtopic":"parser resource exhaustion","concept":"Bounded parsing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should parsers enforce limits on serialized input?","answer":"A parser may consume memory or CPU according to message size, nesting depth, number of elements, reference relationships, or other structural complexity. Explicit limits reduce the risk that valid-looking but resource-intensive input exhausts the service.","testing_guidance":"Test maximum message size, nesting depth, element count, and parser-specific complexity boundaries.","verification_method":"Verify that inputs beyond defined limits are rejected before resource consumption becomes uncontrolled.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Deserialization guidance","Secure parser engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R004"],"supports":["D19-B09-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B09-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Serialization Security","subtopic":"schema evolution","concept":"Secure schema evolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should security-sensitive schema evolution consider?","answer":"Schema evolution should define compatibility, unknown-field handling, default behavior, version negotiation where needed, validation rules, authorization implications, and migration behavior. Security-sensitive fields should not silently acquire permissive defaults when older or newer producers interact with the system.","testing_guidance":"Test older producer/newer consumer, newer producer/older consumer, unknown fields, missing fields, and changed security-relevant defaults.","verification_method":"Verify that every compatibility path preserves required security invariants.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure API evolution principles","Schema validation engineering practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B09-R003"],"supports":["D19-B10-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B10-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Secure Error Handling","subtopic":"error disclosure","concept":"Safe external error responses","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should externally visible security-sensitive errors contain?","answer":"External errors should provide enough information for legitimate clients to understand the supported operation without exposing secrets, internal stack traces, credentials, security-sensitive configuration, or unnecessary implementation details. Internal diagnostic records can preserve additional information under appropriate access controls.","verification_method":"Trigger representative failures and inspect client responses separately from privileged internal diagnostics.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Error Handling guidance","Secure logging principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B10-R002","D19-B10-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B10-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Secure Error Handling","subtopic":"fail-safe behavior","concept":"Security-sensitive failure behavior","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does fail-safe behavior mean for a security control?","answer":"When a security decision cannot be reliably established, the system should not silently grant the protected operation. The exact fallback depends on availability requirements, but security-sensitive uncertainty must not become implicit authorization.","example":"If an authorization policy service is unavailable, the application should not treat the missing policy response as permission to access protected data.","testing_guidance":"Simulate unavailable policy dependencies and verify that protected operations follow the documented failure policy.","verification_method":"Verify that security-decision uncertainty produces an explicitly defined safe outcome.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Fail-safe security engineering principles","NIST access-control guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B04-R003"],"supports":["D19-B10-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B10-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Secure Error Handling","subtopic":"exception boundaries","concept":"Security-aware exception handling","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should applications avoid broad exception handlers that silently continue after security-sensitive failures?","answer":"A broad handler can convert an authorization, validation, cryptographic, or integrity failure into apparently successful behavior. This hides the security event and may cause protected operations to continue under invalid assumptions.","invalid_example":"try:\n    authorize(request)\nexcept Exception:\n    pass\nperform_protected_operation()","corrected_pattern":"Treat authorization failure as an explicit terminal outcome and record appropriate diagnostic evidence without exposing sensitive details.","failure_mode":"Security control failure is interpreted as success or ignored.","root_cause":"Exception handling does not preserve the security semantics of the failed operation.","verification_method":"Inject security-control failures and verify that protected operations cannot continue without an explicit valid security decision.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure exception-handling principles","OWASP authorization guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B10-R002"],"supports":["D19-B10-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B10-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Secure Error Handling","subtopic":"security observability","concept":"Security event observability","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What makes a security event useful for later investigation?","answer":"Useful security telemetry preserves event type, time, relevant principal or service identity, affected resource where appropriate, outcome, correlation information, and enough contextual evidence to reconstruct the security decision without unnecessarily storing sensitive secrets or protected payloads.","constraints":["Logging must respect privacy and data-minimization requirements.","Sensitive values should not be copied merely for diagnostic convenience.","Clock and correlation assumptions should be documented."],"verification_method":"Review representative security events and determine whether investigators can distinguish the event, actor, resource, outcome, and correlation context without accessing unnecessary secrets.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Logging guidance","NIST incident-response and audit guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B05-R005"],"supports":["D19-B10-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B10-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"foundation coverage gate","concept":"Security foundation verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must the initial D19 security foundation be able to verify before moving to deeper security topics?","answer":"The foundation must connect assets and security properties to threats, trust boundaries, authentication, authorization, secrets, input validation, injection defenses, cryptographic usage, serialization controls, and secure failure behavior. Each high-impact control should have an explicit negative test or verification method.","completion_criteria":["Security properties are explicitly modeled.","Threats map to controls.","Trust boundaries are identified.","Authentication and authorization are separated.","Object-level authorization is represented.","Secrets have lifecycle controls.","Security-sensitive input is validated server-side.","Interpreter boundaries have injection defenses.","Cryptographic primitives and key management are treated separately.","Untrusted serialization is bounded and safely parsed.","Security failures cannot silently become authorization success.","Sensitive observability is controlled.","All records have provenance and evidence classification.","No execution or benchmark is claimed without actual evidence."],"final_status":"NOT_LOCKED","reason":"B1-B10 establishes the D19 foundation. D19 remains open for later batches covering deeper cryptography, web/application security, dependency and supply-chain security, filesystem and process security, memory safety, secure networking, vulnerability classes, security testing, formal security properties, and advanced verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19 Security Engineering scope","Autonomous Software Engineering Brain quality-first dataset governance"],"relationships":{"governs":["D19-B01-R001","D19-B02-R001","D19-B03-R001","D19-B04-R001","D19-B05-R001","D19-B06-R001","D19-B07-R001","D19-B08-R001","D19-B09-R001","D19-B10-R001"],"supports":["D19-B11-R001"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B11-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Cross-Site Request Forgery","concept":"CSRF threat model","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"When is an application vulnerable to cross-site request forgery?","answer":"CSRF can occur when a browser automatically supplies authentication credentials with a state-changing request and the server lacks sufficient evidence that the request was intentionally initiated by the legitimate application context.","security_implications":"The attack does not require stealing the victim's credential; it abuses the browser's authenticated request capability.","testing_guidance":"For state-changing browser operations, test requests originating outside the expected application context and verify that the server rejects unauthorized cross-site requests.","verification_method":"Verify that state-changing operations require an appropriate CSRF defense when browser-managed authentication makes CSRF applicable.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Cross-Site Request Forgery Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B11-R002","D19-B11-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B11-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"CSRF defenses","concept":"CSRF token validation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can synchronizer-style CSRF tokens reduce CSRF risk?","answer":"The server associates a secret or unpredictable request token with the legitimate application context and requires the expected token on applicable state-changing requests. An attacker who can cause a cross-site request should not normally be able to obtain the required token from the protected application context.","constraints":["The token must be protected against disclosure.","Validation must occur server-side.","The token mechanism must match the application's session and deployment model."],"testing_guidance":"Submit valid requests with the correct token, missing tokens, invalid tokens, tokens from another session, and expired tokens where expiration applies.","verification_method":"Verify that protected state-changing requests cannot succeed without a valid CSRF proof.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Cross-Site Request Forgery Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B11-R001"],"supports":["D19-B11-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B11-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"SameSite cookies","concept":"Cookie SameSite policy","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How can SameSite cookie behavior contribute to CSRF defense?","answer":"The SameSite attribute can restrict when browsers send cookies in cross-site contexts, reducing some CSRF attack opportunities. It should be treated as part of a broader security design rather than assuming that cookie policy alone satisfies every CSRF requirement.","security_implications":"Browser behavior, application architecture, legitimate cross-site workflows, and authentication mechanisms determine whether a particular SameSite configuration is sufficient.","testing_guidance":"Test same-site and cross-site navigation/request scenarios supported by the application and verify cookie transmission behavior on the supported browser baseline.","verification_method":"Verify the deployed cookie attributes and confirm that their behavior matches the application's CSRF threat model.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Cross-Site Request Forgery Prevention Cheat Sheet","RFC 6265bis"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B11-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B11-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"CSRF scope","concept":"State-changing operations require security classification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should applications identify every state-changing operation when designing CSRF defenses?","answer":"Protecting only obvious HTML form endpoints is insufficient if state can also change through APIs, JSON requests, asynchronous actions, alternate HTTP methods, administrative interfaces, or indirect workflows. The security model should enumerate all operations capable of changing protected state.","testing_guidance":"Build an operation inventory and test every state-changing path, including alternate endpoints and methods.","verification_method":"Trace each state transition to its CSRF protection decision.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP CSRF Prevention guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B11-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B11-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"CSRF regression","concept":"CSRF regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a CSRF regression matrix cover?","answer":"A useful matrix covers protected and unprotected operation classes, valid and invalid tokens where applicable, same-site and cross-site request contexts, authenticated and unauthenticated states, session changes, alternate request methods, and legitimate application workflows.","test_case":{"inputs":["valid_authenticated_request","missing_csrf_proof","invalid_csrf_proof","cross_site_request","expired_session","new_session_after_token_rotation"],"expected_result":"Only requests satisfying the applicable security policy are permitted to change protected state."},"verification_method":"Run the matrix after authentication, cookie, routing, frontend, and authorization changes that could alter CSRF behavior.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP CSRF Prevention guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B11-R002","D19-B11-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B12-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Cross-Site Scripting","concept":"Context-specific XSS prevention","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must XSS defenses depend on the output context?","answer":"HTML text, HTML attributes, JavaScript, CSS, URLs, and other interpreters have different parsing rules. Data safe for one context may remain executable or ambiguous in another. Prevention should therefore use context-appropriate encoding, safe APIs, and output handling.","security_implications":"Generic string replacement or a single global escaping function cannot reliably protect every output context.","verification_method":"Identify every interpreter context receiving untrusted data and verify the defense appropriate to that context.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B07-R003"],"supports":["D19-B12-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B12-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Cross-Site Scripting","concept":"Output encoding versus input filtering","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is input filtering alone not a complete XSS defense?","answer":"The same input can be interpreted differently in different contexts, and filtering cannot reliably enumerate all dangerous syntax. Output handling must ensure that untrusted data is represented as data in the exact context where it is consumed.","tradeoff":"Input validation remains useful for enforcing application data constraints, but it should not be treated as the sole XSS control.","verification_method":"Review both input validation and every output sink receiving untrusted values.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R002","D19-B12-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B12-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Content Security Policy","concept":"Content Security Policy as defense in depth","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What role can Content Security Policy play in web security?","answer":"Content Security Policy can constrain which resource and script behaviors browsers are permitted to execute, reducing the impact of some classes of injection when correctly deployed. It does not replace safe output handling, input validation, authorization, or secure application design.","security_implications":"An incorrectly designed or overly permissive policy can provide limited protection while creating false confidence.","testing_guidance":"Deploy policy in an appropriate reporting or controlled mode first where operationally suitable, inspect violations, then verify enforcement behavior.","verification_method":"Verify the effective browser policy and test representative permitted and prohibited resource behaviors.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["W3C Content Security Policy specification","OWASP Content Security Policy guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B12-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B12-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"DOM security","concept":"Client-side trust boundaries","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can DOM-based injection remain possible even when server-side HTML output is safe?","answer":"Client-side code can read attacker-influenced values from URLs, storage, messages, or server responses and place them into dangerous DOM sinks. Server-side output encoding therefore does not automatically secure later client-side interpretation.","testing_guidance":"Inventory browser-side sources and sinks and test whether attacker-controlled values can reach executable or HTML-interpreting sinks.","verification_method":"Trace untrusted data through client-side transformations to final DOM sinks and verify safe APIs or context-appropriate encoding.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B12-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B12-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"XSS verification","concept":"XSS sink inventory","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an engineering team verify XSS defenses systematically?","answer":"Create an inventory of server-side and client-side sinks where untrusted data can become HTML, script, URL, CSS, or other executable/interpreted content. For each sink, identify its context, data source, sanitization or encoding strategy, and regression tests.","verification_method":"Require every security-sensitive sink to have an identified safe-handling strategy and negative test coverage.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP XSS Prevention guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B12-R001","D19-B12-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B13-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Clickjacking","concept":"Framing control","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can unauthorized framing of an application create security risk?","answer":"An attacker-controlled page may visually embed a legitimate application and attempt to trick a user into interacting with controls they did not intend to activate. Framing protections can reduce this attack surface.","security_implications":"The appropriate defense depends on application requirements for legitimate embedding and browser capabilities.","verification_method":"Attempt to embed protected application pages from unauthorized origins and verify the configured framing policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Clickjacking Defense Cheat Sheet","Content-Security-Policy specification"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B13-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B13-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"security headers","concept":"Security response headers as explicit policy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should security-related HTTP response headers be treated as explicit application policy?","answer":"Headers can influence browser behavior related to framing, content interpretation, transport security, resource loading, and other security properties. Their values should therefore be intentionally configured, documented, and regression-tested rather than inherited accidentally from defaults.","testing_guidance":"Inspect security-relevant response headers for representative routes and deployment configurations.","verification_method":"Compare observed headers against the documented security policy and browser-support requirements.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secure Headers Project","Relevant W3C specifications"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B13-R003","D19-B13-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B13-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"MIME confusion","concept":"Content type integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should applications send an accurate content type and control browser content interpretation where applicable?","answer":"Ambiguous content types can cause clients or intermediaries to interpret data differently from the application's intended representation. Accurate media types and appropriate browser policies reduce ambiguity at the content-consumption boundary.","testing_guidance":"Serve representative content with correct and incorrect content types and verify expected browser handling.","verification_method":"Verify that sensitive content is delivered with an intentional media type and appropriate interpretation policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secure Headers Project","HTTP semantics standards"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B12-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B13-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"transport security policy","concept":"HTTP Strict Transport Security","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What security property does HSTS provide?","answer":"HTTP Strict Transport Security instructs conforming browsers to use HTTPS for applicable future connections to a protected host according to the policy. It helps reduce downgrade and accidental insecure-transport exposure after the policy is established.","constraints":"Deployment must account for domain scope, subdomains, certificate readiness, and recovery implications before applying broad policies.","verification_method":"Verify the deployed policy and test browser behavior against the intended host scope.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 6797","OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B13-R002","D19-B15-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B13-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"browser policy regression","concept":"Security header regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should browser security policies be regression-tested after infrastructure or framework changes?","answer":"Reverse proxies, web servers, frameworks, middleware, CDNs, and deployment templates can alter or remove security headers without changing application source code. Security headers therefore require deployment-level verification.","test_case":{"checks":["framing_policy","content_type_policy","transport_security_policy","content_security_policy_if_used","cookie_security_attributes"],"expected_result":"Observed behavior remains consistent with the approved security policy."},"verification_method":"Run automated HTTP-level checks against representative production-like deployment configurations.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secure Headers Project"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B13-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B14-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"API authorization","concept":"API authorization at the resource boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must APIs perform authorization on the actual requested resource rather than trusting client-supplied ownership fields?","answer":"Client-controlled identifiers and ownership claims are untrusted input. The server must derive or verify resource ownership and authorization from trusted identity and policy context before returning or modifying protected resources.","invalid_example":"if request.json['owner_id'] == current_user.id: allow_operation()","corrected_pattern":"Resolve the target resource and evaluate authorization against server-side resource ownership and current policy.","testing_guidance":"Change resource identifiers and ownership fields independently and verify that unauthorized objects remain inaccessible.","verification_method":"Verify authorization using trusted server-side state rather than client assertions.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B04-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B14-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"mass assignment","concept":"Explicit writable-field control","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can binding arbitrary request fields directly to domain objects create a security vulnerability?","answer":"An attacker may submit fields that the endpoint did not intend to expose, such as role, ownership, approval, status, or internal control fields. Explicitly defining writable fields prevents accidental privilege or state changes.","invalid_example":"user.update_from_json(request.json)","corrected_pattern":"Extract only fields explicitly permitted for this operation, then validate and authorize each security-sensitive change.","testing_guidance":"Submit known protected field names alongside legitimate fields and verify that protected fields cannot be modified.","verification_method":"Compare API input schema with the actual mutable domain fields and verify that protected fields are excluded or separately authorized.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","OWASP Mass Assignment guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B14-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B14-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"object property authorization","concept":"Property-level authorization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can object-level authorization still be insufficient when an API returns or modifies many properties?","answer":"A caller may legitimately access an object but lack permission to view or modify particular properties such as financial, administrative, security, or personally sensitive fields. Authorization may therefore need to operate at property or operation granularity.","testing_guidance":"Test the same object under principals with different property permissions.","verification_method":"Verify that restricted properties are omitted or protected independently of general object access.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B04-R002","D19-B14-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B14-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"API rate controls","concept":"API resource-consumption controls","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should API security consider resource consumption in addition to authentication and authorization?","answer":"An authenticated and authorized client can still exhaust computational, database, storage, queue, or downstream resources through high-volume or high-cost requests. Security controls should account for request rate, concurrency, payload size, operation cost, and dependency capacity where relevant.","testing_guidance":"Test high-frequency, high-concurrency, large-payload, and expensive-operation scenarios under controlled conditions.","verification_method":"Verify that resource controls activate according to documented limits without bypassing authorization requirements.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","Secure resource-management principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B14-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B14-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"API Security","subtopic":"API security regression","concept":"API authorization regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an API authorization regression matrix distinguish?","answer":"It should distinguish unauthenticated, authenticated-but-unauthorized, authorized, privileged, cross-tenant, expired-session, altered-object, altered-property, and invalid-input cases where applicable.","test_case":{"expected":"Each operation produces the policy-defined result for each principal/resource combination; no unauthorized state transition succeeds."},"verification_method":"Maintain the matrix as a security regression suite and update it whenever API resources, roles, tenants, or authorization policies change.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B14-R001","D19-B14-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B15-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Transport Security","subtopic":"TLS","concept":"TLS certificate hostname verification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must a TLS client verify that the certificate identity matches the intended server identity?","answer":"Encryption alone does not prove that the connection is to the intended endpoint. Certificate chain validation and hostname or identity verification establish the authenticated server identity required by the trust model.","security_implications":"Disabling hostname or certificate verification can convert an encrypted connection into one vulnerable to active interception.","invalid_example":"TLS client configured to accept any certificate or ignore hostname mismatch.","corrected_pattern":"Use the platform or library's standard certificate and hostname verification with a trusted certificate store.","verification_method":"Test valid certificates, expired certificates, untrusted issuers, hostname mismatches, and intentionally invalid certificates.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 5280","OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B15-R002","D19-B15-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B15-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Transport Security","subtopic":"TLS","concept":"Certificate verification bypass is not a production TLS solution","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is disabling certificate verification an unsafe workaround for TLS failures?","answer":"It suppresses evidence that the authenticated transport trust model is failing instead of resolving the underlying certificate, hostname, trust-store, or deployment problem. The connection may remain encrypted while the endpoint identity is no longer authenticated.","failure_mode":"Client accepts an untrusted or mismatched endpoint.","root_cause":"Verification has been disabled rather than fixing the trust configuration.","solution":"Correct certificate issuance, hostname configuration, trust-store configuration, or deployment identity and retain verification.","testing_guidance":"Force certificate and hostname failures and verify that the client rejects them.","verification_method":"Inspect TLS configuration and ensure verification cannot be disabled through ordinary production configuration.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","RFC 5280"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B15-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B15-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Transport Security","subtopic":"TLS trust stores","concept":"Trust-store governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should trust stores be managed as security configuration?","answer":"The trust store determines which certificate authorities or trust anchors are accepted for certificate validation. Uncontrolled modification can either break legitimate connections or expand the set of trusted issuers beyond the intended security boundary.","verification_method":"Inventory trust stores, permitted trust anchors, ownership, update process, and deployment differences.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 5280","OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B15-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B15-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Transport Security","subtopic":"TLS protocol configuration","concept":"Protocol and cipher configuration must follow supported security guidance","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should TLS protocol and cipher configuration be version-aware rather than copied from generic examples?","answer":"Supported protocol versions, cipher suites, library defaults, interoperability requirements, and security guidance evolve. A configuration appropriate for one runtime or deployment can be obsolete or incompatible in another.","constraints":["Record the runtime/library version.","Prefer secure library defaults when appropriate.","Verify interoperability against required peers."],"verification_method":"Evaluate the actual deployed TLS configuration against current authoritative guidance for the supported runtime and protocol versions.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["IETF TLS specifications","OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B15-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B15-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Transport Security","subtopic":"TLS testing","concept":"TLS negative testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Which TLS failures should a security regression suite deliberately test?","answer":"At minimum, where applicable, test untrusted certificates, hostname mismatch, expired certificates, invalid chains, unsupported protocol versions, unacceptable cryptographic parameters, and revoked or otherwise disallowed identities according to the application's certificate-validation policy.","expected_test_result":"The client rejects connections that violate the configured trust and protocol policy and accepts only connections satisfying that policy.","verification_method":"Use controlled test certificates and protocol configurations rather than weakening production verification.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","IETF TLS specifications"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B15-R001","D19-B15-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B16-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"File and Filesystem Security","subtopic":"file permissions","concept":"Least-privilege filesystem access","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should application processes have only the filesystem permissions they require?","answer":"Excessive filesystem privileges increase the impact of application compromise, malicious input, dependency compromise, and accidental writes. Processes should receive only the read, write, execute, and directory permissions required for their intended operation.","verification_method":"Inventory filesystem permissions and test access outside the intended application directories.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST least-privilege principles","OWASP File Upload guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B04-R005"],"supports":["D19-B16-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B16-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"File and Filesystem Security","subtopic":"file upload","concept":"Secure file-upload pipeline","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What security controls should a file-upload pipeline consider?","answer":"A secure upload design should consider authentication and authorization, file-size limits, allowed file types, content validation, safe storage location, generated server-side names where appropriate, executable-content handling, malware scanning where required, download authorization, and isolation from application code execution.","constraints":["File-type checks should not rely solely on client-provided metadata.","Storage and retrieval policy must be explicit.","Security requirements depend on file types and business purpose."],"verification_method":"Test unauthorized uploads, oversized files, unexpected types, misleading filenames, duplicate names, and retrieval authorization.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B16-R003","D19-B16-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B16-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"File and Filesystem Security","subtopic":"filename security","concept":"Server-controlled storage names","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should applications avoid trusting uploaded filenames as filesystem paths?","answer":"Client-controlled filenames can contain traversal syntax, platform-specific path semantics, unexpected characters, collisions, misleading extensions, or other values unsuitable for direct filesystem use. Applications should separate user-visible naming from storage identity.","corrected_pattern":"Generate or assign a server-controlled storage identifier and preserve the original filename only as untrusted metadata when needed.","testing_guidance":"Test traversal strings, path separators, reserved names, Unicode variations, duplicate names, and very long names.","verification_method":"Verify that the storage path is derived from controlled identifiers rather than directly from user input.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","OWASP Path Traversal guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B07-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B16-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"File and Filesystem Security","subtopic":"temporary files","concept":"Secure temporary-file handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can temporary files become a security boundary?","answer":"Temporary files can contain sensitive information and may be exposed through predictable names, unsafe permissions, shared directories, stale cleanup, or concurrent access. Secure temporary-file APIs should provide controlled creation and permissions appropriate to the data.","testing_guidance":"Inspect temporary-file naming, permissions, lifecycle, cleanup, and concurrent access under realistic execution conditions.","verification_method":"Verify that temporary files are created atomically where required, with appropriate permissions and controlled lifecycle behavior.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP secure temporary-file practices","Operating-system filesystem security principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B16-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B16-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"File and Filesystem Security","subtopic":"symlink attacks","concept":"Filesystem race and symlink considerations","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can checking a path before opening it still be unsafe in some filesystem scenarios?","answer":"A separate check and use operation can create a time-of-check/time-of-use race in which the filesystem target changes between validation and use. Symbolic links and concurrent modifications can be relevant attack paths.","solution":"Prefer filesystem APIs and operations that combine validation and use safely or provide appropriate no-follow or directory-relative semantics where supported.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled concurrent filesystem changes to evaluate whether validation and use can be separated by an attacker-controlled race.","verification_method":"Verify that security-sensitive file operations cannot be redirected between the authorization check and the actual operation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-367","Operating-system filesystem security guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B07-R004","D19-B16-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B17-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Process Security","subtopic":"process privileges","concept":"Privilege separation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why can splitting privileged and unprivileged operations improve security?","answer":"Privilege separation reduces the amount of code and input processing that must operate with elevated authority. If an unprivileged component is compromised, the attacker does not automatically receive the privileges required by the protected component.","tradeoff":"Additional process or IPC boundaries introduce complexity, synchronization requirements, and new trust interfaces.","verification_method":"Map privileged operations and verify that unprivileged components cannot directly exercise privileged capabilities.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Privilege separation security architecture principles","NIST least-privilege guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B04-R005"],"supports":["D19-B17-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B17-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Process Security","subtopic":"environment variables","concept":"Environment variables are not inherently secret storage","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should applications avoid assuming environment variables are automatically safe for secrets?","answer":"Environment values can be exposed through process inspection, debugging facilities, crash diagnostics, inherited subprocess environments, deployment tooling, or accidental logging depending on the operating system and runtime. Their suitability depends on the threat model and execution environment.","security_implications":"A secret-management design must evaluate the complete exposure path rather than relying on the storage label 'environment variable'.","verification_method":"Trace secret access through the process, child processes, diagnostics, deployment system, and observability tooling.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","Operating-system process security principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B05-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B17-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Process Security","subtopic":"resource limits","concept":"Process resource containment","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security architecture consider CPU, memory, file descriptors, subprocess count, and other process resources?","answer":"A compromised or malformed workload can exhaust process or host resources even without gaining additional privileges. Resource limits and workload isolation can constrain the blast radius of resource-exhaustion conditions.","testing_guidance":"Exercise controlled resource exhaustion and verify that limits prevent one workload from consuming resources required by other protected workloads.","verification_method":"Verify configured resource boundaries and observe failure behavior under controlled stress.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure resource-management principles","NIST system resilience guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R004","D19-B14-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B17-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Process Security","subtopic":"subprocess inheritance","concept":"Controlled subprocess environment","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should applications explicitly control security-sensitive subprocess inheritance?","answer":"Subprocesses may inherit environment values, file descriptors, working directories, credentials, or other process state. Unnecessary inheritance can expose secrets or privileges and expand the impact of a compromised child process.","testing_guidance":"Inspect inherited descriptors, environment variables, working directory, privileges, and credentials for subprocesses.","verification_method":"Verify that only explicitly required state crosses the process boundary.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Operating-system process security principles","OWASP Command Injection guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B07-R002","D19-B17-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B17-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Process Security","subtopic":"process security testing","concept":"Privilege-boundary regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should process privilege security tests verify?","answer":"Tests should verify that unprivileged operations cannot perform privileged actions, compromised child processes do not inherit unnecessary authority, protected resources remain inaccessible, and expected privilege transitions occur only through controlled mechanisms.","expected_test_result":"Unauthorized privileged operations are rejected while legitimate operations continue under the documented privilege model.","verification_method":"Execute the test suite under representative service identities and deployment configurations.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Least-privilege engineering practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B17-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B18-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Dependency Security","subtopic":"dependency inventory","concept":"Software dependency inventory","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why should software maintain an explicit dependency inventory?","answer":"An inventory establishes what external components are part of the software supply chain and enables version tracking, vulnerability assessment, license analysis, update management, and incident response.","required_fields":["component","version","source","direct_or_transitive","integrity_reference_where_available","owner"],"verification_method":"Generate or review a dependency inventory from authoritative build metadata and compare it with deployed artifacts where possible.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Secure Software Development Framework","CISA software supply-chain guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B18-R002","D19-B18-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B18-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Dependency Security","subtopic":"version management","concept":"Dependency version pinning and controlled updates","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What security trade-off exists between unrestricted dependency ranges and tightly controlled versions?","answer":"Broad version ranges can simplify updates but increase the possibility of unexpected behavior or supply-chain changes entering builds. Tightly controlled versions improve reproducibility and change control but require an active update process to receive security fixes.","tradeoff":"The correct strategy depends on the build system, release cadence, ecosystem, and security requirements; version control should be paired with timely vulnerability remediation.","verification_method":"Verify that dependency updates are intentional, reproducible, reviewed, and traceable to a known version.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Reproducible-build and software supply-chain practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B18-R001"],"supports":["D19-B18-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B18-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Dependency Security","subtopic":"vulnerability management","concept":"Vulnerability presence versus exploitability","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should a dependency vulnerability record distinguish presence from exploitability?","answer":"A vulnerable component can exist in software while the vulnerable functionality is unreachable, disabled, protected by other controls, or otherwise outside the relevant threat path. Conversely, exploitability can depend on deployment configuration and attacker access. Presence alone should not be silently treated as equivalent to demonstrated exploitation.","verification_method":"Trace the vulnerable component, affected functionality, reachable attack path, deployment conditions, and applicable mitigations.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST vulnerability-management principles","CVE/CVSS ecosystem guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B18-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B18-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Dependency Security","subtopic":"dependency confusion","concept":"Dependency source integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can dependency source ambiguity create supply-chain risk?","answer":"When package resolution permits multiple sources or names with ambiguous ownership, an unintended package can be selected instead of the intended internal or external component. Secure dependency management should define trusted sources, namespace ownership, resolution rules, and provenance.","testing_guidance":"Test package resolution under controlled repositories and verify that intended namespaces resolve only from authorized sources.","verification_method":"Inspect dependency manifests, repository configuration, namespace policy, and lock information.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CISA software supply-chain guidance","OWASP dependency and supply-chain guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B18-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B18-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Dependency Security","subtopic":"supply-chain verification","concept":"Dependency provenance chain","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified before a third-party dependency becomes part of a trusted build?","answer":"The project should be able to identify the dependency, intended version, source, integrity information where supported, applicable security advisories, licensing constraints, and the process by which the dependency entered the build.","verification_method":"Trace dependency declaration → resolution → fetched artifact → build input → deployed artifact.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","CISA software supply-chain guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B18-R001","D19-B18-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B19-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Memory and Runtime Security","subtopic":"memory safety","concept":"Memory-safety failure classes","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What types of memory-safety defects can create security impact in native software?","answer":"Important classes include out-of-bounds access, use-after-free, double free, invalid lifetime assumptions, uninitialized memory use, integer-related size errors that influence memory operations, and other violations of object or memory invariants.","security_implications":"Depending on the language, runtime, mitigation environment, and reachable code path, memory-safety defects can lead to crashes, data corruption, information disclosure, or code-execution opportunities.","verification_method":"Use language-appropriate static analysis, sanitizers, fuzzing, code review, and runtime protections as applicable.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE memory-safety categories","C/C++ language and security guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B19-R002","D19-B19-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B19-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Memory and Runtime Security","subtopic":"buffer bounds","concept":"Bounds must match allocation and representation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must buffer operations use bounds derived from the actual object and representation?","answer":"A nominally valid length can exceed the actual allocated storage, account for the wrong element size, overflow arithmetic, or omit terminator or metadata requirements. Safe bounds reasoning must connect the operation length to the actual object representation.","invalid_example":"copy(destination, source, user_length) without verifying destination capacity and source validity.","corrected_pattern":"Validate length against the actual destination capacity and source bounds before performing the operation, using APIs that preserve bounds information where available.","testing_guidance":"Test zero, one, exact-boundary, boundary-plus-one, maximum, and arithmetic-overflow cases.","verification_method":"Prove or verify that every memory operation remains within valid object bounds for all permitted inputs.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-119","C/C++ secure coding guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B19-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Memory and Runtime Security","subtopic":"integer safety","concept":"Integer overflow can become a security boundary failure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can integer overflow become a security vulnerability?","answer":"If an integer is used to calculate allocation size, buffer length, array index, offset, or resource quantity, overflow can cause the computed value to differ from the intended value. A later memory or resource operation may then operate on insufficient capacity or incorrect bounds.","testing_guidance":"Test values near representation limits and operations involving multiplication, addition, subtraction, conversion, and signed/unsigned transitions.","verification_method":"Review security-sensitive arithmetic and prove that intermediate values remain within valid ranges before conversion or allocation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE integer-overflow categories","CERT Secure Coding Standards"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B19-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B19-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Memory and Runtime Security","subtopic":"unsafe language boundaries","concept":"FFI boundary validation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why does a foreign-function interface require explicit security reasoning?","answer":"An FFI crosses language and runtime safety models. Ownership, lifetime, nullability, representation, integer widths, error semantics, threading, and memory ownership may differ between components. A memory-safe caller does not automatically make an unsafe native callee safe.","verification_method":"Document ownership and representation contracts at every FFI boundary and test invalid, null, oversized, and lifetime-sensitive inputs.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Language FFI safety practices","C/C++ interoperability guidance"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B19-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B19-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Memory and Runtime Security","subtopic":"memory-safety verification","concept":"Memory-safety verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence can support confidence in memory-sensitive native code?","answer":"Depending on the language and risk, useful evidence includes compiler diagnostics, static analysis, code review, AddressSanitizer or equivalent sanitizers, undefined-behavior detection, fuzzing, targeted boundary tests, and runtime protections. No single technique proves absence of every memory defect.","verification_method":"Map each high-risk component to applicable analysis and testing techniques and preserve the resulting evidence.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["LLVM sanitizer documentation","CWE memory-safety guidance","CERT Secure Coding Standards"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B19-R001","D19-B19-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B20-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"security boundaries","concept":"Security boundary inventory","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should a complete application security-boundary inventory contain?","answer":"It should identify identity boundaries, privilege boundaries, tenant boundaries, process boundaries, network or service boundaries, data-classification boundaries, interpreter boundaries, filesystem boundaries, dependency trust boundaries, and administrative boundaries relevant to the system.","verification_method":"Compare the inventory against architecture, code paths, deployment configuration, data flows, and operational workflows.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST security architecture guidance","Secure architecture principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B20-R002","D19-B20-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B20-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"attack surface","concept":"Attack-surface inventory","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should an attack-surface inventory identify?","answer":"Identify externally reachable interfaces, authenticated interfaces, administrative functions, file-processing paths, interpreters, parsers, dependencies, privileged operations, management channels, data import/export paths, and trust-boundary crossings. The inventory should connect each surface to its security controls and verification evidence.","testing_guidance":"Compare the inventory with deployed routes, ports, processes, APIs, scheduled jobs, plugins, and management interfaces.","verification_method":"Require each exposed capability to have an owner, intended audience, authentication/authorization policy where applicable, and security verification path.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP attack-surface management principles","NIST security engineering guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B20-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B20-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"blast radius","concept":"Blast-radius containment","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should security architecture limit the impact of a compromised component?","answer":"Architectural isolation can limit credentials, data, network reachability, filesystem access, process privileges, tenant visibility, and operational authority available to a compromised component. The goal is to prevent one security failure from automatically becoming unrestricted system compromise.","tradeoff":"Stronger isolation can add latency, operational complexity, deployment overhead, and additional trust interfaces.","verification_method":"Model a compromise of each high-value component and enumerate the maximum authority and data it can reach.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST zero-trust architecture principles","Least-privilege and compartmentalization practices"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B20-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B20-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"security control independence","concept":"Common-mode security failures","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can multiple security controls fail together despite appearing to provide defense in depth?","answer":"Controls may share the same identity provider, parser, secret, library, configuration source, administrator, network path, or incorrect assumption. A shared failure mode can therefore defeat several apparent layers simultaneously.","root_cause":"The architecture counted correlated controls as independent controls.","solution":"Identify shared dependencies and explicitly model common-mode failure in security analysis.","verification_method":"For every critical control, identify upstream dependencies whose compromise or failure could bypass multiple controls.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Security architecture and defense-in-depth principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B01-R005","D19-B20-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B20-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"foundation gate","concept":"D19 B1-B20 security architecture gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be true before D19 proceeds beyond its foundational security architecture layer?","answer":"The dataset must represent security properties, threats, trust boundaries, authentication, authorization, secrets, input validation, injection defenses, browser security, API security, transport security, filesystem and process security, dependency security, memory-safety concerns, attack surface, blast radius, and common-mode failures. Each major security boundary must have an associated control, failure mode, testing strategy, verification method, evidence classification, and provenance.","coverage_requirements":["Threat modeling","Authentication","Authorization","Secrets","Input validation","Injection prevention","Web security","API security","TLS and transport security","Filesystem security","Process security","Dependency security","Memory/runtime security","Attack-surface analysis","Blast-radius containment","Common-mode failure analysis"],"status":"NOT_LOCKED","reason":"B1-B20 establishes the first major D19 security layer. The dataset remains open for deeper security domains, secure coding across target languages, cryptographic implementation details, vulnerability-specific records, supply-chain verification, security testing, fuzzing, static analysis, dynamic analysis, formal security properties, incident-oriented debugging, and cross-language security patterns.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19 Security Engineering scope","Autonomous Software Engineering Brain quality-first dataset governance"],"relationships":{"governs":["D19-B01-R001","D19-B02-R001","D19-B03-R001","D19-B04-R001","D19-B05-R001","D19-B06-R001","D19-B07-R001","D19-B08-R001","D19-B09-R001","D19-B10-R001","D19-B11-R001","D19-B12-R001","D19-B13-R001","D19-B14-R001","D19-B15-R001","D19-B16-R001","D19-B17-R001","D19-B18-R001","D19-B19-R001"],"supports":["D19-B21-R001"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B21-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Authenticated Encryption","concept":"AEAD provides confidentiality and integrity together","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is authenticated encryption with associated data useful for application security?","answer":"AEAD combines encryption of plaintext with authentication of ciphertext and associated data, allowing an application to detect unauthorized modification while protecting plaintext confidentiality. The associated data can authenticate metadata without encrypting it.","constraints":["Nonce requirements are algorithm-specific and must be respected.","Successful decryption must not be treated as proof of application-level authorization.","Key management remains a separate security responsibility."],"verification_method":"Verify that modified ciphertext or associated data is rejected and that the application does not process unauthenticated plaintext.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 5116","NIST authenticated-encryption guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B08-R001","D19-B08-R005"],"supports":["D19-B21-R002","D19-B21-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B21-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Nonce Management","concept":"Nonce uniqueness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is nonce management a critical requirement for many AEAD constructions?","answer":"Some AEAD algorithms require nonces to be unique for a given key. Reusing a nonce under the same key can undermine confidentiality and authenticity guarantees, with the severity depending on the algorithm and misuse pattern.","security_implications":"Nonce generation and storage must be designed as part of the cryptographic protocol rather than left to accidental caller behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test nonce generation, persistence, concurrency, restart behavior, and key rotation. For algorithms requiring uniqueness, verify that the same key cannot be used with a repeated nonce.","verification_method":"Document the algorithm's nonce requirements and verify the implementation enforces them across processes, threads, restarts, and key lifetimes.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 5116","NIST SP 800-38D","Algorithm-specific cryptographic specifications"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B21-R001"],"supports":["D19-B21-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B21-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Associated Data","concept":"Authenticated metadata","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should associated data be used in an authenticated-encryption design?","answer":"Associated data is appropriate when metadata must be authenticated but does not need confidentiality, such as protocol identifiers, record types, version information, or selected routing metadata. The exact fields must be defined by the protocol rather than implicitly including arbitrary mutable metadata.","tradeoff":"Including a field as associated data binds it cryptographically to the ciphertext; legitimate changes then require a new authentication operation.","verification_method":"Modify each authenticated metadata field independently and verify that the protected record is rejected when the field no longer matches the authenticated value.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 5116","NIST authenticated-encryption guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B21-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B21-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Cryptographic API usage","concept":"Use established cryptographic APIs","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should application developers avoid implementing cryptographic primitives themselves?","answer":"Cryptographic security depends on subtle algorithmic, randomness, key-management, encoding, nonce, side-channel, and protocol-composition details. Established libraries provide reviewed implementations and standardized interfaces, although correct API usage remains the application's responsibility.","failure_mode":"A custom primitive or incorrect low-level implementation introduces an unknown security defect.","solution":"Use well-maintained, appropriate cryptographic libraries and document the security properties required from the API.","verification_method":"Review cryptographic dependencies, algorithms, modes, key sizes, nonce handling, and protocol composition against authoritative guidance.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST cryptographic guidance","OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B08-R001"],"supports":["D19-B21-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B21-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Cryptographic regression","concept":"Cryptographic negative-test matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should cryptographic negative testing verify?","answer":"Negative tests should verify rejection of modified ciphertext, altered authenticated metadata, invalid keys, malformed encodings, unsupported algorithms, invalid key states, and nonce misuse conditions that the implementation is required to detect or prevent.","expected_test_result":"Invalid cryptographic inputs do not silently produce accepted plaintext or bypass the intended integrity boundary.","verification_method":"Use deterministic test vectors and controlled fault injection where appropriate; preserve expected failure semantics as regression evidence.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST cryptographic testing guidance","RFC 5116"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B21-R001","D19-B21-R002","D19-B21-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B22-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Password Storage","concept":"Password hashing","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should passwords normally be stored using password hashing rather than reversible encryption?","answer":"Password authentication requires verification rather than recovery of the original password. Password-hashing functions are designed to make large-scale offline guessing more expensive while storing a verifier instead of a reversible plaintext-equivalent secret.","constraints":["Use a password-hashing algorithm appropriate to current security guidance.","Use a unique salt per password.","Cost parameters must be selected for the deployment environment and reviewed over time."],"verification_method":"Inspect stored records to verify that plaintext passwords or reversible password encryption are not used as the password verifier.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-63B"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B08-R002"],"supports":["D19-B22-R002","D19-B22-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B22-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Password Storage","concept":"Password salt","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What security purpose does a unique password salt provide?","answer":"A salt makes each password hash instance distinct even when users choose the same password and prevents attackers from directly reusing precomputed values across accounts. The salt is not required to be secret, but it must be unique according to the password-hashing design.","verification_method":"Verify that each stored password verifier contains or references the correct unique salt and that password changes generate a new salt.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-63B"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B22-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B22-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Password hashing cost","concept":"Adaptive password-hashing cost","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should password-hashing cost be calibrated rather than copied blindly?","answer":"A higher computational or memory cost can increase resistance to password guessing but also consumes server resources during authentication. The appropriate parameters depend on the selected algorithm, hardware, concurrency, latency requirements, and current security guidance.","tradeoff":"Increasing cost improves resistance against some offline attacks but increases legitimate authentication resource consumption.","verification_method":"Measure the implementation under the actual supported deployment profile and record the selected parameters and rationale. Do not represent an unmeasured value as a benchmark.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-63B"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B22-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B22-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"Password reset","concept":"Password reset must establish a new authenticated state","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is password-reset functionality a security-sensitive authentication flow?","answer":"A password reset can replace the credential protecting an account and therefore must not rely on weaker authentication than the security boundary it replaces. Reset tokens or recovery mechanisms require controlled issuance, expiry, single-use behavior where appropriate, confidentiality, and account-state handling.","security_implications":"A weakness in recovery can bypass otherwise strong authentication.","verification_method":"Test token issuance, expiration, reuse, interception resistance, account enumeration behavior, and session invalidation according to the application's threat model.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Forgot Password Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-63B"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B03-R003","D19-B05-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B22-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"Credential lifecycle","concept":"Credential change and session lifecycle","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can changing a password require session-management actions?","answer":"Existing authenticated sessions, refresh tokens, API credentials, or remembered authentication state may remain usable after a password changes unless the application's session policy explicitly handles them. Credential changes should therefore be evaluated together with session and token lifecycle policy.","verification_method":"Change a password and test existing sessions, refresh credentials, remembered devices, and other authentication artifacts according to the documented policy.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-63B"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B03-R003","D19-B22-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B23-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"Multi-Factor Authentication","concept":"MFA factor independence","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What security property distinguishes multi-factor authentication from multiple credentials of the same factor type?","answer":"MFA combines authentication evidence from distinct factor categories, such as knowledge, possession, or inherence. Requiring two passwords does not provide the same factor diversity as combining different categories.","verification_method":"Map each authentication factor to its factor category and verify that the authentication policy actually requires distinct categories.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SP 800-63B"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B03-R002"],"supports":["D19-B23-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B23-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"MFA recovery","concept":"Recovery is part of the MFA security boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must MFA recovery be analyzed as part of MFA security rather than treated as ordinary support functionality?","answer":"A recovery process can provide an alternative path around the primary authentication factors. If recovery is materially weaker than the MFA policy, an attacker who compromises recovery evidence may bypass the intended authentication strength.","testing_guidance":"Model lost-device, replacement-device, backup-code, support-assisted, and account-recovery paths and compare their assurance with the normal authentication path.","verification_method":"Verify that every recovery path has explicit authentication requirements, authorization, auditing, and abuse controls.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SP 800-63B","OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B23-R001","D19-B22-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B23-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"Session fixation","concept":"Session identifier rotation after authentication","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should applications consider rotating session identifiers when authentication state changes?","answer":"If an attacker can cause a victim to use a known or attacker-selected pre-authentication session identifier, retaining that identifier after authentication can allow session fixation. Rotating the identifier at an authentication boundary breaks the association with the pre-authentication state.","verification_method":"Attempt to establish a pre-authentication session identifier and determine whether the authenticated session receives a new identifier.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Session Fixation guidance","OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B03-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B23-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"Token security","concept":"Bearer-token exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must bearer authentication tokens be treated as sensitive credentials?","answer":"A bearer token generally grants its holder the ability to authenticate as the token subject within its scope and lifetime. Disclosure can therefore enable unauthorized use without recovering the user's original password.","security_implications":"Tokens require appropriate transport protection, storage controls, expiration or revocation strategy, scope restrictions, and logging precautions.","verification_method":"Trace token exposure through URLs, logs, browser storage, telemetry, referrers, error messages, and network boundaries.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 6750","OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B05-R005","D19-B23-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B23-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"Authentication regression","concept":"Authentication-state transition testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Which authentication transitions deserve security regression tests?","answer":"Test unauthenticated to authenticated, authenticated to logged-out, password changed, MFA changed, session expired, token expired, recovery completed, privilege changed, and account-disabled transitions as applicable to the system.","expected_test_result":"Each transition creates exactly the authentication state and credential/session behavior specified by policy.","verification_method":"Model authentication as an explicit state machine and test every security-sensitive transition.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Authentication and Session Management guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B03-R003","D19-B22-R005","D19-B23-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B24-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Authorization Engineering","subtopic":"RBAC","concept":"Role-based authorization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the security purpose of role-based access control?","answer":"RBAC associates permissions with roles and assigns users or service identities to roles, allowing authorization policy to be managed independently of individual application code paths. It is useful when organizational permissions naturally map to stable role structures.","tradeoff":"Overly broad roles can violate least privilege, while excessive role proliferation can make policy difficult to reason about and maintain.","verification_method":"Map every sensitive operation to required permissions and verify role assignments against the intended policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST RBAC model"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B04-R003","D19-B04-R005"],"supports":["D19-B24-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B24-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Authorization Engineering","subtopic":"ABAC","concept":"Attribute-based authorization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can attribute-based authorization be useful?","answer":"ABAC evaluates attributes of the subject, resource, action, and environment against policy. It can express conditions that do not map cleanly to fixed roles, such as tenant, resource classification, ownership, geographic context, or operational state.","tradeoff":"Attribute policies can become difficult to audit if attributes, policy precedence, and evaluation semantics are poorly controlled.","verification_method":"For each sensitive decision, record the attributes used, policy rule selected, and resulting authorization decision.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SP 800-162"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B04-R003"],"supports":["D19-B24-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B24-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Authorization Engineering","subtopic":"Policy evaluation","concept":"Authorization decision traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive authorization decisions be explainable at the policy level?","answer":"When a request is denied or permitted, engineers need enough evidence to determine which principal, resource, action, policy, attributes, and decision path produced the result. Traceability improves debugging, incident investigation, and regression verification without exposing unnecessary sensitive data.","verification_method":"Verify that security audit records can reconstruct the decision while respecting privacy and secret-handling requirements.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST access-control guidance","Security auditability principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B10-R004","D19-B24-R001","D19-B24-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B24-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Authorization Engineering","subtopic":"Confused deputy","concept":"Confused-deputy prevention","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a confused-deputy security failure?","answer":"A privileged component becomes confused about whose authority it is exercising and performs an action for an untrusted requester using privileges the requester does not possess. Preventing it requires preserving requester identity and intended authorization context across trust boundaries.","root_cause":"The privileged component authorizes based on its own authority or an ambiguous delegated request rather than validating the originating principal and permitted operation.","solution":"Propagate explicit authorization context and enforce policy at the resource boundary.","verification_method":"Test requests where a low-privilege principal attempts to induce a higher-privilege component to perform an unauthorized action.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-441","Capability and authorization security principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B04-R005"],"supports":["D19-B24-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B24-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Authorization Engineering","subtopic":"Authorization testing","concept":"Authorization property matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an authorization property matrix test?","answer":"Test principals, resources, actions, tenant boundaries, object ownership, privileged properties, delegation context, authentication state, and relevant environmental attributes. Each combination should produce a documented allow or deny result.","expected_test_result":"No unauthorized combination succeeds and every explicitly authorized combination remains functional.","verification_method":"Generate tests from the authorization policy rather than relying only on manually selected happy-path cases.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST access-control guidance","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B24-R001","D19-B24-R002","D19-B24-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B25-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Race Conditions","concept":"Security-sensitive race condition","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a race condition become a security vulnerability?","answer":"A race occurs when the correctness or security of an operation depends on timing between concurrent actors. If authorization, validation, resource acquisition, or state transitions occur in separate steps, another actor may change the relevant state between those steps.","verification_method":"Identify security-sensitive check-use sequences and test concurrent operations under controlled scheduling or stress conditions.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-362","CWE-367"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B25-R002","D19-B25-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B25-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"TOCTOU","concept":"Time-of-check/time-of-use security gap","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is separate security validation followed by a later use operation dangerous?","answer":"The state validated during the check may no longer be true when the use occurs. An attacker or concurrent process can change the resource, identity, permission, path, or value between the two operations.","invalid_example":"if is_allowed(path): open(path)","corrected_pattern":"Use an atomic or capability-oriented operation where possible, or bind the validation and use to the same protected resource state.","testing_guidance":"Attempt controlled state changes between validation and use and verify that authorization cannot be bypassed.","verification_method":"Determine whether the security property is guaranteed across the complete operation rather than only at the initial check.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-367","CWE-362"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B16-R005","D19-B25-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B25-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Atomicity","concept":"Security state transitions should preserve invariants atomically where required","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can atomic state transitions be necessary for security properties?","answer":"If a security invariant spans multiple related values, exposing intermediate states can allow another actor to observe or exploit an invalid combination. Atomic transactions, locks, compare-and-swap operations, or equivalent mechanisms can preserve the invariant across the transition.","constraints":["The appropriate atomicity mechanism depends on the storage and concurrency model.","Locks alone do not guarantee security if the invariant crosses independent systems."],"verification_method":"Identify the invariant and verify that concurrent observers cannot cause or exploit an invalid intermediate state.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Concurrency and transactional integrity principles","CWE-362"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B25-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B25-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Concurrency security","concept":"Lock scope and privilege scope","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can concurrency-control design affect security?","answer":"A lock that protects too little state may leave a security invariant exposed, while a lock covering unrelated operations can create denial-of-service opportunities or deadlocks. Security-critical synchronization should protect exactly the state required by the invariant while minimizing unnecessary contention.","verification_method":"Document the protected invariant, lock scope, lock ordering, failure behavior, and concurrency assumptions.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Concurrency security principles","CWE-362"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B25-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B25-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Race regression","concept":"Concurrency security regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should security-sensitive concurrency behavior be regression-tested?","answer":"Tests should exercise competing operations, repeated execution, adversarial ordering, retries, timeouts, process restarts, and partial failures where those events can alter security state. Deterministic synchronization hooks are preferable to relying solely on random timing.","expected_test_result":"Security invariants remain true under every tested interleaving and no unauthorized state transition occurs.","verification_method":"Combine deterministic concurrency tests with stress, race detection, and targeted invariant checks where supported by the language/runtime.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-362","Concurrency testing practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B25-R001","D19-B25-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B26-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"Template Injection","concept":"Server-side template injection boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is allowing untrusted input to become template source dangerous?","answer":"Template engines interpret template syntax as instructions. If attacker-controlled input becomes template source rather than template data, the attacker may influence evaluation and potentially access data, invoke functions, or achieve code execution depending on the engine and configuration.","verification_method":"Trace untrusted values into template compilation or evaluation APIs and verify that they are supplied as data to trusted templates.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Server-Side Template Injection guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B07-R005"],"supports":["D19-B26-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B26-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"Template Injection","concept":"Template source versus template data","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What is the safe conceptual separation for template rendering?","answer":"Trusted application code should define the template source, while untrusted values should enter only through the template engine's data-binding mechanism. User input should not dynamically define executable template structure.","invalid_example":"render_template(user_supplied_template)","corrected_pattern":"render_template(trusted_template, user_supplied_values)","testing_guidance":"Submit template syntax and expression-like payloads as data and verify that they remain inert.","verification_method":"Review template compilation paths and confirm that untrusted input cannot become executable template source.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Server-Side Template Injection guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B26-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B26-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"LDAP injection","concept":"Interpreter-specific parameterization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why does LDAP input require LDAP-specific escaping or parameterization rather than SQL escaping?","answer":"Each interpreter has its own syntax and escaping rules. SQL escaping rules do not establish safety for LDAP filters or distinguished names. Security controls must use the parameterization or escaping mechanism defined for the target protocol and context.","verification_method":"Identify every LDAP query construction path and verify that user-controlled values cannot alter query structure.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP LDAP Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet","LDAP standards"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B07-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B26-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"Expression languages","concept":"Expression-language injection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should untrusted values not become expression-language source?","answer":"Expression engines may evaluate operators, property access, method calls, or other executable constructs. If untrusted input controls the expression itself, validation becomes dependent on the complete language grammar and runtime capabilities.","solution":"Keep expressions trusted and supply user-controlled values through explicit data parameters where the engine supports them.","verification_method":"Trace user input into expression compilation or evaluation APIs and test expression syntax as data.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Injection Prevention guidance","CWE injection categories"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B26-R001","D19-B26-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B26-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Injection Security","subtopic":"Injection boundary testing","concept":"Interpreter boundary inventory","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can a security engineer systematically find injection risks across an application?","answer":"Inventory every boundary where data enters an interpreter or language: SQL, shell, templates, LDAP, XPath, regular-expression engines, expression languages, serializers, browser DOM APIs, and other parser/evaluation contexts. For each boundary determine whether untrusted data can influence syntax rather than only values.","verification_method":"Map source → transformation → interpreter → output and require a context-specific defense for every untrusted syntax boundary.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet Series"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B07-R005","D19-B26-R001","D19-B26-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B27-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Cross-Origin Resource Sharing","concept":"CORS is not an authentication mechanism","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should CORS configuration not be treated as an authorization system?","answer":"CORS controls which browser origins are permitted to make certain cross-origin requests and read browser responses. It does not replace server-side authentication or authorization and does not prevent non-browser clients from sending requests.","security_implications":"An API must enforce authorization independently of whether a request satisfies browser CORS policy.","verification_method":"Send equivalent requests outside the browser security model and verify that server-side authorization remains enforced.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Fetch Standard","MDN CORS documentation","OWASP CORS guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B04-R001"],"supports":["D19-B27-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B27-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"CORS","concept":"Explicit origin policy","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should cross-origin access policies explicitly define allowed origins and credentials behavior?","answer":"A broad or dynamically reflected origin policy can expose authenticated responses to unintended web origins. The policy should explicitly represent the application's legitimate cross-origin clients and whether credentialed requests are required.","constraints":["Credentialed cross-origin behavior has stricter browser policy requirements.","Origin validation must not be based on unsafe substring or prefix matching."],"testing_guidance":"Test allowed origins, unauthorized origins, credentialed requests, preflight behavior, null origins where relevant, and malformed origin values.","verification_method":"Compare actual response headers with the approved cross-origin policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Fetch Standard","OWASP CORS guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B27-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B27-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"CORS","concept":"Preflight is not authorization","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why must a server not treat successful CORS preflight as proof that a user is authorized?","answer":"A preflight establishes whether a browser may perform a cross-origin operation according to CORS rules. It does not establish the identity or authorization of the user or client for the protected resource.","verification_method":"Test a valid preflight followed by an unauthorized request and verify that resource authorization is still enforced.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Fetch Standard","OWASP CORS guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B27-R001","D19-B27-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B27-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"CORS caching","concept":"CORS policy and intermediary caching","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can CORS responses require careful cache handling?","answer":"If a response's access policy depends on the request Origin, an intermediary that caches the response without varying appropriately can serve a response with headers generated for a different origin. Cache behavior must therefore be consistent with the origin-dependent policy.","verification_method":"Test cache hits across multiple origins and inspect whether origin-specific access-control headers remain correct.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Fetch Standard","HTTP caching standards"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B27-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B27-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"CORS regression","concept":"Cross-origin policy regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should CORS regression tests cover?","answer":"Test allowed and disallowed origins, methods, headers, credential modes, preflight requests, actual requests, redirects where applicable, cache behavior, and non-browser clients.","expected_test_result":"Only explicitly permitted browser cross-origin interactions succeed while server-side authentication and authorization remain independently enforced.","verification_method":"Run the matrix against the actual deployment topology including proxies and caches where applicable.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Fetch Standard","OWASP CORS guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B27-R001","D19-B27-R002"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B28-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Request Smuggling","concept":"HTTP request desynchronization","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can disagreement between HTTP components create request-smuggling risk?","answer":"When a frontend proxy, load balancer, gateway, or backend parses request boundaries differently, attacker-controlled bytes can be interpreted as belonging to different requests by different components. This parser disagreement can cause request desynchronization and security-boundary bypasses.","security_implications":"The risk depends on the exact HTTP versions, intermediaries, parser behavior, connection reuse, and request framing rules.","verification_method":"Inventory every HTTP intermediary and verify that their request-framing behavior is compatible and unambiguous.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP HTTP Request Smuggling guidance","HTTP semantics and messaging standards"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B28-R002","D19-B28-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B28-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Parser differentials","concept":"Consistent request parsing","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security-sensitive HTTP boundaries minimize parser differentials?","answer":"A request that has one meaning at a security gateway but another meaning at the backend creates a mismatch in the security model. Components should use compatible protocol semantics and reject ambiguous or invalid requests consistently.","verification_method":"Compare HTTP parser configurations, protocol versions, framing rules, and rejection behavior across all intermediaries.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP HTTP Request Smuggling guidance","HTTP specifications"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B28-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B28-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Proxy trust","concept":"Security decisions must not rely blindly on intermediary-added headers","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should applications carefully validate forwarded identity and routing headers?","answer":"Headers such as forwarding metadata can be supplied or modified by clients unless a trusted proxy boundary guarantees their provenance. Security decisions based on client-controlled headers can therefore be forged.","verification_method":"Document trusted proxy boundaries and test direct and proxied requests with forged forwarding headers.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP IP address and forwarding-header security guidance","HTTP proxy security principles"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B28-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B28-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"Proxy trust","concept":"Trusted-proxy chain","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What must be defined before an application trusts proxy-derived client information?","answer":"Define which proxies are trusted, how they authenticate to downstream services, which headers they are allowed to set, whether direct access is possible, and how the application handles requests that bypass or violate the expected proxy chain.","verification_method":"Test direct backend access, forged proxy headers, multiple proxy hops, and malformed forwarding metadata.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP proxy/header security guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B28-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B28-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Web Application Security","subtopic":"HTTP parser testing","concept":"Request-framing regression suite","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should request-framing security tests verify?","answer":"Test ambiguous or malformed framing, conflicting length indicators, chunked requests, protocol-version transitions, connection reuse, intermediary/backend combinations, and rejection consistency according to the deployed HTTP stack.","expected_test_result":"All components interpret valid requests consistently and reject ambiguous or invalid framing rather than assigning conflicting request boundaries.","verification_method":"Run controlled protocol-level tests against the complete proxy-to-backend topology.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP HTTP Request Smuggling guidance","HTTP specifications"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B28-R001","D19-B28-R002"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B29-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Input and Parser Security","subtopic":"Parser differentials","concept":"Canonical parser consistency","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security decisions and subsequent processing use compatible canonical interpretations of data?","answer":"If one component validates one representation while another interprets a different representation, validation can be bypassed through encoding, normalization, duplicate fields, alternate separators, or parser differences. Security validation should therefore occur against the representation that will actually be consumed.","verification_method":"Map each transformation and parser between input receipt and security-sensitive use and test equivalent representations for consistent interpretation.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation guidance","Secure parser design principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B06-R003","D19-B28-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B29-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Input and Parser Security","subtopic":"Duplicate parameters","concept":"Duplicate-field ambiguity","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can duplicate request parameters create a security problem?","answer":"Different frameworks, proxies, libraries, and application components may select the first value, last value, all values, or a merged representation. If security validation and business logic choose different values, an attacker may exploit the disagreement.","verification_method":"Test duplicate query, form, header, and structured-data fields through the complete request path and verify consistent handling.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP input-validation principles","HTTP implementation security guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B29-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B29-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Input and Parser Security","subtopic":"Unicode security","concept":"Unicode normalization and confusables","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can Unicode representation affect security decisions?","answer":"Visually similar characters, multiple Unicode representations, normalization behavior, and script mixing can cause identifiers, filenames, domains, or policy values to appear equivalent to humans while remaining distinct to software, or vice versa.","constraints":["Normalization must be appropriate to the protocol and data model.","Do not normalize blindly when the underlying specification requires exact code-point semantics."],"verification_method":"Define the canonical representation for each security-sensitive identifier and test normalization, confusable, and mixed-script cases.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Unicode Standard","UTS #39 Unicode Security Mechanisms"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B29-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B29-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Input and Parser Security","subtopic":"Identifier security","concept":"Security-sensitive identifier comparison","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should security-sensitive identifiers be compared when canonicalization matters?","answer":"The comparison procedure must follow the identifier's governing specification, including case, normalization, encoding, and allowed character rules. Generic string comparison is not automatically correct for every protocol or identifier class.","verification_method":"Document the identifier specification and test canonical-equivalent, non-equivalent, confusable, malformed, and boundary values.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Unicode Standard","Protocol-specific identifier specifications"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B29-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B29-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Input and Parser Security","subtopic":"Parser robustness","concept":"Parser differential regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should parser-security regression tests compare?","answer":"Compare the security gateway, validation layer, parser, business logic, storage layer, and downstream service interpretations for the same logical input. Include alternate encodings, duplicate fields, boundary sizes, malformed structures, normalization variants, and ambiguous representations.","expected_test_result":"Security validation and final business interpretation remain consistent, or ambiguous input is rejected explicitly.","verification_method":"Use canonical test corpora and compare parsed representations across security-sensitive boundaries.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure parser design principles","OWASP input-validation guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B29-R001","D19-B29-R002","D19-B29-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B30-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security testing","concept":"Security regression tests must encode security invariants","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a security regression test more useful than a generic functional test?","answer":"A security regression test explicitly encodes a security property such as unauthorized access must remain impossible, secrets must not appear in logs, invalid cryptographic data must be rejected, or untrusted input must remain data rather than executable syntax.","verification_method":"For every high-risk control, identify the invariant, adversarial input, expected security decision, and evidence captured by the test.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","OWASP testing guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B01-R004"],"supports":["D19-B30-R002","D19-B30-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B30-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security testing","concept":"Negative-path coverage","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should security testing emphasize negative and adversarial paths?","answer":"Security failures often occur when input, identity, state, timing, configuration, or dependency behavior violates assumptions. Testing only successful workflows leaves these boundary conditions insufficiently exercised.","testing_guidance":"For each security control, define valid, invalid, unauthorized, malformed, expired, replayed, concurrent, oversized, and dependency-failure cases where relevant.","verification_method":"Trace each security requirement to at least one adversarial test or justify why adversarial testing is not applicable.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Web Security Testing Guide","NIST SSDF"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B30-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B30-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Fuzzing","concept":"Security-focused fuzzing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What makes fuzzing useful for security engineering?","answer":"Fuzzing systematically supplies varied or malformed inputs to expose parser defects, crashes, unexpected state transitions, resource-exhaustion conditions, and input-handling assumptions that may be missed by hand-written examples.","constraints":["Fuzz targets should have explicit input boundaries and safety controls.","A crash is evidence of a defect, not proof of exploitability.","Coverage and corpus quality should be measured when meaningful rather than fabricated."],"verification_method":"Preserve crashing inputs, minimize reproducible cases, classify root causes, add regression tests, and rerun the target after remediation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Fuzzing guidance","LLVM libFuzzer documentation"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B30-R004","D19-B30-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B30-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Static analysis","concept":"Static-analysis findings require triage","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should static-analysis findings not automatically be treated as confirmed vulnerabilities?","answer":"Static analysis can identify suspicious patterns without executing the full application context. Findings may be true defects, exploitable vulnerabilities, benign patterns, or cases requiring additional analysis. Each finding should therefore retain its evidence and triage state.","verification_method":"Classify findings as confirmed, likely, false positive, not applicable, or unresolved according to documented criteria, preserving the analyzer version and rule identifier.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Static-analysis engineering practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B18-R003","D19-B30-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B30-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security verification evidence","concept":"Security evidence chain","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence chain should connect a security requirement to implementation confidence?","answer":"A strong chain is security requirement → threat or abuse case → security control → implementation location → adversarial test → observed result → verification interpretation → provenance. The chain should preserve uncertainty rather than converting incomplete evidence into a verified claim.","verification_method":"Audit representative high-risk controls end to end and verify that every link is identifiable and consistent.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","D19 dataset governance model"],"relationships":{"governs":["D19-B01-R004","D19-B02-R005","D19-B10-R005","D19-B21-R005","D19-B24-R005","D19-B25-R005","D19-B29-R005"],"supports":["D19-B31-R001"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B31-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Digital Signatures","concept":"Signature authenticity versus confidentiality","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What security property does a digital signature provide?","answer":"A digital signature can provide evidence that a message was signed by the holder of the signing key and that the signed data has not been modified, subject to the signature algorithm, key protection, verification process, and trust model. A signature does not provide confidentiality.","constraints":["The verifier must obtain and trust the correct public key.","The signed representation must be unambiguous.","Private-key protection remains essential."],"verification_method":"Verify signatures over known test vectors, modified messages, incorrect keys, malformed signatures, and ambiguous serialization cases.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Digital Signature Standard","IETF cryptographic protocol specifications"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B08-R001","D19-B21-R001"],"supports":["D19-B31-R002","D19-B31-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B31-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Digital Signatures","concept":"Verify before trusting signed data","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must an application verify a digital signature before acting on signed data?","answer":"The signature is the security evidence establishing authenticity and integrity. Processing a message as trusted before verification can allow attacker-controlled content to influence application behavior before the cryptographic trust decision is complete.","failure_mode":"Application parses, stores, authorizes, or executes signed content before verifying the signature.","root_cause":"Cryptographic verification was treated as a post-processing step instead of a security gate.","solution":"Perform signature verification before security-sensitive use of the protected content.","verification_method":"Inject modified, unsigned, and incorrectly signed messages and verify that security-sensitive processing does not occur before successful verification.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Digital Signature Standard","Secure protocol design principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B31-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B31-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Signature Context","concept":"Domain separation for signed messages","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should security-sensitive signed messages identify their protocol or semantic context?","answer":"If identical bytes can be interpreted as different message types or actions by different components, a valid signature may be reused in an unintended context. Explicit protocol identifiers, message types, versions, or domain-separation data can bind the signature to the intended semantic context.","security_implications":"Without context binding, a signature that is valid for one operation may become dangerous if another operation accepts the same signed representation.","verification_method":"Attempt to replay a valid signature in every supported message context and verify that cross-context reuse is rejected.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Cryptographic protocol domain-separation principles","IETF protocol design practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B31-R001","D19-B31-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B31-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Signature Replay","concept":"Signature validity does not imply freshness","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can a valid digital signature still be replayed?","answer":"A signature proves authenticity and integrity of the signed representation but does not inherently prove that the message is new. If an operation must occur only once or within a specific time or state, the protocol needs an explicit freshness mechanism such as a nonce, sequence number, expiration, or state transition.","testing_guidance":"Replay an otherwise valid signed message and verify that the application enforces the required freshness policy.","verification_method":"Document the freshness invariant and verify that the verifier checks it before performing the protected operation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Cryptographic protocol replay-resistance principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B31-R002","D19-B31-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B31-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Signature Testing","concept":"Signature negative-test matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Which signature failures should be tested?","answer":"Test modified payloads, wrong public keys, malformed signatures, unsupported algorithms, wrong protocol context, replayed messages, expired messages, invalid key status, and serialization differences where applicable.","expected_test_result":"Only signatures satisfying the complete cryptographic and protocol policy are accepted.","verification_method":"Maintain deterministic test vectors and adversarial regression cases for each signature verification boundary.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST cryptographic testing guidance","Cryptographic protocol testing principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B31-R001","D19-B31-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B32-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"OAuth 2.0","concept":"OAuth authorization versus authentication","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should OAuth authorization be distinguished from user authentication?","answer":"OAuth defines mechanisms for delegated authorization to protected resources. Establishing the identity of an end user requires an authentication protocol or profile designed for that purpose, such as OpenID Connect when applicable.","constraints":["Do not infer user identity merely from the presence of an OAuth access token.","The authorization server, resource server, client, and identity provider roles must be explicitly modeled."],"verification_method":"Trace the token's intended audience, scope, issuer, and protocol role before using it for identity or authorization decisions.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 6749","OpenID Connect Core specification"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B03-R001","D19-B04-R001"],"supports":["D19-B32-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B32-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"OAuth 2.0","concept":"Authorization code flow with protected redirect handling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What security properties must an OAuth authorization-code integration preserve?","answer":"The client must use a redirect URI policy consistent with the authorization server, protect the authorization code exchange, use appropriate client authentication or public-client protections, and employ the protocol's recommended anti-interception mechanism such as PKCE where applicable.","constraints":["Exact requirements depend on client type and authorization-server profile.","Redirect URI matching must not permit attacker-controlled destinations."],"verification_method":"Test authorization-code interception, altered redirect URIs, missing or incorrect PKCE values, expired codes, and code reuse.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 6749","RFC 7636","OAuth Security Best Current Practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B32-R001"],"supports":["D19-B32-R003","D19-B32-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B32-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"OAuth 2.0","concept":"Redirect URI integrity","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is redirect URI validation a security boundary in OAuth integrations?","answer":"The authorization response can contain credentials or authorization codes intended for the legitimate client. Redirecting those values to an attacker-controlled endpoint can expose the authorization result.","failure_mode":"Authorization server accepts a loosely matched or attacker-controlled redirect URI.","root_cause":"Redirect URI validation relies on unsafe prefix, substring, wildcard, or dynamic matching.","solution":"Use an explicitly registered redirect URI policy appropriate to the client and authorization-server configuration.","verification_method":"Test exact, altered, encoded, nested, user-info, subdomain, and scheme variations against the configured redirect policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["RFC 6749","OAuth Security Best Current Practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B32-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B32-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"OAuth Tokens","concept":"Token audience restriction","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should a resource server validate the intended audience of a token where the token format and protocol require it?","answer":"A token intended for one resource server should not automatically become a credential for another resource server. Audience restrictions reduce cross-service token substitution and clarify the security boundary represented by the token.","verification_method":"Present a token issued for another resource or audience and verify that the resource server rejects it.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice","JWT specifications where JWT is used"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B32-R001","D19-B32-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B32-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"OAuth Testing","concept":"OAuth trust-chain verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified in an OAuth integration before accepting a protected operation?","answer":"Verify the authorization protocol state, token issuer where applicable, token audience, required scopes or permissions, expiration, client context, redirect integrity, and resource-server authorization policy according to the selected OAuth profile.","verification_method":"Create a trust-chain matrix mapping authorization server → client → token → resource server → resource authorization decision.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OAuth Security Best Current Practice","RFC 6749"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B32-R002","D19-B32-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B33-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"OpenID Connect","concept":"ID token versus access token","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should an OpenID Connect ID token not be treated as a general-purpose API access token?","answer":"An ID token communicates authentication-related claims for the OpenID Connect client, while an access token represents authorization to a resource server. Their intended audiences, processing rules, and security semantics are different.","verification_method":"Trace every token-consuming component and verify that each token type is accepted only in its intended protocol role.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OpenID Connect Core specification"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B32-R001","D19-B32-R004"],"supports":["D19-B33-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B33-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"OpenID Connect","concept":"Issuer and audience validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should OpenID Connect clients validate issuer and audience claims?","answer":"Issuer validation establishes which identity provider issued the identity assertion, while audience validation establishes that the assertion was intended for the relying party. Accepting either value without appropriate validation can allow tokens issued for another trust context to be misused.","verification_method":"Test tokens with incorrect issuer, audience, expiration, nonce, signature, and other required protocol properties.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OpenID Connect Core specification"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B33-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B33-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"OpenID Connect","concept":"Nonce binding","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security purpose does the OpenID Connect nonce provide?","answer":"The nonce binds an authentication request to the corresponding identity assertion and helps the client detect replay or substitution of an assertion from another authentication transaction.","testing_guidance":"Alter, omit, reuse, and cross-request the nonce and verify that the client rejects assertions that do not match the authentication transaction.","verification_method":"Verify that the nonce generated for the authentication request is checked against the received identity assertion before establishing the authenticated session.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OpenID Connect Core specification"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B33-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B33-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"Identity claims","concept":"Identity claim trust boundaries","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should application authorization not blindly trust arbitrary identity claims?","answer":"An identity assertion can contain claims that are authentic but still insufficient for a particular authorization decision. The application must determine which claims are authoritative, current, relevant, and permitted to influence access policy.","verification_method":"Map every authorization-relevant claim to its authoritative issuer, validation rule, freshness requirement, and policy use.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OpenID Connect Core specification","NIST identity guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B24-R002","D19-B33-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B33-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Authentication Security","subtopic":"Federated identity testing","concept":"Federated authentication regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should federated authentication regression tests cover?","answer":"Test issuer mismatch, audience mismatch, signature failure, expired assertions, nonce mismatch, altered claims, authorization-code reuse, redirect mismatch, account-linking ambiguity, and identity-provider failure conditions where applicable.","expected_test_result":"Only assertions satisfying the configured trust relationship and authentication transaction are accepted.","verification_method":"Maintain protocol-level negative tests independent of the identity provider's normal success path.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OpenID Connect Core specification","OAuth Security Best Current Practice"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B33-R002","D19-B33-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B34-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Key Management","concept":"Key separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should cryptographic keys generally be separated by purpose?","answer":"Using one key for unrelated cryptographic purposes can create unintended protocol relationships and make compromise or misuse affect multiple security functions. Purpose-specific keys reduce cross-protocol coupling and simplify lifecycle management.","verification_method":"Inventory cryptographic keys and record each key's algorithm, purpose, scope, owner, lifetime, and permitted operations.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST key-management guidance","Cryptographic protocol design principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B08-R004","D19-B21-R004"],"supports":["D19-B34-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B34-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Key Management","concept":"Key lifecycle state","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should cryptographic keys have explicit lifecycle states?","answer":"Keys may transition through generation, activation, use, suspension, rotation, compromise, revocation, archival, and destruction states. Explicit lifecycle state prevents applications from treating every stored key as equally valid for every operation.","verification_method":"Model permitted operations for each key state and test prohibited use after rotation, revocation, compromise, or destruction.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B08-R004"],"supports":["D19-B34-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B34-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Key rotation","concept":"Key rotation requires compatibility with existing protected data","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why is key rotation more than generating a replacement key?","answer":"Systems must define how newly generated data uses the new key, how previously protected data remains accessible, how key identifiers are represented, how old keys are retired, and how compromised keys are handled. Rotation without lifecycle coordination can cause either data loss or continued use of an unsafe key.","verification_method":"Test new writes, old reads, rotation interruption, restart during rotation, old-key retirement, and compromised-key handling.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B34-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B34-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Key destruction","concept":"Cryptographic deletion semantics","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why does key destruction affect the security of encrypted data?","answer":"If encrypted data remains accessible but the key is securely unavailable, cryptographic protection can become an intentional data-destruction mechanism. However, copies of keys, backups, caches, replicas, and recovery systems must be considered before claiming that key destruction renders all copies inaccessible.","verification_method":"Trace every key replica and recovery path and verify the documented destruction policy.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","Cryptographic key lifecycle principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B05-R003","D19-B34-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B34-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Cryptographic Engineering","subtopic":"Key-management verification","concept":"Key-management invariant matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What invariants should be verified for production cryptographic keys?","answer":"Each key should have an identified purpose, owner, algorithm, permitted operations, lifecycle state, storage boundary, rotation policy, compromise response, recovery policy, and destruction policy. A key must not be accepted outside its authorized purpose or lifecycle state.","verification_method":"Audit representative keys from generation through destruction and verify every lifecycle transition.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B34-R001","D19-B34-R002"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B35-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"Secret Distribution","concept":"Secret exposure surface","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why must secret management include distribution and consumption paths rather than only storage?","answer":"A secret can be protected at rest but exposed while being injected into processes, transferred between services, copied into logs, included in crash artifacts, passed to child processes, or retained in memory beyond its intended lifetime. The complete secret lifecycle is therefore the relevant security boundary.","verification_method":"Trace secret source → distribution mechanism → process → consumer → logs/telemetry → rotation and revocation paths.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST key-management principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B05-R003","D19-B17-R002"],"supports":["D19-B35-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B35-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"Secret rotation","concept":"Rotation must reach every active consumer","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can secret rotation fail even when the secret store has been updated?","answer":"Existing processes, caches, replicas, queues, configuration snapshots, or external systems may continue using the old value. Rotation is complete only when the intended consumers have transitioned according to the security policy and the old credential has been invalidated where required.","verification_method":"Track the secret version through every consumer and verify transition and old-secret invalidation behavior.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B35-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B35-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"Secret leakage","concept":"Secret redaction is not proof of secret safety","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why is log redaction alone insufficient for protecting secrets?","answer":"A secret may leak through structured fields, stack traces, metrics, tracing attributes, exception objects, command arguments, environment inspection, debugging tools, or downstream systems before a redaction filter is applied.","verification_method":"Search all observability and diagnostic paths for known test secrets and verify that sensitive values do not leave the intended security boundary.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B05-R005","D19-B10-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B35-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"Credential revocation","concept":"Rotation and revocation are distinct operations","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must secret rotation and revocation be modeled separately?","answer":"Rotation introduces a replacement credential according to a planned lifecycle, while revocation invalidates a credential because it should no longer be trusted. A compromised credential may require immediate revocation even if normal rotation is not scheduled.","verification_method":"Test compromised-secret response independently from normal scheduled rotation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance","NIST credential lifecycle principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B35-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B35-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Secrets Management","subtopic":"Secret lifecycle testing","concept":"Secret lifecycle regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should secret lifecycle testing cover?","answer":"Test creation, distribution, normal consumption, rotation, failed rotation, consumer restart, stale cache, compromise response, revocation, recovery, logging, backup, and destruction according to the secret's security policy.","expected_test_result":"Secrets remain inaccessible outside authorized consumers and obsolete or revoked credentials behave according to policy.","verification_method":"Use non-production test credentials and preserve lifecycle evidence without exposing the secret values themselves.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B35-R001","D19-B35-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B36-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Regular Expression Security","concept":"Regular-expression denial of service","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can regular expressions create denial-of-service risk?","answer":"Some regular-expression engines and patterns can require excessive computation for specially constructed inputs. A security-sensitive parser that applies such a pattern to attacker-controlled input can become a resource-exhaustion target.","constraints":["Risk depends on the specific regex engine and pattern.","Input limits alone may not eliminate pathological matching behavior."],"verification_method":"Analyze expressions for problematic backtracking structures and test adversarial inputs under resource limits.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Regular Expression Denial of Service guidance","CWE-1333"],"relationships":{"supports":["D19-B36-R002","D19-B36-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B36-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Regular Expression Security","concept":"Bounded parsing","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How can applications reduce parser and regex resource-exhaustion risk?","answer":"Apply appropriate input-size limits, computational limits where the runtime supports them, safer parsing algorithms, constrained patterns, and explicit rejection of inputs outside the supported grammar. Resource controls should be matched to the actual parser behavior.","verification_method":"Test maximum supported inputs, adversarial inputs, malformed structures, and repeated parsing under controlled concurrency.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Regular Expression Denial of Service guidance","CWE-1333"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B06-R004","D19-B36-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B36-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion","concept":"Algorithmic complexity as a security property","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can algorithmic complexity become a security concern for attacker-controlled input?","answer":"An attacker may choose input that drives an algorithm toward its worst-case time or memory behavior. If the cost grows sufficiently with input size, an apparently correct algorithm can become a denial-of-service vector.","verification_method":"Identify attacker-controlled inputs, derive or measure relevant complexity where practical, and enforce appropriate bounds.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE resource-exhaustion categories","Algorithmic complexity principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B14-R004","D19-B36-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B36-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion","concept":"Per-request resource budgets","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should security-sensitive request processing have explicit resource budgets?","answer":"A request can consume CPU, memory, storage, connections, subprocesses, parser depth, queue capacity, or downstream calls. Explicit budgets limit how much one request can consume and provide a deterministic failure boundary.","tradeoff":"Overly aggressive limits can reject legitimate workloads; limits should therefore be derived from documented functional requirements and tested under representative conditions.","verification_method":"Exercise requests at, below, and above each documented resource limit and verify predictable rejection without cross-request resource leakage.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP denial-of-service guidance","Secure resource-management principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B17-R003","D19-B36-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B36-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Secure Coding","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion","concept":"Resource-exhaustion regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should security regression tests measure for resource exhaustion?","answer":"Test maximum input size, nesting depth, parser work, concurrent requests, memory allocation, file descriptors, subprocess count, queue growth, and downstream call amplification where applicable.","expected_test_result":"The system rejects or throttles excessive work within documented resource boundaries without allowing one request to destabilize unrelated security-critical operations.","verification_method":"Use controlled load and fault injection; label any measured performance result with its actual environment and methodology.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP denial-of-service guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B36-R003","D19-B36-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B37-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant identity must be server-derived","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should multi-tenant systems avoid trusting client-supplied tenant identifiers for authorization?","answer":"A client can modify any field it controls. The effective tenant context must be derived from authenticated identity and authorized tenancy relationships, then enforced at the resource boundary.","invalid_example":"tenant_id = request.json['tenant_id']; query_resources(tenant_id)","corrected_pattern":"Resolve the caller's authorized tenant context from trusted identity and policy, then constrain the resource query to that context.","testing_guidance":"Attempt cross-tenant object identifiers, altered tenant fields, and mixed-tenant batch operations.","verification_method":"Prove that changing client-controlled tenant identifiers cannot change the caller's authorized tenant scope.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","NIST access-control principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B04-R004","D19-B14-R001"],"supports":["D19-B37-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B37-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant isolation must cover indirect data paths","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is direct database filtering insufficient as the complete tenant-isolation model?","answer":"Cross-tenant exposure can occur through caches, search indexes, object storage, logs, asynchronous jobs, analytics, exports, background workers, shared queues, or derived data even when primary database queries filter tenant identifiers correctly.","verification_method":"Map every persistence, cache, asynchronous, export, search, and observability path that can contain tenant-scoped data.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST access-control principles","Multi-tenant security architecture practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B37-R001","D19-B10-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B37-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant context propagation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should tenant context be propagated explicitly across internal service boundaries?","answer":"A downstream component cannot safely infer tenancy from an ambiguous request or global process state. Explicitly authenticated and authorized tenant context allows each service to apply its own resource-level isolation policy.","constraints":["Propagation does not replace downstream authorization.","Tenant context must be integrity-protected across trust boundaries."],"verification_method":"Test missing, altered, conflicting, and stale tenant context across service boundaries.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST zero-trust architecture principles","Multi-tenant authorization practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B24-R004","D19-B37-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B37-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Cross-tenant negative testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a multi-tenant security regression matrix cover?","answer":"Test direct object access, list queries, search, exports, file retrieval, caches, asynchronous jobs, batch operations, administrative functions, analytics, and error responses across authorized and unauthorized tenant contexts.","expected_test_result":"No tested path exposes or modifies another tenant's protected data unless explicitly authorized by a documented administrative policy.","verification_method":"Generate tenant-pair tests and verify isolation at every data access boundary.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","NIST access-control principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B37-R002","D19-B37-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B37-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Tenant Isolation","concept":"Tenant isolation evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is required to claim tenant isolation?","answer":"Evidence should connect tenant identity derivation, authorization policy, resource queries, service-boundary propagation, secondary data stores, caches, asynchronous workflows, exports, and adversarial cross-tenant tests. A database schema alone is not sufficient evidence.","verification_method":"Perform an end-to-end tenant-data lineage audit and preserve test results for every identified boundary.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST zero-trust principles","Multi-tenant security engineering practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B37-R001","D19-B37-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B38-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Logging","subtopic":"Audit Logging","concept":"Security audit events versus application logs","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security audit records be distinguished from ordinary diagnostic logs?","answer":"Security audit records need durable, structured evidence about security-relevant actions, identities, policy decisions, and state transitions. Diagnostic logs primarily support troubleshooting and may have different retention, integrity, and privacy requirements.","verification_method":"Inventory security-relevant events and verify that each has an appropriate audit record independent of optional debugging verbosity.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","NIST audit and accountability guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B10-R004","D19-B24-R003"],"supports":["D19-B38-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B38-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Logging","subtopic":"Audit Integrity","concept":"Audit-log tamper resistance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security audit records be protected against unauthorized modification?","answer":"If an attacker can freely modify or delete audit evidence after compromising a system, investigators may lose the ability to reconstruct security events. Integrity protection can include restricted write paths, centralized collection, append-oriented storage, access controls, and other mechanisms appropriate to the threat model.","constraints":["No single logging control guarantees tamper resistance against every privileged compromise.","Retention and privacy requirements must also be considered."],"verification_method":"Test unauthorized modification, deletion, replay, timestamp alteration, and access-control failures against the audit pipeline.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST audit and accountability guidance","OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B38-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B38-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Logging","subtopic":"Sensitive Data","concept":"Auditability without secret disclosure","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should security logs provide useful evidence without recording secrets?","answer":"Logs should record security-relevant identifiers, event types, policy decisions, and outcome metadata while excluding passwords, private keys, session credentials, bearer tokens, and other secrets. Sensitive values should be represented by controlled identifiers or non-reversible metadata where appropriate.","verification_method":"Inject known test secrets through authentication, authorization, error, and audit paths and search every logging destination for leakage.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B05-R005","D19-B35-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B38-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Logging","subtopic":"Event correlation","concept":"Security event correlation identifiers","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should security events carry correlation information?","answer":"Correlation identifiers allow events from authentication, authorization, application, proxy, and downstream services to be related to the same operation or security investigation without requiring sensitive payloads to be copied into every record.","constraints":["Correlation identifiers must not themselves become authorization credentials or contain sensitive information.","Identifier uniqueness and propagation requirements should be documented."],"verification_method":"Trace a controlled security event across service boundaries and verify consistent correlation without secret propagation.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","Distributed security observability principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B38-R001","D19-B38-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B38-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Security Logging","subtopic":"Audit verification","concept":"Security audit completeness matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can security audit coverage be verified systematically?","answer":"Map each security-sensitive state transition and administrative action to an expected audit event, required fields, actor identity, target resource, result, timestamp, correlation context, and retention policy. Test both successful and rejected actions.","verification_method":"Compare the audit matrix with implementation paths and execute representative allow, deny, error, and recovery scenarios.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST audit and accountability guidance","OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B38-R001","D19-B38-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B39-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Secure Configuration","concept":"Security configuration as controlled input","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security configuration be treated as a security-sensitive input?","answer":"Authentication modes, authorization policies, TLS settings, trust stores, allowed origins, debug modes, network exposure, secret locations, and feature flags can materially change security behavior. Configuration therefore requires validation, ownership, version control, and deployment verification.","verification_method":"Inventory security-sensitive configuration and verify that unsafe values are rejected or explicitly controlled.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Secure Software Development Framework","OWASP secure configuration guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B01-R002","D19-B13-R002"],"supports":["D19-B39-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B39-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Secure Configuration","concept":"Fail-closed security configuration","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should a missing or invalid security configuration cause startup or operation to fail?","answer":"When an omitted or malformed value would weaken a security boundary, silently selecting an insecure default can be unsafe. The application should reject invalid security-critical configuration or enter a documented safe state rather than silently weakening protection.","failure_mode":"Missing security configuration silently activates permissive authentication, authorization, TLS, network, or secret behavior.","verification_method":"Remove or corrupt each security-critical configuration value and verify the documented safe failure behavior.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure configuration principles","OWASP secure configuration guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B10-R002","D19-B39-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B39-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Debug Features","concept":"Production debug controls","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can development and debug features become security vulnerabilities in production?","answer":"Debug modes may expose stack traces, internal state, administrative endpoints, interactive consoles, verbose errors, test credentials, or relaxed validation. Development conveniences must therefore be explicitly controlled and disabled or restricted in production according to the deployment threat model.","verification_method":"Enumerate debug and development features and test production-like deployments for their availability and access controls.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Configuration Cheat Sheet","OWASP Error Handling guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B10-R001","D19-B39-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B39-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Configuration Drift","concept":"Security configuration drift","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can configuration drift create a security vulnerability?","answer":"Different environments, replicas, regions, containers, or services can silently diverge in security-sensitive configuration. A control that is correct in one instance may therefore be absent or weakened elsewhere.","root_cause":"Security configuration is not versioned, validated, compared, or continuously checked against the approved state.","solution":"Represent security-sensitive configuration as controlled, versioned state and validate deployed instances against the expected policy.","verification_method":"Compare configuration across representative instances and intentionally introduce controlled drift to verify detection.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST configuration-management principles","Secure deployment practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B39-R001","D19-B39-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B39-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Configuration Testing","concept":"Security configuration regression gate","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a security configuration regression gate verify?","answer":"Verify authentication settings, authorization defaults, TLS verification, trusted issuers, secret sources, allowed network exposure, browser security policy, debug state, dependency sources, filesystem permissions, and other security-critical deployment settings relevant to the application.","expected_test_result":"Every deployed environment satisfies the approved security configuration policy or produces an explicit controlled exception.","verification_method":"Run automated configuration checks against deployment artifacts and representative running instances.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Secure Software Development Framework","OWASP secure configuration guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B39-R001","D19-B39-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B40-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Assurance","concept":"Security requirement traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a security requirement be connected to implementation and evidence?","answer":"Each significant security requirement should trace to its threat or security property, design control, implementation boundary, negative test, verification evidence, and operational owner. A requirement without an implementation or verification path should remain unresolved rather than being treated as satisfied.","verification_method":"Perform bidirectional traceability from requirement to evidence and from security control back to the requirement it satisfies.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Systems security assurance practices"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B02-R005","D19-B30-R005"],"supports":["D19-B40-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B40-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Assurance","concept":"Security evidence levels","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security knowledge distinguish design evidence from execution evidence?","answer":"A design review can show that a control is intended, while execution evidence can demonstrate that a specific implementation behaved as expected under a particular test. Neither should be silently substituted for the other.","constraints":["Execution evidence must identify the environment and test scope.","A passing test does not prove universal security correctness.","Illustrative code is not execution evidence."],"verification_method":"Classify each security record by evidence type and reject claims whose evidence level does not support the stated conclusion.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","D19 dataset governance model"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B30-R004","D19-B40-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B40-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Assurance","concept":"Security control independence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an assurance review determine whether multiple controls actually provide independent defense?","answer":"For each control, identify shared dependencies such as identity systems, keys, libraries, configuration, network paths, administrators, parsers, and data sources. Controls sharing the same failure mode should not be counted as fully independent layers.","verification_method":"Construct a control-dependency graph and identify common-mode failure nodes.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Defense-in-depth principles","Security architecture assurance practices"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B20-R004"],"supports":["D19-B40-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B40-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Assurance","concept":"Residual risk after control verification","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does successful verification of a security control not eliminate all security risk?","answer":"A test establishes evidence about the tested behavior and scope. Unobserved paths, environmental differences, new attack techniques, configuration changes, dependency changes, and incorrect threat assumptions can leave residual risk.","verification_method":"Record tested scope, assumptions, limitations, untested paths, and remaining uncertainty alongside the verification result.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST risk-management and assurance principles","NIST SSDF"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B40-R002","D19-B40-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B40-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"D19 Phase Gate","concept":"Security engineering foundation gate after B40","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must remain true after D19 B31-B40 before deeper security coverage continues?","answer":"The dataset must preserve explicit cryptographic trust boundaries, authentication protocol distinctions, key lifecycle semantics, secret-distribution controls, parser and resource-exhaustion defenses, tenant isolation, security audit evidence, secure configuration controls, and evidence-based security assurance. These records extend B1-B30 rather than replacing them.","coverage_requirements":["Digital signatures and replay resistance","OAuth and federated authentication security","Key separation and lifecycle","Secret distribution and revocation","Parser and regex resource security","Tenant isolation","Security audit evidence","Secure configuration","Security requirement traceability","Evidence classification and residual risk"],"status":"NOT_LOCKED","reason":"B31-B40 completes another major D19 layer but does not close the dataset. Remaining D19 work should deepen secure implementation patterns across target languages, XML and serialization security, filesystem/process hardening, vulnerability-specific implementation cases, dependency and build integrity, security testing techniques, fuzzing, static/dynamic analysis, formal/security properties, and cross-language secure coding coverage without duplicating established records.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19 Security Engineering scope","Autonomous Software Engineering Brain quality-first dataset governance"],"relationships":{"governs":["D19-B31-R001","D19-B32-R001","D19-B33-R001","D19-B34-R001","D19-B35-R001","D19-B36-R001","D19-B37-R001","D19-B38-R001","D19-B39-R001"],"su