{"record_id":"D19-B41-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Parser Security","subtopic":"XML Security","concept":"XML external entity processing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can XML external entity processing create a security vulnerability?","answer":"If an XML parser resolves attacker-controlled external entities, parsing untrusted XML may cause unintended local-file access, network requests, resource consumption, or information disclosure. Applications that do not require external entities should disable the relevant external-resource behavior.","failure_mode":"Untrusted XML is parsed with external entity resolution enabled.","root_cause":"Parser security features are left at permissive defaults or are not explicitly configured.","solution":"Use a parser configuration appropriate to the trust boundary and disable unnecessary external entity and external resource processing.","verification_method":"Submit XML containing external entity declarations and verify that prohibited external resources are neither accessed nor returned.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP XML Security guidance","CWE-611"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B09-R004","D19-B06-R003"],"supports":["D19-B41-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B41-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Parser Security","subtopic":"XML Security","concept":"XML parser feature allowlisting","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should XML parser capabilities be explicitly selected instead of relying on defaults?","answer":"XML parsers can expose features such as external entities, DTD processing, external schemas, XInclude, expansion, and entity substitution. The secure configuration depends on which capabilities the application actually needs.","constraints":["Parser APIs differ by language and library.","A setting that disables one feature may not disable another related resource mechanism."],"verification_method":"Document every enabled XML feature and test prohibited parser behaviors independently.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP XML Security guidance","Language-specific XML parser documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B41-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B41-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Parser Security","subtopic":"XML Security","concept":"XML entity expansion resource exhaustion","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can XML entity expansion cause resource exhaustion?","answer":"Nested or repeated entity expansion can cause the parser to consume excessive CPU or memory when processing comparatively small input. This is a parser resource-security problem even when external entity access is disabled.","verification_method":"Use bounded adversarial XML inputs and verify parser resource limits and deterministic rejection behavior.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP XML Security guidance","CWE-776"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B36-R003","D19-B41-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B41-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Parser Security","subtopic":"XML Security","concept":"XML schema validation versus parser safety","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Does XML schema validation by itself make XML parsing safe?","answer":"No. Schema validation constrains document structure and values according to the selected schema, but parser-level behaviors such as external entity processing, resource access, expansion, and resource limits remain separate security concerns.","verification_method":"Test parser security features independently from schema-valid and schema-invalid documents.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP XML Security guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B09-R003","D19-B41-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B41-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Parser Security","subtopic":"XML Security","concept":"XML parser security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an XML security regression suite test?","answer":"Test external entity declarations, DTD processing, entity expansion, external schemas, XInclude where supported, deeply nested structures, oversized documents, malformed encodings, schema violations, and resource-limit boundaries.","expected_test_result":"Unsupported or unsafe parser behaviors are rejected without unintended file access, network access, excessive resource consumption, or sensitive error disclosure.","verification_method":"Run parser-specific adversarial fixtures against every supported parser configuration.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP XML Security guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B41-R001","D19-B41-R003","D19-B41-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B42-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Archive Extraction","concept":"Archive path traversal","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can extracting an untrusted archive be dangerous?","answer":"Archive entries may contain absolute paths or traversal components that cause extracted files to escape the intended destination directory. Archive extraction therefore requires path validation before writing files.","failure_mode":"Archive member names are joined directly with the extraction directory and written without containment verification.","root_cause":"The application trusts archive metadata as safe filesystem paths.","solution":"Resolve each destination path under a controlled extraction root and reject entries that escape that root or violate the application's filename policy.","verification_method":"Test absolute paths, parent traversal, encoded traversal, platform-specific path forms, symlinks, and nested archive structures.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","CWE-22"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B07-R004","D19-B16-R002"],"supports":["D19-B42-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B42-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Archive Extraction","concept":"Archive extraction containment","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What invariant should secure archive extraction preserve?","answer":"Every extracted destination must remain inside the designated extraction root and must satisfy the application's allowed-file policy before creation or replacement.","constraints":["Path normalization must account for the target operating system and filesystem semantics.","Symlinks and hard links require separate handling."],"verification_method":"Generate adversarial archive names and verify that every resulting filesystem operation remains within the intended root.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","CWE-22"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B42-R001","D19-B16-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B42-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Archive Extraction","concept":"Archive bomb resource exhaustion","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can a valid archive become a resource-exhaustion attack?","answer":"An archive can contain highly compressed or extremely numerous entries that expand into excessive disk space, memory, CPU work, or file-system metadata operations. Archive security therefore requires resource limits in addition to path validation.","verification_method":"Test compressed-size-to-expanded-size ratios, entry counts, nesting, filename lengths, and total extraction limits.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","Archive resource-exhaustion security principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B36-R003","D19-B42-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B42-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Archive Extraction","concept":"Archive link handling","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must symbolic and hard links in untrusted archives be handled explicitly?","answer":"A link entry can cause the apparent extraction path to refer to a different filesystem location or existing inode. Validating only the textual archive path is therefore insufficient when link semantics can alter the final write target.","verification_method":"Test symbolic links, hard links, link chains, existing destination files, and link targets outside the extraction root.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet","Filesystem security principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B16-R005","D19-B42-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B42-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Archive Extraction","concept":"Archive security verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is required before treating an archive extraction component as security-safe?","answer":"Evidence should cover destination containment, link handling, overwrite policy, file-type restrictions, total extracted size, entry count, nesting limits, failure cleanup, partial extraction behavior, and concurrent extraction behavior.","verification_method":"Map each archive security invariant to adversarial tests and inspect the implementation for every filesystem operation performed during extraction.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP File Upload Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B42-R001","D19-B42-R003","D19-B42-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B43-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Path Canonicalization","concept":"Canonical path versus lexical path","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is lexical path normalization not always equivalent to filesystem containment?","answer":"A lexical path operation reasons about path text, while the filesystem may resolve symbolic links, mount points, junctions, case behavior, or other platform-specific semantics. Security decisions involving real filesystem objects must account for the actual filesystem resolution model.","verification_method":"Test symlinks, case variations, mount boundaries where applicable, relative paths, and concurrent filesystem changes.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-22","OWASP Path Traversal guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B07-R004","D19-B16-R005"],"supports":["D19-B43-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B43-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Path Canonicalization","concept":"Canonicalization before authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should filesystem authorization be applied to the canonical security-relevant interpretation of a path?","answer":"Different path representations can identify the same filesystem object. If authorization evaluates one representation while the filesystem operates on another, an attacker may exploit the mismatch to bypass the intended boundary.","verification_method":"Test alternate encodings, separators, traversal components, symlinks, and platform-specific path representations.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-22","OWASP Path Traversal guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B43-R001","D19-B06-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B43-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"TOCTOU","concept":"Filesystem check-use race","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can checking a filesystem path before using it be unsafe?","answer":"Between the security check and the filesystem operation, another process or attacker may alter the referenced object. The object used after the check may therefore differ from the object that was authorized.","root_cause":"Security authorization and filesystem use are separated by an attacker-observable race window.","solution":"Use operating-system primitives and APIs that bind validation and operation where possible, and minimize race-sensitive path workflows.","verification_method":"Introduce controlled filesystem changes between check and use and verify that the protected operation cannot be redirected.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-367","Operating-system filesystem security principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B25-R002","D19-B16-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B43-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Filesystem APIs","concept":"Descriptor-based security","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can operating on already-authorized filesystem handles be safer than repeatedly resolving attacker-controlled paths?","answer":"A handle or descriptor can represent a filesystem object that has already passed the relevant authorization decision, reducing some path-reinterpretation and race opportunities. Exact guarantees depend on the operating system and API.","constraints":["APIs and guarantees differ across operating systems and languages.","Handle-based access does not automatically establish authorization."],"verification_method":"Review the complete path-resolution and handle-lifetime model for the target operating system.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Operating-system filesystem security documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B43-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B43-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Filesystem Security","subtopic":"Filesystem Assurance","concept":"Filesystem race regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should filesystem authorization race conditions be tested?","answer":"Run concurrent operations that change file targets, links, permissions, and directory structure while security-sensitive file operations execute. The test must distinguish deterministic protection from timing-dependent success.","expected_test_result":"Unauthorized filesystem targets are never operated on even when filesystem state changes during the security-sensitive operation.","verification_method":"Use controlled concurrency and platform-specific filesystem primitives; document any limitations in reproducing races.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-367","Filesystem concurrency testing principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B43-R003","D19-B43-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B44-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"JavaScript and TypeScript","concept":"Prototype pollution","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can unsafe object-property manipulation create prototype-pollution risk in JavaScript?","answer":"Applications that merge or assign attacker-controlled property paths into ordinary JavaScript objects can unintentionally modify inherited object behavior or shared prototypes. The risk depends on the operation, runtime, libraries, and property names accepted.","failure_mode":"Untrusted nested property paths are recursively merged into objects without restricting dangerous property paths or object semantics.","root_cause":"Application treats arbitrary object paths as harmless data.","solution":"Use schema-constrained data structures, explicit property allowlists, safe merge mechanisms, and library versions with appropriate security fixes.","verification_method":"Test attacker-controlled nested keys and prototype-sensitive property paths against every object merge or path-assignment boundary.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Prototype Pollution Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-1321"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B06-R005","D19-B09-R003"],"supports":["D19-B44-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B44-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"JavaScript and TypeScript","concept":"Schema validation versus object safety","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Does TypeScript typing or JSON schema validation alone prevent prototype pollution?","answer":"No. Static types and schema validation can constrain expected data but do not automatically make every runtime object operation safe. Security depends on the actual runtime merge, assignment, deserialization, and object-construction behavior.","verification_method":"Test runtime operations independently from compile-time type checking and schema validation.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Prototype Pollution Prevention Cheat Sheet","TypeScript runtime semantics"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B44-R001","D19-B09-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B44-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"JavaScript and TypeScript","concept":"Prototype-sensitive property allowlisting","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is explicit property allowlisting useful for security-sensitive JavaScript objects?","answer":"Allowlisting limits the set of properties an untrusted input may modify and prevents unexpected object paths from becoming executable or security-sensitive state. It also makes the accepted data contract explicit.","verification_method":"Submit unknown, nested, inherited, and prototype-sensitive properties and verify deterministic rejection.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Prototype Pollution Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B44-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B44-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"JavaScript and TypeScript","concept":"Dynamic property access security","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can dynamically constructing property paths from user input be dangerous?","answer":"A dynamic property path can cross object boundaries or modify security-sensitive fields that were not intended to be writable. The risk increases when recursive setters or generic merge utilities are used without an explicit data model.","verification_method":"Trace every user-controlled property path to its final object operation and verify that the target properties are authorized.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Prototype Pollution Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B44-R001","D19-B44-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B44-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"JavaScript and TypeScript","concept":"Prototype-pollution regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should prototype-pollution security tests cover?","answer":"Test dangerous property names, nested paths, recursive merges, array/object transitions, inherited properties, object construction, serialization/deserialization boundaries, and security-sensitive configuration objects.","expected_test_result":"Untrusted data cannot modify shared object prototypes or unauthorized security-sensitive properties.","verification_method":"Run the same adversarial fixtures through every supported parser, merge utility, and object-construction path.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Prototype Pollution Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-1321"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B44-R001","D19-B44-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B45-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"C and C++","concept":"Memory lifetime validity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is memory lifetime a security boundary in C and C++?","answer":"Using an object after its lifetime has ended can produce undefined behavior and may enable memory corruption or information disclosure. Pointer validity therefore must be reasoned about together with allocation, ownership, lifetime, and synchronization.","verification_method":"Use compiler diagnostics, sanitizers where applicable, static analysis, targeted tests, and ownership/lifetime review.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["C and C++ language specifications","CWE memory-safety weakness classes"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B19-R001","D19-B19-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B45-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"C and C++","concept":"Integer conversion security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can signedness and integer conversion create security vulnerabilities?","answer":"Conversions between signed and unsigned types or between integer widths can change numeric interpretation or truncate values. When the resulting value controls buffer sizes, indexes, allocation, lengths, or security limits, the conversion can become a security boundary failure.","verification_method":"Test boundary values, negative inputs, maximum representable values, narrowing conversions, and arithmetic overflow paths.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["C and C++ language specifications","CWE-190","CWE-681"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B19-R003","D19-B36-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B45-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"C and C++","concept":"Unsafe memory API boundaries","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why are unchecked buffer and string operations security-sensitive in C and C++?","answer":"Operations that do not enforce destination capacity can write beyond allocated memory when inputs exceed assumptions. The resulting corruption can affect control data, adjacent objects, or security state.","verification_method":"Review buffer-size derivation and use dynamic analysis or compiler-supported instrumentation to exercise boundary conditions.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE-120","CWE-787"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B19-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B45-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"C and C++","concept":"RAII and cleanup correctness","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can deterministic resource ownership reduce security failures in C++?","answer":"RAII ties resource acquisition and release to object lifetime, reducing some cleanup omissions for memory, locks, files, and other resources. It does not automatically establish correct authorization or prevent all lifetime and concurrency errors.","tradeoff":"RAII is a resource-management technique, not a complete security model.","verification_method":"Test exceptional control flow and early returns to ensure security-sensitive resources are released or transitioned correctly.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["C++ language/library specifications","C++ Core Guidelines"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B25-R004","D19-B45-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B45-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"C and C++","concept":"Memory-safety assurance matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What complementary evidence should be used for security-sensitive C and C++ code?","answer":"Use multiple evidence sources such as compiler diagnostics, static analysis, sanitizers, unit tests, fuzzing, code review, ownership/lifetime reasoning, and targeted boundary tests. No single technique detects every memory-safety defect.","verification_method":"Map each relevant memory-safety invariant to at least one static or dynamic detection method and document remaining blind spots.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["CWE memory-safety guidance","C++ Core Guidelines","Compiler sanitizer documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B45-R001","D19-B45-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B46-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"Python","concept":"Dynamic typing does not replace runtime validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must Python applications validate untrusted values at runtime?","answer":"Python type annotations do not by themselves enforce runtime input validity. Network payloads, configuration, files, environment values, and plugin inputs remain untrusted until explicitly validated and constrained.","verification_method":"Pass incorrect types, unexpected structures, boundary values, and malicious strings through public input boundaries.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Python language documentation","OWASP input-validation guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B06-R001","D19-B44-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B46-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"Java","concept":"Type safety versus security validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why does Java's type system not eliminate security input-validation requirements?","answer":"Java type correctness does not establish that a value is authorized, semantically valid, bounded, canonicalized, or safe for a particular interpreter or resource boundary. Security validation remains necessary at trust boundaries.","verification_method":"Test semantically invalid but type-correct values at network, file, authorization, and interpreter boundaries.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Java language specification","OWASP input-validation guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B06-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B46-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"Rust","concept":"Rust memory safety does not imply application security","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does Rust memory safety not eliminate security engineering requirements?","answer":"Rust's ownership and type mechanisms address important classes of memory and concurrency errors, but applications can still contain authentication, authorization, cryptographic, logic, parser, resource, configuration, and protocol vulnerabilities.","verification_method":"Evaluate Rust components against application-level security requirements independently of compiler safety guarantees.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Rust language documentation","Rustonomicon","OWASP secure-design principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B19-R001","D19-B01-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B46-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"Go","concept":"Go memory safety versus concurrency and authorization safety","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does Go's managed memory model not eliminate security vulnerabilities?","answer":"Garbage collection reduces certain memory-management errors, but race conditions, incorrect authorization, unsafe subprocess use, parser/resource exhaustion, insecure configuration, and application logic flaws remain possible.","verification_method":"Combine race detection and language tooling with explicit application security tests and authorization verification.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Go language documentation","Go race detector documentation","OWASP secure-design principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B25-R001","D19-B17-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B46-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Language Security","subtopic":"Cross-Language Security","concept":"Language guarantee versus application invariant","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should the software engineering brain distinguish language guarantees from application security invariants?","answer":"Language guarantees describe properties enforced by a language or runtime, while application security invariants describe what the system must preserve across business logic, protocols, data, identity, resources, and deployment. Strong language guarantees can reduce some failure classes but cannot substitute for application-level security requirements.","verification_method":"For each security requirement, identify whether it is guaranteed by the language, enforced by libraries, implemented by application logic, or dependent on deployment configuration.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Language specifications for Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Java, C, C++, Rust, Go","Secure software engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B46-R001","D19-B46-R002","D19-B46-R003","D19-B46-R004"],"supports":["D19-B46-R005"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B47-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Software Supply Chain Security","subtopic":"Dependency Integrity","concept":"Dependency lock versus dependency trust","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Does a lockfile prove that a dependency is trustworthy?","answer":"No. A lockfile can make dependency resolution more reproducible by recording selected versions or artifacts, but it does not prove that the dependency itself is benign, uncompromised, correctly maintained, or appropriate for the application's threat model.","verification_method":"Separate dependency identity, artifact integrity, provenance, vulnerability status, maintainer trust, and application usage during supply-chain review.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Secure Software Development Framework","OWASP Software Supply Chain Security guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B18-R002","D19-B18-R005"],"supports":["D19-B47-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B47-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Software Supply Chain Security","subtopic":"Build Integrity","concept":"Dependency resolution versus artifact verification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should dependency selection and artifact integrity be treated as separate security checks?","answer":"Selecting an intended package version does not by itself prove that the downloaded artifact corresponds to the expected source or publisher output. Artifact integrity mechanisms can provide additional evidence about what was actually consumed.","verification_method":"Verify package identity, expected artifact integrity metadata, source/release relationship, and repository configuration according to the ecosystem.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","SLSA framework concepts","Language ecosystem package-management documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B47-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B47-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Software Supply Chain Security","subtopic":"Build Integrity","concept":"Reproducible build evidence","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What security value can reproducible builds provide?","answer":"When a build can be reproduced from controlled inputs and produces equivalent artifacts, independent parties can compare outputs and investigate unexpected changes. Reproducibility does not by itself prove that the source or build process is secure.","verification_method":"Rebuild a controlled release using documented inputs and compare the resulting artifact according to the project's reproducibility definition.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Reproducible Builds project","NIST SSDF"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B47-R002","D19-B47-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B47-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Software Supply Chain Security","subtopic":"Build Isolation","concept":"Build environment trust boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should build environments be treated as security boundaries?","answer":"A compromised build environment can alter source processing, dependencies, generated files, artifacts, signing operations, or release metadata. Build isolation and controlled credentials reduce the ability of an unrelated compromise to modify trusted outputs.","verification_method":"Inventory build credentials, network access, mutable inputs, plugins, generated artifacts, signing operations, and release permissions.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","SLSA framework concepts"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B47-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B47-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Software Supply Chain Security","subtopic":"Build Assurance","concept":"Software supply-chain evidence chain","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a trustworthy software supply-chain evidence chain connect?","answer":"The evidence chain should connect source revision, dependency inputs, build environment, build process, generated artifact, testing evidence, signing or release metadata, and deployment identity. Missing links should remain explicit rather than being inferred as trusted.","verification_method":"Construct a provenance graph and verify that every production artifact has traceable inputs and documented trust boundaries.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","SLSA framework concepts"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B47-R001","D19-B47-R003","D19-B47-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B48-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Static Analysis","concept":"Taint analysis","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What security problem does taint analysis attempt to detect?","answer":"Taint analysis tracks data originating from designated untrusted sources through program transformations and checks whether it reaches security-sensitive sinks without an appropriate sanitization or validation boundary.","constraints":["Analysis quality depends on source/sink modeling, language semantics, interprocedural coverage, and framework knowledge.","A taint finding is evidence for investigation, not automatic proof of exploitability."],"verification_method":"Use controlled source-to-sink test cases and compare analyzer findings with manually established dataflow.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Code Review guidance","Static-analysis dataflow principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B30-R004","D19-B07-R005"],"supports":["D19-B48-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B48-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Static Analysis","concept":"Security source and sink modeling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why must security static analysis model framework-specific sources and sinks?","answer":"A generic analyzer may not recognize application-specific request inputs, deserializers, authorization boundaries, database APIs, command execution functions, template engines, or sensitive data sinks. Accurate models materially affect detection coverage.","verification_method":"Create representative source-to-sink fixtures for each major framework and verify analyzer recognition.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP static-analysis guidance","Static-analysis framework documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B48-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B48-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Static Analysis","concept":"False-positive triage","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security static-analysis findings be triaged instead of blindly suppressed?","answer":"A finding may be a true vulnerability, a valid but unreachable path, a safe use not understood by the analyzer, or a modeling limitation. Suppressing findings without preserving rationale can permanently hide recurring security defects.","verification_method":"Classify findings, document justification, retain suppression scope, and periodically review suppression rules for drift.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP static-analysis guidance","NIST SSDF"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B30-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B48-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Static Analysis","concept":"Path-sensitive security analysis","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can path sensitivity matter for security static analysis?","answer":"A security flaw may occur only when several conditions hold simultaneously, such as authentication state, user-controlled input, configuration, or error paths. Analyses that reason about control-flow conditions can reduce both missed relationships and misleading findings compared with purely local pattern matching.","verification_method":"Use fixtures where the same sink is safe on one path and unsafe on another and evaluate whether the analysis distinguishes them.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Static-analysis dataflow principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B48-R001","D19-B48-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B48-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Static Analysis","concept":"Static-analysis assurance boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What can and cannot be concluded from a clean security static-analysis result?","answer":"A clean result means the configured analysis found no reported issue under its rules, models, configuration, and analysis limits. It does not prove absence of vulnerabilities outside those assumptions or implementation paths.","verification_method":"Record analyzer version, rules, source coverage, framework models, exclusions, suppressions, and analysis limitations with every security scan.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Static-analysis tool assurance principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B40-R002","D19-B48-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B49-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Fuzzing","concept":"Coverage-guided fuzzing","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"What is the purpose of coverage-guided fuzzing?","answer":"Coverage-guided fuzzing uses execution feedback to prioritize generated inputs that exercise previously unexplored program paths. It is particularly useful for finding unexpected parser, state-machine, memory-safety, and robustness failures.","constraints":["Coverage is a signal for exploration, not a proof of security correctness.","Harness quality strongly affects the usefulness of the campaign."],"verification_method":"Track corpus growth, code coverage, unique failures, deduplicated crashes, and reproducibility of discovered cases.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["LLVM libFuzzer documentation","OWASP fuzzing guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B30-R003","D19-B41-R005"],"supports":["D19-B49-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B49-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Fuzzing","concept":"Security fuzzing harness design","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What makes a security fuzzing harness useful?","answer":"A useful harness isolates a meaningful input boundary, minimizes irrelevant setup, exposes security-relevant parser or state behavior, prevents uncontrolled external side effects, and provides deterministic or reproducible failure conditions where practical.","verification_method":"Run known-invalid, boundary, and seed inputs through the harness and verify that failures identify the intended component rather than unrelated infrastructure.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["LLVM libFuzzer documentation","OWASP fuzzing guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B49-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B49-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Fuzzing","concept":"Crash deduplication","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should fuzzing results be deduplicated by failure behavior rather than raw input count?","answer":"Thousands of generated inputs may trigger the same underlying defect. Grouping failures by stack, state, sanitizer signature, or other evidence helps distinguish distinct root causes from repeated manifestations.","verification_method":"Cluster discovered failures using deterministic signatures and manually validate representative clusters.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Fuzzing engineering practices","LLVM sanitizer and fuzzing documentation"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B49-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B49-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Fuzzing","concept":"Stateful protocol fuzzing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can stateful fuzzing discover vulnerabilities that single-message fuzzing misses?","answer":"Many security properties depend on sequences such as authenticate → authorize → modify → revoke, or handshake → renegotiate → close. Stateful fuzzing explores invalid transitions and sequence interactions that cannot be represented by isolated inputs.","verification_method":"Define protocol states and valid/invalid transitions, then generate sequences that intentionally violate expected state progression.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Protocol fuzzing principles","State-machine security testing practices"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B23-R005","D19-B49-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B49-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Fuzzing","concept":"Fuzzing evidence limits","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What can a completed fuzzing campaign legitimately prove?","answer":"A fuzzing campaign provides evidence about the generated input space, harness, execution environment, duration, coverage, and observed failures. It cannot prove that untested states, inputs, environments, or semantic security properties are vulnerability-free.","verification_method":"Record campaign configuration, corpus, coverage, duration, sanitizer configuration, discovered failures, and known blind spots.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Fuzzing engineering principles","NIST software testing principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B40-R002","D19-B49-R001","D19-B49-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B50-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Assurance","subtopic":"Formal Security Properties","concept":"Security invariant formalization","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is the value of expressing a security requirement as an invariant?","answer":"An explicit invariant states what must remain true across all relevant system states and transitions. This makes the requirement suitable for systematic reasoning, model checking, property-based testing, or runtime verification where appropriate.","verification_method":"Translate the security requirement into a precise predicate and identify the states and transitions over which it must hold.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Formal methods security principles","NIST assurance principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B01-R004","D19-B40-R001"],"supports":["D19-B50-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B50-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Assurance","subtopic":"Formal Security Properties","concept":"Authorization safety invariant","knowledge_type":"FORMAL_RULE","question":"What is an example of an authorization safety invariant?","answer":"For every protected operation, the operation is permitted only when the current principal, resource, action, tenant context, and policy state satisfy the authorization predicate defined by the system.","constraints":["The predicate must identify the authoritative identity and resource state.","The invariant must cover every operation path, not only the primary API."],"verification_method":"Map every protected operation to the authorization predicate and test both direct and indirect invocation paths.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Access-control formalization principles","NIST access-control guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B24-R005","D19-B37-R001"],"supports":["D19-B50-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B50-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Assurance","subtopic":"Formal Security Properties","concept":"State-transition security verification","knowledge_type":"ALGORITHM","question":"How can security properties be verified across state transitions?","answer":"Model security-relevant states and transitions, define allowed transitions and invariants, then verify that each transition preserves the required properties or fails safely. This can be implemented through formal methods, model checking, property-based tests, or carefully structured runtime assertions depending on system complexity.","verification_method":"Construct a transition graph and check every security-sensitive transition against the declared invariants.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Formal methods security principles","State-machine verification practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B23-R005","D19-B50-R001","D19-B50-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B50-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Assurance","subtopic":"Assurance Composition","concept":"Compositional security reasoning","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can proving individual components secure fail to prove the composed system secure?","answer":"Components interact through shared state, assumptions, identities, protocols, configuration, parsers, and trust boundaries. A security property proven under one component's assumptions may fail when another component violates those assumptions.","verification_method":"Record assumptions and guarantees at each component boundary and verify that downstream components satisfy the assumptions required by upstream security properties.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Formal methods compositional reasoning","Security architecture assurance principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B20-R004","D19-B40-R003"],"supports":["D19-B50-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B50-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"D19 Phase Gate","concept":"Security engineering coverage gate after B50","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What has D19 covered through B50 and what remains before the dataset can be locked?","answer":"D19 now covers foundational security properties, threat modeling, authentication and authorization, secrets, validation, injection, cryptography, serialization, secure errors, web/API security, TLS, filesystem/process/dependency security, concurrency, OAuth/federated identity, key lifecycle, parser-specific security, archive and filesystem races, cross-language secure coding, software supply-chain assurance, static analysis, fuzzing, and formal security-property reasoning.","coverage_requirements":["No unresolved major security domain may be hidden by record count.","Target-language implementation coverage must be audited across Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Java, C, C++, Rust, and Go where the concept is language-applicable.","Invalid implementations, corrected implementations, tests, verification evidence, version applicability, provenance, contradictions, and duplicates must be audited.","Security concepts that belong to later specialized datasets must be linked rather than duplicated."],"status":"NOT_LOCKED","reason":"B50 is a coverage gate, not a final lock. A complete D19 lock requires whole-dataset deduplication, contradiction review, provenance audit, implementation/code applicability audit, target-language coverage audit, security-testing coverage audit, validation-status audit, and explicit identification of remaining security domains before a new dataset version is locked.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19 Security Engineering scope","Autonomous Software Engineering Brain quality-first dataset governance"],"relationships":{"governs":["D19-B41-R001","D19-B42-R001","D19-B43-R001","D19-B44-R001","D19-B45-R001","D19-B46-R001","D19-B47-R001","D19-B48-R001","D19-B49-R001","D19-B50-R001"],"supports":["D19-B51-R001"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B51-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Server-Side Request Forgery","concept":"SSRF trust-boundary enforcement","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is SSRF prevention more than rejecting localhost URLs?","answer":"An SSRF defense must reason about the complete request path, including URL parsing, hostname resolution, redirects, IP-address interpretation, DNS rebinding possibilities, proxy behavior, IPv4 and IPv6 representations, and the final connection destination. Blocking a small list of textual hostnames is not a complete network trust boundary.","failure_mode":"Application validates a URL string and then allows a separate HTTP client to resolve and connect to a destination that was not covered by the original policy.","root_cause":"Authorization is applied to one representation of the destination while the network client operates on another.","solution":"Define an explicit outbound-request policy and enforce it consistently across parsing, resolution, redirect handling, and connection establishment.","verification_method":"Test private-address forms, loopback representations, IPv4/IPv6 forms, redirects, DNS changes, encoded hosts, and proxy paths.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-918"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B07-R005","D19-B29-R001"],"supports":["D19-B51-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B51-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Server-Side Request Forgery","concept":"SSRF redirect validation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must SSRF policy be applied to redirects as well as the initial URL?","answer":"A permitted initial destination can redirect the client to a destination that violates the outbound security policy. Each redirect target must therefore be evaluated under the same or an explicitly defined policy before the next request is issued.","verification_method":"Create redirect chains from permitted destinations to restricted destinations and verify that policy is enforced at every redirect.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B51-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B51-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Server-Side Request Forgery","concept":"SSRF DNS resolution policy","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can validating a hostname before DNS resolution be insufficient for SSRF defense?","answer":"A hostname can resolve to an address that differs from what the initial textual validation implied, and DNS results can change over time. The security decision therefore has to account for the address actually used by the network connection and the trust model of the resolver and connection stack.","verification_method":"Test DNS responses that change between validation and connection and verify that restricted destinations cannot be reached.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-918"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B51-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B51-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Server-Side Request Forgery","concept":"SSRF egress allowlisting","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is outbound destination allowlisting useful for SSRF defense?","answer":"When an application has a small, known set of legitimate outbound destinations, explicit allowlisting can reduce the attack surface more reliably than attempting to enumerate every prohibited network destination.","tradeoff":"Allowlisting requires maintaining destination identity and may need explicit handling for failover, service discovery, proxies, and infrastructure changes.","verification_method":"Attempt connections to authorized and unauthorized destinations and verify that policy decisions remain stable across DNS, redirects, and deployment changes.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B51-R001","D19-B51-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B51-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Server-Side Request Forgery","concept":"SSRF verification matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an SSRF regression matrix include?","answer":"Test loopback, private addresses, link-local addresses, IPv4 and IPv6 representations, DNS changes, redirects, alternate URL schemes, malformed URLs, proxy routes, authentication-bearing URLs, and authorized external destinations.","expected_test_result":"Only destinations permitted by the outbound security policy are reachable, and rejected destinations produce controlled failures without leaking internal response data.","verification_method":"Run the same destination policy tests against every HTTP client and service that can make outbound requests.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet","CWE-918"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B51-R001","D19-B51-R002","D19-B51-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B52-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Command and Process Security","concept":"Argument injection versus command injection","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"How does argument injection differ from shell command injection?","answer":"Command injection occurs when attacker-controlled data changes shell command syntax or causes additional commands to execute. Argument injection can occur even without a shell when untrusted data is interpreted as a special option or control argument by the target executable.","failure_mode":"Application safely avoids shell interpretation but passes attacker-controlled values as unrestricted process arguments.","root_cause":"The application treats all process arguments as ordinary data without understanding the executable's option semantics.","solution":"Use fixed executable paths, structured argument construction, explicit argument allowlists, and option-boundary controls appropriate to the target program.","verification_method":"Test values beginning with option prefixes, separator values, unexpected flags, and executable-specific special arguments.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet","CWE-88"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B07-R002","D19-B17-R004"],"supports":["D19-B52-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B52-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Command and Process Security","concept":"Fixed executable identity","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive subprocess execution identify the intended executable explicitly?","answer":"Searching for an executable through mutable environment state or accepting attacker-controlled executable names can cause a different program to execute. Security-sensitive process creation should establish the executable identity and controlled execution environment explicitly.","verification_method":"Alter PATH-like environment state and executable-resolution inputs and verify that only the intended executable can be invoked.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet","Operating-system process security principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B17-R004","D19-B52-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B52-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Command and Process Security","concept":"Subprocess environment minimization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should a privileged or security-sensitive subprocess receive a minimal environment?","answer":"Inherited environment variables can influence library loading, configuration, locale, search paths, credentials, temporary directories, and program behavior. Passing only required environment values reduces unintended trust relationships.","verification_method":"Compare the child process environment with the documented allowlist and test behavior when unexpected environment variables are injected.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Operating-system process security principles","OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B17-R004","D19-B35-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B52-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Command and Process Security","concept":"Process privilege and executable trust","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should be verified before a security-sensitive process is granted elevated privileges?","answer":"Verify executable identity, file permissions, working directory, environment, inherited file descriptors, arguments, configuration sources, secret exposure, resource limits, and the minimum privileges required for the operation.","verification_method":"Construct a process-boundary checklist and test each inherited or attacker-influenced property.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Operating-system privilege-separation principles","NIST least-privilege guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B17-R001","D19-B17-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B52-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"Command and Process Security","concept":"Process-boundary negative testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should subprocess security tests cover?","answer":"Test shell metacharacters, option injection, executable substitution, PATH manipulation, environment manipulation, inherited descriptors, unexpected working directories, malformed arguments, excessive resource consumption, and abnormal child termination.","expected_test_result":"The process boundary permits only intended execution and fails safely when its security assumptions are violated.","verification_method":"Run adversarial process-launch fixtures under a controlled test environment.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B52-R001","D19-B52-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B53-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Host header trust","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can blindly trusting an HTTP Host header create a security vulnerability?","answer":"The Host header is request data and may be attacker-controlled unless constrained by a trusted deployment boundary. Using it directly for password-reset URLs, redirects, tenant routing, security decisions, or origin construction can allow attacker-controlled destinations or routing.","verification_method":"Send unexpected, duplicate, malformed, and attacker-controlled Host values through every feature that derives security-sensitive URLs or routing decisions.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Host Header Injection guidance","CWE-346"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B28-R003","D19-B32-R003"],"supports":["D19-B53-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B53-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Trusted origin construction","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should security-sensitive absolute URLs be constructed?","answer":"Use a server-controlled canonical origin or a trusted deployment configuration rather than deriving the security-sensitive origin directly from arbitrary request headers.","constraints":["Proxy deployments require an explicitly configured trusted-proxy model.","The canonical origin must itself be protected configuration."],"verification_method":"Alter request host, scheme, forwarded headers, and port values and verify that security-sensitive generated URLs remain within the approved origin policy.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Host Header Injection guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B53-R001","D19-B39-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B53-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Password-reset URL poisoning","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is request-derived origin data dangerous in password-reset links?","answer":"If an application constructs a password-reset URL from an attacker-controlled host or forwarded header, the resulting link may direct a legitimate user to an attacker-controlled destination while carrying a security-sensitive reset token.","failure_mode":"Reset URL origin is derived from unvalidated request headers.","root_cause":"Application treats request routing metadata as trusted canonical origin data.","solution":"Use an explicitly configured canonical origin for security-sensitive links.","verification_method":"Generate reset requests with manipulated host and proxy headers and verify the link origin remains controlled.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Host Header Injection guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B53-R001","D19-B53-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B53-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Forwarded-header trust boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why must forwarded headers have an explicit trust model?","answer":"Headers such as X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Host, and Forwarded can be inserted or modified by clients unless a trusted proxy boundary strips and reconstructs them. Applications must know which proxy hops are authoritative before using these values for security decisions.","verification_method":"Test direct client access, trusted-proxy access, multiple proxy hops, duplicated forwarded headers, and malformed chains.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Host Header Injection guidance","RFC 7239"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B28-R003","D19-B53-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B53-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Host and forwarded-header regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should HTTP host-trust testing cover?","answer":"Test canonical host values, unexpected hosts, duplicate Host headers, alternate ports, forwarded host chains, direct access bypassing proxies, malformed forwarding metadata, password-reset links, redirects, tenant routing, and absolute URL generation.","expected_test_result":"Security-sensitive routing and URL generation remain bound to the documented trusted-origin policy.","verification_method":"Execute the matrix against each deployment topology that supports forwarded request metadata.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Host Header Injection guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B53-R001","D19-B53-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B54-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Open redirect","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can an unrestricted redirect parameter become a security vulnerability?","answer":"An application that redirects users to attacker-controlled destinations can facilitate phishing, trust abuse, token leakage in unsafe designs, or security-boundary confusion. Redirect targets should therefore be constrained according to the application's legitimate navigation requirements.","verification_method":"Test absolute URLs, protocol-relative URLs, encoded destinations, nested URLs, alternate schemes, and valid internal paths.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards guidance","CWE-601"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B32-R003","D19-B53-R002"],"supports":["D19-B54-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B54-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Relative redirect allowlisting","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is a relative redirect policy useful?","answer":"If the application only needs to navigate within its own origin, accepting validated relative paths can eliminate the need to trust arbitrary external origins. The path must still be interpreted safely and constrained against traversal or parser ambiguity.","tradeoff":"Applications that legitimately support external destinations require a stronger destination allowlist or explicit trusted-origin model.","verification_method":"Test relative paths, absolute paths, protocol-relative URLs, encoded separators, and external destinations.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B54-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B54-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Redirect and authentication interaction","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should redirect handling be reviewed together with authentication flows?","answer":"Authentication and authorization responses can contain sensitive state, codes, or tokens. A redirect boundary that is safe for ordinary navigation may be unsafe when used after authentication or credential recovery.","verification_method":"Map every redirect endpoint to the data carried through the redirect and verify that destination policy matches the sensitivity of that data.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards guidance","OAuth Security Best Current Practice"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B32-R003","D19-B53-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B54-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Redirect chain security","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive redirect destinations be evaluated at the final destination boundary?","answer":"A permitted redirect can lead to another redirect or intermediary that ultimately reaches an untrusted destination. Security-sensitive flows should define whether redirect chains are allowed and, if so, apply the appropriate destination policy at every step.","verification_method":"Test multi-hop redirect chains crossing from allowed to disallowed destinations.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B54-R001","D19-B54-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B54-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Vulnerability-Specific Security","subtopic":"HTTP Security","concept":"Open-redirect regression suite","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an open-redirect regression suite cover?","answer":"Test internal paths, external origins, protocol-relative URLs, encoded destinations, nested redirect parameters, redirect chains, authentication callbacks, password-reset flows, and malformed URL representations.","expected_test_result":"Only destinations permitted by the documented redirect policy are accepted.","verification_method":"Apply the suite to every redirect helper and security-sensitive flow rather than testing only one endpoint.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B54-R001","D19-B54-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B55-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Data Security","subtopic":"Sensitive Data Handling","concept":"Data minimization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does collecting less sensitive data reduce security risk?","answer":"Every retained sensitive value creates additional exposure through storage, backups, logs, caches, exports, access control, and breach impact. If a value is not required for the system's legitimate purpose, not collecting or retaining it can remove an entire class of security obligations.","verification_method":"Inventory sensitive fields and document the functional requirement, retention period, access boundary, and disposal rule for each.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","OWASP sensitive-data guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B01-R003","D19-B35-R001"],"supports":["D19-B55-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B55-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Data Security","subtopic":"Sensitive Data Handling","concept":"Sensitive data classification","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should sensitive data be classified by security impact rather than by filename alone?","answer":"Security-sensitive data can appear in structured fields, free text, binary objects, metadata, derived datasets, telemetry, or temporary files. Classification should therefore follow the actual security properties and consequences of exposure.","verification_method":"Trace representative sensitive values through creation, transformation, storage, transmission, logging, export, and deletion.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST data-security principles","OWASP sensitive-data guidance"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B55-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B55-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Data Security","subtopic":"Sensitive Data Handling","concept":"Derived sensitive data","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can derived data remain sensitive even after the original value is transformed?","answer":"Hashes, embeddings, tokens, aggregates, identifiers, and analytical features can retain security or privacy significance depending on reversibility, linkage, context, and attacker knowledge. Transformation does not automatically remove sensitivity.","verification_method":"Assess whether transformed data can identify, correlate, reconstruct, authenticate, or reveal protected information under the relevant threat model.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST privacy and data-security principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B55-R001","D19-B08-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B55-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Data Security","subtopic":"Retention","concept":"Security retention boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should sensitive-data retention have an explicit security policy?","answer":"Longer retention increases the period during which sensitive information can be accessed, copied, breached, or misused. Retention should therefore have a documented purpose, access policy, storage boundary, and disposal mechanism.","verification_method":"Trace representative sensitive records through their full retention lifecycle and verify that expiration causes the intended disposal or de-identification behavior.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST data-security principles","NIST Privacy Framework"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B55-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B55-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Data Security","subtopic":"Sensitive Data Handling","concept":"Sensitive-data lineage matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a sensitive-data security lineage record contain?","answer":"It should connect source, classification, purpose, transformations, authorized consumers, storage locations, encryption or protection requirements, logging restrictions, retention, exports, backups, deletion, and incident response dependencies.","verification_method":"Build a lineage graph for representative sensitive fields and verify that no untracked storage or transmission path exists.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST data-security principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B55-R001","D19-B55-R002","D19-B55-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B56-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Vulnerability Management","concept":"Vulnerability presence versus exposure path","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why does the presence of a dependency vulnerability not automatically establish application exploitability?","answer":"Exploitability depends on whether the vulnerable code is present, reachable, invoked under the relevant conditions, exposed to an attacker, and affected by the vulnerable behavior in the deployed configuration. Presence is an important signal but not equivalent to demonstrated exploitation.","verification_method":"Trace vulnerable dependency component → application usage → reachable code path → attacker-controlled preconditions → vulnerable behavior.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST vulnerability-management principles","CVE/CWE methodology"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B18-R003","D19-B48-R003"],"supports":["D19-B56-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B56-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Vulnerability Management","concept":"Reachability analysis","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What does dependency reachability analysis attempt to establish?","answer":"Reachability analysis attempts to determine whether application execution can invoke vulnerable functionality under realistic deployment conditions. It can improve prioritization but may be limited by dynamic loading, reflection, plugins, generated code, configuration, and incomplete program models.","verification_method":"Combine dependency graph information with static call analysis, runtime evidence where available, configuration analysis, and manual review of relevant vulnerable paths.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST vulnerability-management principles","Software composition analysis practices"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B56-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B56-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Vulnerability Management","concept":"Vulnerability remediation decision evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should support a dependency vulnerability disposition?","answer":"The disposition should record affected version, deployed version, vulnerable component, reachable functionality, exposure conditions, available fix or mitigation, deployment constraints, compensating controls, and residual uncertainty.","verification_method":"Require every accepted, mitigated, upgraded, or deferred vulnerability to reference evidence supporting the chosen disposition.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST vulnerability-management principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B56-R001","D19-B18-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B56-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Vulnerability Management","concept":"Transitive dependency risk","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must security review include transitive dependencies?","answer":"An application can consume code indirectly through another dependency. Vulnerable or compromised transitive components can therefore enter the runtime or build environment without being directly declared by the application.","verification_method":"Generate the complete resolved dependency graph for each supported build and deployment artifact and compare it with approved policy.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","OWASP Software Supply Chain Security guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B18-R001","D19-B47-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B56-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Vulnerability Management","concept":"Dependency vulnerability regression process","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should happen when a dependency vulnerability is discovered?","answer":"Identify affected artifacts and versions, determine exposure and reachability, select remediation or mitigation, test compatibility and security behavior, deploy the approved change, verify the resulting artifact, and preserve the decision evidence.","verification_method":"Maintain a vulnerability-to-remediation trace linking the original finding to the verified deployed state.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST vulnerability-management principles","NIST SSDF"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B56-R001","D19-B56-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B57-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Incident-Oriented Debugging","concept":"Security incident evidence preservation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why should security debugging preserve evidence before aggressively modifying the affected system?","answer":"Changing state can destroy volatile evidence, alter logs, overwrite files, rotate credentials, or obscure the sequence of events needed to determine root cause. Incident-oriented debugging should balance containment with evidence preservation according to the incident response plan.","verification_method":"Document which evidence is volatile, which containment actions modify state, and how evidence integrity is maintained.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Computer Security Incident Handling Guide","NIST incident-response guidance"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B38-R002","D19-B40-R004"],"supports":["D19-B57-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B57-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Incident-Oriented Debugging","concept":"Security debugging timeline","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What should a security debugging timeline connect?","answer":"The timeline should connect observed events, authentication activity, authorization decisions, configuration changes, deployments, dependency changes, network activity, process execution, errors, alerts, and response actions using reliable timestamps and correlation identifiers.","verification_method":"Construct a timeline from independent evidence sources and identify clock uncertainty, missing intervals, and contradictory events.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST incident-response guidance","OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B38-R004"],"supports":["D19-B57-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B57-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Incident-Oriented Debugging","concept":"Symptom versus root cause","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security debugging distinguish symptoms from root causes?","answer":"The first visible failure may be downstream of the actual security defect. For example, an authorization denial, service crash, or unusual network request may be an effect of a compromised or misconfigured upstream component. Remediation should target the causal chain rather than only the first symptom.","verification_method":"Build an evidence-backed causal chain and verify each transition rather than selecting a root cause from the first observed alert.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST incident-response guidance","Root-cause analysis engineering principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B57-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B57-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Incident-Oriented Debugging","concept":"Containment versus remediation","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should incident containment be distinguished from permanent remediation?","answer":"Containment reduces immediate exposure or attacker capability, while remediation removes or corrects the underlying vulnerability or compromised condition. A containment action can reduce risk without proving that the original cause has been eliminated.","verification_method":"Track each incident action as containment, eradication/remediation, recovery, or verification and require evidence appropriate to each category.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST Computer Security Incident Handling Guide"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B57-R003","D19-B40-R004"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B57-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Incident-Oriented Debugging","concept":"Incident hypothesis testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should competing incident hypotheses be evaluated?","answer":"Each hypothesis should identify expected evidence, contradictory evidence, missing evidence, and discriminating tests. The investigation should preserve uncertainty when available evidence does not uniquely identify the cause.","verification_method":"Maintain a hypothesis-evidence matrix and update confidence only when new evidence materially supports or contradicts a hypothesis.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST incident-response guidance","Evidence-based incident investigation principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B57-R002","D19-B57-R003"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B58-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Regression","concept":"Security regression after refactoring","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can security properties regress even when functional tests continue to pass?","answer":"A refactor can preserve normal functional behavior while changing authorization order, validation boundaries, parser behavior, error handling, configuration defaults, data exposure, or race behavior. Security invariants therefore require dedicated regression tests.","verification_method":"Run security-specific negative tests and invariant checks after security-relevant refactoring.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Secure refactoring principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B24-R005","D19-B30-R001"],"supports":["D19-B58-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B58-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Regression","concept":"Security-sensitive API contract preservation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must be preserved when changing a security-sensitive API?","answer":"The change must preserve authentication requirements, authorization semantics, input validation, error behavior, data exposure boundaries, resource limits, audit events, and security-relevant defaults unless the change intentionally modifies the security contract.","verification_method":"Compare the old and new security contracts and execute regression tests for each affected invariant.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Secure API design principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B58-R001","D19-B40-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B58-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Regression","concept":"Negative-test preservation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should security negative tests survive code refactoring?","answer":"Negative tests encode prohibited states and attacker behaviors. Removing them because implementation structure changed can silently remove evidence that the security boundary still rejects the same classes of invalid behavior.","verification_method":"Trace each security negative test to the invariant it protects and update the test only when the security contract intentionally changes.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Security regression engineering principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B30-R002","D19-B58-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B58-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Regression","concept":"Security contract diffing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can a software engineering brain detect security-relevant changes during refactoring?","answer":"Compare changes in trust boundaries, dataflow, authorization predicates, parser configurations, cryptographic operations, subprocess calls, filesystem access, dependency versions, configuration, logging, and error paths. Changes affecting these areas should trigger targeted security review and tests.","verification_method":"Generate a security-impact diff from source and configuration changes and map affected components to security regression suites.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Secure code review principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B58-R001","D19-B58-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B58-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Regression","concept":"Security regression gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should be required before merging security-sensitive changes?","answer":"Require relevant security tests, static-analysis review where applicable, dependency and configuration review, threat-model impact assessment, security-invariant verification, and documented exceptions for unresolved findings.","verification_method":"Implement a change-classification matrix that maps security-sensitive modifications to mandatory evidence.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Secure code review principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B58-R001","D19-B58-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B59-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Security","concept":"Security boundary composition","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should security controls be analyzed across multiple layers instead of independently?","answer":"An input may cross network, parser, authentication, authorization, filesystem, process, dependency, and data boundaries before reaching a sensitive operation. A weakness in one layer can invalidate assumptions made by another.","verification_method":"Construct an end-to-end trust-boundary graph from external input to security-sensitive effect.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST zero-trust principles","Secure architecture principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B20-R001","D19-B40-R004"],"supports":["D19-B59-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B59-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Security","concept":"Parser-to-authorizer mismatch","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can different components interpreting the same input differently create a security vulnerability?","answer":"If one component validates or authorizes a representation that another component interprets differently, an attacker may construct input that passes the first security check but has a different meaning at the second boundary.","failure_mode":"Security validation and security-sensitive interpretation use different parsing, canonicalization, duplicate-field, encoding, or normalization rules.","root_cause":"No single canonical interpretation exists for the protected input.","solution":"Define and enforce a canonical representation before security decisions and maintain consistent parsing rules across components.","verification_method":"Generate semantically ambiguous inputs and compare interpretations across every component in the request path.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["OWASP parser security guidance","HTTP request smuggling principles","D19-B29-R001"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B29-R001","D19-B28-R002"],"supports":["D19-B59-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B59-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Security","concept":"Canonical security representation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security decisions operate on the same representation used by the protected operation?","answer":"A mismatch between the representation authorized and the representation executed creates a confused-deputy-like validation gap. Security decisions should therefore be bound as closely as practical to the canonical representation consumed by the protected operation.","verification_method":"Trace the exact representation at validation, authorization, transformation, and execution boundaries.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Secure parser design principles","D19-B06-R003","D19-B29-R001"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B59-R002"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B59-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Security","concept":"Security control dependency graph","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should a security control dependency graph represent?","answer":"It should represent security controls, assumptions, shared dependencies, trust boundaries, data transformations, identities, keys, configuration, and failure modes. The graph helps identify controls whose security depends on the same underlying component.","verification_method":"Build graph edges from each control to the assumptions and components it depends on and identify common-mode failure nodes.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Defense-in-depth principles","Formal security architecture practices"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B40-R003","D19-B59-R001"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B59-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cross-Layer Security","concept":"End-to-end security verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an end-to-end security claim be verified?","answer":"Trace the security property from the originating threat through every transformation and trust boundary to the final protected effect. Verify that each component preserves the required invariant and that no unverified interpretation or shared dependency breaks the chain.","verification_method":"Create an evidence chain linking threat → requirement → control → implementation → test → observed result → residual uncertainty.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Security assurance principles"],"relationships":{"related_to":["D19-B40-R001","D19-B59-R002","D19-B59-R004"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B60-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"Threat-to-code-to-test traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should connect a threat model to executable security evidence?","answer":"A mature security trace should connect threat → asset → security requirement → control → implementation boundary → failure mode → negative test → verification evidence → residual risk. Missing links should remain explicit rather than being inferred as complete.","verification_method":"Perform bidirectional traceability from threats to tests and from security tests back to the threat or invariant they protect.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","NIST risk-management principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B02-R005","D19-B40-R001","D19-B58-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B60-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"Security evidence hierarchy","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should the knowledge fabric distinguish different forms of security evidence?","answer":"Evidence should distinguish specification or documentation, static reasoning, code inspection, automated test execution, dynamic analysis, production observation, incident evidence, and formal proof. Stronger evidence for one claim does not automatically prove unrelated claims.","verification_method":"Assign an evidence type and scope to each security assertion and prevent unsupported escalation from illustrative knowledge to verified fact.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST assurance principles","Autonomous Software Engineering Brain evidence model"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B40-R002","D19-B48-R005","D19-B49-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B60-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"Security uncertainty preservation","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"How should unresolved security uncertainty be represented?","answer":"When available evidence cannot establish whether a security property holds, the knowledge fabric should preserve the state as UNKNOWN or CONFLICTED with supporting evidence, assumptions, and required verification steps rather than converting uncertainty into a security claim.","verification_method":"Audit security records for unsupported certainty and require explicit evidence before changing UNKNOWN or CONFLICTED states.","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_FACT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain truth model","Security assurance principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B40-R002","D19-B57-R005"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B60-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"Security regression corpus","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"What should a reusable security regression corpus contain?","answer":"It should contain valid and invalid inputs, known failure modes, security invariants, exploit-shaped test cases, boundary cases, parser ambiguities, authorization violations, resource-exhaustion cases, dependency regressions, configuration failures, and previously discovered defects with provenance and expected outcomes.","verification_method":"Link each regression case to the security property, implementation boundary, historical failure or threat model, expected result, and verification status.","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","provenance":["NIST SSDF","Software security regression-testing principles"],"relationships":{"extends":["D19-B30-R001","D19-B58-R003","D19-B49-R003"]}}
{"record_id":"D19-B60-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"D19 Coverage Gate","concept":"D19 B1-B60 security engineering gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be true before D19 can move toward final locking?","answer":"D19 B1-B60 now forms a broad security-engineering knowledge layer covering security foundations, threat modeling, authentication, authorization, secrets, validation, injection, cryptography, serialization, secure errors, web/API security, TLS, filesystem/process/dependency security, concurrency, OAuth/OIDC, key lifecycle, XML and archive security, filesystem races, cross-language secure coding, supply-chain integrity, static analysis, fuzzing, formal security properties, SSRF, command/argument injection, Host-header and redirect security, sensitive-data handling, vulnerability reachability, incident-oriented debugging, security regression, cross-layer security, and integrated assurance.","coverage_requirements":["Whole D19 duplicate audit across B1-B60","Exact and semantic near-duplicate detection","Contradiction and version-conflict audit","Provenance quality audit","Target-language coverage audit for Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Java, C, C++, Rust, and Go","Code applicability audit for every security topic","Invalid-code and corrected-code coverage audit where implementation is applicable","Security test and verification linkage audit","Execution and validation status audit","Formal/informal evidence classification audit","Unresolved UNKNOWN and CONFLICTED knowledge audit","Cross-dataset relationship audit to prevent duplication with later security/testing/validation datasets"],"status":"NOT_LOCKED","reason":"B60 is a major integrated coverage gate, not a lock. D19 must undergo the full dataset audit before authoritative locking. Any missing high-value security domain should be added only when it represents genuinely distinct required knowledge; record count must not be used as the completion criterion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19 Security Engineering scope","Autonomous Software Engineering Brain quality-first dataset governance"],"relationships":{"governs":["D19-B51-R001","D19-B52-R001","D19-B53-R001","D19-B54-R001","D19-B55-R001","D19-B56-R001","D19-B57-R001","D19-B58-R001","D19-B59-R001"],"supports":["D19-AUDIT-001"]}}

{"record_id":"D19-B61-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Software Supply-Chain Security","subtopic":"Build Integrity","concept":"Reproducible builds","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why are reproducible builds security-relevant?","answer":"A reproducible build process allows independently performed builds from the same declared source and build inputs to produce equivalent artifacts, making unexpected build-time modification easier to detect. Reproducibility does not by itself prove that the source is trustworthy or that every build dependency is benign.","principle":"Build reproducibility increases the ability to compare independently produced artifacts and detect unexplained build differences.","requirements":["Deterministic or sufficiently reproducible build inputs","Declared build dependencies","Controlled build environment","Artifact comparison procedure"],"constraints":["Reproducibility does not establish source-code trust","Non-deterministic tools may require explicit handling"],"failure_mode":"Two builds from ostensibly identical source inputs produce materially different artifacts without an explainable input difference.","root_cause":"Undeclared build inputs, timestamps, environment-dependent behavior, nondeterministic tooling, or uncontrolled dependencies.","solution":"Identify and control build inputs, eliminate unnecessary nondeterminism, record exceptions, and compare independently generated artifacts.","testing_guidance":"Perform independent builds from equivalent source and declared dependencies and compare resulting artifacts using an appropriate reproducibility criterion.","verification_method":"Verify that equivalent declared inputs produce equivalent artifacts and that any differences have documented causes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["SLSA","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B18-R005","D19-B56-R005","D19-B58-R005","D19-B60-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B61-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Software Supply-Chain Security","subtopic":"Artifact Integrity","concept":"Artifact signing and verification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What security property does artifact signing provide?","answer":"Artifact signing can provide evidence that a verifier possesses a trusted signing key corresponding to the artifact signature and that the verified artifact has not changed since signing. Verification must also establish that the signing identity and trust policy are authorized for the artifact's intended use.","rule":"Never treat a cryptographically valid signature as sufficient authorization without validating the signer identity, trust relationship, artifact identity, and applicable policy.","requirements":["Cryptographic signature","Trusted verification mechanism","Signer identity binding","Artifact identity binding","Explicit trust policy"],"failure_mode":"A valid signature is accepted even though it was produced by an unauthorized signer or for a different artifact purpose.","root_cause":"Signature validity was incorrectly treated as equivalent to authorization.","solution":"Separate cryptographic verification from identity, provenance, scope, and authorization checks.","testing_guidance":"Test valid signatures, modified artifacts, wrong signing identities, expired or revoked trust material where applicable, and signatures applied to an unintended artifact or context.","verification_method":"Verify signature cryptographically and independently evaluate signer authorization and artifact-policy binding.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["SLSA","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B08-R004","D19-B18-R005","D19-B34-R001","D19-B60-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B61-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Software Supply-Chain Security","subtopic":"Build Environment","concept":"Build isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security-sensitive builds be isolated from unrelated credentials and workloads?","answer":"A compromised build step can expose credentials, source material, signing capabilities, or artifacts available to its execution environment. Build isolation reduces the reachable security boundary and limits the consequences of a compromised build process.","principle":"A build process should receive only the capabilities and sensitive inputs required for the specific build operation.","security_implications":["Credential exposure can enable downstream compromise","Broad filesystem or network access increases blast radius","Signing capabilities require especially narrow access"],"failure_mode":"A compromised dependency or build script accesses unrelated credentials or release-signing material.","root_cause":"Overprivileged build execution environment.","solution":"Use isolated build environments, minimize credentials, restrict network and filesystem access, and separate build and signing privileges.","testing_guidance":"Attempt controlled access to resources outside the build's declared capability set and verify that access is denied.","verification_method":"Review effective build permissions and execute negative tests against protected resources.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","SLSA"],"relationships":["D19-B05-R003","D19-B17-R001","D19-B17-R004","D19-B20-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B61-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Software Supply-Chain Security","subtopic":"Provenance","concept":"Build provenance","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is build provenance?","answer":"Build provenance is information describing how an artifact was produced, including relevant source, build process, dependencies, environment, and builder identity or related attestations. Its security value depends on the integrity and trustworthiness of the provenance itself.","principle":"Artifact provenance should be treated as security evidence whose origin and integrity must be evaluated rather than as unquestionable truth.","requirements":["Artifact identity","Source identity or reference","Builder identity or environment","Relevant dependency information","Integrity-protected provenance"],"failure_mode":"A deployment system accepts attacker-controlled or unverifiable provenance as evidence of artifact trustworthiness.","root_cause":"Provenance was consumed without verifying its issuer, integrity, scope, or consistency with the artifact.","solution":"Verify provenance authenticity and evaluate its fields against deployment policy before using it as a security decision input.","verification_method":"Submit valid, modified, mismatched, incomplete, and unauthorized provenance to the verification process and confirm policy-consistent decisions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["SLSA","in-toto"],"relationships":["D19-B18-R005","D19-B56-R003","D19-B60-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B61-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Software Supply-Chain Security","subtopic":"Build Assurance","concept":"Build security verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a build-security verification matrix cover?","answer":"A build-security verification matrix should connect source integrity, dependency integrity, build isolation, declared inputs, provenance, artifact integrity, signing, verification policy, and release authorization to concrete tests or evidence.","testing_guidance":"Maintain positive and negative verification cases for each security boundary rather than relying on a single successful build.","verification_method":"Require every security control to have an identified evidence source and an associated verification method.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","SLSA"],"relationships":["D19-B61-R001","D19-B61-R002","D19-B61-R003","D19-B61-R004","D19-B60-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B62-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Release Security","subtopic":"Release Integrity","concept":"Release artifact authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should release authorization be separate from successful artifact creation?","answer":"A build succeeding demonstrates that a build process completed according to its execution conditions; it does not establish that the resulting artifact is authorized for production release. Release authorization must evaluate artifact identity, provenance, security policy, environment, and approval requirements.","rule":"Do not equate build success with release authorization.","failure_mode":"Any successfully built artifact is automatically promoted to a production environment.","root_cause":"Build completion was incorrectly used as the release security decision.","solution":"Introduce an explicit release-policy evaluation between artifact creation and deployment authorization.","testing_guidance":"Test artifacts that build successfully but violate vulnerability, provenance, signer, environment, or policy requirements.","verification_method":"Verify that unauthorized artifacts remain blocked even when their builds complete successfully.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","SLSA"],"relationships":["D19-B61-R002","D19-B61-R004","D19-B60-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B62-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Release Security","subtopic":"Deployment Integrity","concept":"Artifact-to-deployment binding","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security problem occurs when deployment references are mutable?","answer":"If a deployment selects artifacts through mutable identifiers without verifying immutable identity, the artifact retrieved at deployment time may differ from the artifact that was reviewed or authorized.","principle":"Security-sensitive deployment decisions should bind authorization to an immutable or cryptographically verifiable artifact identity.","failure_mode":"A tag or mutable reference points to a different artifact after security approval.","root_cause":"Authorization was bound to a mutable name rather than immutable artifact identity.","solution":"Record and verify immutable artifact identifiers or cryptographic digests during approval and deployment.","testing_guidance":"Change a mutable artifact reference after approval and verify that deployment detects the identity mismatch.","verification_method":"Compare the approved artifact identity with the artifact actually consumed by deployment.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B61-R002","D19-B62-R001","D19-B58-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B62-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Release Security","subtopic":"Promotion","concept":"Environment promotion integrity","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should promotion between environments preserve artifact identity?","answer":"Promoting the same verified artifact reduces uncertainty introduced by rebuilding or transforming software between environments. If an artifact is rebuilt, transformed, or replaced, the security evidence associated with the original artifact may no longer directly apply.","principle":"Production deployment should preserve the identity of the artifact for which security evidence and authorization were established.","failure_mode":"A different artifact is rebuilt for production after security testing was performed on another artifact.","root_cause":"Environment promotion was implemented as a new build instead of artifact promotion.","solution":"Promote the verified artifact where practical, or require a new evidence and authorization chain for any transformation.","verification_method":"Verify artifact identity across test, staging, approval, and production deployment stages.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B62-R001","D19-B62-R002","D19-B60-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B62-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Release Security","subtopic":"Emergency Release","concept":"Emergency release controls","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should emergency security releases preserve security controls?","answer":"An emergency release may use an expedited path, but it should retain essential identity, authorization, provenance, testing, audit, and rollback evidence. Speed should change the workflow latency rather than silently remove critical security boundaries.","requirements":["Explicit emergency classification","Artifact identity","Authorized approver or policy","Minimum required validation","Audit evidence","Rollback or recovery strategy"],"failure_mode":"Emergency status bypasses all security verification and creates an untracked production artifact.","root_cause":"Emergency workflow was designed as an unrestricted bypass rather than a constrained alternative path.","solution":"Define a separate emergency policy with reduced but explicit controls and mandatory post-release review where appropriate.","verification_method":"Exercise the emergency path and verify that required security evidence and audit records remain present.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B20-R003","D19-B57-R004","D19-B62-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B62-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Release Security","subtopic":"Release Verification","concept":"Release integrity regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should release-integrity regression tests verify?","answer":"Release-integrity tests should verify artifact identity, provenance validity, signer authorization, policy evaluation, environment binding, promotion consistency, emergency-path controls, and rejection of tampered or unauthorized artifacts.","test_case":{"positive":["authorized artifact","valid provenance","authorized signer","matching immutable identity"],"negative":["modified artifact","wrong signer","missing provenance","mutable-reference substitution","policy violation"]},"expected_test_result":"Only artifacts satisfying the complete release policy are authorized for deployment.","verification_method":"Execute the release verification suite against both valid and deliberately invalid release evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","SLSA"],"relationships":["D19-B62-R001","D19-B62-R002","D19-B62-R004","D19-B58-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B63-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Software Update Security","subtopic":"Update Authenticity","concept":"Secure update verification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must a secure software update mechanism verify before installation?","answer":"A secure update mechanism should verify the update's authenticity and integrity, compatibility with the target system, applicable authorization policy, and version or rollback constraints before installation.","rule":"Never install an update solely because it is newer or was successfully downloaded.","failure_mode":"A client installs an attacker-controlled or incompatible package because its version number appears newer.","root_cause":"Update selection lacked cryptographic identity and policy validation.","solution":"Verify update identity, signature/trust policy, compatibility metadata, and version constraints before installation.","verification_method":"Test valid, tampered, unauthorized, incompatible, and downgrade update packages.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","TUF"],"relationships":["D19-B61-R002","D19-B62-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B63-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Software Update Security","subtopic":"Rollback Protection","concept":"Downgrade attack prevention","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can allowing arbitrary software downgrades create a security vulnerability?","answer":"An older but validly signed release may contain vulnerabilities that were fixed in newer releases. If an attacker can cause a system to install an older trusted version, cryptographic authenticity of the older artifact does not make the downgrade safe.","failure_mode":"An attacker causes a system to replace a patched version with an older vulnerable version.","root_cause":"Update policy validates authenticity but does not enforce an appropriate version floor or rollback policy.","solution":"Define and verify version constraints, rollback authorization, and recovery conditions.","testing_guidance":"Attempt unauthorized downgrades using validly signed older artifacts and verify policy rejection.","verification_method":"Verify that cryptographically valid but policy-forbidden older versions cannot be installed through the normal update path.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["TUF","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B63-R001","D19-B61-R002","D19-B62-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B63-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Software Update Security","subtopic":"Update Availability","concept":"Update failure recovery","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should happen when a security update is interrupted during installation?","answer":"The update mechanism should maintain a recoverable state, prevent partial installation from being treated as a successful update, preserve system integrity, and provide a controlled recovery path.","failure_mode":"Power loss or process termination leaves a partially updated installation that is reported as current.","root_cause":"Update installation lacked atomicity, transactional recovery, or explicit installation-state tracking.","solution":"Use atomic replacement or transactional update mechanisms where supported and explicitly track installation states.","verification_method":"Interrupt installation at controlled stages and verify that the system reaches a known valid state rather than an ambiguous partially updated state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B25-R003","D19-B63-R001","D19-B57-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B63-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Software Update Security","subtopic":"Update Metadata","concept":"Update metadata trust","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must update metadata itself be protected and verified?","answer":"Update metadata can influence which software an endpoint downloads or installs. If metadata is modified or replayed without detection, an attacker may redirect clients to unauthorized, stale, or vulnerable artifacts even when artifact verification exists.","rule":"Treat update metadata as security-sensitive input and verify its integrity, authenticity, freshness, and policy scope where applicable.","failure_mode":"An attacker modifies update metadata to select a malicious or obsolete package.","root_cause":"Metadata was treated as ordinary untrusted transport data without an authenticated update policy.","solution":"Authenticate security-sensitive metadata and enforce freshness and policy constraints.","verification_method":"Test modified, stale, replayed, malformed, and unauthorized metadata.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["TUF"],"relationships":["D19-B09-R004","D19-B63-R001","D19-B63-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B63-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Software Update Security","subtopic":"Update Assurance","concept":"Update security verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What dimensions should be covered by software-update security testing?","answer":"Testing should cover authenticity, integrity, version policy, downgrade resistance, compatibility, metadata integrity, interrupted installation, recovery, rollback authorization, and failure reporting.","verification_method":"Maintain explicit positive and negative cases for each update security boundary and require evidence before treating the update mechanism as verified.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["TUF","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B63-R001","D19-B63-R002","D19-B63-R003","D19-B63-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B64-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Configuration Integrity","concept":"Security configuration baseline","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is a security configuration baseline?","answer":"A security configuration baseline is an explicitly defined set of security-relevant configuration expectations against which an actual system configuration can be evaluated. It should identify required values, permitted ranges or alternatives, applicability conditions, and exceptions.","principle":"Security-sensitive configuration should be explicit, reviewable, versioned, and testable rather than existing only as undocumented operational assumptions.","requirements":["Defined security properties","Applicability scope","Versioned baseline","Exception handling","Verification method"],"failure_mode":"Security behavior depends on undocumented defaults or operator-specific configuration assumptions.","root_cause":"Security configuration requirements were not represented as explicit policy.","solution":"Represent security-sensitive configuration as versioned, testable policy with controlled exceptions.","verification_method":"Compare effective configuration against the applicable baseline and record deviations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B39-R001","D19-B39-R005","D19-B64-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B64-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Configuration Integrity","concept":"Effective configuration versus declared configuration","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why can declared configuration differ from effective security configuration?","answer":"Configuration files, environment variables, command-line options, inherited settings, defaults, feature flags, runtime overrides, and deployment layers can all influence effective behavior. A security review that examines only one declaration source may miss the actual runtime configuration.","principle":"Security verification should evaluate effective configuration at the enforcement boundary rather than assuming one configuration source represents runtime behavior.","failure_mode":"A secure configuration file exists but a runtime override disables the intended control.","root_cause":"Configuration precedence and runtime composition were not included in security verification.","solution":"Model configuration precedence and inspect effective values at runtime.","verification_method":"Change each relevant configuration layer and verify the resulting effective security behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B39-R001","D19-B64-R001","D19-B64-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B64-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Configuration Drift","concept":"Security configuration drift detection","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should security configuration drift be detected?","answer":"Drift detection should compare the effective configuration of a system against its approved security baseline, distinguish intentional approved changes from unauthorized deviations, and preserve evidence of the detected difference.","rule":"A configuration difference is not automatically a security incident; it must be evaluated against authorization, scope, and expected state.","failure_mode":"A security-relevant configuration changes outside the approved baseline and remains undetected.","root_cause":"No continuous or event-triggered comparison exists between effective state and authorized baseline.","solution":"Implement controlled baseline comparison with explicit exception and authorization handling.","verification_method":"Introduce authorized and unauthorized configuration changes and verify correct classification and alerting.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B64-R001","D19-B64-R002","D19-B38-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B64-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Configuration Exceptions","concept":"Controlled security exceptions","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should an exception to a security configuration baseline be represented?","answer":"A security exception should identify the affected scope, deviation, justification, authorization, owner, expiration or review condition, compensating controls where applicable, and verification requirements.","rule":"An exception should be an explicit governed state, not an undocumented permanent deviation.","failure_mode":"A temporary security exception becomes an untracked permanent configuration.","root_cause":"Exception handling lacked ownership, expiration, or review requirements.","solution":"Represent exceptions as versioned governance records with explicit lifecycle and compensating-control information.","verification_method":"Create, expire, modify, and revoke exceptions and verify that effective policy reflects their lifecycle correctly.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B20-R003","D19-B64-R001","D19-B64-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B64-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Security Operations","subtopic":"Configuration Assurance","concept":"Configuration security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should configuration security regression testing verify?","answer":"Configuration regression tests should verify security defaults, effective-value precedence, required controls, prohibited configurations, exception handling, drift detection, and behavior after deployment or upgrade.","expected_test_result":"Approved secure configurations produce required security behavior and unauthorized or prohibited configurations are rejected or detected according to policy.","verification_method":"Run configuration tests against baseline, boundary, invalid, overridden, and drifted states.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B64-R001","D19-B64-R002","D19-B64-R003","D19-B64-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B65-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Capability Security","concept":"Capability-based authorization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the security value of capability-based authorization?","answer":"Capability-based authorization represents authority through possession or presentation of an unforgeable or otherwise controlled capability, allowing authority to be scoped to particular resources or operations. The security model depends on protecting, constraining, and revoking capabilities appropriately.","principle":"Authority should be as narrowly scoped as practical to the resource and operation that requires it.","requirements":["Unforgeable or strongly protected capability","Explicit scope","Controlled distribution","Revocation or expiration strategy where required"],"failure_mode":"A capability grants broader authority than the operation requires.","root_cause":"Authority scope was defined too broadly.","solution":"Issue narrowly scoped capabilities and validate their intended audience, operation, resource, and lifecycle.","verification_method":"Attempt operations outside capability scope and verify denial.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","Capability-based security literature"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R005","D19-B24-R001","D19-B24-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B65-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Capability Security","concept":"Authority attenuation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What does authority attenuation mean in a capability security model?","answer":"Authority attenuation means deriving a capability with no greater authority than the authority of the original capability and preferably with a narrower resource, operation, or scope.","rule":"Delegated authority should not silently expand the authority of the delegating principal.","failure_mode":"A component delegates a restricted operation but the resulting credential grants broader permissions.","root_cause":"Delegation logic created authority from role or context rather than constraining existing authority.","solution":"Define explicit attenuation rules and reject capability derivations that expand authority.","verification_method":"Test delegated capabilities against the original scope and verify that permissions cannot increase through delegation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":["Capability-based security literature"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R005","D19-B65-R001","D19-B20-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B65-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Authority Delegation","concept":"Confused authority delegation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can delegated authority create a confused-deputy condition?","answer":"A component can become a confused deputy when it uses its own broader authority to perform an operation on behalf of an untrusted caller without ensuring that the caller is authorized for the specific target operation.","failure_mode":"A low-privilege caller causes a high-privilege service to perform an operation against a resource the caller could not access directly.","root_cause":"The service validated caller identity but failed to bind the requested resource and operation to caller authorization.","solution":"Evaluate delegated requests against both the caller's authority and the service's authority, and bind authorization to the concrete resource and operation.","verification_method":"Test requests where the service has access but the requesting principal does not.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["CWE-441","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R004","D19-B14-R001","D19-B65-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B65-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Capability Lifecycle","concept":"Capability revocation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should capability revocation be designed when capabilities may remain valid after issuance?","answer":"The system should define whether capabilities are short-lived, centrally revocable, indirectly revocable through parent state, or bound to another authorization condition. The chosen mechanism must match the threat model and required revocation latency.","requirements":["Defined revocation model","Known revocation latency","Protected revocation state where required","Verification of revoked capability behavior"],"failure_mode":"A capability remains usable after the security policy requires its authority to be removed.","root_cause":"Capability lifecycle did not include an effective revocation mechanism.","solution":"Use appropriate expiration, revocation, indirection, or policy revalidation mechanisms.","verification_method":"Revoke or expire a capability and verify that subsequent use is rejected within the required security window.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","NIST SP 800-63"],"relationships":["D19-B23-R004","D19-B65-R001","D19-B65-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B65-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Authority Modeling","concept":"Authority-boundary verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified in a capability and delegated-authority model?","answer":"Verification should cover capability scope, delegation attenuation, resource binding, operation binding, expiration, revocation, confused-deputy resistance, and cross-tenant or cross-principal access boundaries.","verification_method":"Construct positive and negative authorization cases for every authority transition and verify that no transition increases authority unexpectedly.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","CWE-441"],"relationships":["D19-B65-R001","D19-B65-R002","D19-B65-R003","D19-B65-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B66-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Multi-Tenant Security","concept":"Tenant context propagation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must tenant context be preserved across service boundaries?","answer":"A tenant authorization decision can become invalid if tenant identity or scope is lost, overwritten, or ambiguously reconstructed between components. Every security-sensitive operation should preserve and verify the tenant context that authorizes access.","rule":"Tenant context must be explicitly bound to security-sensitive requests and must not be inferred solely from mutable or user-controlled resource fields.","failure_mode":"A downstream service performs an operation under the wrong tenant context.","root_cause":"Tenant identity was omitted, implicitly inferred, or trusted from an unverified request field.","solution":"Propagate authenticated tenant context through trusted boundaries and verify it against the requested resource.","verification_method":"Send cross-tenant requests with conflicting tenant identifiers and verify that authorization fails closed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R004","D19-B14-R001","D19-B37-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B66-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Multi-Tenant Security","concept":"Cross-tenant reference confusion","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can an otherwise valid resource identifier create a tenant-isolation vulnerability?","answer":"A resource identifier can be syntactically valid and still belong to another tenant. Authorization must therefore evaluate ownership or permitted tenant scope rather than treating possession of a valid identifier as sufficient authority.","failure_mode":"Tenant A submits a valid identifier belonging to Tenant B and receives or modifies the corresponding resource.","root_cause":"Resource lookup was performed before tenant authorization or without tenant-scoped filtering.","solution":"Bind resource access to authenticated tenant scope and perform authorization before exposing or modifying the resource.","verification_method":"Use valid identifiers from another tenant in read, update, delete, and indirect-reference operations and verify denial.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP API Security Top 10","CWE-639"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R002","D19-B14-R001","D19-B66-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B66-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Multi-Tenant Security","concept":"Tenant isolation across asynchronous jobs","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION","question":"Why must tenant authorization survive asynchronous processing?","answer":"An asynchronous worker may execute later and outside the original request context. If tenant scope is not explicitly persisted and verified with the job, the worker can process another tenant's resource using ambient or stale authority.","requirements":["Persist tenant context with job authorization","Validate job ownership before execution","Prevent worker-wide ambient tenant authority"],"failure_mode":"A queued job executes against resources outside the tenant scope under which it was created.","root_cause":"Job execution relied on worker process context instead of explicit job-scoped authorization.","solution":"Bind jobs to immutable tenant and resource authorization metadata and revalidate it at execution time.","testing_guidance":"Create jobs for multiple tenants and attempt queue manipulation or worker-context changes before execution.","verification_method":"Verify that job execution authorization is derived from the job's authenticated scope rather than worker-global state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B37-R001","D19-B66-R001","D19-B66-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B66-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Multi-Tenant Security","concept":"Tenant isolation through derived data","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can derived or cached data violate tenant isolation even when primary storage is isolated?","answer":"Search indexes, caches, analytics tables, temporary files, logs, embeddings, materialized views, and asynchronous outputs can reproduce tenant-sensitive information outside the primary authorization boundary.","principle":"Tenant isolation must cover all representations and processing stages of tenant-sensitive data, not only the authoritative database.","failure_mode":"A cache or derived dataset returns information belonging to another tenant.","root_cause":"Secondary data stores were excluded from the tenant threat model.","solution":"Apply tenant scope to derived-data generation, storage, retrieval, deletion, and observability paths.","verification_method":"Trace tenant-sensitive data through primary and derived representations and execute cross-tenant retrieval tests at each boundary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R001","D19-B55-R005","D19-B66-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B66-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Multi-Tenant Security","concept":"End-to-end tenant isolation matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should an end-to-end tenant-isolation matrix contain?","answer":"The matrix should cover API requests, resource identifiers, databases, caches, queues, asynchronous workers, files, search indexes, logs, analytics, exports, notifications, and administrative paths.","verification_method":"For each data representation and execution path, verify same-tenant access, cross-tenant denial, administrative exceptions, asynchronous execution, and cleanup behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B37-R001","D19-B66-R001","D19-B66-R002","D19-B66-R003","D19-B66-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B67-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Policy decision point versus policy enforcement point","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should policy decision and policy enforcement be conceptually separated?","answer":"A policy decision point determines whether an operation is authorized according to defined policy, while an enforcement point prevents unauthorized operations from proceeding. Separating the concepts helps identify cases where a correct decision exists but is not actually enforced.","principle":"An authorization decision has no security effect unless the enforcement boundary reliably applies it.","failure_mode":"A service computes DENY but continues executing the protected operation.","root_cause":"Decision and enforcement responsibilities were not bound into one verifiable control path.","solution":"Define an explicit decision-to-enforcement contract and verify fail-closed behavior.","verification_method":"Force deny decisions and verify that protected operations cannot continue.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R003","D19-B65-R003","D19-B67-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B67-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Fail-closed authorization enforcement","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should happen when the authorization service cannot make a trustworthy decision?","answer":"For security-sensitive operations, inability to establish a trustworthy authorization decision should normally prevent the protected operation unless an explicitly defined emergency or degraded-mode policy permits otherwise.","rule":"Network, policy-engine, dependency, or parsing failures must not silently become authorization success.","failure_mode":"An authorization dependency timeout causes the application to continue as if access were allowed.","root_cause":"Availability handling was allowed to override authorization safety without an explicit policy.","solution":"Define security-safe degraded behavior and distinguish authorization uncertainty from authorization approval.","verification_method":"Simulate dependency timeouts, malformed decisions, unavailable policy engines, and corrupted authorization context.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B10-R002","D19-B24-R003","D19-B67-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B67-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Policy Consistency","concept":"Distributed policy consistency","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can inconsistent authorization policy across services create a security vulnerability?","answer":"If different services interpret the same identity, resource, operation, or policy differently, an attacker may route an operation through the service with the weaker interpretation.","failure_mode":"Service A denies an operation while Service B accepts an equivalent request because their policy semantics differ.","root_cause":"Authorization policy was duplicated without a shared semantic contract or consistency verification.","solution":"Define canonical authorization semantics and test equivalent decisions across enforcement boundaries.","verification_method":"Submit equivalent authorization scenarios to every relevant enforcement point and compare expected decisions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R005","D19-B59-R002","D19-B67-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B67-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Policy Change","concept":"Authorization policy change verification","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should be verified when changing a security authorization policy?","answer":"A policy change should be evaluated against affected resources, operations, principals, tenants, delegation paths, deny rules, exception rules, and previously established security invariants before deployment.","requirements":["Explicit policy diff","Affected-scope analysis","Positive and negative regression cases","Authorization review","Deployment evidence"],"failure_mode":"A small-looking policy change unintentionally grants access to an unrelated resource class.","root_cause":"Policy changes were reviewed syntactically without analyzing semantic authorization impact.","solution":"Perform semantic policy-diff analysis and run targeted authorization regression suites.","verification_method":"Compare decisions before and after the change over representative and adversarial authorization cases.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R005","D19-B58-R004","D19-B67-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B67-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Policy Assurance","concept":"Authorization enforcement regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an authorization enforcement regression matrix verify?","answer":"It should verify allow and deny semantics, dependency failure behavior, tenant boundaries, delegation, policy changes, enforcement after denial, and consistency across services.","expected_test_result":"Every protected operation follows the intended authorization policy and cannot bypass the enforcement boundary through failure or alternate execution paths.","verification_method":"Execute equivalent scenarios through all relevant interfaces and dependency states.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B67-R001","D19-B67-R002","D19-B67-R003","D19-B67-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B68-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Data Integrity","concept":"Security-sensitive state integrity","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must security-sensitive state have explicit integrity guarantees?","answer":"Security decisions may depend on state such as authorization status, account recovery state, revocation state, policy version, or security configuration. If that state can be modified without appropriate integrity controls, a valid decision mechanism can still produce an unsafe result.","principle":"Security-critical state must have an integrity boundary appropriate to the threats that can modify it.","failure_mode":"An attacker changes security state without bypassing the authorization algorithm itself.","root_cause":"The state store was trusted without protecting its integrity or change authorization.","solution":"Identify security-sensitive state, restrict mutation, authenticate changes, audit transitions, and verify state integrity where required.","verification_method":"Attempt unauthorized state mutations and verify that both the mutation and resulting security decision are prevented or detected.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":["D19-B01-R004","D19-B20-R001","D19-B68-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B68-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"State Integrity","concept":"Security-state transition authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive state transitions be authorized individually?","answer":"A principal authorized to read or use a security state is not necessarily authorized to mutate it. Sensitive transitions such as granting access, changing recovery factors, disabling controls, or altering trust state should have explicit authorization rules.","rule":"Do not infer mutation authority from read authority or from general account ownership.","failure_mode":"A principal with legitimate read access changes a security-sensitive state transition.","root_cause":"State mutation permissions were broader than state observation permissions.","solution":"Define explicit authorization for each security-sensitive state transition.","verification_method":"Test each transition with principals having read-only, ordinary, elevated, and unauthorized privileges.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R003","D19-B68-R001","D19-B68-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B68-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"State Integrity","concept":"Security-state race resistance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can a race condition invalidate an otherwise correct security decision?","answer":"A system may authorize an operation against one security state and then execute it after the state has changed. If the authorization and protected action are not safely coordinated, a time-of-check/time-of-use condition can create an authorization bypass.","failure_mode":"Authorization succeeds for a state that is subsequently revoked before the protected operation executes.","root_cause":"Security decision and security-sensitive state transition were not coordinated according to the required consistency model.","solution":"Use atomic transitions, transaction boundaries, version checks, or reauthorization where required by the threat model.","verification_method":"Revoke or change the relevant state between authorization and execution and verify that the protected operation cannot use stale authority.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["CWE-367","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B25-R001","D19-B25-R003","D19-B68-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B68-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"State Integrity","concept":"Security-state auditability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is useful when investigating an unexpected security-state change?","answer":"Useful evidence includes the affected state identifier, previous and resulting values where safe, authorized actor or service identity, authorization decision, timestamp with appropriate ordering information, request or transaction correlation, policy version, and change source.","constraints":["Do not log secrets or sensitive values unnecessarily","Audit records themselves require integrity and access controls"],"verification_method":"Perform a controlled security-state change and verify that the audit record supports reconstruction of who, what, when, why, and under which policy context.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-92"],"relationships":["D19-B38-R001","D19-B38-R004","D19-B55-R005","D19-B68-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B68-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"State Integrity","concept":"Security-state verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified for security-sensitive state?","answer":"Verification should cover authorized mutation, unauthorized mutation, concurrent transitions, stale-state use, integrity failures, auditability, recovery, and cross-tenant boundaries where applicable.","verification_method":"Exercise every security-critical state transition under normal, concurrent, failure, and adversarial conditions and compare observed behavior with explicit invariants.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","CWE-367"],"relationships":["D19-B68-R001","D19-B68-R002","D19-B68-R003","D19-B68-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B69-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Degraded Operation","concept":"Security-safe degraded mode","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is a security-safe degraded mode?","answer":"A security-safe degraded mode preserves critical security invariants when a dependency, subsystem, or external service becomes unavailable. It explicitly defines which functions may continue, which must stop, and what authority remains valid.","principle":"Availability degradation must not silently expand security authority.","failure_mode":"A security dependency becomes unavailable and the application grants broader access to preserve availability.","root_cause":"Availability fallback was designed without a security invariant analysis.","solution":"Define explicit degraded-mode policies and preserve deny-by-default behavior for operations whose authorization cannot be established safely.","verification_method":"Disable security dependencies and verify permitted degraded operations and prohibited security-sensitive operations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B10-R002","D19-B47-R005","D19-B67-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B69-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Degraded Operation","concept":"Security-critical dependency classification","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should dependencies be classified for security-safe failure behavior?","answer":"Dependencies should be classified according to whether their failure affects confidentiality, integrity, authorization, authentication, availability, auditability, or recovery. The classification should drive the permitted degraded behavior.","requirements":["Dependency inventory","Security-impact classification","Failure-mode definition","Fallback policy","Verification cases"],"failure_mode":"A dependency failure is handled uniformly even though some failures affect authorization and others affect only non-security functionality.","root_cause":"Dependency criticality was modeled only as availability rather than security impact.","solution":"Classify dependency failure by security consequence and define explicit fallback behavior per class.","verification_method":"Inject failures into each dependency class and verify the corresponding security-safe behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B20-R001","D19-B47-R005","D19-B69-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B69-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Degraded Operation","concept":"Stale authorization evidence","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"When can cached authorization information become security-sensitive?","answer":"Cached authorization information can become unsafe when permissions, tenant membership, revocation, or policy conditions can change faster than the cache validity period. The acceptable staleness depends on the security property being protected.","failure_mode":"A revoked permission remains effective because an authorization cache has not expired.","root_cause":"Cache lifetime was selected without considering authorization-change latency.","solution":"Define authorization cache freshness requirements from the threat model and use invalidation or revalidation where necessary.","verification_method":"Revoke authorization and measure whether the system continues to permit the protected operation beyond the defined security window.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B42-R001","D19-B68-R003","D19-B69-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B69-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Degraded Operation","concept":"Security fallback transparency","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security-relevant fallback decisions be observable?","answer":"A fallback path can materially change security behavior. Recording which fallback occurred, why it occurred, what policy applied, and what authority remained available allows security verification and incident investigation without exposing secrets.","rule":"Security-relevant fallback behavior should produce sufficient structured evidence to distinguish normal operation from degraded security state.","verification_method":"Trigger each defined fallback and verify that the resulting evidence identifies the dependency failure and security mode without logging prohibited sensitive data.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-92"],"relationships":["D19-B38-R001","D19-B69-R001","D19-B69-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B69-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Degraded Operation","concept":"Degraded-mode security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should degraded-mode security tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover dependency outage, timeout, malformed response, stale response, partial network failure, unavailable policy service, unavailable authentication service, recovery, and transition back to normal operation.","expected_test_result":"Every degraded state follows its explicitly defined security policy and never silently increases authority.","verification_method":"Inject controlled failures and verify security invariants at each transition into, during, and out of degraded mode.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B69-R001","D19-B69-R002","D19-B69-R003","D19-B69-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B70-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Assurance","concept":"Security control dependency graph","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should security controls be represented as a dependency graph?","answer":"Security controls often depend on other controls, trusted data, identity state, configuration, cryptographic material, enforcement mechanisms, and observability. A dependency graph makes hidden common dependencies and single points of security failure visible.","principle":"Security assurance should evaluate not only individual controls but also the dependencies required for those controls to remain effective.","failure_mode":"A security control is reported as active even though a required dependency has failed or become untrusted.","root_cause":"Control status was tracked independently from its security dependencies.","solution":"Represent control dependencies explicitly and derive assurance from verified dependency state.","verification_method":"Disable or invalidate each critical dependency and verify that dependent control assurance changes appropriately.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B20-R001","D19-B59-R004","D19-B69-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B70-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Assurance","concept":"Security invariant traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a security invariant be connected to implementation evidence?","answer":"Each important invariant should map to the enforcement mechanism, affected code or configuration, negative and positive tests, verification method, evidence source, and known limitations. This creates traceability from security intent to operational evidence.","requirements":["Explicit invariant","Enforcement location","Test linkage","Verification method","Evidence provenance","Known limitations"],"verification_method":"Select each invariant and trace it through policy, implementation, tests, verification, and evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B01-R004","D19-B20-R005","D19-B60-R001","D19-B70-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B70-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Assurance","concept":"Security assurance confidence versus implementation status","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why must security assurance confidence be separated from implementation status?","answer":"A security control can be conceptually well supported but not implemented, implemented but not tested, tested but not comprehensively verified, or verified only within a limited scope. Conflating these states creates false assurance.","principle":"Knowledge confidence, implementation status, execution status, and verification status must remain independently represented.","verification_method":"Audit each security control and record separate values for conceptual evidence, implementation presence, execution evidence, and verification scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R002","D19-B60-R003","D19-B70-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B70-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Assurance","concept":"Security evidence completeness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a security evidence chain complete enough for a security claim?","answer":"A security evidence chain should connect the security claim to its scope, assumptions, implementation or control, applicable test evidence, verification method, provenance, execution or validation status, and known uncertainty or limitations.","rule":"Do not promote an unsupported implementation assumption into a verified security claim.","verification_method":"For each claim, check whether every required evidence link exists and whether any missing or conflicting evidence is explicitly represented.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R002","D19-B60-R003","D19-B70-R002","D19-B70-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B70-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Assurance","concept":"D19 B1-B70 security engineering gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be audited before D19 can be considered complete?","answer":"D19 must undergo a whole-dataset audit covering duplicate and near-duplicate records, semantic overlap, contradictions, version conflicts, provenance quality, canonical schema compliance, target-language implementation coverage, invalid and corrected code coverage, test-to-code linkage, verification linkage, execution and validation status accuracy, UNKNOWN and CONFLICTED knowledge, security edge cases, and cross-dataset relationships.","requirements":["Whole-dataset deduplication","Contradiction audit","Provenance audit","Schema normalization","Python coverage","JavaScript coverage","TypeScript coverage","Java coverage","C coverage","C++ coverage","Rust coverage","Go coverage","Implementation coverage audit","Invalid-code audit","Corrected-code audit","Testing and verification linkage audit","Execution-status audit","Cross-dataset relationship audit"],"constraints":["Do not mark D19 locked solely because B70 is complete","Do not fabricate execution or verification evidence","Do not silently alter previously locked records","Schema corrections require versioned change management"],"verification_method":"Run the complete D19 audit and produce a versioned lock report with explicit unresolved gaps and final status LOCKED or NOT_LOCKED.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B20-R005","D19-B30-R005","D19-B40-R005","D19-B60-R005","D19-B70-R001","D19-B70-R002","D19-B70-R003","D19-B70-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B71-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Database Security","concept":"Database privilege separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should application database identities have narrowly scoped privileges?","answer":"A database identity used by an application should have only the permissions required for its legitimate operations. Excessive database privileges can convert an application-layer compromise into broader database compromise.","principle":"Database authorization should follow least privilege independently of application-layer authorization.","requirements":["Dedicated application identities","Explicit read/write scope","Restricted administrative operations","Separate migration or maintenance privileges where practical"],"failure_mode":"A compromised application account can create users, alter security configuration, access unrelated datasets, or perform administrative operations.","root_cause":"Application runtime identity was granted administrative database privileges.","solution":"Separate runtime and administrative identities and grant the runtime identity only required privileges.","verification_method":"Attempt representative unauthorized database operations using the runtime identity and verify denial.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Database Security guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R005","D19-B17-R001","D19-B20-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B71-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Database Security","concept":"Database authorization versus application authorization","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should database privilege and application authorization not be treated as the same control?","answer":"Application authorization determines whether a principal may perform an application operation, while database privileges determine what the database identity can perform. A defect in application authorization can have greater consequences when the underlying database identity has excessive authority.","principle":"Defense in depth requires independently appropriate application and database authorization boundaries.","failure_mode":"An application authorization bypass exposes a database identity with broad access to unrelated records or administrative operations.","root_cause":"Database privilege scope was assumed to compensate for application authorization, or vice versa.","solution":"Design both authorization layers explicitly and verify their combined security properties.","verification_method":"Test authorization bypass scenarios and determine the maximum database capability reachable after the bypass.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R003","D19-B66-R002","D19-B71-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B71-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Database Security","concept":"Row-level authorization boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security risk exists when database queries retrieve more rows than the caller is authorized to see?","answer":"Retrieving a broad result set and filtering it later can expose unauthorized records through memory, logs, timing, pagination metadata, counts, or application bugs. Security-sensitive filtering should be enforced as close to the data-access boundary as practical.","failure_mode":"The application retrieves records from multiple tenants and attempts to filter unauthorized rows after retrieval.","root_cause":"Authorization was implemented only after broad data access.","solution":"Bind tenant or principal scope into the database query or enforce an equivalent trusted data-access boundary.","verification_method":"Request records belonging to multiple security scopes and verify that unauthorized records never cross the protected data-access boundary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B66-R002","D19-B71-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B71-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Database Security","concept":"Sensitive database backup protection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must database backups be included in the security boundary?","answer":"Backups can contain large amounts of sensitive application data and may have weaker operational controls than the primary database. A secure primary database does not protect an unprotected copy of the same data.","rule":"Backup confidentiality, integrity, access control, retention, and deletion requirements should be derived from the sensitivity of the protected data.","failure_mode":"A backup containing sensitive records is accessible to personnel or systems outside the intended authorization scope.","root_cause":"Backup storage was treated as operational infrastructure rather than another representation of protected data.","solution":"Apply appropriate encryption, access control, lifecycle, monitoring, and recovery verification to backups.","verification_method":"Test backup access using unauthorized identities and verify encryption, authorization, retention, and deletion controls.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R001","D19-B55-R005","D19-B66-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B71-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Database Security","concept":"Database security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should database security regression tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover runtime privilege boundaries, application-to-database authorization, tenant isolation, unauthorized administrative operations, backup access, sensitive-data handling, and failure behavior.","expected_test_result":"Database operations remain within the intended security boundary under normal, unauthorized, and application-compromise scenarios.","verification_method":"Execute positive and negative database authorization cases using identities representing each relevant trust level.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Database Security guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B71-R001","D19-B71-R002","D19-B71-R003","D19-B71-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B72-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Message Queue Security","concept":"Message producer authentication","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should message consumers authenticate message producers?","answer":"A queue or topic can transport messages from multiple producers. Without producer authentication or equivalent integrity controls, an attacker may inject messages that appear operationally legitimate.","rule":"Security-sensitive messages must have a trustworthy producer identity or integrity mechanism appropriate to the threat model.","failure_mode":"An unauthorized producer injects a message that triggers a privileged workflow.","root_cause":"The consumer trusted queue membership or message format without verifying message origin.","solution":"Authenticate producers and authorize them for the specific message type or operation.","verification_method":"Inject messages from unauthorized producer identities and verify rejection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP API Security guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B03-R001","D19-B04-R003","D19-B66-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B72-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Message Queue Security","concept":"Message consumer authorization","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must message consumers enforce authorization independently of producer authentication?","answer":"An authenticated producer may be authorized to publish some message types but not others. Consumer-side authorization prevents a trusted producer or compromised publisher from invoking operations outside its permitted scope.","principle":"Authentication establishes identity; message authorization determines whether that identity may cause the requested operation.","failure_mode":"Any authenticated producer can publish privileged command messages.","root_cause":"Producer authentication was incorrectly treated as universal message authorization.","solution":"Authorize message types, destinations, resources, and operations explicitly.","verification_method":"Attempt privileged message publication using authenticated identities with narrower permissions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B03-R001","D19-B04-R003","D19-B72-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B72-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Message Queue Security","concept":"Message replay protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can replaying a previously valid message be a security vulnerability?","answer":"A valid message may authorize or trigger a state-changing operation only once or within a limited context. Replaying the same message can repeat an operation that was already authorized.","failure_mode":"A previously processed privileged message is submitted again and causes the operation to execute a second time.","root_cause":"The system verified message authenticity but did not enforce freshness, uniqueness, or idempotent processing where required.","solution":"Use message identifiers, freshness constraints, sequence information, idempotency controls, or equivalent replay defenses appropriate to the workflow.","verification_method":"Replay valid messages under different timing and delivery conditions and verify that unauthorized repeated effects do not occur.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","OWASP API Security guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B61-R002","D19-B72-R001","D19-B72-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B72-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Message Queue Security","concept":"Message identity and idempotency","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should message identity support secure processing?","answer":"A security-sensitive message should have an identity or equivalent processing key that allows the consumer to distinguish a new operation from duplicate delivery. Idempotency behavior must match the business and security semantics of the operation.","rule":"Do not assume that queue-level delivery semantics alone provide application-level replay protection.","failure_mode":"Duplicate delivery creates duplicate privileged side effects.","root_cause":"The consumer treated delivery as unique execution without application-level deduplication or idempotency semantics.","solution":"Define message identity and duplicate-processing behavior explicitly.","verification_method":"Deliver identical messages multiple times and verify the expected single-effect or safely repeatable behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B72-R003","D19-B63-R003","D19-B66-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B72-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Message Queue Security","concept":"Message security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should message-security regression tests verify?","answer":"Tests should cover producer authentication, message authorization, integrity, replay, duplicate delivery, tenant context, malformed messages, unauthorized message types, and consumer failure behavior.","expected_test_result":"Only authenticated and authorized messages produce permitted effects, while replayed, malformed, cross-tenant, and unauthorized messages are safely rejected or handled according to policy.","verification_method":"Exercise every security-sensitive message type with valid, invalid, replayed, duplicated, and cross-scope inputs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B72-R001","D19-B72-R002","D19-B72-R003","D19-B72-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B73-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Webhook Security","concept":"Webhook authenticity verification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a security-sensitive webhook establish message authenticity?","answer":"The receiver should verify an authenticity mechanism defined by the webhook protocol, such as a cryptographic signature or authenticated transport, using trusted verification material and the exact message representation required by the protocol.","rule":"Do not treat an arbitrary shared identifier, source IP address, or HTTP user-agent value as sufficient webhook authenticity.","failure_mode":"An attacker sends a forged webhook that appears structurally identical to a legitimate event.","root_cause":"The receiver trusted unprotected request metadata instead of authenticated event content.","solution":"Verify the webhook's documented authenticity mechanism before processing the event.","verification_method":"Modify the payload, signature, timestamp, source metadata, and verification context independently and verify rejection where appropriate.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Web Security Testing Guide","NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":["D19-B21-R001","D19-B61-R002","D19-B72-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B73-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Webhook Security","concept":"Webhook signature canonicalization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can inconsistent webhook payload canonicalization break signature security?","answer":"If the sender and receiver compute signatures over different representations of the same logical request, verification may fail or an intermediary may exploit parser differences. The signed representation must be precisely defined and processed consistently.","failure_mode":"The receiver verifies a normalized representation different from the representation actually protected by the sender.","root_cause":"Signature input boundaries or canonicalization rules were ambiguous or inconsistently implemented.","solution":"Define the exact signed bytes or canonical representation and verify before security-sensitive parsing or transformation where required.","verification_method":"Test whitespace, encoding, field ordering, duplicate fields, line endings, and content transformations according to the protocol specification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","OWASP Web Security Testing Guide"],"relationships":["D19-B29-R001","D19-B29-R002","D19-B73-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B73-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Webhook Security","concept":"Webhook freshness and replay resistance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security-sensitive webhooks often include freshness controls?","answer":"A valid webhook can be captured and replayed later even when its signature remains cryptographically valid. Freshness controls such as timestamps, sequence numbers, or replay identifiers can restrict acceptance to the intended delivery context.","failure_mode":"An attacker replays an old valid webhook after the original event has already been processed.","root_cause":"Signature authenticity was implemented without event freshness or duplicate-processing controls.","solution":"Use protocol-supported freshness and replay-detection mechanisms appropriate to the event semantics.","verification_method":"Replay valid historical events inside and outside the accepted freshness window and verify policy-consistent behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","OWASP Web Security Testing Guide"],"relationships":["D19-B72-R003","D19-B73-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B73-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Webhook Security","concept":"Webhook processing isolation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should webhook processing be isolated from privileged internal operations?","answer":"External webhook input is untrusted even when authenticated. Separating ingestion, validation, authorization, and privileged execution limits the impact of malformed, replayed, compromised, or incorrectly authorized events.","principle":"Authentication of an external event should not automatically place that event inside a trusted internal execution boundary.","failure_mode":"A webhook handler directly performs privileged internal operations before complete validation and authorization.","root_cause":"External event ingestion and privileged execution were coupled into one trust boundary.","solution":"Use staged validation and explicit authorization before privileged side effects.","verification_method":"Submit malformed, authenticated-but-unauthorized, replayed, and cross-scope events and verify that privileged execution remains protected.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Web Security Testing Guide","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B02-R001","D19-B72-R002","D19-B73-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B73-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Application Security","subtopic":"Webhook Security","concept":"Webhook security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should webhook security regression tests cover?","answer":"Webhook tests should cover authenticity, exact signed content, freshness, replay, malformed payloads, authorization, tenant scope, duplicate delivery, processing isolation, and downstream failure behavior.","expected_test_result":"Only events satisfying authenticity, freshness, authorization, and schema requirements can trigger permitted side effects.","verification_method":"Execute valid and adversarial webhook scenarios against the complete ingestion-to-effect path.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Web Security Testing Guide","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B73-R001","D19-B73-R002","D19-B73-R003","D19-B73-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B74-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Side-Channel Security","concept":"Timing side channels","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What is a timing side channel?","answer":"A timing side channel occurs when observable execution-time differences correlate with secret or security-sensitive information and allow an observer to infer information that should remain protected.","principle":"Security-sensitive operations should avoid unnecessary data-dependent observable behavior when the threat model includes timing observation.","failure_mode":"Response timing systematically differs according to a secret value or secret-dependent branch.","root_cause":"Secret-dependent control flow or processing cost is externally observable.","solution":"Use appropriate constant-time or timing-resistant primitives and reduce unnecessary secret-dependent observable behavior where required.","constraints":["Constant-time behavior is context- and implementation-dependent","Do not claim timing resistance without appropriate validation"],"verification_method":"Analyze secret-dependent operations and, where appropriate, perform controlled timing analysis without treating statistical observation as universal proof.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","CWE-208"],"relationships":["D19-B08-R001","D19-B08-R003","D19-B74-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B74-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Side-Channel Security","concept":"Secret-dependent comparison","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can ordinary equality comparison be security-sensitive for secret authentication material?","answer":"Some comparison implementations may terminate early after detecting a mismatch, potentially creating observable timing differences correlated with the matching prefix. Where timing leakage matters, a comparison mechanism designed for secret material should be used.","rule":"Use an established constant-time comparison primitive when comparing secrets or authentication values under a threat model where timing leakage is relevant.","security_implications":["Timing leakage may reveal partial secret information","Custom comparison implementations are difficult to reason about safely"],"verification_method":"Review the comparison primitive and confirm that it is intended for secret material by authoritative documentation; do not substitute an unverified custom implementation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python standard library documentation for hmac.compare_digest","CWE-208"],"relationships":["D19-B08-R001","D19-B74-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B74-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Side-Channel Security","concept":"Security-sensitive error timing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can externally visible error behavior leak security information?","answer":"Different processing paths for valid versus invalid credentials, resource existence, authorization state, or account status can expose information through timing, status codes, response size, or other observable behavior.","failure_mode":"An attacker distinguishes valid accounts or resources by measuring response differences.","root_cause":"Security-sensitive states produced distinguishable external behavior without considering the threat model.","solution":"Identify externally observable differences and minimize or intentionally manage them where information disclosure matters.","verification_method":"Compare observable behavior for representative valid, invalid, unauthorized, and nonexistent cases and evaluate whether differences expose protected information.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet","CWE-208"],"relationships":["D19-B03-R004","D19-B10-R001","D19-B74-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B74-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Side-Channel Security","concept":"Side-channel threat-model scoping","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should side-channel mitigations be threat-model driven?","answer":"Side-channel defenses can impose implementation complexity or performance costs, and different attackers have different observation capabilities. The required protection should therefore be derived from the assets, attacker proximity, observable channels, and security consequences.","principle":"Do not assume every timing, cache, power, or microarchitectural difference is equally exploitable in every deployment; explicitly model the relevant observation capability.","verification_method":"Document the attacker observation model, protected asset, observable channel, expected leakage consequence, and selected mitigation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","CWE-208"],"relationships":["D19-B02-R003","D19-B74-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B74-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Side-Channel Security","concept":"Side-channel assurance matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a side-channel security review record?","answer":"The review should record the protected secret, observable channel, attacker capability, secret-dependent behavior, mitigation, implementation dependency, measurement or analysis method, and residual uncertainty.","verification_method":"Ensure each claimed side-channel mitigation has an explicit threat-model scope and supporting evidence rather than an unqualified security claim.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["CWE-208","NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":["D19-B74-R001","D19-B74-R002","D19-B74-R003","D19-B74-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B75-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cryptographic Protocol Use","concept":"Cryptographic context binding","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should cryptographic outputs be bound to their intended context?","answer":"A cryptographically valid value may be reused incorrectly if the protocol does not bind it to the intended purpose, audience, operation, protocol version, or message context. Context binding reduces cross-protocol and cross-purpose misuse.","principle":"Security-sensitive cryptographic material should be unambiguously associated with its intended protocol context and purpose.","failure_mode":"A valid token, signature, or cryptographic value generated for one purpose is accepted in another security context.","root_cause":"Verification checked cryptographic validity without checking protocol-purpose binding.","solution":"Include or otherwise enforce explicit context, audience, purpose, and protocol binding where required.","verification_method":"Attempt to reuse valid cryptographic values across supported contexts and verify rejection when the context does not match.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","RFC 8725"],"relationships":["D19-B21-R003","D19-B23-R004","D19-B61-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B75-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cryptographic Protocol Use","concept":"Algorithm agility without algorithm confusion","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should systems support cryptographic algorithm agility without creating algorithm-confusion vulnerabilities?","answer":"Supported algorithms should be explicitly defined by policy and bound to the protocol context. The verifier should not blindly accept attacker-selected algorithms or interpret an algorithm identifier outside the intended security policy.","rule":"Algorithm negotiation or identification must be constrained by an explicit allowlist and interpreted consistently by all security-critical components.","failure_mode":"An attacker selects a weaker, unsupported, or semantically different algorithm that the verifier accepts.","root_cause":"Algorithm identifiers were treated as unrestricted input.","solution":"Use explicit algorithm policy, reject unsupported algorithms, and verify negotiated algorithm semantics consistently.","verification_method":"Test unsupported, deprecated, ambiguous, and attacker-selected algorithm identifiers and verify policy-consistent rejection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["RFC 8725","NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":["D19-B08-R001","D19-B21-R004","D19-B29-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B75-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cryptographic Protocol Use","concept":"Signature verification scope","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why is verifying a signature different from verifying the meaning of the signed message?","answer":"A signature establishes cryptographic integrity and a relationship to a signing key under the applicable verification rules. It does not automatically establish that the message is semantically valid, authorized, current, intended for the recipient, or safe to execute.","principle":"Cryptographic verification and semantic authorization must remain separate security decisions.","failure_mode":"A system executes a validly signed message without checking its target, operation, freshness, authorization, or schema.","root_cause":"Signature validity was incorrectly treated as complete authorization.","solution":"Verify signature, message structure, context, freshness, authorization, and business/security constraints independently.","verification_method":"Use correctly signed but semantically unauthorized or context-invalid messages and verify rejection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","RFC 8725"],"relationships":["D19-B61-R002","D19-B72-R002","D19-B73-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B75-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cryptographic Protocol Use","concept":"Cryptographic failure classification","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"How should cryptographic verification failures be classified?","answer":"Failures should distinguish malformed input, unsupported algorithms, invalid signatures or tags, unavailable verification keys, trust-policy rejection, context mismatch, expired or otherwise unacceptable credentials, and policy violations. Different causes may require different security responses.","failure_mode":"All cryptographic failures are collapsed into a generic success or retry path that obscures security meaning.","root_cause":"Failure semantics were not modeled explicitly.","solution":"Represent cryptographic failure classes separately while avoiding disclosure of sensitive internal details to untrusted callers.","verification_method":"Generate each supported failure condition and verify correct internal classification and safe external handling.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":["D19-B10-R001","D19-B21-R005","D19-B75-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B75-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cryptographic Protocol Use","concept":"Cryptographic protocol regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should cryptographic protocol regression tests verify?","answer":"Tests should cover supported algorithms, context binding, signature or tag verification, invalid cryptographic material, unsupported algorithms, key-policy failures, semantic authorization, and failure classification.","expected_test_result":"Only cryptographically valid and policy-valid operations are accepted, and cryptographic validity cannot bypass semantic security requirements.","verification_method":"Execute positive and negative protocol cases across supported versions and policy boundaries.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-57","RFC 8725"],"relationships":["D19-B75-R001","D19-B75-R002","D19-B75-R003","D19-B75-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B76-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Platform Security","subtopic":"Container Security","concept":"Container isolation boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should containers not automatically be treated as equivalent to separate physical machines?","answer":"Container isolation depends on the host kernel, runtime configuration, namespaces, capabilities, filesystem boundaries, and other platform controls. A container compromise can potentially affect the host or other workloads when isolation assumptions are incorrect.","principle":"Container boundaries must be modeled according to the actual underlying isolation mechanisms and threat model.","failure_mode":"A workload is granted host-sensitive capabilities or mounts that undermine the intended isolation boundary.","root_cause":"Container isolation was assumed rather than explicitly configured and verified.","solution":"Minimize privileges, capabilities, host mounts, namespaces exceptions, and runtime authority according to workload requirements.","verification_method":"Inspect effective container capabilities and attempt controlled access to host-sensitive resources.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-190","CIS Docker Benchmark"],"relationships":["D19-B17-R001","D19-B20-R003","D19-B76-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B76-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Platform Security","subtopic":"Container Security","concept":"Container capability minimization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should container capabilities be minimized?","answer":"Additional Linux capabilities can grant processes privileged operations that are unnecessary for the workload and can increase the impact of a compromise.","rule":"Grant only the capabilities required by the workload and remove unnecessary privileges.","failure_mode":"A compromised application uses an unnecessary capability to cross an intended security boundary.","root_cause":"The container inherited a broad default capability set without workload-specific review.","solution":"Explicitly review and minimize effective capabilities and verify the resulting runtime configuration.","verification_method":"Enumerate effective capabilities and test that prohibited privileged operations are unavailable.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-190","CIS Docker Benchmark"],"relationships":["D19-B17-R001","D19-B76-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B76-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Platform Security","subtopic":"Container Security","concept":"Host filesystem exposure","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can host filesystem mounts weaken container security?","answer":"A container process with access to sensitive host paths may read or modify host configuration, credentials, runtime state, or other workloads depending on mount permissions and platform behavior.","failure_mode":"A workload accesses a host path that was not required for its legitimate function.","root_cause":"Broad or writable host filesystem mounts were granted to the container.","solution":"Avoid unnecessary host mounts and make required mounts narrowly scoped and appropriately read-only where possible.","verification_method":"Inventory mounts and attempt access outside the declared workload data scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-190","CIS Docker Benchmark"],"relationships":["D19-B16-R001","D19-B76-R001","D19-B76-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B76-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Platform Security","subtopic":"Container Security","concept":"Container image versus runtime security","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why does scanning a container image not prove runtime container security?","answer":"Image analysis can identify known software, configuration, or dependency issues present in the image, but runtime behavior also depends on privileges, mounts, network access, secrets, host configuration, orchestration policy, and workload interactions.","principle":"Container security requires both artifact-level and runtime-level assurance.","failure_mode":"A scanned image is considered secure despite runtime configuration granting excessive authority.","root_cause":"Artifact security and runtime security were treated as one assurance claim.","solution":"Evaluate image provenance and vulnerabilities separately from runtime privilege and isolation configuration.","verification_method":"Use a trusted image with intentionally insecure runtime settings and verify that runtime security controls still detect or prevent the unsafe configuration.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-190","CIS Docker Benchmark"],"relationships":["D19-B61-R004","D19-B64-R002","D19-B76-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B76-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Platform Security","subtopic":"Container Security","concept":"Container security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should container security regression tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover effective capabilities, privileged mode, host mounts, namespace boundaries, runtime identities, network exposure, secrets access, image provenance, and deployment configuration.","expected_test_result":"The workload receives only its declared runtime authority and cannot access protected host or workload resources outside its security boundary.","verification_method":"Run policy and runtime tests against secure, boundary, and intentionally overprivileged configurations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-190","CIS Docker Benchmark"],"relationships":["D19-B76-R001","D19-B76-R002","D19-B76-R003","D19-B76-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B77-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cloud and Platform Identity","concept":"Workload identity separation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should workload identities be separated from human identities?","answer":"Workloads have different lifecycle, authentication, authorization, rotation, and operational requirements from human users. Reusing human credentials for automated workloads increases exposure and makes lifecycle governance harder.","principle":"Automated workloads should use identities designed for workload lifecycle and least-privilege authorization.","failure_mode":"A long-lived human credential is embedded into or shared by multiple automated services.","root_cause":"Workload authentication was implemented using human credential mechanisms.","solution":"Use dedicated workload identities with scoped permissions and controlled lifecycle management.","verification_method":"Inspect workload authentication material and verify that identities are unique, scoped, and independently revocable.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-63","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B05-R001","D19-B05-R003","D19-B17-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B77-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cloud and Platform Identity","concept":"Workload identity lifecycle","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should a workload identity lifecycle contain?","answer":"The lifecycle should define issuance, authentication, authorization, rotation or renewal, revocation, replacement, compromise response, and decommissioning. Identity validity should not outlive the workload's legitimate authority unnecessarily.","requirements":["Identity issuance policy","Scope definition","Rotation or renewal policy","Revocation mechanism","Decommissioning procedure","Compromise response"],"failure_mode":"A decommissioned or compromised workload retains valid authority.","root_cause":"Identity lifecycle was coupled neither to workload lifecycle nor to authorization state.","solution":"Automate or explicitly govern identity lifecycle transitions and verify authority removal.","verification_method":"Create, rotate, disable, compromise, and decommission workload identities and verify expected authorization changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-63","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B23-R004","D19-B77-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B77-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cloud and Platform Identity","concept":"Workload identity confusion","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can one workload accidentally operate with another workload's identity?","answer":"Shared credentials, inherited execution context, overly broad metadata access, copied tokens, or incorrect identity propagation can cause a workload to obtain or present authority belonging to another workload.","failure_mode":"Service A accesses a resource using Service B's identity or credentials.","root_cause":"Identity boundaries were not explicitly enforced between workloads.","solution":"Use unique workload identities, restrict identity-token access, and verify identity-resource binding at trust boundaries.","verification_method":"Attempt cross-workload credential and identity substitution and verify rejection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-63"],"relationships":["D19-B77-R001","D19-B77-R002","D19-B67-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B77-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cloud and Platform Identity","concept":"Metadata service protection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can unrestricted workload access to platform identity metadata be dangerous?","answer":"Platform metadata endpoints may expose temporary credentials, identity information, configuration, or other privileged material. A compromised workload that can access another workload's metadata context may obtain unintended authority.","failure_mode":"A workload accesses identity metadata belonging to a broader platform or another security context.","root_cause":"Metadata access was not restricted according to workload identity and network boundaries.","solution":"Restrict metadata access to the intended workload context and minimize the authority represented by retrieved credentials.","verification_method":"Attempt metadata access from unauthorized workload contexts and verify denial or appropriately scoped responses.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet"],"relationships":["D19-B51-R001","D19-B77-R001","D19-B77-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B77-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Cloud and Platform Identity","concept":"Workload identity verification matrix","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should workload identity testing verify?","answer":"Testing should cover identity uniqueness, scope, propagation, token or credential isolation, lifecycle transitions, metadata access, revocation, cross-workload substitution, and authorization consistency.","verification_method":"Exercise workload identities through issuance, normal operation, delegation, failure, rotation, revocation, and compromise scenarios.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-63"],"relationships":["D19-B77-R001","D19-B77-R002","D19-B77-R003","D19-B77-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B78-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Observability","concept":"Security event integrity","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must security audit events themselves be protected?","answer":"Security events are evidence used for detection, investigation, compliance, and verification. If an attacker can freely modify or delete relevant records, the evidence chain can become unreliable.","principle":"Security telemetry requires integrity and access controls appropriate to its investigative and governance role.","requirements":["Controlled event generation","Protected storage","Restricted modification","Retention policy","Time or ordering information"],"failure_mode":"An attacker modifies security events to conceal a security-sensitive action.","root_cause":"Audit storage was writable by the same compromised principal or lacked integrity controls.","solution":"Separate event generation from protected storage authority and restrict modification or deletion.","verification_method":"Attempt unauthorized modification or deletion of security events and verify prevention or reliable detection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B38-R001","D19-B68-R004","D19-B78-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B78-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Observability","concept":"Audit-log authorization separation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should application principals generally not have unrestricted authority to alter their own security audit records?","answer":"If an application can freely alter the evidence describing its own security actions, compromise of the application can undermine incident reconstruction and control verification.","rule":"Security audit storage authority should be separated from ordinary application mutation authority where the threat model requires trustworthy evidence.","failure_mode":"A compromised service deletes or rewrites records documenting its own unauthorized actions.","root_cause":"Audit data and application data shared the same unrestricted mutation authority.","solution":"Use separate storage or authorization boundaries and tightly control correction or retention operations.","verification_method":"Attempt audit-event modification using ordinary application credentials and verify denial.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B38-R004","D19-B78-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B78-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Observability","concept":"Audit event completeness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes an audit event useful for security investigation?","answer":"A useful security event should preserve sufficient context to identify the relevant principal or service, action, target, outcome, time or ordering, authorization context, and correlation information while avoiding unnecessary sensitive-data exposure.","rule":"Security logging should optimize for evidentiary usefulness without turning logs into uncontrolled copies of sensitive data.","verification_method":"Perform representative security operations and determine whether resulting events support reconstruction of the relevant security decision and action.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R005","D19-B68-R004","D19-B78-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B78-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Observability","concept":"Security telemetry availability versus confidentiality","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why must security telemetry balance investigative usefulness with sensitive-data exposure?","answer":"Detailed telemetry can improve detection and investigation but can also create additional copies of secrets, credentials, personal data, or sensitive business information. Security logging must therefore apply data minimization and access controls while preserving the evidence required by the threat model.","tradeoff":"More detail can improve investigation while increasing confidentiality and retention risk.","solution":"Define event fields according to investigative requirements, classify sensitive fields, restrict access, and avoid logging secrets or unnecessary sensitive payloads.","verification_method":"Review representative security events for both investigative sufficiency and prohibited sensitive-data exposure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B05-R005","D19-B55-R001","D19-B78-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B78-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Observability","concept":"Security telemetry regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should security telemetry regression tests verify?","answer":"Tests should verify event generation, event completeness, authorization context, correlation, sensitive-data minimization, event integrity, retention behavior, and protection against unauthorized modification or deletion.","expected_test_result":"Security events remain sufficiently useful for investigation without exposing prohibited sensitive information or allowing ordinary application identities to tamper with evidence.","verification_method":"Generate controlled security events and evaluate the resulting records against an explicit evidence and privacy/security policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B78-R001","D19-B78-R002","D19-B78-R003","D19-B78-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B79-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Recovery","concept":"Security control recovery state","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security controls have explicit recovery states?","answer":"A control can be active, degraded, disabled, recovering, or restored. Treating recovery as an implicit transition can create periods where the system incorrectly reports security protection as fully restored before verification is complete.","principle":"Security recovery should be represented as an explicit state transition with verification requirements.","failure_mode":"A failed security control is marked healthy immediately after restart without verifying its configuration, dependencies, or enforcement behavior.","root_cause":"Recovery status was inferred from process availability rather than control effectiveness.","solution":"Define explicit recovery states and require verification before returning a control to trusted operational status.","verification_method":"Interrupt and restore the control, then verify dependencies, configuration, enforcement, and evidence before declaring recovery complete.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-61"],"relationships":["D19-B69-R001","D19-B70-R001","D19-B79-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B79-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Recovery","concept":"Recovery verification before trust restoration","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should a recovered security component be verified before being trusted again?","answer":"Restart or replacement confirms process availability but does not prove that configuration, identity, dependencies, policy, keys, state, or enforcement behavior are correct. Trust should be restored only after the required recovery evidence is established.","rule":"Availability after recovery is not equivalent to verified security readiness.","failure_mode":"A restarted component immediately receives sensitive traffic or authority despite incomplete security initialization.","root_cause":"Operational health was treated as security health.","solution":"Separate liveness, readiness, and security-assurance states and require security-specific checks before authority restoration.","verification_method":"Recover a component with intentionally incorrect security configuration and verify that it remains outside the trusted state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-61"],"relationships":["D19-B79-R001","D19-B67-R002","D19-B70-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B79-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Recovery","concept":"Compromise recovery versus ordinary restart","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why is recovery after suspected compromise different from an ordinary service restart?","answer":"A normal restart assumes the service state and dependencies remain trustworthy. Suspected compromise invalidates that assumption and may require credential rotation, artifact replacement, forensic preservation, trust re-establishment, configuration review, and explicit authorization before normal authority is restored.","principle":"Recovery strategy must reflect the reason the security boundary became untrusted.","failure_mode":"A potentially compromised component is restarted from the same potentially compromised state and immediately returned to normal trust.","root_cause":"Operational recovery and security recovery were treated as identical procedures.","solution":"Define a compromise-recovery path that establishes a new trusted state rather than merely restarting the existing one.","verification_method":"Exercise a simulated compromise and verify that the system follows the security-recovery workflow rather than the ordinary restart path.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B57-R001","D19-B57-R004","D19-B79-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B79-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Recovery","concept":"Recovery trust anchor validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be validated before restoring trust after a security-relevant recovery?","answer":"Validation should include artifact identity, configuration baseline, identity and credential state, cryptographic material where applicable, dependencies, authorization policy, security telemetry, and required control behavior.","verification_method":"Compare recovery state against approved trust anchors and execute security regression tests before authority restoration.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B61-R004","D19-B64-R001","D19-B68-R001","D19-B79-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B79-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Recovery","concept":"Security recovery regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should security recovery tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover ordinary restart, dependency recovery, configuration corruption, credential compromise, artifact replacement, policy restoration, telemetry recovery, trust re-establishment, and refusal to restore authority when verification fails.","expected_test_result":"Security authority is restored only after the recovery path establishes the required trusted state.","verification_method":"Inject controlled recovery failures and verify explicit state transitions and security-safe outcomes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B79-R001","D19-B79-R002","D19-B79-R003","D19-B79-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B80-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"Security claim scope binding","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must every security claim have an explicit scope?","answer":"A security property may hold for one implementation, configuration, version, threat model, deployment boundary, or operation but not universally. Binding the claim to its scope prevents local evidence from being incorrectly generalized.","rule":"Security claims must identify applicable implementation, version, configuration, threat model, and verification scope where these materially affect validity.","failure_mode":"A control verified in one deployment is represented as universally secure across unsupported environments.","root_cause":"Evidence scope was omitted from the security claim.","solution":"Attach scope, assumptions, evidence level, and limitations to each security assurance record.","verification_method":"Audit claims for explicit scope and reject claims whose evidence does not cover the stated deployment conditions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R003","D19-B70-R003","D19-B80-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B80-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"Security assumption tracking","knowledge_type":"ASSUMPTION","question":"Why should security assumptions be explicitly represented?","answer":"Security controls can depend on assumptions about trusted infrastructure, network boundaries, identity providers, administrators, cryptographic key protection, parser behavior, or deployment configuration. If an assumption changes, the associated security claim may no longer hold.","principle":"Security assumptions are part of the assurance boundary and should be traceable to the claims that depend on them.","requirements":["Explicit assumption","Affected controls","Evidence or rationale","Change impact","Review condition"],"verification_method":"Identify assumptions for each high-value security claim and evaluate what happens when each assumption is violated.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B02-R003","D19-B70-R002","D19-B80-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B80-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"Security uncertainty propagation","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"What should happen when evidence is insufficient to establish a security property?","answer":"The system should preserve the uncertainty rather than converting missing evidence into a positive security assertion. An unknown or unresolved security condition may require additional verification, restricted operation, explicit risk acceptance, or another governed response.","rule":"Absence of evidence must not be represented as evidence of security.","verification_method":"Inject incomplete, conflicting, or unavailable evidence into the assurance process and verify that the resulting state remains UNKNOWN or otherwise explicitly unresolved.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R003","D19-B70-R003","D19-B80-R001","D19-B80-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B80-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"Security contradiction handling","knowledge_type":"CONFLICTED","question":"How should contradictory security evidence be represented?","answer":"Contradictory evidence should remain explicitly represented as a conflict until its provenance, scope, version, execution status, or semantic interpretation resolves the disagreement. One source should not silently overwrite another merely because it appears newer or more convenient.","rule":"Contradictions must be traceable to their competing evidence records and resolution process.","verification_method":"Create conflicting evidence with different scopes or versions and verify that the assurance layer preserves the conflict until an explicit resolution is established.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R003","D19-B70-R004","D19-B80-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B80-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Integrated Security Assurance","concept":"D19 B1-B80 completion gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be completed before D19 can be locked?","answer":"D19 must pass a whole-dataset quality audit covering exact, near-duplicate, semantic, and implementation-level duplication; schema normalization; contradictions and version conflicts; provenance quality; code applicability; implementation coverage; Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Java, C, C++, Rust, and Go coverage where applicable; invalid and corrected code; security-specific edge cases; test linkage; verification linkage; execution and validation status accuracy; UNKNOWN and CONFLICTED records; cross-dataset relationships; and unresolved knowledge gaps.","requirements":["Whole-dataset deduplication","Semantic overlap audit","Contradiction audit","Version audit","Provenance audit","Schema audit","Code applicability audit","Implementation coverage audit","Invalid-code coverage audit","Corrected-code coverage audit","Testing linkage audit","Verification linkage audit","Execution-status audit","Validation-status audit","UNKNOWN/CONFLICTED audit","Cross-dataset relationship audit"],"constraints":["B80 completion does not itself constitute a lock","No fabricated execution or benchmark evidence","No silent mutation of previously generated records","Schema corrections require a new dataset version","Coverage must be judged by scope rather than record count"],"verification_method":"Run the complete D19 audit, resolve or explicitly preserve all known conflicts and gaps, produce the versioned lock report, and set final status only to LOCKED when every required quality gate passes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R005","D19-B70-R005","D19-B80-R001","D19-B80-R002","D19-B80-R003","D19-B80-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B81-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous Job Security","concept":"Authorization at deferred execution","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must authorization be reconsidered when a user request creates an asynchronous job?","answer":"The authorization context that existed when a job was submitted may change before the job executes. A queued job must therefore carry sufficient trusted context and enforce the authorization semantics appropriate to the execution time and operation.","rule":"Do not assume that authorization at job submission automatically remains valid at execution time.","failure_mode":"A user submits a permitted job and later loses access, but the worker executes the operation using stale authority.","root_cause":"Submission-time authorization was treated as permanent execution authority.","solution":"Define whether authorization is evaluated at submission, execution, or both, and enforce that policy explicitly.","verification_method":"Submit an authorized job, revoke or change the principal's authority before execution, and verify the documented policy outcome.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R003","D19-B24-R003","D19-B72-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B81-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous Job Security","concept":"Trusted job payload versus untrusted job parameters","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why must queued job metadata and user-controlled job parameters be distinguished?","answer":"A queue message may contain trusted execution metadata generated by the system and untrusted parameters supplied by a caller. Treating every field as trusted can allow a caller to alter tenant identity, execution privilege, destination, or operation type.","principle":"Trust classification must be preserved across asynchronous boundaries.","failure_mode":"A caller modifies a job field intended to identify the authorized tenant or privileged operation.","root_cause":"The worker trusted serialized job metadata without validating its provenance.","solution":"Separate trusted execution context from untrusted parameters and authenticate or integrity-protect security-sensitive metadata.","verification_method":"Modify individual job fields and verify that protected metadata cannot be elevated or reassigned by the caller.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B09-R003","D19-B29-R001","D19-B72-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B81-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous Job Security","concept":"Cross-tenant queued-job isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can asynchronous workers accidentally cross tenant boundaries?","answer":"Cross-tenant execution can occur when tenant context is omitted, inferred from mutable state, copied incorrectly, or trusted from a caller-controlled field. The worker must resolve and enforce tenant scope through a trusted authorization boundary.","failure_mode":"A worker processes a job using resources belonging to a different tenant.","root_cause":"Tenant identity was transported as untrusted job data or was stored in shared mutable worker state.","solution":"Bind tenant scope to authenticated job context and verify resource ownership at execution time.","verification_method":"Submit jobs from multiple tenants, reorder their execution, and attempt deliberate tenant-context substitution.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","OWASP Authorization guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R004","D19-B66-R002","D19-B81-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B81-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous Job Security","concept":"Privileged job worker separation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should highly privileged asynchronous workers be isolated from ordinary job processing?","answer":"A worker that can perform privileged operations represents a high-value security boundary. Mixing untrusted or low-privilege jobs with privileged execution can increase the blast radius of a queue, parser, dependency, or job-validation compromise.","principle":"Separate execution pools, identities, queues, or authorization boundaries when job classes have materially different security authority.","failure_mode":"A compromised ordinary job gains access to a worker identity capable of privileged operations.","root_cause":"All jobs shared one execution identity and security boundary.","solution":"Partition privileged workloads and minimize the authority of each worker class.","verification_method":"Submit low-privilege jobs to the system and verify that they cannot execute through privileged worker paths.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B17-R001","D19-B20-R003","D19-B81-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B81-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Asynchronous Job Security","concept":"Asynchronous authorization regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should asynchronous authorization tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover authorization changes between submission and execution, tenant substitution, replay, duplicate delivery, malformed job metadata, privilege separation, cancellation, and worker identity boundaries.","expected_test_result":"Queued work executes only under the authority and scope defined by the system's explicit asynchronous security policy.","verification_method":"Run controlled jobs through submission, queuing, delay, retry, execution, and cancellation while changing authorization state between stages.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B81-R001","D19-B81-R002","D19-B81-R003","D19-B81-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B82-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Object Storage Security","concept":"Object storage authorization boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must object-storage access be authorized independently of application URL visibility?","answer":"An object identifier appearing in an application response does not establish that every caller may retrieve the underlying object. Object storage requires explicit authorization appropriate to the object, tenant, operation, and access mechanism.","principle":"Object identifiers are references, not authorization grants.","failure_mode":"A user obtains another user's object identifier and directly retrieves the object from storage.","root_cause":"Object access relied on identifier secrecy or application routing rather than authorization.","solution":"Enforce object-level authorization or tightly scoped signed access mechanisms at the storage boundary.","verification_method":"Attempt direct access using identifiers belonging to another authorization scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B14-R001","D19-B24-R003","D19-B71-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B82-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Object Storage Security","concept":"Pre-signed access scope","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What security properties should a temporary object-storage access grant constrain?","answer":"A temporary access grant should be constrained by the intended object or resource, permitted operation, validity period, and other supported policy dimensions. A broad or long-lived grant increases the impact of disclosure.","rule":"Temporary access should grant the minimum authority and lifetime required by the intended operation.","failure_mode":"A leaked temporary URL or token permits unintended objects, operations, or prolonged access.","root_cause":"The access grant was broader or longer-lived than the workflow required.","solution":"Constrain resource, operation, expiration, and applicable conditions according to the storage protocol.","verification_method":"Inspect generated temporary grants and attempt access to alternate objects, operations, and time windows.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":["D19-B05-R003","D19-B82-R001","D19-B82-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B82-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Object Storage Security","concept":"Temporary access revocation limitation","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can temporary object-storage access be difficult to revoke immediately?","answer":"Some temporary access mechanisms are self-contained and remain valid until their expiration or until an underlying authorization dependency changes. This can limit immediate revocation compared with server-side authorization decisions.","tradeoff":"Self-contained temporary access can reduce repeated authorization checks while making immediate revocation more difficult.","solution":"Use short validity periods, revocable underlying credentials or policies where supported, and server-mediated access when immediate revocation is a requirement.","verification_method":"Test access after authorization revocation and document the actual revocation semantics of the selected mechanism.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-57"],"relationships":["D19-B82-R002","D19-B77-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B82-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Object Storage Security","concept":"Object key trust boundaries","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should object-storage keys be treated as untrusted identifiers?","answer":"Object keys may originate from users, external systems, imported datasets, or compromised clients. Using them directly in authorization, filesystem paths, tenant selection, or policy expressions can create injection, traversal, or confused-deputy risks.","failure_mode":"A user-controlled object key changes the resource or security scope selected by the application.","root_cause":"Identifier syntax was treated as trusted policy input.","solution":"Validate object identifiers and derive security scope from trusted metadata rather than from attacker-controlled naming alone.","verification_method":"Submit traversal-like, ambiguous, cross-tenant, encoded, and duplicate object identifiers and verify consistent resource selection and authorization.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","CWE-22"],"relationships":["D19-B06-R003","D19-B07-R004","D19-B29-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B82-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Object Storage Security","concept":"Object-storage security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should object-storage security regression tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover object authorization, tenant isolation, temporary access scope, expiration, direct access, key validation, upload/download permissions, metadata exposure, and revocation behavior.","expected_test_result":"Only authorized operations on authorized objects are possible, and temporary access follows its documented scope and lifetime.","verification_method":"Exercise direct and mediated access using valid, invalid, cross-tenant, expired, altered, and replayed access grants.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP API Security guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B82-R001","D19-B82-R002","D19-B82-R003","D19-B82-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B83-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Configuration","concept":"Security configuration baseline","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive configuration have an explicit baseline?","answer":"Without a known baseline, configuration changes cannot be reliably distinguished from intended policy, accidental drift, or unauthorized modification. A baseline makes security expectations explicit and testable.","rule":"Security-sensitive configuration should be represented as versioned, reviewable policy rather than undocumented runtime state.","requirements":["Defined security settings","Version identity","Change ownership","Validation rules","Rollback or recovery process"],"verification_method":"Compare deployed configuration against the approved baseline and identify unauthorized or unexplained deviations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-128"],"relationships":["D19-B06-R001","D19-B20-R002","D19-B83-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B83-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Configuration","concept":"Security configuration drift","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is security configuration drift?","answer":"Security configuration drift occurs when deployed security-relevant settings diverge from the intended approved state over time through manual changes, automation errors, upgrades, recovery actions, or environmental differences.","failure_mode":"A deployment silently changes an authorization, TLS, filesystem, runtime, or network security setting.","root_cause":"Configuration state was not continuously reconciled with the approved security baseline.","solution":"Detect and classify configuration drift and require governed remediation for security-sensitive deviations.","verification_method":"Introduce controlled configuration changes and verify that the system detects, records, and handles the drift according to policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-128","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B83-R001","D19-B79-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B83-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Configuration","concept":"Fail-secure configuration defaults","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should happen when a security-sensitive configuration value is missing or invalid?","answer":"The system should select the security behavior explicitly defined by policy rather than silently falling back to an insecure permissive mode. Where safe operation is impossible, fail-closed behavior may be required.","principle":"Configuration failure must have an intentional security outcome.","failure_mode":"A missing security setting causes authentication, authorization, TLS verification, or access restrictions to be disabled.","root_cause":"The implementation treated configuration absence as permission to use permissive defaults.","solution":"Define secure defaults and explicitly reject unsupported or ambiguous configurations.","verification_method":"Remove, corrupt, or alter security-sensitive configuration and verify the documented secure failure behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","OWASP Secure Coding Practices"],"relationships":["D19-B10-R002","D19-B83-R001","D19-B83-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B83-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Configuration","concept":"Configuration provenance","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive configuration changes have provenance?","answer":"Provenance identifies who or what changed a security property, from which version or source, under what approval or deployment event, and with what validation. Without provenance, incident investigation and configuration assurance become weaker.","rule":"Security-sensitive configuration should be traceable to an authoritative source and controlled change event.","verification_method":"Select representative deployed settings and trace them back to their source, version, change event, and validation evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-128","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R001","D19-B78-R001","D19-B83-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B83-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Configuration","concept":"Configuration security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should security configuration regression tests verify?","answer":"Tests should cover baseline conformance, missing values, invalid values, insecure overrides, version changes, recovery behavior, authorization settings, cryptographic settings, and configuration provenance.","expected_test_result":"Approved security configuration is preserved, deviations are detected, and invalid or ambiguous settings produce policy-defined safe outcomes.","verification_method":"Test configuration transitions from valid to missing, invalid, changed, and recovered states and verify enforcement at each stage.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-128","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B83-R001","D19-B83-R002","D19-B83-R003","D19-B83-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B84-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Feature and Policy Controls","concept":"Security feature-flag authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive feature flags be treated as authorization-controlled configuration?","answer":"A feature flag can enable or disable authentication, authorization, validation, encryption, logging, or other security controls. Unrestricted flag modification can therefore become a security privilege.","rule":"Security-sensitive feature flags require explicit authorization, provenance, change control, and validation.","failure_mode":"A developer or compromised service changes a security flag and disables a protective control.","root_cause":"Feature-flag administration was treated as ordinary application configuration.","solution":"Classify security-sensitive flags separately and restrict their mutation to authorized identities and workflows.","verification_method":"Attempt unauthorized flag modification and verify denial, auditability, and unchanged security behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-128"],"relationships":["D19-B83-R001","D19-B04-R005","D19-B78-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B84-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Feature and Policy Controls","concept":"Fail-safe feature-flag rollback","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should security-sensitive feature rollback behave when the new configuration is unsafe?","answer":"Rollback should restore a known safe state rather than merely reverting to the immediately previous state, because the previous state may itself be compromised or invalid.","principle":"Security rollback targets an approved trusted state, not simply the last observed state.","failure_mode":"An unsafe configuration is reverted to another unverified configuration that retains the security defect.","root_cause":"Rollback semantics were based on chronology instead of security trust.","solution":"Maintain versioned trusted configurations and validate the target state before restoring authority.","verification_method":"Perform rollback from multiple configuration states and verify that only approved security states can become active.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-128","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B79-R002","D19-B83-R001","D19-B84-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B84-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Feature and Policy Controls","concept":"Security kill-switch governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","question":"Why should emergency security controls have predefined governance?","answer":"Emergency controls can materially change system behavior and may themselves create denial-of-service, data-loss, or authorization risks. Their activation criteria, authority, scope, expiration, and recovery should therefore be explicitly governed.","rule":"Emergency security controls require predefined activation authority, auditability, scope, and recovery semantics.","failure_mode":"An emergency control is activated indefinitely, by an unauthorized identity, or without a safe recovery path.","root_cause":"Emergency mechanisms were implemented without lifecycle governance.","solution":"Define who can activate the control, under what evidence, for what scope, for how long, and how normal operation is safely restored.","verification_method":"Exercise emergency activation and expiration using authorized and unauthorized identities and verify every governance transition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-61"],"relationships":["D19-B79-R001","D19-B84-R001","D19-B84-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B84-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Feature and Policy Controls","concept":"Time-bounded security overrides","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should temporary security overrides normally have explicit expiration?","answer":"Temporary exceptions can become permanent security weaknesses when their lifecycle is not enforced. Explicit expiration reduces the chance that emergency or testing privileges remain active indefinitely.","rule":"Temporary security overrides should have an enforced expiration unless a separately governed process converts them into a permanent approved state.","verification_method":"Create a temporary override and verify that it expires or is explicitly renewed according to policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-128"],"relationships":["D19-B83-R002","D19-B84-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B84-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Feature and Policy Controls","concept":"Security override regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should security override tests verify?","answer":"Tests should cover authorization, activation evidence, scope, expiration, auditability, rollback, renewal, concurrent changes, and failure behavior for security-sensitive feature flags and emergency controls.","expected_test_result":"Only authorized, scoped, time-valid security overrides can alter protected behavior, and recovery returns the system to a verified state.","verification_method":"Exercise valid, unauthorized, expired, overlapping, malformed, and revoked overrides.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-128"],"relationships":["D19-B84-R001","D19-B84-R002","D19-B84-R003","D19-B84-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B85-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Policy decision versus policy enforcement","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should policy decision and policy enforcement be treated as separate concepts?","answer":"A policy engine can produce a correct decision while the enforcing component fails to apply it, applies it to the wrong resource, or uses stale context. Security assurance therefore requires both decision correctness and enforcement correctness.","principle":"A valid policy decision does not prove that the protected operation was actually constrained.","failure_mode":"The policy engine denies an operation but the downstream service performs it anyway.","root_cause":"Decision output was not reliably bound to the enforcement boundary.","solution":"Trace policy decisions to the exact enforcement point and verify fail-safe behavior when policy evaluation is unavailable or inconsistent.","verification_method":"Force allow, deny, timeout, stale-policy, and enforcement-failure scenarios and verify the actual side effect.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R003","D19-B67-R002","D19-B85-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B85-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Fail-closed policy dependency","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should happen when a security authorization dependency is unavailable?","answer":"The required behavior depends on the operation and threat model, but security-sensitive operations should not silently become authorized merely because the policy dependency failed.","rule":"Authorization dependency failure must have an explicit policy outcome; permissive fallback requires deliberate security justification rather than accidental behavior.","failure_mode":"A policy-service timeout causes the application to allow an operation that normally requires authorization.","root_cause":"Availability fallback was implemented without a security policy for authorization failure.","solution":"Define explicit failure semantics and distinguish operations that can safely degrade from those that must fail closed.","verification_method":"Interrupt the policy dependency and test each security-sensitive operation independently.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B10-R002","D19-B47-R004","D19-B85-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B85-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Stale authorization context","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can stale authorization context create security vulnerabilities?","answer":"Authorization decisions may depend on changing roles, tenant membership, resource ownership, revocation, or risk state. Reusing stale context can allow an operation after the principal's authority has changed.","failure_mode":"A cached authorization result remains valid after the underlying permission has been revoked.","root_cause":"Authorization cache lifetime exceeded the acceptable security freshness requirement.","solution":"Define security-sensitive authorization freshness requirements and invalidate or re-evaluate decisions when relevant state changes.","verification_method":"Change authorization state while cached decisions exist and verify behavior against the defined freshness policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B48-R002","D19-B85-R001","D19-B85-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B85-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Authorization decision provenance","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What provenance should accompany a high-impact authorization decision?","answer":"The system should retain sufficient internal evidence to determine the principal, requested operation, resource, relevant policy version, important attributes or context, decision outcome, and enforcement result without unnecessarily storing sensitive data.","rule":"High-impact security decisions should be traceable to the policy and context that produced and enforced them.","verification_method":"Trace representative authorization decisions from request through policy evaluation to final enforcement outcome.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R003","D19-B78-R003","D19-B85-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B85-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Policy Enforcement","concept":"Authorization enforcement regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should end-to-end authorization enforcement tests verify?","answer":"Tests should cover policy allow and deny decisions, stale context, policy dependency failure, enforcement failure, resource mismatch, tenant mismatch, policy-version changes, and audit traceability.","expected_test_result":"The actual side effect always conforms to the applicable authorization policy and defined failure semantics.","verification_method":"Test authorization decisions together with the final resource operation rather than testing the policy engine in isolation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B85-R001","D19-B85-R002","D19-B85-R003","D19-B85-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B86-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Protocol Downgrade Security","concept":"Security protocol downgrade resistance","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should protocol negotiation prevent unintended downgrade to weaker security properties?","answer":"Negotiation between multiple protocol versions or security capabilities can become an attack surface if an adversary can force both parties into a weaker but still accepted mode. Supported versions and downgrade signals must therefore be constrained by protocol policy.","rule":"Only explicitly supported security modes may be negotiated, and downgrade protections defined by the protocol should be enforced.","failure_mode":"An attacker interferes with negotiation and causes endpoints to use a weaker security protocol or feature set.","root_cause":"The implementation accepted the negotiated result without validating downgrade constraints.","solution":"Use protocol-defined downgrade protections and explicit minimum security policy.","verification_method":"Simulate negotiation interference and verify rejection or detection of prohibited downgrade paths.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["RFC 8446","NIST SP 800-52"],"relationships":["D19-B15-R004","D19-B75-R002","D19-B86-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B86-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Protocol Downgrade Security","concept":"Security capability negotiation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should optional security capabilities be represented explicitly during negotiation?","answer":"Implicit assumptions about optional capabilities can produce inconsistent security states between components. Explicit capability negotiation allows each endpoint to determine what security properties are actually established rather than assuming them from configuration or version alone.","principle":"Security capability state should be explicit, authenticated where required, and interpreted consistently by all participating components.","failure_mode":"One component assumes a security capability is active while another operates without it.","root_cause":"Capability state was inferred rather than negotiated or verified.","solution":"Define capability semantics and validate the negotiated state before security-sensitive processing.","verification_method":"Test mismatched capability sets and verify deterministic, secure negotiation outcomes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["RFC 8446","NIST SP 800-52"],"relationships":["D19-B75-R002","D19-B86-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B86-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Protocol Downgrade Security","concept":"Version interoperability versus security acceptance","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why is interoperability with an older protocol version not automatically a security requirement?","answer":"Supporting an older version may improve compatibility but can also preserve weaker security properties or obsolete mechanisms. Compatibility policy must therefore be separated from the minimum security level the deployment is willing to accept.","tradeoff":"Broader interoperability can preserve legacy compatibility while increasing the supported security attack surface.","verification_method":"Document supported versions, security properties, deprecation policy, and explicit minimum acceptable security level.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-52","RFC 8446"],"relationships":["D19-B15-R004","D19-B86-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B86-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Protocol Downgrade Security","concept":"Downgrade detection evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence is useful when determining whether a protocol downgrade occurred?","answer":"Relevant evidence can include negotiated protocol version, security capabilities, endpoint policy, downgrade indicators defined by the protocol, and the observed negotiation sequence. Evidence must be interpreted according to the protocol specification rather than guessed from a single field.","verification_method":"Capture protocol negotiation evidence under normal and intentionally degraded conditions and verify that downgrade detection follows documented protocol semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["RFC 8446","NIST SP 800-52"],"relationships":["D19-B86-R001","D19-B86-R002","D19-B86-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B86-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Protocol Downgrade Security","concept":"Protocol downgrade regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should protocol downgrade regression tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover supported versions, unsupported versions, negotiated capabilities, forced downgrade attempts, endpoint policy mismatch, protocol-specific downgrade indicators, and safe failure behavior.","expected_test_result":"The implementation never accepts a prohibited weaker security mode and correctly handles legitimate interoperability cases within policy.","verification_method":"Exercise every supported negotiation path plus adversarial downgrade attempts.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["RFC 8446","NIST SP 800-52"],"relationships":["D19-B86-R001","D19-B86-R002","D19-B86-R003","D19-B86-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B87-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Inter-Service Trust","concept":"Service identity audience restriction","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should service authentication tokens be restricted to an intended audience?","answer":"A token proving identity to one service should not automatically be accepted by unrelated services. Audience restriction limits token reuse across service boundaries and reduces the impact of token disclosure or confused delegation.","rule":"Service credentials or tokens should be accepted only by explicitly intended audiences according to the protocol and authorization model.","failure_mode":"A token issued for Service A is accepted by Service B because both services trust the same issuer without audience enforcement.","root_cause":"Issuer trust was treated as sufficient authorization for every service.","solution":"Validate audience or equivalent recipient binding before accepting service credentials.","verification_method":"Present a valid token issued for another service and verify rejection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["RFC 7519","RFC 8725","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B75-R001","D19-B77-R001","D19-B87-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B87-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Inter-Service Trust","concept":"Issuer trust versus authorization","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why does trusting a credential issuer not automatically authorize every operation represented by its credentials?","answer":"Issuer trust establishes that the verifier accepts credentials produced under that issuer's trust model. Authorization still requires checking claims, audience, scope, resource, operation, and current policy as applicable.","principle":"Credential provenance and authorization are separate security decisions.","failure_mode":"A service accepts any credential from a trusted issuer regardless of audience or operation scope.","root_cause":"Credential authenticity was treated as universal authorization.","solution":"Validate all security-relevant claims and apply service-specific authorization policy.","verification_method":"Use valid credentials with different audiences, scopes, subjects, and operations and verify policy-consistent decisions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["RFC 7519","RFC 8725","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B23-R004","D19-B75-R003","D19-B87-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B87-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Inter-Service Trust","concept":"Transitive service trust","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can transitive service trust increase blast radius?","answer":"If Service A can invoke Service B and Service B can invoke Service C using authority that implicitly represents A, a compromise of A may gain indirect access to C beyond A's intended scope.","failure_mode":"A compromised low-privilege service reaches a high-value downstream service through a trusted intermediary.","root_cause":"Delegated authority and downstream authorization were not constrained to the original operation scope.","solution":"Model service-to-service delegation explicitly and restrict downstream authority to the minimum required operation and resource.","verification_method":"Compromise or impersonate a lower-privilege service in a controlled environment and trace reachable downstream authority.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B20-R003","D19-B24-R004","D19-B87-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B87-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Inter-Service Trust","concept":"Delegation scope preservation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should delegated service authority not silently expand downstream?","answer":"A service may be authorized to perform a narrow operation on behalf of another principal. If downstream services interpret the delegation as broader authority, the effective privilege can grow at every hop.","principle":"Delegation should preserve or intentionally constrain authority rather than implicitly amplify it.","verification_method":"Trace a delegated request across service boundaries and compare effective authority at each hop against the originating authorization scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R005","D19-B87-R001","D19-B87-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B87-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Inter-Service Trust","concept":"Service trust regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should service-to-service trust tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover issuer trust, audience, scope, delegation, downstream authorization, token substitution, cross-service replay, service impersonation, and trust-boundary failure.","expected_test_result":"Each service accepts only credentials and delegated authority appropriate to its own security boundary and requested operation.","verification_method":"Exercise valid and deliberately mismatched service credentials across every relevant service boundary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-207","RFC 8725"],"relationships":["D19-B87-R001","D19-B87-R002","D19-B87-R003","D19-B87-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B88-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion Security","concept":"Security resource budgets","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security-sensitive operations have explicit resource budgets?","answer":"Attackers can exploit CPU, memory, storage, connection, parser, concurrency, or downstream-service consumption even when input is syntactically valid. Explicit resource budgets constrain the impact of abusive requests.","rule":"Security-sensitive processing should have bounded resource consumption appropriate to the operation and threat model.","failure_mode":"A valid request consumes unbounded resources and degrades service availability.","root_cause":"Validation checked correctness but not computational or resource cost.","solution":"Define and enforce limits for size, depth, concurrency, time, memory, retries, and downstream work where applicable.","verification_method":"Submit increasingly expensive valid inputs and verify that resource limits terminate or constrain processing safely.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Denial of Service guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B06-R004","D19-B17-R003","D19-B88-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B88-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion Security","concept":"Nested input complexity limits","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can syntactically valid nested input still create a denial-of-service risk?","answer":"Deeply nested arrays, objects, expressions, compressed content, or recursive structures can require disproportionate parsing or processing resources while remaining formally valid.","failure_mode":"An attacker submits valid but extremely deep or computationally expensive structured input.","root_cause":"Validation enforced syntax and schema but omitted complexity limits.","solution":"Apply depth, node-count, expansion, time, and memory constraints appropriate to the parser and workload.","verification_method":"Generate valid inputs at increasing structural complexity and verify bounded resource use and controlled rejection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Denial of Service guidance","CWE-400"],"relationships":["D19-B06-R004","D19-B09-R004","D19-B88-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B88-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion Security","concept":"Security timeout versus resource budget","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why is a timeout alone not a complete defense against resource exhaustion?","answer":"A timeout limits elapsed processing time but may not constrain memory growth, concurrency, storage consumption, downstream requests, or cumulative work across many requests. Resource controls must address the relevant dimensions independently.","principle":"Security resource protection requires multidimensional budgets rather than a single elapsed-time limit.","verification_method":"Test resource-exhaustion scenarios that consume memory, concurrency, storage, and downstream work while remaining within individual request timeouts.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Denial of Service guidance","CWE-400"],"relationships":["D19-B48-R002","D19-B88-R001","D19-B88-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B88-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion Security","concept":"Cumulative resource abuse","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can individually acceptable requests still cause a security availability failure?","answer":"An attacker can issue many requests whose individual resource consumption stays below per-request limits but whose aggregate consumption exceeds system capacity.","failure_mode":"Per-request limits pass while aggregate concurrency, storage, queue depth, or downstream load becomes excessive.","root_cause":"Resource controls were evaluated only at request level rather than across relevant aggregation scopes.","solution":"Combine per-request limits with rate, concurrency, quota, queue, tenant, identity, and system-wide resource controls as required.","verification_method":"Generate distributed or repeated requests that individually remain within limits and measure aggregate resource behavior without inventing performance claims.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Denial of Service guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B46-R001","D19-B46-R004","D19-B88-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B88-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion Security","concept":"Resource exhaustion regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should resource-exhaustion security tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover input size, structural complexity, CPU, memory, storage, concurrency, queue depth, downstream work, retry amplification, and aggregate multi-request consumption.","expected_test_result":"Resource consumption remains within defined security budgets or terminates in a controlled manner without bypassing authorization or integrity requirements.","verification_method":"Execute bounded stress and adversarial resource cases with explicit measurement methodology; label results as measured only when actually executed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Denial of Service guidance","CWE-400"],"relationships":["D19-B88-R001","D19-B88-R002","D19-B88-R003","D19-B88-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B89-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Update and Patch Handling","concept":"Update authenticity verification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should software updates be authenticated before installation?","answer":"An update channel can be targeted to deliver malicious or modified artifacts. Authenticity verification establishes whether the artifact originated from an accepted source and has not been altered under the applicable trust model.","rule":"Software installation should verify artifact authenticity and integrity using the update system's approved trust mechanism before execution or activation.","failure_mode":"A modified or unauthorized update is installed because transport success was treated as sufficient trust.","root_cause":"Artifact authenticity was not independently verified.","solution":"Verify signed or otherwise authenticated update metadata and artifacts according to the authoritative update protocol.","verification_method":"Alter, replace, or substitute an update artifact and verify that installation is rejected.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-40","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B64-R001","D19-B89-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B89-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Update and Patch Handling","concept":"Update authorization versus update authenticity","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why are update authenticity and update authorization separate checks?","answer":"An artifact can be authentic according to a trusted signer while still being inappropriate for a particular environment, version, architecture, tenant, lifecycle state, or deployment policy.","principle":"Verified origin does not automatically establish that installation is authorized in the current context.","failure_mode":"A legitimately signed but inappropriate artifact is installed because signature verification was treated as complete deployment authorization.","root_cause":"Artifact authenticity and deployment policy were collapsed into one decision.","solution":"Verify both artifact trust and environment-specific update policy.","verification_method":"Present validly authenticated artifacts that violate version, environment, or deployment policy and verify rejection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-40","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B75-R003","D19-B89-R001","D19-B89-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B89-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Update and Patch Handling","concept":"Rollback security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can unrestricted software rollback create a security vulnerability?","answer":"Rollback can intentionally restore a vulnerable version or bypass security fixes. A recovery mechanism must therefore distinguish operational rollback from authorization to restore a known-vulnerable artifact.","failure_mode":"An operator or automated process restores an obsolete version containing a known security weakness without policy approval.","root_cause":"Rollback capability was unrestricted and not tied to security policy.","solution":"Constrain rollback to approved artifacts and require explicit policy for restoring versions with known security impact.","verification_method":"Attempt rollback to prohibited versions and verify enforcement of the update security policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-40","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B79-R002","D19-B89-R002","D19-B89-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B89-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Update and Patch Handling","concept":"Security patch state verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should patch installation status be verified rather than inferred from a deployment command?","answer":"A deployment command may succeed while the target fails to install, activate, restart, load, or enforce the updated security component. Verification must examine the actual resulting state relevant to the security property.","rule":"Update requested, update transferred, update installed, update activated, and security property restored are distinct states.","verification_method":"Verify artifact identity, installed version, activation state, configuration, and relevant security behavior after an update.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-40","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R001","D19-B79-R002","D19-B89-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B89-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Update and Patch Handling","concept":"Secure update regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should secure-update security tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover artifact tampering, signer trust, invalid signatures, unauthorized versions, rollback restrictions, activation failures, configuration preservation, dependency compatibility, and post-update security verification.","expected_test_result":"Only authorized and authenticated updates reach the trusted active state, and failures do not silently produce an insecure deployment.","verification_method":"Exercise valid, altered, revoked, incompatible, prohibited, and rollback update scenarios with explicit state verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-40","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B89-R001","D19-B89-R002","D19-B89-R003","D19-B89-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B90-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"D19 Integrated Assurance","concept":"Security control dependency graph","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"Why should security controls be represented as a dependency graph rather than as an isolated checklist?","answer":"Security properties often depend on multiple controls and assumptions. For example, an authorization decision may depend on identity validity, tenant context, policy freshness, resource ownership, and enforcement integrity. A dependency graph makes these relationships explicit and exposes common failure paths.","principle":"Security assurance should model dependencies between evidence, controls, assumptions, decisions, and enforcement outcomes.","verification_method":"Trace each high-impact security property backward to its dependencies and forward to its enforcement effect.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B20-R001","D19-B60-R001","D19-B85-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B90-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"D19 Integrated Assurance","concept":"Security control common-mode failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a common-mode security failure?","answer":"A common-mode failure occurs when multiple apparently independent security controls depend on the same underlying component, assumption, identity, configuration, parser, trust anchor, or data source, allowing one failure to defeat several controls simultaneously.","failure_mode":"Multiple security layers fail together because they share one compromised trust dependency.","root_cause":"Control independence was assumed without analyzing shared dependencies.","solution":"Identify common dependencies and add genuinely independent controls where the threat model requires them.","verification_method":"Construct the control dependency graph and identify nodes whose compromise simultaneously disables multiple security properties.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B05-R005","D19-B20-R004","D19-B90-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B90-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"D19 Integrated Assurance","concept":"Security evidence freshness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security evidence have a defined freshness requirement?","answer":"Security evidence can become stale when software, configuration, dependencies, identities, policies, threat conditions, or deployment topology change. Evidence that was valid previously may no longer support a current security claim.","rule":"Each security assurance claim should define when its supporting evidence must be revalidated.","verification_method":"Change a dependency or security-relevant configuration and verify that affected evidence is marked stale or requires revalidation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R002","D19-B79-R004","D19-B90-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B90-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"D19 Integrated Assurance","concept":"Security assurance closure criteria","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When can a security finding be considered closed?","answer":"A security finding should not be considered closed merely because code or configuration changed. Closure requires evidence that the identified security condition was addressed, the relevant behavior was tested or otherwise verified, regression risk was considered, and the resulting security claim is within its documented scope.","requirements":["Finding identity","Root cause","Remediation","Affected scope","Verification evidence","Regression assessment","Residual uncertainty"],"verification_method":"Audit a closed finding and trace it from original evidence through remediation to post-remediation verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B57-R003","D19-B58-R005","D19-B90-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B90-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"D19 Integrated Assurance","concept":"D19 B1-B90 pre-lock audit gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be audited before D19 can be declared complete and locked after B90?","answer":"The complete D19 dataset must undergo a whole-dataset audit for scope coverage, exact and semantic duplication, schema normalization, unsupported knowledge types, contradictions, version conflicts, provenance quality, target-language implementation coverage, invalid and corrected code coverage, security edge cases, test linkage, verification linkage, execution and validation truthfulness, UNKNOWN and CONFLICTED records, and cross-dataset relationships. Any remaining material gap must be recorded rather than hidden by record-count completion.","requirements":["Complete B1-B90 coverage audit","Exact and semantic deduplication","Schema normalization","Knowledge-type normalization","Contradiction audit","Version-aware audit","Provenance audit","Implementation and language coverage audit","Invalid/corrected code audit","Test and verification linkage audit","Execution/validation status audit","UNKNOWN/CONFLICTED audit","Cross-dataset relationship audit","Residual-gap report","Versioned lock report"],"constraints":["B90 does not itself prove D19 is LOCKED","Existing records must not be silently rewritten","Corrections require a new dataset version","No fabricated execution, benchmark, or verification evidence","Completion is determined by required coverage and quality gates rather than record count"],"verification_method":"Run the full D19 audit, identify all material gaps and contradictions, resolve or explicitly preserve them with provenance, validate schema and relationships, and produce a versioned lock report before assigning LOCKED status.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R005","D19-B70-R005","D19-B80-R005","D19-B90-R001","D19-B90-R002","D19-B90-R003","D19-B90-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B91-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Logging","concept":"Security event integrity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must security-critical logs be protected against unauthorized modification?","answer":"Security logs may become evidence for authorization decisions, incident investigation, compliance, and forensic reconstruction. If an attacker can silently alter security-critical records, downstream conclusions may be unreliable.","rule":"Security-critical event records require integrity protection appropriate to their threat model and trust boundary.","failure_mode":"An attacker modifies or deletes records describing authentication, authorization, privilege changes, or security configuration changes.","root_cause":"Logs were treated as ordinary application output without an integrity requirement.","solution":"Use controlled write paths, restricted access, append-oriented storage where appropriate, integrity mechanisms, and independent retention controls.","verification_method":"Attempt unauthorized modification or deletion of representative security records and verify detection or prevention according to the logging design.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B10-R004","D19-B78-R001","D19-B90-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B91-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Logging","concept":"Security event completeness","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is logging correctness different from logging completeness?","answer":"A correctly formatted event can still omit the evidence required to reconstruct a security decision or incident. Completeness therefore concerns whether the necessary security state transitions and relevant outcomes are represented, not merely whether emitted events are syntactically valid.","principle":"Security telemetry should be evaluated against explicit evidence requirements for the decisions and investigations it must support.","failure_mode":"Logs contain successful authentication events but omit privilege changes, authorization outcomes, or relevant resource identifiers needed for investigation.","root_cause":"Logging requirements were defined by implementation convenience rather than security evidence needs.","solution":"Define event requirements from security use cases and trace them to emitted, retained, and protected records.","verification_method":"Select representative security scenarios and determine whether the retained events are sufficient to reconstruct the required security state transitions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-92","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B77-R004","D19-B90-R001","D19-B91-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B91-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Logging","concept":"Security log injection","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can untrusted input corrupt security logs?","answer":"User-controlled values inserted into logs can introduce misleading delimiters, forged fields, control characters, or misleading messages. This can compromise log parsing, alerting, investigation, and evidence interpretation.","failure_mode":"An attacker submits input that creates log entries appearing to originate from another event or severity level.","root_cause":"Untrusted data was incorporated into structured or textual logs without safe encoding and schema separation.","solution":"Use structured logging with separate fields and safe serialization rather than constructing security records through ambiguous string concatenation.","verification_method":"Submit control characters, delimiter-like values, multiline content, and parser-sensitive strings and verify that event boundaries and fields remain unambiguous.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","CWE-117"],"relationships":["D19-B06-R005","D19-B91-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B91-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Logging","concept":"Sensitive data exclusion from security telemetry","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security logs avoid unnecessary secrets and sensitive payloads?","answer":"Security telemetry is often broadly replicated, retained, searched, exported, and accessed by operational systems. Recording unnecessary credentials, tokens, private data, or complete request bodies can therefore expand the impact of a logging compromise.","rule":"Log the minimum information required to support the security purpose and avoid storing secrets or sensitive payloads unless explicitly required and appropriately protected.","verification_method":"Review representative security events and classify each field according to necessity, sensitivity, retention, and access requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B05-R005","D19-B55-R001","D19-B91-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B91-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Logging","concept":"Security logging regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should security logging regression tests verify?","answer":"Tests should cover event generation, required fields, event ordering, untrusted-field encoding, sensitive-data exclusion, authorization events, privilege changes, integrity controls, access restrictions, retention, and failure behavior.","expected_test_result":"Required security evidence is generated accurately without exposing prohibited sensitive data or permitting ambiguous event interpretation.","verification_method":"Execute representative security workflows and inspect the resulting structured records against the defined evidence requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-92"],"relationships":["D19-B91-R001","D19-B91-R002","D19-B91-R003","D19-B91-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B92-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Forensic Readiness","concept":"Forensic evidence preservation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What does forensic readiness require before a security incident occurs?","answer":"Forensic readiness requires identifying evidence sources, retention requirements, access controls, timestamps, collection procedures, integrity mechanisms, and ownership before an incident makes the evidence necessary.","objective":"Ensure that relevant security evidence can be collected and interpreted reliably after an incident.","requirements":["Defined evidence sources","Retention policy","Time synchronization strategy","Access controls","Integrity protection","Collection ownership"],"verification_method":"Perform a controlled evidence-readiness exercise and determine whether predefined evidence sources can be collected without relying on undocumented emergency procedures.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-86"],"relationships":["D19-B57-R001","D19-B57-R002","D19-B91-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B92-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Forensic Readiness","concept":"Evidence collection versus evidence interpretation","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why must evidence collection be separated from incident conclusions?","answer":"Collected evidence is an observation or artifact; interpreting it into an incident hypothesis is a separate reasoning step. Treating an interpretation as if it were raw evidence can create confirmation bias and obscure uncertainty.","principle":"Preserve observations separately from hypotheses, interpretations, and conclusions.","verification_method":"Review an incident record and verify that raw evidence, derived observations, hypotheses, and conclusions are separately represented.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-86","NIST SP 800-61"],"relationships":["D19-B57-R003","D19-B60-R003","D19-B90-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B92-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Forensic Readiness","concept":"Time-source consistency","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is time consistency important for security investigations?","answer":"Security investigations often correlate events across hosts, services, identity systems, and infrastructure. Inconsistent or poorly understood timestamps can create false event ordering and incorrect causal conclusions.","rule":"Security event timestamps require a documented time source, representation, precision, and synchronization assumption appropriate to the investigation use case.","verification_method":"Correlate events generated by multiple components and verify that timestamp interpretation and uncertainty are explicitly preserved.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-86","NIST SP 800-92"],"relationships":["D19-B57-R002","D19-B77-R001","D19-B92-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B92-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Forensic Readiness","concept":"Evidence access separation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should evidence collection and evidence alteration privileges be separated where practical?","answer":"If the same identity can freely collect, modify, delete, and approve evidence, compromise of that identity can undermine the evidentiary record. Separation reduces the risk of unilateral manipulation.","rule":"Evidence access should follow least privilege and separation-of-duties requirements appropriate to the evidence's sensitivity and trust model.","verification_method":"Enumerate identities capable of reading, modifying, deleting, exporting, or approving evidence and verify that privileges match policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-86"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R005","D19-B91-R001","D19-B92-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B92-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Forensic Readiness","concept":"Forensic readiness regression test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should forensic readiness be tested?","answer":"A controlled exercise should generate representative security events, collect evidence through the documented process, preserve integrity, correlate timestamps, restrict evidence access, and determine whether the resulting record supports the intended investigative questions.","expected_test_result":"Required evidence can be collected and traced without relying on undocumented assumptions or unverified reconstruction.","verification_method":"Conduct a controlled incident simulation and compare the resulting evidence chain against predefined forensic requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-86"],"relationships":["D19-B92-R001","D19-B92-R002","D19-B92-R003","D19-B92-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B93-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Security","concept":"Backup confidentiality boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must backups receive security controls comparable to the data they contain?","answer":"Backups can contain historical copies of sensitive information, credentials, configuration, deleted records, and security-relevant state. Compromise of backups can therefore bypass controls protecting the live system.","rule":"Backup access, encryption, retention, deletion, and monitoring should reflect the sensitivity and recovery role of the backed-up data.","verification_method":"Classify representative backup contents and verify that backup identities and access policies meet the resulting security requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-34"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R002","D19-B89-R003","D19-B93-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B93-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Security","concept":"Backup integrity verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why is successful backup creation insufficient evidence of recoverability?","answer":"A backup can exist while being incomplete, corrupted, incompatible, inaccessible, or impossible to restore into a trustworthy state. Recovery assurance requires restoration testing and verification of the resulting security properties.","rule":"Backup assurance must distinguish backup creation, backup integrity, restoration, and post-restore security verification.","verification_method":"Restore representative backups in a controlled environment and verify data integrity, configuration, dependencies, access controls, and expected security state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B79-R002","D19-B89-R004","D19-B93-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B93-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Security","concept":"Recovery trust boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can restoring a backup reintroduce security weaknesses?","answer":"A backup may preserve vulnerable software, compromised configuration, stale credentials, unauthorized accounts, or malicious data. Restoration therefore creates a new security decision rather than simply reversing data loss.","failure_mode":"A compromised or obsolete backup is restored and becomes trusted production state.","root_cause":"Recovery was treated as inherently trustworthy without validating the restored state.","solution":"Verify backup provenance, integrity, software/security state, configuration, credentials, and required remediation before granting restored systems normal authority.","verification_method":"Restore backups containing deliberately outdated or altered security state and verify that the recovery process detects and handles the condition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B79-R002","D19-B89-R003","D19-B93-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B93-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Security","concept":"Backup deletion and retention security","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why must backup retention balance recovery requirements and data minimization?","answer":"Long retention can preserve useful recovery history but also extends the period during which sensitive historical data remains exposed. Short retention reduces exposure but can remove recovery or investigative capability.","tradeoff":"Recovery resilience and evidence availability must be balanced against confidentiality, privacy, retention, and deletion requirements.","verification_method":"Map backup retention periods to recovery objectives, security requirements, data classification, and deletion obligations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R004","D19-B93-R001","D19-B93-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B93-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Backup and Recovery Security","concept":"Backup security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should backup security tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover backup authorization, confidentiality, integrity, restoration, stale credentials, vulnerable software state, retention, deletion, provenance, recovery isolation, and post-restore verification.","expected_test_result":"Backups remain protected and recoverable while restored systems do not bypass security validation merely because the source backup was previously trusted.","verification_method":"Perform controlled backup creation, tampering, restoration, expiry, and recovery-isolation scenarios.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B93-R001","D19-B93-R002","D19-B93-R003","D19-B93-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B94-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Privacy-Aware Security Engineering","concept":"Purpose limitation for security data","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should security data collection have an explicit purpose?","answer":"Security systems can accumulate large quantities of identity, behavioral, network, and application data. Without a defined purpose, unnecessary collection increases exposure and makes access, retention, and governance harder to control.","principle":"Collect and retain security data according to explicit security purposes and documented requirements.","verification_method":"For each security data field, identify its purpose, consumers, retention requirement, sensitivity, and deletion or archival rule.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R001","D19-B55-R004","D19-B91-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B94-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Privacy-Aware Security Engineering","concept":"Security telemetry access minimization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should access to security telemetry be limited even when the telemetry was collected for legitimate security purposes?","answer":"Security telemetry may contain sensitive identity, behavior, network, application, or incident information. Legitimate collection does not imply universal access.","rule":"Telemetry access should be limited by role, purpose, tenant, sensitivity, and operational need.","verification_method":"Test telemetry access using identities with different roles and scopes and verify that unauthorized records and fields remain inaccessible.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B24-R005","D19-B55-R002","D19-B91-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B94-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Privacy-Aware Security Engineering","concept":"Security investigation data minimization","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can excessive security-investigation data create a security risk?","answer":"More data can improve investigative possibilities but increases exposure, access requirements, retention obligations, and the consequences of compromise. Investigation design therefore requires balancing evidentiary value with unnecessary collection.","tradeoff":"Investigation completeness must be balanced against confidentiality, privacy, retention, and access risk.","verification_method":"Review investigative data requirements and remove fields that do not materially support the defined investigation objectives.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","NIST SP 800-86"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R001","D19-B92-R001","D19-B94-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B94-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Privacy-Aware Security Engineering","concept":"Privacy-security control conflict","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"How should a conflict between security investigation needs and data-minimization requirements be represented?","answer":"The conflict should be represented explicitly as a requirement or policy tradeoff rather than silently choosing one side. The system should document purpose, authority, scope, retention, access, and residual uncertainty.","principle":"Conflicting security and privacy requirements should remain explicit and governed.","verification_method":"Review representative conflict cases and verify that the decision records both the security need and the data-protection constraint.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R003","D19-B90-R003","D19-B94-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B94-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Privacy-Aware Security Engineering","concept":"Privacy-aware security regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should privacy-aware security regression tests verify?","answer":"Tests should cover collection minimization, telemetry access, tenant boundaries, field-level sensitivity, retention, deletion, investigative access, exports, and unauthorized correlation of protected information.","expected_test_result":"Security functions retain required evidence while unnecessary or unauthorized sensitive-data exposure remains prevented.","verification_method":"Exercise security workflows under multiple roles, tenants, purposes, retention states, and data classifications.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B94-R001","D19-B94-R002","D19-B94-R003","D19-B94-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B95-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security State Integrity","concept":"Security state transition authorization","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security state transitions be explicitly authorized?","answer":"Security state changes such as account disablement, trust establishment, policy activation, recovery, quarantine, or privilege elevation can materially change the system's security posture. Treating them as ordinary data updates can bypass security controls.","rule":"Security-sensitive state transitions require explicit authorization, valid preconditions, and auditable outcomes.","verification_method":"Enumerate security state transitions and test each transition with authorized, unauthorized, invalid-precondition, and concurrent requests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B25-R003","D19-B70-R002","D19-B90-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B95-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security State Integrity","concept":"Security state monotonicity","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does security-state monotonicity mean in a security-sensitive workflow?","answer":"For a defined security property, some transitions should not silently move from a more restrictive or verified state to a weaker state without an explicit authorized transition. The exact monotonic property must be defined per workflow rather than assumed universally.","principle":"Security state invariants should explicitly define prohibited weakening transitions.","verification_method":"Model the relevant state machine and test every transition that could weaken a protected security property.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INFERRED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B10-R002","D19-B25-R003","D19-B95-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B95-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security State Integrity","concept":"Concurrent security-state transition conflict","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can concurrent security state changes violate otherwise correct security rules?","answer":"Two individually valid transitions can conflict when evaluated against the same stale state. Without atomicity, version checks, locking, or equivalent concurrency control, the final state can violate the intended security invariant.","failure_mode":"Two administrators concurrently modify a security policy and the final state loses one security restriction.","root_cause":"State transitions were validated independently without conflict detection or atomic commit semantics.","solution":"Use explicit concurrency control appropriate to the security invariant and reject stale or conflicting updates.","verification_method":"Execute controlled concurrent transitions against the same security state and verify invariant preservation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","D19 internal security-state model"],"relationships":["D19-B25-R001","D19-B25-R003","D19-B70-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B95-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security State Integrity","concept":"Security state recovery invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified after recovering security state from an error or failure?","answer":"Recovery should verify that security-relevant invariants, authorization boundaries, identities, configuration, auditability, and protected-resource state remain consistent. Successful state restoration alone does not prove security restoration.","rule":"Recovery verification must target security properties, not merely data availability.","verification_method":"Induce controlled state corruption or failure, perform recovery, and verify security invariants before returning the recovered component to trusted operation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-34","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B79-R002","D19-B93-R003","D19-B95-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B95-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security State Integrity","concept":"Security state-machine regression matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should security state-machine tests cover?","answer":"Tests should cover valid transitions, invalid transitions, authorization failures, stale state, concurrent updates, rollback, recovery, partial failure, and invariant preservation.","expected_test_result":"Every reachable state conforms to documented security invariants and every prohibited transition is prevented or explicitly governed.","verification_method":"Generate a transition matrix and exercise every security-relevant transition and failure path.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","D19 internal security-state model"],"relationships":["D19-B95-R001","D19-B95-R002","D19-B95-R003","D19-B95-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B96-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Architecture Assurance","concept":"Security assumption register","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should security assumptions be explicitly recorded?","answer":"Security controls often depend on assumptions about trusted components, network boundaries, identity providers, clocks, storage integrity, operators, or protocol behavior. If an assumption becomes false, the associated security claim may no longer hold.","rule":"Security-critical assumptions should be explicitly represented, scoped, sourced, and associated with the controls and claims that depend on them.","verification_method":"Review high-impact security claims and identify all assumptions required for them to remain valid.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","NIST SP 800-207"],"relationships":["D19-B02-R005","D19-B60-R003","D19-B90-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B96-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Architecture Assurance","concept":"Security claim scope","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must security claims have explicit scope?","answer":"A control may protect one resource, protocol, tenant, version, deployment mode, or threat model without protecting others. A claim without scope can be incorrectly generalized beyond the evidence supporting it.","rule":"Every significant security claim should specify the component, environment, threat model, version, and conditions for which it is asserted.","verification_method":"Sample security claims and determine whether their supporting evidence actually covers the stated scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R003","D19-B90-R003","D19-B96-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B96-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Architecture Assurance","concept":"Security evidence contradiction handling","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What should happen when two security evidence sources contradict each other?","answer":"The contradiction must remain explicit until resolved. Evidence should not be silently merged into a stronger claim. The system should preserve source identity, timestamps or versions where available, scope, confidence, and the unresolved conflict.","rule":"Contradictory security evidence must be represented as CONFLICTED or otherwise explicitly unresolved until evidence establishes a defensible resolution.","verification_method":"Introduce contradictory evidence for a controlled security claim and verify that the assurance layer preserves the contradiction instead of selecting an unsupported winner.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R003","D19-B90-R003","D19-B96-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B96-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Architecture Assurance","concept":"Security coverage gap representation","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"How should an important security property be represented when the available evidence does not establish it?","answer":"The property should remain explicitly UNKNOWN or otherwise unverified rather than being inferred from unrelated controls. Missing evidence is itself an assurance state that should be visible to downstream reasoning.","principle":"Absence of evidence is not evidence that the security property holds.","verification_method":"Create a security claim without supporting evidence and verify that downstream assurance logic does not promote it to VERIFIED_FACT.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R003","D19-B90-R003","D19-B96-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B96-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Architecture Assurance","concept":"Security architecture assurance matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a security architecture assurance matrix connect?","answer":"It should connect security claims to assets, threats, controls, assumptions, evidence, implementation points, tests, verification results, versions, and residual uncertainty.","expected_test_result":"Every high-impact security claim has traceable supporting evidence and every identified dependency or gap is explicit.","verification_method":"Build the matrix for representative high-impact claims and identify unsupported, contradictory, stale, or out-of-scope evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B90-R001","D19-B96-R001","D19-B96-R002","D19-B96-R003","D19-B96-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B97-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Representation","concept":"Security fact versus security inference","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why must observed security facts be separated from inferred security conclusions?","answer":"An observed event can support multiple explanations. Treating an inference as a fact removes uncertainty and can cause subsequent controls or investigations to rely on an unsupported premise.","principle":"Observed evidence, derived interpretation, hypothesis, and conclusion must remain distinguishable.","verification_method":"Trace a security conclusion backward and verify that each inference step is explicitly represented rather than encoded as an unqualified fact.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","NIST SP 800-61"],"relationships":["D19-B57-R005","D19-B60-R003","D19-B96-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B97-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Representation","concept":"Security confidence versus verification","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why does high confidence not equal verification?","answer":"Confidence represents belief strength under the available evidence, while verification represents a defined process that establishes whether a specific claim or behavior satisfies its verification criteria. A highly plausible claim may still be unverified.","rule":"Confidence must never be used as a substitute for verification status.","verification_method":"Construct a high-confidence but unverified claim and verify that the assurance system preserves its unverified state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R003","D19-B90-R003","D19-B96-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B97-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Representation","concept":"Security evidence version binding","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should security evidence be bound to software and policy versions when applicable?","answer":"A security result produced for one implementation or configuration may not apply after code, dependency, policy, protocol, or deployment changes. Version binding prevents stale evidence from being generalized to a different system state.","rule":"Security evidence should identify the implementation, configuration, policy, or environment version relevant to the claim whenever that dependency can materially affect validity.","verification_method":"Change a security-relevant version and verify that dependent evidence is either revalidated or marked stale/out-of-scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B18-R002","D19-B78-R002","D19-B90-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B97-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Representation","concept":"Security knowledge deduplication identity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must semantically duplicate security records be detected even when their wording differs?","answer":"Duplicate records can cause retrieval systems to overweight repeated claims, create contradictory variants, and waste model capacity. Deduplication must therefore consider identity, semantic meaning, implementation equivalence, version scope, and evidence provenance.","rule":"Security knowledge deduplication should distinguish true variants from repeated representations of the same underlying claim.","verification_method":"Compare records using canonical concepts, normalized constraints, implementation semantics, scope, version, and provenance before deciding whether they represent distinct knowledge.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R005","D19-B97-R001","D19-B97-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B97-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Security Knowledge Representation","concept":"Security knowledge retrieval evaluation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should retrieval quality for D19 security knowledge be evaluated?","answer":"Evaluation should use representative security queries spanning conceptual definitions, secure implementation, invalid implementation, debugging, testing, threat analysis, architecture, failure modes, version differences, and verification. Relevant records should be measured for retrieval precision, recall, coverage, and false retrieval where a benchmark is actually executed.","rule":"Retrieval quality must be evaluated against a defined query corpus rather than assumed from record count or embedding similarity alone.","verification_method":"Run a versioned retrieval evaluation corpus and record measured results; do not fabricate metrics when evaluation has not been executed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R005","D19-B97-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B98-R001","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Implementation Assurance","concept":"Code applicability classification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must each D19 topic be classified by code applicability?","answer":"Some security concepts require concrete implementation examples while others are primarily architectural, governance, or evidence concepts. Explicit classification prevents both under-generation of necessary code knowledge and meaningless code padding.","rule":"Each D19 topic should be classified as CODE_HEAVY, CODE_RELEVANT, CODE_SUPPORTING, CODE_LIMITED, or CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE.","verification_method":"Audit each topic and compare its applicability classification with its implementation and code-example coverage.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B30-R005","D19-B60-R005","D19-B97-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B98-R002","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Implementation Assurance","concept":"Invalid-code evidence completeness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must accompany an invalid security-sensitive code example?","answer":"An invalid example should identify the failure, observable symptom, likely causes where relevant, root cause when established, corrected approach, applicable security property, and verification method. It must not be presented as executable verified behavior unless actually tested.","requirements":["Invalid implementation","Failure or vulnerability","Root cause when established","Corrected implementation or pattern","Testing guidance","Verification guidance","Execution status"],"verification_method":"Audit invalid-code records for causal explanation, correction, testing linkage, and truthful execution status.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B07-R005","D19-B30-R005","D19-B98-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B98-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Implementation Assurance","concept":"Execution-status truthfulness","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must execution status remain separate from validation status?","answer":"Code can be generated without execution, executed without passing, or pass a narrow test without satisfying the broader security requirement. Combining these states produces false assurance.","rule":"Execution status and validation status must independently represent what was actually executed and what was actually established.","verification_method":"Audit records for unsupported claims such as EXECUTED_PASSED or VERIFIED when no corresponding evidence exists.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R005","D19-B97-R002","D19-B98-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B98-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Secure Implementation Assurance","concept":"Security performance claim classification","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"How should security-related performance claims be represented?","answer":"Performance claims should distinguish MEASURED, ESTIMATED, INFERRED, and UNKNOWN. A theoretical expectation or generated example must not be presented as an executed benchmark.","rule":"No security-performance benchmark may be labeled measured without actual execution and recorded methodology.","verification_method":"Audit performance-related records for measurement methodology, execution evidence, environment, workload, and result provenance.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B17-R003","D19-B88-R005","D19-B98-R003"]}

