{"record_id":"D19-B100-R003","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Final Dataset Completion","concept":"D19 machine-readability gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be verified for D19 to remain directly usable by the Autonomous Software Engineering Brain?","answer":"Every record must be machine-parseable, uniquely identifiable, schema-compatible, relationship-resolvable, semantically classified, provenance-bearing, version-aware, and explicit about uncertainty and verification state. Human-readable interpretation must not be required to recover critical security semantics.","verification_method":"Parse the full dataset, validate identifiers and relationships, enumerate schema violations, validate controlled vocabularies, and verify that required security semantics are represented structurally.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B97-R004","D19-B98-R001","D19-B99-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B100-R004","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Final Dataset Completion","concept":"D19 regression baseline","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should become the regression baseline once D19 is audited?","answer":"The authoritative D19 version should preserve stable record identities, normalized schema, deduplication decisions, contradiction classifications, provenance, relationship mappings, coverage results, and known limitations so future changes can be evaluated against the same baseline.","rule":"Future D19 changes require versioned comparison and regression validation against the authoritative locked baseline.","verification_method":"Generate a baseline manifest containing record identities, hashes or equivalent stable fingerprints, relationship inventory, schema version, and audit results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B60-R005","D19-B97-R003","D19-B99-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D19-B100-R005","dataset_id":"D19","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Security Engineering","subtopic":"Final Dataset Completion","concept":"D19 final lock protocol","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is the correct final process for locking D19?","answer":"B100 completes the planned generation sequence but does not itself constitute proof of lock. The complete D19 dataset must first pass structural validation, semantic deduplication, contradiction analysis, provenance audit, implementation/code applicability audit, testing and verification linkage audit, relationship validation, uncertainty audit, residual-gap review, and regression-baseline creation. Only then should a new authoritative dataset version be designated LOCKED.","requirements":["Complete B1-B100 dataset","Whole-dataset structural validation","Exact and semantic deduplication","Schema normalization","Canonical knowledge-type normalization","Contradiction/version/scope audit","Provenance audit","Implementation and language coverage audit","Invalid/corrected implementation audit","Test and verification linkage audit","Execution/validation truthfulness audit","UNKNOWN/CONFLICTED audit","Cross-dataset relationship audit","Residual-gap register","Regression baseline","Versioned lock report"],"constraints":["B100 generation completion is not equivalent to LOCKED status","No silent modification of existing records","Corrections require a new dataset version","No fabricated execution or verification","No padding to increase record count","Any material unresolved gap must remain explicit"],"expected_behavior":"A successful audit produces a reproducible D19 lock report identifying the authoritative version, coverage, quality findings, unresolved limitations, and baseline identity. If any material quality gate fails, D19 remains NOT_LOCKED until corrected through a new version and re-audit.","verification_method":"Execute the complete D19 audit and lock protocol. Assign LOCKED only after all mandatory gates pass; otherwise preserve NOT_LOCKED with an explicit defect or gap register.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D19-B90-R005","D19-B99-R001","D19-B99-R002","D19-B99-R003","D19-B99-R004","D19-B99-R005","D19-B100-R001","D19-B100-R002","D19-B100-R003","D19-B100-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B01-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Software Testing Foundations","subtopic":"Testing Purpose","concept":"Testing as evidence generation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the fundamental purpose of software testing?","answer":"Software testing provides evidence about observed behavior under defined conditions. A test result establishes what happened for the tested inputs, environment, configuration, and assertions; it does not by itself prove universal correctness.","principle":"Treat test execution as bounded evidence about specified behavior rather than as proof that software is universally correct.","requirements":["Defined behavior under test","Controlled or documented inputs","Observable expected result","Explicit pass/fail criteria"],"failure_mode":"A project interprets a passing test suite as proof that all possible inputs and environments are correct.","root_cause":"Test evidence was generalized beyond the scope of the tested conditions.","solution":"Record test scope, assumptions, environment, coverage limitations, and verification criteria separately from the pass result.","testing_guidance":"For every important test suite, identify the behavior, input domain, environment assumptions, oracle, and limitations.","verification_method":"Inspect whether test results explicitly identify the behavior and conditions they establish.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["IEEE 29119 Software Testing","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R002","D20-B01-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B01-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Software Testing Foundations","subtopic":"Test Oracle","concept":"Test oracle definition","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a test oracle?","answer":"A test oracle is the mechanism or criterion used to determine whether observed behavior is acceptable for a given test. The oracle may be an exact expected value, an invariant, a reference implementation, a model, a property, a contract, or another explicitly justified criterion.","principle":"A test without a meaningful oracle may execute successfully while failing to establish whether the behavior is correct.","edge_case":"For nondeterministic or distributed behavior, the oracle may need to express allowed properties or ranges instead of one exact output.","verification_method":"Determine what assertion or criterion converts the observed test result into pass or fail and verify that the criterion is justified by the intended behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R001","D20-B02-R003","D20-B03-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B01-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Software Testing Foundations","subtopic":"Test Scope","concept":"Test level versus test purpose","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should test level and test purpose not be treated as the same classification?","answer":"Unit, integration, system, and acceptance describe levels or scopes at which testing can occur, while purposes such as regression, security, performance, compatibility, and fault handling describe what the test is intended to establish. The dimensions can intersect.","principle":"Represent test scope and test purpose as separate dimensions.","example":{"test_scope":"integration","test_purpose":"regression","behavior":"authorization decision remains consistent across service boundary"},"failure_mode":"A team assumes that an integration test automatically provides regression coverage or that a unit test cannot test a security property.","root_cause":"Test classification collapsed independent dimensions into one label.","solution":"Store scope, purpose, target behavior, oracle, and environment independently.","verification_method":"Audit test metadata and determine whether scope and purpose can be queried independently.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R001","D20-B01-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B01-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Software Testing Foundations","subtopic":"Test Levels","concept":"Unit test boundary","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes a test meaningfully unit-scoped?","answer":"A unit-scoped test focuses on a defined software unit and controls or isolates surrounding dependencies sufficiently that failures can be attributed primarily to the unit under test. The exact unit boundary depends on architecture and language rather than on a universal line-count or function-size rule.","rule":"Define the unit boundary by the behavior and dependency isolation needed to produce useful failure attribution.","failure_mode":"A purported unit test depends on live databases, networks, external services, clocks, or global state so heavily that failures cannot be attributed to the unit.","root_cause":"The test boundary was defined by naming convention rather than diagnostic usefulness.","solution":"Control relevant dependencies using appropriate seams, fakes, stubs, deterministic fixtures, or other justified isolation mechanisms.","testing_guidance":"A unit test should make failure localization substantially easier without replacing the need for higher-level tests.","verification_method":"Intentionally break the unit and selected dependencies separately and determine whether the test distinguishes the resulting failures.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R003","D20-B02-R002","D20-B04-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B01-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Software Testing Foundations","subtopic":"Test Evidence","concept":"Pass result versus requirement satisfaction","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why does a passing test not necessarily prove that the software satisfies the complete requirement?","answer":"A test can pass because its assertion is incomplete, its inputs cover only a narrow subset, its environment differs from production, or the requirement contains behavior that the test does not exercise. Passing therefore establishes the tested assertion, subject to test validity, rather than the complete requirement.","failure_mode":"A requirement is marked fully satisfied because one narrow test passed.","root_cause":"Requirement-to-test traceability and coverage boundaries were not evaluated.","solution":"Map requirements to representative positive, negative, boundary, failure, security, performance, and integration tests where applicable.","verification_method":"For a selected requirement, enumerate its acceptance conditions and determine whether the test set covers each material condition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R001","D20-B02-R004","D20-B10-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B02-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Test Design","subtopic":"Equivalence Partitioning","concept":"Behaviorally distinct input partitions","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does equivalence partitioning improve test design?","answer":"Equivalence partitioning divides an input domain into classes expected to behave similarly under the specified rules, allowing representative tests to cover distinct behavioral classes without enumerating every possible input.","objective":"Reduce redundant tests while retaining representation of materially different input behavior.","requirements":["Explicit input domain","Defined validity rules","Behaviorally meaningful partitions"],"edge_case":"Partitions must change when business rules distinguish values that initially appear equivalent.","failure_mode":"A partition is defined only by data type while security, boundary, or business rules create materially different behavior inside it.","root_cause":"Partitioning was based on syntax rather than behavioral equivalence.","solution":"Partition according to the actual decision rules, constraints, security properties, and failure behavior.","testing_guidance":"For each partition, select at least one representative and separately cover boundaries where behavior changes.","verification_method":"Review each partition against the implementation's decision branches and requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R002","D20-B02-R003","D20-B03-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B02-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Test Design","subtopic":"Boundary Value Analysis","concept":"Boundary behavior","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are boundaries disproportionately important in test design?","answer":"Software behavior frequently changes at limits such as minimum and maximum values, length constraints, time windows, collection sizes, numeric ranges, and authorization thresholds. Boundary-focused tests target transitions where implementation mistakes commonly change behavior.","principle":"Test the transition points between valid and invalid or between materially different behavioral regions.","edge_case":"The relevant boundary may be exclusive, inclusive, transformed, or dependent on multiple variables.","failure_mode":"Only typical interior values are tested while limit and just-outside-limit behavior remains untested.","root_cause":"The test set represents common usage but not rule transitions.","solution":"Identify each material boundary and test values on both sides plus the boundary itself where meaningful.","verification_method":"Compare boundary tests against explicit requirement inequalities and implementation predicates.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R001","D20-B02-R004","D20-B03-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B02-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Test Design","subtopic":"Property-Based Testing","concept":"Property-based test oracle","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is property-based testing useful?","answer":"Property-based testing is useful when correctness can be expressed through general properties that should hold across many generated inputs rather than only through a small set of fixed examples.","objective":"Explore broad input spaces while checking invariant or algebraic properties.","example":{"property":"serializing then parsing a supported value preserves its defined semantic value","input_strategy":"generate values from the supported domain","oracle":"parsed semantic value equals the original semantic value"},"failure_mode":"Generated inputs include values outside the documented domain and failures are incorrectly interpreted as implementation defects.","root_cause":"Input generation constraints were not aligned with the specification.","solution":"Define generators from the valid domain and separately create explicit invalid-domain properties.","testing_guidance":"Record the generated input strategy, domain assumptions, property, shrinking/minimization behavior where applicable, and failure reproduction data.","verification_method":"Confirm that a discovered counterexample can be deterministically reproduced from its recorded input or seed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing","property-based testing literature"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R002","D20-B02-R001","D20-B02-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B02-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Test Design","subtopic":"Decision Tables","concept":"Combinatorial business rules","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is a decision table useful for testing?","answer":"Decision tables are useful when behavior depends on combinations of conditions and actions, especially when individual conditions are simple but their interactions create materially different outcomes.","objective":"Make condition combinations explicit and identify missing or contradictory rule combinations.","requirements":["Enumerated conditions","Enumerated outcomes/actions","Defined combination semantics"],"failure_mode":"Tests cover each condition independently but omit combinations that activate different outcomes.","root_cause":"The rule system was modeled as independent conditions instead of a combination space.","solution":"Represent condition combinations explicitly and select tests for every materially distinct rule outcome.","verification_method":"Compare the decision table against implementation branches and requirements for missing, overlapping, or unreachable combinations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R001","D20-B02-R005","D20-B03-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B02-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Test Design","subtopic":"Model-Based Test Design","concept":"State-model-derived tests","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a state model improve testing of stateful software?","answer":"A state model makes valid states, transitions, guards, and terminal or recovery states explicit. Tests can then target both valid transitions and prohibited or failure transitions instead of testing isolated operations without lifecycle context.","objective":"Generate tests from explicit state-transition behavior.","failure_mode":"Each operation is tested independently while illegal sequences and lifecycle-dependent behavior remain untested.","root_cause":"The test design ignored state and sequence semantics.","solution":"Model relevant states and transitions and derive tests for valid, invalid, repeated, concurrent, recovery, and terminal transitions where applicable.","verification_method":"Compare the model's transition set with the implemented state machine and map each security- or correctness-relevant transition to tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing","model-based testing literature"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R004","D20-B03-R005","D20-B05-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B03-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Unit Testing","subtopic":"Test Isolation","concept":"Dependency isolation without behavior substitution","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should dependency isolation preserve in a unit test?","answer":"Isolation should control dependencies needed to make the unit deterministic and diagnostically useful without replacing the behavior of the unit under test. Excessive mocking can cause the test to verify an artificial interaction model instead of actual behavior.","principle":"Isolate external effects while preserving the real logic whose correctness the unit test is intended to establish.","failure_mode":"A test passes because mocks reproduce the expected calls even though the production implementation would fail against the real dependency contract.","root_cause":"The mock encoded the assumed behavior instead of verifying the unit's contract against an appropriate boundary.","solution":"Mock or fake only where isolation provides value, and complement unit tests with contract/integration tests for real dependency behavior.","verification_method":"Compare mocked assumptions with the actual dependency contract and identify behavior that remains untested by the unit suite.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R004","D20-B03-R002","D20-B04-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B03-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Unit Testing","subtopic":"Fixtures","concept":"Deterministic test fixture","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What makes a test fixture reliable?","answer":"A reliable fixture establishes the required starting state deterministically, exposes only the state needed by the test, cleans up correctly, and does not create hidden coupling between tests.","requirements":["Explicit setup","Deterministic state","Controlled dependencies","Cleanup or isolation","Minimal required data"],"failure_mode":"Tests pass or fail depending on execution order because shared fixture state leaks between tests.","root_cause":"Fixture lifecycle and ownership were not isolated.","solution":"Create independent setup state, avoid mutable global fixtures unless explicitly controlled, and verify cleanup or reset semantics.","testing_guidance":"Run tests in different orders and, where practical, independently to detect fixture coupling.","verification_method":"Repeat the same test under fresh and reused fixture conditions and compare outcomes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B04-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B03-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Unit Testing","subtopic":"Exception Testing","concept":"Failure contract testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a unit test verify when an API is expected to reject invalid input?","answer":"The test should verify the defined failure contract, including exception or error type, relevant error semantics, state changes, side effects, and whether invalid input is prevented before protected operations occur.","expected_behavior":"Invalid input is rejected according to the defined contract without unintended state mutation or unsafe side effects.","failure_mode":"The test only asserts that some exception occurred and misses an incorrect exception type or partial state mutation.","root_cause":"Failure behavior was treated as a binary crash rather than a defined contract.","solution":"Assert the meaningful failure contract and inspect relevant post-failure state.","testing_guidance":"Include representative invalid classes and boundary-invalid inputs.","verification_method":"Compare the test assertions with the documented error contract and state invariants.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 Software Testing"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R002","D20-B03-R002","D20-B03-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B03-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Unit Testing","subtopic":"Test Smells","concept":"Over-mocking","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can excessive mocking weaken unit-test value?","answer":"Excessive mocking can couple tests to implementation details, hide integration defects, and allow tests to pass even when real dependencies violate assumptions. The problem is not mocking itself but replacing too much behavior with assumptions encoded by the test.","failure_mode":"A refactor changes internal call structure without changing behavior and causes many tests to fail, while an incompatible real dependency contract remains undetected.","root_cause":"Tests assert implementation interactions rather than externally meaningful behavior or stable contracts.","solution":"Prefer assertions on observable behavior and use mocks only at boundaries where isolation is justified.","testing_guidance":"Review mocks for whether they represent stable contracts or merely internal call sequences.","verification_method":"Refactor an implementation without changing its external behavior and assess whether the test suite remains appropriately stable.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software testing literature","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B04-R001","D20-B06-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B03-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Unit Testing","subtopic":"Determinism","concept":"Test nondeterminism","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What causes a test to become nondeterministic?","answer":"Common causes include uncontrolled time, randomness, concurrency scheduling, external services, shared mutable state, environment-dependent behavior, filesystem ordering, network availability, and asynchronous race conditions.","failure_mode":"The same test alternates between pass and fail without a relevant code change.","root_cause":"The test depends on uncontrolled environmental or scheduling variables.","solution":"Identify the nondeterministic dependency, control it where appropriate, record necessary randomness seeds or timing assumptions, and retain integration tests for behavior that must remain genuinely nondeterministic.","testing_guidance":"Repeat failing tests under controlled and uncontrolled conditions to isolate the source of nondeterminism.","verification_method":"Demonstrate reproducibility after controlling the identified variable without weakening the intended behavior under test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software testing engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R002","D20-B05-R001","D20-B07-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B04-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Integration Testing","subtopic":"Contract Testing","concept":"Provider-consumer contract","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What does a contract test establish between software components?","answer":"A contract test checks that an independently defined interface expectation remains compatible between a provider and its consumer. It focuses on the agreed interaction contract rather than requiring every end-to-end dependency to be exercised for every change.","objective":"Detect interface incompatibilities at component boundaries.","requirements":["Defined interface contract","Provider behavior","Consumer expectations","Version or compatibility scope"],"failure_mode":"Provider and consumer tests each pass independently while their actual interface assumptions differ.","root_cause":"The interface contract was not tested as a shared boundary.","solution":"Represent the contract explicitly and test provider responses and consumer expectations against it.","verification_method":"Change a contract field or behavior incompatibly and verify that the relevant contract test detects the mismatch.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","contract-testing engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B04-R002","D20-B06-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B04-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Integration Testing","subtopic":"Integration Failure Attribution","concept":"Boundary failure localization","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an integration test failure be localized?","answer":"Identify the first violated contract or observable boundary, then distinguish producer behavior, transport or infrastructure behavior, consumer interpretation, environment configuration, and test-harness faults. The failing test alone does not establish which layer is defective.","procedure":["Capture input and request context","Identify boundary contract","Compare actual versus expected behavior","Determine first observable divergence","Trace dependency/environment evidence","Assign root cause only when supported"],"failure_mode":"A downstream assertion failure is automatically attributed to the downstream component.","root_cause":"Failure localization was based on test location rather than causal evidence.","solution":"Trace the failure from the first violated contract and preserve intermediate observations.","verification_method":"Use a controlled fault injection at different integration layers and verify that the diagnostic procedure identifies the changed layer.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software debugging engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R001","D20-B04-R001","D20-B05-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B04-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Integration Testing","subtopic":"Database Integration","concept":"Transaction-boundary testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should database integration tests verify beyond returned values?","answer":"They should verify transaction boundaries, commit or rollback behavior, constraint enforcement, isolation assumptions, resource cleanup, and the persisted state required by the application contract.","failure_mode":"A test asserts a returned object but never verifies whether the transaction committed correctly or whether partial state remained after failure.","root_cause":"The test treated persistence as an implementation detail rather than part of the observable contract.","solution":"Assert relevant persistent state and failure atomicity at the integration boundary.","verification_method":"Inject a failure between representative database operations and verify the resulting transaction state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","database transaction engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R003","D20-B04-R002","D20-B05-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B04-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Integration Testing","subtopic":"External Dependencies","concept":"Controlled external-service testing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should external services be tested without making the entire suite dependent on their availability?","answer":"Use a layered strategy: isolated tests for local logic, contract or protocol tests for the interface, controlled integration tests against the real dependency where valuable, and failure tests for unavailable or degraded dependency behavior.","tradeoff":"More isolation improves determinism and speed, while real dependency tests reveal compatibility and environment defects.","failure_mode":"All tests use mocks and therefore miss real protocol or configuration incompatibilities.","root_cause":"Isolation was treated as a substitute for integration validation.","solution":"Retain a deliberate subset of real-dependency tests and make their environment requirements explicit.","verification_method":"Compare mocked contract behavior with selected real-dependency executions and investigate any divergence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software integration testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B04-R001","D20-B04-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B04-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Integration Testing","subtopic":"Integration Test Isolation","concept":"Test environment contamination","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can one integration test contaminate another?","answer":"Shared databases, queues, files, accounts, caches, network resources, environment variables, or persistent service state can survive one test and change the initial conditions of another.","failure_mode":"A test passes alone but fails when executed after another test.","root_cause":"The environment contains shared mutable state without reliable isolation or cleanup.","solution":"Use isolated namespaces, transactional rollback, unique test identifiers, controlled cleanup, disposable environments, or equivalent mechanisms appropriate to the resource.","testing_guidance":"Run tests in different orders and in repeated fresh environments to expose contamination.","verification_method":"Identify shared state resources and demonstrate that resetting them restores deterministic outcomes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software integration testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R002","D20-B03-R005","D20-B04-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B05-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Test Reliability","subtopic":"Flaky Tests","concept":"Flaky-test classification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a flaky test?","answer":"A flaky test is a test whose outcome changes across repeated executions under materially equivalent conditions without a corresponding intended behavior change. Flakiness is a property of observed test reliability, not a synonym for any test failure.","failure_mode":"A team repeatedly reruns a failing test until it passes and records the pass without investigating outcome variability.","root_cause":"The source of nondeterminism was not identified.","solution":"Capture repeated outcomes, environment, timing, concurrency, randomness, dependencies, and test history; classify the cause before deciding on remediation.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled repetition and preserve failure artifacts rather than masking failures with automatic retries alone.","verification_method":"Demonstrate stable behavior across repeated executions after the causal source of flakiness is addressed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software testing engineering practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R005","D20-B05-R002","D20-B05-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B05-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Test Reliability","subtopic":"Failure Reproduction","concept":"Minimal reproducible test failure","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why should a failing test be reduced to the smallest useful reproducer?","answer":"A minimal reproducer removes irrelevant variables, improves causal analysis, accelerates debugging, and creates a durable regression test once the defect is understood.","procedure":["Preserve the original failure evidence","Identify required inputs and environment","Remove irrelevant setup","Reduce input size or execution path","Repeat until further reduction changes the failure","Record the minimized case"],"failure_mode":"Debugging remains dependent on a large production scenario containing many unrelated variables.","root_cause":"The failure was never reduced to its causal conditions.","solution":"Minimize the input, state, dependency set, and execution path while preserving the failure.","verification_method":"Confirm that the reduced case reproduces the original defect and that further removal eliminates or materially changes it.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DEBUGGING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software debugging and testing engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B04-R002","D20-B05-R003","D20-B06-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B05-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Test Reliability","subtopic":"Regression Tests","concept":"Regression test as defect memory","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What makes a regression test valuable after a defect is fixed?","answer":"A useful regression test preserves the previously violated behavior contract and fails if a future change reintroduces the same defect class. It should encode the causal condition or invariant rather than merely reproduce an incidental implementation detail.","principle":"Convert verified defect knowledge into a stable executable check at the appropriate test level.","failure_mode":"A regression test asserts an incidental internal detail and therefore survives only until the next harmless refactor or fails to detect the actual defect.","root_cause":"The test captured implementation shape rather than the violated behavior.","solution":"Write the regression test around the externally relevant invariant or failure contract.","verification_method":"Reintroduce the original defect in a controlled branch and verify that the regression test fails; then apply the fix and verify that it passes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software regression-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R003","D20-B05-R002","D20-B06-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B05-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Test Reliability","subtopic":"Retry Behavior","concept":"Test retries versus test correctness","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can automatic test retries hide defects?","answer":"Retries can convert intermittent failures into apparent passes without establishing whether the underlying cause is harmless environmental noise or a real correctness, concurrency, reliability, or infrastructure defect.","failure_mode":"A test fails once, passes on retry, and the CI system reports success without preserving the initial failure.","root_cause":"Retry policy was used as a result transformation rather than as an explicitly bounded diagnostic mechanism.","solution":"Preserve initial failures, record retry outcomes separately, and establish a policy for when retries are acceptable.","verification_method":"Inspect CI results and verify that initial failures remain observable and classified even when a subsequent retry passes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software testing engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B05-R001","D18-B49-R001","D20-B05-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B05-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Test Reliability","subtopic":"Test Observability","concept":"Failure artifact preservation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should be preserved when an automated test fails?","answer":"Depending on the test, useful artifacts can include input data, expected and actual results, stack traces, logs, environment metadata, dependency versions, random seeds, timestamps, test order, screenshots or traces, and relevant state snapshots.","rule":"Failure artifacts must be sufficient to support reproduction and causal analysis without exposing prohibited sensitive data.","security_implications":"Test artifacts can contain credentials, tokens, personal data, production data, or secrets and therefore require security controls.","verification_method":"Review representative failure artifacts and determine whether they support reproduction while respecting data-handling requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software testing engineering practice"],"relationships":["D19-B91-R004","D20-B05-R001","D20-B05-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B06-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Change Impact","concept":"Risk-based regression selection","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should regression tests be selected after a code change?","answer":"Regression selection should consider changed code, dependency relationships, affected interfaces, data flows, security properties, historical failures, configuration impact, and the consequences of incorrect behavior. Running every test is valuable when practical, but selection should not rely solely on file-level proximity.","procedure":["Identify changed behavior and dependencies","Map affected requirements and interfaces","Identify security and data boundaries","Review historical defects","Select targeted regression tests","Run broader suites according to risk and system cost"],"failure_mode":"Only tests located in the changed file are executed while affected downstream behavior remains untested.","root_cause":"Impact analysis was reduced to source-file proximity.","solution":"Build regression scope from behavioral and dependency impact.","verification_method":"Compare selected tests with dependency and requirement graphs for representative changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software regression-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B05-R003","D20-B06-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B06-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Regression Scope","concept":"Behavioral impact graph","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can dependency graphs improve regression selection?","answer":"A behavioral dependency graph connects changed implementation points to callers, data consumers, interfaces, policies, and tests. It helps identify affected behavior that is not necessarily located near the modified source code.","objective":"Select regression tests from behavioral impact rather than textual proximity alone.","failure_mode":"A low-level parser change breaks downstream authorization or serialization behavior but only parser-local tests are run.","root_cause":"Regression analysis lacked dependency propagation.","solution":"Maintain or derive relationships between implementation, contracts, components, requirements, and tests.","verification_method":"Introduce a controlled change with a known downstream effect and verify that graph-based selection identifies the affected test region.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software testing and dependency-analysis practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R001","D19-B59-R004","D20-B10-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B06-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Refactoring","concept":"Behavior-preserving refactoring test strategy","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should testing establish after a behavior-preserving refactor?","answer":"Testing should establish that the intended external behavior, contracts, error semantics, security properties, and relevant performance constraints remain within the defined acceptance boundaries while allowing internal implementation structure to change.","failure_mode":"Tests fail because internal structure changed even though behavior remained correct, or tests pass while a public contract changed unintentionally.","root_cause":"Tests were either overly coupled to implementation or insufficiently coupled to observable behavior.","solution":"Use behavior-focused tests supplemented by targeted contract, security, and integration checks.","verification_method":"Compare pre- and post-refactor behavior against explicit contracts and run targeted regression tests derived from the change impact.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software refactoring testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R004","D20-B05-R003","D24"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B06-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Bug Reproduction","concept":"Regression test lifecycle","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is the correct relationship between defect reproduction and regression testing?","answer":"A defect should first be reproduced sufficiently to establish the failure condition. Once the expected behavior and root cause are understood, a regression test should preserve the violated condition and remain linked to the defect or requirement.","procedure":["Reproduce defect","Capture failing evidence","Identify expected behavior","Determine causal condition","Encode regression test","Verify test fails before fix where practical","Apply fix","Verify test passes","Retain test in regression suite"],"verification_method":"Trace a regression test from original defect evidence through fix verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software regression-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B05-R002","D20-B05-R003","D20-B06-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B06-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Regression Testing","subtopic":"Regression Suite Maintenance","concept":"Obsolete regression test detection","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should regression suites be reviewed for obsolete tests?","answer":"A test can become obsolete when its requirement is removed, an interface is retired, its assertion no longer represents intended behavior, or a stronger test subsumes it. Keeping obsolete tests indefinitely increases maintenance cost and can obscure meaningful failures.","failure_mode":"A test remains because it has always existed even though the behavior it asserts is no longer required.","root_cause":"Test suites were treated as append-only collections without lifecycle management.","solution":"Periodically classify tests as active, redundant, obsolete, flaky, or requiring redesign, while preserving evidence for historical requirements when needed.","verification_method":"Map candidate tests to current requirements, behavior, dependencies, and failure history before removal or replacement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software test-maintenance practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R001","D20-B06-R003","D20-B10-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B07-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Failure-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Negative Testing","concept":"Negative-path coverage","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must tests include invalid and failure conditions?","answer":"Software correctness includes how the system rejects invalid input, handles dependency failure, preserves invariants during errors, and reports failures. Testing only successful paths leaves important behavior unspecified and unverified.","principle":"Test valid behavior and relevant invalid, failure, boundary, and recovery behavior according to the system's risk and contract.","failure_mode":"The implementation works for valid requests but corrupts state or leaks information when validation or dependencies fail.","root_cause":"Failure behavior was not treated as part of the contract.","solution":"Define negative-path tests for important invalid inputs, dependency failures, authorization failures, resource exhaustion, and recovery conditions.","verification_method":"Review requirements and failure modes and verify that material negative paths have explicit tests or documented rationale for exclusion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software reliability testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R003","D20-B04-R002","D19-B30-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B07-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Failure-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Fault Injection","concept":"Controlled fault injection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of fault injection testing?","answer":"Fault injection deliberately introduces controlled failures into dependencies, resources, timing, communication, or components to determine whether the system handles expected failure conditions safely and according to its resilience contract.","objective":"Validate failure-handling and recovery behavior under controlled adverse conditions.","failure_mode":"The system appears reliable under normal conditions but fails unsafely when a dependency becomes unavailable or returns malformed behavior.","root_cause":"Failure handling was inferred rather than exercised.","solution":"Inject bounded, reproducible faults at relevant boundaries and verify expected containment, fallback, retry, timeout, rollback, or recovery behavior.","security_implications":"Fault injection must not expose production systems or create uncontrolled destructive effects.","verification_method":"Run controlled failure scenarios in an isolated environment and compare observed behavior with the documented failure contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software resilience testing practice"],"relationships":["D18-B47-R001","D18-B49-R001","D20-B07-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B07-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Failure-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency Testing","concept":"Schedule-sensitive defects","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can concurrency defects evade ordinary repeated tests?","answer":"A race condition may depend on a narrow execution interleaving that ordinary runs rarely produce. Repeating the same workload without controlling or increasing schedule diversity does not guarantee meaningful concurrency coverage.","failure_mode":"A race condition appears only under particular thread, task, process, timing, or resource schedules.","root_cause":"The test did not exercise the relevant interleavings or shared-state transitions.","solution":"Use controlled synchronization points, stress, schedule perturbation, deterministic concurrency tools where available, and explicit invariant checks.","testing_guidance":"Test both successful concurrent operation and prohibited state transitions under contention.","verification_method":"Construct a controlled interleaving that exposes the suspected race and verify the invariant before and after remediation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software concurrency testing literature"],"relationships":["D19-B25-R005","D20-B03-R005","D20-B07-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B07-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Failure-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Resource Exhaustion","concept":"Resource-boundary testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should tests exercise resource limits?","answer":"Unbounded memory, CPU, file descriptors, connections, queue depth, recursion, input size, or task concurrency can turn malformed or high-volume inputs into availability failures. Tests should verify that defined limits produce controlled behavior.","failure_mode":"A large or nested input causes excessive resource consumption instead of a bounded rejection.","root_cause":"Resource constraints were absent or untested.","solution":"Define resource budgets and test behavior at, near, and beyond the relevant limits.","performance_implications":"Resource-boundary tests should establish behavior and safety rather than inventing performance numbers without measurement.","verification_method":"Execute controlled boundary and over-limit cases and verify bounded rejection, graceful degradation, or documented behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP testing guidance","software reliability testing practice"],"relationships":["D19-B06-R004","D19-B88-R001","D20-B02-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B07-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Failure-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Recovery Testing","concept":"Recovery invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should recovery tests verify after a failure?","answer":"Recovery tests should verify not only that the service becomes available again but also that required state, data integrity, authorization boundaries, idempotency properties, queues, transactions, and externally visible contracts remain correct.","rule":"Availability after recovery is insufficient if recovery violates correctness or security invariants.","verification_method":"Inject a controlled failure, execute the documented recovery path, and verify all defined recovery invariants before normal operation resumes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESILIENCE_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software resilience engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B04-R003","D20-B07-R002","D20-B07-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B08-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Security Properties","concept":"Security invariant testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should security requirements become executable tests?","answer":"A security requirement should be translated into an observable invariant or negative property wherever practical. Tests then attempt both allowed and prohibited operations and verify authorization, isolation, confidentiality, integrity, and failure behavior.","objective":"Turn security requirements into repeatable evidence rather than relying only on code inspection.","example":{"requirement":"A tenant cannot access another tenant's object","positive_case":"authorized tenant accesses its own object","negative_case":"tenant requests another tenant's object","oracle":"cross-tenant access is rejected without disclosure of protected content"},"verification_method":"Execute representative positive and negative cases across relevant identities, resources, and tenant scopes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Web Security Testing Guide","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R004","D19-B30-R001","D20-B07-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B08-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Authorization","concept":"Authorization matrix testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an authorization test matrix contain?","answer":"It should vary identity or role, resource ownership, action, tenant or scope, resource state, and relevant contextual conditions, then assert the permitted or denied outcome for each materially distinct combination.","failure_mode":"Tests verify only an authorized happy path and never attempt cross-user, cross-tenant, privilege-boundary, or object-level access.","root_cause":"Authorization was tested as a static role check rather than a contextual decision.","solution":"Build an explicit authorization matrix covering representative allow and deny conditions.","verification_method":"Execute the matrix and verify that every prohibited combination is denied without unintended information disclosure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet","NIST SP 800-53"],"relationships":["D19-B04-R002","D19-B24-R005","D20-B08-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B08-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Input Security","concept":"Parser differential testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should security-sensitive parsers be tested for ambiguous interpretations?","answer":"If two components interpret the same input differently, validation performed by one component may not constrain the interpretation performed by another. This can create security bypasses and inconsistent application behavior.","failure_mode":"A gateway validates one interpretation while a backend parser interprets the same bytes differently.","root_cause":"Multiple parsers or normalization stages lacked a canonical interpretation contract.","solution":"Test ambiguous encodings, duplicate fields, normalization differences, malformed structures, and parser boundary cases across all relevant components.","verification_method":"Feed equivalent and ambiguous inputs through each parsing boundary and compare normalized representations and security decisions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet","OWASP HTTP Request Smuggling guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B29-R001","D19-B28-R002","D20-B02-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B08-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Secrets","concept":"Secret leakage test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can automated tests detect accidental secret exposure?","answer":"Tests and static checks can inspect generated logs, errors, serialized responses, artifacts, configuration output, and telemetry for known test secrets or secret-shaped values. Detection should use controlled test credentials rather than real production secrets.","requirements":["Synthetic test secrets","Defined exposure surfaces","Detection patterns","Failure handling"],"security_implications":"Real credentials must not be introduced into tests merely to verify that they are not leaked.","verification_method":"Inject synthetic secrets into representative internal paths and verify that protected outputs, logs, errors, and artifacts do not expose them unexpectedly.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet","OWASP Secrets Management guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B05-R005","D19-B91-R004","D20-B08-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B08-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Security Regression","concept":"Security regression preservation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must happen to security tests during refactoring?","answer":"Security tests that encode active security requirements must remain linked to those requirements and must not be removed or weakened merely because internal implementation changes. Tests may be redesigned when the security contract changes, but that change must be explicit.","rule":"Security regression coverage is part of the security contract and requires deliberate review during code changes.","verification_method":"Compare changed code paths against affected security requirements and verify that corresponding security tests remain effective.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP Testing Guide","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"relationships":["D19-B58-R001","D19-B58-R003","D20-B06-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B09-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Test Automation","subtopic":"Test Pyramid","concept":"Test portfolio balance","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the useful idea behind a test portfolio or test pyramid?","answer":"Different test levels provide different trade-offs among execution cost, isolation, realism, diagnostic precision, and coverage. A healthy portfolio deliberately combines levels rather than relying exclusively on either small isolated tests or expensive end-to-end tests.","tradeoff":"More isolated tests generally improve speed and localization, while higher-level tests expose integration and environment behavior; neither level substitutes completely for the other.","failure_mode":"The suite contains only end-to-end tests and becomes slow and diagnostically ambiguous, or contains only isolated tests and misses integration defects.","solution":"Select test levels according to the behavior and failure modes that must be established.","verification_method":"Classify the suite by test level, purpose, runtime characteristics, and defect-detection role and identify material gaps or excessive duplication.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software testing engineering literature","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R003","D20-B04-R001","D20-B06-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B09-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Test Automation","subtopic":"Test Selection","concept":"Change-aware test execution","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When can selective test execution be justified?","answer":"Selective execution can be justified when a reliable impact model identifies tests affected by a change and an appropriate broader safety net remains available. The selection mechanism must account for indirect dependencies and avoid turning optimization into systematic blind spots.","constraints":["Reliable dependency or impact model","Known limitations","Fallback broader suite","Monitoring of missed-impact history"],"failure_mode":"Tests unrelated by file path are skipped even though the changed behavior affects their dependencies.","root_cause":"Selection used textual proximity instead of behavioral impact.","solution":"Use dependency-aware selection and periodically validate selection effectiveness against full-suite results.","verification_method":"Compare selected-test outcomes with full-suite outcomes across representative historical changes and measure missed relevant tests if actually executed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software test selection engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R001","D20-B06-R002","D20-B10-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B09-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Test Automation","subtopic":"Test Data","concept":"Synthetic test data","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is synthetic test data often preferable to production data in automated tests?","answer":"Synthetic data reduces exposure of sensitive information, makes scenarios reproducible, permits deliberate boundary construction, and allows tests to encode rare failure conditions without depending on production state.","security_implications":"Synthetic data does not automatically guarantee safety if it contains copied credentials, personal information, or production-derived secrets.","requirements":["Known schema","Controlled values","Sensitive-data review","Reproducibility"],"verification_method":"Inspect test datasets for production-derived sensitive values and verify deterministic generation or documented provenance.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","software testing engineering practice"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R001","D20-B03-R002","D20-B08-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B09-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Test Automation","subtopic":"CI Reliability","concept":"Environment parity","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can a test passing in CI fail in production even when the code is identical?","answer":"Runtime versions, operating systems, dependencies, environment variables, network behavior, filesystem semantics, resource limits, configuration, identity, external services, and deployment topology can differ. Test evidence is therefore bounded by its environment.","rule":"Important production-relevant behavior requires tests in environments that represent the relevant production assumptions.","verification_method":"Compare test and production-relevant environment properties and identify untested differences capable of changing behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software deployment testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R001","D20-B04-R004","D20-B09-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B09-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Test Automation","subtopic":"Automation Failure Analysis","concept":"Test infrastructure failure versus product failure","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why must test infrastructure failures be distinguished from product failures?","answer":"A failed test can result from defective product behavior, defective test logic, unavailable infrastructure, corrupted fixtures, dependency failure, environment drift, or harness defects. Treating every failed test as a product defect creates incorrect engineering signals.","failure_mode":"A network outage in the test environment is recorded as an application regression.","root_cause":"The test result lacked infrastructure-health and harness validity evidence.","solution":"Classify the failure using test-harness, environment, dependency, and product evidence before assigning ownership.","verification_method":"Introduce controlled infrastructure faults and verify that the diagnostic system distinguishes them from product behavior failures.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["software testing engineering practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119"],"relationships":["D20-B04-R002","D20-B05-R001","D20-B05-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B10-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Test Verification","subtopic":"Coverage","concept":"Coverage metric limitation","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why does high code coverage not prove high software quality?","answer":"Coverage metrics measure which selected structural elements were exercised under a defined test execution, not whether the tests contained meaningful assertions, represented correct requirements, explored important inputs, detected defects, or validated security and performance properties.","rule":"Coverage is evidence about exercised structure and must not be treated as a universal correctness metric.","failure_mode":"A project declares software well tested solely because line or branch coverage exceeds a threshold.","root_cause":"A structural metric was substituted for behavioral and requirement evidence.","solution":"Combine structural coverage with requirement coverage, risk-based scenarios, negative paths, mutation or defect-detection evidence where appropriate, security, performance, and integration testing.","verification_method":"Review high-coverage tests with intentionally weakened assertions or injected defects and determine whether coverage remains high while defect detection fails.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software testing engineering literature"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B02-R003","D20-B10-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B10-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Test Verification","subtopic":"Mutation Testing","concept":"Mutation-based test effectiveness","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"What does mutation testing attempt to measure?","answer":"Mutation testing introduces controlled small changes, or mutants, into code and evaluates whether the test suite detects them. It can reveal tests that execute code without asserting behavior strongly enough to detect meaningful faults.","objective":"Evaluate the defect-detection strength of a test suite beyond structural execution coverage.","failure_mode":"Tests execute a changed branch but still pass because their assertions do not constrain the changed behavior.","root_cause":"Execution coverage was mistaken for fault-detection effectiveness.","solution":"Use appropriate mutation operators and analyze surviving mutants, while distinguishing equivalent or intentionally unkillable mutants.","verification_method":"Run mutation analysis with an actual tool and record measured results; never fabricate mutation scores.","performance_implications":"Mutation analysis can be computationally expensive and should be scoped according to repository size and risk.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_TECHNIQUE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["mutation testing research literature","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119"],"relationships":["D20-B10-R001","D20-B01-R002","D20-B05-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B10-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Test Verification","subtopic":"Requirement Traceability","concept":"Requirement-to-test traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should requirement-to-test traceability establish?","answer":"Traceability should show which tests provide evidence for each material requirement or acceptance condition and, where relevant, which requirements are affected by a changed implementation or failing test.","rule":"Material requirements should have explicit test evidence or an explicit documented reason why executable testing is not applicable.","verification_method":"Build a requirement-to-test matrix and identify uncovered, weakly covered, obsolete, or conflicting tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B06-R001","D20-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B10-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Test Verification","subtopic":"Test Suite Quality","concept":"Test-suite health audit","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What dimensions should be used to audit a test suite?","answer":"A serious test-suite audit should examine correctness of oracles, requirement coverage, defect-detection value, determinism, isolation, execution reliability, maintenance burden, duplication, environment validity, security of test data, performance cost, and relationship to current implementation and requirements.","requirements":["Oracle validity","Requirement coverage","Failure-path coverage","Reliability","Isolation","Environment validity","Security","Performance","Deduplication","Traceability"],"verification_method":"Produce a versioned test-suite health report and classify each material finding as defect, gap, redundancy, infrastructure issue, or accepted limitation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","software test engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B05-R001","D20-B06-R005","D20-B09-R005","D20-B10-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B10-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Software Testing","subtopic":"Foundation Quality Gate","concept":"D20 B1-B10 foundation coverage gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be true before moving beyond the D20 foundation batches?","answer":"The foundation must distinguish testing from proof, define test oracles, cover test levels and purposes, establish representative test-design techniques, address unit and integration boundaries, handle nondeterminism and failure diagnosis, preserve regression knowledge, include security and resource-boundary testing, and distinguish structural coverage from actual test effectiveness.","requirements":["Testing evidence model","Test oracle model","Test-level distinctions","Input partitioning","Boundary testing","Property/state-based testing","Unit isolation","Integration contracts","Failure testing","Regression testing","Security testing","Coverage limitations"],"verification_method":"Audit B1-B10 for duplicate concepts, schema consistency, distinct knowledge contribution, provenance, testability, implementation relevance, and missing foundational topics before generating later D20 batches.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance specification","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R001","D20-B02-R001","D20-B03-R001","D20-B04-R001","D20-B05-R001","D20-B06-R001","D20-B07-R001","D20-B08-R001","D20-B09-R001","D20-B10-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B11-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Test Doubles","subtopic":"Stub Mock Fake Spy","concept":"Test double role distinction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What distinguishes a stub, mock, fake, and spy in software testing?","answer":"A stub supplies controlled responses, a mock is configured to verify expected interactions, a fake provides a simplified working implementation, and a spy records calls or behavior for later verification. The distinction is based on the testing role, not merely the library API used to create the object.","principle":"Choose a test double according to the behavior that must be controlled or observed; avoid selecting one merely because a framework calls it a mock.","constraints":["The double must preserve the relevant contract of the real dependency.","Interaction verification should be used only when interaction behavior is itself part of the requirement."],"expected_behavior":"The test isolates the intended dependency while retaining meaningful evidence about the system under test.","failure_mode":"Using an interaction-focused double when the requirement concerns externally observable state.","root_cause":"The test encodes an implementation detail instead of the required behavior.","solution":"Prefer state, result, or contract assertions unless the interaction itself is contractually significant.","testing_guidance":"Create paired tests demonstrating state-based verification and interaction-based verification for the same dependency.","verification_method":"Review whether each assertion corresponds to a stated behavioral requirement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 software testing principles","Established test-double and unit-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B03-R004","D20-B04-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B11-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Test Doubles","subtopic":"Interaction Verification","concept":"Mock interaction over-specification","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can excessive mock interaction assertions make tests fragile?","answer":"A test becomes fragile when it asserts internal call order, call counts, or implementation-specific interactions that are not part of the externally required behavior. Refactoring can then break the test without changing the observable contract.","principle":"Verify interactions only when interaction semantics are a meaningful part of the contract.","invalid_example":"A test requires helper A to call helper B exactly once and in a specific internal sequence even though the public requirement specifies only the final result.","failure_mode":"Behavior-preserving refactoring causes unrelated test failures.","root_cause":"The test is coupled to internal implementation structure.","solution":"Replace unnecessary interaction assertions with observable-result, state, contract, or property assertions.","testing_guidance":"Refactor the implementation without changing its public behavior and verify whether the test suite remains valid.","verification_method":"Classify every mock assertion as either contract-relevant or implementation-specific; remove unjustified implementation-specific assertions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established unit-testing and test-double engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R004","D20-B06-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B11-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Test Doubles","subtopic":"Fake Implementations","concept":"Contract fidelity of fakes","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What makes a fake useful for integration-oriented testing?","answer":"A useful fake implements the important observable contract of a real dependency while remaining deterministic and controllable in the test environment. A fake that omits important failure, consistency, ordering, or validation behavior can create false confidence.","requirements":["Define which real dependency behaviors the fake represents.","Document intentionally unsupported behaviors.","Keep the fake's observable contract aligned with the tested integration boundary."],"failure_mode":"Tests pass against a fake while production fails against the real dependency.","root_cause":"The fake models only the happy path or an incomplete contract.","solution":"Maintain explicit fake-contract tests and periodically compare fake behavior with the real dependency contract.","testing_guidance":"Test normal responses, documented failure modes, validation rules, ordering semantics, and relevant resource limits.","verification_method":"Run contract tests against both the fake and the real dependency where feasible and compare the specified observable behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established contract-testing and integration-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B04-R001","D20-B04-R004","D20-B11-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B11-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Test Doubles","subtopic":"Spy Verification","concept":"Recording behavior without changing dependency semantics","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is a spy appropriate in a test?","answer":"A spy is appropriate when the test needs to observe calls or interactions while preserving the relevant dependency behavior, or when interaction evidence must be captured for later assertions. Spying should not silently alter the semantics of the dependency.","principle":"Observation should not invalidate the behavior being tested.","constraints":["The spy must preserve relevant return values, exceptions, ordering, and side effects unless deliberate control is part of the test.","Sensitive information should not be recorded unnecessarily."],"failure_mode":"Instrumentation changes timing or behavior and causes the test to validate the instrumentation rather than the system.","root_cause":"The spy is not behaviorally transparent for the tested scenario.","solution":"Use minimal observation, document altered behavior, and prefer a dedicated test double when behavior must be controlled rather than observed.","testing_guidance":"Compare execution with and without the spy for the same deterministic scenario where feasible.","verification_method":"Verify that observed calls correspond to the real contract and that instrumentation does not introduce unintended behavior.","security_implications":"Captured arguments may contain credentials, personal data, tokens, or other sensitive values; redact or avoid recording them.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established test-observability and unit-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B11-R001","D20-B30-R004","D19-B05-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B11-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Test Doubles","subtopic":"Test Double Selection","concept":"Double selection decision rule","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an engineer select a test double?","answer":"First identify the testing objective, then determine whether the dependency must be controlled, observed, simulated, or replaced. Select a stub for controlled responses, a mock when contract-relevant interactions must be verified, a fake when a simplified working implementation is useful, and a spy when observation of behavior is required. Use the real dependency when replacing it would remove important integration evidence.","procedure":["State the behavior being tested.","Identify dependency behavior that must remain real.","Identify dependency behavior that must be controlled.","Select the least coupled double that provides the required evidence.","Add contract tests when the double represents a real external boundary."],"tradeoffs":{"stub":"Simple control but limited interaction evidence.","mock":"Strong interaction evidence but greater coupling to implementation.","fake":"Useful realistic behavior but requires maintenance of a second implementation.","spy":"Useful observation but can introduce instrumentation effects.","real_dependency":"High integration fidelity but potentially higher cost, nondeterminism, or environmental requirements."},"testing_guidance":"Review test-double choice during test-suite maintenance and refactoring.","verification_method":"Confirm that the selected double supports the stated test oracle without hiding behavior that the test is supposed to validate.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 software testing principles","Established test-double engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B03-R004","D20-B04-R001","D20-B11-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B12-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Assertions and Test Oracles","subtopic":"Assertion Strength","concept":"Strong versus weak assertions","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What makes a test assertion sufficiently strong?","answer":"A strong assertion checks the behavior required by the specification with enough specificity to distinguish correct behavior from important incorrect behavior. A weak assertion can pass while the implementation violates meaningful requirements.","principle":"Assertions should reject materially incorrect behavior, not merely confirm that execution completed.","invalid_example":"assert result is not None","corrected_pattern":"Assert the required value, invariant, state transition, or contract property rather than merely checking object existence.","failure_mode":"A defect remains undetected because the test passes for both correct and incorrect implementations.","root_cause":"The oracle has insufficient discriminating power.","solution":"Strengthen assertions around externally meaningful behavior and critical invariants.","testing_guidance":"Mutation testing can reveal whether assertions detect representative changes to the implementation.","verification_method":"Introduce controlled mutations and determine whether the test fails for requirement-relevant deviations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established software testing and test-oracle practice"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R002","D20-B10-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B12-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Assertions and Test Oracles","subtopic":"Collection Semantics","concept":"Order-sensitive versus order-insensitive assertions","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When should a collection test assert ordering?","answer":"A collection test should assert ordering only when ordering is part of the contract. If the specification defines the result as a set or otherwise permits arbitrary ordering, an order-sensitive assertion can create false failures and unnecessary coupling.","requirements":["Identify whether ordering is specified.","Preserve ordering assertions when order has semantic meaning.","Use order-independent comparison when ordering is explicitly unspecified."],"failure_mode":"A behaviorally correct implementation fails because elements are returned in a different permitted order.","root_cause":"The test assumes a stronger contract than the specification defines.","solution":"Align the oracle with the documented collection semantics.","testing_guidance":"Include both ordered and unordered scenarios where the API supports distinct semantics.","verification_method":"Compare the assertion semantics against the authoritative API or requirement specification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established software testing and API contract-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R002","D20-B02-R001","D20-B06-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B12-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Assertions and Test Oracles","subtopic":"Numeric Assertions","concept":"Tolerance-based numeric verification","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should floating-point results often be tested with an appropriate tolerance instead of exact equality?","answer":"Floating-point computations can produce small representation and rounding differences. When the specification permits a numerical tolerance, the test should encode that tolerance rather than requiring bit-for-bit equality. The tolerance must come from the domain requirement or numerical analysis, not from an arbitrary value chosen only to make the test pass.","constraints":["Tolerance must be justified by the expected numerical error or domain requirement.","Tolerance must not be so broad that materially incorrect results pass."],"failure_mode":"Exact equality produces false failures or an unjustified broad tolerance hides real defects.","root_cause":"The test oracle does not represent the numerical contract.","solution":"Define and document an appropriate absolute, relative, or domain-specific tolerance.","testing_guidance":"Test values near the accepted tolerance boundary and values just outside it.","verification_method":"Trace the selected tolerance to a documented requirement or numerical error analysis.","performance_implications":"Tolerance computation itself is normally negligible, but test suites should avoid unnecessary repeated high-cost numerical calculations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NUMERICAL_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established numerical software testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R002","D20-B07-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B12-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Assertions and Test Oracles","subtopic":"Exception Testing","concept":"Exception contract verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a test verify when an API is expected to reject invalid input with an exception?","answer":"The test should verify the documented exception type and, when contractually relevant, stable error properties such as an error code or structured field. It should also verify that invalid input does not cause unintended side effects when the requirement specifies transactional or atomic behavior.","requirements":["Exception type must be part of or compatible with the documented contract.","Error-message text should not be asserted when wording is intentionally non-contractual.","Side-effect assertions should reflect actual atomicity requirements."],"failure_mode":"The test passes because any exception occurred even though the wrong exception or an unintended side effect occurred.","root_cause":"The oracle checks only failure occurrence rather than the failure contract.","solution":"Assert contract-relevant failure classification and relevant state invariants.","testing_guidance":"Test representative invalid inputs and verify both the failure classification and required post-failure state.","verification_method":"Trace exception assertions to API documentation and error-handling requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established API and failure-contract testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R003","D20-B07-R001","D20-B10-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B12-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Assertions and Test Oracles","subtopic":"Property Assertions","concept":"Invariant-oriented assertions","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are property or invariant assertions useful in software testing?","answer":"Property assertions verify relationships or invariants that should remain true across a range of valid inputs rather than checking only one example output. They can detect broader classes of defects when the property accurately represents the specification.","principle":"A property is valuable as a test oracle only when it is independently justified by the system's semantics.","example_properties":["A normalized identifier remains canonical after repeated normalization.","A collection transformation preserves required cardinality.","A parser rejects malformed input without producing an unauthorized semantic object."],"failure_mode":"A supposedly general property is incorrect or weaker than the actual requirement.","root_cause":"The property was inferred without sufficient specification evidence.","solution":"Derive properties from formal requirements, domain invariants, documented contracts, or independently established semantics.","testing_guidance":"Combine property checks with representative example-based tests and boundary cases.","verification_method":"Review each property against the authoritative specification and test counterexamples.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROPERTY_BASED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established property-based testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D20-B01-R002","D20-B07-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B13-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Test Data Engineering","subtopic":"Deterministic Test Data","concept":"Stable test-data generation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should generated test data remain reproducible?","answer":"Generated test data should be reproducible when reproducibility is required for debugging and regression analysis. Deterministic seeds, explicit generators, recorded inputs, or persisted failure cases can provide a stable path from a failing test to the data that caused it.","requirements":["The source of generated data must be identifiable.","A failure must be reproducible without relying on hidden mutable state.","Randomness should be controlled or captured when reproducibility matters."],"failure_mode":"A failure disappears because the next execution generates different data.","root_cause":"The test does not preserve the input state that produced the failure.","solution":"Record the seed, generated input, or equivalent replay artifact.","testing_guidance":"Force a known seed or replay a captured failing case and verify identical logical input generation.","verification_method":"Execute the same test configuration repeatedly and confirm deterministic reproduction when determinism is a stated requirement.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established property-based and randomized testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B05-R002","D20-B05-R005","D20-B02-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B13-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Test Data Engineering","subtopic":"Test Data Builders","concept":"Builder-based complex fixture construction","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are test-data builders useful for complex domain objects?","answer":"A test-data builder centralizes construction of valid baseline objects while allowing individual tests to override only the fields relevant to the scenario. This reduces fixture duplication and makes the test's intended variation explicit.","principle":"A test should expose the data variation relevant to its behavior while hiding irrelevant construction detail.","failure_mode":"Shared fixtures silently change unrelated tests or make required fields difficult to identify.","root_cause":"Fixture construction is globally coupled or mutable.","solution":"Use immutable or freshly constructed builders with explicit per-test overrides.","testing_guidance":"Verify that modifying one builder instance cannot mutate data used by another test.","verification_method":"Inspect fixture isolation and execute tests in different orders where feasible.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established test-fixture engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R002","D20-B05-R005","D20-B13-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B13-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Test Data Engineering","subtopic":"Combinatorial Testing","concept":"Pairwise test selection","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"When can pairwise testing reduce a large test-data combination space?","answer":"Pairwise testing selects combinations so that every relevant pair of parameter values occurs in at least one test case. It can reduce the number of cases compared with exhaustive Cartesian-product testing when pairwise interaction coverage is an appropriate risk model.","constraints":["Pairwise coverage does not guarantee coverage of three-way or higher-order interactions.","Domain-invalid combinations must be excluded explicitly.","Critical combinations may require additional targeted cases."],"procedure":["Identify parameters and valid values.","Remove invalid combinations according to domain constraints.","Generate a pairwise covering set.","Add risk-critical higher-order combinations.","Verify coverage against the intended parameter model."],"failure_mode":"A defect requiring a three-way or higher-order interaction remains undetected.","root_cause":"Pairwise coverage was incorrectly treated as exhaustive interaction coverage.","solution":"Combine pairwise selection with targeted higher-order and boundary cases.","testing_guidance":"Report uncovered higher-order interactions separately from pairwise coverage.","verification_method":"Programmatically or analytically verify that every valid parameter-value pair is represented.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established combinatorial testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R001","D20-B02-R004","D20-B07-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B13-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Test Data Engineering","subtopic":"Sensitive Test Data","concept":"Production-data minimization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What principle should govern use of production data in testing?","answer":"Production data should not be copied into test environments merely for convenience. Testing should prefer synthetic, masked, minimized, or otherwise appropriately controlled data that preserves the properties required by the test without unnecessary exposure of sensitive information.","requirements":["Identify sensitive fields before data movement.","Minimize data to what the test actually requires.","Apply appropriate access, retention, and handling controls when controlled real data is unavoidable."],"failure_mode":"Sensitive production information is exposed through test fixtures, logs, snapshots, or developer environments.","root_cause":"Test data was treated as ordinary non-sensitive input.","solution":"Use synthetic or minimized data and establish explicit controls for any unavoidable real-data use.","security_implications":"Test artifacts can become secondary data stores and must be included in the applicable security and retention boundary.","testing_guidance":"Scan test fixtures and artifacts for credentials, personal data, tokens, and other sensitive values.","verification_method":"Trace each non-synthetic dataset to an approved purpose and verify minimization and handling controls.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["NIST Privacy Framework","OWASP testing and sensitive-data handling guidance"],"relationships":["D19-B55-R001","D19-B55-R002","D20-B09-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B13-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Test Data Engineering","subtopic":"Boundary Data Preservation","concept":"Explicit boundary and failure-value persistence","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should important boundary values be preserved as regression data?","answer":"When a boundary value exposes a defect or represents a critical contract limit, preserve the exact or semantically equivalent input as a named regression case. The case should document the boundary condition and expected behavior so that future changes cannot silently remove the scenario.","procedure":["Identify the triggering boundary.","Capture the minimal reproducing input.","Record the expected behavior.","Add the case to the appropriate regression suite.","Link it to the originating defect or requirement.","Review whether the boundary remains contractually relevant."],"failure_mode":"A previously discovered boundary defect reappears because the original case was not retained.","root_cause":"Failure evidence was not converted into durable regression knowledge.","solution":"Promote significant failure inputs into version-controlled regression cases.","testing_guidance":"Verify that the regression case fails against the known defective behavior and passes against the corrected behavior when such evidence is available.","verification_method":"Trace the regression case to the defect, requirement, or invariant it protects.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_REGRESSION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established regression-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B05-R003","D20-B06-R004","D20-B07-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B14-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Parameterized Testing","subtopic":"Data-Driven Tests","concept":"Parameterized scenario coverage","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of parameterized tests?","answer":"Parameterized tests execute the same test logic against multiple explicitly defined inputs and expected outcomes. They reduce duplicated test code while preserving distinct scenario evidence when each parameter set represents meaningful behavior.","principle":"Parameterization should compress repeated test structure, not hide meaningful scenario differences.","failure_mode":"A large parameter table becomes opaque and failures become difficult to diagnose.","root_cause":"Too many semantically unrelated scenarios were combined under one test structure.","solution":"Group parameters by shared behavioral contract and give scenarios meaningful identifiers.","testing_guidance":"Ensure each parameter set has a clear expected result and that failure output identifies the relevant case.","verification_method":"Review whether every parameter row represents a distinct requirement, boundary, or failure condition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established data-driven testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R001","D20-B02-R004","D20-B13-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B14-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Parameterized Testing","subtopic":"Scenario Identity","concept":"Diagnostic parameter naming","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should parameterized test cases have stable diagnostic identities?","answer":"When a parameterized case fails, engineers need to identify the exact scenario quickly. Stable case identifiers or descriptive parameter names make failures actionable and prevent a large parameterized test from becoming an undifferentiated failure source.","requirements":["Each scenario should expose the input dimensions relevant to diagnosis.","Failure reporting should identify the failing parameter combination.","Names should remain stable unless the underlying scenario changes."],"failure_mode":"A test failure reports only a generic parameter index.","root_cause":"The test framework configuration or test data lacks meaningful scenario identity.","solution":"Provide descriptive case IDs or parameter labels and include relevant input context in failure artifacts without exposing sensitive data.","verification_method":"Review representative failure output and confirm that the failing scenario can be uniquely identified.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_AUTOMATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established parameterized-testing and test-diagnostics practice"],"relationships":["D20-B05-R005","D20-B14-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B14-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Parameterized Testing","subtopic":"Scenario Matrix","concept":"Explicit scenario matrix","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test engineer convert a multidimensional requirement into test scenarios?","answer":"Identify the independent dimensions, valid values, constraints, and risk-critical interactions, then construct a scenario matrix that provides deliberate coverage instead of accidental combinations.","procedure":["Extract dimensions from the requirement.","Enumerate meaningful values or equivalence classes.","Remove invalid combinations.","Identify boundary and high-risk interactions.","Select representative combinations using an explicit coverage strategy.","Map each scenario to an expected outcome or oracle."],"constraints":["Do not assume all Cartesian combinations are required.","Do not use pairwise coverage when higher-order interaction is known to matter.","Preserve explicit coverage for critical combinations."],"testing_guidance":"Record the coverage rationale so future changes can determine which scenarios must be added or removed.","verification_method":"Audit scenario coverage against requirements, constraints, boundaries, and identified risks.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 testing principles","Established combinatorial test-design practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R004","D20-B13-R003","D20-B14-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B14-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Parameterized Testing","subtopic":"Invalid Combinations","concept":"Constraint-aware scenario generation","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must invalid parameter combinations be represented explicitly in test design?","answer":"A multidimensional input space often contains combinations that are syntactically possible but semantically invalid. Test generation must encode those constraints so invalid combinations are either deliberately tested as rejection cases or excluded from valid-behavior coverage.","failure_mode":"The generator creates meaningless cases or falsely reports coverage for scenarios the product is not required to support.","root_cause":"Domain constraints were omitted from the test model.","solution":"Represent validity constraints separately from parameter values and test important invalid combinations as negative cases.","testing_guidance":"Maintain examples of both invalid combinations that must be rejected and combinations that are simply excluded from the supported domain.","verification_method":"Review generated cases against the authoritative domain constraints.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established model-based and combinatorial testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R004","D20-B07-R001","D20-B13-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B14-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Parameterized Testing","subtopic":"Data-Driven Regression","concept":"Regression scenario evolution","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should parameterized regression data evolve when requirements change?","answer":"Regression parameters should be reviewed when the contract changes rather than automatically preserved forever. Each retained, modified, or removed scenario should have a reason tied to current requirements, historical defects, or meaningful risk.","principle":"Regression suites preserve useful evidence, not obsolete assumptions.","failure_mode":"Obsolete scenarios remain indefinitely and obscure failures or increase maintenance cost.","root_cause":"Regression data has no ownership or lifecycle review.","solution":"Review scenario relevance during requirement and implementation changes and preserve historically important cases when they still protect meaningful behavior.","testing_guidance":"Tag scenarios by requirement, defect, risk, or historical reason where practical.","verification_method":"Audit representative parameterized suites for stale cases and missing newly required cases.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_REGRESSION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established regression-suite maintenance practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R004","D20-B06-R005","D20-B14-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B15-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Testing","subtopic":"Async Task Lifecycle","concept":"Task cleanup verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should asynchronous tests verify about spawned tasks?","answer":"An asynchronous test should ensure that tasks created during the test are either awaited to completion, deliberately cancelled and awaited, or otherwise lifecycle-managed according to the API contract. Unmanaged tasks can leak work across test boundaries and create nondeterministic failures.","requirements":["Every test-created task must have an explicit lifecycle.","Cancellation must be handled according to the runtime and API semantics.","Test completion must not leave unintended background work active."],"failure_mode":"A previous test leaves a task running that affects a later test.","root_cause":"Task ownership and cleanup were not defined.","solution":"Track spawned tasks and verify completion or controlled cancellation during teardown.","testing_guidance":"Run tests repeatedly and in isolation and inspect for pending-task warnings or cross-test interference.","verification_method":"At test teardown, verify that no unexpected test-owned tasks remain active.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ASYNCHRONOUS_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established asynchronous programming and testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R005","D20-B05-R001","D20-B07-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B15-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Testing","subtopic":"Cancellation","concept":"Cancellation as observable behavior","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should cancellation behavior be tested explicitly?","answer":"Cancellation is often part of an asynchronous API's contract. Tests should verify that cancellation stops or interrupts work as specified, propagates through the relevant task boundary, releases required resources, and does not incorrectly report successful completion.","requirements":["Define the cancellation boundary.","Define expected task state after cancellation.","Define resource-cleanup requirements.","Distinguish cancellation from ordinary failure and timeout when the API does so."],"failure_mode":"A cancelled operation continues performing side effects or is incorrectly reported as successful.","root_cause":"Cancellation was treated as an exceptional implementation detail rather than a contract state.","solution":"Add explicit cancellation scenarios and post-cancellation invariant checks.","testing_guidance":"Cancel during relevant lifecycle points, including before work begins, during awaited operations, and near completion where practical.","verification_method":"Verify task state, returned/error classification, side effects, and cleanup after cancellation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ASYNCHRONOUS_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established asynchronous and concurrent testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B07-R005","D20-B15-R001","D18-B48-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B15-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Testing","subtopic":"Timeout Testing","concept":"Timeout versus cancellation distinction","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should asynchronous tests distinguish timeout from cancellation?","answer":"A timeout is a time-based condition in which an operation exceeds an allowed duration; cancellation is an explicit request or lifecycle transition that asks work to stop. They may interact, but tests should verify the distinct semantics required by the API.","failure_mode":"A timeout is treated as proof that cancellation succeeded or cancellation is treated as proof that an operation exceeded its deadline.","root_cause":"The test conflates different control-flow states.","solution":"Create separate timeout and cancellation scenarios and verify their documented outcomes.","testing_guidance":"Test an operation that exceeds its timeout without explicit cancellation and a separately cancelled operation.","verification_method":"Verify error classification, task state, resource cleanup, and side effects for each scenario.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ASYNCHRONOUS_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established asynchronous testing practice","D18-B48 distributed deadline concepts"],"relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D20-B07-R004","D20-B15-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B15-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency Determinism","concept":"Controlled scheduling","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can asynchronous tests expose race conditions without relying only on timing luck?","answer":"Tests can deliberately control or influence scheduling points, synchronization barriers, task interleavings, and resource states so that specific concurrency orderings become reproducible. Sleeping for arbitrary durations is not a reliable substitute for synchronization control.","anti_pattern":"Insert fixed sleeps and assume the desired race condition will occur consistently.","failure_mode":"The test is flaky because timing varies across machines or runs.","root_cause":"Concurrency behavior depends on uncontrolled scheduling rather than an explicit test scenario.","solution":"Use synchronization primitives, controlled barriers, deterministic schedulers where available, or targeted instrumentation to create the required interleaving.","testing_guidance":"Repeat the controlled schedule and verify the expected invariant under the targeted interleaving.","verification_method":"Demonstrate that the test can reproduce the targeted ordering independently of machine speed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CONCURRENCY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established concurrent and asynchronous testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B07-R003","D20-B05-R001","D19-B25-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B15-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Asynchronous Testing","subtopic":"Async Failure Isolation","concept":"Shared event-loop state isolation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can shared asynchronous runtime state contaminate tests?","answer":"Shared event loops, global task registries, caches, connection pools, callbacks, or background workers can retain state after one test and affect later tests. Isolation requires explicit ownership and cleanup of runtime resources.","failure_mode":"Tests pass individually but fail when executed as a suite or in a different order.","root_cause":"Asynchronous runtime resources outlive the scenario that created them.","solution":"Scope runtime resources to the test or fixture lifecycle and verify teardown.","testing_guidance":"Run tests in randomized or reversed order where supported and compare results with isolated execution.","verification_method":"Inspect and test lifecycle cleanup for event-loop-bound resources and background tasks.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ASYNCHRONOUS_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established asynchronous testing and test-isolation practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B05-R001","D20-B15-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B16-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Clock Abstraction","concept":"Injectable time source","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can production code benefit from an explicit clock abstraction when time affects behavior?","answer":"An explicit time source allows tests to control the observed time without depending on the machine's wall clock. This makes expiration, scheduling, time-window, and temporal state-transition tests deterministic when the design requires controllable time.","requirements":["The application must use the controlled time source for behavior under test.","The abstraction must preserve the relevant production time semantics.","Tests must not accidentally mix controlled time with uncontrolled wall-clock reads."],"failure_mode":"A test freezes one clock source while production logic reads another source.","root_cause":"Time dependencies are implicit and inconsistent.","solution":"Centralize or inject the relevant time source and audit time reads in the tested path.","testing_guidance":"Advance the controlled clock across important boundaries such as expiry and validity transitions.","verification_method":"Trace all time reads involved in the tested behavior and verify they use the intended source.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TESTABILITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established deterministic testing and time-dependent software engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R002","D20-B16-R002","D18-B48-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B16-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Monotonic Time","concept":"Monotonic elapsed-time measurement","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should elapsed-duration tests use a monotonic time source?","answer":"Wall clocks can move forward or backward because of clock synchronization or administrative changes. A monotonic source is intended for measuring elapsed duration and avoids interpreting wall-clock adjustments as negative or unexpectedly large elapsed time.","constraints":["Use the runtime's appropriate monotonic clock for elapsed-duration measurement.","Do not use monotonic time as a replacement for civil timestamps when calendar time is required."],"failure_mode":"A duration test becomes flaky when the system wall clock changes during execution.","root_cause":"Elapsed time was derived from a civil-time clock.","solution":"Use a monotonic time source for duration measurement and a wall-clock source only where calendar timestamps are required.","testing_guidance":"Design a clock-controlled test where wall-clock adjustment does not change the expected elapsed duration.","verification_method":"Inspect the implementation's time source according to the semantic purpose of each time value.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RUNTIME_ENGINEERING_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established operating-system and runtime timekeeping practice"],"relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D20-B16-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B16-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Expiration Boundaries","concept":"Temporal boundary-value analysis","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Which temporal boundaries deserve explicit tests?","answer":"Tests should cover just-before, exactly-at, and just-after important temporal boundaries when those distinctions can change behavior. Examples include token expiry, cache freshness, lease expiration, retry deadlines, and validity windows.","procedure":["Identify the temporal boundary.","Define the expected behavior before the boundary.","Define behavior exactly at the boundary when meaningful.","Define behavior after the boundary.","Control the time source or otherwise make the scenario reproducible."],"failure_mode":"Off-by-one or clock-precision defects occur only near expiration.","root_cause":"Testing covered typical times but not semantic boundaries.","solution":"Apply temporal boundary-value analysis with an explicit precision model.","testing_guidance":"Use the same time precision and comparison semantics as the production contract.","verification_method":"Trace boundary expectations to the specification and implementation comparison semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established boundary-value analysis","Established temporal software testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R002","D20-B07-R004","D20-B16-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B16-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Time Zones","concept":"Civil-time and timezone test matrix","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should timezone-dependent behavior be tested with more than one local timezone?","answer":"Calendar rules, daylight-saving transitions, offsets, and date-boundary behavior can differ across time zones. Tests should cover the actual supported timezone semantics rather than assuming that local development time represents all production cases.","requirements":["Define whether behavior is based on UTC, local civil time, user timezone, or another explicit model.","Include relevant offset and daylight-saving transitions when supported.","Use deterministic timezone configuration in tests."],"failure_mode":"A date or scheduling defect appears only in a particular timezone or daylight-saving transition.","root_cause":"The test environment exercised only one timezone assumption.","solution":"Construct a timezone-aware test matrix based on supported behavior and known temporal boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Include representative positive and negative offset zones and relevant daylight-saving transition cases when applicable.","verification_method":"Compare observed results against authoritative timezone and application requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEMPORAL_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established timezone-aware software testing practice","IANA Time Zone Database concepts"],"relationships":["D20-B16-R003","D20-B07-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B16-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Temporal Test Isolation","concept":"Test clock leakage","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a controlled test clock leak between tests?","answer":"A mutable global clock, singleton time provider, shared fixture, or improperly restored monkey patch can leave one test's time state active for another test. The resulting failures can depend on test order.","failure_mode":"Later tests observe an unexpected frozen or advanced time.","root_cause":"The test-controlled temporal state is shared beyond its intended lifecycle.","solution":"Scope the clock override to the smallest required lifecycle and guarantee restoration during teardown.","testing_guidance":"Run affected tests in different orders and verify that each begins from its declared temporal state.","verification_method":"Inspect ownership and cleanup of the controlled time source and execute order-dependent regression checks.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_ISOLATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established test-isolation and deterministic-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R002","D20-B05-R001","D20-B16-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B17-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Temporary Resources","concept":"Isolated temporary filesystem state","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What makes temporary filesystem state suitable for tests?","answer":"Test filesystem resources should be isolated, uniquely scoped, cleaned up after use, and created with appropriate permissions. Tests should not depend on a developer's existing working directory or shared files.","requirements":["Use test-owned temporary paths.","Avoid collisions between concurrent tests.","Clean up resources even after failures.","Do not place secrets or unnecessary production data in temporary artifacts."],"failure_mode":"Tests interfere through shared files or leave artifacts that affect subsequent runs.","root_cause":"Filesystem state is outside the test lifecycle boundary.","solution":"Use isolated temporary directories or equivalent scoped resources with guaranteed cleanup.","testing_guidance":"Run filesystem tests concurrently where supported and verify isolation and cleanup.","verification_method":"Inspect temporary-resource ownership and verify no unexpected artifacts remain after the suite.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_FILESYSTEM_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established filesystem testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R002","D20-B05-R005","D19-B16-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B17-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Path Semantics","concept":"Cross-platform path testing","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should filesystem tests avoid assuming one platform's path semantics?","answer":"Path separators, case sensitivity, reserved names, symbolic links, permissions, and filesystem behavior can vary across operating systems and filesystems. Portable software should test the semantics it claims to support rather than embedding one platform's assumptions.","constraints":["Define supported operating systems and filesystem semantics.","Use platform-aware path construction.","Include platform-specific negative cases where the application handles them."],"failure_mode":"Tests pass on one operating system but fail or expose security defects on another supported platform.","root_cause":"The test suite encoded platform-specific assumptions as universal behavior.","solution":"Use portable path APIs and a platform coverage matrix for supported environments.","testing_guidance":"Run relevant filesystem tests on each supported platform or equivalent representative environments.","verification_method":"Review path construction, normalization, permission assumptions, and platform-specific behavior against the support matrix.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_CROSS_PLATFORM_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established cross-platform filesystem engineering practice"],"relationships":["D19-B16-R001","D19-B29-R003","D20-B17-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B17-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Process Testing","subtopic":"Subprocess Isolation","concept":"Controlled process execution","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should subprocess tests verify beyond exit status?","answer":"Subprocess tests should verify the intended command identity, arguments, relevant environment, exit classification, standard output/error behavior, timeout or cancellation semantics, and required side effects. Exit status alone may not prove the contract was satisfied.","requirements":["Define the subprocess contract.","Control environment variables and working directory as needed.","Capture relevant outputs without exposing secrets.","Test failure and timeout paths where supported."],"failure_mode":"The wrong executable or environment produces a superficially successful exit status.","root_cause":"The test oracle checks only process completion.","solution":"Assert contract-relevant process identity, arguments, outputs, and side effects.","security_implications":"Environment and command-line data may contain sensitive values and should be handled accordingly.","testing_guidance":"Include successful, invalid-argument, missing-resource, and timeout scenarios when relevant.","verification_method":"Trace subprocess assertions to the process contract and security boundary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROCESS_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established subprocess testing practice","D19-B52 command/process security concepts"],"relationships":["D19-B07-R002","D19-B52-R001","D20-B12-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B17-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Permission Boundaries","concept":"Filesystem authorization testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should tests verify filesystem permission boundaries?","answer":"Security-focused filesystem tests should attempt authorized and unauthorized operations using representative identities or permission contexts and verify that access is granted or denied according to policy.","requirements":["Define the expected access policy.","Test both allowed and denied operations.","Use realistic permission boundaries for the supported deployment model."],"failure_mode":"An application can access files outside its intended security boundary.","root_cause":"Only successful-path filesystem behavior was tested.","solution":"Add negative authorization cases and verify access-control invariants.","testing_guidance":"Test read, write, create, rename, delete, and traversal-sensitive operations where relevant.","verification_method":"Compare observed access decisions against the documented filesystem security policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["OWASP filesystem/path security guidance","Established security testing practice"],"relationships":["D19-B16-R001","D19-B16-R005","D20-B08-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B17-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Symlink and Race Conditions","concept":"Race-aware filesystem testing","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a filesystem security test need race-oriented scenarios?","answer":"A path may be validated at one time and replaced or redirected before the subsequent operation. Tests for security-sensitive filesystem operations should consider whether the implementation relies on a check-then-use sequence that permits state changes between validation and use.","failure_mode":"A path that passed validation is changed before the privileged operation occurs.","root_cause":"Validation and use are not sufficiently atomic or bound to the same object semantics.","solution":"Use safer filesystem primitives and designs that reduce check/use races, and test relevant race conditions where feasible.","testing_guidance":"Where practical, create controlled concurrent modifications around the validation/use boundary and verify the security invariant.","verification_method":"Review the implementation for TOCTOU exposure and combine static reasoning with targeted runtime tests.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["CWE-367 Time-of-check Time-of-use","Established filesystem security testing practice"],"relationships":["D19-B16-R005","D19-B25-R002","D20-B07-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B18-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Network Testing","subtopic":"Network Failure Simulation","concept":"Controlled network fault testing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should network-dependent software be tested against network failures?","answer":"Create controlled scenarios for relevant network failure classes such as connection refusal, timeout, connection reset, partial response, DNS failure, protocol error, and rate limiting, then verify the application's classification, recovery, cancellation, and user-visible behavior.","procedure":["Identify network failure classes relevant to the dependency.","Inject or simulate one failure class at a time.","Verify error classification and evidence.","Verify timeout or cancellation behavior.","Verify retry behavior when permitted.","Verify that retries do not violate idempotency or resource budgets.","Verify final failure propagation when recovery is impossible."],"failure_mode":"The application treats all network failures as equivalent and applies unsafe recovery behavior.","root_cause":"Failure taxonomy was not represented in tests.","solution":"Build a network failure matrix tied to the request lifecycle and recovery policy.","testing_guidance":"Include both transient and persistent failure scenarios.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior against the documented network recovery policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established distributed-systems and network testing practice","D18-B49 retry and recovery concepts"],"relationships":["D18-B49-R001","D18-B48-R001","D20-B07-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B18-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Network Testing","subtopic":"Partial Responses","concept":"Truncated-response testing","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should network clients test truncated or incomplete responses?","answer":"A connection can terminate after only part of a response has been received. Clients must distinguish complete valid responses from partial data and avoid silently treating incomplete input as successful completion.","failure_mode":"A client accepts a truncated response as valid data.","root_cause":"Completion was inferred from received data rather than protocol framing or explicit completion semantics.","solution":"Test premature connection closure, incomplete frames, incomplete bodies, and parser termination according to the protocol contract.","testing_guidance":"Inject truncation at multiple response positions, including before headers, during headers, and during body or frame processing where applicable.","verification_method":"Verify parser state, completion classification, resource cleanup, and error propagation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established protocol and network-client testing practice"],"relationships":["D18-B45-R001","D18-B77-R001","D20-B07-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B18-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Network Testing","subtopic":"Connection Pooling","concept":"Connection reuse and pool isolation","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should connection-pool tests verify?","answer":"Connection-pool tests should verify reuse, expiration, maximum capacity, acquisition timeout, release behavior, failure recovery, and isolation requirements. The pool must not leak connections or incorrectly reuse connections across incompatible security or protocol contexts.","requirements":["Define pool capacity and lifecycle semantics.","Define connection eligibility for reuse.","Define behavior when capacity is exhausted.","Define cleanup after connection failure."],"failure_mode":"Connections leak, exhaustion occurs unexpectedly, or an incompatible connection is reused.","root_cause":"Pool lifecycle and reuse invariants were not tested.","solution":"Create explicit lifecycle and exhaustion tests and verify connection ownership.","testing_guidance":"Test normal reuse, pool exhaustion, connection failure, cancellation during acquisition, and cleanup.","verification_method":"Inspect pool metrics/state and verify every acquired resource reaches the expected terminal lifecycle state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established connection-pooling engineering practice","D18-B73 ephemeral-port and connection-churn concepts"],"relationships":["D18-B73-R001","D18-B73-R003","D18-B48-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B18-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Network Testing","subtopic":"TLS Failure Testing","concept":"Negative TLS scenarios","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Which TLS failures should a network test suite distinguish?","answer":"Tests should distinguish certificate validation failure, hostname mismatch, trust-chain failure, unsupported protocol or negotiation failure, expired or otherwise invalid credentials where applicable, and deliberate connection termination. Disabling certificate verification is not a valid way to make a negative test pass.","requirements":["Use a controlled test certificate or test trust configuration.","Preserve certificate validation in the tested client.","Classify the expected failure explicitly."],"failure_mode":"The test disables TLS verification and therefore cannot detect certificate-validation regressions.","root_cause":"The test bypasses the security control it is intended to verify.","solution":"Construct a controlled trust environment and assert the expected secure failure.","testing_guidance":"Test valid trusted certificates and representative invalid certificates separately.","verification_method":"Verify that invalid certificate scenarios fail for the expected security reason while valid trusted scenarios succeed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TRANSPORT_SECURITY_TESTING","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19-B15-R001","D19-B15-R002","Established TLS security testing practice"],"relationships":["D19-B15-R005","D18-B75-R001","D20-B08-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B18-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Network Testing","subtopic":"Retry Testing","concept":"Retry policy verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should tests verify a network retry policy?","answer":"Tests should verify that retryable failures are retried only within the configured attempt, time, and resource budgets, while non-retryable failures are not retried. They should also verify backoff behavior and preservation of request semantics such as idempotency requirements.","requirements":["Define retryable and non-retryable failure classes.","Define retry count and time budget.","Define backoff policy.","Define request-safety requirements.","Define final failure behavior."],"failure_mode":"A transient failure triggers an uncontrolled retry storm or a non-idempotent operation is repeated unsafely.","root_cause":"Retry policy is not represented as an explicit testable contract.","solution":"Build a deterministic retry matrix using controlled failure injection and observable attempt records.","testing_guidance":"Test zero, one, and multiple retryable failures; persistent failure; non-retryable failure; timeout exhaustion; and cancellation.","verification_method":"Count actual attempts and compare timing/resource usage with the declared retry budget and classification policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_NETWORK_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D18-B49-R001","D18-B49-R002","Established retry testing practice"],"relationships":["D18-B49-R001","D18-B49-R003","D18-B48-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B19-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Database Testing","subtopic":"Transactional Behavior","concept":"Transaction rollback verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should database tests verify when an operation is transactional?","answer":"A transactional test should verify both successful commit behavior and failure behavior, including whether the required state changes are fully rolled back or otherwise preserved according to the transaction contract.","requirements":["Define the transaction boundary.","Define expected committed state.","Define expected failed state.","Define behavior for relevant constraint or dependency failures."],"failure_mode":"A failed transaction leaves partial state changes.","root_cause":"The test verified only successful execution and did not assert post-failure state.","solution":"Add failure-path tests that inspect all contract-relevant state after rollback or recovery.","testing_guidance":"Trigger failures at meaningful points within the transaction and verify atomicity where atomicity is required.","verification_method":"Compare database state before and after both successful and failed transactions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established database transaction testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B04-R003","D20-B12-R004","D20-B07-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B19-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Database Testing","subtopic":"Migration Testing","concept":"Schema migration compatibility","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a database migration test verify?","answer":"Migration tests should verify that the migration produces the intended schema and data state, preserves required data, handles representative existing states, and supports the application's compatibility requirements during the deployment transition.","requirements":["Define supported pre-migration schema states.","Define expected post-migration schema.","Define data-preservation requirements.","Define application compatibility requirements during transition."],"failure_mode":"A migration succeeds syntactically but corrupts data or leaves the application unable to operate.","root_cause":"The test checked migration completion without validating semantic state and compatibility.","solution":"Test representative real schema histories, data shapes, rollback or recovery procedures where supported, and application compatibility.","testing_guidance":"Include empty, minimal, representative, large, and historically relevant schema states where risk warrants.","verification_method":"Compare schema metadata and business-relevant data invariants before and after migration.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_MIGRATION_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established database migration testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R004","D20-B19-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B19-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Database Testing","subtopic":"Isolation","concept":"Database test isolation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can database tests prevent state leakage between cases?","answer":"Database tests can isolate state through transaction rollback, disposable databases or schemas, controlled fixtures, or other environment-specific isolation mechanisms. The correct mechanism depends on the database behavior and what the test is intended to validate.","constraints":["The isolation mechanism must not hide behavior the test is intended to validate.","Tests involving transaction semantics must reflect the actual production transaction model.","Concurrent tests require additional isolation analysis."],"failure_mode":"One test leaves rows, schema changes, locks, or configuration state that changes another test's result.","root_cause":"Database state lifetime exceeds the intended test boundary.","solution":"Choose an explicit isolation strategy and verify cleanup and concurrency behavior.","testing_guidance":"Run tests repeatedly, in different orders, and concurrently where the test infrastructure supports it.","verification_method":"Verify that each test begins from its declared database state and leaves no unintended state behind.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established database testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R002","D20-B05-R001","D20-B19-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B19-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Database Testing","subtopic":"Constraint Testing","concept":"Database constraint enforcement","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should database constraints be tested rather than assumed?","answer":"Constraints such as uniqueness, referential integrity, nullability, and domain restrictions are part of the persisted-data contract when the schema enforces them. Tests should verify expected application behavior when those constraints accept or reject operations.","requirements":["Identify constraints that form part of the application contract.","Test valid operations.","Test representative constraint violations.","Verify application error classification and state after rejection."],"failure_mode":"The application handles constraint violations incorrectly or assumes a constraint that is not actually enforced.","root_cause":"Database semantics were treated as implicit and unverified.","solution":"Include constraint behavior in integration tests and verify schema state as part of migration testing.","testing_guidance":"Exercise each contract-critical constraint with both valid and invalid data.","verification_method":"Inspect schema metadata and execute representative acceptance/rejection scenarios where available.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established relational database testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B19-R002","D20-B19-R001","D20-B12-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B19-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Database Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Concurrent database operation testing","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should concurrency-sensitive database behavior be tested explicitly?","answer":"Correctness under concurrent transactions can differ from sequential behavior because of isolation levels, locking, race conditions, unique constraints, and transaction timing. Tests should model the concurrency property that the application actually requires.","failure_mode":"Two concurrent operations both appear successful even though the intended invariant permits only one.","root_cause":"The test suite validates only sequential execution.","solution":"Construct controlled concurrent scenarios around critical invariants and verify the resulting database state.","testing_guidance":"Use barriers or synchronization controls to make relevant interleavings reproducible instead of relying solely on timing luck.","verification_method":"Verify final state and operation outcomes against the documented concurrency invariant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_CONCURRENCY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established database concurrency testing practice"],"relationships":["D19-B25-R001","D19-B25-R003","D20-B07-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B20-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Test Environment Engineering","subtopic":"Environment Parity","concept":"Relevant environment parity","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does environment parity mean for reliable testing?","answer":"Environment parity means that the test environment reproduces the production characteristics that can materially affect the behavior being tested. It does not require every environment detail to be identical.","principle":"Match behaviorally relevant dependencies and configuration while explicitly documenting intentional differences.","failure_mode":"Tests pass in an environment whose behavior differs materially from production.","root_cause":"The test environment omitted a production-relevant dependency, configuration, runtime, protocol, or resource constraint.","solution":"Identify behaviorally relevant environment dimensions and include them in an explicit parity matrix.","testing_guidance":"Maintain a matrix covering runtime versions, database behavior, network protocols, configuration, permissions, external services, and resource constraints as relevant.","verification_method":"Review each production dependency for either parity evidence or an explicit validated test substitute.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_ENVIRONMENT_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established software testing and deployment engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B04-R004","D20-B09-R004","D18-B50-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B20-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Test Environment Engineering","subtopic":"Configuration Control","concept":"Versioned test configuration","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should test configuration be version-controlled?","answer":"Version-controlled test configuration makes test behavior reproducible and allows failures to be correlated with changes in environment assumptions. Hidden local configuration can invalidate test evidence.","requirements":["Record relevant runtime and dependency versions.","Version test configuration that materially affects behavior.","Separate secrets from version-controlled configuration while preserving reproducible non-secret settings."],"failure_mode":"A test passes locally but fails elsewhere because untracked configuration differs.","root_cause":"Test behavior depends on unmanaged environment state.","solution":"Declare and version relevant configuration and validate required environment assumptions at test startup.","security_implications":"Secrets must not be committed merely for reproducibility; secure secret injection should be used instead.","verification_method":"Compare test runs using the declared configuration and detect undeclared environmental dependencies.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_ENVIRONMENT_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19-B05-R002","Established reproducible-build and test-environment practice"],"relationships":["D20-B09-R004","D19-B05-R002","D20-B20-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B20-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Test Environment Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Availability","concept":"Controlled external dependency availability","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should tests handle external dependencies that are unavailable or unreliable?","answer":"Tests should explicitly distinguish product behavior from infrastructure or dependency availability. Depending on the test objective, the dependency can be replaced with a contract-tested fake, controlled service, local instance, or real integration environment.","failure_mode":"A product test is reported as a product failure when the external dependency was unavailable.","root_cause":"The test does not capture dependency health or distinguish infrastructure failure from product failure.","solution":"Define dependency availability as part of the test environment contract and preserve evidence about infrastructure failures.","testing_guidance":"Simulate unavailable, slow, malformed, and recovering dependencies where relevant.","verification_method":"Classify failures using dependency evidence and verify that product assertions are not evaluated as if the dependency were healthy when it was not.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_INTEGRATION_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D20-B04-R004","D20-B09-R005","Established integration-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B11-R003","D20-B18-R001","D20-B09-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B20-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Test Environment Engineering","subtopic":"Resource Constraints","concept":"Resource-constrained test environments","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should important resource limits be represented in testing?","answer":"Software can behave correctly under unlimited local resources while failing under production-relevant memory, CPU, file descriptor, connection, storage, or concurrency limits. Tests should reproduce resource constraints when those limits are part of the operational contract or risk model.","requirements":["Identify resource limits that materially affect behavior.","Apply controlled limits where practical.","Verify both graceful degradation and failure behavior when limits are reached."],"failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion causes uncontrolled failure despite nominal functional tests passing.","root_cause":"The test environment provides resources that production does not guarantee.","solution":"Add resource-constrained scenarios to the appropriate test level and verify documented behavior at the limit.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled resource limits and measure only when actual measurement is performed; do not invent capacity figures.","performance_implications":"Any capacity or performance result must be labeled according to actual evidence; this record does not provide a benchmark.","verification_method":"Verify behavior at configured resource boundaries and compare it with the declared operational requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RESOURCE_BOUNDARY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established reliability and resource-boundary testing practice","D20-B07-R004"],"relationships":["D20-B07-R004","D21","D18-B73-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B20-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Test Environment Engineering","subtopic":"Environment Health","concept":"Pre-test environment health gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should automated test suites validate environment health before evaluating product assertions?","answer":"A test should establish that required infrastructure, dependencies, configuration, permissions, and runtime prerequisites are available before interpreting product-level failures. Otherwise environment faults can be misclassified as product defects.","requirements":["Define required environment prerequisites.","Check them before or as part of test setup.","Preserve evidence when prerequisites are unavailable.","Do not silently skip product assertions while reporting success."],"failure_mode":"A dependency outage causes tests to be skipped or misclassified as passing.","root_cause":"Environment readiness is not represented as an explicit test state.","solution":"Model environment readiness as a prerequisite state and classify unavailable-environment outcomes separately from product pass/fail.","testing_guidance":"Deliberately disable a required dependency and verify that the suite produces a clear infrastructure/environment classification rather than a false product result.","verification_method":"Inspect test-result semantics and confirm that environment failure cannot be represented as a successful product verification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_ASSURANCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established test-infrastructure reliability practice","D20-B09-R005"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B09-R005","D20-B20-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B21-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"API Testing","subtopic":"API Contract Testing","concept":"API contract conformance","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should an API contract test verify?","answer":"An API contract test verifies that an implementation conforms to the agreed request, response, error, and protocol semantics at the tested boundary. It should distinguish syntactic conformance from behavioral and semantic conformance.","requirements":["Define request schema and required fields.","Define response schema and required fields.","Define status and error semantics.","Define relevant authentication and authorization expectations.","Define compatibility requirements for supported clients."],"failure_mode":"An API satisfies schema validation while violating semantic or error-handling requirements.","root_cause":"The contract test verifies structure but not behavior.","solution":"Combine schema validation with behaviorally meaningful contract assertions.","testing_guidance":"Test valid requests, invalid requests, boundary values, documented errors, and compatibility-sensitive fields.","verification_method":"Trace each assertion to the authoritative API contract and verify that undocumented assumptions are not treated as contract requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_API_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 testing principles","Established API contract-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B04-R001","D20-B01-R002","D19-B14-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B21-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"API Testing","subtopic":"API Compatibility","concept":"Backward-compatible API evolution","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should API tests detect breaking changes?","answer":"Compatibility tests should exercise previously supported request and response behavior and identify changes that violate the declared compatibility policy. Compatibility is defined by the actual API contract rather than by whether the implementation still parses old requests.","principle":"A compatibility test must encode the compatibility boundary explicitly.","failure_mode":"An API change is classified as compatible because requests still parse even though required response semantics changed.","root_cause":"Compatibility was reduced to syntactic acceptance.","solution":"Test contract-relevant fields, status semantics, error behavior, optionality, defaults, and versioning rules.","testing_guidance":"Maintain representative contract fixtures from supported client versions and test them against the new implementation.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior with the declared compatibility policy for each supported version.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_API_COMPATIBILITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established API versioning and contract-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B21-R001","D20-B06-R004","D19-B86-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B21-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"API Testing","subtopic":"Error Contract Testing","concept":"Structured error behavior","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should API error tests verify?","answer":"API error tests should verify the documented status classification, stable machine-readable error identifiers or fields when defined, response schema, security-sensitive information exposure, and required state invariants. Exact human-readable messages should be tested only when they are contractually significant.","requirements":["Define expected error classification.","Define stable machine-readable error fields.","Define security disclosure constraints.","Define required post-failure state."],"failure_mode":"The API returns an error but exposes internal details or uses an incorrect error classification.","root_cause":"The test checks only that some error occurred.","solution":"Assert the complete contract-relevant failure behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test malformed input, unauthorized access, forbidden access, missing resources, conflicts, dependency failures, and server-side failures as applicable.","verification_method":"Trace error assertions to the API specification and security requirements.","security_implications":"Error responses must not expose credentials, secrets, internal stack traces, or unnecessary infrastructure details.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_API_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19-B10-R001","D19-B10-R004","Established API testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B12-R004","D19-B14-R005","D19-B10-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B21-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"API Testing","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Idempotent-operation regression testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should an API test verify an idempotent operation?","answer":"The test should execute the operation repeatedly under the conditions defined by its contract and verify that repeated application produces the same externally required effect, while distinguishing identical final state from identical response metadata when those differ.","requirements":["Define the operation's idempotency semantics.","Define whether repeated requests require the same response or only the same effect.","Control request identifiers or idempotency keys when applicable.","Verify side-effect count or equivalent invariant."],"failure_mode":"Repeated requests create duplicate side effects despite an idempotency requirement.","root_cause":"The test checks response success without verifying externally observable effects.","solution":"Assert the relevant state or side-effect invariant across repeated requests.","testing_guidance":"Test immediate repetition, retry after timeout, retry after ambiguous network failure, and duplicate delivery where applicable.","verification_method":"Compare final state and side-effect evidence against the documented idempotency contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_API_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D18-B41 HTTP idempotency concepts","Established API testing practice"],"relationships":["D18-B41-R001","D18-B49-R001","D20-B18-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B21-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"API Testing","subtopic":"API Test Matrix","concept":"API behavioral coverage matrix","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should an API test matrix be constructed?","answer":"Construct the matrix from endpoints or operations, authentication states, authorization states, input partitions, boundaries, dependency conditions, expected success and failure semantics, and compatibility requirements. Prioritize combinations according to risk rather than generating every Cartesian combination indiscriminately.","procedure":["Inventory API operations.","Identify authentication and authorization states.","Partition request inputs.","Identify boundary and malformed inputs.","Identify dependency and infrastructure failure states.","Map expected response and state behavior.","Select risk-critical combinations.","Trace scenarios to requirements and contracts."],"testing_guidance":"Keep the matrix version-aware and update it when the API contract changes.","verification_method":"Audit matrix coverage against the API specification and identified security, reliability, and compatibility risks.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_API_TEST_DESIGN_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 testing principles","Established API testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R004","D20-B08-R002","D20-B21-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B22-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"System Testing","subtopic":"End-to-End Scope","concept":"End-to-end behavioral boundary","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should an end-to-end test prove?","answer":"An end-to-end test should prove a meaningful user or system workflow across the production-relevant component boundaries included in its scope. It should not attempt to prove every internal implementation detail of every component.","principle":"End-to-end tests provide system-level evidence across real or production-representative boundaries; lower-level tests remain responsible for detailed component behavior.","failure_mode":"A large end-to-end suite becomes slow, fragile, and difficult to diagnose because it duplicates lower-level coverage.","root_cause":"The test level is being used to verify behaviors better covered elsewhere.","solution":"Reserve end-to-end tests for critical workflows and cross-boundary invariants while maintaining unit and integration evidence underneath.","testing_guidance":"Map each end-to-end scenario to a critical workflow, business invariant, or cross-system contract.","verification_method":"Review whether each scenario requires the included component boundaries to provide meaningful evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SYSTEM_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 testing principles","Established system and end-to-end testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R003","D20-B04-R001","D20-B22-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B22-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"System Testing","subtopic":"Critical Workflows","concept":"Critical-path workflow testing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should critical end-to-end workflows be selected?","answer":"Select workflows based on business or system criticality, failure impact, cross-boundary complexity, historical defects, security relevance, and operational risk. Avoid selecting workflows solely because they are easy to automate.","procedure":["Identify critical user or system outcomes.","Map the component and dependency boundaries crossed.","Identify failure impact and risk.","Select representative successful and failure workflows.","Define the required end-to-end oracle.","Link each workflow to lower-level supporting tests."],"failure_mode":"The end-to-end suite has high execution cost but provides little evidence for important system behavior.","root_cause":"Scenario selection was driven by convenience rather than risk and system criticality.","solution":"Use explicit risk-based workflow selection and periodically review coverage.","verification_method":"Trace selected workflows to documented requirements, risks, incidents, or critical business operations.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_RISK_BASED_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 risk-based testing principles","Established end-to-end testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R001","D20-B22-R001","D20-B22-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B22-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"System Testing","subtopic":"Cross-Boundary State","concept":"End-to-end state invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should end-to-end tests verify state across component boundaries?","answer":"A workflow can return a successful response while leaving inconsistent state in another component. End-to-end tests should verify critical cross-boundary state invariants when those invariants form part of the workflow contract.","requirements":["Identify the authoritative state for each invariant.","Define eventual versus immediate consistency expectations.","Define the observation mechanism.","Define acceptable intermediate states when asynchronous processing is involved."],"failure_mode":"The front-end or initiating service reports success while downstream state is missing or inconsistent.","root_cause":"The test verifies only the initiating response rather than the complete workflow outcome.","solution":"Assert the required downstream state using the correct consistency model and observation point.","testing_guidance":"Include success, partial failure, retry, and recovery scenarios for critical workflows.","verification_method":"Compare final system state with the workflow invariant after allowing only the documented convergence period.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEM_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established end-to-end and distributed-system testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B07-R005","D18-B50-R003","D20-B22-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B22-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"System Testing","subtopic":"Failure Attribution","concept":"Cross-layer failure evidence","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can end-to-end test failures be difficult to diagnose?","answer":"An end-to-end failure can originate in any participating component, dependency, environment layer, or test harness. The test should preserve enough evidence to distinguish product failure from infrastructure, dependency, configuration, and test-environment failure.","failure_mode":"A single end-to-end failure is reported without request identifiers, component evidence, dependency status, or environment state.","root_cause":"The test captures only the final assertion and discards intermediate evidence.","solution":"Capture structured evidence at relevant boundaries while controlling sensitive-data exposure and telemetry volume.","testing_guidance":"Preserve timestamps, correlation identifiers, relevant request/response metadata, component outcomes, and dependency classifications.","verification_method":"Verify that a representative failure can be traced to the first known failed stage using preserved evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DIAGNOSTICS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D18-B77 end-to-end network correlation concepts","Established distributed-test diagnostics practice"],"relationships":["D18-B77-R001","D20-B05-R005","D20-B20-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B22-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"System Testing","subtopic":"End-to-End Suite Governance","concept":"End-to-end test portfolio governance","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should an end-to-end test suite remain maintainable?","answer":"An end-to-end suite should have explicit ownership, criticality, environment requirements, expected execution cost, failure diagnostics, and retirement criteria. Tests that no longer protect meaningful behavior should be reviewed rather than retained indefinitely.","requirements":["Each critical scenario has an identified purpose.","Environment dependencies are explicit.","Failure evidence is actionable.","Scenario relevance is periodically reviewed."],"failure_mode":"The suite grows continuously while execution time and diagnostic cost increase without proportional coverage value.","root_cause":"There is no lifecycle governance for end-to-end scenarios.","solution":"Review scenarios based on risk, historical value, redundancy, maintenance cost, and current requirements.","verification_method":"Perform periodic portfolio audits and trace representative scenarios to current requirements or known risks.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_SUITE_GOVERNANCE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established test-suite maintenance practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R004","D20-B10-R004","D20-B22-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B23-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"UI Testing","subtopic":"User Interface Behavior","concept":"UI behavior versus implementation structure","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What should a UI test primarily verify?","answer":"A UI test should primarily verify user-observable behavior, accessibility-relevant semantics, and important workflow outcomes rather than fragile implementation details such as internal DOM structure or CSS class names that are not contractual.","principle":"Automated UI tests should target stable user-facing semantics whenever possible.","failure_mode":"Minor UI refactoring causes widespread test failures without changing user-visible behavior.","root_cause":"Selectors and assertions are coupled to implementation details.","solution":"Use stable semantic selectors and assertions tied to user-observable behavior.","testing_guidance":"Prefer accessible roles, labels, names, stable identifiers, and documented interaction semantics over generated CSS or DOM structure.","verification_method":"Review selectors and assertions for dependency on non-contractual implementation details.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_UI_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established browser/UI testing practice","Established accessibility-oriented testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R003","D20-B23-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B23-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"UI Testing","subtopic":"Stable Selectors","concept":"Semantic selector strategy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What makes a UI selector resilient to ordinary implementation changes?","answer":"A resilient selector identifies an element through stable semantics such as an accessible role, label, associated text, or an explicitly designated test identifier when semantic selectors are insufficient. Selectors should represent the intended interaction rather than incidental layout structure.","constraints":["The selector must identify the intended element unambiguously within the tested state.","Test identifiers should remain stable and should not encode transient implementation details.","Accessibility semantics must not be weakened merely to support testing."],"failure_mode":"Tests break when CSS classes, layout containers, or generated DOM structure changes.","root_cause":"Selectors were based on incidental presentation details.","solution":"Prefer semantic selectors and deliberately designed stable identifiers.","verification_method":"Refactor non-functional UI structure and verify that tests remain valid.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_UI_TEST_AUTOMATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established browser automation practice","Established accessibility testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B23-R001","D20-B06-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B23-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"UI Testing","subtopic":"Accessibility","concept":"Accessibility behavior verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should accessibility semantics be included in UI testing?","answer":"Accessibility semantics are part of user-observable interface behavior for supported assistive technologies and keyboard interaction. UI tests should verify important labels, roles, focus behavior, keyboard operation, and state announcements where those are part of the interface requirements.","requirements":["Identify supported accessibility behavior.","Verify meaningful accessible names and roles.","Verify keyboard navigation for applicable workflows.","Verify focus movement and state changes where required."],"failure_mode":"The visual workflow works with a mouse but is inaccessible through the supported interaction model.","root_cause":"UI testing covered visual interaction only.","solution":"Include accessibility-oriented interaction and semantic checks in the test strategy.","testing_guidance":"Combine automated semantic checks with appropriate higher-level accessibility evaluation; automated checks do not prove complete accessibility.","verification_method":"Verify required semantics against the UI accessibility requirements and supported interaction model.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_ACCESSIBILITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["W3C Web Accessibility Initiative guidance","Established accessibility testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B23-R001","D20-B23-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B23-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"UI Testing","subtopic":"Stateful Interfaces","concept":"UI state transition testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should tests cover stateful UI workflows?","answer":"Tests should identify meaningful UI states and transitions and verify that actions produce the permitted transitions and preserve required invariants. This is more robust than testing isolated screenshots or clicks without state semantics.","requirements":["Define important UI states.","Define allowed transitions.","Define actions that trigger transitions.","Define required state invariants."],"failure_mode":"A control is enabled or visible in a state where it should not be usable, or a transition leaves stale state.","root_cause":"The test checks isolated elements instead of state transitions.","solution":"Model critical workflows as state transitions and assert state-dependent behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test normal transitions, invalid actions, cancellation, retry, refresh, and restoration where relevant.","verification_method":"Compare observed transitions against the declared UI state model.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_STATE_BASED_UI_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established state-based testing practice","Established UI testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R005","D20-B07-R005","D20-B23-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B23-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"UI Testing","subtopic":"Visual Regression","concept":"Visual regression scope","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What can visual regression testing prove and what can it not prove?","answer":"Visual regression testing can detect changes in rendered appearance relative to a defined baseline. It does not by itself prove semantic correctness, accessibility, functional behavior, or that a visual difference is a defect.","principle":"A visual diff is evidence of rendering change, not an automatic correctness verdict.","failure_mode":"Every pixel difference is treated as a product defect or every unchanged screenshot is treated as proof of functional correctness.","root_cause":"Visual evidence was interpreted beyond what it measures.","solution":"Review visual changes against expected design and combine visual tests with functional and accessibility tests.","testing_guidance":"Control viewport, fonts, browser/runtime versions, data, timing, and rendering conditions where visual determinism is required.","verification_method":"Classify visual differences using the intended baseline and corroborating functional evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VISUAL_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established visual regression testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B23-R003","D20-B23-R004","D20-B05-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B24-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Database Integration Testing","subtopic":"Realistic Database Behavior","concept":"Real-database integration evidence","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why can database integration tests require a real database engine?","answer":"A fake or in-memory substitute may not reproduce SQL semantics, transaction behavior, constraints, locking, isolation, indexing, data types, or engine-specific behavior that the application depends on. Real-engine testing is therefore necessary when those behaviors are part of the integration contract.","constraints":["The chosen test database should match the production engine when engine-specific behavior matters.","Substitutes may still be useful for lower-level tests when their limitations are understood."],"failure_mode":"Application tests pass against a substitute but fail against the production database engine.","root_cause":"Important database semantics were not represented by the test dependency.","solution":"Use real-engine integration tests for behavior that cannot be faithfully represented by the substitute.","testing_guidance":"Separate fast unit tests from targeted real-database integration tests.","verification_method":"Identify production-dependent database semantics and verify that each is tested against an appropriate implementation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_INTEGRATION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established database integration-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B04-R003","D20-B19-R001","D20-B19-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B24-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Database Integration Testing","subtopic":"Schema Compatibility","concept":"Application-schema compatibility","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should integration tests verify application compatibility with the database schema?","answer":"Tests should execute representative application operations against the intended schema version and verify that queries, writes, constraints, migrations, and transaction semantics remain compatible with the deployed application expectations.","requirements":["Define supported application/schema version combinations.","Test representative reads and writes.","Test contract-critical constraints and transaction behavior.","Test migration transition states when required."],"failure_mode":"The schema is valid but application queries or writes no longer match its expected contract.","root_cause":"Schema testing and application compatibility testing were separated too far.","solution":"Add compatibility tests that exercise real application behavior against supported schema states.","verification_method":"Map representative application operations to schema contracts and validate them across supported transition states.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_COMPATIBILITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established database migration and integration testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B19-R002","D20-B21-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B24-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Database Integration Testing","subtopic":"Query Result Semantics","concept":"Null and empty-state distinction","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should database integration tests distinguish null, empty, missing, and zero-like values?","answer":"Different database and application semantics can assign different meanings to NULL, an empty collection, a missing row, an empty string, or a numeric zero. Tests should encode the distinction when the domain contract differentiates them.","failure_mode":"A query or mapper converts distinct database states into one application value and loses required information.","root_cause":"The test oracle treats semantically distinct values as equivalent.","solution":"Create explicit cases for each contract-relevant representation and verify mapping behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test absent rows, nullable columns, empty collections, empty strings, and zero values where each has distinct domain meaning.","verification_method":"Trace each expected representation to the schema and application domain contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established database and data-mapping testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R001","D20-B12-R005","D20-B19-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B24-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Database Integration Testing","subtopic":"Query Pagination","concept":"Pagination boundary testing","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Which database-backed pagination cases should be tested?","answer":"Pagination tests should cover empty results, first and last pages, exact page boundaries, incomplete final pages, duplicate or missing records under the chosen ordering strategy, invalid page parameters, and concurrent data changes when those changes can affect correctness.","requirements":["Define stable ordering semantics.","Define page-size constraints.","Define behavior for invalid or out-of-range parameters.","Define consistency expectations during concurrent changes."],"failure_mode":"Records are skipped or duplicated across pages.","root_cause":"Pagination relies on unstable ordering or ambiguous boundary semantics.","solution":"Use an explicit stable ordering strategy and test boundary and mutation scenarios relevant to the consistency model.","testing_guidance":"Test page sizes around boundary values and datasets with counts exactly divisible and not divisible by the page size.","verification_method":"Compare the union and ordering of retrieved records with the expected pagination contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DATABASE_API_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established pagination and database testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R002","D20-B24-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B24-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Database Integration Testing","subtopic":"Database Failure Classification","concept":"Database infrastructure versus product failure","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should database test failures be classified?","answer":"A failed database-backed test should distinguish application logic failure, schema incompatibility, database availability failure, migration failure, transaction behavior failure, and test-environment failure when evidence permits. A generic database exception is insufficient for reliable diagnosis.","failure_mode":"All database errors are reported as product defects.","root_cause":"The test harness does not preserve database state and infrastructure evidence.","solution":"Capture structured database and environment evidence and classify the first known failed stage.","testing_guidance":"Preserve database version, schema state, migration state, relevant transaction outcome, and infrastructure status without exposing sensitive data.","verification_method":"Verify that representative failures can be mapped to a meaningful failure category.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DIAGNOSTICS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D20-B05-R005","D20-B20-R005","Established database testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B19-R001","D20-B22-R004","D20-B20-R005"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B25-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Message and Event Testing","subtopic":"Delivery Semantics","concept":"At-most-once at-least-once exactly-once claims","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should tests distinguish message delivery semantics?","answer":"Tests must reflect the actual delivery guarantee relevant to the messaging system: at-most-once permits loss without redelivery, at-least-once permits duplicate delivery, and exactly-once semantics require a precisely defined scope because end-to-end exactly-once effects are not established merely by a transport label.","constraints":["Define the scope of the guarantee.","Test duplicate, loss, ordering, and retry behavior according to the actual system.","Do not infer exactly-once business effects from a lower-level delivery property."],"failure_mode":"The consumer assumes messages cannot be duplicated when the transport permits redelivery.","root_cause":"Delivery semantics were not represented in the test model.","solution":"Build tests around the declared delivery guarantee and application idempotency requirements.","testing_guidance":"Inject duplicate, delayed, reordered, and lost messages where the system permits or simulates those conditions.","verification_method":"Trace the claimed delivery behavior to authoritative middleware documentation and observed system semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEM_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established messaging-system testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B21-R004","D18-B49-R001","D20-B25-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B25-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Message and Event Testing","subtopic":"Duplicate Delivery","concept":"Consumer idempotency testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should a message consumer be tested for duplicate delivery?","answer":"The test should deliver the same logical message more than once and verify that the resulting externally observable effect satisfies the consumer's idempotency contract. Duplicate processing may be acceptable internally if the final required effect remains correct.","requirements":["Define the message identity semantics.","Define the idempotency boundary.","Define expected side effects.","Define behavior when duplicate messages arrive after partial processing."],"failure_mode":"Duplicate delivery produces duplicate business side effects.","root_cause":"The consumer assumes delivery uniqueness that is not guaranteed.","solution":"Implement and test an appropriate deduplication or idempotent-effect strategy.","testing_guidance":"Test immediate duplicates, delayed duplicates, duplicates after retry, and duplicates after consumer restart where relevant.","verification_method":"Compare final state and side-effect evidence with the idempotency invariant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_EVENT_DRIVEN_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established event-driven and distributed-systems testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B21-R004","D20-B25-R001","D20-B25-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B25-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Message and Event Testing","subtopic":"Ordering","concept":"Message ordering contract","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a message-ordering test verify?","answer":"An ordering test should verify only the ordering guarantees actually required by the consumer or protocol. It should define the ordering key, scope, permitted reordering, and behavior when messages arrive outside the expected order.","requirements":["Define ordering scope.","Define the ordering key.","Define allowed reordering.","Define expected consumer behavior for out-of-order events."],"failure_mode":"The consumer relies on global ordering when the system guarantees ordering only per key or partition.","root_cause":"The test assumes a stronger ordering contract than the messaging system provides.","solution":"Encode the actual ordering guarantee and add explicit out-of-order scenarios.","testing_guidance":"Test same-key ordering, different-key concurrency, delayed messages, and duplicate delivery where relevant.","verification_method":"Compare the test assumptions with authoritative messaging-system semantics and the application contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_EVENT_SYSTEM_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established messaging and distributed-system testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B25-R001","D20-B25-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B25-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Message and Event Testing","subtopic":"Poison Messages","concept":"Malformed or permanently failing message handling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should messaging tests include permanently failing messages?","answer":"A malformed or unsupported message can repeatedly fail processing and prevent healthy messages from progressing depending on the queue and consumer design. Tests should verify bounded failure handling, quarantine or dead-letter behavior where applicable, and preservation of evidence.","failure_mode":"One message is retried indefinitely and consumes processing capacity.","root_cause":"The consumer lacks a bounded failure policy.","solution":"Define maximum retry behavior, quarantine or dead-letter handling, and operator-visible evidence for permanently failing messages.","testing_guidance":"Inject malformed, unsupported-version, and semantically invalid messages and verify the documented terminal handling.","verification_method":"Verify retry count, terminal disposition, message preservation requirements, and downstream processing continuity.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_MESSAGE_PROCESSING_TESTING","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established queue and event-processing reliability practice"],"relationships":["D18-B49-R003","D20-B18-R005","D20-B25-R005"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B25-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Message and Event Testing","subtopic":"Consumer Recovery","concept":"Consumer restart and recovery testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should message-consumer restart tests verify?","answer":"Restart tests should verify recovery of consumer state, correct handling of unacknowledged or in-flight messages according to delivery semantics, preservation of required offsets or checkpoints, and absence of duplicate business effects beyond the documented contract.","requirements":["Define checkpoint or acknowledgment semantics.","Define restart recovery behavior.","Define duplicate-processing tolerance.","Define required state reconstruction."],"failure_mode":"A restart loses progress or repeats effects incorrectly.","root_cause":"Consumer recovery semantics were not tested against actual delivery and state guarantees.","solution":"Test interruption at meaningful processing stages and verify recovery invariants.","testing_guidance":"Interrupt before processing, during processing, after side effect, and before acknowledgment where those states are relevant.","verification_method":"Compare recovered consumer state and final business effects with the declared recovery contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_EVENT_PROCESSING_TESTING","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established event-driven reliability testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B25-R001","D20-B25-R002","D20-B25-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B26-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Distributed Systems Testing","subtopic":"Partial Failure","concept":"Partial dependency failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should distributed-system tests model partial failure rather than only total outage?","answer":"Distributed systems can remain partially available while individual dependencies, replicas, network paths, partitions, or operations fail. Partial failure can produce inconsistent states and behavior that a complete outage test does not expose.","failure_mode":"One dependency fails while others continue, causing the application to enter an untested intermediate state.","root_cause":"The test model assumes dependencies fail as one unit.","solution":"Test isolated dependency failures and verify degradation, fallback, consistency, and recovery behavior.","testing_guidance":"Fail one component at a time while keeping other required components available.","verification_method":"Verify the resulting state and behavior against the system's partial-failure contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEM_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established distributed-systems reliability testing practice"],"relationships":["D18-B50-R003","D20-B18-R001","D20-B22-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B26-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Distributed Systems Testing","subtopic":"Network Partition","concept":"Partition behavior testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a distributed-system partition test verify?","answer":"A partition test should verify the behavior of communicating components when network communication is unavailable or selectively impaired, including timeout, retry, consistency, duplicate processing, failover, and recovery semantics relevant to the architecture.","requirements":["Define affected communication paths.","Define expected availability behavior.","Define consistency expectations.","Define recovery behavior after communication resumes."],"failure_mode":"Components make incompatible assumptions while partitioned and produce unsafe or unreconcilable state.","root_cause":"Partition behavior was not represented in the architecture's test model.","solution":"Inject controlled partitions and verify declared invariants and recovery procedures.","testing_guidance":"Test one-way and two-way communication loss when relevant, and verify recovery after reconnection.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior with the architecture's consistency and availability contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEM_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established distributed-systems testing practice"],"relationships":["D18-B50-R003","D20-B26-R001","D20-B26-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B26-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Distributed Systems Testing","subtopic":"Recovery","concept":"Recovery convergence testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What does recovery convergence testing verify?","answer":"Recovery convergence testing verifies that after a transient distributed failure ends, components move toward the required consistent or converged state within the semantics and timing guarantees defined by the system.","requirements":["Define the target converged state.","Define acceptable intermediate states.","Define recovery trigger.","Define any bounded convergence requirement."],"failure_mode":"Components recover connectivity but remain permanently divergent.","root_cause":"Recovery was tested only for availability restoration, not state correctness.","solution":"Assert post-recovery invariants and convergence behavior.","testing_guidance":"Capture state before failure, during failure, immediately after recovery, and after the documented reconciliation period.","verification_method":"Compare the final state against the required convergence invariant rather than merely checking that services are reachable.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_RECOVERY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established distributed-system recovery testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B22-R003","D20-B26-R002","D18-B50-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B26-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Distributed Systems Testing","subtopic":"Clock and Ordering","concept":"Distributed timestamp limitations","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should distributed tests avoid treating timestamps as universal event ordering?","answer":"Different machines can observe different clock values, and clock synchronization has finite accuracy. A timestamp can provide useful temporal evidence but does not automatically establish causal ordering between distributed events.","constraints":["Use explicit correlation, sequence, logical-clock, or protocol evidence when causal ordering matters.","Document timestamp precision and clock assumptions."],"failure_mode":"A test concludes that event A caused event B solely because A has an earlier wall-clock timestamp.","root_cause":"Temporal observation was mistaken for causal evidence.","solution":"Use architecture-appropriate ordering or correlation mechanisms.","testing_guidance":"Test events with close timestamps and independent processing paths to expose incorrect ordering assumptions.","verification_method":"Trace ordering assertions to the system's actual causal or sequencing mechanism.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established distributed-systems reasoning practice"],"relationships":["D18-B77-R001","D20-B22-R004","D20-B16-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B26-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Distributed Systems Testing","subtopic":"Fault Injection Scope","concept":"Controlled fault injection boundaries","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should fault injection be scoped in distributed-system tests?","answer":"Fault injection should target a specific failure mechanism while preserving enough observability to determine the resulting system behavior. Broad uncontrolled disruption can make the result uninterpretable.","procedure":["Define the hypothesis or invariant under test.","Identify the smallest relevant failure boundary.","Inject the fault deterministically where practical.","Capture system and dependency evidence.","Observe recovery and final invariants.","Remove the fault and verify recovery behavior."],"constraints":["Do not inject faults that exceed the intended test scope without a documented reason.","Production fault injection requires separate authorization and safety controls."],"verification_method":"Verify that the observed failure corresponds to the injected mechanism and that required post-fault invariants hold.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_FAULT_INJECTION_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established fault-injection and resilience-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B07-R002","D20-B26-R001","D20-B26-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B27-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Compatibility Testing","subtopic":"Version Compatibility","concept":"Compatibility matrix","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a compatibility test matrix represent supported software versions?","answer":"A compatibility matrix should explicitly identify supported producer, consumer, runtime, protocol, schema, or dependency versions and test the combinations that the compatibility policy requires. Unsupported combinations should be classified rather than accidentally treated as supported.","procedure":["Enumerate supported version dimensions.","Define compatibility policy for each dimension.","Identify required version combinations.","Add transition and boundary cases.","Test representative supported combinations.","Record unsupported combinations separately."],"failure_mode":"A combination works accidentally and is later treated as an officially supported configuration.","root_cause":"Support status was inferred from incidental test success.","solution":"Separate observed compatibility from declared support and encode the support policy explicitly.","verification_method":"Trace each supported matrix cell to a compatibility requirement or release policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_COMPATIBILITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established compatibility and integration testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B21-R002","D20-B24-R002","D19-B86-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B27-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Compatibility Testing","subtopic":"Schema Evolution","concept":"Producer-consumer schema compatibility","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should schema-evolution tests verify in producer-consumer systems?","answer":"Schema-evolution tests should verify that supported old and new producers and consumers can exchange data according to the declared compatibility policy, including optionality, defaults, field removal, field addition, and unknown-field behavior where applicable.","requirements":["Define schema compatibility direction.","Define required and optional fields.","Define default semantics.","Define unknown-field behavior.","Test supported producer-consumer version pairs."],"failure_mode":"A consumer fails or misinterprets data after a compatible schema change.","root_cause":"Schema compatibility was evaluated syntactically without testing actual producer-consumer behavior.","solution":"Execute representative cross-version exchange tests against the supported compatibility matrix.","verification_method":"Compare observed serialization/deserialization and semantic interpretation with the declared schema compatibility policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SCHEMA_COMPATIBILITY_TESTING","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established schema-evolution and contract-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B27-R001","D20-B25-R003","D20-B21-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B27-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Compatibility Testing","subtopic":"Runtime Compatibility","concept":"Runtime-version regression testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should supported runtime versions be tested explicitly?","answer":"Language-runtime behavior, standard-library behavior, compiler behavior, dependency support, and platform interfaces can differ across versions. A declared runtime support range requires evidence for the relevant versions or an explicit basis for excluding them.","requirements":["Declare supported runtime versions.","Identify version-sensitive dependencies or language behavior.","Test representative supported versions.","Record unsupported or unverified versions explicitly."],"failure_mode":"The application is declared compatible with a runtime version that was never actually validated.","root_cause":"Support status was inferred from source compatibility alone.","solution":"Maintain a version-aware validation matrix and distinguish verified support from declared intent.","verification_method":"Trace runtime support claims to test or authoritative compatibility evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_VERSION_COMPATIBILITY_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established multi-version software testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B27-R001","D20-B20-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B27-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Compatibility Testing","subtopic":"Protocol Evolution","concept":"Protocol backward compatibility","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should protocol compatibility be tested across versions?","answer":"Protocol compatibility tests should exercise supported combinations of protocol versions and capabilities and verify negotiation, message interpretation, error handling, and security constraints. A connection succeeding is not sufficient if the negotiated behavior violates the protocol contract.","requirements":["Define supported protocol versions.","Define capability negotiation rules.","Define fallback or rejection behavior.","Define security acceptance criteria."],"failure_mode":"Older and newer implementations connect but silently select an unsafe or semantically incompatible mode.","root_cause":"Protocol negotiation was tested only for connectivity.","solution":"Assert negotiated protocol and capability state as part of compatibility tests.","verification_method":"Inspect the negotiated protocol state and compare it with the compatibility and security policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PROTOCOL_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established protocol interoperability testing practice","D18-B75 ALPN and negotiation concepts"],"relationships":["D18-B75-R001","D18-B76-R004","D19-B86-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B27-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Compatibility Testing","subtopic":"Compatibility Evidence","concept":"Verified versus assumed compatibility","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should a knowledge fabric represent software compatibility claims?","answer":"Compatibility should be represented separately as declared, observed, verified, unsupported, or unknown rather than treating every successful local execution as proof of broad compatibility.","truth_model":["DECLARED_SUPPORT is a policy claim.","OBSERVED_SUCCESS is evidence from a specific test context.","VERIFIED_SUPPORT requires defined validation evidence.","UNSUPPORTED means the combination is outside the declared support boundary.","UNKNOWN means insufficient evidence exists."],"failure_mode":"A single successful test is generalized into a universal compatibility claim.","root_cause":"The evidence scope and support scope are conflated.","solution":"Record compatibility claims with explicit version, environment, evidence, and validation scope.","verification_method":"Audit compatibility records for version, environment, test evidence, and declared support boundaries.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset truth-model requirements","Established compatibility testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B10-R005","D20-B27-R001","D20-B27-R003"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B28-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Advanced Test Design","subtopic":"Metamorphic Testing","concept":"Metamorphic relation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a metamorphic relation in software testing?","answer":"A metamorphic relation specifies how outputs should relate when a valid transformation is applied to an input. It is useful when determining an exact expected output for every generated input is difficult but relationships between executions can be specified.","principle":"The transformation and expected relation must be justified independently by the system's semantics.","example":"For a function whose result should be invariant under permutation of an unordered input collection, applying a permutation should preserve the result.","failure_mode":"An incorrect metamorphic relation causes valid implementations to fail or invalid implementations to pass.","root_cause":"The relation was assumed rather than derived from the specification.","solution":"Derive metamorphic relations from documented invariants and domain semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test multiple valid transformations and include counterexamples near relevant boundaries.","verification_method":"Independently review each relation against the authoritative specification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_METAMORPHIC_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established metamorphic testing literature and practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D20-B12-R005","D20-B28-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B28-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Advanced Test Design","subtopic":"Differential Testing","concept":"Differential implementation comparison","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is differential testing useful?","answer":"Differential testing compares behavior from two or more implementations, versions, configurations, or independent mechanisms when equivalent behavior is expected. It can expose discrepancies without requiring a complete standalone oracle.","constraints":["The compared implementations must have sufficiently defined common semantics.","Differences must be analyzed rather than automatically assigning correctness to one implementation.","Known intentional differences must be modeled explicitly."],"failure_mode":"A discrepancy is treated as proof that one implementation is wrong even though the implementations have different valid semantics.","root_cause":"The comparison relationship was underspecified.","solution":"Define the shared semantic domain and classify expected versus unexpected differences.","testing_guidance":"Use independent implementations or versions where practical and preserve inputs producing discrepancies.","verification_method":"Investigate each differential result against the authoritative specification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_DIFFERENTIAL_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established differential testing practice","Established compiler and parser testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B28-R001","D20-B29-R003","D20-B30-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B28-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Advanced Test Design","subtopic":"Golden Tests","concept":"Golden or reference artifact testing","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What are the risks of golden-file testing?","answer":"Golden tests compare generated output with a stored reference artifact. They can efficiently detect unintended output changes, but the reference can become stale, overly broad, or difficult to review, causing legitimate changes to be rejected or incorrect behavior to be preserved.","failure_mode":"A golden artifact is updated automatically without independent review and thereby records the new behavior as correct.","root_cause":"The reference artifact is treated as an oracle without validation.","solution":"Require deliberate review of golden updates and keep artifacts focused on contract-relevant output.","testing_guidance":"Review changes semantically, minimize irrelevant formatting noise, and preserve regression cases that represent meaningful behavior.","verification_method":"Trace golden artifacts to requirements or intentionally accepted reference behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_REGRESSION_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established snapshot and golden-file testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R003","D20-B10-R003","D20-B28-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B28-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Advanced Test Design","subtopic":"Snapshot Testing","concept":"Snapshot scope control","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can broad snapshot tests create low-value test noise?","answer":"A broad snapshot may capture implementation details, unstable metadata, timestamps, identifiers, formatting, or unrelated fields that are not part of the behavior being tested. Small unrelated changes then produce large diffs that require review without providing meaningful defect evidence.","invalid_example":"Snapshotting an entire response object containing timestamps and generated identifiers when the requirement concerns only a small stable subset.","solution":"Snapshot only contract-relevant representations or normalize intentionally variable fields before comparison.","testing_guidance":"Identify which snapshot fields are semantically meaningful and which are intentionally variable.","verification_method":"Review representative snapshot diffs and classify each captured field as contract-relevant, intentionally variable, or accidental noise.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SNAPSHOT_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established snapshot-testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B12-R001","D20-B28-R003","D20-B05-R001"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B28-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Advanced Test Design","subtopic":"Oracle Construction","concept":"Independent oracle evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should important test oracles be independently justified?","answer":"A test cannot provide strong evidence when the expected result is generated by the same flawed assumption or implementation logic as the system under test. Important oracles should be derived from independent specifications, invariants, reference implementations, domain rules, or other defensible evidence.","principle":"Oracle independence reduces the risk that the test and implementation share the same defect.","failure_mode":"A defective implementation passes because the expected result was produced using the same faulty algorithm.","root_cause":"The oracle is not independent from the implementation under test.","solution":"Use independent calculations, specifications, reference behavior, invariants, or metamorphic/differential relationships where appropriate.","verification_method":"Trace the oracle's origin and determine whether it depends materially on the implementation being tested.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_ORACLE_PRINCIPLE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established software testing and oracle-construction practice"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R002","D20-B12-R005","D20-B28-R001","D20-B28-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B29-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Parser and Serialization Testing","subtopic":"Parser Robustness","concept":"Malformed-input parser testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should parser tests verify for malformed input?","answer":"Parser tests should verify that malformed input is rejected or handled according to the format contract, that errors are classified appropriately, and that partial or ambiguous input does not become an unintended valid semantic object.","requirements":["Define valid grammar or schema.","Define representative malformed forms.","Define resource limits where relevant.","Define expected failure behavior."],"failure_mode":"Malformed input is partially accepted with unintended semantics.","root_cause":"Parser tests cover only successful examples or fail to inspect semantic output.","solution":"Add malformed, truncated, ambiguous, and boundary inputs with explicit failure or normalization expectations.","testing_guidance":"Include syntax errors, missing required fields, duplicate fields where relevant, invalid encodings, oversized values, and truncation.","verification_method":"Compare parser behavior with the authoritative format specification and semantic invariants.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PARSER_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established parser and serialization testing practice","D19-B29 parser-security concepts"],"relationships":["D19-B29-R001","D19-B29-R002","D19-B29-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B29-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Parser and Serialization Testing","subtopic":"Round-Trip Testing","concept":"Serialization round-trip invariant","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What does a serialization round-trip test verify?","answer":"A round-trip test verifies that serializing and then deserializing a supported value preserves the semantics required by the format contract. Exact byte equality is not necessarily required unless the format specifies canonical serialization.","requirements":["Define semantic equivalence.","Define canonicalization requirements if any.","Identify fields intentionally omitted or normalized during serialization."],"failure_mode":"Round-tripping changes meaningful data or produces a semantically different object.","root_cause":"Serialization or deserialization loses, changes, or ambiguously reconstructs required information.","solution":"Assert semantic equivalence according to the format contract.","testing_guidance":"Test ordinary values, optional fields, boundary values, nested structures, and values requiring escaping or normalization.","verification_method":"Compare the original semantic object with the deserialized result using a contract-aware equivalence relation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SERIALIZATION_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established serialization and parser testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B28-R001","D20-B29-R001","D19-B09-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B29-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Parser and Serialization Testing","subtopic":"Differential Parser Testing","concept":"Parser implementation differential","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can differential parser testing identify security-relevant inconsistencies?","answer":"If two components interpret the same serialized input differently, an attacker or malformed producer may exploit the discrepancy between validation, routing, authorization, and execution layers. Differential testing can expose inputs whose interpretations diverge.","requirements":["Use the same input corpus across compared parsers or parser stages.","Define the semantic properties that should agree.","Investigate discrepancies rather than automatically treating one parser as authoritative."],"failure_mode":"One layer accepts or interprets data differently from another security-sensitive layer.","root_cause":"Parser implementations or configuration have inconsistent semantics.","solution":"Align parsing rules or establish an explicit canonical interpretation boundary.","testing_guidance":"Include duplicate fields, encoding variations, malformed structures, ambiguous values, and boundary inputs where applicable.","verification_method":"Compare normalized semantic outputs and classify discrepancies against the authoritative format specification.","security_implications":"Parser discrepancies can create validation-bypass, request-smuggling, or authorization-confusion conditions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19-B29-R001","D19-B29-R002","Established differential testing practice"],"relationships":["D19-B28-R001","D19-B29-R001","D20-B28-R002"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B29-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Parser and Serialization Testing","subtopic":"Resource Limits","concept":"Parser resource-boundary testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should tests verify parser resource limits?","answer":"Parser tests should exercise input sizes and structural complexity near documented resource limits and verify bounded behavior when limits are exceeded. The test should distinguish legitimate large inputs from pathological complexity.","requirements":["Define size and complexity limits.","Define expected rejection or degradation behavior.","Define timeout and memory-related safety expectations where applicable."],"failure_mode":"A parser consumes disproportionate resources when processing nested, repetitive, or oversized input.","root_cause":"Resource limits were unspecified or untested.","solution":"Encode parser resource budgets as testable constraints and test representative boundary conditions.","testing_guidance":"Test just below, at, and above important limits and include structurally complex cases where relevant.","verification_method":"Verify rejection or bounded behavior against documented resource constraints; do not invent capacity numbers.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PARSER_SECURITY_TESTING","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19-B09-R004","D19-B88-R002","Established parser security testing practice"],"relationships":["D19-B09-R004","D19-B88-R002","D20-B07-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B29-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Parser and Serialization Testing","subtopic":"Canonicalization","concept":"Canonical representation testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should canonicalization tests verify?","answer":"Canonicalization tests should verify that semantically equivalent inputs are normalized according to the defined canonical representation and that security-sensitive decisions use the intended canonical form. They should also verify that distinct values are not incorrectly collapsed.","requirements":["Define the canonical representation.","Define equivalence rules.","Define values that must remain distinct.","Define ordering or normalization semantics where relevant."],"failure_mode":"Two representations that should be equivalent are treated differently, or distinct security-sensitive values are incorrectly treated as identical.","root_cause":"Canonicalization rules are inconsistent or applied at different stages.","solution":"Centralize or explicitly define canonicalization and test representative equivalent and non-equivalent inputs.","verification_method":"Compare canonical outputs against the authoritative normalization rules and security invariants.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PARSER_AND_SECURITY_TESTING_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D19-B06-R003","D19-B29-R003","Established canonicalization testing practice"],"relationships":["D19-B06-R003","D19-B29-R003","D20-B29-R001"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B30-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Test Architecture","subtopic":"Test Pyramid","concept":"Balanced test portfolio","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE","question":"How should a test portfolio balance fast and high-fidelity tests?","answer":"A balanced test portfolio uses different test levels according to the evidence each provides: fast isolated tests for local behavior, integration tests for component contracts, and system or end-to-end tests for critical cross-boundary workflows. The portfolio should be risk-driven rather than mechanically optimized for a fixed ratio.","principle":"Test distribution should follow required evidence, failure cost, execution cost, and diagnostic value.","failure_mode":"The suite relies heavily on slow high-level tests or contains many fast tests that omit critical integration behavior.","root_cause":"Test architecture was optimized for a simplistic test-count ratio instead of evidence needs.","solution":"Design the portfolio around coverage, reliability, execution cost, and diagnostic usefulness.","verification_method":"Audit test levels against requirements, architecture boundaries, risks, and observed failure modes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_ARCHITECTURE_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Established test-pyramid and test-portfolio engineering practice"],"relationships":["D20-B01-R003","D20-B22-R001","D20-B10-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B30-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Test Architecture","subtopic":"Test Ownership","concept":"Requirement and test ownership","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","question":"Why should tests have explicit ownership and purpose?","answer":"Ownership and purpose make failures actionable, support lifecycle maintenance, and prevent tests from becoming abandoned assertions whose relevance is unknown. Each important test should have a reason for existence tied to a requirement, invariant, risk, defect, or contract.","requirements":["Identify the protected behavior or risk.","Define ownership or maintenance responsibility.","Define relevant environment and execution requirements.","Define retirement or review conditions."],"failure_mode":"A failing test cannot be interpreted because no one knows what behavior it protects.","root_cause":"Test provenance and ownership were not maintained.","solution":"Attach purpose, ownership, and provenance metadata to important tests or test suites.","verification_method":"Audit representative tests for traceability to requirements, risks, defects, or contracts.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain dataset governance requirements","Established test-suite governance practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R004","D20-B10-R003","D20-B30-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B30-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Test Architecture","subtopic":"Traceability","concept":"Requirement-to-test-to-result chain","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a test system preserve traceability from requirement to evidence?","answer":"Create a traceability chain connecting requirement or invariant, test design, concrete test case, execution context, observed result, failure evidence when applicable, and verification interpretation. This prevents a test result from being detached from what it actually proves.","procedure":["Assign stable identifiers to requirements or invariants.","Link test cases to the protected requirement.","Record execution context and version.","Record observed result and evidence.","Classify failures separately from product correctness.","Record verification interpretation and limitations."],"verification_method":"Audit representative chains from requirements to executed evidence and verify that no unsupported inference is inserted between result and conclusion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain evidence-model requirements","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 traceability principles"],"relationships":["D20-B10-R003","D20-B21-R001","D20-B30-R004"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B30-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Test Architecture","subtopic":"Failure Triage","concept":"Evidence-preserving failure triage","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is a robust sequence for triaging an automated test failure?","answer":"Preserve the failure artifact first, determine reproducibility, classify environment versus product failure, identify the first failed observable stage, isolate the smallest reproducing case, compare against requirements and recent changes, then create or update a regression case when a durable defect is confirmed.","procedure":["Preserve logs, inputs, versions, configuration, and relevant artifacts.","Determine whether the failure reproduces.","Check test-environment health.","Identify the first known failed stage.","Minimize the reproducing scenario.","Compare behavior with the requirement or contract.","Identify root cause using evidence.","Add regression coverage for confirmed durable defects."],"constraints":["Do not overwrite the original evidence before analysis.","Do not classify a failure as a product defect solely from the final assertion.","Do not claim root cause without sufficient evidence."],"verification_method":"Review the triage record for evidence supporting each classification and conclusion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_TEST_DIAGNOSTICS_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["D20-B05-R005","D20-B22-R004","Established failure-triage practice"],"relationships":["D20-B05-R002","D20-B05-R003","D20-B20-R005","D20-B30-R003"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B30-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Test Architecture","subtopic":"Foundation Gate","concept":"D20 B1-B30 coverage gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be checked before considering the first 30 D20 batches complete?","answer":"The first 30 batches must be reviewed for conceptual coverage, test-design depth, implementation relevance, failure-oriented testing, API/system/UI/database/network/distributed testing, compatibility, parser testing, test-oracle quality, provenance, schema consistency, deduplication, and truthfulness of execution and validation status.","requirements":["Review all D20-B01 through D20-B30 records.","Detect exact, near-duplicate, semantic, and implementation-level duplication.","Audit canonical knowledge-type usage.","Audit evidence and provenance quality.","Verify execution and validation claims are truthful.","Check relationships and stable record IDs.","Identify implementation and language coverage gaps for future batches.","Do not interpret this gate as a D20 lock."],"verification_method":"Run a dataset-level audit over records B1-B30 and produce a residual-gap report before extending or locking the dataset.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Autonomous Software Engineering Brain D01-D58 dataset governance specification"],"relationships":["D20-B10-R005","D20-B30-R003","D20-B30-R004"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B31-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Security Test Strategy","concept":"Security invariants as test oracles","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should security invariants be used as test oracles?","answer":"Security tests should verify explicit security invariants such as unauthorized operations remaining denied, protected data remaining inaccessible, and security-sensitive state transitions obeying authorization rules.","principle":"A security test is stronger when its expected result is derived from a security invariant rather than merely from an observed implementation response.","objective":"Verify security properties independently of incidental implementation details.","requirements":["Define the security invariant","Identify the protected boundary","Construct authorized and unauthorized cases","Verify the invariant after the operation"],"constraints":["A passing status code alone does not prove the security invariant","The test oracle must correspond to the actual security requirement"],"expected_behavior":"Authorized behavior satisfies the permitted security transition while unauthorized behavior does not cross the protected boundary.","failure_mode":"A test accepts an expected HTTP status while protected state or data is nevertheless exposed.","root_cause":"The test oracle validates a surface response instead of the underlying security property.","testing_guidance":"Assert both the externally observable response and the protected-state/data invariant when applicable.","verification_method":"Trace the test assertion to the documented security requirement or invariant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B08-R001","D20-B21-R001","D19-B14-R005"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-53","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"security_implications":"Security tests should fail when protected behavior is violated even if the transport-level response appears normal."}
{"record_id":"D20-B31-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Authorization","concept":"Authorization matrix coverage","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should an authorization test matrix cover?","answer":"An authorization matrix should cover identities or roles, resources, operations, relevant resource ownership or tenancy, permitted actions, denied actions, and boundary conditions.","procedure":["Enumerate security principals or representative roles","Enumerate protected resources","Enumerate operations","Define permitted combinations","Define denied combinations","Test cross-tenant and ownership boundaries where applicable","Verify both response and protected-state behavior"],"constraints":["The matrix must reflect the actual authorization model","Do not assume role names imply permissions without the governing policy"],"expected_behavior":"Every tested authorization combination produces behavior consistent with the declared policy.","failure_mode":"A role can perform an operation on a resource outside its authorized scope.","root_cause":"Missing or incorrectly evaluated authorization condition.","testing_guidance":"Include positive, negative, ownership-boundary, tenant-boundary, and privilege-transition cases where applicable.","verification_method":"Compare observed authorization behavior against the authoritative authorization policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B08-R002","D19-B10-R001","D19-B86-R003"],"provenance":["OWASP Authorization guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"security_implications":"Incomplete authorization matrices can leave privilege-boundary defects undetected."}
{"record_id":"D20-B31-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Fuzz Testing","concept":"Security-relevant fuzzing objective","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What makes fuzz testing security-relevant?","answer":"Security-relevant fuzzing systematically supplies malformed, unexpected, boundary, or adversarial inputs to a target while checking for violations such as crashes, memory-safety failures, parser inconsistencies, excessive resource consumption, or security invariant violations.","requirements":["Define target and input boundary","Define input validity and mutation strategy","Define observable failure conditions","Preserve reproducing inputs","Classify failures independently"],"constraints":["Fuzzing does not prove absence of vulnerabilities","Input generation alone is not a security oracle"],"expected_behavior":"Invalid or adversarial inputs are handled according to the target's contract without violating security invariants.","failure_mode":"The target crashes, enters unsafe state, leaks information, or consumes unbounded resources.","testing_guidance":"Retain minimized reproducing inputs and associate each confirmed defect with a regression test.","verification_method":"Reproduce the failure with the preserved input and verify the corrected implementation against the same regression case.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R002","D20-B29-R001","D19-B12-R003"],"provenance":["OWASP Testing Guide","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"security_implications":"Fuzzing can expose parser, memory, validation, and resource-handling weaknesses but requires independent failure classification."}
{"record_id":"D20-B31-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Static Analysis","concept":"Static analysis result verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should static-analysis findings be incorporated into testing?","answer":"Static-analysis findings should be triaged against actual code paths, data flow, configuration, and security requirements before being classified as defects, false positives, or unresolved findings.","principle":"A tool finding is evidence requiring analysis, not automatic proof of exploitable behavior.","failure_mode":"A scanner result is treated as confirmed vulnerability without validating applicability.","root_cause":"Confusion between automated detection output and verified defect status.","testing_guidance":"Record tool, version, rule, location, finding, applicability analysis, disposition, and supporting evidence.","verification_method":"Independently inspect the relevant path and confirm whether the reported condition violates a requirement or security invariant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B30-R003","D19-B51-R006","D19-B89-R004"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","OWASP guidance"],"security_implications":"Untriaged scanner findings can hide real defects among false positives or cause incorrect security conclusions."}
{"record_id":"D20-B31-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Dynamic Analysis","concept":"Dynamic security test evidence","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What evidence should dynamic security tests preserve?","answer":"Dynamic security tests should preserve the test configuration, target version, relevant identity and authorization context, input, request or operation sequence, observed response, resulting state, and failure evidence needed for reproduction.","requirements":["Identify exact target version","Capture relevant security context","Preserve inputs and sequence","Capture observable effects","Preserve reproducibility information"],"constraints":["Sensitive credentials and secrets must not be stored in test artifacts","Environment-dependent findings must retain sufficient environment context"],"expected_behavior":"A confirmed failure can be reproduced or independently verified from preserved evidence without exposing secrets.","failure_mode":"A security finding cannot be reproduced because essential context was discarded.","root_cause":"Insufficient security-test evidence capture.","testing_guidance":"Sanitize secrets while preserving security-relevant context.","verification_method":"Attempt reproduction using the preserved sanitized evidence and compare the observed behavior with the original finding.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R005","D20-B30-R004","D19-B51-R009"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-61","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"],"security_implications":"Poor evidence preservation weakens incident analysis and security regression testing."}
{"record_id":"D20-B32-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Secrets","concept":"Secret leakage regression testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How can automated tests detect accidental secret exposure?","answer":"Tests can inspect controlled logs, error payloads, serialized responses, generated artifacts, and telemetry for prohibited secret patterns or known test credentials while avoiding the use of real secrets.","requirements":["Use synthetic or dedicated test credentials","Define prohibited exposure surfaces","Scan relevant artifacts","Verify sanitization"],"constraints":["Pattern matching cannot detect every form of secret exposure","Real production credentials should not be introduced solely for testing"],"expected_behavior":"Controlled secret-like test values do not appear in prohibited externally visible or persistent artifacts.","failure_mode":"A credential, token, or secret-bearing value appears in logs, responses, exceptions, or artifacts.","root_cause":"Missing or incomplete redaction/sanitization at an output boundary.","testing_guidance":"Use unique synthetic markers so leakage can be detected reliably.","verification_method":"Search all intended output surfaces for the unique marker and verify that only explicitly permitted locations contain it.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B08-R004","D19-B51-R009","D19-B60-R003"],"provenance":["OWASP Logging guidance","NIST SP 800-53"],"security_implications":"Secret leakage can convert otherwise isolated test environments into credential-exposure paths."}
{"record_id":"D20-B32-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Dependency Security","concept":"Dependency vulnerability regression","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should dependency security findings become regression evidence?","answer":"A confirmed dependency-related defect should produce a reproducible regression condition where practical, such as a vulnerable version being rejected, a vulnerable behavior being blocked, or a required patched version being enforced.","principle":"Dependency scanning and behavioral regression testing answer different questions and should not be conflated.","failure_mode":"A dependency is upgraded but the application remains vulnerable because application-level exposure was not tested.","root_cause":"Dependency-version remediation was treated as equivalent to vulnerability remediation.","testing_guidance":"Connect dependency policy checks with application behavior tests for security-critical dependencies.","verification_method":"Verify both dependency policy compliance and the application behavior relevant to the vulnerability.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B59-R002","D20-B06-R003","D20-B31-R004"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","OWASP Dependency guidance"],"security_implications":"Version remediation without exposure verification may leave application-specific vulnerable paths untested."}
{"record_id":"D20-B32-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Input Validation","concept":"Validation bypass testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should input-validation bypass cases be designed?","answer":"Validation tests should exercise alternate representations and parser boundaries that could cause validation and downstream processing to interpret the same input differently.","requirements":["Identify validation boundary","Identify downstream parser or interpreter","Define canonical semantic representation","Test equivalent and ambiguous representations"],"constraints":["Test cases must remain within authorized test environments","Do not assume a representation is equivalent without evidence"],"expected_behavior":"Inputs rejected by the security policy cannot reach a downstream state where they become accepted through alternate interpretation.","failure_mode":"Validation accepts or normalizes an input differently from the downstream security-sensitive component.","root_cause":"Parser or canonicalization mismatch across layers.","testing_guidance":"Focus on semantic equivalence, encoding boundaries, normalization, and duplicate parser behavior.","verification_method":"Compare the validation interpretation with the downstream interpretation and verify that the security policy remains invariant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B29-R003","D19-B09-R002","D19-B31-R004"],"provenance":["OWASP Input Validation guidance"],"security_implications":"Validation differentials can create security-policy bypasses."}
{"record_id":"D20-B32-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Authentication","concept":"Authentication lifecycle testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should authentication lifecycle tests verify?","answer":"Authentication tests should cover successful authentication, invalid credentials, expiration, logout or revocation where applicable, session or token renewal, repeated failure behavior, and security-sensitive state transitions defined by the system.","requirements":["Define authentication state model","Define credential/token lifecycle","Define failure semantics","Define revocation expectations"],"constraints":["The exact lifecycle depends on the authentication protocol and application contract"],"expected_behavior":"Authentication state changes occur only through permitted transitions and expired or revoked credentials are not accepted where the contract requires rejection.","failure_mode":"Expired, revoked, or otherwise invalid authentication material remains usable.","root_cause":"Incomplete lifecycle enforcement or inconsistent validation across endpoints.","testing_guidance":"Test the same credential across lifecycle transitions and protected operations.","verification_method":"Compare observed state transitions with the authoritative authentication lifecycle specification.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B07-R003","D19-B79-R001","D20-B21-R005"],"provenance":["OWASP Authentication guidance","NIST SP 800-63"],"security_implications":"Lifecycle defects can invalidate otherwise correct credential validation."}
{"record_id":"D20-B32-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Security Testing","subtopic":"Session Security","concept":"Session isolation testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What does session isolation testing verify?","answer":"Session isolation testing verifies that authenticated state, authorization context, and security-sensitive data belonging to one session or principal cannot be incorrectly reused by another.","requirements":["Create distinct test principals or sessions","Perform equivalent operations","Compare accessible state","Test session transitions"],"constraints":["The test must distinguish intentional shared resources from protected session state"],"expected_behavior":"Each session observes only state permitted by its own security context and the declared sharing model.","failure_mode":"One session can access another session's protected state or security context.","root_cause":"Incorrect session association, caching, context propagation, or authorization evaluation.","testing_guidance":"Use unique per-session markers to make cross-session contamination observable.","verification_method":"Verify that markers and protected state remain isolated according to the authorization model.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B31-R002","D20-B24-R003","D19-B54-R004"],"provenance":["OWASP Session Management guidance"],"security_implications":"Session isolation failures can expose data or privileges across security principals."}
{"record_id":"D20-B33-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Load Testing","concept":"Load test purpose","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What does load testing establish?","answer":"Load testing evaluates system behavior under a defined workload and operating condition, including throughput, latency, resource consumption, error behavior, and stability relative to explicit requirements or expectations.","principle":"A load-test result is meaningful only in relation to the workload, environment, system version, measurement method, and acceptance criteria.","requirements":["Define workload model","Define environment","Define measured metrics","Define acceptance criteria","Record system version"],"constraints":["Results from one environment do not automatically generalize to another","No performance result should be fabricated or extrapolated without evidence"],"expected_behavior":"The system behavior under the specified workload is measured and compared with explicitly defined criteria.","testing_guidance":"Record workload intensity, concurrency, duration, input distribution, dependencies, and resource conditions.","verification_method":"Reproduce or independently validate the measurement under the same declared conditions when required.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R004","D21"],"provenance":["ISO/IEC 25010","established performance-testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN","performance_implications":"Performance measurements must remain scoped to their workload and environment."}
{"record_id":"D20-B33-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Stress Testing","concept":"Stress test objective","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What distinguishes stress testing from ordinary load testing?","answer":"Stress testing deliberately evaluates behavior as workload or resource pressure approaches or exceeds normal operating conditions, with emphasis on degradation, failure behavior, recovery, and safety boundaries.","principle":"The purpose is not merely to obtain a maximum throughput number but to characterize behavior under excessive pressure.","expected_behavior":"The system exhibits bounded, observable degradation or failure and recovers according to its defined resilience behavior.","failure_mode":"The system enters uncontrolled failure, loses required invariants, or cannot recover as specified.","testing_guidance":"Define the stress variable, termination condition, safety boundary, and recovery observation before execution.","verification_method":"Compare degradation and recovery behavior with explicit resilience requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R004","D20-B26-R001","D21"],"provenance":["ISO/IEC 25010","established performance-testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B33-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Spike Testing","concept":"Sudden workload change","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should spike testing verify?","answer":"Spike testing verifies system behavior when workload changes abruptly, including queue growth, latency changes, admission control, resource saturation, error behavior, and recovery after the spike.","requirements":["Define baseline workload","Define workload transition","Define safety limits","Measure during spike","Measure recovery"],"expected_behavior":"The system handles the defined abrupt workload transition according to its capacity and degradation contract without violating critical invariants.","failure_mode":"A sudden workload increase causes uncontrolled queue growth, resource exhaustion, or persistent degraded state.","testing_guidance":"Measure both transient behavior and post-spike recovery.","verification_method":"Verify that observed degradation and recovery satisfy the declared service and resilience requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B33-R001","D20-B48-R001","D21"],"provenance":["established performance-testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B33-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Soak Testing","concept":"Long-duration stability testing","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What is the purpose of soak testing?","answer":"Soak testing evaluates behavior over an extended workload period to reveal gradual resource growth, degradation, leaks, accumulation, or stability problems that short tests may not expose.","requirements":["Define sustained workload","Define observation interval","Track relevant resources","Define termination and failure criteria"],"constraints":["Duration alone does not prove production stability","Resource trends must be interpreted with environmental context"],"expected_behavior":"Resource and performance behavior remains within defined acceptable bounds for the declared test duration or any observed degradation is correctly characterized.","failure_mode":"Resource usage or latency progressively deteriorates under sustained operation.","root_cause":"Potential resource leak, accumulation, exhaustion, degradation, or environmental effect requiring further investigation.","testing_guidance":"Compare resource and latency trends rather than relying only on final measurements.","verification_method":"Repeat or isolate the suspected cause and determine whether the observed trend is reproducible.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B33-R001","D20-B05-R005","D21"],"provenance":["established performance-testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B33-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Capacity Testing","concept":"Capacity boundary identification","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a capacity boundary be established?","answer":"Capacity testing identifies the workload range in which defined service and resource requirements continue to be satisfied, using measured evidence rather than an assumed maximum.","procedure":["Define service requirements","Define workload dimensions","Increase workload according to a controlled plan","Measure relevant service and resource metrics","Identify the first requirement violation","Repeat to confirm the boundary when necessary"],"constraints":["Capacity depends on workload shape and environment","A single scalar capacity number can be misleading"],"expected_behavior":"The resulting capacity statement is explicitly scoped to workload, configuration, environment, and acceptance criteria.","testing_guidance":"Report capacity as a measured region or workload condition when a single number would conceal important dimensions.","verification_method":"Repeat measurements or independently reproduce the identified boundary under equivalent conditions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B33-R001","D20-B27-R001","D21"],"provenance":["established performance-testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B34-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Measurement","concept":"Performance measurement validity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a performance measurement interpretable?","answer":"An interpretable performance measurement identifies the software version, environment, workload, measurement boundary, metric definition, observation period, and relevant system conditions.","principle":"A performance number without measurement context is incomplete evidence.","failure_mode":"A latency or throughput value is compared across tests with materially different workload or environment conditions.","root_cause":"Missing measurement metadata or uncontrolled experimental variables.","testing_guidance":"Treat measurement metadata as part of the test result rather than optional commentary.","verification_method":"Check that all variables necessary to interpret the metric are recorded and consistent with the comparison being made.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B33-R001","D20-B05-R005","D21"],"provenance":["established performance-testing methodology"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B34-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Latency","concept":"Latency distribution testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should latency testing examine distributions rather than only averages?","answer":"Latency distributions expose tail behavior and variability that an average can conceal, making them useful for evaluating user-visible or service-level latency requirements.","requirements":["Define request population","Capture individual observations or sufficient distribution data","Define relevant percentile or tail criteria","Associate measurements with workload and environment"],"constraints":["Percentiles do not by themselves explain causality","Reported percentiles require a sufficiently representative sample"],"expected_behavior":"Latency acceptance is evaluated against the declared distribution-based requirement.","testing_guidance":"Use the metric required by the service contract rather than choosing a percentile solely because it is conventional.","verification_method":"Confirm the calculation method, sample population, workload, and acceptance criterion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B33-R001","D20-B34-R001","D21"],"provenance":["established performance-testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B34-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Throughput","concept":"Throughput correctness","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What must be verified before interpreting throughput measurements?","answer":"The test must establish what constitutes a completed operation and ensure that measured throughput represents successful, semantically valid work rather than merely generated requests or transport activity.","failure_mode":"A load generator reports high request rate while many operations fail or produce invalid results.","root_cause":"Throughput metric measures attempted work instead of successfully completed required work.","testing_guidance":"Pair throughput with success/error counts and semantic correctness checks.","verification_method":"Verify that counted operations satisfy the test's completion and correctness criteria.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B21-R001","D21"],"provenance":["established performance-testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B34-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Resource Saturation","concept":"Resource saturation diagnosis","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a performance test distinguish CPU, memory, I/O, network, and dependency saturation?","answer":"Correlate service metrics with resource-specific measurements and workload stages, then test whether changing or isolating the suspected resource changes the observed bottleneck.","procedure":["Identify performance symptom","Collect service and resource metrics","Correlate timing with resource utilization","Isolate candidate bottleneck where safe","Repeat measurement","Record evidence and uncertainty"],"constraints":["High utilization is evidence of pressure but not automatically proof of causality","Resource metrics require time alignment with service measurements"],"expected_behavior":"The resulting diagnosis identifies the evidence supporting the suspected bottleneck and records unresolved alternatives when causality is not established.","failure_mode":"A resource with high utilization is declared the root cause without causal evidence.","root_cause":"Correlation incorrectly treated as causation.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled variation or isolation when practical.","verification_method":"Confirm that the proposed bottleneck explains the observed behavior under controlled evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B22-R004","D18-B77-R001","D21"],"provenance":["established performance engineering practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B34-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Performance Testing","subtopic":"Performance Regression","concept":"Performance regression test preservation","knowledge_type":"REGRESSION_TEST","question":"When should a performance finding become a regression test?","answer":"A measured performance defect should become a regression test when the degraded behavior is reproducible, materially important, and has an explicit measurable acceptance condition that remains meaningful for the maintained system.","requirements":["Reproducible degradation","Defined metric","Defined workload","Defined acceptance criterion","Stable enough measurement method"],"constraints":["No fabricated threshold should be introduced","Highly noisy measurements may require statistical treatment rather than a simple pass/fail threshold"],"expected_behavior":"Future changes are checked against the known performance requirement under a declared test condition.","testing_guidance":"Preserve workload and environment metadata with the regression test.","verification_method":"Verify that the test can distinguish the known regression from normal measurement variation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R003","D20-B06-R003","D21"],"provenance":["established performance-testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B35-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Resilience","concept":"Failure injection objective","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should controlled failure injection verify?","answer":"Controlled failure injection verifies system behavior when a specified dependency, resource, communication path, or component becomes unavailable or degraded.","procedure":["Define failure hypothesis","Identify smallest safe failure boundary","Inject the failure deterministically","Observe system behavior","Verify invariants and recovery","Preserve evidence"],"constraints":["Injection must remain within authorized and safe test boundaries","The injected failure must be distinguishable from unrelated environmental failures"],"expected_behavior":"The system follows its defined degradation, isolation, fallback, and recovery behavior without violating critical invariants.","failure_mode":"A failure injection causes uncontrolled collateral effects or reveals an undefined recovery state.","testing_guidance":"Start with the smallest failure domain that can test the hypothesis.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior and recovered state against explicit resilience requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R002","D20-B26-R005","D18-B50-R003"],"provenance":["established resilience-testing practice","NIST SP 800-53"],"security_implications":"Failure injection requires controlled authorization and isolation to avoid unintended impact."}
{"record_id":"D20-B35-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Recovery","concept":"Recovery invariant testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should recovery tests verify after a component failure?","answer":"Recovery tests should verify that required data, state, authorization, ordering, and service invariants remain correct after the component resumes or the dependency recovers.","principle":"Availability restoration alone does not establish correct recovery.","expected_behavior":"The system returns to an acceptable operational state without violating required invariants or silently losing required work.","failure_mode":"The service becomes available again but state, data, ordering, or authorization is incorrect.","root_cause":"Recovery procedure restored availability without restoring semantic correctness.","testing_guidance":"Define pre-failure and post-recovery invariants explicitly.","verification_method":"Compare preserved pre-failure evidence and expected invariants with post-recovery state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R005","D20-B26-R003","D18-B50-R003"],"provenance":["established resilience engineering practice"],"security_implications":"Incorrect recovery can reintroduce authorization or data-integrity failures."}
{"record_id":"D20-B35-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Degradation","concept":"Graceful degradation testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should graceful degradation be tested?","answer":"Tests should disable or constrain a non-critical capability and verify that dependent functionality follows its documented degraded behavior while preserving critical invariants.","requirements":["Identify degraded capability","Define expected fallback","Define unavailable functions","Define preserved critical invariants"],"expected_behavior":"Non-critical functionality degrades within its contract while critical functions remain safe and correct.","failure_mode":"A non-critical dependency failure causes unrelated critical functions to fail or enter unsafe state.","root_cause":"Insufficient failure isolation or undefined fallback behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test both the degraded path and restoration path.","verification_method":"Verify that the degraded state and subsequent recovery satisfy the documented service contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B26-R001","D20-B35-R001","D18-B47-R005"],"provenance":["established resilience engineering practice"],"security_implications":"Fallback paths must preserve security and authorization invariants."}
{"record_id":"D20-B35-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Retry Behavior","concept":"Retry amplification testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should tests detect harmful retry amplification?","answer":"Tests should create controlled dependency failure and observe whether multiple layers independently retry the same operation, increasing load or delaying recovery beyond defined limits.","requirements":["Identify retrying layers","Define dependency failure","Record retry counts and timing","Observe resulting load","Verify retry budget behavior"],"failure_mode":"Nested retries amplify requests during dependency failure.","root_cause":"Independent retry policies lack coordination or bounded budgets.","testing_guidance":"Test both single-layer and multi-layer retry configurations.","verification_method":"Compare observed retries with declared retry budgets and recovery requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B49-R001","D18-B46-R003","D20-B26-R001"],"provenance":["established distributed-systems reliability practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B35-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Reliability Testing","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Timeout recovery testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should timeout tests verify?","answer":"Timeout tests should verify classification of timeout conditions, cancellation or abandonment semantics, resource cleanup, retry behavior, and resulting state after the timed-out operation.","requirements":["Define timeout boundary","Inject or induce controlled delay","Observe cancellation behavior","Check resource cleanup","Check retry semantics","Verify final state"],"failure_mode":"A timeout returns to the caller while the underlying operation continues incorrectly or leaves resources/state inconsistent.","root_cause":"Timeout treated as only a response deadline rather than a lifecycle event.","testing_guidance":"Test both local timeout and downstream timeout cases.","verification_method":"Verify the post-timeout lifecycle and resource state rather than only the returned error.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D18-B49-R001","D20-B35-R002"],"provenance":["established distributed-systems reliability practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B36-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testability Engineering","subtopic":"Determinism","concept":"Deterministic test design","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What makes a test deterministic?","answer":"A test is deterministic when the same controlled inputs, environment, dependencies, scheduling assumptions, and relevant state produce a stable interpretation of the expected result.","principle":"Determinism requires control or explicit modeling of sources of variability rather than merely rerunning a failing test.","failure_mode":"A test passes or fails unpredictably across repeated executions without an identified state change.","root_cause":"Uncontrolled time, randomness, concurrency, external dependencies, environment state, or shared mutable state.","testing_guidance":"Control nondeterministic inputs and preserve the conditions required for reproduction.","verification_method":"Repeat under equivalent controlled conditions and determine whether outcome stability is established.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B03-R005","D20-B05-R001","D20-B36-R002"],"provenance":["established software testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B36-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testability Engineering","subtopic":"Time","concept":"Controllable time source","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should time-dependent code expose a controllable time source for testing?","answer":"A controllable time source allows tests to exercise expiration, scheduling, timeout, and date-dependent behavior without relying on wall-clock timing or waiting for real time to pass.","requirements":["Separate application time access from direct wall-clock access","Allow tests to provide controlled time","Preserve production semantics"],"constraints":["The abstraction must not change the intended production time semantics","Monotonic time and wall-clock time serve different purposes"],"expected_behavior":"Tests can advance or select relevant time values deterministically while production uses the appropriate real clock source.","failure_mode":"Tests depend on real elapsed time and become slow or flaky.","root_cause":"Time was treated as an uncontrollable global dependency.","testing_guidance":"Use separate abstractions for wall-clock timestamps and elapsed-duration measurement where required.","verification_method":"Exercise boundary transitions at controlled times and verify expected state changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D20-B36-R001","D20-B07-R003"],"provenance":["established software testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B36-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testability Engineering","subtopic":"Randomness","concept":"Controlled randomness","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should randomness be handled in deterministic tests?","answer":"Tests should control or record the randomness source when the behavior under test depends on random values, while preserving the distinction between deterministic reproducibility and production randomness requirements.","requirements":["Inject or control random source","Record seed or generated test input where appropriate","Test relevant boundary distributions"],"constraints":["A fixed seed does not prove correctness across the full input space","Cryptographic randomness must not be replaced with predictable randomness in production"],"expected_behavior":"A failing randomized test can reproduce its input sequence while production retains its required randomness properties.","failure_mode":"A random failure cannot be reproduced or a test accidentally weakens production randomness.","root_cause":"Randomness source was not separated from test control.","testing_guidance":"Preserve the exact generated case when a failure occurs.","verification_method":"Replay the recorded input or seed under the same implementation version and environment assumptions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D19-B18-R002","D20-B36-R001"],"provenance":["established testing practice","NIST cryptographic guidance"],"security_implications":"Predictable randomness must never be introduced into security-sensitive production paths merely to simplify testing."}
{"record_id":"D20-B36-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testability Engineering","subtopic":"Dependency Seams","concept":"Test seam selection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a useful test seam?","answer":"A test seam is a controlled boundary where a dependency or behavior can be substituted, observed, or constrained without changing the intended production contract.","principle":"A test seam should isolate a relevant source of variability while preserving meaningful production behavior.","failure_mode":"A substitute removes the behavior that the test was supposed to verify.","root_cause":"The seam was placed at the wrong abstraction boundary.","testing_guidance":"Prefer seams at stable contractual boundaries and keep substitutes behaviorally relevant.","verification_method":"Confirm that the test still exercises the production logic and contract being evaluated.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B04-R001","D20-B30-R001"],"provenance":["established software testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B36-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testability Engineering","subtopic":"Observability","concept":"Test-oriented observability","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should observability support testing without coupling tests to implementation details?","answer":"Observability should expose stable evidence about externally meaningful behavior, state transitions, failures, and timing while avoiding dependence on incidental internal structure.","requirements":["Define evidence needed for diagnosis","Expose stable semantic events or metrics","Preserve correlation context","Control sensitive data"],"failure_mode":"Tests depend on unstable internal logs or implementation-specific details.","root_cause":"Observability was designed without distinguishing diagnostic evidence from implementation structure.","testing_guidance":"Prefer structured semantic events, metrics, and correlation identifiers over raw text matching.","verification_method":"Verify that test diagnostics remain useful across behavior-preserving implementation changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B77-R001","D20-B05-R005","D20-B30-R004"],"provenance":["established observability and testing practice"],"security_implications":"Observability used for testing must not expose credentials or sensitive data."}
{"record_id":"D20-B37-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Testing","subtopic":"Race Conditions","concept":"Race-condition test design","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can a test expose a race condition?","answer":"A race-condition test increases the probability of an unsafe interleaving by controlling or perturbing scheduling around a shared-state operation, then verifies the required concurrency invariant.","requirements":["Identify shared state","Define concurrency invariant","Create competing operations","Control or perturb synchronization points","Repeat enough to exercise relevant schedules"],"constraints":["A race test cannot prove all schedules are safe","Timing-based reproduction is weaker than deterministic schedule control"],"expected_behavior":"Concurrent operations preserve the defined invariant across relevant interleavings.","failure_mode":"The invariant is violated under a particular interleaving.","root_cause":"Unsynchronized or incorrectly synchronized shared-state transition.","testing_guidance":"Prefer deterministic synchronization hooks when available over arbitrary sleeps.","verification_method":"Capture the interleaving or synchronization evidence responsible for the failure and reproduce it where possible.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R003","D20-B36-R001","D20-B37-R002"],"provenance":["established concurrent-testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B37-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Testing","subtopic":"Atomicity","concept":"Atomic state transition testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should atomic state transition tests verify?","answer":"They should verify that observers cannot observe forbidden intermediate states and that competing operations produce only states permitted by the concurrency contract.","expected_behavior":"Each committed operation produces an allowed state transition and intermediate invalid states are not externally observable where atomicity is required.","failure_mode":"A reader observes partial state or two writers create a state that neither operation alone permits.","root_cause":"Non-atomic update sequence or insufficient synchronization/isolation.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent readers and writers around the critical transition.","verification_method":"Check observed states against the formal or documented state-transition invariant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B37-R001","D20-B24-R002","D18-B50-R003"],"provenance":["established concurrency and database testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B37-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Testing","subtopic":"Deadlocks","concept":"Deadlock-oriented testing","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What evidence indicates a possible deadlock?","answer":"Evidence may include operations remaining blocked without expected progress, mutually waiting resources, reproducible lock-order conditions, and diagnostics identifying blocked participants.","failure_mode":"Concurrent operations remain unable to progress because each requires a resource held by another.","root_cause":"Cyclic resource dependency or incompatible synchronization ordering.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled concurrency and bounded test timeouts; preserve lock or synchronization diagnostics where available.","verification_method":"Reproduce the blocking relationship and verify that the corrected synchronization order or design removes the cycle without introducing another race.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B37-R001","D20-B35-R005"],"provenance":["established concurrent-systems testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B37-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Testing","subtopic":"Duplicate Effects","concept":"Concurrent idempotency testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should concurrent duplicate requests be tested for idempotency?","answer":"Submit logically duplicate operations concurrently and verify that the resulting externally visible effect satisfies the operation's idempotency contract rather than merely checking that each request returned successfully.","requirements":["Define logical operation identity","Generate concurrent duplicates","Observe final state and side effects","Define acceptable result"],"expected_behavior":"Concurrent duplicates produce the same permitted externally visible effect as required by the idempotency contract.","failure_mode":"Concurrent duplicate requests create multiple unintended effects.","root_cause":"Idempotency key, uniqueness constraint, synchronization, or transaction design is insufficient.","testing_guidance":"Inspect final state and side-effect count where observable.","verification_method":"Compare the final state against the documented idempotency invariant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B41-R001","D20-B21-R004","D20-B37-R001"],"provenance":["established distributed-systems testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B37-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Concurrency Testing","subtopic":"Cancellation","concept":"Concurrent cancellation testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should cancellation tests verify in concurrent systems?","answer":"Cancellation tests should verify that cancellation propagates according to the contract, resources are released, shared state remains valid, and already-completed work is not incorrectly rolled back or duplicated.","requirements":["Define cancellation boundary","Start concurrent work","Trigger cancellation","Observe propagation","Verify resource and state invariants"],"failure_mode":"Cancellation leaves orphaned work, leaked resources, partial invalid state, or duplicated completion.","root_cause":"Cancellation semantics are inconsistent across task boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Test cancellation before start, during execution, and after completion where those states are meaningful.","verification_method":"Verify final task state, resources, and externally visible effects against the cancellation contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B45-R001","D18-B48-R001","D20-B35-R005"],"provenance":["established asynchronous and concurrency testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B38-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Path Handling","concept":"Filesystem path boundary testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What should filesystem path tests verify?","answer":"Filesystem tests should verify normalization, allowed roots, relative and absolute path behavior, missing paths, symlink behavior where relevant, and rejection of paths outside the permitted boundary.","requirements":["Define filesystem boundary","Define allowed path semantics","Test valid and invalid representations","Verify resolved target where security-sensitive"],"expected_behavior":"Only paths permitted by the filesystem contract are accessed or modified.","failure_mode":"A path representation bypasses the intended directory boundary.","root_cause":"Validation and filesystem resolution use inconsistent semantics.","testing_guidance":"Test equivalent path representations and security-sensitive resolution behavior.","verification_method":"Compare the resolved target with the authorized boundary and verify the policy before access.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B32-R003","D20-B32-R003","D20-B29-R005"],"provenance":["OWASP Path Traversal guidance","established filesystem testing practice"],"security_implications":"Filesystem boundary errors can cause unauthorized data access or modification."}
{"record_id":"D20-B38-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Resource Cleanup","concept":"Filesystem resource cleanup","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should filesystem resource cleanup be tested?","answer":"Tests should verify that files, descriptors, temporary artifacts, and locks are released or removed according to the contract after success, failure, cancellation, and exceptional termination.","requirements":["Define cleanup contract","Test success path","Test failure path","Test cancellation path","Inspect resource state"],"failure_mode":"Temporary or exclusive resources remain after an operation terminates.","root_cause":"Cleanup is not guaranteed across all control-flow paths.","testing_guidance":"Assert cleanup after both expected and exceptional outcomes.","verification_method":"Inspect the resource state after termination and verify the documented cleanup invariant.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B35-R005","D20-B07-R004","D19-B37-R004"],"provenance":["established software reliability practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B38-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Process Testing","subtopic":"Subprocess Lifecycle","concept":"Process lifecycle testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should subprocess tests verify?","answer":"Subprocess tests should verify correct startup, input/output handling, exit-status interpretation, timeout or cancellation behavior, cleanup, and handling of abnormal termination.","requirements":["Define process contract","Define expected exit behavior","Define timeout/cancellation semantics","Capture output safely","Verify cleanup"],"failure_mode":"A child process remains running, output is mishandled, or an abnormal exit is treated as success.","root_cause":"Incomplete process lifecycle handling.","testing_guidance":"Test success, non-zero exit, missing executable, blocked I/O, timeout, and termination paths where applicable.","verification_method":"Compare process state, exit semantics, outputs, and cleanup against the declared contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B52-R001","D20-B35-R005","D20-B22-R004"],"provenance":["established process-integration testing practice"],"security_implications":"Unsafe subprocess handling can create command-execution or resource-management vulnerabilities."}
{"record_id":"D20-B38-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Process Testing","subtopic":"Exit Status","concept":"Exit-status semantic testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should process exit status be tested separately from process completion?","answer":"A process completing does not imply successful completion; tests must verify that the exit status and resulting outputs satisfy the application contract.","failure_mode":"Any process termination is interpreted as success.","root_cause":"Completion state was conflated with successful execution.","testing_guidance":"Exercise successful and unsuccessful exit paths and verify caller behavior for each.","verification_method":"Assert explicit mapping from exit status to application outcome.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B38-R003"],"provenance":["established process-integration testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B38-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Network Testing","subtopic":"Network Failure Simulation","concept":"Controlled network failure testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should network failure tests verify?","answer":"Network failure tests should exercise timeout, connection refusal, connection reset, partial response, DNS failure, and dependency unavailability cases when they are relevant to the application's network contract.","requirements":["Define network failure condition","Control test environment","Observe client behavior","Verify retry/cancellation/error semantics","Verify resource cleanup"],"expected_behavior":"The application classifies and handles the injected network condition according to its contract without leaking resources or corrupting state.","failure_mode":"A network failure produces incorrect retries, leaked connections, ambiguous state, or misleading error classification.","root_cause":"Network failure modes were treated as equivalent or incompletely modeled.","testing_guidance":"Test failure types separately because they can have different semantics and recovery requirements.","verification_method":"Compare observed classification and recovery with the network contract and preserved failure evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B49-R002","D18-B48-R003","D20-B35-R005"],"provenance":["established network reliability testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B39-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Infrastructure","subtopic":"Environment Isolation","concept":"Test environment isolation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should test environments be isolated?","answer":"Isolation reduces unintended coupling between tests and external state, improving reproducibility and making failures attributable to the system under test rather than contamination from unrelated activity.","requirements":["Control shared resources","Define environment ownership","Reset mutable state","Prevent cross-suite interference"],"failure_mode":"A test outcome depends on artifacts or state left by another test or process.","root_cause":"Shared mutable environment without adequate isolation or reset.","testing_guidance":"Use isolated resources or deterministic reset procedures appropriate to the test level.","verification_method":"Run the test independently and in varying suite order to detect environmental dependence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B04-R005","D20-B05-R001","D20-B09-R004"],"provenance":["established test engineering practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B39-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Infrastructure","subtopic":"Environment Parity","concept":"Environment parity boundaries","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What does environment parity mean in testing?","answer":"Environment parity means reproducing the environmental properties that materially affect the behavior under test; it does not require every environment characteristic to be identical.","principle":"Parity should be defined by behaviorally relevant dependencies rather than superficial similarity.","failure_mode":"A test environment is considered equivalent despite differences in runtime, protocol, storage, networking, configuration, or dependency behavior that affect the result.","root_cause":"Environment equivalence was assumed rather than specified.","testing_guidance":"Document which environment properties are contractually relevant for each test class.","verification_method":"Compare declared relevant properties between test and target environments.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B09-R004","D20-B27-R001","D20-B34-R001"],"provenance":["established test engineering practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B39-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Infrastructure","subtopic":"Test Data","concept":"Deterministic synthetic test data","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What makes synthetic test data useful for automated testing?","answer":"Synthetic test data is useful when it is deterministic, representative of required partitions and boundaries, safe to use, traceable to the test objective, and sufficiently varied to expose relevant behavior.","requirements":["Define data purpose","Cover required partitions","Avoid real sensitive data","Make generation reproducible where needed","Preserve expected relationships"],"constraints":["Synthetic data can miss production distributions","Representativeness must be assessed against the test objective"],"expected_behavior":"The dataset reliably exercises the intended test conditions without exposing real sensitive information.","testing_guidance":"Include boundary and invalid data intentionally rather than relying only on random generation.","verification_method":"Map generated data categories to the requirements and test design.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B02-R001","D20-B02-R002","D20-B09-R003"],"provenance":["established software testing practice"],"security_implications":"Synthetic data reduces unnecessary exposure of production-sensitive information."}
{"record_id":"D20-B39-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Infrastructure","subtopic":"Configuration","concept":"Configuration-sensitive test coverage","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should configuration-dependent behavior be tested?","answer":"Configuration tests should cover supported configuration classes, required defaults, invalid combinations, security-sensitive settings, and behavior changes explicitly defined by the configuration contract.","requirements":["Enumerate supported configuration dimensions","Define defaults","Identify invalid combinations","Test security-sensitive settings","Verify effective configuration"],"failure_mode":"A configuration combination produces behavior inconsistent with documented policy or defaults.","root_cause":"Configuration parsing, precedence, defaulting, or validation defect.","testing_guidance":"Test effective configuration rather than only configuration-file syntax.","verification_method":"Compare effective runtime configuration and resulting behavior with the authoritative configuration contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B53-R001","D20-B27-R001","D20-B21-R005"],"provenance":["established configuration-testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B39-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Infrastructure","subtopic":"Harness Failures","concept":"Test harness health classification","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"How should test-harness failure be distinguished from product failure?","answer":"A harness failure occurs when the test infrastructure cannot reliably establish or observe the intended test condition; a product failure occurs when the system under test violates the tested contract under a valid test condition.","principle":"The validity of the test condition must be established before interpreting the product result.","failure_mode":"Harness malfunction is reported as a product defect or a product defect is hidden by a harness error.","root_cause":"Test infrastructure evidence was not separated from product evidence.","testing_guidance":"Record setup, fixture, environment, observation, and product-result evidence separately.","verification_method":"Re-run or independently validate the test condition before assigning the failure to the product.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B09-R005","D20-B22-R004","D20-B30-R004"],"provenance":["established test engineering practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B40-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testing Strategy","subtopic":"Risk-Based Testing","concept":"Risk-weighted test selection","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should tests be selected when exhaustive execution is infeasible?","answer":"Select tests according to requirement criticality, failure impact, change scope, defect history, security exposure, dependency complexity, and evidence value rather than using test count alone.","procedure":["Identify changed and affected behaviors","Assess business and technical risk","Map available tests to risks","Prioritize high-value coverage","Record omitted coverage and rationale"],"constraints":["Risk assessment is not proof of correctness","Low-priority areas should not be silently treated as verified"],"expected_behavior":"Test execution prioritizes high-risk behavior while preserving visibility into untested areas.","testing_guidance":"Use dependency and behavioral impact information to refine selection.","verification_method":"Audit the selection against stated risk criteria and affected behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B06-R001","D20-B22-R002","D20-B30-R001"],"provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119","established risk-based testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B40-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testing Strategy","subtopic":"Change Impact","concept":"Change-aware test prioritization","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can code changes guide test prioritization?","answer":"Test prioritization can use changed files, affected dependencies, behavior mappings, requirement links, historical defects, and risk metadata to identify tests most likely to provide evidence about the change.","requirements":["Map changes to affected behavior","Map behavior to tests","Account for risk and historical defects","Preserve broader regression requirements"],"failure_mode":"A narrow change-based selection omits indirect or shared behavior affected by the change.","root_cause":"Impact analysis considered only direct code references.","testing_guidance":"Combine structural dependency analysis with semantic behavior and requirement relationships.","verification_method":"Review whether the selected tests cover direct, indirect, and critical shared effects.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B06-R001","D20-B06-R002","D20-B30-R003"],"provenance":["established regression-testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B40-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testing Strategy","subtopic":"Test Selection","concept":"Test selection evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should justify omitting a test from a change-specific run?","answer":"Omission should be supported by a documented scope, dependency or impact analysis, risk rationale, and knowledge that the test is covered elsewhere when required.","principle":"Not running a test is itself a test-strategy decision that requires traceable justification when coverage matters.","failure_mode":"Tests are omitted based on intuition or execution convenience without evidence.","root_cause":"Test selection has no explicit coverage model.","testing_guidance":"Record selected, omitted, and mandatory tests separately.","verification_method":"Audit omissions against change impact, requirements, and risk policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B40-R001","D20-B40-R002","D20-B30-R003"],"provenance":["established regression-testing practice"]}
{"record_id":"D20-B40-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testing Strategy","subtopic":"Test Cost","concept":"Test cost versus diagnostic value","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should test execution cost be balanced against diagnostic value?","answer":"Test suites should balance execution cost against risk coverage, failure diagnostic quality, feedback latency, and required confidence; faster tests are not automatically better if they provide weak evidence.","tradeoffs":["Fast tests improve feedback latency","Broader tests increase coverage of cross-boundary behavior","Highly integrated tests may provide stronger system evidence but weaker failure localization","Expensive tests may be reserved for risk-driven gates"],"constraints":["No universal test-level ratio applies to every system","Cost must be measured or explicitly characterized rather than fabricated"],"expected_behavior":"The test portfolio provides sufficient evidence for its risk profile within operational constraints.","testing_guidance":"Track execution time, failure diagnostic quality, maintenance burden, and coverage purpose.","verification_method":"Periodically review whether expensive tests provide unique evidence and whether fast tests remain meaningful.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B30-R001","D20-B09-R001","D20-B40-R001"],"provenance":["established software testing practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"}
{"record_id":"D20-B40-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Testing Strategy","subtopic":"D20 Coverage","concept":"D20 B31-B40 quality gate","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be checked before accepting D20 B31-B40?","answer":"D20 B31-B40 should be checked for security-testing depth, performance-testing methodology, resilience testing, concurrency and environment testability, evidence preservation, provenance, deduplication, schema compliance, and absence of fabricated execution or measurement claims.","requirements":["Security testing coverage reviewed","Performance testing coverage reviewed","Reliability and resilience coverage reviewed","Concurrency coverage reviewed","Filesystem/process/network testing reviewed","Testability and environment coverage reviewed","Schema consistency checked","Duplicates checked","Provenance checked","Execution and benchmark claims checked"],"constraints":["This gate does not lock the entire D20 dataset","Code and language implementation coverage remains a separate audit requirement"],"expected_behavior":"B31-B40 is internally coherent, non-duplicative, evidence-backed, and truthful about validation and execution status.","testing_guidance":"Run structural and semantic dataset audits before advancing to the next batch.","verification_method":"Compare records against the D20 canonical schema, quality-first rules, prior D20 coverage, and provenance requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B30-R005","D20-B40-R001","D20-B40-R004"],"provenance":["D20 dataset governance specification"],"status":"NOT_LOCKED"}

{"record_id":"D20-B41-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Pipeline Gates","concept":"Test gate semantics","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","question":"What should a CI/CD test gate mean?","answer":"A test gate should evaluate explicitly defined evidence and acceptance conditions before allowing the associated workflow to proceed.","principle":"A gate is an evidence-based decision boundary, not merely a command that happens to run tests.","requirements":["Define required tests","Define pass/fail interpretation","Define infrastructure-failure handling","Define bypass authority","Preserve evidence"],"constraints":["A green command does not prove universal correctness","A gate must distinguish product failures from test-infrastructure failures"],"expected_behavior":"The pipeline proceeds only when the declared gate conditions are satisfied or an explicitly authorized exception is recorded.","failure_mode":"A pipeline proceeds because a command returned success despite missing, skipped, or invalid test evidence.","root_cause":"Gate semantics are based on process exit status alone.","testing_guidance":"Test the gate itself using passing, failing, skipped, unavailable, and infrastructure-failure scenarios.","verification_method":"Verify that each gate outcome maps deterministically to its documented policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B09-R005","D20-B30-R003","D20-B40-R003"],"provenance":["established CI/CD testing practice","NIST SP 800-218 SSDF"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B41-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Pipeline Gates","concept":"Required versus advisory tests","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"What is the difference between required and advisory test results?","answer":"A required result can block the governed workflow when its acceptance condition is violated; an advisory result provides evidence or warning without automatically blocking progression.","principle":"Test importance must be represented explicitly rather than inferred from whether a test happened to run.","failure_mode":"A critical failing test is treated as informational or a non-critical diagnostic test unexpectedly blocks deployment.","root_cause":"Test severity and gate policy are not explicitly modeled.","testing_guidance":"Assign gate semantics to test groups and verify both blocking and non-blocking outcomes.","verification_method":"Review the test classification against release or change-control policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B40-R001","D20-B41-R001"],"provenance":["established CI/CD governance practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B41-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Pipeline Gates","concept":"Skipped-test semantics","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should skipped tests be distinguished from passed tests?","answer":"A skipped test provides no execution evidence for its intended behavior and therefore should not normally be interpreted as equivalent to a passing test.","principle":"Absence of execution evidence is not positive verification.","failure_mode":"A required test is skipped because of configuration or infrastructure conditions and the pipeline treats the result as passed.","root_cause":"Result-state semantics collapse skipped and passed outcomes.","testing_guidance":"Represent PASS, FAIL, SKIPPED, BLOCKED, NOT_RUN, and INFRASTRUCTURE_FAILURE distinctly where useful.","verification_method":"Check that required coverage remains satisfied only by valid executed evidence or explicitly authorized policy exceptions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B09-R005","D20-B41-R001"],"provenance":["established test-result governance practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B41-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Pipeline Reliability","concept":"Fail-closed critical test gate","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_RULE","question":"When should a critical test gate fail closed?","answer":"A critical gate should fail closed when required evidence cannot be established and the policy does not explicitly permit progression under that condition.","constraints":["Fail-closed behavior must be defined by policy","Emergency bypass must remain separately authorized and auditable"],"expected_behavior":"Missing, invalid, or ambiguous evidence prevents progression unless an explicit exception mechanism is invoked.","failure_mode":"Unavailable testing infrastructure silently allows a change to proceed as though required tests passed.","root_cause":"Missing evidence is treated as positive evidence.","testing_guidance":"Simulate test-service outage, malformed results, missing artifacts, and incomplete execution.","verification_method":"Verify that the gate distinguishes unavailable evidence from successful evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B41-R001","D20-B41-R003","D20-B39-R005"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","established release-engineering practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B41-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Pipeline Evidence","concept":"Immutable test-result evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be preserved with a CI/CD test result?","answer":"A useful test result should preserve the tested revision, test definition or version, execution context, result state, relevant logs or artifacts, and evidence required to interpret the outcome.","requirements":["Identify source revision","Identify test version","Identify environment","Record result state","Preserve diagnostic artifacts"],"constraints":["Sensitive data must be protected","Large artifacts should use controlled retention rather than indefinite storage"],"expected_behavior":"A reviewer can determine what was tested, under which conditions, and what evidence produced the result.","failure_mode":"A historical green result cannot be associated with the code or environment that produced it.","root_cause":"Insufficient provenance in CI test artifacts.","testing_guidance":"Treat test-result provenance as part of the result record.","verification_method":"Attempt to reconstruct the execution context from stored evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R005","D20-B30-R003","D20-B41-R001"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","established CI/CD practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B42-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Parallel Execution","concept":"Test parallelization correctness","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What must be verified when tests are parallelized?","answer":"Parallel execution must preserve test semantics, isolation, deterministic result interpretation, resource safety, and required ordering constraints.","requirements":["Identify tests safe for parallel execution","Isolate shared state","Declare required ordering","Control resource contention","Compare results with a valid sequential baseline where necessary"],"failure_mode":"Tests pass sequentially but fail or corrupt state when executed concurrently.","root_cause":"Hidden shared state, resource contention, ordering dependency, or non-thread-safe test infrastructure.","testing_guidance":"Run suites under different valid parallelization levels and inspect failures for infrastructure versus product causes.","verification_method":"Verify that parallel execution preserves the same tested contract and does not introduce test-induced behavior changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B39-R001","D20-B37-R001","D20-B36-R001"],"provenance":["established test-infrastructure practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"} 
{"record_id":"D20-B42-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Test Sharding","concept":"Shard completeness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should test sharding verify?","answer":"Sharding should partition the intended test set without silently omitting, duplicating, or inconsistently executing tests.","requirements":["Define authoritative test inventory","Define deterministic partitioning","Track shard membership","Aggregate all shard results"],"failure_mode":"A test is absent from every shard or appears inconsistently across shards.","root_cause":"Non-deterministic or incomplete shard partitioning.","testing_guidance":"Validate the union and overlap of shard inventories before interpreting aggregate results.","verification_method":"Compare the aggregated executed set with the authoritative test inventory.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B41-R003","D20-B30-R003","D20-B40-R003"],"provenance":["established CI/CD testing practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B42-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Test Sharding","concept":"Shard isolation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How should sharded tests remain isolated?","answer":"Each shard should receive independent test resources or controlled namespaces so that concurrent execution does not alter the behavior or evidence of another shard.","constraints":["Shared resources may be used only when their semantics are explicitly controlled","Resource isolation must include data, ports, files, services, and external state where relevant"],"failure_mode":"One shard changes state consumed by another shard.","root_cause":"Parallel test resources were not partitioned or reset correctly.","testing_guidance":"Use unique resource identifiers and verify cleanup after each shard.","verification_method":"Run shards concurrently and inspect for cross-shard contamination.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B39-R001","D20-B42-R001"],"provenance":["established test-infrastructure practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B42-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Parallel resource budgeting","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why must parallel test execution account for resource budgets?","answer":"Increasing test concurrency can increase CPU, memory, storage, network, process, database, and external-service pressure, causing infrastructure failures that are unrelated to the product.","principle":"Parallelism is bounded by the resources required to execute and observe the tests correctly.","failure_mode":"Increasing workers creates failures that disappear when concurrency is reduced.","root_cause":"Test parallelism exceeds available infrastructure capacity or service quotas.","testing_guidance":"Measure or explicitly characterize resource constraints and distinguish infrastructure saturation from product defects.","verification_method":"Repeat the failing test at controlled concurrency and compare evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B34-R004","D20-B39-R005","D21"],"provenance":["established test-infrastructure and performance practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"} 
{"record_id":"D20-B42-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Execution Scheduling","concept":"Test scheduling by dependency","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should tests be scheduled when some tests depend on earlier evidence?","answer":"Tests with genuine evidence dependencies should execute after their prerequisites, while independent tests should remain eligible for parallel execution.","procedure":["Identify test dependencies","Separate true dependencies from incidental ordering","Build a dependency graph","Schedule independent tests concurrently","Validate prerequisite results before dependent interpretation"],"failure_mode":"A test consumes invalid or unavailable prerequisite state.","root_cause":"Implicit ordering dependency was not represented explicitly.","testing_guidance":"Do not introduce global serialization merely to hide unknown dependencies.","verification_method":"Audit the dependency graph and verify each required prerequisite relationship.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B30-R003","D20-B42-R001"],"provenance":["established CI/CD testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B43-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Test cache correctness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"When is a cached test result valid?","answer":"A cached result is valid only when the cache key captures every input whose change could alter the test outcome or its interpretation.","requirements":["Identify source inputs","Identify dependency versions","Identify configuration","Identify environment factors that affect the test","Construct an appropriate cache key"],"failure_mode":"A stale result is reused after a relevant input changed.","root_cause":"Cache key omits a behavior-affecting dependency or environment input.","testing_guidance":"Intentionally modify each cache-key dependency and verify invalidation.","verification_method":"Compare cache reuse behavior against the defined dependency set.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B27-R001","D20-B41-R005","D20-B43-R002"],"provenance":["established build/test caching practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B43-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Negative cache invalidation testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should test-cache invalidation be tested?","answer":"A cache should be tested against controlled changes to source, dependencies, configuration, runtime, and other declared cache-key inputs to confirm that affected results are invalidated.","requirements":["Define cache-key inputs","Change one input at a time where practical","Observe reuse or invalidation","Verify resulting execution"],"failure_mode":"A changed dependency or configuration reuses an obsolete successful test result.","root_cause":"Incomplete dependency modeling in cache invalidation.","testing_guidance":"Include both positive reuse cases and negative invalidation cases.","verification_method":"Verify that each behavior-affecting change invalidates the cached evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B43-R001","D20-B27-R003"],"provenance":["established CI/CD caching practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B43-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Artifact identity","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should identify a reusable test artifact?","answer":"A reusable artifact should be associated with the exact inputs and generation context necessary to determine whether it remains semantically valid.","requirements":["Source revision","Build or test configuration","Relevant dependency versions","Generator/tool version","Artifact identity"],"constraints":["Artifact names alone are insufficient evidence of equivalence"],"expected_behavior":"Consumers can determine whether an artifact was generated from compatible inputs.","verification_method":"Trace the artifact to its complete declared provenance.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B41-R005","D20-B43-R001"],"provenance":["established software supply-chain and CI/CD practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B43-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Caching","concept":"Cache poisoning resistance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What security properties should test caching preserve?","answer":"Test caching should prevent untrusted or incorrectly scoped artifacts from being reused as trusted evidence for a different codebase, tenant, configuration, or security context.","requirements":["Define artifact trust boundary","Scope cache keys appropriately","Control write permissions","Validate artifact provenance"],"failure_mode":"An artifact generated in one trust context is consumed as valid evidence in another.","root_cause":"Cache namespace or authorization boundary is insufficient.","testing_guidance":"Test cross-project, cross-branch, cross-tenant, and privilege-boundary reuse where applicable.","verification_method":"Attempt controlled cross-boundary artifact reuse and verify rejection or isolation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B14-R005","D20-B43-R001","D20-B41-R005"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","established CI/CD security practice"],"security_implications":"Incorrect cache trust can turn stale or untrusted artifacts into false security or quality evidence."} 
{"record_id":"D20-B43-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"CI/CD Testing","subtopic":"Cache Governance","concept":"Cache bypass verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why should cache bypass be tested?","answer":"Cache bypass provides a controlled way to determine whether a suspected failure or stale result originates from cached evidence rather than the current test execution.","procedure":["Identify suspicious cached result","Disable or invalidate the relevant cache","Re-run under equivalent conditions","Compare results and evidence"],"expected_behavior":"The uncached result clarifies whether cached evidence materially affected the prior result.","failure_mode":"A stale cache masks a current failure.","root_cause":"Cached evidence was trusted without confirming current execution.","verification_method":"Compare cache-enabled and cache-bypassed results with identical declared inputs.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B43-R001","D20-B39-R005","D20-B05-R002"],"provenance":["established test-debugging practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B44-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Results","subtopic":"Result Semantics","concept":"Test result state model","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What result states should a mature testing system distinguish?","answer":"A mature test system should distinguish at least successful execution, failed assertion or behavior, skipped/not-run, blocked, infrastructure failure, invalid test condition, and unknown result where those states are operationally meaningful.","principle":"Result-state precision prevents absence of evidence from being misinterpreted as successful evidence.","expected_behavior":"Every result has a state whose semantics are explicitly defined.","verification_method":"Audit result mappings from test runner output to the knowledge fabric and CI gate.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B41-R003","D20-B39-R005","D20-B30-R003"],"provenance":["established test-result engineering practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B44-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Results","subtopic":"Failure Classification","concept":"Assertion failure versus infrastructure failure","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"How should assertion failures be distinguished from infrastructure failures?","answer":"An assertion failure indicates that a valid test condition produced behavior inconsistent with its expected result; an infrastructure failure means the test could not reliably establish or observe that condition.","failure_mode":"A runner outage, dependency startup failure, or environment problem is recorded as a product assertion failure.","root_cause":"Failure classification does not model test-condition validity.","testing_guidance":"Preserve setup and infrastructure evidence alongside assertion evidence.","verification_method":"Determine whether the test condition was valid before attributing the failure to the product.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B09-R005","D20-B22-R004","D20-B44-R001"],"provenance":["established test engineering practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B44-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Results","subtopic":"Failure Triage","concept":"Failure clustering","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How can related test failures be clustered for triage?","answer":"Failures can be clustered using shared first-failed stage, stack signatures, affected component, error type, dependency condition, test setup, and temporal correlation while retaining individual evidence.","constraints":["Similarity does not prove common root cause","Clusters should remain traceable to individual failures"],"procedure":["Normalize failure evidence","Identify candidate similarity features","Cluster related failures","Select representative evidence","Validate common-cause hypothesis","Preserve individual records"],"verification_method":"Check whether clustered failures share independently supported causal evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R002","D18-B77-R002","D20-B44-R002"],"provenance":["established failure-analysis practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B44-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Results","subtopic":"Failure Evidence","concept":"Minimal failure artifact","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is a minimal failure artifact?","answer":"A minimal failure artifact contains the smallest set of evidence required to reproduce or independently analyze the failure while preserving the relevant test context.","requirements":["Test identity","Code revision","Input or stimulus","Relevant configuration","Observed result","Expected result","Failure evidence"],"constraints":["Minimization must not remove information required to distinguish competing causes"],"procedure":["Preserve original evidence","Remove irrelevant inputs or environment state","Reproduce failure","Confirm minimized artifact still fails","Store original-to-minimized relationship"],"verification_method":"Re-run the minimized case and verify that the relevant failure remains reproducible.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R002","D20-B05-R005","D20-B44-R003"],"provenance":["established debugging and regression-testing practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B44-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Test Results","subtopic":"Result Integrity","concept":"Test-result tamper awareness","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should test-result integrity matter?","answer":"Automated decisions may rely on test evidence, so systems should protect result provenance and integrity against accidental corruption or unauthorized modification.","requirements":["Identify trusted result producer","Protect result storage or transport","Associate results with exact inputs","Detect unexpected modification"],"failure_mode":"A modified or forged result is accepted as authentic test evidence.","root_cause":"Test evidence lacks integrity or provenance controls.","testing_guidance":"Use integrity mechanisms appropriate to the CI environment and threat model.","verification_method":"Verify that modified result artifacts are detected or rejected according to policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B41-R005","D20-B43-R004","D19-B60-R005"],"provenance":["NIST SP 800-218 SSDF","established CI/CD security practice"],"security_implications":"Untrusted test evidence can undermine release and security gates."}

{"record_id":"D20-B45-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Flaky Tests","subtopic":"Quarantine","concept":"Flaky-test quarantine semantics","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should happen when a test is identified as flaky?","answer":"A flaky test may be quarantined from blocking workflows only under an explicit policy that preserves visibility, ownership, evidence, and a remediation path; quarantine must not be equivalent to deleting the test.","requirements":["Record flakiness evidence","Assign ownership","Define quarantine scope","Preserve execution results","Define remediation deadline or lifecycle"],"constraints":["Quarantine must not silently reduce required coverage","Repeated retries do not establish correctness"],"expected_behavior":"The test remains visible and tracked while its blocking behavior follows explicit policy.","failure_mode":"A failing test is permanently ignored after being labeled flaky.","root_cause":"Flakiness classification became a defect-disposal mechanism.","testing_guidance":"Track failure frequency, conditions, suspected causes, and remediation status.","verification_method":"Audit quarantined tests and verify active ownership and lifecycle state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R001","D20-B05-R004","D20-B44-R001"],"provenance":["established test reliability practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B45-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Flaky Tests","subtopic":"Diagnosis","concept":"Flaky-test reproduction matrix","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should suspected flakiness be investigated?","answer":"Investigate suspected flakiness across repetition, execution order, concurrency, environment, timing, random seed, dependency state, and resource conditions to identify variables correlated with failure.","procedure":["Preserve failing evidence","Repeat under equivalent conditions","Vary one relevant condition at a time where practical","Record pass/fail distribution","Identify reproducible triggers","Classify uncertainty"],"constraints":["Correlation does not prove cause","Repeated success does not prove the test is deterministic"],"verification_method":"Confirm the suspected trigger through controlled reproduction or retain the cause as unresolved when evidence is insufficient.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B36-R001","D20-B37-R001","D20-B44-R004"],"provenance":["established flaky-test investigation practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B45-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Flaky Tests","subtopic":"Retries","concept":"Retry-limited diagnostic reruns","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why should test retries not be used as a substitute for fixing flaky tests?","answer":"Retries can provide diagnostic evidence about intermittency but can also hide failures and inflate apparent pass rates if treated as proof of correctness.","failure_mode":"A test fails initially, passes on retry, and the system records only the final pass.","root_cause":"Retry result semantics discard the initial failure evidence.","testing_guidance":"Record every attempt and distinguish initial failure from eventual retry success.","verification_method":"Audit whether retry history remains available and whether gate policy treats it correctly.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R004","D20-B41-R005","D20-B44-R001"],"provenance":["established test reliability practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B45-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Flaky Tests","subtopic":"Root Cause","concept":"Flakiness cause taxonomy","knowledge_type":"CLASSIFICATION","question":"What common cause classes should be considered for flaky tests?","answer":"Relevant cause classes include uncontrolled time, randomness, concurrency, shared state, external dependencies, resource contention, environment drift, asynchronous propagation, and test-harness defects.","constraints":["The taxonomy is a diagnostic aid, not proof of cause","A test may have multiple interacting causes"],"testing_guidance":"Map evidence to candidate causes before selecting a root cause.","verification_method":"Require supporting evidence for the final cause classification and retain unresolved alternatives when necessary.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B36-R001","D20-B37-R001","D20-B39-R005","D20-B45-R002"],"provenance":["established test-reliability practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B45-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Flaky Tests","subtopic":"Regression","concept":"Flaky regression test recovery","knowledge_type":"REGRESSION_TEST","question":"When should a quarantined regression test return to blocking status?","answer":"A quarantined test should return to blocking status after the underlying cause is addressed and evidence demonstrates that the test now provides sufficiently reliable evidence for its intended contract.","requirements":["Identify root cause or documented residual uncertainty","Implement correction","Reproduce the former failure condition","Repeat reliability validation","Restore gate classification"],"verification_method":"Review correction evidence and confirm the test's intended signal is reliable under relevant conditions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B45-R001","D20-B45-R002","D20-B06-R004"],"provenance":["established regression-test lifecycle practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B46-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Async Testing","subtopic":"Task Lifecycle","concept":"Asynchronous task lifecycle testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should asynchronous task tests verify?","answer":"Tests should verify task creation, scheduling, completion, failure, cancellation, timeout, cleanup, and result propagation according to the asynchronous contract.","requirements":["Define task states","Define completion semantics","Define cancellation semantics","Define resource ownership","Define exception propagation"],"failure_mode":"A task appears complete while work remains active or an exception is lost.","root_cause":"Task lifecycle state is not correctly observed or propagated.","testing_guidance":"Exercise success, failure, cancellation, timeout, and abandonment paths.","verification_method":"Inspect final task state and externally visible effects after each lifecycle transition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B35-R005","D20-B37-R005","D18-B44-R001"],"provenance":["established asynchronous testing practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B46-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Async Testing","subtopic":"Exception Propagation","concept":"Async exception observation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must asynchronous exceptions be explicitly observed?","answer":"An asynchronous task can fail independently of the caller's immediate control flow, so tests must explicitly await, inspect, or otherwise observe task completion and exception state.","failure_mode":"A background task fails but the test passes because the failure was never observed.","root_cause":"Task completion was not joined or inspected.","testing_guidance":"Ensure every task created by the test has an intentional lifecycle and observation policy.","verification_method":"Verify that task failures cannot escape the test's evidence boundary unnoticed.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B46-R001"],"provenance":["established asynchronous testing practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B46-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Async Testing","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Async backpressure testing","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What should asynchronous backpressure tests verify?","answer":"Backpressure tests should verify that producers cannot indefinitely overwhelm consumers when the system's contract requires bounded buffering or controlled flow.","requirements":["Define producer and consumer rates","Define buffer or queue contract","Observe queue growth","Verify admission or flow-control behavior"],"failure_mode":"Queue growth becomes unbounded or memory/resource usage grows without the required control.","root_cause":"Missing or ineffective flow-control mechanism.","testing_guidance":"Exercise producer rates above consumer capacity and verify bounded behavior.","verification_method":"Compare observed queue and resource behavior with the declared flow-control contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B45-R001","D18-B46-R003","D21"],"provenance":["established asynchronous and streaming-systems practice"],"performance_claim_type":"UNKNOWN"} 
{"record_id":"D20-B46-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Async Testing","subtopic":"Event Ordering","concept":"Asynchronous event-order testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should asynchronous event ordering be tested?","answer":"Tests should define which ordering guarantees are required and verify those guarantees without assuming global ordering where the system contract provides only partial or key-scoped ordering.","requirements":["Define ordering scope","Generate distinguishable events","Observe delivery or processing order","Verify allowed reorderings"],"failure_mode":"The implementation violates required ordering or the test incorrectly rejects permitted reordering.","root_cause":"Ordering contract is unspecified or tested at the wrong scope.","testing_guidance":"Represent event identities and expected ordering relations explicitly.","verification_method":"Compare observed order against the documented partial or total ordering contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B25-R003","D20-B26-R004","D18-B44-R002"],"provenance":["established distributed-systems testing practice"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B46-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Async Testing","subtopic":"Async Resource Cleanup","concept":"Async resource cleanup","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should asynchronous cleanup tests verify?","answer":"Tests should verify that asynchronous tasks release owned resources after success, failure, cancellation, and timeout, including connections, files, subscriptions, locks, and queued work where applicable.","failure_mode":"Cancelled or failed asynchronous work retains resources or callbacks indefinitely.","root_cause":"Cleanup depends on normal completion and does not cover exceptional lifecycle states.","testing_guidance":"Inspect resource counts or ownership markers after every terminal task state.","verification_method":"Verify that resource ownership returns to the expected postcondition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B38-R002","D20-B46-R001","D20-B35-R005"],"provenance":["established asynchronous reliability practice"]}

