{"record_id":"D20-B52-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Formal and Property Testing","subtopic":"Properties","concept":"Property-based test validity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a property suitable for property-based testing?","answer":"A useful property expresses an independently justified invariant, relation, or contract that should hold across a meaningful input domain rather than merely restating one implementation path.","requirements":["Define input domain","Define invariant or relation","Define expected applicability","Identify exceptional cases"],"failure_mode":"The property is too weak to detect meaningful defects or simply duplicates implementation logic.","root_cause":"Property lacks an independent behavioral basis.","testing_guidance":"Review properties against requirements, mathematical invariants, protocol rules, or independently justified semantics.","verification_method":"Trace each property to its authoritative rationale and evaluate whether it can distinguish relevant incorrect behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D20-B28-R005","D20-B47-R002"],"provenance":["established property-based testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B52-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Formal and Property Testing","subtopic":"Properties","concept":"Precondition-aware properties","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must property-based tests model input preconditions explicitly?","answer":"A property may only be valid for a defined subset of inputs; applying it outside that domain can create false failures or cause the generator to spend effort on irrelevant cases.","requirements":["Define valid domain","Define invalid domain","Define expected handling for invalid inputs","Constrain or classify generated cases"],"failure_mode":"A property fails because the generated input violates an unstated precondition.","root_cause":"Input-domain assumptions were not encoded in the test.","testing_guidance":"Distinguish generated inputs that satisfy the property precondition from inputs intended to test rejection behavior.","verification_method":"Verify that every property failure occurs within its declared applicability domain.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D20-B29-R001","D20-B52-R001"],"provenance":["established property-based testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B52-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Formal and Property Testing","subtopic":"Shrinking","concept":"Failure-case minimization","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"Why is shrinking useful in property-based testing?","answer":"Shrinking attempts to reduce a failing generated input to a smaller case that still demonstrates the failure, improving diagnosis and regression-test construction.","requirements":["Preserve failure condition","Reduce input complexity","Retain relevant environmental assumptions"],"constraints":["A smaller input is not necessarily a complete explanation of the root cause","Shrinking may remove context that is causally relevant if the reducer is inappropriate"],"expected_behavior":"The minimized case remains a valid reproducer of the relevant failure.","verification_method":"Re-run the minimized case and confirm the failure remains attributable to the same tested property.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B44-R004","D20-B47-R004","D20-B52-R001"],"provenance":["established property-based testing practice"]}

