{"record_id":"D20-B53-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Protocol Testing","subtopic":"Protocol State","concept":"Protocol state-machine testing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a network or application protocol be tested as a state machine?","answer":"Represent protocol states, legal messages, sequencing constraints, negotiation outcomes, error responses, and termination behavior, then exercise both valid and invalid message sequences.","requirements":["Protocol state model","Message grammar","Ordering rules","Error semantics","Termination rules"],"constraints":["Transport success does not establish protocol correctness","Protocol specifications may permit multiple valid sequences"],"procedure":["Define protocol states","Define valid and invalid messages","Generate representative sequences","Inject protocol errors where safe","Verify responses and state transitions"],"verification_method":"Compare observed protocol behavior with the applicable protocol specification and implementation contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B51-R001","D20-B27-R004","D18-B75-R001"],"provenance":["established protocol-testing practice","applicable protocol specifications"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B53-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Protocol Testing","subtopic":"Protocol Negative Testing","concept":"Unexpected-message handling","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What should protocol tests verify when unexpected messages arrive?","answer":"They should verify that malformed, out-of-order, unsupported, duplicated, or contextually invalid messages are handled according to the protocol contract without producing unsafe state transitions.","failure_mode":"An unexpected message causes an unauthorized transition, inconsistent state, crash, or resource leak.","root_cause":"Protocol state validation or error handling is incomplete.","testing_guidance":"Exercise invalid message classes appropriate to the protocol and verify state preservation and bounded resource use.","verification_method":"Inspect both protocol response and resulting internal or externally observable state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B29-R001","D20-B53-R001","D20-B07-R002"],"provenance":["established protocol-security testing practice","OWASP testing guidance"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B53-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Protocol Testing","subtopic":"Capability Negotiation","concept":"Capability negotiation testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should protocol capability negotiation be tested?","answer":"Test compatible capabilities, partial overlap, no overlap, malformed capability declarations, unsupported versions, and downgrade or fallback behavior where the protocol defines them.","requirements":["Define supported capability set","Define fallback semantics","Define unsupported behavior","Define security constraints"],"failure_mode":"Endpoints select an unsupported or weaker behavior or fail to reject incompatible negotiation.","root_cause":"Capability negotiation logic does not correctly enforce supported combinations.","testing_guidance":"Verify both negotiated result and the evidence used to select it.","verification_method":"Compare negotiation outcomes with the declared compatibility and security policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B75-R001","D20-B27-R004","D19-B80-R003"],"provenance":["established protocol-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B53-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Protocol Testing","subtopic":"Message Boundaries","concept":"Protocol framing tests","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why should protocol tests exercise message-framing boundaries?","answer":"Framing errors can cause truncation, message concatenation, parsing ambiguity, or desynchronization, especially when transport chunks do not align with application message boundaries.","requirements":["Test minimal valid frame","Test boundary-sized frame","Test fragmented delivery","Test coalesced delivery","Test truncated frame"],"failure_mode":"The parser assumes one transport read equals one complete message.","root_cause":"Transport segmentation was incorrectly treated as protocol framing.","testing_guidance":"Vary delivery segmentation independently from logical message boundaries.","verification_method":"Verify that identical logical messages are parsed consistently across valid transport chunking patterns.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B45-R001","D20-B29-R001","D20-B53-R001"],"provenance":["established network protocol testing practice"]}

