{"record_id":"D20-B54-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Compiler and Language Tool Testing","subtopic":"Parser Testing","concept":"Compiler parser negative testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should compiler parser tests include beyond valid syntax?","answer":"Compiler parser tests should include malformed syntax, ambiguous constructs, boundary tokens, nesting limits where contractually relevant, invalid combinations, and error-recovery behavior.","requirements":["Valid syntax corpus","Invalid syntax corpus","Expected diagnostic behavior","Relevant lexical boundaries"],"failure_mode":"Malformed source causes a crash, accepts invalid syntax, or produces unusable diagnostics.","root_cause":"Parser validation or recovery behavior is incomplete.","testing_guidance":"Separate syntax acceptance from diagnostic-quality expectations where the language specification permits implementation variation.","verification_method":"Compare parser behavior with the language grammar and documented diagnostic contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B29-R001","D20-B53-R002"],"provenance":["language specification and compiler-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B54-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Compiler and Language Tool Testing","subtopic":"Compiler Differential Testing","concept":"Compiler differential testing","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"How can multiple compiler implementations be used for testing?","answer":"The same valid program can be compiled or interpreted by independent implementations, versions, or execution modes and compared for specified semantic behavior.","requirements":["Common language semantics","Comparable execution conditions","Defined observable outputs"],"constraints":["Implementation differences may be permitted by the language","Undefined or implementation-defined behavior must not be treated as a universal oracle"],"failure_mode":"A compiler disagreement is incorrectly classified as a defect when the specification permits multiple behaviors.","root_cause":"The test domain includes unspecified or implementation-defined semantics without classification.","verification_method":"Check each disagreement against the authoritative language specification before classifying it.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R002","D20-B54-R001"],"provenance":["compiler differential-testing literature","language specifications"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B54-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Compiler and Language Tool Testing","subtopic":"Compiler Pipeline","concept":"Compiler-stage isolation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should compiler tests identify the stage at which a failure occurs?","answer":"Separating lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, intermediate representation, optimization, code generation, linking, and execution stages narrows diagnosis and prevents downstream symptoms from being mistaken for root causes.","failure_mode":"A runtime failure is attributed to code generation when the semantic analysis already produced incorrect input.","root_cause":"Stage boundaries are not represented in test evidence.","testing_guidance":"Use stage-specific artifacts or diagnostics when available and identify the first meaningful divergence.","verification_method":"Trace the failing input through relevant compiler stages and identify the earliest incorrect artifact.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B22-R004","D20-B44-R003"],"provenance":["established compiler-debugging practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B54-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Compiler and Language Tool Testing","subtopic":"Optimization","concept":"Optimization-preservation testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should optimization tests verify?","answer":"Optimization should preserve the language-defined observable behavior of the program within the semantics and permitted implementation freedoms of the language.","requirements":["Define observable behavior","Identify optimization boundary","Compare optimized and reference behavior"],"failure_mode":"An optimization changes defined program behavior.","root_cause":"Optimization transformation violates semantic preservation conditions.","testing_guidance":"Use carefully selected programs covering control flow, arithmetic, memory behavior, concurrency semantics where applicable, and language-defined edge cases.","verification_method":"Compare optimized behavior with a trusted semantic reference or independently justified oracle.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R002","D20-B54-R002"],"provenance":["compiler correctness literature","applicable language specifications"]}

