{"record_id":"D20-B55-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Path Semantics","concept":"Filesystem path portability testing","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What filesystem path differences should portable software tests consider?","answer":"Portable tests should account for path separators, case sensitivity, reserved names, Unicode normalization behavior, symbolic links, permissions, maximum path constraints where relevant, and platform-specific semantics.","constraints":["Only behavior supported by the target platform matrix should be required","Do not invent platform limits"],"failure_mode":"Software passes on one filesystem semantics model but fails on another supported platform.","root_cause":"Tests assumed one platform's path semantics were universal.","testing_guidance":"Run representative path cases across the declared platform matrix.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior with the supported platform contract and language/runtime semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B27-R003","D20-B29-R001"],"provenance":["platform documentation","established portability-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B55-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Resource Lifecycle","concept":"Filesystem resource cleanup testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should filesystem cleanup tests verify?","answer":"Tests should verify that temporary files, directories, locks, handles, and other owned resources are cleaned up after success, failure, cancellation, and interrupted execution where relevant.","failure_mode":"Repeated tests leave artifacts that alter subsequent behavior or exhaust resources.","root_cause":"Cleanup is executed only on the normal success path.","testing_guidance":"Force representative failures and verify cleanup postconditions.","verification_method":"Inspect filesystem state and resource ownership after each terminal test path.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B46-R005","D20-B09-R004"],"provenance":["established filesystem-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B55-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Atomicity","concept":"Filesystem atomic-operation testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should filesystem operations requiring atomicity be tested?","answer":"Tests should verify the documented atomicity guarantee under success and relevant interruption or concurrency scenarios, rather than assuming that a sequence of filesystem calls is automatically atomic.","requirements":["Define atomicity contract","Define observable intermediate states","Define failure conditions","Define concurrent access conditions where relevant"],"failure_mode":"Readers observe partial state or a failed update leaves an inconsistent filesystem representation.","root_cause":"The implementation assumes atomicity not provided by the underlying operation sequence.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled interruption or concurrent observation where safely supported.","verification_method":"Verify that externally observable states remain within the declared atomicity contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R005","D19-B40-R003"],"provenance":["platform filesystem specifications","established filesystem reliability practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B55-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Permission Boundaries","concept":"Filesystem authorization testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What should filesystem permission tests verify?","answer":"Tests should verify that operations succeed only under the permissions and identity context intended by the application and that denial behavior does not leak sensitive information or bypass policy.","requirements":["Define actor identity","Define permitted operations","Define denied operations","Define expected error semantics"],"failure_mode":"An application accesses or modifies a resource outside its authorized filesystem boundary.","root_cause":"Permission enforcement is absent, incorrectly scoped, or confused with application-level validation.","testing_guidance":"Test both allowed and denied operations under representative identities and filesystem states.","verification_method":"Confirm authorization outcome and resulting filesystem state independently.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B14-R001","D20-B08-R002"],"provenance":["OWASP testing guidance","platform filesystem security documentation"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B55-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Filesystem Testing","subtopic":"Filesystem Faults","concept":"Filesystem failure simulation","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Which filesystem failures should be tested when they materially affect application behavior?","answer":"Relevant failures include unavailable paths, permission denial, missing resources, read-only conditions, partial I/O failure, storage exhaustion, concurrent modification, and interrupted operations where the environment can safely reproduce them.","constraints":["Fault injection must remain controlled","Do not infer a specific failure probability from a simulated condition"],"testing_guidance":"Inject one failure class at a time and verify application error classification, cleanup, recovery, and user-visible behavior.","verification_method":"Compare observed handling with the declared filesystem failure contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R002","D20-B39-R005","D20-B55-R003"],"provenance":["established fault-injection and filesystem-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B56-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Process Testing","subtopic":"Subprocess Lifecycle","concept":"Process lifecycle verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should subprocess tests verify?","answer":"Subprocess tests should verify creation, argument and environment propagation, standard input/output/error handling, exit status, timeout, cancellation, signal handling where applicable, and cleanup.","requirements":["Define process contract","Define expected exit behavior","Define timeout semantics","Define resource ownership"],"failure_mode":"A child process remains running after the test or its output and exit state are misinterpreted.","root_cause":"Process lifecycle management is incomplete.","testing_guidance":"Exercise success, nonzero exit, timeout, cancellation, malformed output, and startup failure.","verification_method":"Verify both parent-observed result and actual process/resource termination state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B46-R001","D20-B46-R005","D19-B31-R001"],"provenance":["established subprocess-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B56-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Process Testing","subtopic":"Exit Status","concept":"Exit-code semantics","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should tests verify process exit semantics separately from output?","answer":"A process can emit expected-looking output while returning a failure status, or return success while producing unexpected output; exit status and output are distinct parts of many process contracts.","failure_mode":"Tests assert only output and miss an unsuccessful process state.","root_cause":"The test treats output as a complete process oracle.","testing_guidance":"Assert exit status, output streams, and side effects according to the process contract.","verification_method":"Verify each declared process-result component independently.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B01-R002","D20-B56-R001"],"provenance":["established process-integration testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B56-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Process Testing","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Subprocess timeout testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should a subprocess timeout test verify?","answer":"It should verify timeout detection, process termination or cancellation semantics, cleanup of descendants where required, classification of the timeout, and preservation of diagnostic evidence.","failure_mode":"The parent reports a timeout while the child continues executing or retains resources.","root_cause":"Timeout handling does not fully govern the process lifecycle.","testing_guidance":"Use a controlled long-running test process and verify both parent and child states after timeout handling.","verification_method":"Confirm that the documented post-timeout state is reached and observable evidence is retained.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D20-B56-R001","D20-B56-R005"],"provenance":["established subprocess reliability practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B56-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Process Testing","subtopic":"Environment","concept":"Subprocess environment isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should subprocess tests control environment inheritance?","answer":"Inherited environment variables, working directories, credentials, locale settings, and executable search paths can materially change subprocess behavior and may expose unintended secrets or dependencies.","requirements":["Define allowed environment","Define working directory","Define executable resolution","Control sensitive variables"],"failure_mode":"A test passes only because it inherits an undeclared environment dependency or leaks sensitive state to the child process.","root_cause":"Environment inheritance is implicit and uncontrolled.","testing_guidance":"Test with a minimal declared environment and compare behavior against the normal execution context.","verification_method":"Trace subprocess inputs to explicit environment configuration.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B31-R001","D20-B39-R004"],"provenance":["established process-security and reproducibility practice"],"security_implications":"Uncontrolled environment inheritance can expose credentials and create hidden execution dependencies."} 
{"record_id":"D20-B56-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Process Testing","subtopic":"Process Failure","concept":"Process-start failure classification","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"How should subprocess-start failures be classified?","answer":"Startup failures should distinguish executable resolution, permission denial, invalid arguments or environment, resource limitations, missing dependencies, and test-infrastructure failures where evidence supports those classifications.","failure_mode":"All subprocess startup failures are reported as generic application failures.","root_cause":"The test harness discards the underlying operating-system or runtime evidence.","testing_guidance":"Preserve the original exception, exit information when available, environment, command identity, and execution context without exposing secrets.","verification_method":"Trace the classification to the earliest available failure evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B44-R002","D20-B56-R001"],"provenance":["platform process documentation","established failure-analysis practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B57-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Clock Control","concept":"Controllable time abstraction","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should time-dependent code expose a controllable time source for tests?","answer":"A controllable time source allows tests to exercise expiration, scheduling, ordering, and boundary behavior deterministically without relying on wall-clock passage.","requirements":["Separate time acquisition from business logic","Provide test-controlled time source","Define timezone and clock semantics"],"failure_mode":"Tests depend on real elapsed time and become slow or nondeterministic.","root_cause":"Production time acquisition is directly embedded in behavior under test.","testing_guidance":"Advance controlled time across boundaries and verify behavior without waiting for real time.","verification_method":"Confirm that test outcomes remain deterministic for the same controlled clock state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B03-R005","D20-B45-R002","D18-B48-R001"],"provenance":["established deterministic-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B57-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Boundary Time","concept":"Temporal boundary testing","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Which temporal boundaries deserve explicit tests?","answer":"Relevant boundaries include expiration instants, scheduling thresholds, date changes, daylight-saving transitions where applicable, leap-related calendar behavior, timeout thresholds, and clock rollback or forward behavior where supported.","constraints":["Only boundaries relevant to the application's time model should be included","Do not assume a universal timezone or calendar rule"],"testing_guidance":"Define expected behavior immediately before, at, and after each contractually relevant boundary.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior with the declared temporal semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B02-R002","D20-B57-R001"],"provenance":["established time-dependent software testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B57-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Timeout Semantics","concept":"Monotonic versus wall-clock testing","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should elapsed-duration tests distinguish monotonic time from wall-clock time?","answer":"Elapsed-duration measurement should use a time source whose semantics are appropriate for duration measurement; wall-clock time can change because of clock synchronization or administrative adjustments.","principle":"Clock semantics must match the behavior being tested.","failure_mode":"A duration test fails or expires incorrectly because wall-clock time moved unexpectedly.","root_cause":"Wall-clock time was used for an elapsed-duration contract without accounting for clock adjustments.","verification_method":"Verify that duration calculations use the time semantics required by the implementation contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B48-R001","D20-B57-R001"],"provenance":["established systems-programming and testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B57-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Scheduling","concept":"Timer cancellation testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should timer cancellation tests verify?","answer":"They should verify that cancellation prevents the promised future action when cancellation occurs before the applicable execution point and that already-started work follows its defined cancellation semantics.","requirements":["Define cancellation boundary","Define action-start semantics","Define cleanup behavior"],"failure_mode":"A cancelled timer still performs its side effect or leaves resources active.","root_cause":"Cancellation state is not synchronized with action execution or cleanup.","testing_guidance":"Test cancellation before scheduling, immediately before execution, and after execution begins where those states exist.","verification_method":"Observe both action occurrence and resource lifecycle after cancellation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B46-R001","D20-B57-R001"],"provenance":["established asynchronous timing-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B57-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Time-Dependent Testing","subtopic":"Time Zones","concept":"Timezone conversion testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should timezone-sensitive behavior be tested?","answer":"Tests should distinguish absolute instants from local representations and exercise conversions across relevant offsets, daylight-saving transitions, ambiguous local times, and invalid or unsupported timezone identifiers where applicable.","failure_mode":"An event occurs at the correct instant but is represented or scheduled incorrectly in a local timezone.","root_cause":"The implementation conflates local time representation with absolute time semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use explicit instants and timezone identifiers rather than relying on the test machine's local timezone.","verification_method":"Compare converted instants and representations against an authoritative time model or library contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B57-R001","D20-B57-R002"],"provenance":["established date/time testing practice","relevant platform date-time specifications"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B58-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Network Testing","subtopic":"Network Failure Simulation","concept":"Network dependency fault testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What network dependency failures should integration tests simulate when relevant?","answer":"Relevant cases include connection refusal, timeout, DNS failure, connection reset, partial response, protocol error, rate limiting, unavailable dependency, and malformed dependency responses.","constraints":["Simulation must reflect the application's supported failure model","Do not infer production frequency from injected faults"],"testing_guidance":"Inject one failure condition at a time and verify classification, timeout, retry, fallback, cleanup, and recovery behavior.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior with the declared network failure contract and preserve evidence of the injected condition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D18-B49-R001","D18-B50-R002","D20-B26-R001"],"provenance":["established distributed/network testing practice"]}

