{"record_id":"D20-B71-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Invariants","concept":"Invariant-driven testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should invariants be used to strengthen software tests?","answer":"An invariant should define a condition that remains true across the relevant execution states, and tests should evaluate that condition before, during, or after state-changing operations where the contract requires it.","requirements":["Define invariant","Define applicability scope","Identify state transitions that may affect it","Define observable verification method"],"constraints":["An invariant must be independently justified","An invariant is not automatically true merely because existing code assumes it"],"failure_mode":"A test validates individual outputs while a persistent state invariant is violated.","root_cause":"The test oracle does not represent the system property that must remain stable.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate invariants after successful, failed, cancelled, concurrent, and recovery paths where relevant.","verification_method":"Trace each invariant to a requirement, specification, mathematical property, or independently justified system rule.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R005","D20-B28-R001","D20-B51-R004"],"provenance":["established invariant-based testing practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 testing concepts"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B71-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Invariants","concept":"Temporal invariant testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What is a temporal invariant in software testing?","answer":"A temporal invariant constrains how system behavior may evolve over a sequence of events rather than only specifying one isolated state.","requirements":["Define relevant event sequence","Define temporal condition","Define state or event observations"],"failure_mode":"Each individual state appears valid while the sequence violates a required ordering or lifecycle rule.","root_cause":"Testing validates snapshots but not behavior across transitions.","testing_guidance":"Test sequences including normal progression, interruption, repetition, cancellation, and recovery where applicable.","verification_method":"Evaluate the invariant over the complete event trace rather than only the final state.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B51-R001","D20-B51-R004","D20-B46-R004"],"provenance":["established state-based and temporal testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B71-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Invariants","concept":"Conservation-property testing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can conservation properties be used as test oracles?","answer":"A conservation property checks that a quantity, relationship, or accounting identity remains consistent across transformations when the system contract requires conservation.","requirements":["Define conserved quantity","Define allowed transformations","Define precision/tolerance rules if applicable"],"failure_mode":"A transformation creates or loses state that should have been conserved.","root_cause":"A state transition incorrectly updates one or more related quantities.","testing_guidance":"Apply transformations and verify the conservation relation rather than relying only on individual output examples.","verification_method":"Calculate the property independently from the implementation under test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D20-B28-R001"],"provenance":["established property-based testing practice","established invariant-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B71-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Invariants","concept":"Invariant violation evidence","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"What evidence should be preserved when an invariant fails?","answer":"Preserve the relevant input, initial state, event sequence, observed state, invariant definition, expected condition, implementation version, and execution context required to reproduce and classify the violation.","requirements":["Stable test identity","Input/state evidence","Invariant definition","Observed violation","Execution context"],"failure_mode":"An invariant failure is reported without enough state or sequence evidence to reproduce it.","root_cause":"The test framework captures only the final assertion message.","verification_method":"Confirm that the preserved artifact is sufficient to reconstruct the failing invariant evaluation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R005","D20-B44-R004","D20-B71-R001"],"provenance":["established failure-artifact and invariant-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B71-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Invariants","concept":"Invariant independence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should important invariants be independently justified from the implementation?","answer":"An invariant derived directly from implementation behavior can reproduce the same defect and therefore provide weak oracle evidence.","requirements":["Independent specification or rationale","Implementation-independent calculation where practical","Traceable expected condition"],"verification_method":"Review whether the invariant can remain valid if the implementation changes while preserving the intended behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R005","D20-B52-R001","D20-B71-R001"],"provenance":["established test-oracle design practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B72-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Oracle Construction","concept":"Independent test oracle","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a test oracle independent?","answer":"An oracle is independent when its expected result is derived from a specification, mathematical relation, reference implementation, trusted external artifact, or other evidence that is not simply the same logic being tested.","requirements":["Identify oracle source","Identify oracle assumptions","Define applicability"],"failure_mode":"The test reproduces the production algorithm to calculate its expected answer.","root_cause":"Oracle and implementation share the same defect mechanism.","verification_method":"Compare the oracle's derivation path with the implementation under test and document independence assumptions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R005","D20-B52-R001","D20-B71-R005"],"provenance":["established software testing oracle practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B72-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Oracle Construction","concept":"Reference implementation oracle","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When can a reference implementation act as a test oracle?","answer":"A reference implementation can provide oracle evidence when it is independently developed or independently justified and its behavior is appropriate for the tested domain; identical implementation logic should not be treated as independent evidence.","constraints":["Reference implementation may contain defects","Performance or scalability characteristics may differ","Specification remains authoritative when conflicts arise"],"verification_method":"Validate the reference implementation's provenance, scope, assumptions, and known limitations before using it as an oracle.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R002","D20-B69-R001","D20-B72-R001"],"provenance":["established differential-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B72-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Oracle Construction","concept":"Partial oracle","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a partial oracle?","answer":"A partial oracle verifies only a subset of properties that are known and testable, leaving other behavior explicitly unverified rather than inventing an expected result.","requirements":["Define verified property subset","Identify unverified dimensions","Preserve limitations"],"failure_mode":"A test is interpreted as proving complete correctness when it verifies only selected properties.","root_cause":"Oracle scope is not represented in the test evidence.","verification_method":"Record exactly which properties the oracle establishes and which remain unknown.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B10-R001","D20-B72-R001"],"provenance":["established test-oracle and verification practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B72-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Oracle Construction","concept":"Oracle uncertainty","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"How should uncertain expected behavior be represented in testing knowledge?","answer":"Expected behavior should be marked unresolved or partially specified rather than converted into a fabricated assertion. The uncertainty should identify what evidence is missing.","requirements":["Record unresolved behavior","Identify evidence gap","Preserve affected test scope"],"constraints":["Do not silently choose an arbitrary expected result","Do not classify absence of an oracle as a passing condition"],"verification_method":"Require authoritative specification, independent evidence, or explicit stakeholder resolution before promoting the behavior to a definitive oracle.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"INTERNAL_DATASET_GOVERNANCE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B01-R002","D20-B72-R003"],"provenance":["D20 testing evidence governance"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B72-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Oracle Construction","concept":"Oracle mutation challenge","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"How can mutation testing evaluate oracle strength?","answer":"Mutation testing modifies implementation behavior in controlled ways and checks whether the test suite detects the introduced faults; surviving mutants indicate behavior that may not be adequately distinguished by existing tests.","requirements":["Defined mutation operator set","Stable test suite","Mutant classification policy"],"constraints":["A surviving mutant does not automatically prove a test defect","Equivalent mutants may be behaviorally indistinguishable"],"verification_method":"Classify surviving mutants and determine whether they represent meaningful missing behavioral evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B10-R002","D20-B47-R003"],"provenance":["mutation-testing research and established practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B73-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Metamorphic Testing","concept":"Metamorphic relation validity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be true before a metamorphic relation is used as a test oracle?","answer":"The relation must be justified by the semantics of the operation and must specify how transformed inputs should relate to transformed outputs.","requirements":["Source input domain","Input transformation","Expected output relation","Applicability conditions"],"failure_mode":"A transformation is assumed to preserve behavior when the underlying operation does not guarantee that relation.","root_cause":"The metamorphic relation was selected from intuition rather than semantics.","verification_method":"Trace the relation to mathematical, domain, protocol, or specification semantics.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R001","D20-B52-R001","D20-B72-R001"],"provenance":["metamorphic testing research","established property-based testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B73-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Metamorphic Testing","concept":"Permutation invariance","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"When is permutation invariance a valid metamorphic relation?","answer":"It is valid only when the operation's semantics guarantee that reordering relevant independent inputs does not change the required result.","requirements":["Define independent inputs","Define operation semantics","Define expected relation"],"failure_mode":"A test assumes order independence for an order-sensitive operation.","root_cause":"The relation ignores ordering semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use permutation transformations only for operations whose contract establishes commutativity or order independence.","verification_method":"Validate the relation against the operation specification before using it as an oracle.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B73-R001","D20-B28-R001"],"provenance":["established metamorphic-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B73-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Metamorphic Testing","concept":"Scale transformation testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How can scaling transformations test numerical or algorithmic behavior?","answer":"When a mathematical or domain relation defines how outputs should change under scaling of inputs, transformed cases can test that relation without requiring a manually calculated oracle for every input.","constraints":["Floating-point rounding and overflow must be considered","The relation must be valid for the tested domain"],"testing_guidance":"Define tolerance and domain boundaries explicitly rather than using arbitrary equality.","verification_method":"Compare transformed output with the mathematically justified relation and record any tolerance basis.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D20-B73-R001"],"provenance":["metamorphic testing research","numerical testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B73-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Metamorphic Testing","concept":"Metamorphic relation composition","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"Can metamorphic relations be composed?","answer":"Multiple valid transformations can sometimes be composed, but the combined relation must remain within the domain where each constituent relation is valid.","requirements":["Valid individual relations","Compatible transformation domains","Defined composition semantics"],"failure_mode":"A combined transformation crosses a condition under which one constituent relation no longer holds.","root_cause":"Applicability conditions were not propagated through composition.","verification_method":"Check each transformation's preconditions and the composed relation independently.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B73-R001","D20-B73-R003"],"provenance":["metamorphic testing research"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B73-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Metamorphic Testing","concept":"Metamorphic failure diagnosis","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a metamorphic test failure be diagnosed?","answer":"Preserve the source input, transformation, source output, transformed input, transformed output, relation definition, applicability conditions, and environment before determining whether the implementation, oracle, or transformation is defective.","procedure":["Capture source case","Capture transformed case","Verify relation applicability","Reproduce both executions","Check oracle derivation","Classify failure"],"verification_method":"Do not attribute the failure to the implementation until the metamorphic relation itself has been independently validated.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B44-R004","D20-B73-R001","D20-B72-R004"],"provenance":["metamorphic testing research","established failure-analysis practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B74-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Differential Testing","concept":"Differential testing domain","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"When is differential testing appropriate?","answer":"Differential testing is appropriate when multiple implementations, versions, execution modes, or independent transformations can be given equivalent inputs and their observable behavior can be meaningfully compared.","requirements":["Comparable input domain","Comparable observable outputs","Defined disagreement policy"],"constraints":["Differences may be legitimate","Undefined or unspecified behavior weakens differential evidence"],"verification_method":"Classify the semantic domain before interpreting disagreements as defects.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R002","D20-B54-R002","D20-B69-R001"],"provenance":["differential testing research","established testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B74-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Differential Testing","concept":"Version differential testing","knowledge_type":"REGRESSION_TEST","question":"How can different software versions be compared through differential testing?","answer":"The same controlled corpus can be executed against supported versions and behavior differences can be classified as intentional changes, compatibility regressions, bug fixes, or unresolved discrepancies.","requirements":["Version identity","Stable corpus","Expected compatibility policy","Comparable environment"],"verification_method":"Map each difference to release or compatibility documentation before classifying it as a regression.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B27-R003","D20-B54-R005","D20-B59-R005"],"provenance":["established regression and differential-testing practice"]}

