{"record_id":"D20-B74-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Differential Testing","concept":"Differential-output normalization","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why may differential test outputs require normalization?","answer":"Equivalent implementations can differ in formatting, ordering of non-semantic fields, diagnostic wording, or representation while preserving the required semantic result.","requirements":["Define semantic fields","Define ignorable representation differences","Preserve raw outputs for diagnosis"],"failure_mode":"Formatting differences generate false differential failures.","root_cause":"Comparison operates on representations rather than the behavioral contract.","verification_method":"Compare normalized semantic representations while retaining raw artifacts.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B69-R003","D20-B74-R001"],"provenance":["established differential-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B74-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Differential Testing","concept":"Differential disagreement triage","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a differential disagreement be triaged?","answer":"First confirm equivalent inputs and environments, then classify the behavior under the relevant specification, identify the earliest divergence, preserve all artifacts, and determine whether the discrepancy is expected, implementation-specific, or defective.","procedure":["Verify input equivalence","Verify environment","Normalize outputs","Check specification","Locate first divergence","Classify discrepancy","Create regression evidence if confirmed"],"verification_method":"Require evidence for the selected classification and retain unresolved disagreements as unresolved rather than forcing a conclusion.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B44-R003","D20-B54-R003","D20-B69-R005"],"provenance":["established differential-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B75-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Reference Artifacts","concept":"Golden artifact scope","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When should golden artifacts be used as test oracles?","answer":"Golden artifacts are appropriate when a stable, reviewed representation captures required behavior and changes to that representation are intentionally governed.","requirements":["Stable artifact identity","Review provenance","Change policy","Comparison semantics"],"failure_mode":"Large snapshots change frequently and tests accept changes without determining whether behavior actually changed.","root_cause":"Artifact governance is missing.","verification_method":"Require review and classification for golden-artifact changes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R003","D20-B48-R002"],"provenance":["established golden-master and snapshot-testing practice"]}

