{"record_id":"D20-B75-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Reference Artifacts","concept":"Golden artifact update review","knowledge_type":"REGRESSION_TEST","question":"How should intentional golden-artifact changes be reviewed?","answer":"A golden artifact change should be accompanied by evidence that the underlying behavioral change is intentional, within scope, and compatible with the updated contract.","requirements":["Original artifact","New artifact","Behavioral diff","Change rationale","Updated contract"],"verification_method":"Review semantic differences rather than accepting every snapshot update automatically.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B48-R002","D20-B75-R001"],"provenance":["established snapshot/golden-artifact governance practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B75-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Reference Artifacts","concept":"Golden artifact minimization","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"Why should golden artifacts be minimized to behaviorally meaningful content?","answer":"Minimizing irrelevant representation reduces review noise and prevents unrelated formatting changes from obscuring meaningful behavioral differences.","requirements":["Identify semantic fields","Remove unstable metadata where appropriate","Preserve required behavior evidence"],"failure_mode":"A tiny formatting or timestamp change creates a large artifact diff.","root_cause":"The artifact contains uncontrolled incidental representation.","verification_method":"Verify that the retained artifact fields correspond to the behavior being tested.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B74-R004","D20-B75-R001"],"provenance":["established snapshot-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B75-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Reference Artifacts","concept":"Golden artifact false-confidence control","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is the danger of treating an approved golden artifact as proof of correctness?","answer":"A golden artifact proves only that current behavior matches the artifact under the tested conditions; if the artifact or test domain is wrong, the suite can consistently validate incorrect behavior.","failure_mode":"Incorrect behavior becomes permanently accepted because the test artifact was never independently validated.","root_cause":"The golden artifact is treated as an unquestionable oracle.","verification_method":"Periodically validate important golden artifacts against specifications, independent references, or other evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B28-R005","D20-B72-R001","D20-B75-R002"],"provenance":["established test-oracle governance practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B76-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Formal Specification","concept":"Specification-to-test traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should formal or semi-formal specifications connect to tests?","answer":"Each test should identify the specification clause, property, invariant, state transition, or requirement it provides evidence for, while preserving the possibility that one requirement needs multiple tests.","requirements":["Stable requirement identity","Stable test identity","Explicit trace relationship","Evidence status"],"verification_method":"Audit bidirectional traceability from requirements to tests and tests to requirements.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B30-R003","D20-B71-R001"],"provenance":["ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 testing concepts","requirements traceability practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B76-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Formal Specification","concept":"Uncovered specification clause","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"What should happen when a specification clause has no corresponding test evidence?","answer":"The clause should be marked as uncovered or otherwise classified according to the verification plan; absence of a test must not be represented as evidence of satisfaction.","requirements":["Identify uncovered clause","Record verification status","Preserve rationale if testing is intentionally omitted"],"verification_method":"Audit specification-to-test mappings and explicitly classify gaps.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B10-R003","D20-B76-R001"],"provenance":["established requirements-verification practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B76-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Formal Specification","concept":"Requirement decomposition for testing","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should broad requirements be decomposed into testable obligations?","answer":"Decompose a broad requirement into observable behaviors, preconditions, postconditions, invariants, error conditions, security constraints, performance constraints where applicable, and environmental assumptions.","procedure":["Identify requirement","Separate normative clauses","Define observable outcomes","Define failure conditions","Define evidence required","Map tests to obligations"],"verification_method":"Ensure each decomposed obligation has an appropriate verification method or explicit unresolved status.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B21-R005","D20-B76-R001"],"provenance":["requirements engineering and verification practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B76-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Formal Specification","concept":"Proof obligation versus test obligation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the distinction between a proof obligation and a test obligation?","answer":"A proof obligation requires establishing a property through a formal reasoning method within defined assumptions, while a test obligation requires obtaining empirical evidence through selected executions. Neither should be silently represented as the other.","requirements":["Identify verification method","Record assumptions","Record evidence scope"],"verification_method":"Label formal reasoning and empirical execution evidence separately in the knowledge fabric.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B01-R005","D20-B70-R002"],"provenance":["formal methods literature","established software verification practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B76-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Formal Specification","concept":"Assumption tracking","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must verification assumptions be recorded?","answer":"A verification result is meaningful only within the assumptions under which the property, model, proof, or test was established.","requirements":["Record environmental assumptions","Record input assumptions","Record model assumptions","Record tool/version assumptions"],"failure_mode":"A verification result is generalized beyond the conditions under which it was established.","root_cause":"Assumption scope was omitted from the evidence record.","verification_method":"Audit each verification artifact for explicit assumptions and applicability boundaries.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B52-R002","D20-B76-R004"],"provenance":["formal verification practice","D20 evidence-governance principles"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B77-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency Verification","concept":"Schedule-independent concurrency testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a concurrency test robust against scheduling variation?","answer":"A robust concurrency test asserts synchronization guarantees and allowed outcomes rather than assuming one particular interleaving or timing sequence.","requirements":["Define synchronization contract","Define allowed outcome set","Control only necessary scheduling points"],"failure_mode":"The test passes or fails depending on incidental thread scheduling.","root_cause":"The test encodes timing rather than concurrency semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use synchronization primitives, barriers, controlled schedulers, or repeated interleavings where appropriate.","verification_method":"Determine whether every accepted outcome satisfies the concurrency contract.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R003","D20-B64-R003","D20-B67-R003"],"provenance":["established concurrency-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B77-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency Verification","concept":"Concurrency invariant testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should concurrency tests verify in addition to absence of crashes?","answer":"They should verify shared-state invariants, ownership rules, ordering constraints, progress guarantees, and resource lifecycle properties required by the system contract.","failure_mode":"The program does not crash but silently violates shared-state consistency.","root_cause":"Concurrency testing uses process stability as its only oracle.","verification_method":"Assert invariants after relevant synchronization points and terminal states.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B71-R001","D20-B77-R001"],"provenance":["established concurrency-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B77-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency Verification","concept":"Deadlock detection evidence","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should deadlock testing be represented?","answer":"Deadlock tests should define the expected progress property, controlled workload, timeout or detection mechanism, and evidence needed to distinguish a deadlock from a slow but progressing operation.","failure_mode":"A timeout is automatically classified as deadlock without sufficient evidence.","root_cause":"No progress criterion or diagnostic evidence exists.","testing_guidance":"Capture thread/task states and synchronization evidence where supported.","verification_method":"Require evidence of blocked dependency cycles or equivalent progress failure before classifying a deadlock.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B57-R003","D20-B44-R002","D20-B77-R001"],"provenance":["established concurrency-debugging and testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B77-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency Verification","concept":"Race reproduction matrix","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should intermittent concurrency failures be reproduced systematically?","answer":"Vary relevant scheduling, workload, timing, processor or runtime conditions, synchronization points, and repetition while preserving each failure artifact and environment identity.","procedure":["Capture failing workload","Identify concurrency boundaries","Vary controlled schedule factors","Repeat under equivalent conditions","Preserve first reproducible trace","Classify evidence"],"constraints":["Repetition count alone does not prove race absence","Performance characteristics should not be inferred without measurement"],"verification_method":"Compare successful and failing schedules and identify the earliest divergent synchronization condition.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R002","D20-B64-R003","D20-B68-R004"],"provenance":["established concurrency debugging practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B77-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Concurrency Verification","concept":"Concurrency test nondeterminism audit","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should concurrency tests be audited for nondeterminism?","answer":"Review timing dependencies, uncontrolled shared state, random scheduling assumptions, resource contention, external dependencies, and insufficient synchronization, then classify whether nondeterminism is intentional test variation or accidental instability.","verification_method":"Repeat the test under controlled and varied scheduling conditions and preserve evidence of any divergent results.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B03-R005","D20-B45-R004","D20-B77-R001"],"provenance":["established flaky-test and concurrency-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B78-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Fault Injection","concept":"Fault injection oracle","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a fault-injection test verify?","answer":"A fault-injection test should verify the system's documented behavior under a deliberately introduced failure, including classification, recovery, cleanup, bounded retries, and resulting state where applicable.","requirements":["Defined injected fault","Expected failure classification","Recovery contract","Cleanup contract"],"failure_mode":"The injected failure occurs but the test checks only that an exception was raised.","root_cause":"Fault-injection tests lack recovery and state oracles.","verification_method":"Evaluate the complete failure contract, not merely failure occurrence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R002","D20-B58-R001","D20-B71-R001"],"provenance":["established fault-injection testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B78-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Fault Injection","concept":"Single-fault attribution","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should controlled fault injection normally isolate one primary injected fault at a time?","answer":"Single-fault injection improves causal attribution by reducing ambiguity about which injected condition caused the observed behavior, while combined faults can be used separately when multi-fault resilience is itself the test objective.","constraints":["Some resilience requirements explicitly require multiple simultaneous failures","Injection infrastructure must not introduce an unrecognized second fault"],"verification_method":"Record all injected conditions and infrastructure effects for each test execution.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B07-R002","D20-B26-R005"],"provenance":["established fault-injection practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B78-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Fault Injection","concept":"Fault-injection safety boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What safety boundary should govern fault injection?","answer":"Fault injection should be constrained to explicitly authorized test environments and controlled resources, with mechanisms that prevent unintended impact on production or unrelated systems.","requirements":["Authorized environment","Scoped target","Rollback or cleanup mechanism","Audit trail"],"constraints":["Do not treat production experimentation as equivalent to controlled testing","Injection mechanisms must themselves be tested"],"verification_method":"Verify target identity, authorization, scope, and cleanup before executing an injection.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D19-B51-R004","D20-B07-R002"],"provenance":["established fault-injection governance practice","OWASP security testing principles"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B78-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Fault Injection","concept":"Recovery convergence testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should recovery after an injected fault be verified?","answer":"Recovery testing should verify that the system returns to an explicitly defined healthy or stable state without losing required data, duplicating side effects, or retaining stale failure state.","requirements":["Define pre-fault state","Define failure condition","Define recovery state","Define convergence criteria"],"failure_mode":"The service becomes available again but internal state remains inconsistent.","root_cause":"Recovery tests validate availability without validating state convergence.","verification_method":"Compare post-recovery state against the declared convergence invariants.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B26-R003","D20-B71-R001","D18-B50-R003"],"provenance":["established resilience and fault-injection testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B78-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Fault Injection","concept":"Fault injection evidence preservation","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What evidence should be retained from a fault-injection test?","answer":"Retain the injected fault identity, target scope, timing, configuration, application version, observed behavior, logs, recovery state, cleanup status, and test classification.","requirements":["Injection identity","Execution context","Observed result","Recovery evidence","Cleanup evidence"],"verification_method":"Confirm that the evidence distinguishes the injected condition from unrelated infrastructure failures.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B05-R005","D20-B49-R005","D20-B78-R001"],"provenance":["established fault-injection evidence practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B79-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Security Verification","concept":"Authorization invariant testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should authorization invariants be tested across workflows?","answer":"Tests should verify that every protected operation maintains the required actor-resource-action relationship across direct access, alternate workflows, state transitions, retries, and error paths.","requirements":["Actor identity model","Resource scope","Allowed actions","Denied actions","State-dependent authorization rules"],"failure_mode":"Authorization is checked on the primary path but bypassed through an alternate workflow or state transition.","root_cause":"Authorization coverage is endpoint-based rather than behavior- and state-based.","testing_guidance":"Build an authorization matrix and exercise equivalent operations through different supported paths.","verification_method":"Verify both allowed and denied cases against the authoritative policy.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B08-R002","D19-B14-R001","D20-B76-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP authorization testing guidance","NIST SP 800-53 access-control principles"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B79-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Security Verification","concept":"Sensitive-data test artifact control","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should tests prevent sensitive data from leaking into test artifacts?","answer":"Test fixtures, logs, failure artifacts, snapshots, and diagnostics should use controlled non-sensitive data or approved synthetic representations unless real data is explicitly authorized and protected.","requirements":["Data classification","Fixture policy","Artifact retention policy","Redaction controls"],"failure_mode":"Secrets or personal data appear in logs, snapshots, or failure reports.","root_cause":"Test diagnostics capture unrestricted input or environment state.","verification_method":"Scan representative artifacts and review fixture provenance and retention controls.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B08-R004","D20-B49-R004","D19-B54-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP logging and sensitive-data guidance","NIST security practices"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B79-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Security Verification","concept":"Security regression preservation","knowledge_type":"REGRESSION_TEST","question":"What should happen after a confirmed security defect is fixed?","answer":"A representative regression test should preserve the previously violated security property, with enough evidence to detect recurrence without encoding unnecessary exploit-specific assumptions.","requirements":["Confirmed defect","Security property","Regression input","Expected secure behavior","Provenance"],"verification_method":"Verify that the regression fails against the vulnerable behavior and passes against the corrected behavior when safe and reproducible.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B08-R005","D20-B47-R004","D19-B60-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP security testing guidance","established security regression practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B79-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Security Verification","concept":"Parser differential security testing","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why can parser disagreement be security-relevant?","answer":"If two components interpret the same input differently, validation and enforcement may occur over different semantic representations, potentially creating inconsistent security decisions.","requirements":["Equivalent input representation","Multiple parser paths","Security-relevant semantic comparison"],"failure_mode":"A front-end parser rejects or sanitizes a value while a downstream parser interprets it differently.","root_cause":"Parser behavior is not semantically aligned across security boundaries.","verification_method":"Compare normalized interpretations and verify consistent security policy decisions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B29-R003","D19-B29-R001","D20-B74-R001"],"provenance":["OWASP parser and input-validation guidance","established differential security testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B79-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Security Verification","concept":"Security test oracle minimization","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should security tests avoid overfitting to one implementation detail?","answer":"A security test should preserve the security property that must hold rather than depend unnecessarily on a particular internal implementation, error message, or code structure.","failure_mode":"A secure refactor breaks the security suite even though the security invariant remains satisfied.","root_cause":"The test verifies implementation shape rather than security behavior.","verification_method":"Review whether assertions remain valid under behavior-preserving secure refactoring.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B06-R003","D20-B79-R001","D20-B79-R003"],"provenance":["OWASP security testing guidance","established regression-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B80-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Performance Verification","concept":"Performance test oracle boundaries","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What must be defined before a performance test can produce meaningful evidence?","answer":"A performance test requires a defined workload, environment, measurement method, metric, acceptance condition, and reproducibility context; otherwise the result should not be interpreted as a general performance claim.","requirements":["Workload definition","Environment identity","Metric definition","Measurement method","Acceptance criterion","Relevant configuration"],"constraints":["No benchmark result may be fabricated","Performance evidence is scoped to its measured conditions"],"verification_method":"Verify that the recorded result includes enough context to interpret the measurement correctly.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"NOT_VALIDATED","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B41-R005","D20-B70-R004"],"provenance":["established performance-testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B80-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Performance Verification","concept":"Performance regression threshold testing","knowledge_type":"REGRESSION_TEST","question":"How should performance regression thresholds be governed?","answer":"A performance regression threshold should be tied to a defined workload and measurement methodology, with expected variance and environment scope documented rather than treated as an absolute universal number.","requirements":["Stable workload","Measurement methodology","Environment scope","Threshold rationale","Variance policy"],"failure_mode":"A normal measurement fluctuation causes a false regression or a real regression is hidden by an excessively broad threshold.","root_cause":"Thresholds lack evidence-based scope and variance handling.","verification_method":"Review threshold history and measurement conditions before changing acceptance criteria.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B80-R001","D20-B06-R004"],"provenance":["established performance-regression testing practice"]}

{"record_id":"D20-B80-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Performance Verification","concept":"Load-model validity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must a performance test workload represent the intended operational behavior?","answer":"A benchmark using an unrealistic workload can produce technically correct measurements that do not answer the engineering question being asked.","requirements":["Representative workload model","Defined traffic or operation mix","Relevant data sizes","Relevant concurrency or arrival pattern"],"failure_mode":"Optimization decisions are based on a workload that differs materially from supported operational usage.","root_cause":"Workload assumptions were not validated against the target use case.","verification_method":"Compare workload characteristics with the documented operational model and record mismatches.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":["D20-B80-R001","D20-B70-R004"],"provenance":["established performance-engineering practice"]}

