{"record_id":"D20-B81-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Model-based testing","concept":"Model-to-implementation behavioral conformance","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should model-based testing verify?","answer":"Model-based testing should verify that observable implementation behavior conforms to the explicitly defined model for the tested state, transition, input, and output domain.","principle":"The model is a test oracle or behavioral reference only to the extent that its semantics are explicitly defined and independently justified.","requirements":["Define model states and transitions","Define observable outputs and permitted behaviors","Generate or select tests from the model","Record deviations between model and implementation"],"constraints":["An incomplete or incorrect model can produce misleading tests","Model equivalence does not prove unrestricted implementation correctness"],"failure_mode":"Tests execute successfully while failing to exercise or verify required model behavior.","solution":"Trace model states and transitions to concrete test cases and preserve evidence for every conformance decision.","testing_guidance":"Include nominal, invalid, boundary, transition, and recovery paths represented by the model.","verification_method":"Check that every tested implementation outcome is evaluated against the corresponding model-defined behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Model-based testing practice","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 testing principles"],"relationships":["D20-B28-R005","D20-B76-R001","D20-B80-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B81-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Model-based testing","concept":"State transition coverage","knowledge_type":"METRIC","question":"What does state transition coverage measure?","answer":"State transition coverage measures which defined model transitions have been exercised by tests; it does not by itself establish that transition semantics or requirements are correct.","principle":"Structural coverage of a model is evidence of exercised model structure, not proof of implementation correctness.","testing_guidance":"Track visited states and transitions and identify reachable but untested transitions.","failure_mode":"High transition coverage is interpreted as complete behavioral verification.","solution":"Combine transition coverage with requirement coverage, oracle evidence, invalid-path testing, and outcome verification.","verification_method":"Compare executed transition identifiers against the authoritative model transition inventory.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Model-based testing practice","Software testing coverage principles"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R001","D20-B76-R002","D20-B80-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B81-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Model-based testing","concept":"Unreachable model behavior","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"How should unreachable model states or transitions be handled?","answer":"Unreachable model states or transitions should be identified explicitly and classified as either intentional, constrained by preconditions, or evidence of a model or implementation defect.","principle":"Untested behavior must not be silently treated as irrelevant merely because test generation cannot reach it.","failure_mode":"Coverage gaps are hidden by treating unreachable transitions as automatically excluded.","solution":"Perform reachability analysis, document the governing preconditions, and preserve unresolved reachability questions as verification gaps.","testing_guidance":"Attempt representative paths to the disputed state and record the blocking precondition or implementation behavior.","verification_method":"Review each excluded state or transition against documented requirements and model constraints.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Model-based testing practice","Formal state-space analysis principles"],"relationships":["D20-B07-R005","D20-B26-R003","D20-B76-R002"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B81-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Model-based testing","concept":"Model mutation testing","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"Why mutate a behavioral model during testing?","answer":"Mutating a behavioral model can evaluate whether the test suite is sensitive to meaningful changes in modeled behavior rather than merely exercising model structure.","principle":"A test suite that cannot distinguish a meaningful model mutation may lack adequate behavioral discrimination.","constraints":["Mutations must represent meaningful semantic changes","Equivalent mutations may not be useful","Mutation results require interpretation"],"failure_mode":"Model mutations are generated mechanically without determining whether each mutation changes observable behavior.","solution":"Define mutation operators with explicit semantic intent and classify surviving mutations.","testing_guidance":"Record the original model behavior, mutation, generated tests, observed detection result, and survivor classification.","verification_method":"Verify that each surviving mutation has a documented explanation such as equivalent behavior, unreachable behavior, weak oracle, or missing test.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESEARCH_SUPPORTED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Mutation testing research","Model-based testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B72-R005","D20-B47-R003","D20-B80-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B81-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Model-based testing","concept":"Model drift","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is model drift in a testing system?","answer":"Model drift occurs when the behavioral model used for testing no longer accurately represents the current requirements, supported behavior, or intended implementation contract.","failure_mode":"Tests continue passing against an obsolete model while current required behavior changes.","cause":"Model maintenance is disconnected from requirement and implementation evolution.","root_cause":"The model is treated as static test infrastructure instead of versioned engineering knowledge.","solution":"Version models, link model changes to requirements and tests, review behavioral deltas, and preserve historical versions for regression analysis.","testing_guidance":"Require model-version identity in generated test artifacts and compare model changes against affected tests.","verification_method":"Verify that every active model version has an identified scope, provenance, owner, and requirement relationship.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Model-based testing practice","Configuration and requirements traceability principles"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R004","D20-B27-R001","D20-B49-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B82-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"State-space testing","concept":"State-space explosion","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can exhaustive state-space testing become infeasible?","answer":"The number of possible system states and execution paths can grow rapidly as state variables, concurrency, inputs, and transitions increase.","principle":"Exhaustive exploration is feasible only when the relevant state space is sufficiently bounded or reduced without invalidating the verification objective.","performance_implications":"State-space size can dominate test-generation and exploration cost; no universal performance threshold should be assumed.","solution":"Use justified state abstraction, equivalence classes, partial-order reduction, bounded exploration, representative scenarios, or targeted risk-based exploration.","testing_guidance":"Document the explored state space, reduction technique, assumptions, and excluded regions.","verification_method":"Review whether the reduction preserves the property being tested for the stated verification scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Formal verification literature","Model checking practice"],"relationships":["D20-B26-R005","D20-B77-R001","D20-B80-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B82-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"State-space testing","concept":"State abstraction","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"When is state abstraction useful in testing?","answer":"State abstraction is useful when multiple concrete states are equivalent with respect to the property or behavior being verified and can therefore be represented by a smaller abstract state space.","principle":"An abstraction is valid only relative to the property and observations it is intended to preserve.","failure_mode":"Distinct states with materially different behavior are collapsed into one abstract state.","solution":"Define abstraction criteria explicitly and verify that relevant properties remain distinguishable after abstraction.","testing_guidance":"Test representative concrete states within each abstract equivalence class and preserve the abstraction mapping.","verification_method":"Review the abstraction relation against the target property and required observable behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Formal methods","Model checking principles"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R001","D20-B81-R002","D20-B82-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B82-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"State-space testing","concept":"Partial-order reduction","knowledge_type":"TECHNIQUE","question":"What problem does partial-order reduction address?","answer":"Partial-order reduction reduces redundant exploration of concurrent execution orderings when different interleavings are equivalent with respect to the property under verification.","principle":"Concurrency exploration can omit redundant schedules only when the reduction preserves the verification property.","constraints":["Applicability depends on independence assumptions","Incorrect reduction can hide concurrency defects","The preserved property must be explicitly defined"],"testing_guidance":"Record the scheduling reduction rule and retain targeted tests for interactions that the reduction may not cover.","verification_method":"Verify the reduction assumptions against the concurrency semantics and target property.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESEARCH_SUPPORTED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Model checking research","Concurrency verification literature"],"relationships":["D20-B26-R002","D20-B77-R001","D20-B77-R004"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B82-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"State-space testing","concept":"Bounded exploration","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What does bounded verification establish?","answer":"Bounded verification establishes properties only within the explicitly defined exploration bound and assumptions.","principle":"A property verified up to a bound must not be represented as universally verified beyond that bound.","failure_mode":"A bounded result is interpreted as proof of unrestricted behavior.","solution":"Store the bound, assumptions, explored domain, and property scope with the verification result.","verification_method":"Check that generated reports and downstream reasoning preserve the bounded scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Formal verification literature","Bounded model checking principles"],"relationships":["D20-B76-R004","D20-B80-R001","D20-B82-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B82-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"State-space testing","concept":"State-space verification evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should accompany a state-space verification result?","answer":"A state-space verification result should identify the model or implementation version, explored domain, reduction or abstraction rules, property definition, assumptions, result, unresolved regions, and evidence artifacts.","requirements":["Version identity","Property identity","Exploration scope","Reduction assumptions","Result evidence","Unresolved or excluded states"],"verification_method":"Reject verification records that omit the scope necessary to interpret the result.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Formal verification practice","Verification evidence principles"],"relationships":["D20-B75-R002","D20-B76-R005","D20-B80-R004"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B83-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Property-based testing","concept":"Property scope validity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a property suitable for property-based testing?","answer":"A suitable property defines a meaningful relationship or invariant that should hold over a clearly specified input domain and observable behavior.","principle":"A property is useful only when its domain, preconditions, expected relation, and failure interpretation are sufficiently precise.","failure_mode":"A vague property passes while failing to distinguish incorrect behavior.","solution":"Define preconditions, transformation or invariant, expected relation, and observable failure evidence explicitly.","testing_guidance":"Include valid, boundary, invalid, and adversarial inputs where the property permits them.","verification_method":"Review whether the property can distinguish at least the relevant classes of incorrect behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Property-based testing practice","Software testing literature"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D20-B71-R001","D20-B72-R003"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B83-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Property-based testing","concept":"Generator validity","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must generated test inputs be validated?","answer":"Generated inputs must satisfy the intended domain and distribution constraints so that failures represent meaningful behavior rather than malformed generators or unintended invalid input classes.","failure_mode":"The generator produces mostly irrelevant or invalid inputs and the test suite provides misleading coverage.","solution":"Specify generator preconditions, validity rules, distribution assumptions, and rejected-input handling.","testing_guidance":"Measure or inspect generated input classes and retain representative failing inputs.","verification_method":"Verify generator outputs against the declared input domain and test intent.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Property-based testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R003","D20-B29-R001","D20-B83-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B83-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Property-based testing","concept":"Shrinking correctness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should test-case shrinking preserve?","answer":"Shrinking should preserve the failure condition relevant to the property while reducing the input toward a smaller reproducible counterexample.","principle":"A smaller input is useful only if it remains a valid counterexample under the same test semantics.","failure_mode":"A shrinker changes the input domain or failure cause and produces a misleading minimal case.","solution":"Verify that the minimized input still satisfies required preconditions and reproduces the target property violation.","testing_guidance":"Store the original failing input, shrink steps or final minimized input, property result, and execution context.","verification_method":"Re-run or independently verify the minimized counterexample when execution is available.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Property-based testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B05-R002","D20-B72-R005","D20-B83-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B83-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Property-based testing","concept":"Stateful property testing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"When is stateful property-based testing useful?","answer":"Stateful property-based testing is useful when correctness depends on sequences of operations and evolving state rather than isolated function inputs.","principle":"Generated operation sequences should be constrained by valid state transitions and evaluated against explicit state properties or a reference model.","testing_guidance":"Define commands, preconditions, state transitions, postconditions, and failure artifacts for generated sequences.","failure_mode":"Generated operations violate state preconditions so frequently that failures do not provide meaningful system evidence.","solution":"Use a state model to constrain command generation and compare observed state with expected state.","verification_method":"Verify that each generated sequence has a valid model trace and an explicit property evaluation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Property-based testing practice","State-machine testing principles"],"relationships":["D20-B81-R001","D20-B73-R001","D20-B26-R003"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B83-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Property-based testing","concept":"Property suite adequacy","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a property suite be evaluated?","answer":"A property suite should be evaluated by the behaviors and defect classes it can distinguish, not by the number of generated examples alone.","principle":"Input volume is not equivalent to semantic coverage.","testing_guidance":"Use mutation analysis, targeted counterexamples, boundary classes, and independent oracle checks to assess property strength.","verification_method":"Document surviving meaningful mutations and unresolved property gaps.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESEARCH_SUPPORTED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Property-based testing literature","Mutation testing research"],"relationships":["D20-B47-R003","D20-B72-R005","D20-B80-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B84-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Specification-based testing","concept":"Specification ambiguity detection","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"What should testing do when a requirement has multiple plausible interpretations?","answer":"Testing should expose the ambiguity rather than silently selecting an interpretation and treating the resulting behavior as verified.","failure_mode":"Different implementations or tests encode incompatible interpretations of the same requirement.","root_cause":"The specification lacks an explicit observable contract or decision rule.","solution":"Record competing interpretations, identify affected behavior, and resolve the requirement before declaring conformance.","testing_guidance":"Create separate test cases for materially different interpretations when clarification is unavailable.","verification_method":"Require an explicit specification decision or documented assumption for the selected interpretation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Requirements engineering practice","Software testing principles"],"relationships":["D20-B76-R005","D20-B28-R005","D20-B49-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B84-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Specification-based testing","concept":"Negative requirement testing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should explicitly forbidden behavior be tested?","answer":"Forbidden behavior is part of the effective contract when requirements define what the system must reject, deny, prevent, or never perform.","principle":"Verification must cover both required behavior and explicitly prohibited behavior.","testing_guidance":"Represent forbidden outcomes as negative assertions with precise expected behavior and evidence requirements.","failure_mode":"Tests verify successful scenarios but never establish that prohibited actions are prevented.","solution":"Create negative tests linked directly to each relevant prohibition or security constraint.","verification_method":"Trace each critical prohibition to at least one executable or otherwise independently reviewable verification mechanism.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Software testing practice","Secure software engineering principles"],"relationships":["D20-B07-R001","D20-B08-R001","D20-B79-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B84-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Specification-based testing","concept":"Cross-requirement interaction testing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why test interactions between requirements?","answer":"Requirements that are individually satisfied can still conflict or produce incorrect behavior when their constraints interact during the same workflow.","principle":"Verification should include relevant combinations of requirements, not only isolated requirement checks.","testing_guidance":"Build an interaction matrix for requirements with shared state, resources, permissions, timing, or outputs.","failure_mode":"Each requirement has passing tests but their combined behavior violates one or more constraints.","solution":"Add focused interaction tests for materially coupled requirements and preserve requirement-to-test traceability.","verification_method":"Verify that identified high-risk requirement interactions have explicit expected outcomes.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Requirements-based testing practice"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R002","D20-B30-R003","D20-B76-R003"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B84-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Specification-based testing","concept":"Assumption-sensitive test validity","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must assumptions be attached to specification-based tests?","answer":"A test result is meaningful only within the assumptions under which the requirement, environment, data, and expected behavior were defined.","failure_mode":"A test is reused in an environment or scenario where its original assumptions no longer hold.","solution":"Record assumptions as structured test metadata and invalidate or review tests when affected assumptions change.","verification_method":"Compare active assumptions with the execution context before accepting the result as evidence.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Requirements traceability practice","Verification evidence principles"],"relationships":["D20-B76-R005","D20-B49-R003","D20-B44-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B84-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Specification-based testing","concept":"Specification change impact","knowledge_type":"REGRESSION_TEST","question":"What should happen when a specification changes?","answer":"A specification change should trigger impact analysis across affected implementation behavior, tests, oracles, fixtures, documentation, and dependent requirements.","principle":"Tests encode historical expectations and must be reviewed when the authoritative contract changes.","testing_guidance":"Compare specification versions and identify added, removed, modified, and invalidated test expectations.","verification_method":"Require review evidence showing which tests remain valid, which require modification, and which are intentionally retired.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Requirements change-management practice","Regression testing principles"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R004","D20-B27-R002","D20-B48-R002"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B85-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test adequacy","concept":"Coverage criterion interpretation","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should coverage criteria be interpreted relative to a testing objective?","answer":"A coverage criterion measures a defined dimension of exercised behavior and is meaningful only relative to the risks, requirements, and verification objective it represents.","principle":"No single coverage metric is sufficient evidence of overall correctness.","failure_mode":"A high structural coverage number is used as a substitute for behavioral, security, performance, or requirement coverage.","solution":"Use multiple complementary coverage dimensions tied to explicit verification goals.","verification_method":"Review whether each claimed coverage metric maps to a relevant risk or requirement dimension.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Software testing coverage principles","ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119"],"relationships":["D20-B10-R001","D20-B30-R001","D20-B80-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B85-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test adequacy","concept":"Mutation score interpretation","knowledge_type":"METRIC","question":"How should mutation results be interpreted?","answer":"Mutation results indicate how many defined mutations were detected by the test suite, but surviving mutations require classification because some may be equivalent, unreachable, irrelevant, or evidence of test weakness.","principle":"Mutation results are diagnostic evidence, not a universal correctness score.","constraints":["Mutation operator quality affects interpretation","Equivalent mutants may survive legitimately","The mutation domain must be documented"],"verification_method":"Review surviving mutations and classify their causes before using mutation results for engineering decisions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"RESEARCH_SUPPORTED","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Mutation testing research"],"relationships":["D20-B10-R002","D20-B72-R005","D20-B83-R005"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B85-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test adequacy","concept":"Requirement coverage gap","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a requirement coverage gap?","answer":"A requirement coverage gap exists when a requirement has no adequate verification evidence within the declared verification scope.","failure_mode":"A requirement appears implemented but lacks an associated test, oracle, review, proof, or other justified verification mechanism.","root_cause":"Traceability is incomplete or verification planning did not identify the requirement.","solution":"Link every applicable requirement to one or more verification obligations and classify unresolved obligations explicitly.","verification_method":"Query the requirement-to-verification graph for requirements with no valid verification path.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Requirements traceability practice","Verification engineering principles"],"relationships":["D20-B30-R003","D20-B49-R001","D20-B76-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B85-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test adequacy","concept":"Risk-weighted adequacy","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"How should test adequacy account for risk?","answer":"Test adequacy should consider the consequence and likelihood of relevant failure classes in addition to structural coverage or test volume.","principle":"Verification effort should be proportionate to the material risk and uncertainty of the behavior being tested.","constraints":["Risk estimates may be uncertain","Risk weighting must not erase mandatory requirements","Critical controls may require dedicated verification regardless of estimated likelihood"],"testing_guidance":"Maintain traceability between high-risk behaviors and their dedicated tests, oracles, and verification evidence.","verification_method":"Audit whether high-impact failure classes have explicit verification coverage and independent evidence where required.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Risk-based testing practice","Software assurance principles"],"relationships":["D20-B06-R001","D20-B08-R001","D20-B80-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B85-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test adequacy","concept":"Adequacy claim boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must accompany a claim that a test suite is adequate?","answer":"An adequacy claim must state the criterion, scope, assumptions, covered behavior, excluded behavior, evidence basis, and known unresolved gaps.","principle":"Adequacy is a scoped engineering claim, not an absolute property of a test suite.","verification_method":"Reject unqualified adequacy claims that omit their evaluation criteria or scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Software testing practice","Verification evidence principles"],"relationships":["D20-B10-R004","D20-B80-R005","D20-B85-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B86-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test oracle engineering","concept":"Oracle hierarchy","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How can test oracles be categorized by independence and evidence strength?","answer":"Oracles can range from direct specification assertions to independent reference implementations, differential comparisons, mathematical properties, invariants, and human review; their evidentiary strength depends on independence, precision, and suitability to the behavior.","principle":"An oracle should be evaluated independently from the implementation behavior it is intended to judge.","tradeoff":"Highly independent oracles can be expensive to construct, while implementation-derived oracles can be easier but risk reproducing the same defect.","verification_method":"Record oracle type, independence assumptions, scope, and known limitations with each verification result.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Software testing literature","Oracle problem research"],"relationships":["D20-B72-R001","D20-B72-R002","D20-B72-R003","D20-B74-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B86-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test oracle engineering","concept":"Implementation-derived oracle coupling","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can an oracle derived from the implementation be unsafe?","answer":"An implementation-derived oracle can repeat the same defect, assumptions, or algorithmic error as the system under test and therefore fail to provide independent evidence.","failure_mode":"The implementation and oracle produce the same incorrect result and the test passes.","solution":"Introduce independent specifications, properties, reference algorithms, differential implementations, or manually reviewed expected outcomes where practical.","verification_method":"Assess whether the oracle shares critical logic or data transformations with the implementation.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Software testing literature","Oracle problem research"],"relationships":["D20-B72-R001","D20-B72-R002","D20-B86-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B86-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test oracle engineering","concept":"Oracle disagreement classification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should happen when two valid-looking oracles disagree?","answer":"Oracle disagreement should be preserved as unresolved evidence until the difference is explained through specification, domain constraints, implementation analysis, or independent reference evidence.","failure_mode":"One oracle is arbitrarily selected as correct and the other is discarded.","solution":"Record both oracle outputs, versions, assumptions, scope, and the evidence used to resolve or retain the disagreement.","verification_method":"Require explicit resolution evidence before converting disagreement into a definitive pass or fail.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Testing oracle principles","Evidence-based verification practice"],"relationships":["D20-B72-R004","D20-B74-R005","D20-B44-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B86-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test oracle engineering","concept":"Oracle versioning","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should test oracles be versioned?","answer":"Oracle behavior can change when specifications, reference implementations, models, or expected artifacts change, so verification results require oracle identity and version context.","requirements":["Oracle identifier","Oracle version","Input or domain scope","Expected-result semantics","Change history where available"],"failure_mode":"Historical test results are reinterpreted using a newer oracle without recognizing semantic changes.","solution":"Persist oracle identity and version with test definitions and result artifacts.","verification_method":"Reject historical evidence whose oracle identity cannot be reconstructed when oracle semantics affect the result.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Verification evidence management practice"],"relationships":["D20-B75-R002","D20-B76-R005","D20-B44-R005"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B86-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Test oracle engineering","concept":"Oracle provenance chain","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What provenance should a critical test oracle preserve?","answer":"A critical oracle should preserve its source requirement or specification, construction method, dependencies, version, assumptions, and validation evidence.","principle":"Oracle correctness is itself a verification concern.","verification_method":"Trace critical oracle definitions to authoritative requirements or independently justified properties and record unresolved assumptions.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Verification evidence principles","Requirements traceability practice"],"relationships":["D20-B72-R001","D20-B76-R001","D20-B49-R001"]} 
