{"record_id":"D20-B87-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Advanced Verification Testing","subtopic":"Formal and semi-formal verification","concept":"Proof-to-test complementarity","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do proof and testing complement each other?","answer":"Formal proof can establish properties within a formalized model and assumptions, while testing provides empirical evidence over selected executions and real implementation behavior; neither should automatically be represented as replacing the other across all verification scopes.","tradeoff":"Proof can provide broad guarantees for precisely modeled properties but may require substantial formalization; testing can expose implementation and integration behavior but samples execution space.","verification_method":"Map each verification obligation to proof, test, review, analysis, or a justified combination according to its scope.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Formal methods literature","Software verification practice"],"relationships":["D20-B76-R004","D20-B80-R001","D20-B87-R001"]} 
