{"record_id":"D20-B91-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Python","concept":"Deterministic unit test with explicit oracle","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","language":"Python","language_version":"3.11+","objective":"Demonstrate a minimal deterministic unit test whose expected result is explicit.","source_code":"def normalize_name(value: str) -> str:\n    return \" \".join(value.strip().split()).casefold()\n\n\ndef test_normalize_name() -> None:\n    assert normalize_name(\"  Alice   Smith \") == \"alice smith\"","expected_behavior":"The test checks a deterministic normalization contract using a direct expected value.","testing_guidance":"Keep the test independent from external files, network services, system time, and mutable global state.","verification_method":"Review that the assertion represents the intended contract and that the test input exercises whitespace normalization and case normalization.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Python testing practice","D20 testing governance"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R001","D20-B03-R002","D20-B72-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B91-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"JavaScript","concept":"Explicit asynchronous failure test","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","language":"JavaScript","language_version":"ES2022+","objective":"Demonstrate testing of an asynchronous rejection without converting failure into success.","source_code":"async function loadUser(fetcher, id) {\n    const response = await fetcher(`/users/${id}`);\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n    return response.json();\n}\n\nasync function testLoadUserRejectsOnServerError() {\n    const fetcher = async () => ({ ok: false, status: 503 });\n\n    await expectRejects(\n        () => loadUser(fetcher, \"42\"),\n        \"HTTP 503\"\n    );\n}\n\nasync function expectRejects(operation, expectedMessage) {\n    let rejected = false;\n    try {\n        await operation();\n    } catch (error) {\n        rejected = true;\n        if (error.message !== expectedMessage) throw error;\n    }\n    if (!rejected) throw new Error(\"Expected operation to reject\");\n}","expected_behavior":"The test fails if the operation unexpectedly resolves or if the error contract changes from the expected server-error behavior.","testing_guidance":"The helper is intentionally minimal; production projects should use their selected test framework's native rejection assertions where available.","verification_method":"Inspect that both resolution and incorrect rejection messages cause test failure.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["ECMAScript asynchronous programming practice","D20 asynchronous testing principles"],"relationships":["D20-B46-R002","D20-B21-R003","D20-B44-R002"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B91-R003","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"TypeScript","concept":"Type-safe property assertion","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","language":"TypeScript","language_version":"5.x","objective":"Demonstrate a test helper whose expected value is represented by the same domain type as the implementation contract.","source_code":"type User = { id: string; active: boolean };\n\nfunction isActive(user: User): boolean {\n    return user.active;\n}\n\nfunction assertEqual<T>(actual: T, expected: T): void {\n    if (!Object.is(actual, expected)) {\n        throw new Error(`Expected ${String(expected)}, got ${String(actual)}`);\n    }\n}\n\nfunction testIsActive(): void {\n    const user: User = { id: \"u1\", active: true };\n    assertEqual(isActive(user), true);\n}","expected_behavior":"The test validates the boolean contract while the generic assertion prevents mismatched assertion types at compile time.","testing_guidance":"Compile the test with the project's configured TypeScript compiler settings before treating the example as implementation evidence.","verification_method":"Verify that the assertion compares the returned domain value to the intended expected value and that compilation is part of the test pipeline.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["TypeScript language documentation","D20 implementation-testing governance"],"relationships":["D20-B27-R003","D20-B91-R001","D20-B85-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B91-R004","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Java","concept":"Boundary-value unit test","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","language":"Java","language_version":"17+","objective":"Demonstrate explicit testing of a boundary condition.","source_code":"static int percentage(int value) {\n    if (value < 0 || value > 100) {\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"value outside range\");\n    }\n    return value;\n}\n\n@Test\nvoid acceptsLowerAndUpperBoundaries() {\n    assertEquals(0, percentage(0));\n    assertEquals(100, percentage(100));\n}","test_input":["0","100"],"expected_test_result":"Both boundary inputs return their corresponding values without an exception.","testing_guidance":"Boundary tests should be paired with just-outside-boundary tests when rejection behavior is part of the contract.","verification_method":"Verify that the test cases correspond directly to the declared inclusive domain [0,100].","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Java testing practice","Boundary value analysis"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R002","D20-B07-R004","D20-B85-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B91-R005","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"C++","concept":"Invariant assertion in a unit test","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","language":"C++","language_version":"C++17+","objective":"Demonstrate testing an invariant rather than only a single output.","source_code":"#include <cassert>\n#include <vector>\n\nint sum(const std::vector<int>& values) {\n    int result = 0;\n    for (int value : values) result += value;\n    return result;\n}\n\nvoid test_sum_append_zero_invariant() {\n    std::vector<int> values{2, 3, 5};\n    auto before = sum(values);\n    values.push_back(0);\n    auto after = sum(values);\n    assert(before == after);\n}","expected_behavior":"Appending zero preserves the sum, establishing a specific conservation-style property.","testing_guidance":"The property is scoped to integer arithmetic and does not establish absence of overflow or correctness for all numeric domains.","verification_method":"Verify the invariant independently from the implementation's internal algorithm.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["C++ language practice","Property and invariant testing principles"],"relationships":["D20-B71-R003","D20-B83-R001","D20-B83-R005"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B92-R001","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Rust","concept":"Result-based failure contract testing","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","language":"Rust","language_version":"1.75+","objective":"Demonstrate explicit testing of a fallible API's error contract.","source_code":"fn parse_port(value: &str) -> Result<u16, &'static str> {\n    value.parse::<u16>().map_err(|_| \"invalid port\")\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn rejects_non_numeric_port() {\n    let result = parse_port(\"abc\");\n    assert_eq!(result, Err(\"invalid port\"));\n}","expected_behavior":"Invalid numeric input produces the declared error rather than an implicit panic or successful result.","testing_guidance":"Test representative invalid values and valid boundary values separately.","verification_method":"Verify the expected error is part of the API contract and that the test does not rely on implementation-specific panic behavior.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Rust language testing practice","Failure contract testing principles"],"relationships":["D20-B03-R003","D20-B07-R001","D20-B29-R001"]} 
{"record_id":"D20-B92-R002","dataset_id":"D20","dataset_version":"1.0","topic":"Implementation-Oriented Testing","subtopic":"Go","concept":"Table-driven boundary testing","knowledge_type":"CODE_EXAMPLE","language":"Go","language_version":"1.22+","objective":"Demonstrate compact coverage of multiple explicit input classes.","source_code":"func IsValidAge(age int) bool {\n    return age >= 18 && age <= 120\n}\n\nfunc TestIsValidAge(t *testing.T) {\n    cases := []struct {\n        name string\n        age  int\n        want bool\n    }{\n        {\"below minimum\", 17, false},\n        {\"minimum\", 18, true},\n        {\"maximum\", 120, true},\n        {\"above maximum\", 121, false},\n    }\n\n    for _, tc := range cases {\n        t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n            if got := IsValidAge(tc.age); got != tc.want {\n                t.Fatalf(\"got %v, want %v\", got, tc.want)\n            }\n        })\n    }\n}","test_input":["17","18","120","121"],"expected_test_result":"Each boundary classification matches the declared inclusive range.","testing_guidance":"Keep case names descriptive and ensure each case represents a distinct equivalence or boundary class.","verification_method":"Verify the table covers both valid boundaries and adjacent invalid values.","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"ILLUSTRATIVE","evidence_level":"ESTABLISHED_PRACTICE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":["Go testing practice","Equivalence partitioning and boundary value analysis"],"relationships":["D20-B02-R001","D20-B02-R002","D20-B30-R001"]} 
